window.STATECRAFT_ANALYSIS = {"CA-AB1170":{"summary":"AB 1170 is California's annual code maintenance bill. It makes nonsubstantive technical changes across various code provisions to carry out the Legislative Counsel's recommendations for maintaining the codes. It was signed and chaptered as Chapter 67, Statutes of 2025, on July 28, 2025.","mechanics":["Implements the existing statutory duty of the Legislative Counsel to advise the Legislature on legislation needed to maintain the codes.","Makes only nonsubstantive changes, such as corrections, conforming edits and cleanup, according to the official abstract.","Affects unspecified 'various provisions of the law'. The full text was not available, so the specific code sections amended cannot be confirmed here.","Enacted as Chapter 67, Statutes of 2025, chaptered 2025-07-28."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. This is a routine housekeeping vehicle rather than a gaming policy bill, and it is unusual only in that it may touch many code sections at once without changing their legal effect.","watch":"Check the chaptered text to see whether any technical edits land in Penal Code gambling provisions, the Gambling Control Act (Bus. & Prof. Code §19800 et seq.) or horse-racing wagering statutes, so that compliance citations and internal references stay accurate.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"AB1170","text_chars":0,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:57:52.202Z"},"CA-AB831":{"summary":"AB 831 (Chapter 623, Statutes of 2025) makes it a misdemeanor to operate, conduct, or offer an online sweepstakes game in California. It also makes it a crime for payment processors, financial institutions, geolocation providers, gaming content suppliers, platform providers, and media affiliates to knowingly and willfully support or promote one. It also expands California's existing unfair-practices rules for contests and sweepstakes so they expressly reach internet websites and online apps, and it redefines simulated gambling to include examples such as lottery games, bingo, and sports wagering.","mechanics":["Unfair-practice expansion: the existing ban on electronic simulated-gambling methods tied to sweepstakes prizes now expressly covers 'any method, including an internet website or an online application.' The term 'gambling-themed games' is deleted, and 'gambling' is redefined to include examples such as lottery games, bingo, sports wagering, or any game that mimics or simulates similar gambling.","Payment-system trigger: it is an unfair practice to offer such games using a payment system that lets a person play a simulated gambling program for direct or indirect consideration, with eligibility for a prize, cash or cash equivalents, or a chance to win them. This applies in a business establishment, on the internet, or via an online app. Games that do not award cash prizes or cash equivalents are expressly not made unlawful.","New crime: it is unlawful for any person or entity to operate, conduct, or offer an online sweepstakes game (as defined in the bill) in California.","Secondary liability: any entity, financial institution, payment processor, geolocation provider, gaming content supplier, platform provider, or media affiliate that knowingly and willfully supports, directly or indirectly, the operation, conduct, or promotion of an online sweepstakes game in the state is also liable.","Penalties: misdemeanor punishable by a fine of $1,000 to $25,000, up to one year in county jail, or both. The bill creates a state-mandated local program with no reimbursement required.","Carve-outs: lawful games and methods of gambling enterprises licensed under the Gambling Control Act, and California State Lottery operations, are exempt. So are limited, occasional promotional sweepstakes by for-profit businesses that are incidental to substantial bona fide sales of consumer products or services and are not a vehicle for ongoing gambling."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue on the list is AR HB 1861, which makes operating an illegal online casino or sports betting a felony within a broader interactive gaming framework. AB 831 is narrower in target (online sweepstakes games) and lighter in penalty (misdemeanor), but it reaches further down the supply chain by naming payment processors, geolocation vendors, content suppliers, platforms, and media affiliates. Unlike GA HB 686, HI HB 2570, and HI HB 1308, which would legalize and tax sports wagering, AB 831 authorizes nothing; it is purely prohibitive. KY HB 904, by contrast, regulates rather than bans an adjacent contest product (fantasy contests).","watch":"Watch the 'knowingly and willfully' secondary-liability clause and the express inclusion of sports wagering in the simulated-gambling definition, because vendors, payment partners, and media affiliates that also serve sweepstakes-model or prize-based sports products face misdemeanor exposure in California, where sports betting remains unlicensed and the exemption covers only Gambling Control Act licensees and the State Lottery.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"AB831","text_chars":0,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:58:03.046Z"},"IA-SSB1240":{"summary":"SSB 1240 (renumbered SF 660) appropriates a total of $22 million from Iowa's sports wagering receipts fund (Code section 8.57I) for FY 2025-2026: $14 million to the Department of Education for an education support personnel pay supplement distributed to school districts by enrollment, and $8 million to the Department of Public Safety for the public safety equipment fund. It does not change sports wagering tax rates, licensing, or operator obligations.","mechanics":["Sec. 1: $14,000,000 from the sports wagering receipts fund to the Department of Education for FY 2025-2026 to supplement compensation of education support personnel, defined as regular and part-time non-salaried school district employees.","Each district's share equals $14,000,000 multiplied by its FY 2024-25 budget enrollment divided by the statewide total budget enrollment. Districts must report support-personnel data to the department on or before July 1, 2025 to qualify.","The funds are treated as miscellaneous income under section 257.2, are excluded from district cost computations under chapter 257, and are paid on the foundation aid schedule under section 257.16.","Sec. 2: $8,000,000 from the same fund to the Department of Public Safety for deposit in the public safety equipment fund (section 80.48).","Sec. 3: the education appropriation takes effect upon enactment. The public safety appropriation follows the normal effective date.","Per the explanation, the fund is fed by the internet fantasy sports contest tax (Code 99E.6) and the sports wagering net receipts tax (Code 99F.11(5))."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is a routine appropriation of existing sports wagering and fantasy sports tax revenue. It is notable mainly because it earmarks wagering receipts for non-gaming purposes (school support staff pay and police equipment) rather than for problem-gambling or regulatory programs, and it leaves the underlying tax structure untouched.","watch":"Operators face no direct new obligations here, but growing legislative reliance on sports wagering receipts for recurring education and public safety spending could build pressure to raise the 99F.11 net receipts tax rate in future sessions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5691,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:58:06.975Z"},"GA-HB686":{"summary":"HB 686 (House Higher Education Committee substitute, LC 56 0648S) would legalize online-only sports betting in Georgia, with the Georgia Lottery Corporation acting as both regulator and \"master sports betting licensee.\" It caps the market at 16 Type 1 licenses, most tied to Georgia pro teams and sports venues, and imposes a 27.5% privilege tax on adjusted gross income, with proceeds going to a dedicated trust fund for the constitutionally specified purposes. The bill has a contingent effective date tied to Georgia constitutional provisions, and it died after being reported favorably by substitute on 2026-03-06.","mechanics":["Market structure (50-27-133): no more than 16 Type 1 licenses. Five are reserved for qualifying Georgia pro teams (baseball, football, men's and women's basketball, soccer). One each goes to a 30-year PGA-tour tournament body, a 30-year invitational golf facility, a NASCAR track owner, and the Lottery Corporation. The remaining seven go to online sports betting services providers through public procurement. Each eligible entity may contract with only one platform provider, and entities must give notice of intent within 60 days or be permanently disqualified.","Tax (50-27-150): 27.5% annual privilege tax on adjusted gross income, paid monthly by the services provider. AGI is wagers minus federal excise tax minus winnings paid, including the cash value of merchandise. Negative AGI may be carried forward. The tax is in lieu of other state and local taxes and fees on sports betting operations or proceeds, and it is deposited quarterly into the Georgia Sports Betting Proceeds Trust Fund.","Fees and terms (50-27-130, -131): Type 1 licenses carry a $100,000 application fee and a $1M annual fee. Services provider licenses are $10,000/$100,000, and supplier licenses are $2,000/$20,000. Licenses run five years. Operators must hold a reserve of at least $500,000 or their outstanding wager liabilities, whichever is greater. Operators licensed elsewhere may receive temporary licenses for up to one year.","Launch timing: Lottery rules are due within 90 days, exempt from the Georgia APA. Applications for all 16 licenses open on the same date, and timely applicants get a common go-live date no later than July 31, 2027. No Type 1 licensee may launch until at least one procurement-based provider is licensed.","Wagering scope: bettors must be 21 or older and physically present in Georgia. Wagers covered include in-play, parlay, props and exchange wagering. Proposition bets on collegiate sporting events are banned (50-27-135). Youth events with mostly under-18 participants and Special Olympics are excluded. Official league data is defined for settling tier 2 wagers, but the operative mandate falls in the truncated portion of the text.","Regulatory limits and carve-outs: the corporation may not set hold or payout percentages (50-27-123(b)). Leagues and Type 1 eligible entities may sign revenue-share agreements without corporation approval (50-27-131(d)). Institutional investors are exempt from qualification requirements. The corporation must set responsible gaming rules covering self-exclusion (SSN required), bet limits for problem gamblers, 1-800-GAMBLER notices and advertising guidelines aimed at minors."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity","advertising","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like HI HB 2570 and HI HB 1308, HB 686 creates a full licensing regime from scratch. Hawaii places regulation in DBEDT or the Department of Law Enforcement and taxes through a general excise tax on operator licenses. Georgia instead uses its lottery corporation as regulator and operator, with a team- and venue-tethered cap of 16 licenses. Georgia's 27.5% GGR rate sits well above Colorado's 10% rate (whose deductions CO HB 25-1311 narrows) and is in the range of higher-tax states like Louisiana after LA HB 639. Unlike CO SB 131, Georgia leaves deposit and bet limits to rulemaking rather than fixing statutory caps.","watch":"Watch three things: the scarcity of the seven procurement-based skins and the one-platform-per-Type-1-entity rule; the 27.5% rate, where the only promotional relief is the merchandise-as-winnings language in the AGI definition; and the truncated official league data provisions for tier 2 wagers, which could impose data-purchase costs.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"LC 56 0648S/hs","text_chars":89148,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:58:14.467Z"},"IA-SF605":{"summary":"SF 605 amends Iowa Code section 99F.18 to declare that all winnings from sports wagering authorized under chapter 99F are Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax, with any state tax withheld remitted to the Department of Revenue on the winner's behalf. It also amends section 422.16(2)(d) to require state income tax withholding on sports wagering winnings whenever federal withholding is required on the same winnings under the Internal Revenue Code. The Act takes effect January 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 splits 99F.18 into two subsections: subsection 1 keeps the existing rule for slot machine winnings, and new subsection 2 extends the same earned-income treatment and remittance duty to sports wagering winnings under chapter 99F.","Sec. 2 adds a state withholding trigger for chapter 99F sports wagering that is tied to the federal trigger. State tax must be withheld whenever the Internal Revenue Code requires federal withholding on the same winnings, rather than at a fixed dollar threshold.","Existing state withholding thresholds are unchanged: winnings over $600 under chapters 99B and 99G, over $1,000 under chapter 99D, and over $1,200 for slot machines under chapter 99F.","Operators must remit amounts withheld under 422.16(2) to the Iowa Department of Revenue on behalf of the winner.","Sec. 3 makes the Act effective January 1, 2026. It was signed by the Governor on March 28, 2025."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the operator-side tax measures listed, SF 605 addresses only bettor-level income tax withholding and does not touch the operator tax rate or how it is calculated. Those operator-side measures are LA HB 639, which changes the online sports wagering tax, and CO HB 25-1311, which limits deductions from net sports betting proceeds. It also differs from the comprehensive authorization bills, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570, which create licensing and tax frameworks from scratch. What is unusual is that Iowa ties state withholding to the federal trigger instead of setting its own dollar threshold, so the state obligation will move automatically with any change to federal withholding rules.","watch":"Operators should update payout and withholding systems before January 1, 2026 so that Iowa withholding is triggered in lockstep with federal withholding on sports wagering winnings. They should also watch for Department of Revenue guidance on the withholding rate, remittance timing, and how federal threshold changes flow through.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled with Governor's Action","text_chars":2977,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:58:31.006Z"},"LA-SB339":{"summary":"Act 494 gives Louisiana's gaming regulators (the Gaming Control Board and State Police gaming enforcement division, the Racing Commission, the Office of Charitable Gaming, and the Lottery Corporation via the gaming enforcement division) express statutory authority to obtain state and FBI fingerprint-based criminal history record information on applicants, licensees, permittees, and employees. Under the Gaming Control Board's Title 27 jurisdiction, which expressly includes the Louisiana Sports Wagering Act and Fantasy Sports Contests Act, every license or permit applicant and every person required to be found suitable must now submit a full set of fingerprints for a national check. The Act takes effect August 1, 2026.","mechanics":["New R.S. 27:15(B)(1)(b) requires every applicant for a license or permit, and any person required to be found suitable under Title 27, to submit fingerprints to the gaming enforcement division, which forwards them to the FBI. The board and the division are the designated recipients of the results.","New R.S. 27:20.1 defines 'criminal history record information' to include state arrest, prosecution, and conviction records, including those expunged or dismissed under Code of Criminal Procedure Articles 893 and 894, plus national fingerprint-based records. The definition of 'permit' covers any permit, license, or authorization, or associated application, issued under Title 27.","Use of fingerprints is limited to eligibility determinations under the suitability standards in R.S. 27:28, 234, 235, 306, 427, and 604. The records are confidential under R.S. 27:21 and may be used exclusively to evaluate eligibility or disqualification. They are also available to the board and the Louisiana Department of Justice.","The bureau's costs under R.S. 15:587, including the costs of the FBI check, may be imposed on the individual. Under amended R.S. 47:9065(B), the bureau may charge the Lottery Corporation or the applicant a reasonable processing fee.","Parallel sections are added for the Racing Commission (R.S. 4:147.3, tied to R.S. 4:150 suitability) and the Office of Charitable Gaming (R.S. 4:705.1). R.S. 15:587(C) is rewritten to route state and FBI results to each agency.","Under new R.S. 47:9065(C), the gaming enforcement division may share criminal history results with the Lottery Corporation to determine eligibility of vendors, retailers, and employees."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the Act is unusual in two respects. It is a cross-agency housekeeping statute that recodifies express FBI fingerprint-check authority for every Louisiana gaming regulator at once. It also explicitly sweeps expunged and Article 893/894 dismissed records into what regulators may review, rather than changing any substantive licensing standard, tax, or operating rule.","watch":"Operators should watch how the board applies the new mandatory fingerprinting of 'any person required to be found suitable', which could extend to key persons, owners, vendors, and employees of sports wagering and fantasy licensees, along with the review of expunged or dismissed records and the pass-through of check fees to individuals.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"SB339 Act 494","text_chars":19808,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:59:03.076Z"},"IA-SSB1152":{"summary":"Iowa SSB 1152, a Department of Revenue bill later renumbered SF 605, amends Code section 99F.18 to declare that all winnings from sports wagering authorized under chapter 99F are Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax. It amends section 422.16(2)(d) to require Iowa income tax withholding on sports wagering winnings whenever federal withholding is required on the same winnings under the Internal Revenue Code. Amounts withheld must be remitted to the Department of Revenue on the winner's behalf.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 adds new subsection 2 to Iowa Code 99F.18. It states that all sports wagering winnings under chapter 99F are Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax laws.","Sec. 1 also requires that amounts deducted from sports wagering winnings under 422.16(2) be remitted to the Department of Revenue on behalf of the winner, mirroring the existing slot machine rule in 99F.18(1).","Sec. 2 amends 422.16(2)(d) to tie the state withholding trigger for sports wagering to the federal trigger. Under IRC rules this is generally winnings over $5,000 that are at least 300 times the wager.","The existing withholding thresholds stay the same: $600 for chapter 99B and 99G (lottery) winnings, $1,000 for chapter 99D (pari-mutuel) winnings, and $1,200 for chapter 99F slot machines.","The bill sets no state withholding rate; the rate is governed by existing 422.16.","Sec. 3 makes the Act effective January 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CO HB 25-1311 and LA HB 639, which change operator-level taxes on net sports betting proceeds or online wagering, Iowa SSB 1152 targets only patron-level income tax withholding and leaves operators' gaming tax untouched. It is also far narrower than the framework bills in GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and HI HB 2570, which authorize, license and tax sports wagering wholesale. Its distinguishing feature is that it ties the state withholding trigger to federal rules rather than setting a fixed dollar threshold, as Iowa does for slots, lottery and pari-mutuel winnings.","watch":"Operators should prepare payment and withholding systems so that, from January 1, 2026, Iowa withholding fires automatically on every sports wagering payout that triggers federal withholding (including after any future federal threshold changes), with remittance to the Department of Revenue handled as for slot winnings.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3540,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:59:53.596Z"},"IA-HSB91":{"summary":"Iowa HSB 91, a Department of Revenue proposal later renumbered HF 794, amends Iowa Code section 99F.18 to declare that all winnings from sports wagering authorized under chapter 99F are Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax. It also amends section 422.16(2)(d) to require Iowa income tax withholding on sports wagering winnings whenever federal withholding is required on the same winnings under the Internal Revenue Code. The Act takes effect January 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 adds new subsection 2 to section 99F.18 (Tax on winnings). All winnings from chapter 99F sports wagering are Iowa earned income, and amounts deducted for state tax under section 422.16(2) must be remitted to the Department of Revenue on the winner's behalf.","Sec. 2 amends section 422.16(2)(d) to tie the state withholding trigger for sports wagering to the federal trigger: withhold state tax whenever federal income tax withholding is required on the same winnings under the IRC.","Existing dollar thresholds for other games are unchanged:\n- more than $600 for chapter 99B and 99G (lottery and other) winnings;\n- more than $1,000 for chapter 99D pari-mutuel winnings;\n- more than $1,200 for chapter 99F slot machine winnings.","Covered parties are sports wagering licensees and operators under chapter 99F that pay out winnings subject to federal withholding.","Sec. 3 sets the effective date as January 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CO HB 25-1311 and LA HB 639, which change operator-level taxes, HSB 91 targets bettor income tax withholding. CO HB 25-1311 changes deductions from net sports betting proceeds, and LA HB 639 changes online sports wagering tax rates and dedications. HSB 91 leaves the operator tax untouched and instead imposes a payout-side compliance duty on operators. Also unlike GA HB 686 and HI HB 2570/HB 1308, which would create new sports betting regimes with licensing and gross revenue or excise taxes, Iowa's bill is a narrow administrative fix by the revenue department within an existing legal market. Its most unusual feature is that it sets no fixed dollar threshold for state withholding and instead conforms automatically to the federal withholding trigger.","watch":"Operators should map their payout and withholding systems to the federal IRC withholding rules before January 1, 2026, since the bill sets no fixed dollar threshold. Any future federal change to sports wagering withholding (for example, the trigger amount or the 300-to-1 odds test) would automatically change Iowa withholding obligations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3537,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:59:54.128Z"},"HI-HB2570":{"summary":"HB 2570 HD1 (the \"Representative Daniel Holt Act\") adds a new HRS chapter legalizing mobile-only sports wagering in Hawaii, regulated and licensed by the Department of Business, Economic Development, and Tourism, with at least six operator licenses and a universal launch date. It taxes operators through a new privilege tax under amended HRS §237-13(9), earmarks revenue to UH Manoa athletics, a Native Hawaiian organization, an AFL-CIO affiliate, the general fund, problem gambling, and education, and creates escalating criminal penalties for unlicensed wagering. It also expressly exempts licensed sports wagering and defined fantasy sports contests from the gambling offenses in HRS ch. 712 part III.","mechanics":["Licensing (§-5, §-6): DBEDT must issue a minimum of six sports wagering operator licenses, open only to a \"qualified gaming entity\"; $500,000 initial/renewal fee plus cost-based processing fees; five-year term; temporary licenses valid up to three years on payment of the initial fee; universal start date no later than 180 days after the Act's effective date, with equal launch for entities applying within 30 days of the application window. Supplier licenses cost $10,000 for five years, and DBEDT may accept approvals from other jurisdictions with similar standards.","Scope and wagers (§-1, §-10): mobile or digital platform only, with no kiosks or terminals at physical locations; bettors must be 21 or older and physically in Hawaii unless an interstate or international agreement under §-9 applies. Pro, collegiate, amateur, Olympic, motorsports and e-sports are covered. Youth events are excluded, as are games involving Hawaii-based colleges, including those teams' individual games within tournaments. Layoff wagers are permitted, and accounts may be funded by card, bank transfer, online payment systems, or bonuses and promotions.","Tax (§-12, §237-13(9)): operators pay the §237-13(9) privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts using accrual accounting, in lieu of all other taxes on sports wagering. Adjusted gross receipts are gross receipts less winnings and applicable taxes, including federal excise tax, with no promotional deduction. The rate text in §237-13(9) is not in the excerpt provided. §-12(a) allocates 2% to UH Manoa athletics, 2% to a Native Hawaiian organization, 1% to the Hawaii Employees Lifeline Program (an AFL-CIO affiliate) and 10% to the general fund. §-12(b) separately directs 7% of all taxes to a new DOH problem gambling special fund (§-13) and 7% to education.","Mandatory rulemaking (§-2(f)) covers: geolocation, age and identity verification; integrity and AML; data privacy and cybersecurity; a statewide voluntary exclusion program; patron-set limits on wagers, deposits and time; and advertising rules barring targeting of minors and self-excluded persons. Rules must also prohibit bets on injuries, penalties, player discipline rulings, replay reviews and any other bet type harmful to an athlete or patron. Rules are to be consistent with other jurisdictions where commercially reasonable.","Operator duties (§-7, §-8, §-11): operators need department-approved house rules. They must run suspicious-activity monitoring software, report suspicious betting above an operator-set, department-approved threshold, and display 1-800-GAMBLER in all advertising. They must keep daily records of gross and adjusted gross receipts and honor the exclusion list, which is distributed at least quarterly.","Enforcement (§-4, §-14, §-15): civil fines of up to $5,000 per violation or $10,000 per occurrence, applicable to non-licensees. Unlicensed sports wagering is a misdemeanor with a minimum $10,000 fine for a first offense, a class C felony with a minimum $50,000 fine for a second, and a class B felony with a minimum $100,000 fine for a third or later. Control persons for background checks are 15% owners with control, parents and subsidiaries, and key decision-makers."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HB 686, this is a full legalization-and-tax framework, but Hawaii is mobile-only, bans retail kiosks entirely and sets a floor of six licenses rather than a cap. Unlike KS SB 356, which only authorizes regulations for an existing Kansas market, or CO SB 131, which imposes specific statutory deposit caps, Hawaii leaves responsible-gaming limits and bans on certain prop and bet types to DBEDT rulemaking. On tax base, Hawaii's adjusted gross receipts deduct only winnings and taxes, with no promotional-credit deduction, which goes further than the deduction narrowing at issue in CO HB 25-1311. Its escalating felony penalties for unlicensed operators parallel AR HB 1861's approach.","watch":"Watch the actual §237-13(9) rate, which is absent from this excerpt, and the internally inconsistent revenue allocations in §-12(a) and (b), along with the undefined \"qualified gaming entity\" eligibility standard and the rule-delegated bans on injury, penalty and replay bets that could narrow the prop menu as the bill moves through JHA and CPC.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"HB2570_HD1","text_chars":73237,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:03.005Z"},"IA-HSB330":{"summary":"HSB 330 (renumbered HF 1041) rewrites how Iowa spends money in the sports wagering receipts fund (Code 8.57I), which is fed by the sports wagering net receipts tax (99F.11(5)) and the internet fantasy sports contest tax (99E.6). Starting FY 2025-26 it sets a standing priority list of appropriations. It also makes three $10 million one-time FY 2024-25 appropriations from the fund. It does not change tax rates, licensing or any operator obligations.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 replaces 8.57I(4)-(5) for FY 2025-26 onward with an ordered list paid from each year's deposits: first $1.75M to HHS for behavioral health and problem gambling prevention, treatment and recovery; next $750,000 to the Economic Development Authority, split equally among regional sports authority districts (15E.321); next $1.5M to the Department of Revenue's length of service award program grant fund (100B.52).","Under Sec. 2, the remainder is split 50% to the public safety equipment fund (80.48) and 50% to a new local emergency services equipment grant account run by the Department of Management. Eligible grantees are the 88 least populous counties and small cities as defined in 15.352, and DOM must adopt rules under chapter 17A.","Under Sec. 6, the $1.5M length of service award program allocation takes effect only if HF 1002 or SF 627 (or successor legislation) is enacted.","Sec. 4 appropriates from the fund for FY 2024-25: $10M to the community attraction and tourism fund, $10M to the Destination Iowa fund and $10M to the levee improvement fund. Sec. 5 makes this effective on enactment.","According to the explanation, Secs. 1 and 3 strike three things: the authority to use fund moneys for cash flow, the project reporting requirements, and the requirement that county endowment grantees consider racetrack-operating charities when using sports wagering money (no such transfers occur under current law)."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that the bill deals only with where existing sports wagering and fantasy sports tax revenue goes, not with the rate or base. It also earmarks most of the residual revenue for public safety and rural emergency equipment rather than the general fund or education.","watch":"The bill leaves rates untouched, but operators should watch HF 1041 for amendments, because tying sports wagering receipts to public safety and rural emergency grants creates constituencies that may later push for higher net receipts tax rates or a broader tax base to grow the 50/50 residual pool.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":12702,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:03.679Z"},"AZ-HB2592":{"summary":"HB 2592 is an omnibus tax-base broadening bill that repeals selected Arizona tax exemptions. For gaming, Section 1 amends A.R.S. 5-111(C) so that the state's pari-mutuel percentage, which previously did not apply to wagering on simulcasts of out-of-state races, now also applies to those pools. The rest of the visible text repeals transaction privilege tax retail exemptions for warranty and service contracts and for sales of stocks and bonds, with conforming renumbering across Titles 28 and 42.","mechanics":["Sec. 1, A.R.S. 5-111(C): the state's take of 2% of the first $1,000,000 of daily pari-mutuel pools and 5% of the amount above $1,000,000 is extended to pools on simulcasts of out-of-state races, repealing the existing carve-out.","The existing 5-111 exemptions are retained in the shown text: county fair racing meetings (D), charity racing days (E), unclaimed tickets from unclaimed property law (F), and permittee pari-mutuel receipts and purse monies from Title 42, Chapter 5 TPT (G).","Sec. 5, A.R.S. 42-5061(A): repeals the retail TPT exclusions for sales of warranty or service contracts (former para. 3) and for the sale of stocks and bonds (former para. 7), and renumbers the remaining paragraphs.","Secs. 2-4 (28-2154.01, 42-5008.01, 42-5009) make conforming cross-reference changes to reflect the renumbering.","The title also lists amendments to 42-5071, 42-5072, 42-5075, 42-6017 and 43-1111, but those sections fall outside the supplied text, which is truncated at 42-5061(A)(34).","No effective date or enforcement provision appears in the supplied text. Status: second reading in the House on 2025-01-28."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike LA HB 639 (online sports wagering tax changes) and CO HB 25-1311 (limits on operator deductions from net sports betting proceeds), HB 2592 does not touch Arizona's event wagering tax. Its gaming hook is narrower: removing a pari-mutuel simulcast exemption for horse racing. It also differs from the IA withholding bills (SF 605, SSB 1152, HSB 91), which target bettor winnings rather than operator or permittee liability. What is unusual is that a gaming tax increase is folded into a general exemption-repeal bill instead of a standalone gaming measure.","watch":"Watch whether this exemption-repeal approach widens in amendment to reach event wagering deductions or other Title 5 carve-outs, and how much extending the state's 2% to 5% take to out-of-state simulcast pools would raise costs for racetrack permittees and the advance-deposit or simulcast wagering clients.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced Version","text_chars":150205,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:10.748Z"},"LA-SB325":{"summary":"Act 491 amends R.S. 27:27.2 to require the Louisiana Gaming Control Board to adopt rules creating a separate exclusion list for retail sports books and mobile wagering, covering people already on the general gaming exclusion list, people who threaten violence or harm against anyone involved in a sporting event where the threat relates to sports gaming, and anyone the board finds threatens the state's interests or effective sports wagering regulation. It makes clear that both the board and the division can place people on exclusion lists, bars listing based on race, color, creed, national origin, sex or disability, and re-letters the existing notice, petition, penalty, sanction and hearing provisions. Effective August 1, 2026.","mechanics":["New R.S. 27:27.2(B): the board must adopt rules for a list of persons to be excluded or ejected from retail sports books and barred from the play or operation of mobile wagering under a license or contract issued under Title 27.","Three listing categories under (B): (1) persons excluded under Subsection A; (2) persons who, before, during or after a sporting event, threaten violence or harm against any person involved in the event, where the threat is related to sports gaming; (3) persons the board determines pose a threat to the state's interests, the purposes of Title 27, or the strict and effective regulation of sports wagering.","New (C): the board and the division may both place persons on the list, but not because of race, color, creed, national origin, sex or disability (as defined in R.S. 51:2232).","Carried-over provisions, re-lettered: notice by personal service or certified mail (D); a petition for removal, where the petitioner bears the burden of proof (E); entering or attempting to enter after listing is punishable by up to 6 months' imprisonment and/or a fine of up to $500 (F); and a hearing within 30 days of the petition under R.S. 27:25 (H).","Operator exposure under (G): the board may sanction licensees, permittees or the casino operator for willful failure to exclude a listed person, and may seek suspension or revocation for a pattern of willful failure.","Effective August 1, 2026, as Act No. 491. Video draw poker licensees remain exempt under (I)."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. The unusual feature is that it ties exclusion from sports wagering, including mobile accounts, to threats of violence against athletes, officials or others involved in a sporting event when the threat is linked to betting. That targets bettor harassment rather than the traditional casino exclusion grounds such as cheating or criminal history. The catch-all in (B)(3) gives the board broad discretion to exclude anyone it deems a threat to effective sports wagering regulation.","watch":"Watch the board's implementing rules for how mobile operators must identify, block and eject listed persons, and how 'willful failure to exclude' under Section 27.2(G) will be applied to online accounts, because that failure is a basis for sanctions and, if it forms a pattern, license suspension or revocation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"SB325 Act 491","text_chars":6776,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:13.297Z"},"LA-HB513":{"summary":"Act 810 rewrites Louisiana's college NIL statute (R.S. 17:3701 et seq.) and, in new R.S. 17:3705, extends NIL rights to high school athletes for the first time. The high school provisions ban NIL compensation tied to a list of restricted categories that includes gaming, gambling, or wagering, expressly including sports wagering. The college provisions update agent rules, raise disclosure obligations to a $600 threshold, broaden institutional liability shields, and require management-board NIL policies.","mechanics":["New R.S. 17:3705(E)(2)(d) bars interscholastic athletes (students in grades 9-12 at public or nonpublic high schools) from earning NIL compensation in connection with \"gaming, gambling, or wagering, including but not limited to sports wagering.\" The same list covers alcohol, cannabis, adult entertainment, tobacco and vapor products, weapons, and performance-enhancing drugs.","Under 3705(B), NIL agreements signed by high school athletes under 18 are valid only with written parental or guardian consent. Under 3705(G), athletes must disclose proposed agreements to the school before signing, and under 3705(J) an aggrieved athlete may sue for injunctive relief.","High schools and athletic associations may not bar NIL earnings or representation, or declare an athlete ineligible for engaging in them (3705(C)-(D)). Schools may still restrict NIL activity during team or academic activities, block deals that conflict with existing school agreements, and control use of school marks (3705(F)).","Under R.S. 17:3703(I), college athletes must disclose NIL contracts worth $600 or more to their institution, with reporting rules aligned to the applicable athletic governing commission.","R.S. 17:3703(D) requires athlete agents to be registered under R.S. 4:422 and comply with the federal SPARTA. Section 3703(L) shields institutions, affiliated nonprofits, and staff from liability over third-party NIL deals, except for gross negligence or intentional misconduct.","Section 3703(N) requires management boards to adopt NIL policies covering compensation, institutional involvement, use of marks, representation, education, enforcement, and a compliance contact. Institutions cannot implement the Chapter until their board acts."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like HI HB 2384 and KY SB 3, LA HB 513 adapts state NIL law to the post-settlement institutional-agreement era, with board-adopted policies and disclosure requirements. Unlike either of those, it extends NIL rights to high school athletes and expressly bars minors' NIL deals involving sports wagering. KY HB 904 is a gaming-regulation bill on fantasy contests and is not comparable in substance. The only overlap is that both touch on who may be marketed to or associated with wagering products.","watch":"Operators and their marketing affiliates should screen influencer and endorsement programs to exclude Louisiana high school athletes under R.S. 17:3705(E)(2)(d). Note that this Act imposes no comparable gaming ban on college athletes, so any restrictions there come from LGCB advertising rules and institutional policies.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"HB513 Act 810","text_chars":16928,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:18.886Z"},"HI-HB2384":{"summary":"HB 2384 HD2 adds a new subpart to HRS chapter 304A creating a name, image, and likeness (NIL) framework for University of Hawaii student-athletes. It affirms their right to earn NIL compensation, requires the university to adopt NIL, disclosure, agent, education, and Title IX policies, and requires annual aggregate reporting to the legislature. Its only gaming link is that the university may bar student-athletes from NIL deals involving gambling or sports wagering.","mechanics":["New §304A-(c)(3) lets the university prohibit NIL agreements involving \"gambling, sports wagering, or illegal drugs,\" alongside adult entertainment, firearms, tobacco/vaping, and any industry that conflicts with the university's mission or financial interests.","Student-athletes must disclose third-party NIL deals worth $600 or more, or an alternative threshold set by NCAA rules or federal law. They may rescind any institutional or third-party NIL agreement within at least ten business days of signing.","The university, conferences, and the NCAA may not unduly restrict NIL activity, except to comply with law, post-House settlement governing-body rules, or reasonable, neutral policies on conflicts of interest, time commitments, or prohibited industries.","Annual reports to the legislature begin with the 2028 session and cover state-funded institutional NIL spending by team and gender, compliance measures, and violations. Underlying records are exempt from chapter 92F disclosure.","Section 3 makes a general-fund appropriation for FY2026-27, with the amount left blank, including for direct institutional NIL payments.","Section 5 sets a placeholder effective date of July 1, 3000, a sunset of June 30, 2030, and a planned legislative review in the 2029 session."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like KY SB 3 and LA HB 513, this is a student-athlete NIL bill updating state law after the House v. NCAA settlement. Hawaii's version is limited to the University of Hawaii, whereas LA HB 513 also reaches interscholastic athletes. Unlike KY HB 904, which directly regulates gaming (fantasy contest operators, geolocation), HB 2384 touches wagering only through a permissive prohibited-industries list for NIL endorsements. Its defective 3000 effective date and blank appropriation signal it is still a vehicle for conference negotiation.","watch":"Watch whether the permissive gambling and sports-wagering NIL exclusion in §304A-(c)(3) is hardened into a mandatory ban, or broadened, as the bill moves through EDU and WAM/CPN, since that would limit operator sponsorship and endorsement deals with UH athletes if Hawaii ever authorizes wagering.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"HB2384_HD2","text_chars":13295,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:21.330Z"},"HI-HB1308":{"summary":"HB 1308 HD3 SD2 would legalize mobile-only sports wagering in Hawaii under a new HRS chapter regulated by the Department of Law Enforcement, with at least four operator licenses limited to qualified gaming entities and a separate supplier license. It taxes licensees at 10% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts through a new general excise tax paragraph, section 237-13(9), in lieu of all other taxes on sports wagering, and earmarks part of that revenue for problem gambling and education. It also creates misdemeanor and felony penalties for unlicensed sports wagering and amends the chapter 712 gambling definitions (the supplied text cuts off in Section 3).","mechanics":["Licensing: the Department of Law Enforcement must issue a minimum of four operator licenses (§-5), open only to a 'qualified gaming entity', meaning one already offering online sports wagering in Hawaii or in at least three US jurisdictions. The fee is $250,000 per five-year term plus cost-based processing fees. Supplier licenses cost $10,000 per five-year term. Three-year temporary licenses are available on payment of the initial fee. A universal start date must be set no later than 180 days after the effective date.","Tax: 10% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts under new §237-13(9), which by its terms applies to both licensed operators and suppliers, on the accrual method. The receipts base is gross wagers less winnings paid, with no express deduction for promotional credits. Of collections, 7% goes to a DOH-run problem gambling special fund and 7% to education programs (§-11, §-12).","Wagering scope: mobile or digital platforms only, with no kiosks or retail terminals. Bettors must be 21 or older and physically in Hawaii unless the department signs an interstate or international agreement (§-9). Covered events include professional, collegiate (with no in-state college carve-out), amateur, Olympic, motorsports and e-sports. Youth events are excluded. Props, in-play, parlays, pools and exchange wagering are expressly included, and fantasy contests are excluded.","Operator duties (§-8, §-10): integrity monitoring for volume and odds irregularities, with reporting to the department; suspicious-bet reporting above an operator-set, department-approved threshold; department-approved house rules; '1-800-GAMBLER' in all advertising; daily receipts records; and an employee betting ban. Layoff wagers between operators are allowed. Accounts may be funded by card, bonuses or promotions, bank transfer or e-wallet.","Responsible gaming: a department-run voluntary self-exclusion program of up to lifetime duration. Operators must make reasonable efforts to stop direct marketing to excluded persons, may share the exclusion list with out-of-state affiliates, and receive the list at least quarterly.","Enforcement: civil fines up to $5,000 per violation or $10,000 per occurrence, applicable to non-licensees (§-13). Unlicensed wagering is a misdemeanor with a fine of at least $10,000 on a first offense, a class C felony with at least $50,000 on a second, and a class B felony with at least $100,000 on a third or later offense (§-14)."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HB 686, this is a ground-up legalization act, but Hawaii's version is mobile-only with no retail or tribal/casino tether. It sets a license floor rather than a cap, and access is gated by prior multi-state operating experience. Its 10% rate matches the Colorado rate referenced in CO HB 25-1311, but Hawaii's base already allows deduction only of winnings paid, avoiding the promotional-deduction fight in Colorado. It is well below the rate-hike direction in LA HB 639. Unlike AR HB 1861, which focuses on criminalizing illegal online operators, Hawaii pairs legalization with an escalating felony ladder. It also lacks the deposit-frequency and other consumer-protection limits in CO SB 131.","watch":"Watch whether the bill survives conference after the discharge of House conferees, and whether the drafting that extends the 10% tax to suppliers and the generic 'subject to federal income tax' winnings deduction are fixed. Also watch the department's rulemaking on temporary licenses, the tight 180-day universal start date and the operator-set suspicious-bet threshold.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"HB1308_SD2","text_chars":67585,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:22.790Z"},"CO-SB163":{"summary":"Colorado SB 163 is a regulatory clean-up and enforcement bill for the Limited Gaming Act of 1991 and sports betting law. It lets the Limited Gaming Control Commission delegate more licensing duties to the Division of Gaming, authorizes division investigators to inspect and impound premises suspected of unlicensed gaming or sports betting, and extends the voluntary self-exclusion program to sports betting. It also moves game pre-approval from the commission to the division and clarifies the fingerprint-based background check process.","mechanics":["The commission may delegate to the Division of Gaming licensing duties found anywhere in the gaming act, not only the specific duties currently listed.","Division investigators and their supervisors may inspect, examine, investigate, hold, or impound any premises in Colorado where they suspect unlicensed gaming or unlicensed sports betting.","The division's voluntary self-exclusion program, which currently covers gaming, is expanded to cover sports betting.","A designee of the director is a peace officer while performing their duties, with primary authority that includes enforcing all state laws.","Licensees need prior approval from the division, rather than the commission, before offering certain games.","The bill clarifies the definition of 'race meet', adds other gaming definitions, and clarifies how the commission and the Colorado Bureau of Investigation run fingerprint-based background checks on license applicants. Full text was unavailable, so the specific statutory sections and effective date could not be confirmed."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization and tax bills on the list (GA HB 686, HI HB 2570 and HB 1308, LA HB 639, IA SF 613 and SSB 1157), CO SB 163 does not touch tax rates or market structure; it is administrative and enforcement housekeeping for an existing market. Its enforcement against unlicensed operators parallels AR HB 1861, which makes operating an illegal online casino or sportsbook a felony, though Colorado uses administrative inspection and impoundment rather than new criminal penalties. Its shift of authority from commission to agency resembles Kentucky's HB 566 corporation clean-up and Kansas SB 356's grant of rulemaking authority to the racing and gaming commission.","watch":"Operators should watch for division rulemaking that sets up the sports-betting self-exclusion program, including how exclusion lists are shared with operators, compliance obligations, and liability for serving excluded patrons, along with any faster or stricter game-approval process now that the division, not the commission, grants approval.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Signed Act","text_chars":0,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:42.906Z"},"KY-HB757":{"summary":"HB 757 is Kentucky's 2026 omnibus revenue bill, enacted with an emergency clause as Acts Ch. 161. It updates Kentucky's Internal Revenue Code conformity date to December 31, 2025, decouples from several new federal individual deductions, simplifies the marketplace-provider sales tax nexus test, sunsets the state's three tax increment financing participation programs, and makes technical changes to tax interest and TVA payment reporting. In the portion of the text provided, it does not amend Kentucky's sports wagering licensing or tax provisions.","mechanics":["Section 1 (KRS 139.450): the marketplace-provider collection obligation is triggered only by more than $100,000 in sales. The 200-transaction alternative threshold is deleted, and services provided to Kentucky purchasers are expressly covered.","Section 6 (KRS 141.010(21)): for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2026, the Internal Revenue Code is adopted as in effect on December 31, 2025.","Section 7 (KRS 141.019(2)): Kentucky still disallows federal itemized loss deductions under 26 U.S.C. 165 except wagering losses allowed under Section 165(d). Because of the updated conformity date, that carve-out now follows whatever 165(d) says as of December 31, 2025.","Section 7 also decouples from the new federal deductions for qualified tips (sec. 224), overtime (sec. 225) and passenger vehicle loan interest (sec. 163(h)(4)), and from sec. 174A R&E expensing. It limits mortgage interest to acquisition debt on one principal residence for 2026 and later.","Section 5 (KRS 154.30-030): all three state TIF participation programs sunset on the section's effective date. No new applications or amendments to existing agreements are allowed, and previously approved projects are grandfathered.","Section 2 (KRS 131.183): obsolete 2008 transition language is deleted, keeping prime plus 2% on underpayments and prime minus 2% on overpayments. Section 4 redirects TVA regional development agency reports to the Department for Local Government, which must report to the LRC by December 1."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Iowa's SF 605, SSB 1152 and HSB 91 address state withholding on sports wagering winnings, and Colorado HB 25-1311 and Louisiana HB 639 change operator-level deductions and tax rates. Kentucky HB 757 does none of these, at least in the text provided. Its only gaming touchpoint is the existing individual income tax carve-out for wagering losses under IRC 165(d), now tied to a 2025 federal code. That makes it closer in character to the general tax omnibus bills such as Florida HB 7031 or Arizona SB 1274 than to Georgia HB 686 or Hawaii HB 2570, which are full sports-betting authorization bills.","watch":"The supplied text is cut off partway through Section 8, so check the remaining sections for any changes to Kentucky's wagering excise tax or withholding rules, and confirm whether rolling conformity to the December 31, 2025 IRC imports the federal change limiting 165(d) wagering-loss deductions for Kentucky bettors.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Acts Chapter 161","text_chars":303767,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:02.533Z"},"KY-HB904":{"summary":"HB 904 (Acts Ch. 184) creates a licensing regime for paid fantasy contests under the Kentucky Horse Racing and Gaming Corporation, adds fixed-odds wagering on live horse racing to the KRS 138.552 excise tax, and requires licensed online gaming, sports wagering and fantasy operators to block and suspend accounts of people on a child support arrearage registry. It also adds definitions of \"event contract\" and \"prediction market\" to KRS 230.210, and lets the Lottery Corporation and the Corporation issue regulations without the Governor's signature. The supplied text is truncated partway through Section 14, so any operative provisions using the new prediction-market definitions are not visible here.","mechanics":["Fixed-odds horse racing tax (Section 13, amending KRS 138.552): fixed-odds wagers on live horse racing, and parlays containing one, are taxed at the same rates as sports wagering, 9.75% at the track and 14.25% online. These wagers are carved out of adjusted gross sports wagering revenue. The proceeds go to a new purse stabilization fund (Section 12), which must be held at least 1.5 years and can be spent only to supplement live-meet purses, capped at 10% of the fund.","Fantasy contest licensing (Sections 4-11): providers must be licensed and use geolocation, and players must be 18 or older. Fees (Section 10) are: $7,500 initial and $5,000 renewal for providers already licensed as sports wagering service providers; $15,000 and $10,000 for others; $5,000 and $1,000 for providers under $50,000 in adjusted gross fantasy receipts. Existing operators may keep operating if they apply within 90 days of the regulations, which are due within 180 days. Annual CPA audit and compliance testing are required, deferred 24 months for new licensees (Section 7).","Child support registry (Section 3): the Attorney General compiles a registry of people with arrearages of $500 or more per Title IV-D case, and the Corporation passes it to operators monthly. Operators must screen applicants before opening an account and suspend matching existing accounts within 5 business days, with reinstatement within 5 business days of clearance. Knowing violations can lead to license suspension or revocation, and there is a good-faith safe harbor from private suits. Charitable gaming is exempt.","Integrity (Sections 1, 5, 8, 9): new definitions of prohibited and restricted participants (players, coaches, officials, their family members, and owners of more than 5% of a team), plus a fantasy-tampering offense. Providers must notify the Corporation of match fixing, governing-body rule breaches and illegal activity. The Corporation must investigate allegations and refer credible ones to law enforcement and sports governing bodies.","Definitions (Section 1): adds \"event contract\" (keyed to CEA 7 U.S.C. 1a(19)(i)), \"prediction market\" (any platform for event contracts or speculative positions on future events, in or out of state, including a CFTC designated contract market) and \"fixed-odds wagering.\" It removes the horse racing exclusion from the chapter 230 definition of \"sporting event\" but keeps it in the tax-specific definition.","Responsible gaming and rulemaking (Sections 2, 4, 11): fantasy self-exclusion is folded into the existing program, winnings of self-excluded players are forfeited to problem-gaming charities, and a help statement is required on all marketing. Regulations no longer need the Governor's signature (new KRS Chapter 13A section), and the Corporation may not restrict fantasy contests merely by labeling them sports wagering."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","integrity","prediction","advertising","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike LA HB 639, which raises online sports wagering taxes, and CO HB 25-1311, which narrows operators' deductions, KY HB 904 leaves Kentucky's 9.75%/14.25% rates unchanged and instead applies them to a new product, fixed-odds horse racing, with that revenue reserved for purses. Its prediction-market treatment is definitional (at least in the visible text), whereas IA SF 2085 imposes a permit and a tax on event-contract markets. Its child support arrearage screening and suspension mandate for online operators has no counterpart among the listed bills, such as CO SB 131's deposit limits or the HI sports wagering bills (HB 1308, HB 2570).","watch":"Operators should build monthly child-support registry screening with 5-business-day suspension and reinstatement (knowing violations put the license at risk). They should also check how the new prediction-market and event-contract definitions are used in the untruncated remainder of the Act, since the CFTC-DCM language suggests the Corporation may assert jurisdiction over competing event-contract platforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Acts Chapter 184","text_chars":271437,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:08.543Z"},"LA-SR178":{"summary":"Louisiana Senate Resolution 178 urges and requests the Louisiana Department of Health to study how to modernize the state's problem gambling prevention and education efforts, with a focus on youth, college-age populations and young adults. It is a non-binding simple resolution: it creates no regulatory obligations, but it asks LDH to deliver a written report with findings and recommendations by February 1, 2027.","mechanics":["LDH is urged to study age-appropriate educational resources and curriculum covering gambling-related harms, financial literacy, decision-making, online gaming environments and responsible behavior.","LDH is urged to examine voluntary digital tools and mobile resources for prevention, self-assessment, awareness, early intervention and connection to behavioral health and recovery services.","LDH is to consult with the Department of Education, the Board of Regents, colleges and universities, behavioral health providers, community organizations, researchers and other stakeholders.","A written report is due to the Senate and the David R. Poynter Legislative Research Library under R.S. 24:771 and 772 no later than February 1, 2027.","There are no taxes, fees, operator mandates or enforcement provisions; the whereas clauses cite expanded legal sports wagering and youth exposure through advertising, social media and mobile apps."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the Hawaii sports wagering bills (HB 2570 and HB 1308), SR 178 does not legalize anything or impose any tax. Those bills would create licensing regimes and a Problem Gambling fund, while this resolution only commissions a study. It is also far softer than Colorado SB 131, which imposes direct operator limits such as a cap of 6 deposits per gaming day. SR 178 addresses problem gambling purely through public-health education and voluntary tools, and it places no obligations on licensees.","watch":"Watch the February 1, 2027 LDH report for recommendations that could later become legislation, such as youth-focused advertising restrictions, mandated operator funding for prevention programs or required integration with self-assessment and referral tools.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"SR178 Enrolled","text_chars":3394,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:24.978Z"},"LA-SB135":{"summary":"SB 135 amends R.S. 27:625(G)(2) and (8) to change how the state treasurer distributes Louisiana's tax on sports wagering net gaming proceeds collected under Subsection B between two dedicated funds. As engrossed, both provisions credit 25% of Subsection B collections, capped at $20 million, to the Louisiana Early Childhood Education Fund (R.S. 17:407.30) and to the Supporting Programs, Opportunities, Resources, and Teams (SPORT) Fund (R.S. 17:1792). It does not change the tax rate or any operator obligation.","mechanics":["Amends R.S. 27:625(G)(2): 25% of Subsection B collections, 'not to exceed twenty million dollars,' goes to the Louisiana Early Childhood Education Fund. The digest describes this as tied to net mobile sports wagering proceeds.","Amends R.S. 27:625(G)(8): adds a $20 million cap on the 25% share of Subsection B collections credited to the SPORT Fund, which was previously uncapped per the digest.","Distributions apply each fiscal year only after the Subsection D requirements are met.","Senate Finance amendments dropped an earlier version that guaranteed the Early Childhood fund the greater of 25% or $20 million, removing that floor.","Effective July 1, 2027, moved from July 1, 2026 by committee amendment. Passed the Senate and on 2026-05-18 was referred to the House Committee on Administration of Criminal Justice."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This bill is unusual because it only reallocates already-collected sports wagering tax revenue between two education and youth funds, with no change to rates, licensing or conduct rules. The digest also conflicts with the bill text: it says the $20 million cap on the Early Childhood fund is removed, yet the visible engrossed text keeps the cap. The lost strikethrough formatting makes the final effect on that fund uncertain.","watch":"The bill does not touch operator tax rates as engrossed, but in the House it is a vehicle on R.S. 27:625, so watch for amendments that raise the Subsection B rate or change the deductions behind net gaming proceeds to fund these capped dedications.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"SB135 Engrossed","text_chars":4442,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:34.878Z"},"IA-SF660":{"summary":"SF 660 does not change how sports wagering is taxed, licensed or conducted in Iowa; it spends money from the sports wagering receipts fund (Iowa Code 8.57I). It adds two ongoing claims on the fund and four one-time FY2025-26 appropriations. It also creates a new Iowa major events and tourism grant program (new chapter 15G) and repeals the sports tourism marketing fund (15F.403), moving that fund's balance into the new program.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends 8.57I(3) to make a standing annual appropriation of $8 million from the sports wagering receipts fund to the public safety equipment fund (80.48), starting FY2025-26, exempt from the reporting rules in subsections 4 and 5.","Sec. 2 adds 8.57I(3A): from FY2026-27, the fund must top up the length of service award program grant fund (100B.52) to a $1.5 million balance each July 1. This takes effect only if HF 1002 is enacted (Sec. 8) and is repealed July 1, 2030.","Secs. 3-6 make one-time FY2025-26 appropriations from the fund: $14 million for education support personnel pay, split among school districts by budget enrollment and effective on enactment (Sec. 7); $5 million for the special education division; $4 million to the film production incentive fund, contingent on SF 657; and $4 million to the new major events and tourism fund.","Division II creates chapter 15G. The economic development authority board may give grants to Iowa nonprofit economic development and tourism entities for bid, hosting, sanctioning and operating costs of athletic contests, conventions and music or art festivals. Recipients must put up a 50% match, administration is capped at 5% of the fund, and funding decisions are exempt from chapter 17A.","Division III narrows chapter 15F to an infrastructure-only program and repeals the sports tourism marketing fund (15F.403), transferring its remaining balance to the 15G fund (Sec. 23). Entities cannot receive assistance under both programs."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What stands out is that the title says the bill relates to sports wagering, yet it contains nothing on operators, tax rates, licensing or wagering conduct. Its only link to wagering is using the 8.57I receipts fund as a funding source. The notable step is turning a fund previously spent 'as directed by the general assembly' into a source of permanent and formula-driven claims: the $8 million yearly public safety appropriation and the uncapped length of service award top-up.","watch":"The new standing claims on the sports wagering receipts fund (the $8 million yearly public safety appropriation and the open-ended length of service award top-up) make the state more dependent on sports wagering revenue, which could add pressure in future sessions to raise the wagering tax or fees if receipts fall short.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled with Governor's Action","text_chars":24603,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:47.043Z"},"LA-HB639":{"summary":"Act 298 raises Louisiana's tax on net gaming proceeds from online sports wagering (website or mobile app) from 15% to 21.5% under R.S. 27:625(B), leaving retail wagering through a sports wagering mechanism untouched. It redirects 25% of online sports wagering tax collections to a new Supporting Programs, Opportunities, Resources, and Teams (SPORTS) Fund for Division I university athletic departments and 3% to the Louisiana Postsecondary Inclusive Education Fund, with the remainder going to the state general fund. Despite the digest title, the enrolled text contains no video poker provisions.","mechanics":["R.S. 27:625(B): online sports wagering tax rises from 15% to 21.5% of net gaming proceeds; electronic wagering through an in-person sports wagering mechanism is expressly excluded.","R.S. 27:625(G)(8): 25% of Subsection B collections go to the new SPORTS Fund (R.S. 17:1792), after Subsection D obligations are met.","R.S. 27:625(G)(9)-(10): 3% goes to the Louisiana Postsecondary Inclusive Education Fund (R.S. 17:3138.11), and any remainder goes to the state general fund.","R.S. 17:1792: Board of Regents distributes SPORTS Fund money equally among public universities in FBS and FCS conferences. Permitted uses are scholarships, insurance, medical coverage, facility enhancements, litigation settlement fees and Alston awards, and the money may not supplant other awards.","The text states no separate effective date or change to deductions, licensing or enforcement. The fiscal note estimates about +$34.7 million in revenue."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Colorado HB 25-1311 raises effective tax by restricting operators' deductions against the existing 10% rate, whereas Louisiana HB 639 simply raises the headline online rate by 6.5 points and leaves the deduction base alone. Iowa SF 613 and SSB 1157 combine tax rate and license fee changes with gambling enforcement and dedicate revenue to tourism and major events; Louisiana instead earmarks a fixed share to college athletics, including Alston awards and litigation settlement costs. Hawaii HB 1308 and HB 2570 and Georgia HB 686 would legalize and tax sports betting from scratch, while Iowa SF 605 deals only with winnings withholding.","watch":"Operators should watch the split between online and retail rates and the definition of \"sports wagering mechanism\" in 27:625(B), and should expect the college-athletics earmark to create a constituency for further online rate increases.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"HB639 Act 298","text_chars":4429,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:59.013Z"},"CO-SB131":{"summary":"SB 26-131 layers consumer-protection limits onto Colorado's existing sports betting regime. It bans solicitation push notifications, caps deposits at six per gaming day, prohibits credit card funding, and restricts advertising aimed at under-21 audiences. It also requires annual transactional data submissions, tightens integrity reporting to sports governing bodies, and lets leagues petition to restrict any type of wager on their events.","mechanics":["Online operators may not send push notifications, including texts shown when the app is not open, that solicit bets or deposits (44-30-1501(8.7), 44-30-1506(8)(b)). Deposits are capped at six per individual per operator-defined 24-hour gaming day (1506(8)(a)(II)).","Accepting credit card deposits, directly or indirectly (including via accounts funded by a credit card), is unlawful under 44-30-1511(1)(d). Violations carry a monetary penalty of up to $25,000, in addition to or instead of suspension or revocation (44-30-524(1)(b)(III)).","New 44-30-1506.5 bars operators and their marketing affiliates (paid promoters, excluding broadcasters) from targeting under-21s or creating content clearly meant for them. It also bars advertising on any media whose audience is reasonably expected to be majority under-21, with an exception for public venues where attendee demographics cannot be determined.","Integrity reports under 1506(5)(a)(II)-(III) must now be made 'as soon as practicable' and sent simultaneously to the relevant sports governing body. The division and operators must use 'reasonable efforts' to cooperate with league investigations, replacing the earlier 'good and sufficient reason' standard. Leagues may petition the commission to restrict, limit, or exclude any type of wager on their events, no longer only single-player-outcome bets (1505(7)).","Starting February 1, 2028, and every February 1 after, online operators must submit all transactional data and metrics for the prior year, redacted of PII and exempt from CORA. The division publishes a compiled report by January 1, 2029 and every three years after (44-30-1507.5).","The act takes effect August 12, 2026 (assuming May 13 adjournment), unless a referendum petition is filed, and applies to conduct and agreements on or after that date. It also appropriates $124,623 and 0.5 FTE to the Department of Law, and requires the sports betting fund's water plan transfer to be no less than the prior year's (1509(2)(e))."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills listed (GA HB 686, HI HB 2570 and HB 1308), which create licensing and tax frameworks for new markets, Colorado's SB 131 imposes operational restrictions on a mature legal market. It also differs from tax-focused measures such as LA HB 639 and enforcement-against-illegal-operators bills such as AR HB 1861, because it targets licensed operators' marketing, payment, and engagement practices. Its combination of a hard deposit-frequency cap, a solicitation push-notification ban, and a credit card prohibition with a specific $25,000 penalty is unusual among the listed bills, as is mandatory annual data disclosure feeding a public triennial report.","watch":"Operators should watch how the commission interprets several open provisions: the scope of 'relating to the solicitation of bets or deposits' for push notifications, 'indirectly' funded credit card deposits (such as e-wallets or PayPal backed by cards), affiliate liability under the majority-under-21 audience test, and the breadth of 'all transactional data and metrics,' since these will drive compliance costs from August 12, 2026 onward.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Signed Act","text_chars":11492,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:02:00.054Z"},"IA-SSB1157":{"summary":"SSB 1157 (renumbered SF 613) raises Iowa's sports wagering tax on net receipts from 6.75% to 9%. It replaces the graduated casino adjusted gross receipts tax with flat rates and doubles the casino annual license fee. It also shifts DCI gaming enforcement funding from licensee regulatory fees to an off-the-top credit of AGR tax, and redirects sports wagering receipts to public safety equipment ($8M a year) and a new $15M Iowa Major Events and Tourism Fund.","mechanics":["Sports wagering tax under Iowa Code 99F.11(5)(a) rises from 6.75% to 9% of sports wagering net receipts (Sec. 15); Division I takes effect January 1, 2026 (Sec. 16).","The casino AGR tax drops the 5% (first $1M) and 10% (next $2M) tiers and the variable racetrack rates; it becomes a flat 22% for excursion boats and gambling structures and 24.125% for racetrack enclosures, whether or not they hold a table games license (Secs. 11-12).","The annual license fee for excursion gambling boats and gambling structures doubles from $5 to $10 per person of capacity (Sec. 6).","The DCI enforcement-cost component of the 99D.14 and 99F.10 regulatory fees is struck (Secs. 3-10). From FY2026-27, AGR tax is instead credited to the gaming enforcement revolving fund up to the amount appropriated to DPS, before the remainder goes to the rebuild Iowa infrastructure fund (Sec. 14). Internet fantasy sports background-check fees under 99E.4(4) are unchanged.","Beginning FY2026-27, $8M a year is appropriated from the sports wagering receipts fund to the public safety equipment fund (Sec. 1). A one-time $15M for FY2025-26 goes to the new Iowa Major Events and Tourism Fund under new chapter 15G (Sec. 21).","The new 15G program gives grants to Iowa nonprofits for bid, hosting and sanctioning costs of major events, with a 50% match requirement and a 5% administrative cap (Secs. 17-20). The sports tourism marketing fund (15F.403) is repealed and its balance transferred to the new fund (Secs. 31-32)."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like LA HB 639, which raises Louisiana's online sports wagering tax and dedicates gaming revenue, SSB 1157 combines a sports betting rate increase with earmarks of the proceeds. Its 9% rate on net receipts remains low by national standards. CO HB 25-1311 takes a different route to more revenue: it keeps Colorado's 10% rate but narrows the deductions operators can take in calculating net proceeds, whereas Iowa raises the headline rate and leaves the net receipts base untouched. Unlike GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570, which would legalize and build sports wagering regimes from scratch, Iowa's bill adjusts an existing market and pairs the sports tax change with a flattening of land-based casino AGR taxes that eliminates the low-revenue tiers.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the 9% rate survives in SF 613 or is raised further, and how a January 1, 2026 effective date applies to a tax imposed on receipts 'each fiscal year', which could mean mid-year proration or partial-year compliance issues. Casino-affiliated licensees should also watch the loss of the 5%/10% AGR tiers and the doubled per-capacity license fee.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":40137,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:02:17.919Z"},"CO-HB1168":{"summary":"HB1168 is a supplemental appropriation bill adjusting the current fiscal year budget of the Colorado Department of Revenue. Only the official abstract is available, so the specific line items, amounts, and any changes affecting the Division of Gaming, which sits within the Department of Revenue and regulates limited gaming and sports betting, cannot be confirmed from the text provided.","mechanics":["Makes a supplemental (mid-year) appropriation to the Colorado Department of Revenue, per the official abstract.","Specific dollar amounts, funding sources, and divisions affected are not available because the full text was not provided.","The Department of Revenue houses the Division of Gaming, so gaming-related cash funds or spending authority may be adjusted, but this cannot be verified from the abstract.","Signed by the Governor on 2026-03-12; supplemental appropriations typically take effect on signature for the current fiscal year."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. Supplemental appropriation bills like this are routine budget housekeeping. Based on the abstract alone, it does not appear to change sports-betting policy, tax rates, or licensing rules.","watch":"Pull the chaptered text and check any Division of Gaming or Sports Betting Fund line items for changes in spending authority, staffing, or fund transfers that could signal shifts in regulatory or enforcement capacity.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":0,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:15.128Z"},"CA-AB1840":{"summary":"AB 1840 adds Government Code Section 87451 to bar public officials, designated employees, and lobbyists from knowingly trading prediction market contracts on elections, administrative actions, legislative actions, or governmental decisions in their official duties while holding, or reasonably expecting to obtain through those duties, material nonpublic information about the transaction. It also amends Section 86205 so that a violation of 87451 is a prohibited lobbyist act. Violations carry Political Reform Act penalties, including misdemeanor liability.","mechanics":["Covered persons (Sec. 87451(b)(1)): elected or appointed state or local public officials, Section 87200 filers, designated employees under Section 82019, and lobbyists under Section 82039.","Covered transactions (Sec. 87451(b)(2)): purchase, sale, or exchange of a prediction market contract on administrative actions or elections (both as defined in Sec. 82002), on governmental decisions made in the covered person's official duties, or on actions before the Legislature (Sec. 87102.5(b)(1)).","Prediction market contract (Sec. 87451(b)(4)): any contract, derivative, financial instrument, or investment issued, listed, or offered by a business entity that relates to the outcome, occurrence, or nonoccurrence of a future event; the definition is not limited to CFTC-registered platforms.","Knowledge standard: the person must knowingly trade while possessing material nonpublic information, or while it is reasonably foreseeable they may obtain it in their official duties. MNPI uses a 'reasonable investor' significance test.","Enforcement: misdemeanor under the Political Reform Act. The bill amends an initiative statute, so it needs a 2/3 vote of each house. It is fiscal and contains a state-mandated local program with no reimbursement (Sec. 3).","Status: introduced February 11, 2026; the second committee hearing was canceled at the author's request on April 9, 2026."],"issue_tags":["prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"AB 1840 is closest to NY S09825, which also restricts public officers and legislative staff from trading on information gained in official duties. The New York bill also reaches mobile sports wagering platforms, while AB 1840 covers only prediction market contracts on political and governmental events and also reaches lobbyists and local officials. Unlike the platform-facing regimes in IL HB5059 and NY S09414 (ORACLE Acts), MN HF 4437/SF 4511, NC H1171, and VT H0913, it imposes no obligations or bans on operators and does not treat prediction markets as gambling (contrast IL HB5142). It also contains no tax (contrast NJ A5336/S4447) and no age limits (contrast CT HB05038).","watch":"Operators listing election or policy event contracts should watch whether amendments add platform-side duties such as KYC screening of covered persons, reporting, or account restrictions, and should note that the broad 'prediction market contract' definition could later be borrowed for operator-facing regulation; the canceled hearing suggests the bill may be stalled this session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6436,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:18.601Z"},"AK-HB145":{"summary":"HB 145 creates a new AS 05.18 authorizing mobile-only sports wagering in Alaska, administered by the Commissioner of Revenue, with up to 10 licenses at $100,000 per year and a 20 percent tax on adjusted gross revenue. Applicants must already hold a mobile sports wagering license (directly or through an affiliate or partner) in at least three other states. The bill also excludes AS 05.18 activity from the criminal definition of gambling and authorizes interstate wagering agreements.","mechanics":["Licensing (AS 05.18.010): cap of 10 licenses. The license fee is $100,000 for the initial license and for each annual renewal, plus an optional processing fee. Licenses are non-transferable and require licensure in at least three other states. If applicants exceed slots, the commissioner awards licenses on past experience, projected AGR and projected tax revenue. Applicants must disclose holders of 15 percent or more and submit fingerprint-based or recent out-of-state criminal history checks.","Tax (AS 05.18.090): 20 percent of adjusted gross revenue, defined as wagers less winnings, voided bets and federal excise taxes. Promotional credits are not deductible. Returns and electronic payment are due by the 15th of each month. Negative AGR may be carried forward for up to three years with no carryback or refund unless the license is surrendered. Licensees are exempt from municipal wagering taxes.","Permitted wagers (AS 05.18.060 and .070): single-game bets, parlays, teasers, props, in-play bets, futures, pools and exchange wagering. Wagering events include collegiate and esports events but exclude events where most participants are under 18, other than the Olympics. Bettors must be 21 or older and located in Alaska or in a jurisdiction with an interstate agreement. Layoff wagers between licensees are allowed.","Operator duties (AS 05.18.030 and .050): integrity monitoring software and reporting of suspicious activity, including under 31 C.F.R. 1021.320 SAR rules. Operators must verify identity, advertise to no one under 21, obtain approval of house rules, and display 1-800-GAMBLER. Accounts may be funded by credit card, bonuses and promotions, ACH or e-wallets.","Enforcement (AS 05.18.110): penalties of up to $10,000 per violation, plus suspension or revocation and power to require termination of employees. Section 1 also requires ABC Board liquor license suspension for repeat AS 05.18 violations. Fantasy contests are excluded from the definition.","Effective dates: January 1, 2026, with the regulations section effective immediately. Operations cannot begin until at least three licenses are issued, and the public must receive 14 days' notice."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity","advertising","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HB 686 and the Hawaii bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569), HB 145 would legalize sports wagering from scratch, but it is mobile-only and housed in the Department of Revenue rather than a gaming commission. The Hawaii bills use a general excise tax on operator licenses, whereas Alaska uses a flat 20 percent AGR tax. HB 145's $100,000 annual fee is far below the $15 million license fee proposed in IL HB2878 and HB5034. Its explicit municipal tax exemption parallels IL HB4171's preemption of local sports wagering taxes. Unlike CT HB05229, it contains only a minimal under-21 advertising restriction and no AI or withdrawal rules.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the 20 percent rate, the lack of a promotional-credit deduction, the three-state licensure prerequisite and the 10-license cap with discretionary selection survive committee review in Labor and Commerce and in Finance. They should also watch whether the commissioner's regulations, which may apply retroactively under Section 6(b), add burdens beyond the statute.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":26203,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:21.835Z"},"CO-SB169":{"summary":"SB169 is Colorado's annual Revisor's Bill, which makes nonsubstantive technical corrections across the Colorado Revised Statutes and amends or repeals obsolete, imperfect, or inoperative language while preserving existing legislative intent. Only the official abstract is available, so it is not possible to confirm whether any gaming or sports-betting provisions (e.g., C.R.S. Title 44, Article 30, Part 15) are touched.","mechanics":["Scope: nonsubstantive revision of the Colorado Revised Statutes as amended, per the official abstract.","Function: amends or repeals obsolete, imperfect, and inoperative law; by its terms it is not meant to change the intent, effect, or meaning of existing law.","Status: signed by the Governor on 2026-06-02.","The full chaptered text was unavailable, so specific sections amended, effective dates, and any gaming-related cleanups cannot be identified from the material provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. Revisor's bills like this one are routine housekeeping measures and are unusual only in that they can sweep in technical edits across any title, including gaming statutes, without a substantive policy debate.","watch":"Once the chaptered text is available, check whether any cross-references, definitions, or repealed provisions in Colorado's sports-betting or limited-gaming statutes were altered, since even technical edits can affect compliance citations or licensing language.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":0,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:22.768Z"},"AK-SB194":{"summary":"SB 194 legalizes statewide mobile-only sports wagering in Alaska under a new AS 05.18, administered by the Department of Revenue. It allows up to 10 one-year licenses at $100,000 each and taxes adjusted gross revenue at 20 percent. It also carves licensed wagering out of the criminal gambling definition in AS 11.66.280(3) and adds fingerprint-based background checks for licensees.","mechanics":["Licensing (AS 05.18.010): applicants must hold, or have a subsidiary, partner or joint operating agreement with someone holding, a mobile sports wagering license in at least three other states, and must show their platform has been certified by an independent lab in another US jurisdiction. The fee is $100,000 per year plus a discretionary processing fee. Licenses are non-transferable and capped at 10; if oversubscribed, the commissioner picks based on experience, projected AGR and projected tax revenue.","Tax (AS 05.18.090): 20 percent of adjusted gross revenue, defined as handle less winnings, voided bets and federal excise tax, with no deduction for promotional credits. Returns and payment are due electronically by the 15th of each month. Negative AGR may be carried forward for up to three years, with no carryback or refund unless the licensee surrenders its license. Licensees are exempt from municipal wagering taxes.","Permitted wagers (AS 05.18.060 and .190): explicitly includes proposition, in-play, parlays, teasers, pools, exchange wagering and futures. Wagering events cover collegiate, amateur, professional, esports and motor racing, but exclude events where most participants are under 18 (Olympics excepted). Fantasy contests are excluded from the definition.","Player and conduct rules: bettors must be 21 or older and physically in Alaska or in a jurisdiction with an interstate agreement. Licensees may not advertise to under-21s, must display 1-800-GAMBLER, must get house rules approved, must report suspicious activity including SAR-type disclosures under 31 C.F.R. 1021.320, and may accept funding by card, bank transfer, online payment systems and bonuses.","Enforcement: civil penalties of up to $10,000 per violation, plus suspension, revocation, restriction, cease-and-desist and closure orders. Application information stays confidential for 25 years.","Timing: the Act takes effect January 1, 2026, with the regulations section effective immediately. Operations cannot start until at least three licenses are issued and the Department gives 14 days' public notice."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","prop","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HB 686 and the Hawaii bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569), SB 194 is a ground-up legalization bill. Unlike the Hawaii bills, which place regulation in the Department of Law Enforcement or DBEDT and use a general excise tax on operator licenses, Alaska gives the Department of Revenue sole regulatory authority and imposes a flat 20 percent AGR tax. Its $100,000 annual fee is modest compared with the $15 million master license fee proposed in IL HB2878 and HB5034. Its express exemption from municipal wagering taxes mirrors the home-rule preemption in IL HB4171.","watch":"Watch the three-other-states licensure prerequisite and the 10-license cap with selection based partly on projected tax revenue, both of which favour incumbent national operators, as well as the AGR definition that does not allow a deduction for promotional play at the 20 percent rate.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":26196,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:26.801Z"},"DC-B26-0034":{"summary":"This DC emergency act approves six modifications to Contract No. DCAM-22-CS-RFP-0026 with Chiaramonte Construction Company for renovations to the Public Safety Communications Center building and authorizes payment of up to $13,693,879.06. It contains nothing about sports betting, gaming, wagering or any related regulatory subject.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 approves Modification Nos. 1 through 6 under section 451 of the Home Rule Act (D.C. Official Code § 1-204.51), notwithstanding section 202 of the Procurement Practices Reform Act of 2010.","Sec. 2 authorizes payment in a not-to-exceed amount of $13,693,879.06 for goods and services received and to be received.","Sec. 2 defines the scope as construction services for the Public Safety Communications Center building renovations.","Sec. 3 adopts the CFO's fiscal impact statement.","Sec. 4 makes it an emergency act effective on Mayoral approval and lasting no more than 90 days. It was published as A26-0009 and expires May 21, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. Those bills address sports-wagering authorization and tax (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI SB1569), license fees and definitions (IL HB2878, IL HB5142), advertising and consumer protections (CT HB05229) or broader gaming reform (FL H0189). This DC act is a routine procurement approval for a public-safety building contract and does not mention wagering at all. It was likely matched on keywords or by error.","watch":"Nothing for a sports-betting operator to watch here: this is an expired, gaming-unrelated construction contract approval and can be removed from the tracking list.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":2407,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:28.658Z"},"CT-HB5038":{"summary":"Governor's Bill 5038 imposes consumer-protection conduct rules on prediction market platforms serving Connecticut residents: a 21+ age floor with age and geolocation verification, mandatory voluntary self-exclusion and spend-limit tools, and advertising restrictions aimed at under-21 audiences and college campuses, enforced by Attorney General civil penalties. It does not license, tax or ban prediction markets. It expressly carves out sports wagering, online casino gaming and internet games regulated under chapter 229b, and includes a federal-preemption savings clause. A separate section directs DCP, consulting the AG and the Mohegan and Mashantucket Pequot tribes, to study prediction markets' effects, including on master wagering licensee revenue, and report by February 1, 2027.","mechanics":["Definitions (Sec. 1(a)): a 'prediction market' is any system letting state residents open speculative positions on future-event outcomes in a bid-ask format, regardless of mechanism. It excludes chapter 229b sports wagering, online casino gaming and internet games, and bid-asks tracking security, commodity or interest-rate prices or broad economic measures.","Age and access (Sec. 1(b)-(d)): no registration or positions for anyone under 21. Platforms must verify age 21+ and physical presence in the state before an account opens, and must offer self-exclusion from account creation or trading plus spend limits. On discovering an underage user, the platform must suspend the account, close all positions, pay out funds and bar the user until age 21.","Advertising (Sec. 2): every ad must state the 21+ requirement, and direct or targeted messages need a one-click unsubscribe link. Barred: ads aimed exclusively or primarily at under-21s or college campuses, targeting of self-excluded consumers, imagery or celebrity endorsements appealing to under-21s, misleading content, and placement in media or audiences that are majority or presumed majority under 21.","Enforcement (Sec. 3): AG civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation, rising to $50,000 per violation for a persistent course of conduct. DCP is to adopt implementing regulations (Sec. 4).","Effective dates: Secs. 1-5 take effect July 1, 2027. The Sec. 6 study takes effect July 1, 2026, with its report due February 1, 2027. Sec. 5 provides that any provision a court finds preempted by conflicting federal law yields to federal law."],"issue_tags":["prediction","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"CT HB05038 closely tracks the age-21, registration and verification framework in IL HB5059 and NY S09414 (both ORACLE Acts), but strips it down to a minors-and-advertising regime with AG penalties and no licensing. It contrasts with MN HF 4437/SF 4511 and NC H1171, which would prohibit prediction market wagering outright, and with IL HB5142, which would fold sports-event prediction markets into the Sports Wagering Act; Connecticut instead expressly excludes chapter 229b sports wagering from its definition. Unlike NJ A5336/S4447, it imposes no tax, and its advertising restrictions resemble NY S 10092's minors-focused approach. Distinctively, the Sec. 6 study must measure prediction markets' impact on tribal master wagering licensee revenue and state payments.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the Sec. 6 study (consulting the Mohegan and Mashantucket Pequot tribes, report due February 1, 2027) becomes the basis for later legislation treating sports-event contracts as unlicensed sports wagering, and whether DCP regulations or preemption litigation over CFTC-regulated event contracts reshape the bill's light-touch, non-licensing approach before its July 1, 2027 effective date.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8885,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:32.496Z"},"CT-HB5229":{"summary":"Public Act 26-53 tightens Connecticut's consumer-protection and advertising rules for online gaming and sports wagering licensees. It newly bars gaming advertising in college athletic facilities and on college-run digital channels, and requires geolocation controls for fantasy-only and online lottery/keno platforms. It also orders a Department of Consumer Protection study of prediction markets (report due February 1, 2027) and revises lottery game testing and certification procedures.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (eff. July 1, 2026) amends CGS 12-863(c). Master wagering licensees, online gaming operators, service providers and sports wagering retailers must set up a toll-free number for help resolving platform problems; a prerecorded message routing users to online help is allowed. The number must be displayed conspicuously on each website and app, alongside the existing problem-gambling line, self-exclusion link, time-on-site display, break-in-play tool and balance display.","Sec. 2 (eff. July 1, 2026) amends 12-863(e). A new subdivision (9) bars gaming advertising in athletic facilities on any public or private college campus in Connecticut, and on any website, social media, online service or app run by or for such an institution. The only exception is an announcement, signage or display directed to a general audience.","Sec. 2 also bars ads pertaining exclusively to keno, online lottery or fantasy contests from TV programs aimed primarily at under-18s, judged by a nationally recognized content rating system, and from ad breaks during those programs. The existing ban on targeting self-excluded individuals now expressly covers Internet games and retail sports wagering.","Sec. 3 (eff. from passage) directs the DCP Commissioner to study prediction market platforms, consulting the Attorney General, the Mohegan Tribe, the Mashantucket Pequot Tribe and the Connecticut Lottery Corporation. Topics include under-21 use and advertising, problem gambling, the most common positions taken, and effects on master wagering licensee revenue and state payments. The report is due February 1, 2027.","Sec. 4 (eff. from passage) covers platforms offering exclusively fantasy contests or online lottery draw/keno sales. Their geolocation systems must defeat or block proxies and VPNs, check location at each entry fee or purchase, block transactions when location cannot be determined and show a pop-up, and flag geographically inconsistent account access.","Sec. 5 amends CGS 12-806a(c). Lottery draw games sold in at least 20 states and tested by a lab licensed in at least 20 states are exempt from Connecticut lab certification. DCP may publish technical standards, with updates effective 30 days after posting. Revocation authority and the 'without notice' language are removed; suspension is now allowed where continued operation 'could reasonably cause substantial detriment to the public interest.'"],"issue_tags":["advertising","prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act), which would directly bar under-21s from prediction markets and impose platform duties, Connecticut stops at a study. That study expressly measures prediction markets' effect on master wagering licensee revenue and state payments, which signals possible protective legislation in 2027. Bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569 would create new sports wagering markets. HB05229 instead fine-tunes an existing regime's responsible-gaming, marketing and geolocation rules. Its campus-advertising ban is a targeted conduct rule rather than the broad market overhaul in FL H0189.","watch":"Operators should audit college sponsorships, in-venue signage and university digital placements against the new Sec. 2(9) campus advertising ban, and have the new customer-assistance toll-free number live and displayed by July 1, 2026. They should also track the February 2027 prediction-market report, since its revenue-impact findings could drive competitive or restrictive legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":16323,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:41.970Z"},"DC-B26-0340":{"summary":"This is D.C.'s omnibus FY2026 Budget Support emergency act, and nearly all of it deals with non-gaming budget matters. Two subtitles in Title II are gaming-relevant. Subtitle G (Secs. 2061-2064) carves bingo, sports wagering and games of skill out of the D.C. gaming-contract statute (D.C. Code § 16-1702) and reworks game-of-skill machine licensing between the Office of Lottery and Gaming (OLG) and the Alcoholic Beverage and Cannabis Administration (ABCA); Subtitle H (Secs. 2071-2073) creates a new 'commercial bingo' category limited to on-premises alcohol licensees.","mechanics":["Sec. 2062 adds § 16-1702(b)-(d) so that subsection (a) does not apply to: commercial bingo under § 36-601.01 et seq.; sports wagering under § 36-621.01 et seq. as of May 3, 2019; and games of skill under § 36-641.01 et seq. as of April 27, 2021. This is a retroactive carve-out from the gaming-contract statute.","Games of skill (Secs. 2063-2064): a game of skill retailer must hold a retailer's license plus a game of skill machine endorsement under § 25-113.01(e). ABCA, not OLG, processes and issues retailer's licenses (new § 36-641.08(a-1)). OLG must promptly notify ABCA of any citation, revocation or other enforcement action (new § 36-641.16(f)). A § 25-508 fee is raised from $200 to $500/year.","Commercial bingo is defined as bingo played for consideration, with numbers drawn and called aloud by a person physically present with the players (new § 36-601.01(c)(4A)). Bingo is defined as a 75-ball game on 5x5 cards. The definition excludes charitable bingo under § 36-601.23(b)-(d), bingo played on federal gambling devices (15 U.S.C. 1171(a)), and online or class II facsimile play.","Only on-premises alcohol licensees in the listed classes (C/R, D/R, C/T, D/T, C/H, D/H, C/X, D/X, C/RB, D/RB, C/N, D/N) may offer commercial bingo. They need both an ABC Board endorsement ($300/year) and an OLG license, and OLG may not issue any commercial bingo license before October 1, 2025.","Sec. 2073(b) renames § 36-601.12 the 'Lottery, Gambling, and Gaming Fund' and excludes from it funds generated by the tax under §§ 47-2002(a)(9) and 47-2202(a)(6). The rate of that tax is not shown in the supplied text.","The CFO must adopt rules on the conduct of bingo and raffles to ensure integrity and fair play (rewritten § 36-601.24). The supplied text is truncated at this point, so the remaining rule requirements are not visible."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize","tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What stands out is that a budget support act retroactively exempts legal sports wagering (back to May 3, 2019) and games of skill (back to April 27, 2021) from D.C.'s gaming-contract statute. Effectively, this is a legislative fix that shores up the enforceability of wagering transactions. The act also creates a narrowly fenced commercial bingo product: in person, live-called, alcohol-licensee only, and expressly excluding online or electronic play.","watch":"Operators should confirm how the retroactive § 16-1702(c) carve-out for sports wagering affects litigation risk over pre-enactment wagers, and should watch whether the truncated bingo provisions or the separate §§ 47-2002(a)(9)/47-2202(a)(6) tax are later extended toward electronic or online formats.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":226903,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:48.611Z"},"DC-B26-0620":{"summary":"This emergency act approves, under section 451 of the Home Rule Act, the Amended and Restated Lease Agreement between the Washington Convention and Sports Authority (Events DC) and Washington Nationals Stadium, LLC for Nationals Ballpark, as submitted on March 2, 2026, and authorizes Events DC to enter into it notwithstanding the Procurement Practices Reform Act of 2010 and the 1939 surplus-property sale statute. The enrolled text contains no provisions on sports wagering, gaming, taxation of wagers, or advertising. Any betting-related terms would sit in the underlying lease, which is not reproduced here.","mechanics":["Sec. 2: Council approval of the Amended and Restated Lease Agreement submitted by Events DC on March 2, 2026, with waivers of the Procurement Practices Reform Act of 2010 (D.C. Code § 2-351.01 et seq.) and the 1939 real-estate sale act (D.C. Code § 10-801 et seq.).","Sec. 2: Approval is given under Home Rule Act § 451 (D.C. Code § 1-204.51), which requires Council approval of multiyear contracts.","Sec. 3: Adopts the Budget Director's fiscal impact statement. No figures are included in the text.","Sec. 4: Emergency act effective on Mayoral approval and limited to 90 days. Published as A26-0313 at 73 DCR 006836, it expires July 23, 2026.","The text contains no wagering definitions, rates, licensing terms or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. This act is unusual only in that it is a bare contract-approval vehicle. Any operative terms, including any concession, naming or sponsorship rights that could cover an on-site sportsbook, live in the separately submitted lease document rather than in the act. It neither amends nor references the District's sports wagering statute.","watch":"Obtain the March 2, 2026 lease itself and check whether it reallocates sportsbook concession, exclusivity, sponsorship or revenue-sharing rights at Nationals Park, and track the companion temporary and permanent bills that will replace this act after it expires on July 23, 2026.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":2258,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:53.307Z"},"DC-PR26-0566":{"summary":"This DC Council resolution confirms the Mayor's appointment of Kristi C. Whitfield (Ward 5) to the Board of Library Trustees for a term ending January 5, 2029. It has no connection to sports betting, gaming, or wagering.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 confirms Kristi C. Whitfield as a member of the Board of Library Trustees, established under D.C. Official Code § 39-104 (Act of June 3, 1896, 29 Stat. 244).","The term ends January 5, 2029.","Sec. 3 directs the Council to send a copy of the resolution to the nominee and the Mayor.","Sec. 4 makes it effective immediately. It was adopted March 31, 2026 as Resolution R26-0363 and published in DC Register Vol. 73, p. 006077."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, and this is a routine personnel confirmation, not a gaming measure. It contains no wagering, tax, licensing, or advertising provisions, and it does not even mention gaming in passing. It is best treated as a false positive in a gaming-legislation tracker.","watch":"Nothing to watch for sports-betting operators, because the resolution only fills a library board seat and does not touch the DC Lottery, OLG, or any gaming regulator.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":1022,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:53.985Z"},"DC-B26-0450":{"summary":"This is the District's omnibus FY2026 budget support emergency act. Most of it is unrelated to gaming, but two subtitles matter. Subtitle G retroactively exempts licensed sports wagering (as of May 3, 2019) and games of skill (as of April 27, 2021) from D.C. Code § 16-1702(a), and tightens alcohol-license integration for game-of-skill machines. Subtitle H creates a new commercial bingo regime limited to on-premises alcohol licensees, who need both an ABCA endorsement and an Office of Lottery and Gaming (OLG) license.","mechanics":["Sec. 2062 designates the existing text of D.C. Code § 16-1702 as subsection (a) and adds carve-outs: (a) does not apply to commercial bingo, does not apply to sports wagering under § 36-621.01 et seq. as of May 3, 2019, and does not apply to games of skill under § 36-641.01 et seq. as of April 27, 2021.","Game of skill (Secs. 2063-2064): the § 25-508 fee rises from $200 to $500/year, and § 25-113.01(e)(1) drops the 'on-site sales and consumption permit' holder category. A retailer must hold a retailer's license plus a game of skill machine endorsement, ABCA takes over processing and issuing retailer's licenses, and OLG must notify ABCA of citations, revocations or other enforcement against a licensed establishment.","Commercial bingo definition (Sec. 2073, new § 36-601.01(c)(4A)): bingo played for consideration, with a 75-ball draw announced by a caller physically present with the players. It excludes charitable bingo under § 36-601.23(b)-(d), bingo on gambling devices as defined in 15 U.S.C. 1171(a), and online bingo or Class II facsimile machines as defined in 25 U.S.C. 2703(7).","Commercial bingo licensing: only on-premises retailers in classes C/R, D/R, C/T, D/T, C/H, D/H, C/X, D/X, C/RB, D/RB, C/N and D/N may offer it. Each needs an ABC Board commercial bingo endorsement ($300/year) plus an OLG license, and OLG may not issue licenses before October 1, 2025. The CFO must adopt integrity rules for bingo and raffles.","Funds: the lottery fund at § 36-601.12 is renamed the 'Lottery, Gambling, and Gaming Fund', and revenue from the taxes imposed by §§ 47-2002(a)(9) and 47-2202(a)(6) is excluded from it. The supplied text is truncated, so the substance of those tax provisions is not visible.","Duration: this is a congressional-review emergency act, published at 72 DCR 890, that expires February 22, 2026. Durable effect depends on the companion permanent FY2026 Budget Support Act (D.C. Act 26-148)."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. The unusual feature is that a budget support vehicle carries a retroactive statutory carve-out shielding licensed sports wagering, dating back to the May 3, 2019 effective date of DC's sports wagering law, from the general gaming provision in § 16-1702. It does so alongside a narrowly drawn, in-person-only commercial bingo authorization that expressly bars electronic, online and Class II-style formats.","watch":"Confirm that the retroactive § 16-1702 sports-wagering carve-out, which is significant for defending loss-recovery or contract-voidness claims against licensed operators, is carried into permanent law before this emergency act lapses on February 22, 2026, and review the truncated commercial bingo tax provisions (§§ 47-2002(a)(9), 47-2202(a)(6)) for any spillover onto other gaming revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":242625,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:54.514Z"},"DC-B26-0265":{"summary":"The District's omnibus FY2026 budget support act, effective December 6, 2025, includes two gaming subtitles among dozens of unrelated provisions. Subtitle G (Sec. 2062) retroactively exempts sports wagering (as of May 3, 2019) and games of skill (as of April 27, 2021) from D.C. Code § 16-1702(a), and moves game-of-skill retailer licensing to ABCA. Subtitle H creates a regulated commercial bingo category limited to on-premises alcohol licensees, who must hold both an ABC Board endorsement and an Office of Lottery and Gaming license.","mechanics":["Sec. 2062 adds § 16-1702(b)-(d): subsection (a) does not apply to commercial bingo under § 36-601.01 et seq., to sports wagering under Subchapter II of Chapter 6 of Title 36 as of May 3, 2019, or to games of skill under Subchapter III as of April 27, 2021.","Game of skill (Secs. 2063-2064): a retailer needs a retailer's license plus a game of skill machine endorsement under § 25-113.01(e). The fee in § 25-508 rises from $200 to $500/year. ABCA, not OLG, processes and issues retailer's licenses, and OLG must promptly notify ABCA of any citation, revocation or other enforcement against a licensed establishment.","Commercial bingo is defined (§ 36-601.01(c)(4A)) as bingo for consideration with numbers drawn and called by a person physically present with the players. It excludes charitable bingo, bingo on federally defined gambling devices, and online play or machines that would be a facsimile of IGRA class II gaming.","Only on-premises retailers in classes C/R, D/R, C/T, D/T, C/H, D/H, C/X, D/X, C/RB, D/RB, C/N and D/N may offer commercial bingo. They need a $300/year commercial bingo endorsement from the ABC Board and an OLG license, and no OLG license may issue before October 1, 2025.","The Lottery fund is renamed the 'Lottery, Gambling, and Gaming Fund'. Revenue from the taxes at §§ 47-2002(a)(9) and 47-2202(a)(6) is carved out of the deposit rule, though the visible text does not show what those taxes are.","The CFO must adopt rules on bingo and raffle integrity and fair play, covering application requirements, fees, and device and technology configurations. The enrolled text supplied is truncated mid-section, so the remaining rulemaking items and any later gaming provisions are not visible."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize","integrity","tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the act is unusual because it uses a budget vehicle to declare, retroactive to the 2019 launch of DC sports wagering, that § 16-1702(a) does not apply to authorized sports wagering. It also creates a narrowly drawn, in-person-only commercial bingo category that expressly bars electronic, online and class II-style formats, and it splits gaming licensing between ABCA and OLG.","watch":"Confirm the scope and retroactive effect of the new § 16-1702(c) carve-out for sports wagering, which appears aimed at foreclosing challenges under the District's general gaming statute to bets placed since May 3, 2019. Also track the CFO bingo rules, the as-yet-unseen tax provisions at §§ 47-2002(a)(9) and 47-2202(a)(6), and any expansion of the ABCA licensing role that could reach sports-wagering retail partners.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":226277,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:54.838Z"},"DE-HB314":{"summary":"HB314 creates a new crime of student athlete harassment (new 28 Del. C. § 908) covering threats and sports-related bullying or cyber-bullying of intercollegiate student athletes tied to their athletic performance, and gives convicted offenders' victims a private right of action with $75,000 minimum statutory damages per violation. It also amends the lottery exclusion statute (29 Del. C. § 4835) so that people convicted under § 908 are mandatorily placed on the exclusion list for video lottery, table, Internet lottery and sports lottery games. It further lets the Lottery Director emergency-exclude from sports lottery play anyone charged with, or found to have threatened, such harassment, and directs the Director and the Division of Gaming Enforcement to set up a reporting channel for athletes and coaches.","mechanics":["New § 908(b) offense: intentionally communicating a threat to a student athlete related to performance in an intercollegiate sport, judged on both a subjective intent and a reasonable-person test, or engaging in sports-related bullying or cyber-bullying, which must be repeated, intended to harm, and cause emotional distress.","Penalties under § 908(c): a first offense is a class A misdemeanor and a second or subsequent offense is a class E felony, with exclusive Superior Court jurisdiction; 'student athlete' and 'intercollegiate sport' are defined by cross-reference to 24 Del. C. § 5402.","Private right of action under § 908(e) against a person convicted of the offense, with at least $75,000 in statutory damages per intentional violation plus actual damages, punitive damages, equitable relief, costs and attorney fees.","Electronic communications providers are exempt when acting in the normal course of business, but social network operators serving Delaware must disclose log files and communications on a warrant, a court order based on specific and articulable facts, or user consent; providers get immunity for compliance and may move to quash unduly burdensome orders.","Amended § 4835(c)(3) lets the Director issue an emergency order excluding a person from sports lottery play if the person has been charged under § 908 or a similar out-of-state statute, or has threatened a student athlete over performance in an intercollegiate sport that is the subject of a sports lottery game; new § 4835(j) mandates placing anyone convicted under § 908 or a similar statute on the exclusion list.","New § 4835(k) requires the Director and the Division of Gaming Enforcement to establish and publicize a method for student athletes and intercollegiate coaches to report violations or threats."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike MD HB518, which pairs college athlete protections with problem-gambling and voluntary exclusion regulations, Delaware's bill is built around a new criminal offense and a civil damages remedy, with gaming exclusion as the enforcement hook. It resembles NY A 7903 and S07482 in designating people convicted of crimes against athletes as barred bettors, but NY places the duty to block accounts on account wagering licensees, while Delaware runs it through the State Lottery Director's exclusion list, because Delaware sports betting operates as a state lottery. It does not touch prop-bet restrictions like LA SB354 or NC H828, and it has nothing in common with the NIL bills listed (GA HB383, HI HB 2384, TX HB126).","watch":"Watch how the mandatory § 4835(j) exclusion list, which also reaches convictions under 'similar' statutes in other states, and the emergency exclusions based only on charges or threats will be passed to and enforced by Delaware's sports lottery vendors and casino licensees, including duties to screen accounts and to respond to the new reporting channel; note also that the bill's last recorded action, a failed motion to release on 2026-03-18, suggests it is stalled.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":11021,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:11.015Z"},"GA-HB383":{"summary":"HB383 creates a new Article 32A in Title 20 of the Georgia Code that lets high school student athletes, including eighth graders and home-study participants, receive name, image, or likeness (NIL) compensation, subject to Commissioner of Labor consent under the child-labor provisions of Code Section 39-2-18. Among other limits, it bars NIL deals that can reasonably be perceived as endorsing or promoting casinos and gambling, including sports betting, the lottery, and betting tied to video, online, or mobile games. It also bars pay-for-enrollment and school or booster NIL payments, makes contracts expire automatically after graduation or withdrawal, and stops athletic associations from penalizing NIL activity.","mechanics":["Section 20-2-2105.3(e)(7) prohibits a student athlete from receiving NIL compensation for any conduct that can reasonably be perceived as endorsing or promoting casinos and gambling, including sports betting, the lottery, and betting in connection with video games, online games, and mobile devices. The same list covers alcohol, tobacco, vapor, cannabis, firearms, and any product restricted to people under 18.","Revised Section 39-2-18(b)(4) requires the Commissioner of Labor, before giving the written consent needed for a minor's NIL compensation, to determine that the minor's NIL will not be used for any purpose barred by 20-2-2105.3(e). This builds the gambling ban into the state labor consent process.","Section 20-2-2105.3(b)-(d) bars NIL tied to attending a particular school or college, and bars payments from schools, boosters, or their agents. It also bans use of school marks or references to a school or association in NIL activity, and bars endorsing an NIL payor or its third-party partners during games and competitions (tryouts, practices, and training sessions are exempt).","Under Section 20-2-2105.6, NIL contracts cannot survive past the earlier of 10 days after graduation or 30 days after enrollment ends, and any noncompliant contract terms are void.","Under Sections 20-2-2105.7 and 20-2-2105.8, athletic associations may not penalize athletes or schools for permitted NIL activity. Publicly funded schools may not take part in associations whose governing documents do not comply, and athletes may hire certified agents or attorneys, with agents required to comply with the federal SPARTA.","Under Section 4, the Act takes effect on the Governor's approval (or on becoming law without it) and applies to contracts entered into or renewed on or after that date."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which restricts gaming advertising on college campuses directly through gaming licensees, HB383 places the gambling restriction on the minor's ability to accept NIL compensation rather than on operators. It is also unlike MD HB518, which pairs problem-gambling regulations with college athlete protections inside the gaming code. Its closest analogues are LA HB 513 (interscholastic and intercollegiate NIL) and DE HB314 (student athlete protection), while HI HB 2384 and SB3263 address only university-level NIL. None of the listed summaries indicate that those bills contain an express sports-betting endorsement ban like Georgia's 20-2-2105.3(e)(7).","watch":"Operators should watch the broad 'reasonably can be perceived as endorsing or promoting' standard and the 'third-party partner' language, because sponsorships, affiliate marketing, or social-casino and sweepstakes-style promotions that touch high school athletes (including eighth graders) could be swept in even though the bill imposes no direct penalty on operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":12556,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:14.498Z"},"FL-H189":{"summary":"CS/CS/CS/HB 189 is an omnibus Florida gambling-enforcement bill that tightens Florida Gaming Control Commission ethics and revolving-door rules, raises many gambling offenses from misdemeanors to felonies, and adds licensure consequences for gambling-house and slot-machine convictions. According to its title, it also expressly criminalizes Internet gambling and Internet sports wagering (amended s. 849.08), imposes requirements on fantasy sports contests (new s. 849.0932), prohibits certain gambling advertising (new s. 849.48), and preempts local ordinances (new s. 849.49). The House bill was laid on the table on 2026-03-11 in favor of CS/CS/SB 1580, so the Senate bill is now the live vehicle.","mechanics":["Integrity: amended s. 838.12(3) makes it a third-degree felony to bet on any professional or amateur contest knowing the result is prearranged or fixed. Section 1 expands the Commission's annual report to cover complaints, seized property and forfeitures.","Revolving door: ss. 16.713 and 16.715 bar commissioners and employees from working for, owning, or contracting with any entity that 'conducts or facilitates an activity regulated, enforced, or investigated by the commission, including fantasy sports contests and other betting activities.' The bar runs for 2 years before service, during service, and for 2 years after.","Licensure: new s. 849.023 lets DBPR, the Commission (chs. 550, 551, s. 849.086) and OFR (ch. 560) summarily suspend or revoke a license after a conviction under s. 849.01, 849.03 or 849.15, regardless of adjudication. The same applies if a 'controlling person' (officer, director, or more than 5% equity holder) is convicted. The licensee faces a fine of up to $75,000 but can keep the license by removing the controlling person.","Criminal penalties: s. 849.01 (keeping a gambling house) moves from a second-degree misdemeanor to a third-degree felony. Sections 849.02 and 849.03 add escalating penalties, reaching a second-degree felony for repeat offenses. New s. 849.021 makes it a felony for a government employee to knowingly license, aid or conceal a gambling house.","Pari-mutuel ownership: s. 550.054(12) requires Commission pre-approval for ownership changes of 5% or more unless the owner was previously approved, and changes under 5% must be reported within 20 days. Escheated harness and thoroughbred permits can no longer be reissued.","Amusement machines: under s. 546.10(8), federally chartered veterans' organizations with liquor licenses may seek a binding declaratory statement on a machine, which the Commission must issue within 60 days. The option is unavailable if the machine or premises is under criminal investigation."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing","advertising","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, which authorize, license and tax mobile sports wagering, FL H0189 goes the other way: it is a prohibition and enforcement bill that criminalizes Internet sports wagering outside existing exceptions. Its fantasy-contest provisions (s. 849.0932) sit in the criminal code with Attorney General and state attorney enforcement, unlike IL HB2342's Gaming Board licensing model for fantasy operators. Its advertising ban (s. 849.48) targets illegal gambling generally, rather than regulating licensed-operator marketing as CT HB05229 does.","watch":"Track CS/CS/SB 1580, the live vehicle, for the final text of the s. 849.08 Internet sports wagering prohibition, the s. 849.0932 fantasy contest requirements and the s. 849.48 advertising ban, which are truncated in this excerpt; check how the exceptions are drawn, since offshore, DFS and pick'em operators and their vendors and advertisers could face felony exposure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":127509,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:17.897Z"},"FL-S1580":{"summary":"CS/CS/SB 1580 is a broad Florida illegal-gambling enforcement package. It sharply increases criminal penalties for keeping or facilitating gambling houses, creates new offenses (including betting on a fixed contest), and adds licensure consequences for businesses whose controlling persons commit gambling crimes. According to its title, it also criminalizes Internet gambling, Internet sports wagering and advertising of illegal gambling, preempts local gaming ordinances, and restructures Florida Gaming Control Commission governance and ethics rules; it died in returning messages on 2026-03-13.","mechanics":["s. 849.01: keeping a gambling house, now expressly defined, moves from a second-degree misdemeanor to a third-degree felony for a first offense and a second-degree felony for repeat offenses (or where great bodily harm results). New s. 849.01(3) makes it a third-degree felony, with a mandatory $50,000 fine per violation, to knowingly or in reckless disregard benefit financially from, or participate in a 'course of conduct' providing any service, product or material benefit in furtherance of, a gambling house. Keeping a gambling house is also added as a felony-murder predicate in s. 782.04(3)(s).","s. 838.12(3) (new): staking, betting or wagering on any professional or amateur game, contest, match, race or sport with knowledge that the outcome is prearranged or predetermined is a third-degree felony.","s. 849.023 (new): violations of ss. 849.01, 849.03 or 849.15 are deemed an immediate and serious danger to public health, safety and welfare. DBPR, the Gaming Control Commission or OFR may summarily suspend licenses (alcohol, lodging, pari-mutuel, slot, cardroom, money services) of violators or of entities whose 'controlling person' commits them; a controlling person includes officers, directors and holders of more than 5% equity. Convictions, including withheld adjudication and nolo pleas, can lead to revocation, avoidable by removing the controlling person, and licensees face fines up to $75,000.","Per the bill title (operative text not reproduced in the excerpt provided), the bill also: amends s. 849.08 to define and criminalize playing, operating, conducting or promoting Internet gambling or Internet sports wagering; creates s. 849.48 criminalizing advertising of illegal gambling, with exceptions; creates s. 849.49 preempting local ordinances on certain gaming; and creates s. 849.155 on bulk slot-machine trafficking and a Limited Slot Machine Surrender Program offering immunity.","s. 849.021 (new): a government employee who knowingly certifies, licenses, approves, aids, facilitates or conceals a gambling house commits a third-degree felony, and a second-degree felony on a repeat offense.","Commission and industry changes: the full commission, rather than the chair, appoints the inspector general. Revolving-door restrictions for former staff are expanded to 2 years, covering employment with any regulated person or any party to a commission proceeding, subject to case-by-case waivers. Veterans' organizations may seek binding declaratory statements on amusement machines, decided within 60/90 days. Slot licensees may relocate gaming areas within 1,320 feet in the same county under s. 551.114(4)(b)."],"issue_tags":["integrity","advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the legalization-and-tax bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569), FL S1580 does not authorize or tax any new wagering; it is a criminal enforcement bill aimed at unlicensed gambling, and, per its title, Internet sports wagering outside Florida's compact-based framework. CT HB05229 restricts advertising by licensed operators, whereas FL's advertising provision (per its title) targets promotion of illegal gambling. FL's match-fixing wagering offense in s. 838.12(3) is an integrity measure absent from the listed IL bills. Unlike IL HB5142 and HB5059, nothing in the reproduced FL text addresses prediction markets.","watch":"Although the bill died, a reintroduction would carry the s. 849.01(3) 'reckless disregard' facilitation offense, with a $50,000 per-violation fine, and the reported Internet sports wagering and advertising crimes, which could expose payment processors, suppliers, affiliates and media partners of any operator not authorized under Florida law; operators with Florida-licensed affiliates should also note that s. 849.023 lets regulators summarily suspend licenses based on the conduct of 5%-plus owners.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":137816,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:21.481Z"},"GA-HR753":{"summary":"HR 753, as substituted by the House Rules Committee, is an omnibus study-committee resolution. Sections 1 and 2 create committees on rural internal medicine access and on paternity legitimation, and Section 3 creates the House Study Committee on Gaming in the State of Georgia. That committee is charged with studying the economic, fiscal, workforce and social impacts of legalizing destination resort casinos, pari-mutuel and fixed-odds horse racing, and digital, retail and on-premise sports betting, and with proposing legislation before it is abolished on December 1, 2025.","mechanics":["Section 3(2): seven members, namely the chairs of the House Ways and Means, Regulated Industries, and Economic Development Committees plus four House members appointed by the Speaker, one of whom the Speaker designates as chair.","Section 3(4): the committee must hold at least four public hearings at various locations around the state and at least one meeting to finalize findings.","Section 3(6): unlike the other two committees, which may file a report, the gaming committee is directed that it 'shall adopt specific findings or recommendations that include proposed legislation' and must file a report before abolishment.","Scope per the whereas clauses: revenue and tax potential, job quality, social harms, gaming dollars lost to other states (Nevada, Mississippi, Alabama, North Carolina and Massachusetts are named), and horse-breeding industry benefits from pari-mutuel racing.","Funding comes from House appropriations, with member allowances under O.C.G.A. 28-1-8 capped at three days unless additional days are authorized; all committees are abolished December 1, 2025.","The resolution creates no legal authorization, tax rate or licensing regime. Any legalization would still require voter approval of a constitutional amendment and enabling legislation."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is an unusual vehicle: a gaming study committee bundled into a resolution whose title concerns rural internal medicine. Unlike earlier Georgia legalization measures that went straight to constitutional amendments or enabling statutes, it sets up a pre-session fact-finding process that is required to produce draft legislation.","watch":"Operators should engage with the four or more statewide public hearings and the committee's mandatory proposed legislation due before December 1, 2025, since that report will likely shape the tax rate, mobile versus retail licensing structure, and any bundling of sports betting with casinos and horse racing in Georgia's 2026 session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":13513,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:26.355Z"},"GA-HR450":{"summary":"HR 450 is a proposed amendment to Article I, Section II, Paragraph VIII of the Georgia Constitution that would let the General Assembly authorize and regulate sports betting by general law, subject to voter ratification. It earmarks sports betting tax proceeds to a new Georgia Sports Betting Proceeds Trust Fund for problem-gambling programs and lottery-style education programs such as pre-K and HOPE scholarships. It also allows the Georgia Lottery Corporation to participate in sports betting. The resolution failed on House third reading on 2026-03-06.","mechanics":["Rewritten subparagraph (a) keeps a constitutional ban, enforced by penal laws, on 'all forms of sports betting, pari-mutuel betting, and casino gambling' except as the Paragraph provides; only sports betting is carved out.","New subparagraph (d) authorizes the General Assembly to provide by law for operating and regulating sports betting. Tax proceeds go to the Georgia Sports Betting Proceeds Trust Fund. Other regulatory proceeds first cover regulatory operating expenses and reserves without appropriation, and the net remainder goes to the Trust Fund.","Subparagraph (e)(1) splits Trust Fund appropriations: 15% of the first $150 million each fiscal year goes to problem-gambling prevention and treatment, and 85% of the first $150 million plus everything above $150 million goes to voluntary pre-K and the existing lottery education purposes in subparagraph (c). Under (e)(2), the education share is exempt from the lapse rule in Art. III, Sec. IX, Para. IV(c).","Subparagraph (f) lets the Georgia Lottery Corporation be authorized by law to participate in sports betting. Its participation proceeds follow the lottery education rules in (c), while any tax imposed on its participation follows the Trust Fund rules in (e).","The amendment contains no tax rate, licensing structure, license cap, or mobile-versus-retail rules; all of that is left to implementing legislation.","Section 2 sets the ballot question: authorize sports betting 'to provide additional funding for pre-kindergarten and HOPE scholarships?' Ratification follows Art. X, Sec. I, Para. II."],"issue_tags":["legalize","prop","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Alaska's HB145 and SB194 and Hawaii's HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 are statutory frameworks that directly set licensing, regulators, and taxes for operators. HR 450 is only a constitutional enabling amendment with no operator-facing terms. Its distinctive features are that it hard-codes revenue earmarks into the constitution, including a problem-gambling carve-out capped at 15% of the first $150 million, and that it gives the state lottery corporation an express constitutional role. It also writes explicit prohibitions on pari-mutuel and casino gambling into the constitution. Illinois bills such as HB3159 and HB5143 instead adjust taxes within an already operating sports wagering regime.","watch":"The resolution lost on House third reading, so watch for reconsideration or a successor vehicle, and for whether companion enabling legislation uses subparagraph (f) to channel the market through the Georgia Lottery Corporation, which could shape whether private operators participate and on what terms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":10621,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:27.321Z"},"HI-SB1569":{"summary":"SB1569 SD1 would legalize mobile-only sports wagering in Hawaii under a new HRS chapter administered by the Department of Business, Economic Development, and Tourism (DBEDT), with at least four operator licenses restricted to \"qualified gaming entities\" already live in three or more U.S. states. It taxes adjusted gross sports wagering receipts at 10% through a new paragraph (9) of the general excise tax statute, section 237-13. It also carves sports wagering and fantasy sports contests out of the gambling definitions in section 712-1220. The SD1 carries a placeholder effective date of July 1, 2050 and blank criminal fine amounts and appropriation figures, signalling the bill is still a vehicle for negotiation.","mechanics":["Licensing: DBEDT must issue a minimum of four operator licenses (section -5), open only to entities offering online sports wagering in at least three U.S. jurisdictions. The fee is $250,000 for a five-year term plus cost-based processing fees. A three-year temporary license is available on payment of the initial fee, and a universal start date must be set no later than 180 days after enactment.","Supplier licenses (section -6) cost $10,000 for five years, with temporary licenses available. Suppliers are oddly defined as providers of geolocation and age verification services.","Tax: 10% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts under new HRS 237-13(9), on the accrual method, stated to be in lieu of all other taxes on sports wagering operations. Adjusted gross receipts are gross cash wagers less winnings paid. There is no promotional-credit deduction, and bonuses count as an account-funding method.","Product scope: mobile/digital only, with kiosks and retail terminals barred (section -10(c)). Bettors must be 21+ and physically in Hawaii. Collegiate, amateur, Olympic, motorsport and e-sports events are permitted, and wagering on events where most participants are under 18 is excluded. Exchange wagering, in-play and prop bets are expressly allowed, as are layoff bets between operators and interstate wagering agreements.","Operator duties (section -8): integrity monitoring software, with suspicious activity reported to DBEDT; 1-800-GAMBLER in all advertising; daily receipts records; DBEDT-approved house rules. There is also a voluntary self-exclusion program with lifetime option and at least quarterly list sharing, and an employee betting ban.","Enforcement: civil fines up to $5,000 per violation or $10,000 per occurrence, which can be imposed on unlicensed persons too. Unlicensed sports wagering escalates from a misdemeanor to a class C and then class B felony, with fine amounts left blank. A problem gambling special fund administered by the Department of Health is funded only by appropriation."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like AK HB145 and SB194, SB1569 is a mobile-only legalization bill in a state with no existing casino industry, so licenses go to out-of-state online operators rather than being tethered to land-based venues. Its 10% GGR rate is low compared with the steep rate and license-fee proposals in Illinois, for example IL HB2878 and HB5034 setting a $15 million master license fee. Unlike CT HB05229 or IL HB5059, it contains no detailed advertising, AI-targeting or prediction-market provisions beyond the 1-800-GAMBLER requirement. Unlike GA HR450 and SR131, it proceeds by statute without a constitutional amendment.","watch":"Watch whether the 2050 placeholder effective date, the blank fine amounts and the 10% rate are reset in the 2026 session. Also watch whether the 'minimum of four' license language and the three-jurisdiction qualification gate stay intact, along with the absence of a promotional-play deduction, since these will determine market access and effective tax burden.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":48389,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:36.829Z"},"GA-SR131":{"summary":"SR131 is a proposed amendment to the Georgia Constitution (Art. I, Sec. II, Para. VIII) that would let the General Assembly authorize and regulate sports betting and casino gambling by statute, administered by a gaming commission it must create. The amendment fixes the tax rate and dedicates the proceeds to a new Georgia Gaming Proceeds Fund, with large set-asides for county governments and problem-gambling programs. It takes effect only if passed by the legislature and ratified by voters on the stated ballot question.","mechanics":["New subpara. (e)(1)-(2): the General Assembly may provide by law for sports betting and casino gambling and must create a gaming commission with regulatory and taxing powers. Revised subpara. (a) expressly lists sports betting, pari-mutuel betting and casino gambling as prohibited except as provided, and nothing in (e) authorizes pari-mutuel betting.","Subpara. (e)(3) on casinos: at least eight casino gaming licenses, each for a single facility. No person or entity may hold more than one license or own an interest in more than one licensee. No off-site casino gambling is permitted, which would effectively bar online casino gaming. The text is silent on whether sports betting must be in-person or may be mobile.","Subpara. (e)(4) tax: the rate is fixed at 20 percent of gross income, less operating expenses allowed by law, winnings paid, and reserves permitted by law. The proceeds go to the Georgia Gaming Proceeds Fund. Other regulatory proceeds, such as fees, first fund the commission's operating expenses and reserves, and any remainder goes to the Fund.","Subpara. (e)(5) allocation: the first $2 billion is split evenly among all counties. After that, the first $500 million of each fiscal year's additional proceeds goes evenly to counties until at least $5 billion more has been paid out. At least 5 percent of the remaining proceeds must fund problem-gambling prevention and treatment. The Fund sits outside the general fund and lottery allocation rules, and under (e)(6) appropriations do not lapse.","Section 2: the measure goes to voters under Art. X, Sec. I, Para. II, with the question whether the General Assembly may provide by law for sports betting and casino gambling."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the statutory mobile-wagering frameworks in AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB1308, HB2570 and SB1569, SR131 creates no operative licensing regime itself; it is a constitutional enabling amendment that still needs voter ratification and later implementing legislation. It is also unusual in locking a tax rate (20 percent) and a revenue distribution formula into the constitution. Illinois bills such as HB2878 (a $15 million master license fee) and HB3159 (a 2 percent add-on for fire and EMS) adjust an existing statute and can be changed by ordinary legislation. Its express ban on off-site casino gambling also runs opposite to iGaming authorizations such as IL HB3080 and HB4797.","watch":"Watch three things: the constitutionally fixed 20 percent rate and its unusual net base, since the operating expenses 'allowed by law' will be defined in implementing legislation; the 'no off-site casino gambling' clause, which forecloses iGaming; and whether enabling legislation permits statewide mobile sports betting given the one-license-per-entity ownership limit, which is drafted for casino licenses but could be extended.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7287,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:39.416Z"},"IA-HF2089":{"summary":"HF2089 amends Iowa Code section 99F.6(4)(a)(2) to require casino licensees, when making their existing mandatory charitable distributions (at least 3% of gambling-game adjusted gross receipts and 0.75% of sports wagering net receipts), to direct at least 75% of those moneys pro rata to Iowa counties based on where contributing players reside. It imposes new player-residency recordkeeping, an annual public report to the Racing and Gaming Commission, and civil penalties and license discipline for noncompliance.","mechanics":["Does not change the existing 3% AGR and 0.75% sports wagering net receipts charitable distribution floors; new subparagraph division (b) governs how at least 75% of the required distribution is allocated by county.","County share equals gross receipts from players residing in that county divided by gross receipts from players in all Iowa counties; receipts from nonresidents or players whose county cannot be verified are spread proportionally on the same formula.","Licensees must collect and maintain records of the county of residence of all players or participants and the receipts attributable to each, covering both gambling games and sports wagering.","Annual report to the commission, posted publicly, covering receipts by county, nonresident and unverified receipts, total distributions, and per-county distributions.","Enforcement: civil penalty up to 5% of the total required distribution, deposited in the general fund; the commission may order redistribution; willful or repeated failure may lead to additional discipline including license suspension.","Applies to receipts collected in fiscal years beginning on or after the Act's effective date. The 25% remainder stays distributable as otherwise allowed by law."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. What is unusual is that it ties a licensee's charitable giving to player geography, effectively mandating player-level residency tracking. That is straightforward for online sports wagering accounts with KYC data but hard for anonymous retail casino and slot play. The penalty is measured against the whole required distribution, not just the misallocated portion.","watch":"Watch the player-level county-of-residence tracking requirement, which is hard to meet for anonymous retail gaming. Its interaction with existing operating-agreement distribution terms is also unclear, since the bill's 75% rule is not expressly limited by the \"unless an operating agreement otherwise provides\" clause. Licensees also face penalty exposure of up to 5% of total required distributions plus possible license suspension.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":11222,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:40.068Z"},"HI-SB3263":{"summary":"SB3263 CD1 creates a University of Hawaii name, image, and likeness framework under HRS chapter 304A. It affirms student-athletes' right to earn NIL compensation, requires UH to adopt NIL policies, sets up third-party deal disclosure and annual legislative reporting, and establishes a NIL endowment fund seeded with tuition-fund, general-fund, and matching private money. The only gaming hook is §304A-C(c)(3), which lets UH bar student-athletes from NIL deals involving gambling or sports wagering.","mechanics":["§304A-C(c) permits, but does not require, UH to prohibit NIL agreements involving adult entertainment, firearms, gambling or sports wagering, illegal drugs, tobacco or vaping, or any industry conflicting with the university's mission or financial interests.","§304A-C(d) gives student-athletes at least ten business days after signing any institutional or third-party NIL agreement to rescind it.","§304A-D(a) requires student-athletes to disclose third-party NIL agreements worth $600 or more, or an alternative NCAA or federal threshold UH designates. Disclosed records are confidential and exempt from chapter 92F. Annual aggregate reports to the legislature begin with the 2028 session.","§304A-G endowment: $4M transferred from the UH tuition and fees special fund, $2.5M general-fund appropriation for FY2026-27 (Section 5, effective July 1, 2026), and $5M in university matching gifts. The corpus has a stated $10M minimum, and interest may be spent once the corpus has held $7.5M for three years.","The Act takes effect on approval and sunsets June 30, 2030, with legislative review planned for the 2029 session. Unexpended fund balances lapse to their source."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike MD HB518 (college athlete protections inside a problem-gambling bill), CT HB05229 (restricting gaming advertising on college campuses) and TN SB1662 (funding athletic programs from a fee on sports wagering vendors), Hawaii's bill is a pure NIL statute. It touches wagering only through a discretionary university power to exclude gambling sponsors. It is closer to the NIL bills in WI AB1034, TX HB126, NE LB370 and NH HB312, but unusual in pairing a post-House-settlement framework with a state-seeded NIL endowment and a hard 2030 sunset. Because Hawaii has no legal sports betting, the gambling-sponsorship carve-out has limited immediate commercial effect.","watch":"Watch whether UH exercises its §304A-C(c)(3) discretion to adopt a blanket ban on gambling and sports-wagering NIL deals, since that policy could become a template for other states and would bar operator endorsements with UH athletes if Hawaii ever legalizes sports betting.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":18376,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:40.123Z"},"IL-HB1075":{"summary":"Illinois's Fiscal Year 2026 Budget Implementation Act (Public Act 104-0002) makes the statutory changes needed to carry out the FY2026 budget across many state programs, including agency powers, fund transfers, fund dissolutions and grant program tweaks. In the portion of the text provided, gaming appears only as a source of transfers into the Audit Expense Fund. The text supplied is truncated partway through State Finance Act Section 6z-27, so later articles cannot be assessed here.","mechanics":["Section 5-30 amends 30 ILCS 105/6z-27 to order transfers into the Audit Expense Fund within 30 days after July 1, 2025. The schedule shown includes Sports Wagering Fund $14,696, State Gaming Fund $216,475, Horse Racing Fund $233,685, Illinois Gaming Law Enforcement Fund $1,411 and State Lottery Fund $106,169. The text as provided mixes stricken and new schedules, so treat these figures as indicative.","Section 5-5 gives the Department of Central Management Services authority to procure 'site readiness work' (abatement, remediation, demolition, title and environmental reports) for surplus state real property, under new 20 ILCS 405/405-217.","Section 5-10 raises the SBIR/STTR Phase I matching grant cap from $50,000 to $75,000. It also adds Phase II matching grants of up to $250,000, subject to appropriation, with a lifetime limit of 2 awards.","Section 5-25 reallocates the $4-per-car-year insurer assessment from July 1, 2025: 10% to the State Police Law Enforcement Administration Fund, 10% to the State Police Vehicle Fund and 80% to the Law Enforcement Training Fund.","Section 5-10 dissolves the Small Business Environmental Assistance Fund into the Clean Air Act Permit Fund on July 1, 2025, and Section 5-30 repeals its State Finance Act listing effective January 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. On its own, this is a general budget implementation omnibus. In the text provided, wagering-related funds appear only as line items in the routine Audit Expense Fund sweep, not as changes to sports wagering tax rates, licensing or operations. Those changes, if any, would sit in separate FY2026 revenue legislation not shown here.","watch":"Because the supplied text cuts off mid-Section 6z-27, review the remaining articles of P.A. 104-0002 for any further transfers from, or diversions of, the Sports Wagering Fund or State Gaming Fund, or other amendments touching the Sports Wagering Act or Illinois Gambling Act.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":979334,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:50.410Z"},"IL-HB111":{"summary":"This is Governor Pritzker's June 16, 2026 veto message returning Illinois HB 111, the FY 2027 appropriations act, with item and reduction vetoes totaling $500,418,129,782, and approving all other appropriations. The vetoes correct lines the House and Senate sponsors (Rep. Gabel and Sen. Sims) identified as erroneous or duplicative. Nearly the entire total comes from one typographical line, Article 52, Section 285, enacted at $500,250,000,000. The message contains no sports-betting or gaming provisions on its face.","mechanics":["Authority: Article IV, Section 9(d) of the Illinois Constitution of 1970 (item and reduction veto).","Total vetoed: $500,418,129,782. Of that, $500,250,000,000 is a single typographical error at Art. 52, Sec. 285, p. 430, lines 4-7.","Item vetoes hit lines in Articles 37, 41, 52, 93 and 118. Examples are Art. 93, Secs. 190, 195 and 200 ($20M, $4.5M and $30M) and Art. 52, Secs. 490 and 495 ($17.5M and $17.81M).","Reductions include Art. 15, Secs. 476 and 1295 (each cut from $17M to $1.7M), Art. 52, Sec. 140 (cut from $60M to $45M) and Art. 93, Sec. 40 (cut from $35M to $30M).","The message identifies lines only by article, section, page and line, without naming agencies, funds or purposes, so it cannot be determined from this text whether any vetoed line touches the Gaming Board or wagering-funded programs.","All other appropriations in HB 111 were approved, and the enacted act is PA 104-0464."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is the headline veto figure of roughly $500.4 billion, which is almost entirely one typographical line rather than a real policy cut. The vetoes were coordinated with the bill's own sponsors as technical clean-up, not adversarial line-item cuts.","watch":"Check the underlying enacted text of PA 104-0464 to see whether any vetoed or reduced lines, especially in Articles 52 and 93, correspond to Illinois Gaming Board operations or sports-wagering-funded appropriations, such as the Capital Projects Fund or problem gambling programs, since this veto message itself does not identify them.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Veto Message","text_chars":3255,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:50.958Z"},"IL-HB1704":{"summary":"HB1704 amends Section 6z-27 of the Illinois State Finance Act to update the annual audit-cost sweep, directing the Comptroller and Treasurer to transfer specified amounts from roughly 250 state funds into the Audit Expense Fund within 30 days after July 1, 2025 (changed from 2024). It is a routine shell-style finance bill; gaming-related funds, including the Sports Wagering Fund, appear only as line items among the transferring funds.","mechanics":["Changes the transfer trigger date from July 1, 2024 to July 1, 2025; transfers occur within 30 days or as soon as practical.","Gaming-related line items include Sports Wagering Fund $14,696; State Gaming Fund $1,997 and $216,475; State Lottery Fund $311,489 and $106,169; Horse Racing Fund $233,685; Illinois Gaming Law Enforcement Fund $1,411.","General Revenue Fund is listed twice at $17,653,153 each, and Education Assistance Fund is listed at $2,193,017 and $2,347,928; several funds appear twice in the list.","Federal and State trust funds are excluded unless trustees direct transfers; any shortfall in a listed fund is backfilled from the General Revenue Fund.","Auditor General must return any excess over actual audit costs to the originating fund on or around September 30; the Act is effective immediately upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["sweeps"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual only because it is a recurring Illinois audit-cost transfer vehicle. It touches sports betting only through a $14,696 draw on the Sports Wagering Fund and other modest gaming-fund draws, and it contains no substantive gaming policy. Its last action was a Rule 19(a) re-referral to the Rules Committee on 2025-04-11, which suggests it is likely dormant or held as a shell for later amendment.","watch":"Because this is a re-referred finance shell bill, watch for a floor or committee amendment that repurposes it to carry larger sweeps from the Sports Wagering Fund or State Gaming Fund, or changes to wagering taxes, late in session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":25672,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:52.193Z"},"IL-HB1451":{"summary":"HB1451 amends the Illinois Substance Use Disorder Act to fold gambling disorder more fully into the Department of Human Services' prevention, treatment, licensing and grant authority, largely by adding 'or gambling disorders' throughout the Act. It expands the Sec. 5-20 gambling disorder program to include a toll-free hotline and website, stigma-reduction awareness and suicide-prevention efforts. It also requires each master sports wagering licensee to display a DHS-determined help statement on its portal, website, or computer or mobile app.","mechanics":["Sec. 1-10 adds definitions. 'Gambling disorder' means persistent and recurring maladaptive gambling behavior that disrupts personal, family, or vocational pursuits. 'Gambling' means risking money or other items of value in games of chance, expressly including video gaming and sports betting. 'Gaming' is defined separately as playing video games.","Sec. 5-20(a)-(b): subject to appropriation, DHS (or a contractor) must run a toll-free hotline and website in place of the '800' number, promote awareness of gambling disorder impacts and stigma, and use screening, crisis intervention, treatment and training to reduce gambling-related suicide attempts.","Sec. 5-20(c): DHS determines a statement on obtaining help with a gambling disorder. Licensed gambling establishment owners must post it, and master sports wagering licensees must include it on their portal, website, or app. The provision cross-references Sec. 25.95 of the Sports Wagering Act, among other gaming statutes, for DHS-supplied signage.","Sec. 5-20(d)-(e): DHS may produce prevention literature and curricula, advise State and local officials, and support prevention projects. It may also award grants for local prevention, recognition, response and treatment programs to local health departments, correctional institutions, hospitals, universities, community-based organizations and faith-based organizations, funded by appropriations or outside grants.","Sec. 15-5: makes it unlawful to provide treatment for gambling disorders without a DHS license, extending existing substance use treatment licensure to gambling treatment providers.","Sec. 10-10 and 10-15: the Advisory Council gains duties on gambling disorder prevention, including preventing 'gambling behaviors while gaming by minors', and its public members include representatives of gambling disorder treatment programs. No new tax, fee or operator funding obligation and no effective date appear in the provided text, which is truncated."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the Hawaii sports wagering bills (HI HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569), which create problem gambling programs as part of a full legalization, licensing and tax package, IL HB1451 is a standalone public-health bill inside an existing legal market. It imposes no tax or fee earmark. It also lacks the direct operator conduct rules in CT HB05229 (withdrawal timing, customer service lines, AI-targeting ban) and Indiana HB1078's voluntary exclusion and HB1092's child-support withholding mechanisms. Its only direct operator obligation is displaying a DHS-determined help statement.","watch":"Watch the content, placement and formatting DHS sets for the required help statement on master sports wagering licensees' websites and apps under Sec. 5-20(c), and whether later amendments add an operator-funded assessment to pay for the expanded, appropriation-dependent programs.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":86527,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:01.321Z"},"IL-HB1833":{"summary":"HB1833 creates the Metropolitan Mobility Authority Act, which merges the Chicago Transit Authority, the RTA's Commuter Rail and Suburban Bus Divisions, and the RTA itself into a single Metropolitan Mobility Authority covering Cook, DuPage, Kane, Lake, McHenry and Will counties, and abolishes the Service Boards. It also creates an Equitable Transit-Supportive Development Act and fund, an IDOT Office of Public Transportation Support, and makes the Authority's Chair a nonvoting member of the Tollway board. The text provided contains no sports betting, gaming or wagering provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 3.02: on the effective date, CTA, Metra, Pace and the RTA are consolidated into the Authority. The Authority takes over all of their assets, contracts and ordinances, and existing RTA directors serve as temporary Directors for up to 270 days.","Sec. 2.04: the voting board is 3 Governor appointees, 5 Chicago Mayor appointees, 5 Cook County Board President appointees, and 1 appointee from each collar county. Nonvoting seats include IDOT, the Tollway Chair, labor, business, disability and the Citizen Advisory Board chair. Directors are paid $25,000 a year under Sec. 2.09.","Sec. 3.01: a Transition Committee may be paid up to $150,000 per Director per year. Deadlines run from the effective date: board seated within 1 year, Integration Management Office within 2 years, permanent CEO and final transition plan within 3 years, and full transfer of functions within 4 years.","Sec. 3.03: all employees transfer with their existing collective bargaining agreements and pension obligations. Implementation disputes go to 21-day mediation and then binding three-member arbitration.","Sec. 4.02(12): the Authority may set fines by ordinance of up to $5,000 per offense, effective 10 days after newspaper publication."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a transit governance and consolidation bill, and the portion of text provided does not touch wagering, gaming taxes or gaming licensing. The text supplied is truncated (it ends in Sec. 4.04), so any funding provisions later in the bill could not be reviewed.","watch":"Since the excerpt is truncated, review the full text and any amendments to see whether new transit funding sources, such as gaming or sports-wagering tax revenue or diverted wagering receipts, are added to close the region's transit funding gap.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":813134,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:08.286Z"},"IL-HB2513":{"summary":"HB2513 amends the Illinois Lottery Law to create a dedicated Lyme Disease Innovation instant scratch-off game (new 20 ILCS 1605/21.16.5), with net revenue deposited into the existing Lyme Disease Awareness Fund. It also amends 20 ILCS 805/805-72 to state that the DNR's Lyme Disease Innovation Program is funded partly by that game's revenue. It makes conforming edits to the lottery proceeds-distribution (Sec. 9.3), State Lottery Fund (Sec. 20), and Joint Special Instant Scratch-off (Sec. 21.4) provisions.","mechanics":["New Sec. 21.16.5(a): the game commences January 1, 2026, or as soon as reasonably practical, and must be discontinued December 31, 2035. It is to be offered 'upon the discontinuation of the first game that is discontinued' within the Sec. 21.4 joint game, and the Lottery must consult DNR on design and promotion.","Sec. 21.16.5(c) defines 'net revenue' as ticket sales less prizes and the Department's actual administrative expenses 'solely related' to this game. This is narrower than the Sec. 21.4(d) definition, which also deducts retailer payments and estimated administrative expenses.","Sec. 21.16.5(d) bars the Department from unreasonably diminishing marketing of other instant scratch-off games while Lyme tickets are sold, and Sec. 21.16.5(e) gives the Department discretionary rulemaking authority.","Sec. 21.4(a) adds Sec. 21.16.5 to the list of special causes benefiting from the joint special instant game. That game is capped at 10 causes under Sec. 21.4(c) and sunsets January 1, 2027.","The Sec. 9.3(a) and Sec. 20(n) amendments carve Lyme game proceeds out of the general distribution waterfall that otherwise sends net lottery proceeds to the Common School Fund. Existing references to Sports Wagering Act Sec. 25-70 are left unchanged.","Sec. 805-72(c) funds the Program and its contracted not-for-profit operator and the U of I Prairie Research Institute tick surveillance work from the Fund. All of this remains subject to appropriation under Sec. 805-72(e)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is the drafting tension: the bill both adds the Lyme cause to the Sec. 21.4 joint scratch-off game and creates a standalone Lyme game. The standalone game nominally starts January 1, 2026 but is conditioned on another joint-game cause being discontinued. Its net revenue definition also differs from the joint game's.","watch":"This is a state-run lottery charitable-game bill with no effect on sports wagering licensing, taxes, or operations, so the only point to monitor is whether amendments to Secs. 9.3 or 20 begin touching the Sports Wagering Act Sec. 25-70 lottery sports wagering carve-outs.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":16279,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:08.968Z"},"IL-HB2342":{"summary":"HB2342 creates the Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act, making paid-entry online fantasy contests lawful in Illinois only when offered by an operator licensed by the Illinois Gaming Board. It imposes a monthly tax of 10% to 15% on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts, sets license fees, and adds age, geolocation, AML, integrity and self-exclusion requirements. It also amends the Sports Wagering Act and Criminal Code so fantasy contests are not sports wagering or gambling, but the bill was re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2025-03-21 and appears stalled.","mechanics":["Definition (Sec. 5): a fantasy contest is an online skill contest with an entry fee and prizes disclosed in advance, built from rosters of real athletes or regulated esports players and scored on individual statistics. No winning outcome may rest entirely on a single team's score or spread or solely on one athlete's single performance in one event. The definition expressly covers single-participant contests against an operator-set target score.","Licensing (Secs. 15, 25): a Board license is required, with fingerprint background checks of owners, officers and directors and disclosure of 10%+ owners. Incumbents pay the lesser of 8% of prior-year AGFCR or $1,000,000. New entrants pay $10,000, plus $990,000 once they reach 10,000 active paying Illinois participants. Licenses run 4 years, and renewal costs 1% of AGFCR for the prior 4 years.","Transition: operators already active under a bona fide attorney opinion letter may keep operating if they apply within 90 days after rules are adopted (Sec. 25(b)). Rules are due within 180 days, with emergency rulemaking authorized (Sec. 10(b), Sec. 100).","Tax (Sec. 40): beginning July 1, 2025, a privilege tax of at least 10% and no more than 15% of AGFCR, with the rate set by Board rule. It is calculated on the accrual method, filed and paid monthly, and deposited in the Common School Fund. Fees are split 85% to the State Gaming Fund and 15% to DHS compulsive gambling programs.","Conduct rules (Secs. 10, 20, 45): participants must be 21+ and physically in Illinois, verified by geolocation. Operators must meet BSA/AML standards, verify identity, detect fraud and collusion, and report integrity breaches and match fixing. Problem-gambling messaging is required on platforms and all advertising, and self-excluded players' winnings must be forfeited to problem-gaming programs.","Limits on the Board (Sec. 20(e)-(f)): the Board may not regulate individual contest rules, statistical makeup, or operator platforms, and rule changes alone cannot force license surrender. Operators must also make biennial supplier diversity reports (Sec. 50) and undergo independent CPA audits and annual lab testing (Sec. 30)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","legalize","integrity","prop","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"KY HB 904 likewise places fantasy contest operators under a state gaming regulator (the Kentucky Horse Racing and Gaming Corporation) with geolocation and consumer-protection procedures. Its summary does not indicate a gross-receipts tax comparable to HB2342's 10-15% band or the up-to-$1,000,000 incumbent license fee. FL H0189 addresses fantasy sports contests only as one piece of a broad gambling overhaul, whereas HB2342 is a standalone fantasy licensing and tax statute. HB2342 expressly authorizes player-versus-house target-score contests, which runs opposite to LA SB354's push to prohibit proposition and micro-bets in sports wagering.","watch":"Watch the rate the Board would set within the 10-15% band, the July 1, 2025 tax start date, and the 8%/$1,000,000 incumbent and $990,000 threshold-triggered license fees, along with whether the express inclusion of target-score (pick'em) contests survives any revival given Illinois's sports-wagering licensees' interest in excluding prop-like fantasy products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":43179,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:15.964Z"},"IL-HB2580":{"summary":"HB2580 amends the Illinois Substance Use Disorder Act (20 ILCS 301) to fold gambling disorders more fully into DHS's statutory mission, updating the definition of gambling disorder and adding definitions of \"gambling\" (expressly including sports betting and video gaming) and \"gaming.\" It expands the Section 5-20 gambling disorder program to cover stigma awareness and suicide prevention, and makes DHS responsible for selecting the help statement that licensed gambling establishments must post and master sports wagering licensees must display on their portals, websites, and apps. It also authorizes, but does not require, DHS to advise officials, support prevention projects, and award grants for local gambling prevention and treatment programs.","mechanics":["Sec. 1-10 redefines \"gambling disorder\" as persistent and recurrent problematic gambling behavior leading to clinically significant impairment or distress, replacing \"recurring maladaptive gambling behavior.\" It defines \"gambling\" as risking money or value in games of chance, including video gaming and sports betting.","Sec. 5-20(a) replaces the \"800\" number with a toll-free hotline and website. It adds public awareness of stigma and the impact on families and communities, and adds a new item (5) requiring screening, crisis intervention, and other means to decrease gambling-related suicide attempts, subject to specific appropriation.","Sec. 5-20(c) has DHS select the gambling-help statement that each licensed gambling establishment owner must post and each master sports wagering licensee must include on its portal, website, or mobile app. It adds cross-references to Sports Wagering Act Sec. 25.95 and the Video Gaming Act for DHS-supplied signs.","New Sec. 5-20(d) lets DHS publish prevention literature and curricula, advise State and local officials on prevalence and \"the relationship between gaming and gambling disorders,\" and promote elements such as peer recovery specialists, reentry outreach, judicial training, and targeted mass media on gambling stigma.","New Sec. 5-20(e) authorizes grants to local health departments, correctional institutions, hospitals, universities, and community and faith-based groups for prevention and treatment programs. Funding comes from appropriations plus private, federal, and other sources.","Sec. 15-5 extends DHS treatment licensure to gambling disorder treatment. Other conforming edits add gambling disorders to DHS functions, Advisory Council duties, and Council membership (Sec. 10-15(s) treatment program representatives). No effective date is specified."],"issue_tags":["integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the authorization-and-tax bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308, HB2580 creates no new operator licensing, tax, or wagering rules; it is a public-health and agency-mandate bill. It is closest in spirit to the consumer-protection measures in CT HB05229 (advertising and account withdrawal limits) and IN HB1078 (voluntary exclusion), but it reaches operators only through the DHS-selected help statement on master sports wagering licensee platforms. Its explicit suicide-prevention mandate and broad grant authority are unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch whether DHS uses its new authority under Sec. 5-20(c) to change the content or format of the mandatory problem-gambling statement on master sports wagering licensees' websites and apps, and whether that later becomes a hook for operator-funded assessments, since the bill currently relies only on appropriations and outside grants.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":86557,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:18.042Z"},"IL-HB2761":{"summary":"HB2761 adds Section 25-47 to the Illinois Sports Wagering Act so that, where a public entity that owns a sports facility does not apply for a master sports wagering license, a professional sports team that plays most of its home contests there and has the owner's written authorization may apply to the Illinois Gaming Board instead. The team is deemed a sports facility, must operate through a designee under Section 25-40(a), and pays a $1,000,000 initial fee that is later adjusted to 5% of its first-year handle. The bill was re-referred to the House Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2025-03-21, which effectively stalls it.","mechanics":["Eligibility (Sec. 25-47(a)): the public entity owning the sports facility must not have applied for a master sports wagering license. The team must play the majority of its home contests at that facility and hold written authorization from the public entity.","The team is deemed a 'sports facility' for purposes of the Act. It must operate through a designee as defined in Sec. 25-40(a), and the designee is treated as the facility owner for all purposes under the Act and its rules.","Fee (Sec. 25-47(b)): the initial license fee is $1,000,000. Twelve months after sportsbook operations begin, the fee is adjusted based on 5% of handle from the first 12 months of operations.","The license term is 4 years.","Sec. 25-47(c) authorizes the Illinois Gaming Board to adopt implementing rules.","The bill contains no separate effective date or tax-rate change."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills are either broad authorization-and-tax frameworks for new markets (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569) or consumer-protection and integrity measures (CT HB05229, LA SB354). IL HB2761 instead makes a narrow tweak to an existing licensing category, letting a tenant team stand in for a publicly owned venue. The closest analogue is MA H481, which adjusts a specific license class (category 2 retail licenses), though for a different purpose. The handle-based fee true-up (5% of first-year handle) is unusual among the listed bills, which generally use flat fees or revenue-based taxes.","watch":"Operators should watch the 5% of first-year handle fee true-up, because handle-based charges can far exceed the $1,000,000 base and may shape the economics of designee and market-access deals with team licensees, and should also watch whether the measure revives from the Rules Committee or reappears as an amendment in a larger gaming package.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2912,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:20.923Z"},"IL-HB2755":{"summary":"HB2755, enacted as Public Act 104-0006, is Illinois's FY2026 omnibus revenue act amending multiple tax statutes. The excerpt provided covers only Article 5: a new 2025 tax amnesty window under the Tax Delinquency Amnesty Act and amendments to Section 9 of the Use Tax Act on retailer returns, vendor discounts, and fund deposits. The excerpt cuts off before any wagering or gaming provisions, so the full chaptered text must be checked for gaming articles.","mechanics":["Tax amnesty (35 ILCS 745/10): a new window from October 1, 2025 through November 15, 2025 covers taxable periods ending after June 30, 2018 and before July 1, 2024. Clause (iv) refers to the window ending November 17, 2025, an internal date inconsistency.","Amnesty terms: on full payment of tax due, the Department of Revenue abates interest and penalties and forgoes civil or criminal prosecution. Taxpayers in pending criminal investigations or tax litigation are excluded, and underpayment voids the amnesty.","Amnesty proceeds that would go to the General Revenue Fund are split half to the Common School Fund and half to the GRF, with 2% going to the Tax Compliance and Administration Fund.","Use Tax Act Sec. 9 (35 ILCS 105/9): for returns due on or after January 1, 2025, the 1.75% retailer collection discount is capped at $1,000 per month in the aggregate across the Use, Retailers' Occupation, Service Occupation and Service Use Tax Acts.","Sec. 9 also restates electronic filing and payment thresholds, quarter-monthly payment rules, and monthly earmarks, including McCormick Place Expansion Project Fund deposits rising to $375M in FY2027 and $450M from FY2036."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like NY A 3009, a revenue omnibus that sets gaming tax rates (Part GG) and mobile sports tax problem-gambling funding (Part OO), HB2755 is a broad budget revenue vehicle rather than a standalone gaming bill, but the excerpt shows no gaming-specific part. It is unlike KS SB303, which expressly imposes a 2% excise tax on the amount wagered, and AK HB145/SB194, which create a mobile sports wagering tax. Its closest analogue is an omnibus tax bill such as MN SF2374, where any wagering impact is buried among general tax changes.","watch":"Obtain and review the full text of P.A. 104-0006 beyond this excerpt, since omnibus Illinois revenue acts often carry Sports Wagering Act or gaming tax changes in later articles. Separately confirm whether any operator liabilities collected by the Department of Revenue could qualify for the October to November 2025 amnesty window.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1826514,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:31.096Z"},"IL-HB2880":{"summary":"HB2880 would delete the one-year pre-employment cooling-off period for staff of the Illinois Racing Board and the Illinois Gaming Board. Under current law, neither board may hire someone who, in the prior year, was employed by or paid by a person or entity (or its parent or affiliate) that did business with the board, a racing licensee, or a gambling licensee. The bill keeps the identical one-year bar for board members.","mechanics":["Horse Racing Act, 230 ILCS 5/6(f): removes the references to 'employee' and 'employment' from the one-year look-back, so the bar applies only to Board members within one year before gubernatorial nomination.","Gambling Act, 230 ILCS 10/5(a)(8): strikes the sentence barring Gaming Board employees from having been employed by, or paid by, any entity (or its parent or affiliate) that did business with the Board, a licensee, or a Racing Act licensee in the year before hiring.","Retained in 5(a)(8): no Gaming Board employee may be, or have a spouse, parent or child who is, an official of, or hold a financial interest in or financial relation with, any gambling operator or horse-racing organization in Illinois. Violations remain grounds for termination.","Retained for members: the one-year look-back for Gaming Board members in 5(a)(2), including the public disclosure of prior gaming affiliations within 30 days after nomination. The Racing Board conflict-of-interest, gift and Ethics Act Section 5-45 restrictions in 6(d)-(f) also remain.","The Gaming Board administers the Sports Wagering Act (5(b)(13.2)), so the change reaches staff who regulate sports wagering licensees.","No effective-date provision. The bill was re-referred to the House Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2025-03-21, which effectively stalls it."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analog is FL S1580, which keeps Florida's employment-ineligibility rules for the Gaming Control Commission but lets an ineligible person seek a waiver based on expertise. Illinois HB2880 instead repeals the employee look-back outright, with no case-by-case review. CO SB 163 also concerns regulator structure, but it deals with delegating licensing duties from the commission to the division rather than staff ethics. MD HB 1612 moves the other way on integrity, targeting campaign contributions and independent evaluation of sports wagering.","watch":"If revived, the bill would let the Gaming Board hire recently departed operator, supplier or vendor personnel into roles overseeing sports wagering, but the surviving 'financial interest or financial relation' bar in 230 ILCS 10/5(a)(8) could still disqualify hires with deferred compensation, equity or severance ties to a licensee.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":34593,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:33.416Z"},"IL-HB2949":{"summary":"HB2949 is Illinois's Fiscal Year 2027 Budget Implementation Act, signed as Public Act 104-0466, which makes statutory changes needed to carry out the FY27 budget. The portion of the enrolled text provided amends only the State Employees Group Insurance Act of 1971 and contains no sports-betting or gaming provisions. The excerpt is truncated partway through Section 10(k), so later Articles were not reviewed.","mechanics":["Section 1-1 sets the short title as the Fiscal Year 2027 Budget Implementation Act, and Section 1-5 states its purpose is to implement the FY27 State budget.","Section 5-2 amends Sections 6.5, 6.10, 10, 11 and 13.1 of the State Employees Group Insurance Act (5 ILCS 375).","From July 1, 2026, Sec. 6.10 lets CMS set community college employee and employer contributions to the Community College Health Insurance Security Fund. Each year's rate may not exceed 105% of the prior year's percentage, replacing the 0.1-percentage-point annual cap that applied July 1, 2024 through June 30, 2026.","CMS may hold accounts outside the State treasury to receive transfers from three funds: the Teacher Health Insurance Security Fund, the Community College Health Insurance Security Fund and the Local Government Health Insurance Reserve Fund. Those moneys may be used only for claims payments by administrative service organizations, not for their administrative fees.","Existing TRS retiree premium rules are carried forward, including the cap of 105% of the prior year's premium for FY2008 onward and the 75%/50% fund subsidy split for managed care versus major medical coverage."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is an omnibus budget implementation vehicle. The provided text deals only with public-employee and retiree health insurance financing, with no wagering tax, licensing or integrity provisions.","watch":"Because Illinois has used budget and revenue omnibus bills to change wagering taxes in recent years, operators' counsel should check the full enrolled text of PA 104-0466 beyond this truncated excerpt for any Article amending the Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45) or the Illinois Gambling Act, or redirecting wagering fund transfers.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":1318718,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:35.527Z"},"IL-HB2878":{"summary":"HB2878 amends Sections 25-10 and 25-45 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act to restructure the standalone online master sports wagering license. Per the synopsis, it sets a $250,000 application fee and a $15,000,000 license fee, and removes the statutory cap of 3 online-only licenses and the competitive selection process. It also strips the eligibility, disclosure and diversity-outreach provisions, which would effectively open Illinois to any qualifying online operator. It takes effect immediately upon becoming law.","mechanics":["Sec. 25-45(b): nonrefundable application fee of $250,000 and initial license fee of $15,000,000, compared with the $20,000,000 fee tied to the 3-license competitive process in current subsection (a). The license is valid for 4 years and renewable for $1,000,000 under subsection (c).","Per the synopsis, it removes the provision directing the Board to issue 3 master sports wagering licenses to online sports wagering operators and the public notice and solicitation requirements, so the number of online-only licenses would no longer be capped by statute.","Sec. 25-45(d): states that a master sports wagering licensee may conduct sports wagering over the Internet or through a mobile application. Per the synopsis, the enumerated eligibility criteria (age 21, no felony or Article 28 gambling convictions, no dishonesty or moral turpitude crimes, demonstrated skill, Board standards) are removed.","Subsections (e) through (l) are blanked. These cover public opening of applications, the 90-day winning-bidder selection, the post-selection compliance check, the no-hearing-right clause, application plans as license conditions and the duty to disclose material changes, and DCEO/Board outreach to minority-, women-, veteran- and disability-owned businesses.","Sec. 25-10: a conforming change to definitions, most likely tied to the 'winning bidder' and competitive-selection concept. The struck text is not visible in the version provided.","Section 99: effective immediately upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145 and SB194 and HI HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569, which build mobile sports wagering licensing regimes from scratch, HB2878 retools an existing, mature market by converting a scarce, competitively bid online-only license into what reads as an open-entry license at a fixed fee. MA H481 also adjusts an existing license category, but it targets retail category 2 licenses rather than online access. Unusually, the bill lowers rather than raises the fee and drops a statutory license cap and diversity-outreach mandates, which runs against the general trend of tightening entry.","watch":"Operators should watch whether removing the 3-license cap and the competitive process lets new online-only entrants in for $15 million, eroding the market position of tethered casino and track licensees. They should also check whether the deleted statutory eligibility and disclosure duties are replaced by Board rulemaking under remaining Sec. 25-45(d).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":15955,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:35.558Z"},"IL-HB3080":{"summary":"HB3080 would create a standalone Illinois Internet Gaming Act legalizing online casino games, including slots, table games, poker, live-dealer games and tournaments, under Illinois Gaming Board regulation. Online licenses would be available only to existing casino owners licensees and racetrack organization licensees, who may partner with up to three Internet management service providers. Online gaming revenue would be taxed at 25% of adjusted gross gaming revenue, and sports wagering and skill-based fantasy contests are expressly excluded. The bill was re-referred to the House Rules Committee on 2025-03-21 under Rule 19(a), so it is effectively stalled for now.","mechanics":["Licensing (Sec. 35): an Internet gaming license is issued on request to owners and organization licensees for a $250,000 fee ($100,000 renewal) and runs with the underlying license. The Internet management service provider license costs $100,000, or $50,000 for existing licensees, with a $50,000 four-year renewal. The supplier license costs $75,000 or $50,000, and the occupational license costs $150 or $100.","Fast track for existing operators (Sec. 35(f)-(g)): temporary licenses must be issued within 30 days to holders of Sports Wagering Act licenses or equivalent out-of-state iGaming licenses. Background checks are waived for anyone previously investigated under the Sports Wagering Act.","Workforce condition (Sec. 35(i)): the Board may not license an owners or organization licensee that has cut its workforce by 25% or more since February 28, 2020. The same 25% test applies at each renewal.","Structure (Sec. 20): each licensee may offer up to 3 branded skins. Primary servers may be anywhere in the U.S. for one year, after which they must be located in Illinois.","Tax (Sec. 45): a 25% privilege tax on adjusted gross gaming revenue, which excludes free play and promotional credits. It is the exclusive tax, with local taxes preempted. Negative revenue may be carried forward up to 12 months, and proceeds go to the State Gaming Fund for distribution under Sec. 13 of the Illinois Gambling Act.","Player protection and operations: minimum age is 21. The Board must keep a statewide self-exclusion database exempt from FOIA. Platforms must offer daily, weekly and monthly deposit and spend limits (Sec. 40). Accounts may be shared with sports wagering accounts, and crypto deposits are allowed (Sec. 30). Out-of-state and multi-state compacts such as poker are permitted (Sec. 60). Advertising samples must be kept for 2 years, but no preapproval is required (Sec. 25(h)). Emergency rules are due within 90 days, and the Act takes effect immediately."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, the closest analogues are MA H332 and MA H4431, which likewise propose regulating internet gaming. Most other listed bills concern sports wagering only, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, whereas HB3080 expressly carves out sports wagering and builds on Illinois' existing Sports Wagering Act licensees. Unlike CT HB05229, which would restrict gaming advertising and AI-based bet targeting, HB3080 takes a light-touch approach: it bars Board preapproval of ads and allows advertising from the date of temporary licensure. It also contains an unusual labor-linked eligibility bar, the 25% workforce-reduction test, and an in-state server mandate after one year.","watch":"Operators should watch the Sec. 35(i) workforce-reduction bar, which could disqualify casino or racetrack partners, along with the 3-skin cap, the one-year in-state server requirement, and whether the 25% rate and the sports-wagering-licensee fast track survive any revival of the bill from Rules Committee.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":37061,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:49.364Z"},"IL-HB3276":{"summary":"HB3276 adds Section 25-70a to the Illinois Sports Wagering Act, creating a Department of the Lottery sports wagering pilot program that runs through sports lottery terminals placed at any lottery retailer, including bars, video gaming locations and retail food establishments. The definition of terminal expressly includes Internet, mobile application and other digital wagering. The program sits outside Gaming Board jurisdiction, falls under the Lottery's private management agreement, and splits gross gaming revenue among the State, the central system provider, terminal owners/operators and retailers. It replaces references to the existing Section 25-70 lottery program throughout the Lottery Law and amends Board-powers Section 25-15 to carve out the new program.","mechanics":["Revenue split under 25-70a(g): gross gaming revenue (gross dollars played minus prizes) goes 30% to the State, of which the local municipality receives 5%; 20% to the central system provider; the remainder is split evenly between the terminal owner/operator and the lottery retailer. Subsection (j) separately directs net proceeds into the State Lottery Fund daily or weekly, with the remainder after provider and agent payments transferred on the 15th of each month to the Capital Projects Fund.","Placement and access: terminals may go in any lottery retail location (25-70a(d)). Terminals cannot dispense cash, only receipt tickets (25-70a(e)). The 'sports lottery terminal' definition covers Internet, mobile and digital platforms.","Age limit: retailers may not permit anyone under 21 to use a terminal or sports wagering application; a knowing violation is a business offense with a fine of up to $5,000 (25-70a(f)).","Testing: terminals must be tested and approved under Department rules by accredited independent labs and be ready for play within 90 days of the effective date. Lottery games are folded into the private management agreement and competitive bidding under Lottery Law Section 9.1.","Department authority: exclusive jurisdiction over the program, with implementation required by June 30, 2025. Rulemaking covers penalties, advertising standards, minority business ownership and hardware apportionment. Emergency rulemaking is authorized under new 5 ILCS 100/5-45.65, which sunsets one year after enactment.","Who may own terminals: the Department facilitates purchase or lease, and video gaming terminal operators, Illinois Gambling Act owners licensees and lottery retailers may all own or lease terminals (25-70a(k)). Wager types and payouts are set by Department rule. The bill is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","advertising","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, authorize licensed private mobile operators taxed on adjusted gross revenue under a gaming regulator. HB3276 instead builds a lottery-run retail-terminal and digital channel with a fixed revenue split to retailers, terminal operators and a central provider, outside the Gaming Board. It most resembles IN HB1078 in expanding lottery distribution, but goes much further into sports wagering. Unlike CT HB05229 or LA SB354, it imposes no new consumer-protection or bet-type limits on existing operators.","watch":"Watch whether the bill revives in Rules Committee, because it would create a lottery-controlled, Board-exempt sports wagering channel with mobile and digital reach, placed in thousands of bars and video gaming locations. That channel would compete directly with master sports wagering licensees while escaping the Act's licensing, integrity and tax regime.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":22338,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:51.438Z"},"IL-HB3159":{"summary":"HB3159 amends Section 25-90 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45/25-90) to add a new subsection (f). Beginning July 1, 2026, it imposes an additional 2% tax on adjusted gross receipts from all sports wagers placed within Illinois, on top of the existing privilege taxes. Subject to appropriation, the revenue would go from the Sports Wagering Fund to public, full-time career local government fire departments, fire districts and EMS ambulance departments.","mechanics":["New Sec. 25-90(f): 2% of adjusted gross receipts from sports wagers placed within the State, effective July 1, 2026, imposed 'for the privilege of holding a license to operate sports wagering.'","Expressly stacks 'in addition to' the taxes under subsections (a), (d) and (d-5), which as shown in the bill are the tiered privilege taxes of 20%, 25%, 30%, 35% and 40% on annual AGR across the $30M, $50M, $100M and $200M bands, for both online and retail wagering.","The new subsection does not reference the existing (a-5) 2% surcharge on wagers placed in a home rule county over 3,000,000 (Cook County), so Cook County wagers would appear to bear both surcharges, for a combined 4% on top of the tiered rate.","Proceeds are paid from the Sports Wagering Fund subject to General Assembly appropriation, and only to public, full-time career fire and EMS departments, which by its terms excludes volunteer departments.","Uses the term 'adjusted gross receipts' rather than the Act's 'adjusted gross sports wagering receipts,' mirroring the drafting of (a-5).","Status: introduced, then re-referred to House Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2025-03-21, which effectively stalls it unless revived."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308, which would create new sports wagering markets with their own tax structures, HB3159 adds a dedicated surcharge to an existing, already high-rate progressive regime. Its closest analogue is KS SB303, which would impose a 2% excise tax; however, the Kansas tax is levied on the amount wagered (handle), while the Illinois 2% is levied on adjusted gross receipts. What is distinctive about HB3159 is the earmark of statewide sports wagering tax revenue for career fire and EMS departments, layered over the existing Cook County criminal-justice surcharge.","watch":"Watch for the effective tax stacking: the bill adds 2% statewide on top of the 20–40% tiers and, for Cook County wagers, the existing (a-5) 2%. If revived from Rules, it could also become a vehicle for further amendments to Section 25-90, so check how it interacts with any later changes to that section.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7659,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:53.161Z"},"IL-HB3274":{"summary":"HB3274 would create a statutory safe harbor in the Prizes and Gifts Act for 'prize and gift kiosks.' These are no-purchase-necessary sweepstakes-style devices that promote a bona fide product and can award cash prizes, and they would be lawful if they meet specified technical standards. Machines outside those standards are folded into the Criminal Code's definition of 'gambling device,' and any offense involving them becomes a Class 4 felony. The bill also bars the Illinois Gaming Board from disciplining or delaying video gaming licensees that operate compliant devices, and it prohibits municipalities from restricting any activity exempted under 720 ILCS 5/28-1(b).","mechanics":["Prizes and Gifts Act Sec. 10 defines 'prize and gift kiosk' and imposes standards. Each kiosk needs a revenue-based self-contained fill system that shuts off at the end of a predetermined cycle; time- or spin-based systems are excluded. It also needs a 'route boost plus' monitoring system that records cash in, winnings, entries, malfunctions and disconnections, and remote activations and disabling, and that cannot alter play or read patron personal or financial data. The kiosk may not connect to the Internet or a network except through a redemption vault. Free play is limited to a mail-in code.","New Sec. 33 makes it unlawful to operate a kiosk that fails these standards, sells anything other than a 'bona fide product' (excluding discount coupons, Internet access and phone cards), or offers free-play options beyond the mail-in method. New Sec. 45 requires registration with the Department of Revenue and an annual fee set by DOR. Misfiling a device as a kiosk is treated as filing a false report, with seizure available, and DOR and the Liquor Control Commission (or a public or private task force) have jurisdiction.","Criminal Code 28-2(a)(iii) adds to the 'gambling device' definition vending or electronic machines that award and record credits or are Internet- or network-connected, unless they meet the kiosk fill and monitoring standards. 28-1(c)(2) makes any gambling offense involving such a device a Class 4 felony, and 28-1(b)(13) removes the no-purchase sweepstakes exemption for these devices.","Video Gaming Act Sec. 35 (230 ILCS 40/35) says applicants and licensees do not violate the Act or IGB rules, specifically 11 Ill. Adm. Code 1800.420, and face no discipline, delay or denial for operating devices that are not gambling under 28-1(b) or 28-2(a)(iii).","New 720 ILCS 5/28-1.2 bars any municipality from imposing restrictions or prohibitions on activities lawful under 28-1(b). That subsection covers more than kiosks; it also includes video gaming, casino gaming, pari-mutuel betting and sports wagering under the Sports Wagering Act.","The bill is effective immediately on becoming law. It was re-referred to House Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2025-03-21 and is effectively stalled."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills that deal with unregulated or illegal gaming push toward enforcement. MD HB1226 (Illegal Online Gambling Enforcement Act), LA HB883 (illegal online gambling enforcement) and FL S1580 (illegal gaming penalties) all crack down on gray-market products. HB3274 instead creates a registration-based legal channel for land-based sweepstakes kiosks while criminalizing noncompliant machines. None of the listed bills comparably shields existing gaming licensees from regulator discipline, and none preempts municipal regulation of all exempted gambling activities. Unlike the AK, GA and HI sports wagering authorization bills, it adds no sports-betting framework or tax.","watch":"Watch the breadly worded municipal preemption in new 28-1.2: it would bar local restrictions on all activity exempt under 28-1(b), including sports wagering, while also legitimizing cash-prize sweepstakes kiosks inside video gaming locations outside the Gaming Board's licensing and tax regime.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":30726,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:58.383Z"},"IL-HB3778":{"summary":"HB3778 creates the Metropolitan Mobility Authority Act, consolidating the Chicago Transit Authority, Metra (Commuter Rail Division), Pace (Suburban Bus Division) and the RTA into a single Metropolitan Mobility Authority covering Cook, DuPage, Kane, Lake, McHenry and Will counties, abolishing the Service Boards and repealing the Metropolitan Transit Authority Act and RTA Act. It also creates the Equitable Transit-Supportive Development Act and Fund, an IDOT Office of Public Transportation Support, and adds the Authority Chair as a nonvoting member of the Tollway board. Nothing in the supplied text addresses sports betting, wagering or gaming.","mechanics":["Sec. 3.02: on the effective date, CTA, Metra, Pace and RTA are consolidated into the Authority, which succeeds to all their assets, contracts, ordinances and liabilities.","Sec. 2.04: the Board has 3 Governor appointees, 5 Chicago Mayor appointees, 5 Cook County Board President appointees and 1 each from the DuPage, Kane, Lake, McHenry and Will county board chairs, plus nonvoting members including the IDOT Secretary and the Tollway Chair.","Secs. 2.09 and 3.01: Directors are paid $25,000 a year, and Transition Committee members may receive up to $150,000 more per year on an hourly basis.","Sec. 3.01(c) sets the transition timetable: Board seated within 1 year, permanent CEO within 3 years, all functions transferred within 4 years.","Sec. 4.02(12) lets the Authority set ordinance fines of up to $5,000 per offense, effective 10 days after newspaper publication.","Sec. 3.03: all employees, collective bargaining agreements and pension obligations transfer to the Authority, with impasse disputes going to mediation and then binding arbitration."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a transit governance consolidation bill with no gaming content in the supplied text. Gaming relevance could arise only if the conforming amendments in the unprovided portion touch gaming revenue streams or advertising on transit property, and nothing in the text shown indicates that.","watch":"Check whether the conforming amendments or later funding amendments pledge gaming or sports-wagering tax revenue to the new Authority, or whether Authority ordinances under Sec. 4.02(12) restrict gambling advertising on transit property.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":941053,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:05.391Z"},"IL-HB4549":{"summary":"HB4549 amends Section 6z-27 of the Illinois State Finance Act (30 ILCS 105/6z-27) to reset the annual sweep into the Audit Expense Fund. It directs the Comptroller and Treasurer to move listed sums from many state funds within 30 days after July 1, 2026 (replacing the July 1, 2025 date) to pay for Auditor General audits. Several gaming-related funds are on the list, but the bill does not touch wagering regulation, taxes or operator obligations.","mechanics":["Transfers are due within 30 days after July 1, 2026, or as soon as practical; the date change from 2025 is the only amendment visibly marked in the text.","Gaming-related transfers include the Sports Wagering Fund ($11,518), the State Gaming Fund ($176,882 and a second line of $1,997), the State Lottery Fund ($120,030 and $311,489), the Horse Racing Fund ($205,483) and the Illinois Gaming Law Enforcement Fund ($1,285).","Any shortfall in a source fund is backfilled from the General Revenue Fund.","Amounts transferred beyond actual audit costs are returned to the source fund on or after September 30 at the Auditor General's direction; federal and State trust funds are excluded unless their trustees direct a transfer.","The Act takes effect upon becoming law (Section 99). It was re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, so it is effectively stalled."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is Illinois's routine annual audit-cost transfer, and its gaming content is limited to line items charging audit costs against the Sports Wagering, State Gaming, Lottery and Horse Racing funds. It is unusual only in that several funds appear twice with different amounts, such as the Agricultural Premium Fund and the State Gaming Fund, and the General Revenue Fund and State Pensions Fund appear twice at identical amounts ($17,653,153 and $500,000).","watch":"Nothing here imposes new costs on operators, so the main thing to watch is whether the Sports Wagering Fund ($11,518) and State Gaming Fund draws resurface in the FY2027 budget package, likely through a budget implementation bill, rather than in this stalled stand-alone bill.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":23693,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:27.688Z"},"IL-HB4171":{"summary":"HB4171 adds Section 25-115 to the Illinois Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45/25-115) to make the regulation and licensing of sports wagering, including fees, surcharges or other charges for the privilege of conducting or participating in it, exclusive state functions. It bars home rule units from regulating, licensing or taxing sports wagering or receipts from it. The bill takes effect on becoming law, but it was re-referred to the House Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-04-17, which effectively parks it.","mechanics":["Sec. 25-115(a): the regulation and licensing of sports wagering, including 'fees, surcharges, or other costs for the privilege of conducting or participating in sports wagering,' are declared exclusive powers and functions of the State. Home rule units may not regulate or license sports wagering.","Subsection (a) is framed as a denial of home rule power under Ill. Const. Art. VII, Sec. 6(h), the exclusive-state-exercise clause.","Sec. 25-115(b): home rule units may not impose or collect a tax on sports wagering or on receipts generated from sports wagering.","Subsection (b) is framed as a denial under Art. VII, Sec. 6(g), which requires a three-fifths vote of each house to limit home rule taxing power. That is a higher passage threshold than a simple majority.","The preemption covers both the operator side ('conducting') and the bettor side ('participating'), so per-wager surcharges on patrons are captured as well as taxes on operator receipts.","Section 99 makes the Act effective immediately. The text contains no grandfathering, refund or transition language for any existing local levies."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals with preempting local regulation or taxation of sports wagering. Most of them either authorize and tax wagering at the state level (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) or add state-level levies and withholding: KS SB303 imposes a 2% excise on the amount wagered, and IA SF 605 addresses income tax withholding on winnings. HB4171 runs the other way, protecting operators from an additional local layer of tax and licensing rather than setting state rates. It is notable as a stand-alone, two-subsection preemption bill.","watch":"Watch whether the bill moves out of Rules, given the Rule 19(a) re-referral and the three-fifths vote needed for the Sec. 6(g) tax limit. Also watch whether 'a tax on receipts generated from sports wagering' would be read to reach generally applicable local amusement or gross-receipts taxes applied to wagering, and note that the bill has no language addressing existing local levies already in force.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1548,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:30.610Z"},"IL-HB3733":{"summary":"HB3733 targets so-called sweepstakes or 'no-chance' gaming machines in Illinois. It adds a new category to the Criminal Code's definition of 'gambling device' covering any vending or electronic machine that offers a chance-based prize entry tied to a purchase or to a free coupon or credit. It makes any offense involving such a device a Class 4 felony and removes the Video Gaming Act language that let unlicensed game devices operate if their use was not 'gambling.' The bill was re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27 and is effectively stalled.","mechanics":["New 720 ILCS 5/28-2(a)(iii): a 'gambling device' includes any vending or other electronic machine or device, including credit-awarding machines with a circuit, meter or switch that records credit removal. This applies if the machine offers entry into a contest, sweepstakes or other chance-based selection for a prize, and the offer is incidental to (A) a purchase or (B) the purchase or free receipt of a coupon, voucher or credit redeemable at the machine or elsewhere.","28-1(b)(13) no-purchase-necessary exemption is narrowed: it no longer protects participation accomplished through a 28-2(a)(iii) device.","New 28-1(c)(2): any gambling offense involving a 28-2(a)(iii) device is a Class 4 felony, overriding the usual Class A misdemeanor for a first offense.","The vending-machine exclusion in 28-2(a)(2) becomes subject to 'except as otherwise provided,' and new 28-2(d) states an intent to reach any mechanism using 'subterfuge or pretense' to avoid gambling-device status.","230 ILCS 40/35 (Video Gaming Act) strikes the sentence allowing use of an unlicensed game device in an activity that is not gambling under 28-1(b). Existing Class 4 felony, seizure and destruction provisions for unlicensed credit-awarding chance devices remain.","Effective immediately upon becoming law. The existing sports wagering exemption in 28-1(b)(15) is unchanged."],"issue_tags":["sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue is CA AB 831, which also attacks sweepstakes run on electronic video monitors, but through California's unfair-practices sweepstakes statute rather than by redefining criminal gambling devices with felony penalties. FL S1580 and FL H0189 address illegal gaming machines and criminal penalties more broadly as part of commission and gaming overhauls, while HB3733 is a narrow, device-focused fix. The other listed bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308) concern authorizing and taxing sports wagering, which this bill does not touch.","watch":"The phrase 'vending or other electronic machine or device' combined with the 28-2(d) anti-subterfuge intent is broad enough that a revived version could be argued to reach purchase-linked promotional sweepstakes or online sweepstakes-casino play on personal devices. Operators should watch for amendments clarifying its scope, even though the bill is currently parked in Rules.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21190,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:31.614Z"},"IL-HB3473":{"summary":"HB3473 amends the Illinois Video Gaming Act and the Criminal Code of 2012 to crack down on unlicensed 'skill' and sweepstakes-style machines. It expands the definition of 'gambling device' to cover electronic and internet-connected credit-awarding machines, makes offenses involving them a Class 4 felony, and narrows the no-purchase-necessary exemption so it does not protect those devices. It also creates a Gaming Disparity Task Force, funded by a fee on large terminal operators, to study minority participation in video gaming, direct set-aside payments to a minority-owned terminal operator, and design a sunset process tied to opening video gaming in Chicago.","mechanics":["Criminal Code 28-2(a)(iii) adds to 'gambling device' any vending or other electronic machine that awards credits and has a circuit, meter, or switch that removes and records credits, or that connects to the Internet or a networked system to play or to retrieve data. Carve-outs apply for a 'redemption vault' and for certain non-revenue-based, self-terminating fill systems; the drafting of these carve-outs is garbled.","Section 28-1(b)(13), the no-purchase games of skill or chance exemption, no longer applies where play uses an (a)(iii) device. New 28-1(c)(2) makes any gambling offense involving such a device a Class 4 felony regardless of prior convictions.","Video Gaming Act Sec. 35 adds a safe harbor: applicants and licensees are not in violation of the Act or Board rule 11 Ill. Adm. Code 1800.420, and face no discipline, delay, or license denial, for operating a gaming device that complies with and is not gambling under 28-1(b) or 28-2(a)(iii).","New Sec. 76 creates a 5-member, Governor-appointed Gaming Disparity Task Force. It has data-collection and subpoena powers and must deliver an annual disparity and availability study to the General Assembly by February 1.","The Task Force is funded by a fee it sets, collected by the Gaming Board from terminal operators with at least $5,000,000 in net terminal income in any month. Part of the fee is set aside for a minority business enterprise-owned terminal operator, with the share pegged to that minority group's percentage of the Illinois population.","Sec. 76(h) requires a sunset process for 28-1(b)(13)-exempt devices. Minimum conditions include authorizing video gaming in municipalities over 2,000,000 people (Chicago), deciding all eligible applications there, and an independent audit showing a majority of licensed establishments there are served by MBE terminal operators. The Act is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills authorize or regulate sports wagering, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, or online casino, such as MA H332/S235; HB3473 addresses neither. The closest analogues are FL H0189 (gaming machines, criminal penalties, internet gambling) and FL S1580 and LA HB883 (illegal gaming enforcement). HB3473 is unusual in pairing a felony crackdown on unregulated connected machines with a race-conscious funding set-aside for a minority-owned terminal operator. It also ties the sunset of the no-purchase exemption to Chicago video gaming and MBE majority benchmarks.","watch":"The broad internet-connected 'gambling device' definition and Class 4 felony could reach networked kiosks or promotional and sweepstakes products, even though the sports wagering exemption in 28-1(b)(15) is untouched; any revival from the Rules Committee (re-referred under Rule 19(a) on 2026-04-17) would warrant a review of the carve-out drafting and of the fee on operators above $5M in monthly net terminal income.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":27898,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:37.081Z"},"IL-HB4774":{"summary":"HB4774 amends the Illinois Pull Tabs and Jar Games Act to let video gaming licensees (licensed establishments, truck stops and large truck stops) obtain Department of Revenue licenses to run pull tabs and jar games for charitable purposes. It also adds Section 21 to the Video Gaming Act, letting the Illinois Gaming Board license any licensed video gaming establishment, including fraternal and veterans establishments, to sell sports betting squares as a low-stakes, chance-based promotional game. Squares proceeds may be kept as operating revenue taxed like net terminal income, or directed to charity.","mechanics":["Pull tabs: adds 'video gaming licensee' as a third eligible license class under 230 ILCS 20/2(iii) at the existing $500 annual fee. Applicants must file a sworn statement of the charitable purpose, and the entire net proceeds must go to that purpose (Sec. 3(1)(B), 4(1)).","Existing pull tab limits still apply to video gaming licensees: $2 maximum per chance, $500 maximum single prize, 6,000 tickets per game, $5,000 daily prize cap, 18+ play, and play only on the licensed premises (Sec. 4(4)-(6)).","Sports betting squares (new 230 ILCS 40/21) are licensed by the Illinois Gaming Board on application. The establishment must set a price cap of $100 per square, set a mandatory payout percentage, and publicly post its rules.","Squares must be a fixed-grid pool of randomly assigned numbers. Odds, parlays, proposition bets and any online or mobile component are excluded (Sec. 21(c)).","Squares proceeds may be claimed as operational revenue, which is treated like net terminal income under Section 60 (the video gaming tax), or directed to a charity of the establishment's choice.","The Board oversees compliance and adopts implementing rules. The bill sets no license fee or effective date for the squares license."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","legalize","sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills concern regulated sportsbooks: mobile wagering authorization and tax (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) or restrictions on bet types (LA SB354 bans props and micro-bets). HB4774 instead creates a parallel, non-sportsbook channel for sports-linked pools at bars and truck stops, overseen by the Gaming Board but outside the Sports Wagering Act. Its exclusion of props and parlays echoes LA SB354's skepticism of props, but here the aim is to keep squares distinct from sports wagering rather than to restrict licensed books.","watch":"Watch whether this retail sports-pool product, with its $100-per-square cap, operator-set payout and NTI-equivalent tax, becomes a lightly regulated competitor to licensed sportsbooks or blurs the line between promotional games and sports wagering under the Sports Wagering Act, and push for the Board's rules to cap pool sizes and set integrity and age requirements.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":25538,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:45.492Z"},"IL-HB4619":{"summary":"HB4619 amends Section 13(b) of the Illinois Gambling Act (230 ILCS 10/13) to earmark an additional amount equal to 0.50% of the adjusted gross receipts of the Waukegan riverboat (the licensee under Section 7(e-5)(3)) for the Lake County State's Attorney's Office, to fund its Gun Violence Prevention Initiative and community violence intervention partners. The money comes out of privilege tax revenue already deposited in the State Gaming Fund, so it redirects existing state revenue rather than raising the operator's tax rate. The bill was re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, which in practice stalls it.","mechanics":["New earmark in Sec. 13(b): 0.50% of AGR generated by the Section 7(e-5)(3) riverboat (Waukegan) goes to the Lake County State's Attorney Office, remitted monthly and subject to appropriation.","At least 0.40% of AGR must directly fund the Lake County State's Attorney's Gun Violence Prevention Initiative; the remainder may go to community violence intervention partners.","The earmark is in addition to the existing 5% host-community share for this riverboat, split 70% Waukegan, 10% Park City, 15% North Chicago and 5% Lake County.","Privilege tax rates under subsection (a-5) (15% to 50% graduated on non-table games; 15%/20% on table games) are left unchanged, so the licensee's tax liability does not rise.","No effective date appears in the provided text, which is truncated at subsection (d-7); no sports wagering provisions are touched."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like NY A08167/S05640, which would carve up to 2% of mobile sports tax revenue (capped at $23 million) out for youth sports, and NY S3009 Part OO, which sets the problem-gambling allocation from mobile sports tax, HB4619 reallocates existing gaming tax revenue to a dedicated purpose rather than changing the rate. It is far narrower than any of these: it targets a single casino's AGR and sends the money to a single prosecutor's office. Unlike KS SB303, which would impose a new 2% excise on all sports wagers, or NE LB421/LB438, which revise sports wagering tax distribution statewide, HB4619 touches only casino privilege tax and imposes no new operator burden.","watch":"Although the earmark leaves the Waukegan licensee's tax bill unchanged, operators should watch whether it sets a precedent for facility-specific, AGR-based local earmarks that could later be funded by rate increases, or extended to sports wagering privilege tax, if the State Gaming Fund residual shrinks.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":51289,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:48.922Z"},"IL-HB4797":{"summary":"HB4797 would create the Illinois Internet Gaming Act, legalizing online casino games (slots, table games, poker, live-dealer and tournaments, but not sports wagering or fantasy contests) through Illinois casino owners licensees and racetrack organization licensees, each of which may run up to 3 branded skins directly or through up to 3 licensed Internet management service providers. The Illinois Gaming Board would regulate, with fast-track and temporary licensing for entities already licensed under the Sports Wagering Act. A 25% privilege tax on adjusted gross gaming revenue would go to the State Gaming Fund. The bill was re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, so it is effectively stalled for now.","mechanics":["Tax (Sec. 45): 25% privilege tax on adjusted gross gaming revenue, which excludes free play, promotional credits and non-cashable promotions. This is the exclusive tax, with no local or home-rule levies. Negative monthly revenue may be carried forward for up to 12 months. Of the revenue, 95% is distributed under Sec. 13 of the Illinois Gambling Act and 5% goes to municipalities and counties by population.","Licensing fees (Sec. 35): Internet gaming license is $250,000, with $100,000 renewal on the same term as the underlying owners or organization license. Internet management service provider license is $100,000, or $50,000 if already licensed under other State law, for 4 years with $50,000 renewal. Supplier license is $75,000 or $50,000. Occupational license is $150 or $100.","Licensing access (Sec. 35(f)-(h)): licenses issue on fee payment to holders of equivalent Sports Wagering Act or comparable out-of-state iGaming licenses. Temporary licenses must issue within 30 days, last one year and are renewable. Background checks are waived if already done under the Sports Wagering Act. The Board must set an equal launch date for all licensees.","Workforce condition (Sec. 35(i)): no license for an owners or organization licensee that has cut its workforce by 25% or more since Feb. 28, 2020. No renewal after a 25% cut since the last issuance or renewal.","Platform and patron rules (Secs. 20, 25, 30, 40): minimum age 21 with geolocation. Primary servers may be anywhere in the U.S. for one year, then must be in Illinois. Accounts may be shared with sports wagering accounts and opened remotely. Deposits are allowed by a wide range of methods, including crypto and cash at approved retail locations. Platforms must offer a statewide self-exclusion database and daily, weekly and monthly deposit and spend limits. Operators must keep 2 years of advertising samples, but no advertising preapproval is required.","Other: out-of-state and multijurisdictional wagers (e.g., poker) are allowed via reciprocal agreements (Sec. 60). Home-rule regulation is preempted (Sec. 65). Emergency rules are due within 90 days. Licensees must file annual diversity reports starting April 15, 2027. The Act is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, only MA H332 and MA H4431 appear to address internet casino gaming directly; only titles are available for them, so detailed comparison isn't possible. Most other listed bills, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, would authorize sports wagering rather than iCasino. HB4797 instead explicitly excludes sports wagering and is built on Illinois's existing Sports Wagering Act licensees and shared accounts. Its advertising approach is light-touch (record-keeping only, no preapproval), in contrast to CT HB05229, which would restrict campus advertising and AI-targeted betting.","watch":"Operators should watch the 25% workforce-reduction bar on the land-based partner, the requirement to move primary servers into Illinois after one year, the 3-skin cap per licensee and the 25% rate, all of which could be negotiated if the bill revives from Rules.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":37476,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:54.878Z"},"IL-HB5034":{"summary":"HB5034 amends Section 25-45 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act to replace the current cap of three competitively bid, online-only master sports wagering licenses ($20,000,000 each) with an open licensing path. Applicants would pay a $250,000 application fee and a $15,000,000 license fee. Per the synopsis, it also strips the competitive-selection machinery, the statutory eligibility criteria, the duty of disclosure and the DCEO/Board diversity outreach mandates. The bill was re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, so it is effectively stalled for now.","mechanics":["Sec. 25-45(b): nonrefundable $250,000 application fee and a $15,000,000 initial license fee; the license is valid for 4 years and renewable for 4 years at $1,000,000 (Sec. 25-45(c)).","The synopsis states the bill removes the provision directing the Board to issue 3 online-only master licenses at $20,000,000 through an open and competitive selection process, so no numerical cap would remain in Section 25-45.","Sec. 25-45(d) keeps the authorization that a master sports wagering licensee may conduct sports wagering over the Internet or through a mobile application.","Removed, per the synopsis: the statutory eligibility test (age 21, no felony or Article 28 gambling conviction, no dishonesty or moral-turpitude crimes, skill and knowledge standards) and the 30-day public notice requirement.","Also removed: public opening of applications, the 90-day winning-bidder timeline, the licensee duty to disclose material changes, and the DCEO and Board outreach to minority-, women-, veteran- and disability-owned businesses (former subsections (e)-(l)).","Sec. 25-10 receives a conforming change, likely to the 'winning bidder' definition that is tied to the competitive selection. The bill is effective immediately upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills either legalize mobile wagering from scratch, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, or adjust regulatory administration, such as CO SB 163 on commission delegation and MA H481 on category 2 retail licenses. HB5034 is different because it restructures an existing mature market: Illinois's online-only license tier would move from a capped, competitively bid model to an uncapped, fixed-fee model at a lower price ($15M versus $20M). Unusually, it also deletes statutory suitability criteria and diversity outreach mandates rather than adding consumer-protection or integrity rules, as CT HB05229 and LA SB354 do.","watch":"Watch whether the uncapped $15M online-only license survives revival from the Rules Committee, since it would lower the barrier for new national operators to enter Illinois without a tethered casino, track or sports-facility partner, and its deletion of the Sec. 25-45(d) eligibility criteria would shift suitability standards entirely to Board rulemaking.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":15955,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:56.551Z"},"IL-HB5035":{"summary":"HB5035 deletes the one-year pre-employment cooling-off period for staff of the Illinois Racing Board and the Illinois Gaming Board. Currently, neither agency can hire someone who, in the prior year, was employed by or paid by a person or entity (or its parent or affiliate) doing business with the Board, a racing licensee, or a Gambling Act licensee. The parallel one-year bar for Board members before gubernatorial nomination stays in place.","mechanics":["Section 5 amends 230 ILCS 5/6(f) (Horse Racing Act) to remove employees from the one-year look-back on employment or compensation from entities doing business with the Racing Board, a racing licensee, or a Gambling Act licensee; the bar continues to apply to Board members before nomination.","Section 10 amends 230 ILCS 10/5(a)(8) (Gambling Act) to delete the one-year pre-employment restriction for Gaming Board employees. Because the IGB administers the Sports Wagering Act under Sec. 5(b)(13.2), the change reaches sports-wagering regulatory staff.","The rest of Sec. 5(a)(8) is unchanged: it still bars hiring anyone who, or whose spouse, parent or child, is an official of, or has a financial interest in or financial relation with, an Illinois gambling operator or horse racing organization, and violations remain grounds for termination.","Other ethics rules for both Boards are untouched: the conflict-of-interest and gift bans in 230 ILCS 5/6(d)-(e), the Section 5-45 State Officials and Employees Ethics Act restrictions, and the IGB member one-year bar and disclosure duty in Sec. 5(a)(2).","The bill has no effective date clause or new enforcement provisions. It was re-referred to House Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, which effectively stalls it for now."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue on the list is FL S1580. It lets a person ineligible for employment with the Florida Gaming Control Commission seek a case-by-case waiver based on expertise, whereas HB5035 repeals the Illinois look-back for staff outright with no waiver process or Board review. CO SB 163 also concerns regulator structure, but it addresses how the Colorado commission may delegate licensing duties to its division, not staff eligibility. Most other listed bills, such as AK HB145, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308, authorize or tax sports wagering and do not touch regulator hiring rules.","watch":"Operator counsel should watch whether this revives, because it would let former operator, supplier or vendor employees join IGB sports-wagering staff immediately, which could ease regulatory staffing but invite recusal and appearance-of-conflict challenges; the separate financial-interest bar in Sec. 5(a)(8) still applies.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":34592,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:02.842Z"},"IL-HB5059":{"summary":"HB5059 creates the ORACLE Act, an Attorney General-enforced consumer protection regime for prediction market platforms serving Illinois residents. It sets a 21+ age floor, bans athletic event, political, catastrophic, death and security markets for state users, and imposes responsible gaming, advertising, funding and anti-manipulation duties. It also bars markets that use gaming companies as market makers or revenue-share partners. The bill was re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, so it is effectively stalled for now.","mechanics":["Sec. 10 bars registration or positions by anyone under 21. If an underage account is discovered, the provider must immediately suspend it, close all positions and pay out the funds. Sec. 15 requires exclusion of self-excluded persons, provider and settlement-source insiders, persons with inside information, and any AG-designated categories.","Sec. 20 prohibits athletic event markets for state users. The definition includes horse racing and prop betting but carves out tournament-champion and full-bracket positions. Political, catastrophic event, death and single-company stock-price markets are also banned. Sec. 25 requires disclosure of settlement sources and bars settlement on proprietary or confidential information.","Sec. 30 requires self-exclusion, optional deposit and spend limits with a 14-day wait to raise them, and time-on-platform tools. Only the 1-800-GAMBLER/ILGAMB hotline may be displayed. Sec. 35 bans push notifications about bonuses or markets where the user holds no open position, \"risk-free\" claims, and ads depicting or targeting under-21s. It also requires the hotline on all ads (visible for the full length of a video) and makes providers responsible for affiliate compliance. Sec. 40 bans gift certificates and credit-card funding.","Sec. 45 prohibits offering any market in which a liquidity provider or market maker, or any affiliate, parent, subsidiary, JV or agent of one, knowingly engages in gaming activities in the ordinary course of business anywhere. It also bars markets involving a contract or revenue share with such an entity.","Sec. 60 penalties: up to $10,000 per violation, or $50,000 for a persistent course of conduct. Violations of Secs. 15 or 45 carry the greater of 2x market profits or $50,000. The AG may obtain an injunction, and the court must then order the provider to cease Illinois operations, with $1,000,000 per day for operating in violation of that order. Insider trading is penalized by cross-reference to Sec. 20-70 of the Digital Assets and Consumer Protection Act.","The AG has rulemaking authority (Sec. 65). Sec. 50 voids terms of service that waive rights under the Act. No effective date or licensing process is specified, although Sec. 35 refers to a \"licensed\" platform."],"issue_tags":["prediction","advertising","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest listed analogue is CT HB05038, which also bars prediction market use by, and advertising to, under-21s. HB5059 goes much further by banning entire market categories, including all athletic event markets, and by restricting gaming-industry market makers. CA AB1840 approaches prediction markets only through Political Reform Act ethics rules for officials, whereas Illinois would bar political markets outright. CT HB05229 and LA SB354 regulate licensed sportsbook advertising and prop/micro-bets rather than prediction markets. Most other listed bills (e.g., GA HB 686, HI HB 1308, AK HB145) authorize and tax sports wagering, so the Illinois approach of an AG-run conduct code with no licensing or tax component stands apart.","watch":"Section 45's ban on any market involving a market maker, revenue-share partner or affiliate that engages in gaming in the ordinary course anywhere would effectively shut licensed sportsbook operators and their corporate families out of Illinois prediction market ventures, backed by penalties of 2x profits or $50,000 and possible cease-operations orders.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":19485,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:14.051Z"},"IL-HB5048":{"summary":"HB5048 would legalize and regulate non-networked \"prize and gift kiosks\" (sweepstakes-style devices that promote a bona fide product and award prizes, including cash, with a mail-in free-play option) under the Prizes and Gifts Act. At the same time it makes gambling offenses involving Internet-connected or credit-metering electronic devices a Class 4 felony. It also bars the Gaming Board from penalizing or denying video gaming licensees and applicants over lawful kiosk activity, and voids every municipal restriction on any activity exempted under 720 ILCS 5/28-1(b). The bill was re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, so it is effectively stalled.","mechanics":["Prizes and Gifts Act Sec. 10/33: a prize and gift kiosk must promote a bona fide product, offer a No Payment or Purchase option limited to a mail-in code, and use a revenue-based self-contained fill system that stops after a predetermined cycle. It must also carry a \"route boost plus\" monitoring system that records cash in, winnings, entries, malfunctions and remote activation and disabling, but cannot alter play or read patron financial data. It may not connect to the Internet or a network except through a redemption vault.","Sec. 45: operators must register with the Department of Revenue on Form REG-1-A and register each kiosk as a Coin-Operated Amusement Device (Form RCOA-1), paying annual fees set by the Department. Misclassifying a device is treated as filing a false report, with penalties including seizure. Jurisdiction lies with the Department of Revenue and the Liquor Control Commission or its designee, including any public-private task force.","Criminal Code 28-2(a)(iii): the definition of \"gambling device\" is expanded to any vending or electronic machine that meters credits or connects to the Internet or a network to play or retrieve data, excluding redemption vaults and prize and gift kiosks. New 28-1(c)(2) makes any gambling offense involving such a device a Class 4 felony.","New municipal preemption in 28-1: no municipality may restrict or prohibit any activity lawful under 28-1(b), and existing local restrictions become void. The 28-1(b) list includes video gaming, casino gaming, the lottery, and sports wagering under the Sports Wagering Act (b)(15), not just kiosks.","Video Gaming Act 230 ILCS 40/35: lawful game devices that are not Internet-connected cannot be grounds for discipline, delay or denial. Prior denials based on lawful devices, including those the Board labeled \"sweepstakes devices,\" must be rescinded and licenses issued. Applications disclosing such devices must be processed within 90 days.","Effective immediately upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills that touch unlicensed gaming run in the opposite direction. LA HB883 and MD HB1226 tighten enforcement against illegal online gambling, and FL S1580 revises illegal-gambling penalties. HB5048 instead carves out a registered, tax-administered safe harbor for brick-and-mortar sweepstakes kiosks, keeping only an Internet-connection felony as its enforcement hook. None of the listed bills, including the sports wagering authorizations in AK HB145, GA HB 686 or the HI bills, contains a comparable blanket preemption of local regulation for every exempted gambling activity.","watch":"Watch the municipal preemption clause in 28-1, which would void local restrictions on all 28-1(b) activities including sports wagering and video gaming, and the broad Internet-connected \"gambling device\" felony definition in 28-2(a)(iii), whose interplay with licensed mobile and retail wagering equipment should be confirmed if the bill is revived.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":29796,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:15.565Z"},"IL-HB5077":{"summary":"HB5077 amends the Illinois Sports Wagering Act to authorize interstate pool sports wagering and to remove the cap of three standalone online master sports wagering licenses, letting the Gaming Board issue such licenses without the $20,000,000 competitive-selection model. It also creates a new licensing, conduct and tax regime for fantasy contest operators under the Board, with a 15% privilege tax on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts beginning July 1, 2026. Single-participant contests against the operator are excluded from the definition of a lawful fantasy contest. The bill was re-referred to Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27 and is effectively stalled.","mechanics":["Sec. 25-25(i) allows interstate pool wagering if the pool is expressly permitted in every participating jurisdiction. Illinois taxable receipts equal in-state wagers minus a pro rata share of winnings, calculated to the nearest .01%.","Sec. 25-45 lets the Board issue online master sports wagering licenses, replacing the three-license, $20M competitive process. Fees are a $250,000 nonrefundable application fee and a $15,000,000 initial license fee. Licenses run 4 years with a $1,000,000 renewal fee, and the competitive-bid subsections (e)-(l) are blanked.","Fantasy contest operators must be licensed (Sec. 25-120.3), and unlicensed offering is a Class 4 felony. Small operators (7,500 or fewer patrons in the prior 365 days) pay $500 at application and $300 on renewal. Large operators pay $7,500 and $5,000. Licenses run 2 years. Existing operators may keep operating if they apply within 90 days after implementing rules take effect, and they receive temporary permits.","The 'fantasy contest' definition (Sec. 25-120.1) excludes pool sports wagering, single-participant contests played against the operator, and free contests. Prior single-player contests do not bar licensure only if they stopped by the effective date (Sec. 25-120.3(f)).","Conduct and integrity rules (Secs. 25-120.4 to 25-120.6): participants must be 21 or older and physically in-state, verified by geolocation. Insiders and operator employees may not play. Operators must follow AML, identity verification and fraud/collusion detection requirements, report integrity issues to the Board, and obtain annual CPA audits and annual independent lab certification of their platform.","Tax and administration: a 15% privilege tax on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts from July 1, 2026 (Sec. 25-120.7), deposited to the State Gaming Fund. Fantasy contests are declared not gambling and exempted in Criminal Code 28-1(b)(16). Self-exclusion is extended to fantasy accounts. Emergency rulemaking authority sunsets after one year. The bill is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue is KY HB 904, which also puts fantasy contest operators under the state gaming regulator with geolocation and operator-procedure requirements. KY HB 904's summary does not show HB5077's 15% receipts tax or its exclusion of operator-banked single-participant contests. FL H0189 also addresses fantasy sports contests, but within a broader gambling and enforcement overhaul rather than a licensing-and-tax regime layered onto an existing sports wagering act. Unlike LA SB354, which removes prop betting from permitted wagers, HB5077 leaves the sports wagering menu intact and instead expands market structure through interstate pools and an uncapped online license.","watch":"Watch three things: the exclusion of single-participant contests combined with the Class 4 felony for unlicensed operation, which would push pick'em-style products out of Illinois or into the Sports Wagering Act; the uncapped $15M standalone online license, which could reshape the competitive landscape; and the drafting error in Sec. 25-120.2(c)(2), which routes tax collected under Sec. 25-120.6 (the audit section) when the tax actually sits in Sec. 25-120.7.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":51680,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:19.463Z"},"IL-HB5142":{"summary":"HB5142 amends the definition of \"sports wagering\" in Section 25-10 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45/25-10) to expressly include participation in any prediction market on sports events, portions of sports events, or athlete performance statistics, covering the offering, listing, solicitation, purchase, sale, trading, exchange, or settlement of event contracts and similar instruments. It provides that such activity is sports wagering regardless of peer-to-peer structure, whether the operator is a counterparty, or how the operator labels the product. The practical effect is to bring sports event contracts within the Act's licensing and enforcement regime, though the bill adds no new penalties or licensing provisions of its own.","mechanics":["Single operative change: adds a new sentence to the \"sports wagering\" definition in 230 ILCS 45/25-10. All other definitions are restated unchanged.","Covered instruments include any \"event contract, sports-event contract, or other contract, agreement, or transaction,\" whether styled as a derivative, option, binary contract, or similar instrument, where a person risks something of value on the occurrence, nonoccurrence, or outcome of a sports event, any portion of one, or athlete performance statistics.","Covered conduct spans the full lifecycle: offering, listing, solicitation, purchase, sale, trading, exchange, and settlement.","Anti-recharacterization clause: the activity is sports wagering even if it is peer-to-peer, participants take positions against each other rather than the operator, the operator is not a counterparty, or the operator calls it an investing opportunity, exchange, marketplace, or prediction market.","No effective date, tax rate, penalty, or licensing provision is added; enforcement would flow from the existing Act and the Illinois Gaming Board's authority.","Status: introduced by Rep. Didech and re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, which means it is effectively stalled for now."],"issue_tags":["prediction","licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05038 (age-21 and advertising limits for prediction market platforms) and CA AB1840 (Political Reform Act restrictions on officials and prediction market contracts), HB5142 does not regulate prediction markets as a separate product. Instead, it folds sports event contracts directly into the statutory definition of sports wagering, which subjects them to the state's existing licensing regime. LA SB354 also works through the definition of permitted sports wagers, but in the opposite direction: it removes proposition bets and bans micro-bets. HB5142's anti-recharacterization language aimed at exchange and peer-to-peer structures is unusual among the listed bills and appears designed to set up a state-law conflict with CFTC-regulated designated contract markets.","watch":"Watch whether this definitional expansion revives or is folded into a larger gaming package, because it would set up a direct federal preemption fight with CFTC-registered prediction markets offering sports contracts in Illinois and could reshape the competitive landscape for licensed sportsbooks versus exchange-based competitors.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8226,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:24.708Z"},"IL-HB5139":{"summary":"HB5139 amends the Illinois Video Gaming Act and the Criminal Code of 2012 to crack down on unlicensed sweepstakes, 'skill' and similar chance-based machines while reshuffling video gaming tax revenue. It expands the criminal definition of 'gambling device' to cover vending and electronic machines offering chance-based sweepstakes entries tied to a purchase or coupon, and makes offenses involving such devices a Class 4 felony. It also shifts more of the 35% net terminal income tax to local governments starting January 1, 2027, and repeals Video Gaming Act Section 27 on political-subdivision prohibitions. The bill was re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a) on March 27, 2026, so it is effectively stalled for now.","mechanics":["Criminal Code 28-2(a)(iii): 'gambling device' now includes any vending or other electronic machine, including one that awards credits and has a credit-removal circuit, meter or switch, that offers entry into a contest, sweepstakes or other chance-based selection process. This applies where the entry is incidental to (A) buying an item or service or (B) buying or receiving a redeemable coupon, voucher or credit. New 28-2(d) states an anti-subterfuge intent, and the vending machine exclusion is made subject to this new language.","Criminal Code 28-1(c)(2): any gambling offense involving a 28-2(a)(iii) device is a Class 4 felony, notwithstanding the general Class A misdemeanor rule. The no-purchase-necessary exemption in 28-1(b)(13) is narrowed so it no longer applies where the game runs through a gambling device or by means of the Internet.","Video Gaming Act Sec. 35: adds a safe harbor so an applicant or licensee is not in violation of the Act or 11 Ill. Adm. Code 1800.420, and faces no discipline, delay of Board consideration or license denial, for operating a gaming device that complies with, and is not gambling under, Criminal Code 28-1(b) or 28-2(a)(iii). It deletes the prior 'nothing shall be deemed to prohibit' sentence.","Video Gaming Act Sec. 60(b-5): the 35% tax on net terminal income stays. From January 1, 2027, the split moves from 83.7% Capital Projects Fund / 14.3% Local Government Video Gaming Distributive Fund / 2% State Gaming Fund to 80.84% / 17.16% / 2%.","Repeals Video Gaming Act Section 27, the provision on prohibition of video gaming by political subdivisions."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills are sports-wagering authorization or tax bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569, KS SB303), and none overlaps directly with this one. The closest analogues are MD HB295, which bans operating or promoting 'interactive games' and requires licensees to report ties to illegal-market actors, and FL H0189 and FL S1580, which revise gaming machine rules and illegal-gambling penalties. Unlike those, HB5139 attacks unregulated sweepstakes machines through the criminal definition of 'gambling device' and a felony classification, rather than through a standalone prohibition. It also pairs that with a video gaming tax redistribution.","watch":"Watch the narrowing of the 28-1(b)(13) no-purchase exemption for games conducted 'by means of the Internet', which could reach online sweepstakes casinos and potentially free-to-play contests or promotions run outside the Sports Wagering Act exemption in 28-1(b)(15). Also watch the felony exposure for device-based sweepstakes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":24591,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:26.210Z"},"IL-HB5143":{"summary":"HB5143 amends Section 25-90 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45/25-90) to sunset the per-wager tax on online and mobile sports wagers in subsection (d-7), so that it runs from July 1, 2025 and ends July 1, 2026 instead of continuing indefinitely. The graduated privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts, the 2% Cook County surcharge and the fund-distribution provisions are left unchanged. The bill was re-referred to the House Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on March 27, 2026, so it is stalled for now.","mechanics":["Subsection (d-7) is amended to strike 'each month thereafter' and add 'ending July 1, 2026'. This terminates the per-wager tax of $0.25 per wager on the first 20,000,000 annual combined Tier 1 and Tier 2 wagers and $0.50 per wager above that threshold.","The per-wager tax applies only to wagers placed over the Internet or through a mobile application, and its revenue goes to the General Revenue Fund. The repeal therefore removes that GRF stream.","The bill leaves in place the graduated privilege tax under (d) and (d-5) on annual adjusted gross sports wagering receipts: 20% up to $30M, 25% from $30M to $50M, 30% from $50M to $100M, 35% from $100M to $200M, and 40% above $200M.","The 2% additional tax under (a-5) on wagers placed in a home rule county of over 3,000,000 inhabitants (Cook County), earmarked for criminal justice, is retained. So is the 58/42 split of excess Sports Wagering Fund moneys between the General Revenue Fund and the Capital Projects Fund.","The Section 99 effective date is immediate upon becoming law. There are no new enforcement, licensing or audit changes, and the (e) audit and quarterly compliance-report requirements are unchanged."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike most listed bills, which create or raise sports-wagering taxes, HB5143 is a tax rollback. AK HB145 and SB194 and HI HB 1308 and HB 2570 impose new revenue or excise taxes as part of legalization, and KS SB303 would add a handle-based excise tax of 2% of the amount wagered. Illinois's per-wager levy is itself a rare transaction-count tax, and this bill would make it a one-year experiment rather than a permanent fixture. None of the other listed bills proposes repealing an existing operator tax.","watch":"Operators should track whether the per-wager sunset language is revived from Rules Committee or folded into the FY2027 budget or revenue omnibus, since without it the $0.25/$0.50 per-wager tax under (d-7) continues past July 1, 2026 alongside the 20%–40% graduated rate.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8527,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:27.526Z"},"IL-HB5318":{"summary":"HB5318 amends the Illinois Income Tax Act to end the '80/20' exclusion, under which unitary business group members with 80% or more of their business activity outside the United States are left out of the combined group. The exclusion would apply only to taxable years ending before January 1, 2027, pulling such affiliates into Illinois combined reports after that date. The bill also defines 'United States' for 'foreign person' purposes and adds joint and several liability for members of a combined reporting group; it is a general corporate income tax measure and does not mention wagering.","mechanics":["Sec. 1501 (per synopsis): the 80/20 exclusion of members with 80% or more foreign business activity from the unitary business group applies only to taxable years ending before January 1, 2027.","Sec. 203 addition modifications (D-17) for interest and (D-18) for intangible expenses paid to 80/20 foreign affiliates are limited to taxable years ending on or after December 31, 2004 and before January 1, 2027; the matching subtractions (DD) and (EE) are cut off at the same date. Add-backs for persons excluded under Sec. 1501(a)(27) continue.","For 'foreign person' purposes, 'United States' means the 50 states, D.C., U.S. territories and possessions, and any area where the U.S. asserts jurisdiction or exclusive rights over natural-resource exploration or exploitation (per synopsis).","New Sec. 1009 imposes joint and several liability on members of a combined reporting group (per synopsis; operative text not in the excerpt provided). Sec. 304 apportionment is also amended.","Effective immediately; status is Introduced, with Rep. La Shawn K. Ford added as co-sponsor on 2026-05-25."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals with corporate combined reporting or the unitary group rules. The gaming tax bills, such as AK HB145/SB194 (mobile wagering revenue tax), KS SB303 (2% excise on the amount wagered) and NY A3009/S3009 Part GG (gaming revenue tax rates), impose taxes specific to wagering. HB5318 instead changes the general corporate income tax base, which reaches gaming companies only as multinational corporate taxpayers. The closest analogues are general tax vehicles such as MN SF2374/SF5052, though the summaries given for those bills show no combined-reporting provisions.","watch":"Operators with foreign parents or affiliates that have substantial non-U.S. activity should model the effect of those affiliates entering the Illinois combined group from tax years ending in 2027, along with the new joint and several liability of group members under Sec. 1009.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":288502,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:36.650Z"},"IL-HB5452":{"summary":"HB5452 renames the Illinois Liquor Control Act of 1934 as the Liquor and Hemp Products Control Act and creates a Hemp Products Commission as a division of the Illinois Liquor Control Commission, along with local hemp products control commissions, to license and regulate hemp products. It sets a 21+ age limit, creates a four-tier licensing system, and covers taxation, testing, labeling, advertising and social equity for hemp products. It does not, in the text provided, address sports wagering or gaming. It was re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, which in Illinois usually means it is stalled for the session.","mechanics":["Renames the Liquor Control Act of 1934 to the Liquor and Hemp Products Control Act and makes conforming changes across the statutes. The excerpt shows changes to Election Code Secs. 3-1.3 and 28-6, 20 ILCS 2505/2505-40, Criminal Identification Act Sec. 3.1, and Retailers' Occupation Tax Act Sec. 3.","Establishes the Hemp Products Commission within the Illinois Liquor Control Commission, with the State Commission's liquor powers applied to hemp products, and creates local hemp products control commissions.","Bars giving, selling, transferring or delivering any hemp product to anyone under 21.","Creates four license types: grower/cultivator, processor/manufacturer, distributor/wholesaler, and adult-use hemp products retailer. Fees are referenced in the synopsis but no figures appear in the excerpt.","Imposes a tax on hemp cannabinoid products (no rate appears in the excerpt) and sets labeling, testing, advertising, recordkeeping and social equity rules.","Effective January 1, 2027."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable, because they all deal with sports wagering, iGaming, prediction markets or gaming tax and enforcement. Examples include AK HB145, GA HB 686, MD HB1343 and KS SB303, while HB5452 is a hemp and cannabinoid regulatory bill. The only loose parallel is the 21+ access limit, which echoes CT HB05038's under-21 restriction for prediction markets but applies to a different product. What is unusual about HB5452 is its structure: it places a new product category under the existing liquor regulator rather than creating a stand-alone agency.","watch":"The excerpt does not show whether the statute-wide renaming reaches gaming laws that cross-reference the Liquor Control Act, such as the Video Gaming Act's liquor-licensed establishment provisions, so operators should check the full conforming-changes index; otherwise the bill is stalled in Rules and has no direct sports-betting impact.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":520421,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:45.052Z"},"IL-HB5368":{"summary":"HB5368 amends the Illinois Substance Use Disorder Act (20 ILCS 301) to fold gambling disorder more fully into the Department of Human Services' prevention, treatment, and education mandate alongside substance use disorders. It expands the Section 5-20 gambling disorder program, adding a suicide-reduction element, hotline-and-website language, and permissive authority for prevention literature, curricula, technical support, and grants to local prevention and treatment projects. It creates no new tax, fee, or licensing obligation for gaming operators, and as of 2026-03-27 it has been re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a), so it is effectively stalled.","mechanics":["Sec. 1-10 defines \"gambling\" as betting or wagering on uncertain outcomes, including but not limited to activity regulated by the Illinois Gaming Board. It rewrites \"gambling disorder\" with reference to DSM-5, and defines \"video gaming\" as playing video games, which means consumer video games, not video gaming terminals.","Sec. 5-20(a)-(b): subject to appropriation, the DHS program covers a toll-free hotline and website for crisis counseling and referral, public awareness including stigma, in-service training, and prevalence studies. A new item (a)(5) directs screening, crisis intervention, treatment and similar means to reduce suicide attempts related to gambling disorder.","Sec. 5-20(c) keeps the existing DHS-determined help statement that licensed gambling establishments must post and that master sports wagering licensees must include on their portal, website, or app, with signs supplied under Section 25.95 of the Sports Wagering Act and parallel statutes. The bill adds no new operator duty here.","New Sec. 5-20(d)-(e) gives DHS permissive authority to publish prevention literature, develop curricula, and advise officials, including on \"the relationship between gaming and gambling disorders.\" It may also award grants to local health departments, correctional institutions, hospitals, universities, and community and faith-based organizations for prevention and treatment projects, and may seek private and federal funds.","Sec. 5-10: DHS must collaborate with the State Board of Education on substance use and gambling disorder instructional resources (new (a)(4)(H-5)). DHS may fund parent programs covering healthy video gaming, financial planning, and how to talk about gambling. The Advisory Council's remit under Sec. 10-10(g) includes preventing gambling and gaming by minors.","Sec. 15-10 keeps gambling disorder treatment as an adjunct to any treatment license level of care and to recovery home intervention licenses. The bill is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike MD HB518, which directs the Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to adopt problem-gambling regulations including a voluntary exclusion list, HB5368 places no new obligations on operators or the Illinois Gaming Board. Its changes run entirely through DHS public-health programming. It also contrasts with CT HB05229, which imposes direct operator conduct rules on campus advertising, withdrawals, customer service lines, and AI-targeted betting. It differs from HI HB1308 and HB 2570, which pair a problem gambling fund with a licensing and tax regime, because HB5368 identifies no dedicated revenue source and makes everything subject to appropriation.","watch":"Watch whether a revived version adds an operator-funded assessment or earmark to pay for the expanded Section 5-20 hotline, suicide-prevention, and grant programs, or changes the Section 5-20(c) help-statement content that master sports wagering licensees must display on their apps and websites.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":94967,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:47.179Z"},"IL-HB5443":{"summary":"HB5443 amends the Illinois Sports Wagering Act to bring paid fantasy contests under Illinois Gaming Board licensing, taxation and responsible-gaming rules. It declares licensed fantasy contests are not gambling and makes unlicensed operation a Class 4 felony. The definition excludes single-participant contests played against the operator, which pushes out house-banked pick'em products. It also redefines \"sports wagering\" to exclude fantasy contests, adds rules for interstate pool sports wagering, and appears to strike parts of the master sports wagering license competitive-selection provisions in Sec. 25-45.","mechanics":["Licensing (Sec. 25-120.3): a Board fantasy contest operator license is required. Fees are a $50,000 nonrefundable application fee, a $500,000 license fee due within 30 days of initial issuance, a 4-year term, and a $50,000 renewal fee. Knowingly offering contests without a license is a Class 4 felony, and no one other than an owner or key person may take a revenue share without a license.","Definition (Sec. 25-120.1): a fantasy contest is an online contest of skill between 2 or more participants with an entry fee and prizes announced in advance. It expressly excludes pool sports wagering, single-participant contests against the operator, contests without an entry fee, and any outcome based entirely on a single team's result or solely on a single athlete's single performance in a single event.","Incumbent operators and pick'em history (Sec. 25-120.3(b), (f)): operators already active may continue until 90 days after implementing rules take effect, and get a temporary operating permit if they apply and pay within that window. Prior single-player contests against the operator are not grounds for denial only if they stopped by the effective date.","Tax (Sec. 25-120.7): beginning July 1, 2026, a graduated privilege tax applies to adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts. Rates are 20% up to $3M, 25% from $3M to $5M, 30% from $5M to $10M, 35% from $10M to $20M, and 40% above $20M, with proceeds deposited in the State Gaming Fund.","Conduct and duties (Secs. 25-120.4 to 25-120.6): participants must be 21 or older and physically in Illinois, verified by geolocation. Licensees must apply anti-money-laundering standards, identity verification, and underage and collusion controls, and must report integrity breaches and match fixing to the Board. They also face an annual CPA audit and annual independent lab certification of their platform. Covered team and league insiders and licensee employees are barred from participating.","Other changes: fantasy contests are added to the self-exclusion program (Secs. 25-100, 25-120.8). Interstate pools must be legal in every jurisdiction where offered, with Illinois receipts calculated pro rata (Sec. 25-25(i)). Criminal Code Sec. 28-1(b)(16) exempts licensed fantasy contests from gambling, and a new Sec. 28-8(d) addresses losses on pre-effective-date contests against an operator, though the supplied text is truncated. The bill authorizes one-year emergency rulemaking and is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity","legalize","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"KY HB 904 is the closest analogue: like HB5443, it gives a gaming regulator (the Kentucky Horse Racing and Gaming Corporation) authority over fantasy contest operators and requires geolocation. HB5443 adds a steep 20-40% graduated receipts tax, felony penalties, and an explicit exclusion of player-versus-house contests. FL H0189 also addresses fantasy sports contests within a broad gambling overhaul. HB5443's exclusion of single-athlete, single-event outcomes and house-banked contests parallels in effect LA SB354's removal of proposition bets from permitted wagers, but works through the DFS definition rather than the sportsbook menu. Its tax is on net receipts, unlike KS SB303's 2% excise on amounts wagered.","watch":"Watch whether the definition in Sec. 25-120.1 and the Sec. 28-8(d) loss-recovery language survive as drafted, since together they would shut out house-banked pick'em products and could expose operators to civil claims to recover pre-enactment losses, while the 40% top tax tier would hit even sportsbook-affiliated DFS products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":50956,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:50.364Z"},"IL-HB571":{"summary":"HB0571 is a shell bill that amends only Section 25-1 (230 ILCS 45/25-1), the short-title section of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act, with what the synopsis calls a technical change. It has no substantive effect on sports wagering regulation, taxation or licensing as introduced. Because it is sponsored by Speaker Emanuel \"Chris\" Welch and opens the Sports Wagering Act, it can serve as a vehicle for a substantive amendment later in the 104th General Assembly.","mechanics":["Single operative section (Section 5) amends 230 ILCS 45/25-1, the short title of the Sports Wagering Act (originally P.A. 101-31, eff. 6-28-19).","The change is described only as technical; the printed text contains no rates, thresholds, definitions, licensing terms or enforcement provisions.","Sponsor: Rep. Emanuel \"Chris\" Welch, the House Speaker, which is typical of leadership shell bills held for end-of-session deals.","Status: re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2025-05-31, so it is dormant but can be revived and amended while the 2025-2026 General Assembly is in session."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the full mobile sports wagering frameworks in AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308, which set licensing, tax and regulatory structures, IL HB0571 contains no substantive policy. It also lacks the targeted consumer-protection provisions in CT HB05229 (campus advertising limits, withdrawal rules, AI targeting) and the child-support withholding mandate in IN HB1092. What is unusual is that it is a pure placeholder whose significance lies in its sponsor and its ability to carry later amendments into the Sports Wagering Act.","watch":"Track any floor or committee amendment to HB0571, especially near budget or session deadlines, since a Speaker-sponsored shell opening the Sports Wagering Act could be used to insert changes to the privilege tax, per-wager fees or licensing terms with little advance notice.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":764,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:51.841Z"},"IL-SB2510":{"summary":"SB2510 is Illinois's FY2026 capital appropriations bill, and the text provided is only Governor Pritzker's June 16, 2025 reduction-veto message, not the bill itself. Under Article IV, Section 9(d) of the Illinois Constitution, the Governor cut two line items by a combined $161,156,000 and approved all other appropriations. He said the cut amounts were duplicative new Build Illinois Bond Fund appropriations that pushed spending past the bond authorization in HB3374. The General Assembly did not override, and the reductions stood on October 29, 2025.","mechanics":["Article 129, Section 310 (p. 910, line 1) is reduced from $147,314,964 to $52,852,964, a cut of $94,462,000.","Article 181, Section 5 (p. 3305, line 13) is reduced from $103,608,422 to $36,914,422, a cut of $66,694,000.","The stated rationale is that new Article 129 appropriations were meant to replace portions of existing appropriations and reappropriations in Articles 129 and 181. Those existing amounts were never reduced, so the bill would have exceeded the Build Illinois bond authorization in HB3374.","All other appropriations in SB2510 were approved as passed.","The last recorded action, on 2025-10-29, is that the item/reduction veto stands."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a routine reduction veto correcting a capital-budget drafting error. The veto message contains no reference to sports wagering, gaming taxes, licensing or gaming-funded capital funds. Whether any underlying SB2510 line items draw on gaming-derived revenue cannot be determined from this text.","watch":"Nothing in the veto message touches gaming, but operators may want to check whether any underlying SB2510 capital appropriations are funded from gaming or sports-wagering tax receipts, since pressure on capital funding can feed future proposals to raise Illinois wagering taxes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Veto Message","text_chars":1412,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:59.893Z"},"IL-SB2394":{"summary":"SB2394, the First 2025 General Revisory Act (Public Act 104-0417), is Illinois' routine technical clean-up statute. Section 1(b) says it makes no substantive change; it reconciles conflicting versions of Sections amended by more than one Public Act, renumbers duplicates, fixes cross-references and deletes obsolete text. The excerpt provided does not reach any gaming or wagering statute.","mechanics":["Section 1(b) states the Act is not intended to make any substantive change in the law; it only reconciles multiple amendments and makes technical corrections.","Section 1(d): Public Acts 103-584 through 103-1059 were considered in preparing the combining revisories; source notes at the end of each Section are not part of the text.","Sections 10 and 12 tidy the Regulatory Sunset Act: they correct the professional-licensing Acts listed for repeal in 2029 and 2030 (Secs. 4.39 and 4.40) and repeal obsolete Sec. 4.35.","Section 15 renumbers three duplicate Illinois Administrative Procedure Act emergency-rulemaking sections, all numbered 5-45.55, as 5-45.58, 5-45.59 and 5-45.60, and corrects sunset dates such as 5-45.52.","Section 20 re-letters duplicate Freedom of Information Act exemption paragraphs in 5 ILCS 140/7.5, now (ooo) through (sss), without changing their content.","Section 25 makes grammatical and cross-reference fixes to the Illinois Public Labor Relations Act (Secs. 5 and 15), for example replacing 'this amendatory Act' with the specific Public Act number."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that this is a non-substantive omnibus revisory act rather than gaming legislation. Its only potential relevance to operators is codification: if the full text combines versions of the Sports Wagering Act, Illinois Gambling Act or Video Gaming Act amended by multiple 103rd General Assembly Public Acts, those sections would be restated, not changed.","watch":"Check the full enrolled text, which is truncated here, for any combining revisories of 230 ILCS chapters (Sports Wagering Act, Illinois Gambling Act, Video Gaming Act); those would set the authoritative codified wording of provisions such as the sports wagering tax and licensing sections, even though Section 1(b) disclaims any substantive change.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":4587515,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:01.546Z"},"IL-SB2667":{"summary":"SB2667 amends the Illinois Sports Wagering Act to fold \"awards shows\" into the definition of \"sports event,\" so licensed operators could take wagers on the Emmys, Grammys, Oscars, Tonys, and any other live competition or talent contest. It directs the Illinois Gaming Board to adopt rules permitting awards show wagering within 6 months of the effective date and authorizes emergency rulemaking to do so.","mechanics":["230 ILCS 45/25-10: new definition of \"awards show\" covers the Emmy, Grammy, Academy, and Tony Awards Shows \"or any other live competition or talent contest.\" \"Sports event\" is amended to include any awards show \"unless the context requires otherwise.\"","New 230 ILCS 45/25-66(a): the Board shall adopt rules permitting awards show wagering pursuant to Section 25-20 within 6 months after the effective date.","Section 25-66(b) and new 5 ILCS 100/5-45.65: the Board may use emergency rulemaking under IAPA Section 5-45. The emergency rulemaking section self-repeals one year after the effective date.","Existing definitions are unchanged, including covered persons, prohibited conduct, official league data, and tier 1/tier 2 wagers. They remain athlete- and sports-governing-body oriented and are not adapted to awards shows.","Effective immediately upon becoming law. Status: Senate Committee Amendment No. 1 was re-referred to Assignments on 2026-05-22, so the bill appears stalled."],"issue_tags":["legalize","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills create or restructure a sports wagering market rather than expand the menu of wagerable events. These include AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, and MA's internet gaming bills. The closest analog is MD HB1240, which also expands the \"sporting event\" definition, but to historical horse races. IL SB2667 is unusual in reaching non-athletic entertainment outcomes whose results are known in advance to a small group of insiders such as accounting firms and academy voters. The \"any other live competition or talent contest\" catch-all could sweep in reality TV.","watch":"Watch whether amendments or Board rules narrow the open-ended \"any other live competition or talent contest\" language and address integrity risks specific to predetermined results, since the existing covered-person and official-league-data provisions do not fit awards-show insiders or the organizations that run these shows.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8276,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:02.091Z"},"IL-SB3019":{"summary":"The enrolled portion of SB3019 creates two new Illinois taxes effective January 1, 2027. Article 1, the Targeted Advertising Services Tax Act, imposes a 10% gross receipts tax on providers of programmatic targeted advertising delivered to Illinois user-consumers. Article 3, the Digital Asset Tax Act, imposes a 0.2% tax on customers receiving digital asset exchange, transfer or storage services, collected by digital asset brokers. The supplied text is truncated partway through Article 3, and no wagering or gaming provisions appear in the portion provided.","mechanics":["Sec. 1-15: 10% tax on gross receipts of targeted advertising services provided in Illinois, beginning January 1, 2027. The legal incidence falls on the provider, not the advertiser. News media entity platforms are excluded from the definition of targeted advertising services.","Sec. 1-10 and 1-20(c): only providers with more than $1,000,000 in annual Illinois targeted-ad gross receipts are covered, tested quarterly. Controlled groups under IRC 1563(a) are aggregated as a single entity.","Sec. 1-20: services are sourced to the user-consumer's location. There is a rebuttable presumption of Illinois location based on an Illinois home address, mailing address, IP address or place of primary use, and the provider bears the burden of proof.","Sec. 1-25, 1-35 and 1-50: providers must register by January 1, 2027 and file monthly returns by the 20th, with a 1.75% vendor discount capped at $1,000 per return. Failing to file, failing to keep records or filing a fraudulent return is a Class 3 felony.","Sec. 1-70, 1-900 and 1-905: home rule counties and municipalities are preempted from taxing targeted advertising services.","Sec. 3-20 and 3-15: 0.2% tax on the value of digital assets exchanged, transferred or stored for Illinois customers, beginning January 1, 2027. Remote brokers are covered once Illinois gross receipts reach $100,000 or more."],"issue_tags":["tax","advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike KS SB303, which imposes a 2% excise on the amount of each sports wager, SB3019 does not tax wagering at all. It taxes the digital ad platforms that sportsbooks buy from. It also differs from CT HB05229 and CT HB05038, which regulate the content and audience of gaming and prediction-market advertising, because SB3019 is a pure revenue measure on programmatic ad sellers with no conduct rules. The Illinois approach is closer to Maryland's digital ad tax model than to any of the listed gaming bills, using a 10% flat rate and a low $1M threshold.","watch":"Watch for platforms such as Meta, Google and programmatic DSPs passing the 10% tax through to sportsbook advertising contracts starting January 1, 2027. Also check the untruncated enrolled text for any later Article touching sports wagering taxes, since none appears in the excerpt provided.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":2554556,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:10.406Z"},"IL-SB555":{"summary":"SB0555 is a shell bill that amends Section 25-1 (the short title section) of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act, 230 ILCS 45/25-1, making only what the synopsis calls a technical change. It has no substantive effect on sports wagering regulation, taxation or licensing as introduced. It was filed by Senate President Don Harmon on 1/24/2025 and was re-referred to Assignments under Rule 3-9(a) on 2025-04-11.","mechanics":["Single operative section (Section 5) amends 230 ILCS 45/25-1, the short title of the Sports Wagering Act (Article 25 of P.A. 101-31).","The change is technical wording in the short-title sentence. The printed text reads 'the the Sports Wagering Act', and the edit appears to concern that article. It makes no change to rates, licenses, definitions or enforcement.","No effective date, tax, fee or regulatory provision is included.","Status: introduced, then re-referred to Senate Assignments under Rule 3-9(a) after missing committee deadlines, which leaves it dormant but revivable as a vehicle."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike substantive framework bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, SB0555 contains no provisions on authorization, licensing, taxation or consumer protection. It also differs from targeted measures like CT HB05229 (advertising and AI restrictions) or IN HB1092 (child-support withholding from wagering accounts). What is unusual is that it is purely a placeholder 'shell' bill: it is held by the Senate President and is designed to be amended later with substantive Sports Wagering Act changes.","watch":"Because this is a leadership-sponsored shell bill on the Sports Wagering Act, operators' counsel should monitor it for a late-session gut-and-replace amendment that could insert tax, fee, licensing or other substantive changes with little notice.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":772,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:16.611Z"},"IL-SB3731":{"summary":"SB3731, enacted as Public Act 104-0852, is Illinois's First 2026 General Revisory Act. It reconciles conflicting versions of sections amended by multiple Public Acts, renumbers duplicate section numbers, fixes cross-references, and deletes obsolete text. Section 1(b) states that it is not intended to make any substantive change in the law. The only gaming-related text in the portion provided is the existing Open Meetings Act exception for Illinois Gaming Board deliberations, carried forward unchanged.","mechanics":["Section 1(d): the revisory considered Public Acts 103-1059 through 104-459, and many of the combined sections contain no striking or underscoring because no new changes are made.","Section 5 renumbers duplicate Statute on Statutes sections (for example, the juvenile prostitution prior-prosecutions provision moves from 1.45 to 1.48), and Section 15 renumbers several Administrative Procedure Act emergency-rulemaking sections numbered 5-45.65.","Sections 10 and 11 combine the Regulatory Sunset Act list of Acts repealed January 1, 2031 and repeal Section 4.36.","Section 20 sets out the Open Meetings Act Section 2 text before and after P.A. 104-457 and 104-458 (effective 6-1-26).","In both versions, exception (c)(36) is retained as is: the Illinois Gaming Board may close deliberations that discuss privileged, proprietary, confidential or trade-secret information, or information exempt from disclosure under federal or State law.","Section 25 reconciles the Freedom of Information Act Section 7 exemptions, including the 7(1)(g) trade-secret and commercial-information exemption, before and after P.A. 104-300; the text provided is truncated at this point."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that this is a purely technical omnibus revisory measure that disclaims substantive effect. Its gaming relevance is limited to restating existing confidentiality protections, such as the Gaming Board closed-deliberation exception and the FOIA trade-secret exemption, rather than changing tax, licensing or wagering rules.","watch":"Because the text provided is truncated, review the full enrolled act for any combined or renumbered sections of the Sports Wagering Act, Illinois Gambling Act or Video Gaming Act, and confirm that the reconciled versions accurately reflect the intervening 2025 Public Acts, including any sports-wagering tax changes, with no inadvertent drafting changes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":4581072,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:16.614Z"},"IN-HB1052":{"summary":"HEA 1052 (P.L. 153-2026) is an omnibus administrative bill amending Indiana's horse racing, charity gaming, casino, alcohol and tobacco statutes, mostly effective July 1, 2026. For gaming clients, the key provisions are a new IC 4-33-10-7, which lets the Indiana Gaming Commission levy a $100,000 civil penalty on anyone who knowingly runs an online dual-currency sweepstakes game in Indiana, and amended voluntary exclusion program language in IC 4-33-4-3 that expressly covers sports wagering certificate holders under IC 4-38. It also overhauls racing commission discipline, routing appeals of steward and judge sanctions to the Office of Administrative Law Proceedings.","mechanics":["Sweepstakes (Sec. 15, new IC 4-33-10-7): 'sweepstakes game' means an Internet game, contest or promotion that meets four tests. It is accessible by phone or computer, uses a dual- or multi-currency system exchangeable for cash prizes or cash equivalents (or a chance to win them), and simulates lottery or casino-style gaming, including slots, video poker, table games, bingo or sports wagering. State lottery products and peer-to-peer skill-based poker are excluded.","Sweepstakes penalty: the Gaming Commission may levy a $100,000 civil penalty against an operator or individual who knowingly uses the Internet to conduct a sweepstakes game in Indiana or in a transaction directly involving a person located in Indiana. The text creates no criminal offense and no express supplier, payment-processor or affiliate liability.","Voluntary exclusion (Sec. 14, IC 4-33-4-3(b)): commission rules must cover the following for IC 4-38 sports wagering certificate holders as well as casino facilities. Participants agree to refrain from placing wagers with those holders, and the confidential exclusion list may be shared with them for enforcement. Holders must make all reasonable attempts to stop direct marketing to participants and may not cash participants' checks or extend them credit, though pre-enrollment debts remain collectible.","Racing integrity and licensing (Secs. 2–12): the bill adds a 'judge' definition covering licensed judges and stewards. It adds a new license-denial ground for material misrepresentation of Indiana owned, sired or bred status (IC 4-31-6-6(b)(20)). It reworks the bar on wagering by commission members, senior staff, judges and their spouses at IC 4-35 racinos (Class C infraction, or Class A misdemeanor for a repeat within 5 years).","Racing discipline (IC 4-31-13-2): stewards and judges may impose civil penalties up to $5,000, license suspensions up to 3 years, and exclusions from track premises. Appeals of these sanctions and of breath-test sanctions go to the Office of Administrative Law Proceedings under IC 4-15-10.5 and IC 4-21.5, with the commission bearing a preponderance burden.","Charity gaming (Sec. 13, IC 4-32.3-5-8): meals and social events for workers at charity gaming events are now limited by an 'unreasonable expense' test rather than a 'significant inducement' test. Remaining sections cover alcohol permits and tobacco certificates and are unrelated to gaming."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"CA AB 831 likewise targets online dual-currency sweepstakes. Indiana's approach is narrower: a single flat $100,000 civil penalty enforced by the Gaming Commission against the operator or individual, with no new crime and no express reach to suppliers or payment intermediaries, and with a carve-out for peer-to-peer skill-based poker. Unlike CT HB05229, which layers new consumer-protection duties such as withdrawal rules, customer service lines and AI-targeting limits onto gaming licensees, HB1052's sportsbook-facing change is limited to tying IC 4-38 certificate holders into the casino voluntary exclusion program. Unlike the stand-alone sports betting authorization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), this is an enacted cleanup bill in which gaming provisions sit alongside alcohol and tobacco amendments.","watch":"Watch how the Gaming Commission defines and enforces 'knowingly' and 'transaction directly involving a person located in Indiana' under IC 4-33-10-7, and whether it treats the $100,000 penalty as per violation. Also watch for implementing rules under IC 4-33-4-3 that could tighten sportsbook duties on marketing suppression, credit and data handling for self-excluded patrons.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":89257,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:27.659Z"},"IN-HB1078":{"summary":"HB 1078 amends IC 4-30 to let the Indiana lottery commission sell draw games and eInstant games over the Internet, reversing the current statutory ban on digital draw and scratch-off sales. It keeps the bans on video lottery terminals, lottery couriers, online casino-style games, and digital lottery games that simulate slot machines. It also requires geolocation and age verification rules, a retailer incentive program, and a commission-run voluntary exclusion program for digital lottery purchases.","mechanics":["New IC 4-30-3-20(c) authorizes Internet sales of draw games and eInstant games. An eInstant game is a digital game that, on reveal of numbers, symbols or other insignia, immediately shows whether the player won (IC 4-30-2-3.8).","Amended IC 4-30-3-20(b) still requires express legislative authority for the following, and the ban applies to both the lottery commission and the Indiana Gaming Commission: video lottery terminals; digital lottery games simulating slots (spinning reels, pull mechanism, spinning drum, varied pay lines); lottery courier services; and Internet sales of poker, roulette, slots or blackjack. The exemption for IC 4-38 sports wagering is retained.","IC 4-30-3-7(b), added by floor amendment, requires rules obliging the digital channel contractor to geolocate purchasers and refuse out-of-state payments. It also requires verification that buyers are 18 or older, using commercially reasonable standards, evidence more reliable than self-attestation, and secure data handling.","New IC 4-30-3-17.5 requires a retailer incentive program built with the digital contractor. The commission must hold an annual public meeting to review the impact of online sales on retailers, assess whether the program is working, and recommend changes if it is not.","New IC 4-30-3-21 requires rules for a voluntary exclusion program covering digital lottery games. Under it, the contractor must make reasonable attempts to stop direct marketing to participants and may not accept payment from or extend credit to them, though pre-existing debts remain collectible. The exclusion list is confidential but may be shared with channel operators, or with other entities on request and commission approval. The commission may set fines, fees or civil penalties by rule.","Effective July 1, 2026. The Public Policy Committee reported the bill 9-3, and it was amended on second reading January 13, 2026."],"issue_tags":["legalize","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills authorizes online lottery (iLottery) sales; most authorize or regulate sports wagering, for example AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569. The closest is IL HB3276, which creates a lottery sports wagering pilot, whereas Indiana's bill expressly keeps lottery products separate from IC 4-38 sports wagering. Its responsible-gaming elements (self-exclusion, a halt to marketing to excluded persons, verification stronger than self-attestation) parallel consumer-protection themes in CT HB05229. Unusually, it pairs iLottery authorization with an explicit statutory bar on slot-like digital lottery games and a retailer-protection program.","watch":"Watch how the commission's rules draw the line between permitted eInstant games and the prohibited slot-simulating digital games under amended IC 4-30-3-20(b)(2), since a loose line could create a state-run, iGaming-like competitor for operators' online wallet share; also watch whether the lottery's exclusion list is coordinated with the Indiana Gaming Commission's existing exclusion programs.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":14227,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:31.485Z"},"KS-HB2007":{"summary":"HB 2007 is Kansas's annual reconciliation bill. It merges conflicting 2025 amendments made to the same statutes by separate enacted bills into a single version of each section and repeals the duplicate versions. The provisions in the supplied text cover distinctive license plates, resident-agent resignation, foreign-national contributions to constitutional-amendment campaigns, adult care home definitions, and background checks for adult care workers; none addresses sports betting or gaming.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (K.S.A. 8-1,141): from January 1, 2025, any distinctive license plate may be personalized for double the K.S.A. 8-132(d) fee. It keeps the 250-plate minimum for issuance, the sponsor development payment of up to $5,000, and a $4,000 transfer once 100 paid orders are received for certain plates.","Sec. 2 (K.S.A. 17-7929): a resident agent may resign without naming a successor, effective 30 days after filing. The covered entity then has 60 days to designate a new agent or its organizing documents are forfeited, and service of process then goes to the secretary of state.","Sec. 3 (K.S.A. 25-4180): bars foreign-national contributions to campaigns for or against constitutional amendments. It requires donor certifications (including a $100,000 four-year threshold), allows statutory damages up to twice the prohibited amount, and imposes late-filing penalties of $10 per day capped at $300.","Secs. 4-5 (K.S.A. 39-923, 39-2009): reconcile adult care home definitions and the rules on criminal-history disqualification, waivers and fingerprinting for workers, including a fee of up to $19 for criminal history records.","The title also reconciles K.S.A. 45-229 (open-records exception review), 59-2946, 59-29b46, 72-5170 and 77-440, but those sections fall outside the supplied text, which is truncated in Sec. 5."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What is unusual is that this is a purely technical Kansas reconciliation vehicle, with no substantive gaming policy, that lands in a sports-betting tracker probably because of its omnibus scope. The only conceivable connection is its reconciliation of K.S.A. 45-229, the statute governing legislative review of open-records exceptions, which can include confidentiality protections for gaming-related records; the supplied text does not reach that section, so no such effect can be confirmed.","watch":"Check the full enrolled text of the K.S.A. 45-229 reconciliation (not included in the supplied excerpt) to confirm that no open-records exemption protecting confidential sports-wagering or lottery-gaming operator data was altered or allowed to lapse; otherwise this bill does not affect operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":150338,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:32.783Z"},"IN-HB1092":{"summary":"HEA 1092 is mainly a family and juvenile law bill covering paternity, support payment channels, adoption petitions, child caring institution licensing and background checks, and DCS assessments. Section 1 amends Indiana's sports wagering child support intercept statute, IC 4-38-11-1. It extends the check-and-withhold duty, which previously applied only to certificate holders paying $600+ in-person sports wagering winnings, to vendors (e.g., online/mobile sports wagering operators) whenever a payout triggers a federal Form W-2G filing obligation.","mechanics":["Coverage (IC 4-38-11-1(a)-(b)): the bureau must supply child support delinquency information to vendors as well as certificate holders. Before disbursing a payout, the payer must check whether the claimant is delinquent in child support.","Certificate holders keep the existing trigger: a sports wagering payout of $600 or more claimed in person at the facility.","New vendor trigger: any payout that requires the vendor to file IRS Form W-2G or a substantially equivalent form. The trigger is tied to the federal reporting threshold, not a fixed state dollar figure.","If the claimant is delinquent (subsection (c)), the payer may keep an administrative fee of the lesser of 3% of the amount withheld or $100. The payer must then: withhold the delinquent amount; transmit it to the bureau with the obligor's name, address, SSN, case identifier, payment date and amount, and the payer's name and location; and issue a receipt in the bureau's prescribed form.","The bureau notifies the obligor and holds withheld funds for 10 business days before applying them. Withheld support and the fee take priority over all secured and unsecured claims on winnings except required federal and state tax withholding.","Effective July 1, 2026. The remaining sections (paternity orders, payment through a circuit court clerk or state-approved payment facility, adoption petition disclosures, child caring institution background checks and license denial grounds, DCS school interviews) do not involve gaming."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills deal with child support intercepts on wagering payouts. AK HB145/SB194, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, and GA HB 686 create new licensing and tax frameworks. The Illinois bills (e.g., HB2878, HB3159, HB5142) adjust licensing fees, taxes, or definitions within an existing regime. Unusually, this bill adds an operational compliance duty for online sports wagering vendors inside a child-welfare omnibus. It uses the federal W-2G trigger instead of a state dollar threshold, so the intercept duty moves with any change in federal reporting thresholds.","watch":"Online and mobile sports wagering vendors need payout-flow integration with the child support bureau's delinquency data by July 1, 2026, covering the pre-disbursement check, withholding, data transmission including SSNs, and receipt issuance on every W-2G-triggering payout, along with any bureau guidance on form, timing, and enforcement.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":29306,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:35.741Z"},"KS-HB2063":{"summary":"HB2063 creates a Kansas state conservation fund financed by a recurring $60 million annual transfer from the state general fund. Each July 15 the balance is split three ways: 50% to a working lands conservation fund run by the Kansas Department of Agriculture's division of conservation, 25% to a wildlife conservation fund run by the Secretary of Wildlife and Parks, and the remainder to a Kansas outdoors fund run by the parks division. The bill contains no gaming, wagering or sports-betting provisions of any kind.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(b): $60,000,000 is transferred from the state general fund to the state conservation fund on July 1, 2025 and each July 1 thereafter. The fund is exempt from K.S.A. 75-3722, 75-3725a and 75-3726a, and it may not supplant existing appropriations.","Sec. 1(c)-(e): on each July 15, 50% of the balance goes to the working lands conservation fund, 25% to the wildlife conservation fund, and the remaining balance to the Kansas outdoors fund.","Sec. 1(a)(2): moneys may not fund any fee simple land acquisition by the federal or state government. Sec. 3(g) separately says state-agency fee simple acquisitions are subject to K.S.A. 32-833 at the time of acquisition.","Secs. 2-4: each fund runs a grant program open to public entities, tribal nations, conservation districts, recreation commissions and nonprofits. Applications that capture nonstate matching funds get priority, and the legislature intends moneys to be obligated within 12 months.","Each administering agency must report to the governor and legislature by December 1 annually, covering expenditures and the disposition of applications.","Sec. 5: the act takes effect on publication in the statute book. The bill was stricken from the House calendar under Rule 1507 on 2026-02-19."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the listed gaming bills, HB2063 does not touch wagering, licensing, taxation of gaming revenue or advertising. Several of those bills earmark gaming revenue for non-gaming purposes, such as IL HB3159 (2% of sports wagering adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS) and IA SSB 1157 (gambling revenue for tourism programs). HB2063 instead funds conservation entirely through a fixed general fund transfer, with no gaming revenue source. Its only plausible connection to the tracked issue set is as a competing claimant on general fund dollars, and even that is not stated in the text.","watch":"The bill is effectively dead after being stricken under Rule 1507 and has no direct operator impact, but an operator lawyer should watch any revived version or floor amendment that tries to replace the $60 million general fund transfer with a diversion of Kansas sports wagering or lottery revenues.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":15698,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:41.650Z"},"KS-HB2146":{"summary":"HB2146 amends K.S.A. 74-8711 to double the annual cap on transfers from the Kansas lottery operating fund to two KDADS mental-health funds, the community crisis stabilization centers fund and the clubhouse model program fund, from $8,000,000 to $16,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and each year after. The transfers come only from certified net profits on lottery tickets sold through lottery ticket vending machines. The existing sports wagering revenue allocations in subsections (f) through (h) are restated without change.","mechanics":["Sec. 1, 74-8711(e)(2): the aggregate cap on vending-machine net-profit transfers rises from $8,000,000 to $16,000,000 for FY2026 and each fiscal year thereafter. Prior caps of $9M (FY2024) and $10M (FY2025) are left in place.","74-8711(e)(1), unchanged: the lottery director certifies vending-machine net profits monthly by the 10th, split 75% to the community crisis stabilization centers fund and 25% to the clubhouse model program fund.","74-8711(d), unchanged: the state gaming revenues fund receives the greater of (1) fund moneys in excess of the (c)(1)-(c)(6) purposes or (2) a floor of 30% of monthly non-pull-tab sales and 20% of pull-tab sales, net of estimated returns. A larger (e) transfer could therefore shrink the 'excess' calculation.","74-8711(f)-(h), unchanged: from sports wagering revenues in the lottery operating fund, the first $750,000 goes to the white collar crime fund. Of the remainder, 2% goes to the problem gambling and addictions grant fund and 80% to the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund.","Sec. 3: the act takes effect on publication in the statute book. Requested on behalf of the Kansas Club House Coalition."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The bill is narrow: it reallocates lottery vending-machine profits to mental-health programs rather than changing any gaming tax rate or regulatory rule. Its text also restates, but does not alter, Kansas's sports wagering revenue waterfall, under which 80% of the post-$750,000 remainder goes to the stadium-attraction fund (APEX).","watch":"Although the sports wagering allocations in 74-8711(f)-(h) are untouched, any amendment while this section is open could be used to redirect sports wagering revenue (for example, away from the attracting professional sports fund or toward a higher problem-gambling share), so operators should monitor committee substitutes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7148,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:46.178Z"},"KS-HB2434":{"summary":"Substitute for HB 2434 is Kansas's omnibus supplemental and biennial appropriations bill, adjusting FY2026 amounts and appropriating FY2027 (and some FY2028) funds for state agencies, authorizing capital improvement projects, fees and fund transfers. The portion of text provided covers fee-funded licensing boards, the Legislature, the Governor and the Attorney General. None of it contains any sports-wagering or gaming provision.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 makes FY2026–FY2028 appropriations and exempts the act from K.S.A. 75-6702(a) and the appropriations from K.S.A. 46-155.","Secs. 2–15 adjust FY2026/FY2027 expenditure limits on fee funds of professional licensing boards, e.g. the Bank Commissioner fee fund rises from $13,711,453 to $13,986,695 and the Pharmacy fee fund falls from $3,607,526 to $3,205,483.","Secs. 16–24 lapse FY2026 balances and appropriate FY2027 funds for the Public Disclosure Commission, the Legislature and the Governor, including $25,124,413 for domestic violence prevention grants.","Secs. 25–26 lapse $10,000,000 from the AG's safe and secure firearm detection program, transfer $460,593 from the Kansas endowment for youth fund to the tobacco MSA compliance fund, and set FY2027 AG appropriations.","The title amends K.S.A. 2-223, 12-1775a, 12-5256, 74-50,107, 74-99b34, 76-775, 76-7,107, 79-2989, 79-3425i, 79-34,171 and 82a-955, but the amendment text is not in the excerpt provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the substantive gaming bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194 and GA HB 686 (authorizing and taxing mobile sports wagering), CT HB05229 (advertising and withdrawal rules) and IL HB5142/HB5059 (prediction markets), KS HB2434 is a general budget act with no wagering policy content in the text provided. The closest analogue on the list is DC B26-0034, another fiscal and contract measure listed only because of overlapping keyword matches. Any gaming relevance would come from agency line items in later sections, such as the Racing and Gaming Commission, the Lottery or sports-wagering-funded accounts, which are not in this excerpt.","watch":"Review the later, unprovided sections for any transfers from, or expenditure limits on, the Kansas sports wagering revenues fund, the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund, or Racing and Gaming Commission and Lottery fee funds, since budget provisos like these can quietly redirect wagering revenue or change regulator funding.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":300535,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:51.634Z"},"KS-SB125":{"summary":"SB 125 is Kansas's omnibus appropriations act. It makes supplemental FY2025 appropriations and FY2026–FY2027 appropriations for state agencies, authorizes capital improvement projects, fees and fund transfers, and pays small claims against the state. The Legislature overrode selected line-item vetoes on 2025-04-11. The excerpt provided (through Sec. 28) covers only professional licensing boards and claims and contains no wagering provisions. Gaming relevance turns on the title's amendment of K.S.A. 74-8711 and 79-4804, whose amended text is not in the excerpt.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(c) designates the FY2026 provisions as the omnibus appropriation act of 2025 and the omnibus reconciliation spending limit bill under K.S.A. 75-6702(a).","Secs. 2–5 pay claims against the state: inmate lost-property claims of $30.04 to $100, and $3,100 each from KDADS and KDHE to Hillside Village, LLC for delayed HCBS eligibility processing; Sec. 5(b) requires a written release before payment.","Secs. 6–28 set fee-fund expenditure caps for FY2026 and FY2027 for regulatory boards, for example the bank commissioner fee fund at $13,667,399 and $13,711,453, with official hospitality caps and budget-director-gated litigation funds.","Sec. 12(b) authorizes a barbering examination fee of up to $150 for FY2026–FY2027.","The title amends K.S.A. 74-8711 and 79-4804, statutes tied to Kansas lottery and gaming revenue funds, but the amended text is outside the provided excerpt, so any change to gaming revenue transfers cannot be confirmed here."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the substantive sports wagering bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and IL HB3159, SB 125 neither authorizes, taxes nor regulates wagering. It is a general budget bill. The closest analogue is IA SSB 1157, which pairs appropriations with gaming tax rates and the use of gaming revenue. In SB 125, by contrast, any gaming touchpoint is limited to the statutory amendments of K.S.A. 74-8711 and 79-4804 listed in the title, plus whatever appropriations to gaming-related agencies appear later in the act.","watch":"Pull the full enrolled text of the K.S.A. 74-8711 and 79-4804 amendments and the Kansas Lottery and Racing and Gaming Commission appropriation sections, including any overridden line items, to see whether lottery or gaming revenue transfers or problem-gambling funding that could affect sports-wagering revenue allocations have been redirected.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":392639,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:51.963Z"},"KS-SB303":{"summary":"House Substitute for SB 303 imposes a new 2% privilege tax on the total amount of every sports wager placed with a Kansas lottery gaming facility manager. The tax is paid by the bettor but collected and remitted by the facility manager. It also narrows certain sales tax exemptions, including the lottery ticket exemption, and routes the resulting revenue into a new property tax relief fund. That fund backfills the state school district finance fund so the statewide 20-mill school levy under K.S.A. 72-5142(b) can be reduced.","mechanics":["New Sec. 3(a)-(b): a 2% privilege tax is imposed 'upon the total amount wagered' (handle, not revenue) on each sports wager placed with a lottery gaming facility manager. The bettor bears the tax, and the manager must collect the full amount from the bettor.","New Sec. 3(c): managers must remit monthly, by the 25th of the following month, with a true report on a Department of Revenue form covering all sports wagers accepted that month.","New Sec. 3(d)-(e): proceeds go to the new property tax relief fund. Refunds are paid from a new sports wagering privilege tax refund fund replenished from collections. The director of taxation gets subpoena and records-examination power, and sales tax assessment, collection, penalty and appeal provisions (K.S.A. 79-3610 to 79-3617, 75-5133) apply.","Sec. 5 amends K.S.A. 79-3606 effective July 1, 2026 to discontinue certain sales tax exemptions. Subsection (ee) appears to limit the lottery ticket exemption to multistate lottery association products where imposing sales tax is prohibited, but the strike/insert markup is not visible in the text provided, so the extent of the change should be confirmed.","New Secs. 1-2 and Sec. 4: starting after tax year 2027, the budget director certifies the new sales tax revenue, and the revenue secretary computes a reduced school mill rate (currently 20 mills for 2025-26 and 2026-27), publishing it by February 1. Certified amounts are transferred to the state school district finance fund."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike IL HB3159, which adds 2% of adjusted gross receipts earmarked for fire and EMS, Kansas levies 2% on gross handle and places the legal incidence on the bettor rather than the operator. That makes it closer in structure to Illinois' per-wager tax, which IL HB5143 would sunset on July 1, 2026. Like ME LD1304, it uses gaming revenue to fund property tax relief, but by statute rather than constitutional amendment. It sits apart from the authorization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308, MS HB 4074, NE LB421/LB438) because it layers a new consumer-facing levy onto an existing market rather than setting operator tax rates.","watch":"Watch whether the tax reaches online wagers taken by platform providers operating under a lottery gaming facility manager's license, since the text names only facility managers. Also watch how a 2% handle tax collected from bettors interacts with pricing, odds, promotional and free bets, voided-wager refunds, and the manager's monthly collection and remittance liability.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":159242,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:00.048Z"},"KS-SB315":{"summary":"Substitute for SB 315 is Kansas's omnibus appropriations bill. It makes supplemental FY2026 appropriations and lapses, sets FY2027 (and later) appropriations and expenditure limitations for state agencies, and authorizes capital improvement projects, fees and fund transfers. The portion of the text provided covers fee-funded licensing boards, the Legislature, the Governor and the Attorney General, and contains no sports-betting or gaming provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 1: Makes FY2026–FY2028 appropriations and authorizes capital projects. It exempts the act from K.S.A. 75-6702(a) and the appropriations from K.S.A. 46-155.","Secs. 2–15: Adjust fee-fund expenditure limitations for licensing boards. Examples: State Bank Commissioner raised from $13,711,453 to $13,986,695; Board of Pharmacy cut from $3,607,526 to $3,205,483.","Secs. 16–22: Lapse FY2026 legislative-branch funds, including $2,129,006 from Legislature operations, and set FY2027 amounts, including $24,099,428 for Legislature operations.","Secs. 23–24: Lapse and reappropriate Governor's office accounts, including $25,124,413 for domestic violence prevention grants in FY2027.","Secs. 25–26: Lapse $10,000,000 from the Attorney General's safe and secure firearm detection program. They also transfer $460,593 from the Kansas endowment for youth fund to the tobacco MSA compliance fund and set AG fund appropriations.","Amends K.S.A. 2025 Supp. 2-223, 12-1775a, 12-5256, 74-50,107, 74-99b34, 76-775, 76-7,107, 79-2989, 79-3425i, 79-34,171 and 82a-955. The supplied text does not show the substance of these amendments."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the listed substantive gaming bills, this is a general state budget act rather than gaming policy legislation. Those bills include GA HB 686 (authorizing and taxing sports betting), AK HB145/SB194 (mobile wagering tax), IL HB5143 (ending the per-wager tax) and IL HB5142 (folding prediction markets into the definition of sports wagering). Its closest analogue on the list is DC B26-0034, a fiscal and contract authorization measure with no wagering content. Any gaming relevance would come only from budget lines for gaming-related agencies or funds, and the provided excerpt does not reach those.","watch":"Review the later, unreproduced sections for any appropriations, expenditure limits or transfers touching the Kansas Racing and Gaming Commission, the Kansas Lottery, or funds fed by sports-wagering revenue, since budget-bill transfers can quietly redirect wagering revenue or change regulator funding.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":300452,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:01.630Z"},"KS-SB502":{"summary":"SB502 is a one-operative-section bill that lets the Kansas Racing and Gaming Commission adopt a specific package of proposed permanent sports wagering regulations under K.S.A. 2025 Supp. 77-441. The bill itself contains no substantive rules. It ratifies the adoption of regulations identified in an economic impact statement that the Division of Budget approved on February 13, 2025, and takes effect on publication in the Kansas Register.","mechanics":["Section 1 authorizes the Commission to adopt proposed permanent rules K.A.R. 112-201-1 through 112-201-23, omitting 112-201-14.","Section 1 also covers K.A.R. 112-202-1 and 112-202-2, 112-203-1 through 112-203-8, and 112-204-1 through 112-204-7.","The authority rests on K.S.A. 2025 Supp. 77-441, and the covered regulations are those described in the Division of Budget economic impact statement approved February 13, 2025.","Section 2 makes the act effective on publication in the Kansas Register rather than the statute book, so it takes effect quickly.","The bill sets no tax rates, license fees, thresholds or enforcement provisions of its own. All substance sits in the referenced K.A.R. sections, which are not reproduced in the bill."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, which would create a sports wagering framework with licensing and tax terms, KS SB502 is a purely procedural legislative sign-off on regulations the Commission has already drafted. It is also unlike the Illinois bills that amend the Sports Wagering Act directly, such as HB2878 and HB5034 on license fees or HB5142 on prediction markets. SB502 changes no statutory terms and only clears the way for agency rules. Its unusual feature is the requirement that the legislature approve specific K.A.R. numbers before the agency can adopt them.","watch":"Get the text of the listed K.A.R. 112-201 through 112-204 regulations and the February 13, 2025 economic impact statement, note why 112-201-14 is left out, and be ready for those rules to bind operators as soon as the act is published in the Kansas Register.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1351,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:05.864Z"},"KY-HB869":{"summary":"HB 869 (Acts Ch. 198, signed April 27, 2026, with an emergency clause) is a Kentucky fiscal and economic-development tax bill. It creates a new refundable economic development income tax credit, re-orders the KRS 141.0205 credit stack, and adds a 'heritage county' concept (county population ranking of 97 or more) that loosens thresholds and enlarges benefits across several incentive programs. The portion of the text provided contains no provisions on sports wagering, pari-mutuel wagering or gaming, though the text is truncated partway through Section 9.","mechanics":["Section 1 creates a refundable, nontransferable economic development credit against KRS 141.020, 141.040 and 141.0401 tax for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2026. It is capped at $4,000,000 per year for all approved companies, of which no more than $1,000,000 may go to wages in non-heritage counties. Claim data is expressly not confidential.","Section 2 amends the KRS 141.0205 ordering rules to add the new credit and a certified mixed-use development credit (Section 31) as refundable credits. It also adds an alternative jet fuel producer credit (Section 42) as a nonrefundable credit.","Sections 4-5 change the Bluegrass State Skills Corporation skills training investment credit. The per-trainee cap rises from $2,000 to $4,500 in heritage counties and is set at $3,500 elsewhere. The cap remains $500,000 per company per fiscal year and $2.5M in aggregate, the training completion window extends from one to three years, and the minimum base wage is set at 200% (heritage) or 300% of the federal minimum wage.","Sections 6-7 raise the Kentucky Product Development Initiative maximum award to $2.5M for heritage-county projects, versus $2M elsewhere.","Section 9 lowers the jobs retention eligibility thresholds for heritage-county projects to 250 full-time employees and $25M in eligible costs, versus 1,000 employees and $100M elsewhere.","An emergency clause means the act is effective on signature."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The listed bills are gaming-specific. AK HB145/SB194, HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569 and GA HB686 would authorize and tax sports wagering, IA SF 605 addresses withholding on sports wagering winnings, and IL HB3159 would add a 2% adjusted gross receipts levy. KY HB869 does none of these in the text provided; it is a general business-incentive tax bill built around income tax credits and county-tiered economic development benefits. Nothing comparable is listed, and its distinctive feature is the heritage-county tiering applied across multiple incentive programs.","watch":"Because the supplied text is truncated, an operator's counsel should review the remaining sections for any amendment to Kentucky's wagering excise or pari-mutuel tax provisions or fund allocations, which would take effect immediately under the emergency clause.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":377977,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:15.357Z"},"LA-HB1":{"summary":"HB1 is Louisiana's general appropriations act for Fiscal Year 2026-2027, effective July 1, 2026, funding executive branch agencies, schools, roads and institutions. Gaming appears only as budget units, including appropriations for the regulators and a pass-through of sports wagering revenue to local governments. No substantive gaming or sports-betting policy is changed in the text provided.","mechanics":["Gaming-related budget units listed in the table of contents: 01-254 Louisiana State Racing Commission; 04-141 Office of the Attorney General (Gaming program); 08-419 Office of State Police (Gaming Enforcement Program); 08-423 Louisiana Gaming Control Board.","Schedule 20 includes 20-924 Video Draw Poker – Local Government Aid and 20-926 Sports Wagering Local Allocation Fund, which pass gaming revenue through to local governments. Dollar amounts are not in the excerpt provided.","Section 2.A: if statutorily dedicated or self-generated revenues, which would include gaming fee and tax dedications, fall short, appropriations are reduced accordingly. Increases require approval by the commissioner of administration and the Joint Legislative Committee on the Budget.","Section 6.A bars transfers between departments or schedules unless expressly provided. Transfers within a department require approval by the commissioner of administration and the Joint Legislative Committee on the Budget.","Section 5.A: program descriptions and performance information are not enacted into law."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The notable feature is that Louisiana runs sports wagering proceeds through a dedicated budget unit, 20-926 Sports Wagering Local Allocation Fund, alongside a separate video draw poker local aid line. That makes the annual appropriation the place where local distribution of sports-betting revenue is actually funded.","watch":"Check the full-text figures for the Gaming Control Board, the State Police Gaming Enforcement Program and the Sports Wagering Local Allocation Fund, since regulator funding levels and local revenue distributions affect enforcement capacity and political pressure on future tax or fee changes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":269140,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:16.081Z"},"LA-SB354":{"summary":"SB354 amends Louisiana's sports wagering chapter (Title 27, Chapter 11) to strike \"proposition bets\" from the list of included wager types in the R.S. 27:602(23) definition of \"sports wager.\" It adds definitions of \"proposition bet\" and \"sports micro-bet\" and adds both to the R.S. 27:608(C) list of wagers that no operator may accept or pay. The effect is a flat ban on all prop bets, including player and in-game props, for every licensed operator, retail and mobile alike.","mechanics":["R.S. 27:602(23): deletes \"proposition bets\" from the non-exhaustive list of permitted sports wager types. Single-game, teaser, parlay, over-under, moneyline, pools, exchange, in-game, in-play and straight bets remain.","New R.S. 27:602(29): defines a \"proposition bet\" or \"prop bet\" as a side wager on a part of a sport or athletic event that does not concern the event's final outcome.","New R.S. 27:602(30): defines a \"sports micro-bet\" as a proposition bet wagered live, while the event is ongoing, on the outcome of a play or action in the event.","New R.S. 27:608(C)(5) and (6): no operator may accept or pay any wager on a sports micro-bet or on any proposition bet.","Effective August 1, 2026. The bill has a fiscal note flagging a possible decrease in general fund revenue. No new penalty provisions are added; enforcement runs through existing Chapter 11 limitations and Gaming Control Board authority."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization and tax bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) or the licensing-fee bills (IL HB2878/HB5034), LA SB354 narrows an existing legal market by removing an entire product category. That makes it the most restrictive market-product bill on the list. CT HB05229 targets AI-driven bet targeting and other consumer-protection rules rather than banning bet types outright. IL HB5142, which pulls sports prediction markets into the definition of sports wagering, is the only other listed bill that reshapes the definition of a sports wager, but it expands the definition rather than contracting it. An outright ban on all props, rather than only college-player props or micro-bets, is unusually broad among recent state proposals.","watch":"Watch the very broad \"does not concern the final outcome\" definition, which on its face could sweep in spreads-by-period, first-half, totals-adjacent and same-game parlay legs as well as player props, and press for amendments narrowing it to player or collegiate props or for a delayed effective date ahead of August 1, 2026.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4045,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:16.143Z"},"LA-HB883":{"summary":"Act 182 of 2026 rewrites Louisiana's gambling-by-computer crime (R.S. 14:90.3) to explicitly cover dual-currency sweepstakes-style games, sharply raises operator penalties, and creates new criminal exposure for anyone who knowingly supports or facilitates illegal online gambling, including platform providers, geolocation providers, content suppliers, promoters, endorsers, media affiliates, and merchant payment processors. New R.S. 27:19.1 and 19.2 give the attorney general, and optionally the Gaming Control Board chairman, a cease-and-desist process with a public AG blacklist, subpoena power, and broad civil injunctive remedies, including ex parte takedown and payment-blocking orders. It takes effect August 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Definition (14:90.3(B)): any internet or mobile game, contest, or promotion using a dual-currency system redeemable for cash, prizes, or cash equivalents that simulates gambling is gambling by computer, which squarely targets sweepstakes casinos.","Penalties: clients face up to $500 and/or 6 months (D). Operators face up to $100,000 (raised from $20,000) and/or 5 years (E). Knowing facilitators, including licensed entities, applicants, geolocation providers, content suppliers, promoters, endorsers, and media affiliates, face up to $20,000 and/or 5 years, with fines doubled where a person under 21 is involved (F). Each wager and each promotion is a separate violation (I), and forfeiture of profits and gross receipts is mandatory (J).","Payment processors and platform providers (G, H) are barred from processing or transmitting illegal gambling transactions or information. They are deemed to have constructive knowledge of every person and URL on the AG's list. Running an ad for a listed site is prima facie evidence of support. Good-faith blocking carries tort immunity. Federally insured banks and payment card networks are excluded (R), and a 47 U.S.C. 230 savings clause applies (Q).","Cease and desist (27:19.1): the notice gives a 10-day cure period, is served under the Code of Civil Procedure, and is admissible as prima facie evidence of knowledge. The AG maintains a public listing of noticed operators, URLs, platforms, and processors. Out-of-state site operators who know or should know their content reaches Louisiana submit to state jurisdiction. The AG gets subpoena and injunction authority, and the statute calls for liberal construction.","Civil remedies (27:19.2): courts may order TROs and injunctions, site removal, freezing of bank and credit accounts, and cutting off players' access. Violation is conclusively irreparable harm. Ex parte injunctions are available against platforms and processors, with a hearing within 30 days. Violating an order costs $50,000 for the first violation and $100,000 for each subsequent day or transaction.","Carve-outs: licensed sports wagering under Title 27 Ch. 10 or Title 47 Ch. 10 (P), fantasy sports under R.S. 27:302 (O), on-premises licensed gaming (K, L), securities and commodities exchange transactions (M), and ordinary-course ISPs and service providers unless their primary purpose is gambling (N)."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","integrity","advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308/SB1569, authorize and tax legal sports wagering, while Louisiana's HB883 is purely an enforcement statute aimed at the unlicensed and sweepstakes market. It is closest to FL H0189 and FL S1580, which also tighten illegal-gaming and internet-gambling penalties. HB883 goes further by imposing liability on third-party intermediaries such as payment processors, platforms, geolocation vendors, and endorsers, and by tying it to an AG blacklist with constructive-knowledge and ex parte takedown tools. Unlike IL HB5142, it does not address prediction markets, and the 14:90.3(M) exchange carve-out may be argued to shield exchange-traded event contracts.","watch":"Licensed operators and their vendors, affiliates, and geolocation and content suppliers should screen every partner against the AG's public cease-and-desist list. Subsection F expressly reaches 'licensed entities' that knowingly support or facilitate illegal online gambling, with per-promotion violations, mandatory forfeiture, and doubled fines where under-21 users are involved.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":20174,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:21.735Z"},"LA-SB445":{"summary":"SB445 adds two new revenue streams to the Louisiana Early Childhood Education Fund (R.S. 17:407.30): year-end unspent, non-dedicated Department of Education cash balances swept in under R.S. 39:82(J), and an annual state general fund top-up of up to $30 million beginning July 1, 2027. It also removes the \"subject to legislative appropriation\" condition on BESE awards from the fund. The bill does not amend the existing sports wagering, fantasy sports, or casino tax dedications to the fund. Those dedications are noted only in the digest as present law.","mechanics":["R.S. 39:82(J) is rewritten to direct the treasurer to deposit all year-end cash balances from legislative or Interim Emergency Board appropriations to the Department of Education into the fund. Dedicated funds and balances with a bona fide liability on the last day of the fiscal year are excluded. The rewrite replaces the existing Office of Behavioral Health retention language.","Beginning July 1, 2027, the treasurer must transfer from the general fund each year the difference between $30 million and the fund's unencumbered balance at the start of the prior fiscal year, capped at $30 million per year. These monies are to be used to match one-to-one grants approved by BESE.","The deposits in R.S. 17:407.30(B) remain subordinate to the Bond Security and Redemption Fund requirements under La. Const. Art. VII, §9(B).","Section (D)(1)(a) deletes \"Subject to legislative appropriation,\" so BESE would award fund monies annually without a separate appropriation. The one-to-one nonstate, nonfederal local match requirement for CCAP-eligible slots in Type III centers is retained.","Existing fund sources are untouched, according to the digest. These include casino gaming revenue (R.S. 27:270), sports wagering tax (R.S. 27:625), and fantasy sports tax (R.S. 27:316).","The bill takes effect on the governor's signature or on lapse of time for gubernatorial action. A fiscal note flags a general fund expenditure."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual in that it shores up a fund that already receives sports wagering and fantasy sports tax dedications by adding general fund and agency-sweep money, instead of changing gaming tax rates or dedications. It also makes the fund self-appropriating, which would let BESE spend gaming-derived deposits without annual legislative appropriation.","watch":"Because the bill guarantees a $30 million general fund backstop and does not touch R.S. 27:625, watch for amendments in the Senate Finance Committee that would raise the sports wagering tax rate or its dedication to this fund as an alternative or additional revenue source.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7366,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:25.567Z"},"MA-H1":{"summary":"House 1 is Governor Healey's FY2026 general appropriations bill, proposing $59.58 billion in non-surtax spending plus $1.95 billion tied to the Fair Share income surtax, and setting out consensus revenue estimates by source and fund. In the portion of text provided, gaming appears only as budget lines: Massachusetts Gaming Commission resource totals and references to the gaming-related funds among 'other major funds.' The bill was reported, in part, by H4000, the House's own budget.","mechanics":["Section 1A revenue table lists 'Other major funds' that include the Gaming Local Aid Fund and the Gaming Economic Development Fund. It shows $555.5M in total revenue for that fund group but does not break out gaming receipts separately.","The Independents resource summary for the Massachusetts Gaming Commission shows a $923K budgetary recommendation and $92.82M in federal, trust and intragovernmental service fund spending, for $93.743M total. It also projects $385.966M in budgetary non-tax revenue.","The Section 1A consensus tax table has no separate sports wagering or gaming tax line. Gaming receipts appear to flow as non-tax or departmental revenue rather than tax revenue.","House 1 tax initiatives total $386M: tax-related settlements, capping the charitable deduction, and tax enforcement and loophole closures. In the text provided, none targets wagering.","The text provided is truncated partway through the District Attorneys section. Outside sections and any later Gaming Commission line items or policy riders are not visible and cannot be assessed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What stands out is that the Governor's budget treats gaming purely as a revenue and appropriation matter in the visible text. The Gaming Commission is funded almost entirely off-budget through trust spending ($92.8M) and is projected to return about $386M in non-tax revenue, with no change to sports wagering tax rates or regulation visible in the provided portion.","watch":"Check the untruncated outside sections and the H4000 and Senate budget amendments for any riders that raise the sports wagering tax rate, redirect Gaming Commission trust funds or gaming fund distributions, or add advertising or responsible-gaming conditions, since budget vehicles in Massachusetts are a common route for such changes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":188575,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:27.576Z"},"MA-H3064":{"summary":"H3064 creates a Bilingual Education Trust Fund to pay stipends and loan repayment for bilingual educators, and rewrites the bilingual endorsement and reciprocity rules in chapter 71A. It funds the trust partly by redirecting 5 points of the existing sports wagering excise in chapter 23N, section 14(a), and by imposing a new 15% excise on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts. The bill was sent to a study order (H5238) on 2026-03-16, which in Massachusetts practice usually ends a bill for the session.","mechanics":["Section 5 rewrites the first sentence of G.L. c. 23N, §14(a). It keeps the headline rates of 15% on in-person and 20% on mobile/digital adjusted gross sports wagering receipts. In each case 5 percentage points go to the new Bilingual Education Trust Fund, leaving 10% (in-person) and 15% (mobile) for the Sports Wagering Fund under §17.","Section 5 also adds a new clause (iii): a 15% excise on the adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts of anyone offering fantasy contests 'as defined in section 11M1/2'. Of that, 5% goes to the trust fund and 10% to the Sports Wagering Fund. The bill does not say which chapter section 11M1/2 belongs to (the existing fantasy contest provision is in chapter 12), and it does not define 'adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts'.","Section 4 creates new G.L. c. 29, §2BBBBBB, the trust fund, administered by the Commissioner of Elementary and Secondary Education. Money in the fund is not subject to further appropriation and does not revert to the General Fund. The Commissioner reports to the Legislature annually by October 1.","Section 6 keeps the marijuana retail excise at 10.75% but diverts 2 points of it to the trust fund.","Section 3 adds new c. 71A, §14, funded from the trust. It provides a $10,000 annual stipend for endorsed bilingual educators, $5,000 more for staff certified as bilingual, $1,000 for holders of the state seal of biliteracy, and $1,000 a year for 5 years for graduates of Massachusetts public schools. Educators with 10 years of service in the same district get $30,000 in loan repayment. Stipends count as compensation for teacher retirement purposes.","Effective date is January 1, 2027 (Section 8)."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that an education workforce bill carries two gaming changes. It earmarks a fixed share of the sports wagering excise to a dedicated, non-appropriated trust, which reduces what flows into the Sports Wagering Fund without raising operators' headline rates. It also writes a brand-new 15% excise on daily fantasy operators into the sports wagering statute. A similar bill was filed last session as H3947 (2023-2024).","watch":"The main risk is the new 15% excise on fantasy contest operators in proposed c. 23N, §14(a)(iii), with its undefined 'adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts' base and unclear cross-reference; the bill is likely dead after the study order, but watch for this language resurfacing in revenue or budget vehicles.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":17584,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:39.720Z"},"MA-H432":{"summary":"H432 extends Massachusetts' racing and simulcasting authorizations, which expire December 15, 2025 under chapter 26 of the acts of 2023, to December 15, 2030 across a series of special acts. It keeps the Suffolk County running-horse licensee licensed for simulcast wagering through the later of December 15, 2030 or the end of its category 2 sports wagering license term. It also shields category 2 sports wagering licenses under G.L. c. 23N from any adverse effect if racing or simulcasting laws lapse.","mechanics":["Sections 1-15 replace 'December 15, 2025' with 'December 15, 2030' in the sunset clauses of c. 494 of 1978 (ss. 12A, 13, 15), c. 277 of 1986 (s. 9), c. 114 of 1991 (ss. 3-5), c. 139 of 2001 (s. 45), c. 449 of 2006 (s. 20), c. 194 of 2011 (ss. 92, 112) and c. 10 of 2015 (s. 74). Section 3 cites the amending source as 'said chapter 128', apparently a drafting error for chapter 26.","Section 16 repeals section 23 of chapter 26 of the acts of 2023. The text does not say what that section provides.","Section 17 applies to the Suffolk County running-horse licensee that held live-racing (c. 128A) and simulcast (c. 128C) licenses in 2025. That licensee stays licensed and authorized to simulcast until the later of December 15, 2030 or the expiration, including any renewal, of a c. 23N category 2 sports wagering license it holds as of that date.","Section 17 also sets the following conditions. All days from January 1, 2025 to December 31, 2030 are dark days, so no live racing occurs unless the licensee obtains a supplemental live racing license. Greyhound simulcasts and wagers are barred. Simulcasts must comply with the Interstate Horse Racing Act, 15 U.S.C. 3001 et seq.","For simulcasts from states not requiring IHA approval under 15 U.S.C. 3004(a)(1)(A), approval from the New England HBPA is required. If the HBPA approves a simulcast for one licensee, it must approve it for all eligible licensees.","Section 18 provides that expiration or repeal of c. 128A, c. 128C or related racing laws, or any resulting cessation of simulcasting, cannot suspend, terminate or otherwise adversely affect a category 2 sports wagering license. The holder is deemed authorized to simulcast for the full license term and any renewal."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills address pari-mutuel sunset extensions or the link between racing status and sports wagering eligibility. Florida H0189 touches pari-mutuel permits as part of a broad gambling rewrite. Illinois HB2880 and HB5035 amend the Horse Racing Act only on Racing Board employee restrictions. H432 is unusual because Section 18 decouples category 2 sports wagering licensure from continued simulcasting, a protection for racing-based sportsbook licensees not found in the Alaska, Georgia, Hawaii or Illinois authorization bills.","watch":"Watch whether Section 18's deemed-authorization language for category 2 licensees survives as the bill moves with H341, since without it a lapse in racing or simulcasting authority could jeopardize a racetrack-based sports wagering license under c. 23N.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8171,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:43.370Z"},"MA-H332":{"summary":"H332 would create a new Chapter 23O, the Massachusetts Internet Gaming Act, legalizing online casino games (poker, blackjack, roulette, slots, live dealer and other Commission-approved games) under the Massachusetts Gaming Commission. Licenses would go to existing Chapter 23K casino licensees and to up to four standalone operators. Revenue would be taxed at 20% of adjusted gross receipts, with a promotional-credit deduction that is phased down starting in 2028. On April 2, 2026 the bill was sent to a study order (H5320), which in Massachusetts practice means it is effectively shelved for the 2025-2026 session.","mechanics":["Licensing (§6): Category 1 licenses go to Chapter 23K casino licensees, each allowed up to two platforms and two platform providers, with one brand per platform plus an optional second poker brand. Category 2 licenses for non-casino entities are capped at 4, with one platform each, awarded on eight best-qualified factors if oversubscribed. No partnership with a casino licensee is required (§6(b)(3)).","Fees: $200,000 nonrefundable processing fee (§7). $5,000,000 initial license fee for a 5-year term, renewable for $5,000,000 (§6(g)). A $1,000,000 temporary license is available to a 'qualified gaming entity' (a casino licensee or an operator licensed for iGaming in 3+ US jurisdictions), and all applicants filing within 30 days get the same launch day (§6(c)). Transfer review fee is capped at $200,000. Operators share a $1,000,000 annual Public Health Trust Fund assessment (§15(e)).","Tax (§14): 20% excise on adjusted gross internet gaming receipts, in lieu of all other state and local taxes and fees. Promotional credits are fully deductible until Jan. 1, 2028. After that the deduction is capped at 2.5% of monthly handle, stepping down to 2.25% (July 2029), 2% (July 2030) and 1.75% (from July 1, 2031), with no carryover of excess credits. Negative AGR may be carried forward but not back.","Consumer protection: §4(c) requires rules banning deceptive ads, pop-ups or texts to self-excluded persons, and marketing targeting under-21s. §4(d) requires responsible gaming plans, no credit lines, an unsolicited-free withdrawal right, account closure, and a 1-800-GAMBLER display. Minimum age is 21. The Commission keeps a self-exclusion list and may extend Chapter 23N sports-wagering self-exclusions to iGaming.","Integrity (§§11-12): bars insiders and proxy bettors, requires monitoring systems, immediate reporting of suspicious wagering, and 3-year records of abnormal activity. The Commission must default to requesting anonymized data, and operators get notice and appeal rights before personally identifiable information is demanded.","Market structure: a single patron wallet may be shared with the operator's sports wagering account (§3). Interstate reciprocal agreements are authorized (§4(i), §13(b)). Live dealer studios may be located outside Massachusetts (§13(c)). Supervisory staff need occupational licenses ($100), and Chapter 23K/23N licensees are deemed qualified."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogues on the list are Illinois HB3080 and HB4797, which also create an 'Internet Gaming Act'. Per their descriptions, those bills route iGaming through licensees or management services providers contracted with a licensee. MA H332 instead opens up to four Category 2 licenses to operators with no casino partner. Most other listed bills deal with sports wagering, including AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569 and the Illinois Sports Wagering Act amendments, rather than online casino. H332's statutory phase-down of the promotional-credit deduction to 1.75% of handle is more granular than anything described in the listed bills.","watch":"Watch three things for when this framework resurfaces after the study order: the phased cap on promotional-credit deductions (with a no-carryover rule that mistakenly refers to a 'mobile sports betting operator' in §14(b)(3)), the scarcity and best-qualified criteria for the four standalone Category 2 licenses, and Section 2's redraft of the 'illegal gaming' definition, which drops the fantasy-contest and greyhound exclusions that Section 1 had included.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":73627,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:44.382Z"},"MA-H4037":{"summary":"H4037 is a broad Massachusetts responsible-gaming bill. It links self-exclusion across the lottery, casino (c. 23K), sports wagering (c. 23N) and a new Attorney General fantasy-contest list. It also imposes new advertising restrictions and a 5% excise on celebrity endorsement income, requires betting and loss limits plus daily and monthly activity statements, mandates anonymized player-data sharing for research, and bars sports wagering licensees from limiting or excluding bettors except under the statute or clearly disclosed terms of service. On 2026-03-18 it was accompanied by a new draft, H5258, so the operative text may have changed.","mechanics":["Advertising (Sections 4, 11 and 13): a new c. 23K §72 bars 23K and 23N licensees from advertising, branding, sponsorships or celebrity endorsements deemed to appeal to persons under 21, and from TV, radio, internet, billboard or print ads unless at least 85% of the audience is reasonably expected to be 21 or older. A new c. 23N §4(F) addresses advertising on TV or streaming during broadcasts of sporting events. A single standardized disclaimer with the MA Gambling Helpline number is required in all licensee and contractor advertising (new c. 23K §3(z)).","Excluded persons (Sections 1, 3, 6–7 and 17–18): enrolling on any one of the casino, sports wagering, fantasy or lottery self-exclusion lists can be done together with the others. The commission must offer online and phone enrollment. Operators may not market to anyone on an excluded persons list and must use all available technology (cookies, IP addresses, digital fingerprints) to keep ads from being served to them; MGC regulations will set penalties.","Section 11 / new c. 23K §72(b): the commission must establish a 5% excise tax on income an individual celebrity earns under advertising, sponsorship or endorsement contracts with 23K or 23N licensees, with revenue going to the Public Health Trust Fund.","Section 16 / new c. 23N §12(h): operators must let bettors set per-bet, hourly, daily, weekly and monthly limits, with limit increases capped at once per 24 hours. At registration, bettors must be prompted to set spending and loss limits and to enroll in or opt out of a play-management program such as Play-My-Way. Patrons get daily electronic and monthly mailed statements of bets, wins and losses (opt-out allowed), and operators report annual spend and losses by zip code.","Section 16 / new c. 23N §12(i): a licensee may not exclude an individual or limit their wagers or potential winnings except under a section of c. 23N or a preexisting term of service clearly disclosed when the user logs into the app.","Data, records and information sharing (Sections 14, 15 and 19): operators must supply customer tracking data, which a nonprofit anonymizes (the contract was due by Dec. 31, 2025) for research by qualified researchers. Operator license applications become public records, with trade secrets withholdable at the commission's discretion. The investigations and enforcement bureau may exchange information with law enforcement, other gaming regulators and the FBI."],"issue_tags":["advertising","tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"New Jersey's A 4002/S3419 only require operators to publish account-limitation rules and notify patrons. H4037's §12(i) goes further, effectively banning limiting or excluding bettors except under the statute or terms of service disclosed at login. NJ AR150 merely urges a ban on targeted gambling ads to problem gamblers, whereas H4037 mandates technical suppression of ads to excluded persons. Maryland's HB518 (voluntary exclusion list) and HB1409 (prevalence studies) address similar harms, but none of the listed bills combines an 85% adult-audience ad standard, a celebrity endorsement excise and mandatory anonymized player-data sharing.","watch":"Check whether the new draft H5258 keeps the §12(i) restriction on limiting or excluding bettors, the 85% adult-audience advertising test with its restriction on ads during sports broadcasts, and the mandatory player-data handover, as these carry the largest compliance and commercial impact.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":33371,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:50.261Z"},"MA-H4431":{"summary":"H4431 creates a new M.G.L. chapter 23O authorizing internet casino gaming (iGaming) in Massachusetts, licensed exclusively by the Massachusetts Gaming Commission and limited to existing chapter 23K casino licensees, each of which may contract out up to 3 branded skins. It imposes a 15% tax on adjusted gross revenue, allows multi-state reciprocal agreements, sets detailed responsible-gaming and advertising rules, and separately bans online sweepstakes games with fines of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation. The bill was sent to a study order (H5269) on 2026-03-23, which in Massachusetts practice usually means it will not advance this session.","mechanics":["Eligibility and fees (§6): only chapter 23K gaming licensees qualify for an internet gaming operator license ($100,000 application fee); each licensee may issue up to 3 skins to platform providers ($50,000 nonrefundable fee per skin); supplier licenses carry a $15,000 fee (§11); licenses run 5 years with renewal fees of $100,000, $50,000 and $15,000 (§12); temporary licenses are available in the first 12 months for applicants holding a plenary iGaming license in an equivalent state (§7).","Tax (§13): 15% of adjusted gross revenue, in lieu of all other taxes and fees on internet gaming, paid monthly, with negative months carried forward; an unspecified percentage set by regulation goes to a new Player Health Program Fund (§16).","Player accounts (§14): patrons must be 21 or older and physically located in Massachusetts or a permissible jurisdiction; deposits are capped at $20,000 per 24 hours; credit-card funding is excluded, including via digital wallets and prepaid cards; a single account may be shared with the operator's mobile sports wagering product (§2 definition).","Responsible gaming and advertising (§§3(c), 17, 23-24): ban on using biometric data; commission-designated AI triggers to flag risky play; mandatory deposit, loss, wager and time limits; cool-off periods of at least 72 hours; no ads on college campuses; no 'risk-free' language; no influencers who primarily appeal to under-21s; 1-800-GAMBLER messaging; self-exclusion terms of 1, 3 or 5 years or lifetime, with an opt-in to a national list.","Sweepstakes ban (§19): operating, promoting or assisting online sweepstakes games (dual-currency casino-style, lottery or sports wagering) is unlawful, with fines of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation, possible loss of gaming license, and up to 2 years' imprisonment for repeat offenders.","Enforcement (§§20, 26): tampering with games carries up to 5 years and $50,000 (or $200,000 for entities) plus mandatory license revocation; civil penalties generally require prior written notice and a cure period, except for patterns of noncompliance, willful conduct, integrity breaches or failures to report."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","advertising","sweeps","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the sports-wagering legalization bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), H4431 addresses online casino gaming rather than sports betting; its closest analogues are IL HB3080 and HB4797, which would create an Illinois Internet Gaming Act. It pairs iGaming authorization with an outright online sweepstakes ban carrying criminal exposure, a combination not reflected in the listed titles. Its advertising limits overlap with CT HB05229 (college-campus ad restrictions and AI-related rules), but H4431 takes the opposite approach on AI: it directs AI to detect risky play rather than barring AI-driven bet targeting.","watch":"Watch the casino-tethered three-skin cap, the $20,000 daily deposit limit and mandatory AI risk triggers that would bind sportsbooks offering combined iGaming wallets, and the §19 sweepstakes ban's 'support or assist' language and license-loss penalty, which could reach licensed operators' vendors and affiliates, should the study order give way to a redraft.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":52431,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:54.612Z"},"MA-H481":{"summary":"H481 would amend the definition of \"Category 2 license\" in M.G.L. c. 23N, section 3, so that a Category 2 (retail, in-person) sports wagering licensee could make a capital investment of at least $7,500,000 in a sports wagering facility instead of paying the license fee referenced in \"section 5(f).\" The bill is a single section and adds no other conditions or procedures. As of 2026-03-23 it was accompanied a study order (see H5269), which in Massachusetts practice usually means it will not advance this session.","mechanics":["SECTION 1 replaces the last clause of the c. 23N, section 3 definition of \"Category 2 license\" with a new proviso.","A Category 2 licensee may, \"in lieu of the fee in section 5(f),\" make a capital investment of \"not less than $7,500,000 in a sports wagering facility.\"","The bill does not define what counts as a qualifying capital investment, set a deadline for making it, provide for verification or clawback, or say how it interacts with renewal fees.","The fee cross-reference to \"section 5(f)\" is not explained in the text, and the bill contains no effective date or transition clause.","Only Category 2 (in-person) licensees are covered; Category 1 and Category 3 (mobile) licensees are unaffected."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB2878 and HB5034, which would set a $250,000 application fee and a $15,000,000 master license fee, H481 moves the other way: it lets a retail licensee swap its license fee for facility spending. It does not touch tax rates or mobile authorization, which are the focus of AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and the Hawaii bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569). Allowing capital investment to satisfy a statutory license fee for a retail sportsbook tier is unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch whether the concept comes back out of the H5269 study with a defined meaning of \"capital investment,\" a timing and verification mechanism, and a corrected fee cross-reference, because as drafted it would create a fee-substitute option for Category 2 retail licensees that mobile-only operators could not use.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1599,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:57.165Z"},"MA-H506":{"summary":"H506 would create a PLAY Trust Fund, administered by the Commissioner of Elementary and Secondary Education, to pay for youth athletics and sports fees for K-8 public school students and to fight childhood obesity. One of its revenue sources is 1 per cent of the total excise tax collected from sports wagering operators under M.G.L. c. 23N, section 14. The bill does not change the tax rate or impose any new obligation on operators; it redirects a share of existing sports wagering tax revenue.","mechanics":["Inserts new section 2KKKKKK into M.G.L. c. 29, establishing the PLAY Trust Fund as a separate fund on the Commonwealth's books, administered by the DESE commissioner.","Permitted uses: funding K-8 public school youth athletics, paying sports-related fees that students and families cannot afford, and combating childhood obesity through youth sports.","Fund sources: legislative appropriations, gifts, grants and donations, interest, and 1 per cent of the total excise tax collected from sports wagering operators under c. 23N, section 14.","Money credited to the fund is not subject to further appropriation and does not revert to the General Fund at fiscal year end.","Requires an annual report by October 1 to the House and Senate clerks, the Joint Committee on Education and both Ways and Means committees, covering sources, expenditures, anticipated remaining revenue and the number of students and families funded, to be posted publicly on DESE's website.","No effective date, operator obligations or enforcement provisions; the bill does not expressly amend the existing distribution of sports wagering revenue in c. 23N."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. What is unusual is that the bill uses sports wagering excise revenue as a fixed-percentage earmark for a youth sports and obesity program, rather than for problem-gambling or general-fund purposes, and it does so without touching the 15 and 20 per cent rates or expressly amending the c. 23N distribution scheme. It is one of a recurring type of Massachusetts bills that treat the sports wagering tax as a funding source for unrelated programs.","watch":"Operators bear no direct cost here, but watch whether this and similar earmarks of the c. 23N, section 14 tax build pressure to raise rates in order to fund multiple new programs, and whether the bill is amended to reconcile the 1 per cent diversion with the existing c. 23N revenue distribution.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3407,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:57.464Z"},"MA-H5555":{"summary":"H5555 is Massachusetts' FY2027 general appropriations act, enacted as Chapter 137 of the Acts of 2026, with a total appropriation of about $63.4 billion. In the portion of the text provided, the only gaming content is two non-tax revenue projections in Section 1B. Those cover budgeted transfers from the Massachusetts Gaming Commission and the State Lottery Commission. Nothing in the provided text changes sports wagering or gaming law.","mechanics":["Section 1B projects $372,395,024 in budgeted transfers attributed to the Massachusetts Gaming Commission for FY2027, all unrestricted, with no departmental revenues.","Section 1B projects $1,321,596,996 in budgeted transfers from the State Lottery Commission, plus $30,887 in departmental revenues.","The Section 1A tax revenue table has no separate line for sports wagering or gaming taxes. Gaming money appears only within non-tax revenue: $5,190.6M in consolidated transfers and $27,588.0M in total non-tax revenue.","The act is declared an emergency law, effective immediately for the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2026.","The text supplied is truncated partway through Section 2 (around item 0339-1003). Any Gaming Commission line items or outside sections that might touch chapters 23K or 23N were not available for review."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is an ordinary state budget. Its gaming relevance in the provided text is limited to revenue projections. Sports wagering revenue is folded into the Gaming Commission's budgeted-transfer figure rather than broken out as its own tax line, which makes it hard to isolate expected sports-betting receipts.","watch":"Obtain the full enacted Chapter 137, including the Gaming Commission line items and outside sections, which were not in the text provided, to check for any amendments to chapter 23N, changes to sports wagering tax rates or fund distributions, or new Gaming Commission assessments on operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Bill Text","text_chars":290055,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:05.392Z"},"MA-S2067":{"summary":"S2067 creates a Massachusetts Bilingual Education Trust Fund (new G.L. c.29 §2BBBBBB) to pay bilingual-educator stipends and loan repayment, and funds it partly by earmarking existing sports wagering and marijuana excise revenue. For gaming clients, the key change is Section 5, which rewrites the first sentence of G.L. c.23N §14(a). It keeps the 15% in-person and 20% mobile sports wagering rates, routes 5 points of each to the new fund, and adds a new 15% excise on adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts of fantasy contest operators.","mechanics":["Section 5 (c.23N §14(a)) sets the in-person sports wagering excise at 15% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts: 5% goes to the Bilingual Education Trust Fund and 10% to the Sports Wagering Fund (§17).","Mobile and digital sports wagering stays at 20%: 5% goes to the Bilingual Education Trust Fund and 15% to the Sports Wagering Fund, so the Sports Wagering Fund's share falls.","Section 5 also adds a new 15% excise on the adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts of any person or entity offering fantasy contests as defined in §11M½, split 5% to the trust fund and 10% to the Sports Wagering Fund.","Section 6 keeps the marijuana retail excise at 10.75% but diverts 2 points to the trust fund.","The fund is not subject to further appropriation and does not revert to the General Fund. It pays $10,000 annual stipends to endorsed bilingual educators, plus $5,000, $1,000 and 5-year $1,000 add-ons, and $30,000 in loan repayment after 10 years of service.","Effective date is January 1, 2027 (Section 8). The bill was reported favorably by committee on 2025-12-22 and is now in Senate Ways and Means."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual because it leaves Massachusetts' sports wagering rates unchanged and instead carves an education earmark out of existing revenue. It also folds a new 15% excise on daily fantasy sports into the sports wagering excise statute, a significant expansion of the tax base tucked inside an education-funding bill.","watch":"Watch the new 15% fantasy contest excise in Section 5(iii): it would newly tax DFS operators under c.23N §14 from January 1, 2027, and its placement in a sentence aimed at 'sports wagering operators' raises scope and definitional questions; also watch whether Ways and Means uses the earmark as a vehicle to raise the headline rates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Bill Text","text_chars":17604,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:12.496Z"},"MA-H5576":{"summary":"H5576 is the House-engrossed Massachusetts economic development bond bill, declared an emergency law, that authorizes capital spending through the Executive Office of Economic Development, available until June 30, 2036. The portion of the text provided (roughly pages 1 to 23 of 225) consists almost entirely of section 2 line items and local earmarks and contains no sports wagering or gaming provisions. Any gaming-related language would have to appear in the roughly 200 pages not reproduced here.","mechanics":["Section 1: sums in section 2 are made available, in addition to prior authorizations, until June 30, 2036.","7002-8079: $25M capital grant program for private businesses building or expanding commercial, industrial or manufacturing facilities, with decarbonization, climate-resilience and job-creation criteria.","7002-8080: $75M for AI technology grants, and 7002-8082: $100M for defense-sector capital and infrastructure. Either may be administered by quasi-public agencies such as MassTech or the Life Sciences Center.","7002-8085: $25M downtown and main-street grants, including at least $200,000 for security at the 2027 Boston Calling festival.","7002-8087: local economic development earmarks, including at least $1,000,000 to reimburse businesses in the Gillette Stadium 'clean zone' in Foxborough for revenue lost during the June and July 2026 World Cup games.","Status: a conference committee was appointed on 2026-07-30, so the final text may differ from the House version."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a large omnibus bond and earmark bill. The provided excerpt never mentions wagering, licensing, or the Gaming Commission. The only sports-adjacent item is the World Cup clean-zone revenue reimbursement at Gillette Stadium, which concerns local merchants rather than betting.","watch":"Because Massachusetts economic development and conference-committee vehicles can pick up unrelated policy riders, review the remaining roughly 200 pages and the eventual conference report for any amendments to G.L. c. 23K or c. 23N, including tax rates, advertising limits or Gaming Commission authority.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":205308,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:13.222Z"},"MA-H5258":{"summary":"H5258 is a broad responsible-gaming package. It imposes advertising restrictions and a mandatory standardized problem-gambling disclaimer on Gaming Commission licensees under chapters 23K and 23N, on fantasy contest operators and on the Lottery. It creates an Attorney General self-exclusion list for fantasy contests that is cross-linked to the casino and sports wagering lists, and requires casinos and sports wagering operators to hand customer tracking data to the Commission for anonymized addiction research. It also orders a Commission study of stricter sports betting ad rules, including a whistle-to-whistle ban, and creates a special commission on problem gambling screening and treatment.","mechanics":["Advertising (new 23K §72, also applied to fantasy operators by new ch. 12 §11M3/4(f)): 23K and 23N licensees may not advertise, brand, or sign sponsorships or celebrity endorsements the Commission deems to appeal to under-21s. TV, radio, internet, billboard and print ads are barred unless at least 85% of the audience is reasonably expected to be 21+, based on reliable audience composition data, with penalties set by Commission regulation. A single standardized disclaimer with the MA Gambling Helpline number is required in all licensee and contractor advertising (23K §3(z)), and the same disclaimer applies to fantasy operators and Lottery ads.","Excluded persons: sports wagering operators, qualified gaming entities, casinos and fantasy operators may not market to anyone on an excluded list in any form, including mail, app notifications, digital ads and ads placed in third parties' advertising. They must use 'all technological means available' (cookies, IP addresses, digital fingerprints) to keep ads from reaching those people (23N §13(e)(4); 23K §45(g)). The Commission must offer online and phone self-exclusion, and one request can enroll a person on the casino, sports wagering and new AG fantasy lists together.","Data mandate: casinos (new 23K §73) and sports wagering operators (23N §4(d)(4)) must supply the Commission with customer tracking data from loyalty programs, player tracking, player cards, online transactions and any other system. The Commission must contract a nonprofit to build an anonymizing system by the earlier of June 30, 2027 or six months after the act takes effect. Anonymized data goes to a research facility for qualified researchers, and the Commission must make a good-faith effort to implement the resulting harm-minimization recommendations.","Casino cashless wagering (rewritten 23K §29): per-bet, hourly, daily, weekly and monthly bet and loss limits must be available, and players may raise limits only once per 24 hours. Players receive mailed monthly statements and daily email/app statements (opt-out allowed), are prompted to set spending limits and enroll in a play management program such as Play-My-Way, and casinos must report patron losses by zip code annually.","Other 23N changes: regulations requiring a responsible gaming module for bettors (§4(d)(5)). Adds 'agents, promoters' to §11. Lets the investigations and enforcement bureau share applicant and licensee information with law enforcement, other gaming regulators and the FBI (§16(j)). Expands the §23 research mandate to cover suicide and self-harm among gamblers.","Studies: a Commission study (new 23N §20A) of stricter sports betting ad restrictions, an unfair/deceptive marketing definition, a complaint helpline, a whistle-to-whistle ban and First Amendment issues, due December 31, 2027. Health insurer, GIC and MassHealth screening provisions are amended to include gambling questions. A special commission on problem gambling must report first by June 30, 2028."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Massachusetts goes considerably further than the narrower New Jersey consumer-protection bills. NJ A 4002/S3419 cover account-limit disclosure, NJ A 4003 covers incentives to people using responsible gaming tools, and NJ AR150 is only a non-binding resolution urging bans on targeting problem gamblers; H5258 instead imposes hard statutory bans on marketing to excluded persons and an 85% adult-audience threshold. Its cross-linked self-exclusion lists and mandated research overlap with Maryland HB518 (Commission-run voluntary exclusion list) and MD HB1409/SB862 (prevalence studies). None of those listed bills requires operators to turn over raw player tracking data for anonymized academic research, which is the most unusual feature here.","watch":"Operators should watch the 23N §4(d)(4) customer-data turnover mandate, the open-ended 'all technological means available' duty to keep ads away from excluded persons, the 85% adult-audience test and under-21 'appeal' standard that could restrict sponsorships and celebrity endorsements, and the §20A study that could lay the groundwork for a whistle-to-whistle ad ban.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Bill Text","text_chars":32558,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:14.195Z"},"MA-S234":{"summary":"S234 reorganizes Massachusetts horse racing law by creating a three-member State Racing Board inside the Massachusetts Gaming Commission. It moves the racing and simulcasting statutes (currently chapters 128A and 128C) into new chapters 23K ¼ and 23K ½, and rewrites the Race Horse Development Fund and the thoroughbred and standardbred capital improvement trust funds. It also restates the definition of illegal gaming in G.L. c. 4, §7, Tenth, and lets the Commission grant simulcasting licenses to casino gaming establishments. The text supplied is cut off partway through new c. 23K ¼, §9, and the bill was replaced on 2026-01-05 by a new draft, S2820.","mechanics":["New c. 23K §7A creates a gubernatorially appointed three-member racing board. The chair must have gaming or racing regulatory or industry experience and one member must be an equine veterinarian. The board administers c. 23K ¼ and c. 23K ½ and acts as the host racing and off-track betting commission under the Interstate Horseracing Act (15 U.S.C. 3001). The Commission keeps appellate review over board decisions on pari-mutuel wagering and simulcasting (§7(a)).","Rewritten §7(b) lets the Commission grant simulcasting licenses to gaming establishments under c. 23K ½ §§8–9, after considering the impact on licensed racing facilities.","Race Horse Development Fund distributions (§60): 80% to horsemen's purse accounts, 16% to state breeding programs and 4% to health and pension benefits. Shifts in the thoroughbred/standardbred split are capped at 10 percentage points per year.","Capital improvement trust funds (§§60A–60B): thoroughbred tracks pay the daily breaks less $100,000, which goes to the council on compulsive gambling. Harness tracks pay the breaks plus 2% of exotic handle. Unspent balances sweep to the Race Horse Development Fund each December 31.","Racing meeting licensing (new c. 23K ¼): applications due October 1, a cap of 200 racing days each for running and harness racing, one licensee per county, and a $1.25M surety bond ($250,000 for fairs). Board approval is required for transfers over 10%, and 85% of employees must be Massachusetts residents.","Takeout for running horse wagering is capped at 19% on straight wagers and 26% on exotics. From that, 0.75% goes to the board, 1% to the Massachusetts Thoroughbred Breeders Association and 8.5% or 9.5% to purses. Account wagering and simulcast wagering are carved out of the on-track-only rule (§9(b))."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills restructure pari-mutuel racing regulation. The closest are Illinois HB2880 and HB5035, which only loosen pre-employment restrictions on Illinois Racing Board staff, whereas S234 builds a new racing board, a licensing scheme and fund system from the ground up. Most of the listed bills, such as Alaska HB145/SB194, Georgia HB 686 and Hawaii HB 1308, authorize and tax sports wagering, which S234 does not touch except through the illegal gaming definition. Its distinctive feature is folding racing oversight into the Gaming Commission structure while giving casinos a route to simulcasting licenses.","watch":"Check whether S2820 keeps S234's illegal gaming carve-out for sports wagering 'conducted under chapter 10' (the lottery chapter) rather than chapter 23N, since that apparent drafting error could cast doubt on the exemption for licensed sports wagering, and also watch the casino simulcasting and account wagering provisions for competitive effects.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":144740,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:26.899Z"},"MA-S294":{"summary":"S294 would let a Massachusetts Category 2 sports wagering licensee (the retail, in-person license class under G.L. c. 23N) make a capital investment of at least $7,500,000 in a sports wagering facility instead of paying the fee referenced in \"section 5(f).\" It does this by striking the last clause of the \"Category 2 license\" definition in G.L. c. 23N, §3 and inserting the investment option in its place. On 2025-12-18 it was sent to a study order (S2885), which usually means it is dead for the 2025-2026 session.","mechanics":["Amends the definition of \"Category 2 license\" in G.L. c. 23N, §3 by replacing its last clause.","Gives Category 2 licensees an option: make a capital investment of not less than $7,500,000 in a sports wagering facility \"in lieu of the fee in section 5(f).\"","Covers only Category 2 (retail) licensees, not Category 1 casino licensees or Category 3 mobile operators.","Does not define \"capital investment,\" set a deadline for making it, provide for verification by the Gaming Commission, or include clawback or enforcement terms.","Has no effective date or transition rule for licensees that have already paid the fee.","Referred to Economic Development and Emerging Technologies; accompanied a study order on 2025-12-18 (S2885)."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that it turns a fixed license fee into an optional in-kind investment obligation, a rare approach in sports betting licensing. The text is also thin: it cites \"section 5(f)\" for the fee without saying what that fee is, and it has no mechanism for the Gaming Commission to approve or audit the investment.","watch":"Watch whether the \"section 5(f)\" cross-reference actually reaches the Category 2 license fee, and whether any revived version adds Commission verification, timing, or clawback terms that would determine if the $7.5 million investment option is real relief or a compliance trap for retail licensees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1654,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:29.527Z"},"MA-S2820":{"summary":"S2820 overhauls Massachusetts horse racing regulation by creating a three-member state racing board within the Gaming Commission (new G.L. c. 23K s. 7A) to administer live racing, pari-mutuel wagering and simulcasting, and by recodifying the old chapters 128A and 128C as new chapters 23K ¼ (horse racing meetings) and 23K ½ (simulcasting). It restructures the Race Horse Development Fund and creates thoroughbred and standardbred capital improvement trust funds financed by breaks and wagering set-asides. It also rewrites the c. 4 s. 7 definition of illegal gaming to carve out lottery, chapter 23K casino gaming, sports wagering conducted under chapter 10, pari-mutuel racing, bingo, charitable gaming and fantasy contests.","mechanics":["Racing board (s. 7A): three full-time governor appointees with five-year terms and a 15-year cap, conflict-of-interest bars, and one member required to be an equine veterinarian. The board serves as host racing and off-track betting commission under the Interstate Horseracing Act, and the Gaming Commission hears appeals of board decisions (s. 7).","Race Horse Development Fund (s. 60): distributions split 80% to purse accounts, 16% to breeding programs and 4% to horsemen's health and pension benefits. The thoroughbred/standardbred split cannot shift more than 10 percentage points a year.","Capital improvement trust funds (ss. 60A, 60B): thoroughbred tracks pay daily breaks less $100,000, with that $100,000 going, subject to appropriation, to the Massachusetts council on compulsive gambling. Harness tracks pay breaks plus 2% of exotic handle. Unspent balances revert to the Race Horse Development Fund each December 31.","Racing licensing (c. 23K ¼ ss. 2-4): applications due October 1. Caps of 200 racing days a year each for running and harness meetings, one licensee per county except fairs, and a $1,250,000 surety bond ($250,000 for fairs). The daily fee is the greater of $300 or 0.075% of average daily handle, with a cap of $100 a day for fairs.","Pari-mutuel takeout (c. 23K ¼ s. 9): capped at 19% on straight running-horse wagers and 26% on exotics. From the takeout: 0.75% to the board, 1% to the Massachusetts Thoroughbred Breeders Association, and 8.5% (straight) or 9.5% (exotic) to purses. Simulcast and account wagering under c. 23K ½ are exempted from the track-only wagering restriction.","Other provisions: transfers of more than 10% of a licensee require board approval, with notice only for publicly held companies (s. 7). 85% of track employees must be Massachusetts residents of two years (s. 8). Compulsive gambling helpline notices must be posted (s. 3(m)). The supplied text is truncated partway through s. 9."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","legalize","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual for permanently relocating racing law into the Gaming Commission's chapter 23K structure while placing it under a separate racing board. The Commission keeps simulcasting licensing for casinos and appellate review over the board.","watch":"Watch the rewritten illegal-gaming definition in c. 4 s. 7, which carves out only 'sports wagering conducted under chapter 10' (the lottery chapter) rather than the existing chapter 23N sports wagering framework, and could unintentionally leave chapter 23N operators outside the exclusion; also watch whether the simulcasting and account-wagering provisions of c. 23K ½, cut off in the supplied text, expand online racing wagers that compete with or complement sportsbook offerings.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Bill Text","text_chars":144772,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:30.530Z"},"MA-S235":{"summary":"S235 would create a new chapter 23O, the Massachusetts Internet Gaming Act, legalizing online casino games (poker, blackjack, craps, roulette, slots, live dealer and other Commission-approved games) under Massachusetts Gaming Commission regulation. It sets up two license categories, imposes a 20% excise on adjusted gross internet gaming receipts, and phases down the promotional-credit deduction starting in 2028. The bill was sent to a study order (S2996) on 2026-03-09, which in Massachusetts practice usually means it is effectively shelved for the session.","mechanics":["Licensing (s.6): Category 1 licenses go to existing chapter 23K casino licensees, with up to two platforms or skins each, run through up to two licensed platform providers, plus an optional second brand for poker. Category 2 licenses go to non-casino entities, capped at 4, with one platform each; if more than 4 apply, the Commission picks under 8 competitive factors, including projected tax revenue and history of doing business in Massachusetts.","Fees: $200,000 nonrefundable processing fee (s.7). $5,000,000 initial operator license for a 5-year term and $5,000,000 renewal (s.6(g)). $1,000,000 temporary license for 'qualified gaming entities' (casino licensees, or operators licensed for iGaming in 3+ US states), with simultaneous launch for those applying within 30 days. Transfer review fee capped at $200,000. $100 occupational license fee. A $1,000,000 annual assessment, shared among operators, goes to the Public Health Trust Fund (s.15(e)).","Tax (s.14): 20% excise on adjusted gross internet gaming receipts, in lieu of all other state and local taxes and fees. Monthly returns are due by the 15th. Negative AGR carries forward but not back. Promotional credits are fully deductible until 1/1/2028, then capped as a share of monthly handle: 2.5% through 6/30/2029, stepping down to 1.75% from 7/1/2031, with no carryover of excess credits.","Consumer protection and advertising (s.4(c)-(d), s.13): Commission must ban deceptive ads, ads targeting people under 21, and pop-ups or texts to self-excluded persons. It must require responsible-gaming programs, an annual problem gaming plan, account closure on request, withdrawals by the deposit method without solicitation, no credit lines, and display of 1-800-GAMBLER. Minimum age is 21. A self-exclusion list is created, and the Commission may extend chapter 23N sports-wagering self-exclusions to iGaming.","Integrity (s.11-12): operators must run monitoring systems and report suspicious wagering. They must keep wager-level records, including IP location and 3 years of abnormal-activity records, and provide anonymized account data to regulators by default, with a right to appeal requests for personally identifiable information. Insider and proxy betting is barred.","Interstate and structural provisions: the Commission must pursue multistate reciprocal agreements. Live dealer studios may be located outside Massachusetts. A single patron account may be shared with the operator's sports wagering product. Intermediate data routing does not determine wager location, citing UIGEA."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogues on the list are IL HB3080 and HB4797 (Internet Gaming Act), which likewise authorize online casino gaming. MA S235 is distinctive in opening up to 4 standalone Category 2 licenses for non-casino operators with no tether required (s.6(b)(3)), and in importing a scheduled promotional-deduction phase-down into an iGaming tax. Most other listed bills are sports-wagering authorizations or amendments (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569) or narrower consumer-protection bills. S235's advertising and withdrawal-friction rules partly overlap with CT HB05229, though CT's AI-targeting and campus-advertising restrictions have no counterpart here.","watch":"Watch whether any revived version keeps the 4-license Category 2 cap with its revenue-projection selection criteria, the 2028 promotional-deduction cap (s.14(b)(3) mistakenly refers to a 'mobile sports betting operator'), and the Section 2 rewrite of chapter 4 §7 clause Tenth, which drops fantasy contests and greyhound racing from the 'illegal gaming' exclusions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":73626,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:34.613Z"},"MA-S4":{"summary":"S.4 is the Senate Ways and Means Committee's FY 2027 general appropriations bill, reported as a strike-all amendment to House Bill 5501. It recommends about $63.3 billion in total spending. The excerpt provided contains only the chair's letter, the executive summary and the reporting page, and none of it addresses sports wagering, casinos, the Gaming Commission or gaming revenue.","mechanics":["Total recommended spending is $63,295,391,957: $62,459,094,416 in direct appropriations plus $836,297,541 in retained revenue authorization.","Built on a consensus tax revenue estimate of $42.2 billion for FY 2027, excluding the Fair Share surtax, which is a 2.4 percent increase over the FY 2026 benchmark.","The committee states the budget does not raise taxes or fees and does not draw on the Stabilization Fund. It deposits an additional $51 million, bringing the fund to about $8.2 billion.","Allocates $2.7 billion in Fair Share surtax revenue to education and transportation.","Major local-aid lines are $7.66 billion for Chapter 70 school aid and $1.376 billion in Unrestricted General Government Aid (UGGA).","The excerpt includes no gaming or wagering line items or outside sections. Last action on 2026-05-19 was the rejection of Amendment #304 (Dooner), whose content is not in the text provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What sets this bill apart is that it is an omnibus state budget rather than gaming legislation. Any gaming content would sit in line items or outside sections that are not in the excerpt provided.","watch":"Review the full line-item and outside-section text, and the floor amendments such as #304, for any change to sports wagering tax rates, Gaming Commission funding or the distribution of gaming revenue, despite the committee's statement that the budget raises no taxes or fees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":262297,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:40.254Z"},"MD-HB1078":{"summary":"HB1078 (cross-filed with SB 883) makes the Carroll Park Soccer Stadium and Facility in Baltimore City an eligible Maryland Stadium Authority project, caps Authority bonds for it at $216.6 million, and creates a nonlapsing Carroll Park Soccer Stadium and Facility Fund to service that debt. Starting in fiscal 2028, the Comptroller must pay the stadium's annual debt service out of state sports wagering proceeds before those proceeds go to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund or the General Fund. The bill does not change what operators pay or retain.","mechanics":["Debt service is the first claim on sports wagering money: new State Government §9-1E-12(b)(2)(iii) requires the Comptroller, for FY2028 onward, to send the annual principal and interest on the stadium bonds to the Carroll Park Fund before making the Blueprint distribution and the 5% mobile General Fund carve-out. Econ. Dev. §10-646.5(d) mirrors this.","Operator retention is unchanged: Class A and B facility licensees keep 85% and mobile licensees keep 80% under §9-1E-12(b)(1). The diversion comes entirely from the state's share.","Bonding is capped at $216,600,000 outstanding (§10-628(c)(1)(xii)). Fund revenues can be pledged with a lien that attaches immediately under §10-634.","Bonds cannot be issued until the Authority and United Partnership sign a written operating agreement (§10-646.5(e)).","For each bond issue, the Authority must give the legislature's fiscal committees a comprehensive financing plan at least 45 days in advance and obtain Board of Public Works approval (§10-646.5(b)-(c)).","The Fund keeps its own interest earnings. The bill takes effect July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike IL HB3159, which adds a new 2% levy on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts for fire and EMS departments, MD HB1078 imposes no new charge on operators. Instead it re-sequences how the state's existing share is distributed, so a stadium's debt service is paid ahead of education funding. It is closer in spirit to IA SSB 1157, which ties gambling revenue to tourism and sports infrastructure, and to KS HB 2466's sports authority and stadium financing. However, MD HB1078 is unusual in pledging sports wagering proceeds directly as the repayment source for up to $216.6 million in bonds for a single soccer stadium.","watch":"Operators' retention is untouched, but putting up to $216.6 million of bond debt service ahead of the Blueprint distribution creates a long-term claim on sports wagering revenue, which could fuel future pressure to raise Maryland's sports wagering tax rates to make the Blueprint whole.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21464,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:45.203Z"},"MA-S302":{"summary":"S302 would substantially tighten Massachusetts' sports wagering law (G.L. c. 23N). It would remove in-play and proposition bets from authorized sports wagering, require affordability checks above set wagering thresholds, ban percentage-of-wager or deposit compensation, and raise the 20 per cent tax figure in section 14 to 51 per cent. It would also mandate anonymized player-data reporting for research and bring specified betting promotions within the c. 93A unfair and deceptive practices standard.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends c. 23N §3 so that sports wagering includes neither in-play bets nor proposition bets, leaving straight bets (and other listed types) authorized.","Section 4 amends §11 so that no person may wager more than $1,000 per day or $10,000 per month with an operator unless the operator has done an affordability assessment and found the amounts do not exceed 15% of the bettor's available bank balance.","Section 5 adds §11(l), barring operators, directors, officers, owners, employees, affiliates and subcontractors from receiving compensation based on any percentage of a customer's wagers or deposits, which reaches revenue-share affiliate deals.","Sections 6 and 7 replace the figure \"20\" in §14 (line 4) with \"51\", which on its face raises the 20% sports wagering tax rate to 51%, and double a $1,000,000 figure in §15 (line 37) to $2,000,000.","Section 2 adds \"advertising related to sports wagering during a televised sporting event\" to the enumerated list in §4. Section 10 adds c. 93A §2(d), applying the unfair and deceptive acts standard to advertising of bonus promotions, same-game parlays, odds boosts, reload bonuses and risk-free or no-sweat free bets.","Sections 8 and 9 add suicide and self-harm among gamblers to the §23 research mandate. They also require mobile operators to supply annual customer tracking data, including frequency, length, speed and time of play, amounts wagered, and whether each wager was in-game or a parlay. The data would be anonymized through a nonprofit research entity and made available to qualified researchers."],"issue_tags":["tax","prop","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own terms, S302 is unusual in combining several measures in one bill: a ban on in-play and prop betting, a tax hike to 51% that would be among the highest in the country, mandatory bank-balance-based affordability checks, and a ban on revenue-share compensation. Using c. 93A to police specific promotional products, including same-game parlays and odds boosts, also goes further than typical advertising restrictions because it exposes operators to consumer protection enforcement.","watch":"Watch Senate Ways and Means, now that the committee has reported the bill favorably, for whether the 51% tax figure, the in-play and prop ban, or the affordability-check and revenue-share prohibitions survive. Any of these would sharply change mobile operator economics, affiliate arrangements and product offerings in Massachusetts.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Bill Text","text_chars":6158,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:47.060Z"},"MD-HB1120":{"summary":"HB1120 (Chapter 492) lets a member of the U.S. Foreign Service, or the spouse of one, who relocates to Maryland because of an assignment or detail and holds a license from another state, practise in Maryland under that out-of-state license for the length of the assignment. It does this by adding new State Government Subtitle 10-14A and inserting cross-references to § 10-14A-03 into dozens of occupational licensing statutes. The gaming hook is that the cross-reference is also added to the casino (video lottery and table games) licensing requirement in § 9-1A-06(a) and (c)(1) and to the sports wagering licensing requirement in § 9-1E-05(a).","mechanics":["New § 10-14A-03(a) sets five conditions for practising under another state's license for the duration of the assignment: (1) give the licensing authority a copy of the assignment or detail notification; (2) stay in good standing with the issuing regulator; (3) stay in good standing in all states where a license of similar scope in the same discipline is or was held; (4) have actively used the license in the 2 years before relocating; and (5) comply with the licensing authority's practice, discipline and continuing education standards.","Under § 10-14A-03(b) and § 10-14A-04, practice is limited to the discipline and scope allowed in the issuing state. The licensing authority may not require a Maryland license unless the person seeks a different discipline or scope. The individual is subject to the same disciplinary authority as a Maryland licensee.","§ 9-1E-05(a) now opens with 'Except as provided in § 10-14A-03.' The licensing requirement it qualifies covers sports wagering operators, online operators, suppliers and service providers, and individuals directly employed in the operation of sports wagering.","The same carve-out is added to § 9-1A-06(a), which covers video lottery operators, manufacturers, service providers and video lottery employees. It is also added to § 9-1A-06(c)(1), which bars a casino licensee from employing an unlicensed individual as a video lottery employee.","§ 10-14A-02 excludes occupations covered by an interstate compact and the practice of law. 'License' is defined broadly in § 10-14A-01 to include any permit, certification, registration or other authorization needed for an occupation or profession.","§ 10-14A-05 allows each licensing authority, which would include the Maryland Lottery and Gaming Control Commission, to adopt implementing regulations. The supplied text is truncated, so it does not show an effective date."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals with licensing portability. The closest gaming-licensing items are Illinois HB2880/HB5035, which loosen pre-employment restrictions on Racing Board and Gaming Board staff, and Alaska HB145/SB194, which tie mobile sports wagering licensure to criminal history checks. Neither creates a reciprocity route. What stands out in HB1120 is that a general military-style license portability law is written to reach gaming and sports wagering licenses expressly, rather than exempting them as many universal-recognition statutes do. In principle, that lets an out-of-state gaming employee license substitute for a Maryland occupational license.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the Lottery and Gaming Control Commission uses its § 10-14A-05 rulemaking power to require background or suitability checks and notice procedures for Foreign Service members and spouses working under out-of-state gaming licenses, since the statute itself requires only good standing and a copy of the assignment notice before a licensee may employ them in roles that otherwise need a Maryland occupational license.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":163691,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:55.870Z"},"MD-HB1226":{"summary":"HB1226 creates a new State Government Article Subtitle 1F prohibiting anyone from knowingly operating, offering, promoting, or knowingly supporting (directly or indirectly) illegal online gambling in Maryland, with \"illegal\" defined as anything not expressly authorized by law and \"online gambling\" expressly including online casino games and dual-currency sweepstakes games. It gives the Attorney General's Consumer Protection Division and prosecuting attorneys cease-and-desist, injunction, and ex parte powers against operators, platform providers, payment processors, and other supporters. Violations are also treated as unfair, abusive, or deceptive trade practices under Commercial Law §13-301. Licensed online sports wagering and fantasy operators under Subtitles 1D and 1E are exempt.","mechanics":["Penalties (§9-1F-06): operating/promoting is a misdemeanor, up to 3 years and/or $50,000 first offense and $100,000 each subsequent offense; supporting carries fines up to $25,000 first and $50,000 subsequent. Each wager and each promotion is a separate violation, and courts must order forfeiture of profits and gross receipts.","Mandatory licence consequence (§9-1F-06(D)): the Lottery and Gaming Control Commission must revoke any gaming licence held by a person found to have violated §9-1F-03, with permanent ineligibility. §9-1F-12 separately lets the Commission deny, suspend, condition, or limit licences for noncompliance.","Cease-and-desist regime (§9-1F-04, -09): operators get 10 days to geoblock Maryland. Platform providers, payment processors, geolocation providers, content suppliers, promoters, celebrity endorsers, media affiliates, and licensed entities get 30 days to comply. Orders are prima facie evidence of knowledge, service by publication is allowed without locating the operator, and the AG publishes a public list of operators and URLs.","Intermediaries: financial transaction providers may not knowingly process illegal gambling transactions and are deemed to have constructive knowledge of AG-listed sites (§9-1F-07). Platform providers must obtain a certified copy of the operator's licence or Commission confirmation before hosting any online gambling advertisement (§9-1F-08(B)). Both get tort immunity for voluntary blocking (§9-1F-09).","Court relief (§9-1F-10): TROs, injunctions, account freezes, and ex parte injunctions with a hearing within 30 days. A violation is conclusively irreparable harm. Fines for violating orders are $50,000 then $100,000, per day for platforms and per transaction or act of support for supporters.","Long-arm jurisdiction over out-of-state operators, platforms, and supporters; the Division has rulemaking power; liberal construction is mandated (§9-1F-15). Effective October 1, 2026; cross-filed with SB 652."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","integrity","advertising","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills on the list (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569, and the Illinois iGaming bills HB3080/HB4797), HB1226 authorizes nothing and is purely an enforcement statute aimed at offshore and sweepstakes operators. Its closest analogue is FL S1580/H0189, which also tighten illegal-gambling penalties and address Internet gambling. Maryland goes further by imposing duties and per-transaction fines on payment processors, hosting platforms, geolocation vendors, and celebrity endorsers. It does not address prediction markets, unlike CT HB05038 or IL HB5059/HB5142.","watch":"Licensed sportsbooks are exempt only for activity under Subtitles 1D/1E, so multi-product licensees, their affiliates, vendors, and media partners face mandatory permanent licence revocation under §9-1F-06(D) if found to have even indirectly supported unauthorized online casino or sweepstakes activity. Advertising partners will also now require licence verification under §9-1F-08(B) before running any online gambling ad.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":24629,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:58.509Z"},"MD-HB1240":{"summary":"HB1240 adds historical horse races to Maryland's statutory definition of \"sporting event\" and lets certain retail sports wagering facility licensees (casinos, racetracks, the Timonium fairgrounds licensee and satellite simulcast facilities) offer pari-mutuel wagering on them through Commission-approved terminals, capped at 4,000 statewide. Mobile licensees and most Class B licensees are excluded. Historical horse racing proceeds get their own split modeled on video lottery terminal (VLT) distributions, and the Act takes effect January 1, 2027 only if voters approve it at the November 2026 general election.","mechanics":["Coverage (new § 9-1E-09(f)): Class A-1/A-2 licensees may offer it at video lottery facilities; Class A-2 horse racing licensees at thoroughbred or standardbred race courses; § 11-524 licensees at the Timonium fairgrounds; and satellite simulcast facility owners or lessees. All other facility licensees and all mobile sports wagering licensees are barred.","Format (new § 9-1E-09.1): wagering must be pari-mutuel and player-versus-player, with no wagering against the house and no house retention that depends on any race outcome. Optional player-funded pools may cover minus pools. Races of any breed may be used.","Terminal and display rules: only Commission-approved terminals, with a statewide cap of 4,000. Race identity (location, date, horse and jockey or driver names) must be hidden until the wager is placed, while past-performance data current as of the race date must be shown. Results and a replay must follow the wager.","Other limits: minimum wager is 10 cents, and the minimum payout may not be less than the amount wagered. Wagering is allowed only in Commission-approved designated areas, on approved days and hours, and with written approval of all wager types and pool explanations.","Revenue (§ 9-1E-12(c)): VLT operators retain the same percentage they retain on VLT proceeds under § 9-1A-27; non-VLT licensees retain 60%, compared with 85% on ordinary retail sports wagering. The State share funds local impact grants, the agency, the Purse Dedication Account and the Small, Minority, and Women-Owned Businesses Account at VLT ratios, then 5% to the General Fund, with the remainder to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","Referendum (Section 2): the Act goes to a November 2026 referendum under Art. XIX, § 1(e). If approved it takes effect January 1, 2027; if rejected it is void. Section 3 takes effect July 1, 2026 solely to enable the referendum. The bill is a reintroduction of HB 1048/25 and is cross-filed with SB 639."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses historical horse racing. The Alaska (HB145/SB194), Georgia (HB 686, HR450, SR131) and Hawaii (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) bills would authorize sports wagering from scratch, and Illinois HB5142 expands the sports wagering definition to cover prediction markets. HB1240 instead expands an existing regime to a slot-like pari-mutuel product, routed through the sports wagering statute but taxed and distributed on the VLT model. It is unusual in barring the product online, capping terminals at 4,000 and requiring voter approval as an expansion of commercial gaming.","watch":"Operators should note that the bill never defines \"historical horse race,\" that mobile and most Class B licensees are excluded, and that the Commission controls terminal allocation within the 4,000 cap and approval of every pool and wager type, all of which will shape who captures the market if the November 2026 referendum passes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":23838,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:07.917Z"},"MD-HB1409":{"summary":"HB1409 raises Maryland's effective tax on sports wagering and fantasy competitions by one percentage point and earmarks that new 1% for the Problem Gambling Fund. It also requires the Maryland Department of Health to run a separate prevalence study, with five-year replications, on problem and pathological mobile gambling, and requires annual reporting on how the Fund is spent. It takes effect July 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Retention cuts in State Gov't § 9-1E-12(b)(1): Class A-1/A-2 and B-1/B-2 retail licensees drop from 85% to 84% of proceeds, and mobile licensees from 80% to 79%. This moves the effective rates from 15% to 16% retail and from 20% to 21% online.","Fantasy competition operators drop from 85% to 84% retained under § 9-1D-04(a). The Commission must send 1% of fantasy proceeds to the Problem Gambling Fund, with the rest still going to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","New § 9-1E-12(b)(2)(ii)1 directs the Commission to distribute 1% of 'all sports wagering proceeds' to the Problem Gambling Fund (§ 9-1A-33). The existing 5% General Fund carve-out is reworded from proceeds 'attributable to mobile' to proceeds 'from online' sports wagering.","Health-General § 19-804 splits the prevalence study into in-person and mobile tracks. The initial mobile study must be completed by July 1, 2031 by an independent researcher using current diagnostic criteria, with replications at least every 5 years.","New § 9-1A-33(b)(7) requires the Secretary of Health to report to the General Assembly by November 1, 2027 and annually after that on Problem Gambling Fund spending on treatment and prevention.","Effective date is July 1, 2026. The bill is cross-filed with SB 862 and reintroduces HB 700 of 2025."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the Hawaii bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569), which create a problem-gambling fund inside a brand-new legalization and licensing framework, HB1409 funds an existing fund by shaving operator retention in a mature market. Its earmark-via-rate-increase approach most resembles Illinois HB3159, which adds a 2% AGR levy dedicated to fire and EMS. Illinois HB1451, HB2580 and SB 118 instead expand DHS gambling-disorder education programs without touching tax rates. The dedicated mobile-gambling prevalence study is unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the 1-point retention cut (to 79% mobile, 84% retail and fantasy) survives or is amended, and whether the 1% to the Problem Gambling Fund is read as an additional levy or as a carve-out from state proceeds. The drafting of § 9-1E-12(b)(2)(ii)1 ('1% of all sports wagering proceeds') is ambiguous on that point, and a mobile-gambling prevalence study could later be used to justify further responsible-gaming mandates or rate increases.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6966,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:11.460Z"},"MD-HB1431":{"summary":"HB1431 amends State Government Article § 9-1E-15(h)(4) to require Maryland's Sports Wagering Application Review Commission (SWARC) to accept and consider applications for sports wagering licenses submitted before January 1, 2028, subject to the limitations in § 9-1E-06. In effect, it keeps the licensing window open for new sports wagering facility and mobile applicants through the end of 2027. It sits alongside SWARC's existing diversity and minority-business outreach mandates.","mechanics":["Adds new item (iii) to § 9-1E-15(h)(4): SWARC 'shall' accept and consider an application for a sports wagering license submitted before January 1, 2028.","The purpose clause covers both sports wagering facility licenses and mobile sports wagering licenses.","The new duty is expressly subject to the limitations under § 9-1E-06, the existing licensing provisions, so it does not on its face lift any statutory license-number caps.","Existing duties are retained unchanged: pursuing racial, ethnic and gender diversity in awards, and encouraging MBE and small, minority or women-owned applicants.","Takes effect July 1, 2026. The bill is a reintroduction of HB 526 from the 2024 session and is assigned to House Ways and Means, with a hearing on March 5."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike Illinois HB2878 and HB5034, which raise master sports wagering license fees to a $250,000 application fee plus a $15,000,000 license fee and strip provisions on the number of online licenses, HB1431 changes no fees or caps and only fixes the application window. It is likewise far narrower than the authorizing frameworks in Alaska HB145/SB194, Hawaii HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569 and Georgia HB 686, which build licensing regimes from scratch. It is a single-sentence procedural fix to an existing mature market.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the guaranteed application window through 2027 brings in new mobile or facility competitors under the remaining § 9-1E-06 capacity, and whether amendments in Ways and Means alter those caps or attach equity-related conditions to late applicants.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1950,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:11.499Z"},"MD-HB1343":{"summary":"HB1343 would create a new Subtitle 1F in the State Government Article authorizing the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to license online casino-style gaming, including live dealer games from in-state studios. It sets the state's revenue share and how it is distributed, adds social equity ownership rules, consumer protection and labor peace requirements, and authorizes multistate compacts. The Act is subject to a statewide referendum. Revenue distributions begin in fiscal 2028, and a Video Lottery Facility Employee Displacement Fund is created for displaced casino workers.","mechanics":["Revenue split (§9-1F-05(b)): licensees keep 80% of live dealer proceeds and 45% of all other iGaming proceeds, so the effective state take is 20% and 55%. 'Proceeds' deduct a tiered share of redeemed free play and promotional credits for the first 5 years only, falling from 35% (proceeds under $4M) to 20% (over $12M).","Eligible licensees (§9-1F-03(b)): video lottery operators; sports wagering facility licensees under §9-1E-06(a)(2)(i)2 or 3; and Maryland-headquartered applicants. The Maryland applicants need 10+ years of in-state HQ, 250+ in-state employees, a prior Commission qualification for a 5%+ VLT equity stake (2018-2025), a Maryland brand (waivable after 1 year), and a $5M commitment to a live gaming or film studio.","Social equity licensing: video lottery operators can get an extra license if partnered with a 33% social equity joint venture. Five more licenses (plus any unclaimed VLT licenses) go out in two competitive rounds, the first limited to applicants with 33% social equity ownership. All licensees need at least 5% social equity ownership or a qualifying profit-sharing plan with nonmanagement employees.","Licensing terms: $1M initial license fee, which may be paid in installments, and a 5-year term. Renewal costs 1% of average annual retained proceeds over the prior 3 years. Each licensee may contract with only one Internet gaming operator (§9-1F-02(a)(2)), and ownership cannot be transferred for 3 years.","Supplier and operator rules: no license if an affiliate knowingly takes revenue from FATF blacklist jurisdictions, state sponsors of terrorism, or online casino markets where the activity is prohibited. Annual disclosure of revenue from supplying game content to illegal interactive gaming markets is required. Clear and convincing proof of labor peace agreements and union neutrality is required (§9-1F-04(c)).","Consumer and integrity rules: minimum age 21 with periodic identity reverification; credit card ban; mandatory deposit limits, with increases delayed 24 hours; monthly certification of the problem gambling disclosure; a voluntary exclusion list; no targeting of ads to prohibited or at-risk persons and no false advertising; penalties up to $1M plus a suspension of at least 30 days for tampering with games (§9-1F-07)."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogues on the list are Illinois HB3080 and HB4797 (Internet Gaming Act), which likewise channel iGaming through licensees and contracted management service providers. HB1343 differs by capping each license at one operator, layering heavy social equity set-asides, labor peace mandates, and a statewide referendum, and setting an unusually high 55% effective rate on non-live-dealer play. Most other listed bills, such as Alaska HB145/SB194, Georgia HB 686, and Hawaii HB 1308/SB1569, authorize sports wagering rather than online casino. Connecticut HB05229 overlaps only on consumer protections such as withdrawals and targeting. The bill is a reintroduction of Maryland HB 17 from 2025.","watch":"Operators should watch the 55% rate on non-live-dealer play, the promotional deduction that shrinks with scale and expires after 5 years, the one-operator-per-license market-access limit, and the illegal-market revenue disclosure, which could disqualify suppliers or affiliates active in grey markets.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":55015,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:15.919Z"},"MD-HB1578":{"summary":"Maryland Chapter 82 (HB1578) reauthorizes the State's Minority Business Enterprise (MBE) Program for five more years, moving its sunset from July 1, 2026 to July 1, 2031, and enacts new legislative findings based on the 2025 MBE disparity study. It extends the same 2031 sunset to the MBE compliance obligations that apply to cannabis licensees, offshore wind applicants, public-private partnerships, video lottery terminal (casino) licensees, and sports wagering licensees. It also tightens agency reporting deadlines and makes administrative changes to the Office of Small, Minority, and Women Business Affairs (OSMWBA).","mechanics":["State Government §9–1E–07(b)(4): sports wagering licensees' duty to comply with the MBE Program 'to the extent practicable and authorized by the U.S. Constitution' now sunsets July 1, 2031 instead of July 1, 2026. OSMWBA must set an MBE participation plan within 6 months of license issuance, covering construction, equipment, and ongoing services.","State Government §9–1A–10: video lottery terminal applicants and licensees are now subject to a duty to make a good-faith effort to meet the unit MBE goal, and a new subsection (a) directly requires licensee MBE compliance. OSMWBA monitors compliance and reports to the Commission every 6 months, and the Commission may take immediate action on noncompliance. These provisions sunset July 1, 2031.","New SFP §14–301.1 replaces the prior findings with findings based on the '2025 MBE Disparity Study', citing statistically significant disparities consistent with discrimination, to support Croson narrow-tailoring. The general MBE sunset in §14–309 moves to July 1, 2031, and the next disparity study is due September 30, 2030.","State agency annual MBE and Small Business Reserve reports are now due within 60 days of fiscal year-end instead of 90 days, with new quarterly reporting. OSMWBA's summary reports move to the 5th day of the regular legislative session.","OSMWBA must adopt regulations for a certification review process (§14–503(a)(2)). Joint ventures meeting the size criteria now qualify as a 'small business' (§14–501(d)(4)). The Secretary of Social and Economic Mobility replaces the OSMWBA Special Secretary as chair of the procurement subcabinet, and the Secretary of Labor is added.","Effective June 1, 2026; approved April 14, 2026."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the listed sports wagering bills, which authorize, tax, or regulate wagering directly, this is a procurement reauthorization that touches gaming only by extending MBE obligations on Maryland sports wagering and VLT licensees. Examples of those direct-regulation bills include AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569, and IL HB2878 and HB3159. The closest analogues are other licensing-condition bills such as CO SB 163, but none of the listed bills imposes race-conscious supplier-diversity goals on gaming licensees. That feature is distinctive to Maryland's 2021 sports wagering law, which this bill keeps alive through 2031.","watch":"Operators holding Maryland sports wagering or VLT licenses should plan for five more years of MBE participation plans and compliance monitoring, which for VLT licensees includes semiannual OSMWBA reports to the Commission and the risk of immediate Commission action. They should also track whether the 2025 disparity-study findings withstand post-SFFA equal-protection challenges that could undercut these license conditions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":46074,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:24.186Z"},"MD-HB1612":{"summary":"As introduced, HB1612 amends Md. State Government § 9-1E-17(d) to require, rather than merely permit, any sports wagering licensee or operator that advertises in Maryland to contract with an independent evaluator licensed under § 9-1E-17(b) to evaluate and rate its sports wagering content, experts, influencers, and content partners. The mandate is contingent on the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission licensing at least three independent evaluators, and would take effect July 1, 2026. The current title references campaign finance contributions and a study on independent evaluation, which suggests amendments in the first chamber, but the text provided is the first reader and contains neither provision.","mechanics":["Section 1 changes 'may' to 'shall' in § 9-1E-17(d), converting a voluntary independent-evaluator arrangement into a mandatory one.","Covers any sports wagering licensee or sports wagering operator that advertises in the State; there is no carve-out by size or advertising volume.","The evaluator must rate four categories: sports wagering content, sports wagering experts, sports wagering influencers, and content partners.","Evaluators must be licensed under existing § 9-1E-17(b).","Section 2 makes the Act contingent on the Commission issuing licenses to at least three independent evaluators.","Section 3 sets a July 1, 2026 effective date, subject to the Section 2 contingency; the text adds no new penalty, so enforcement would fall under existing Commission authority."],"issue_tags":["advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest listed analogue is CT HB05229, which regulates operator marketing directly by restricting advertising on college campuses and barring AI-driven bet targeting. Maryland instead imposes a third-party content-rating mandate covering influencers and content partners. The AK (HB145, SB194), GA (HB 686) and HI (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) bills would legalize and build out regulatory frameworks, whereas Maryland's bill tightens one compliance obligation in an existing market. The contingency tying effectiveness to a minimum of three licensed evaluators is unusual and is apparently meant to prevent a single-vendor bottleneck.","watch":"Get the amended (third reader) text, which apparently adds campaign finance contribution limits and replaces or supplements the mandate with a study, and track how many evaluators the Commission licenses, since that count triggers the mandatory-contract obligation and its cost.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"First - Sports Wagering - Independent Evaluation of Sports Wagering","text_chars":1849,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:27.099Z"},"MD-HB1453":{"summary":"HB1453 layers new player-protection and enforcement rules onto Maryland's casino (video lottery) and sports wagering regimes. It caps individual wagers at $100,000 per sporting event, bans credit cards for online sports wagering transactions, and raises the Commission's maximum per-violation fine tenfold. It also reallocates 1% of fantasy and online sports wagering proceeds to the Problem Gambling Fund and directs credit-extension rules and a central credit list for video lottery licensees.","mechanics":["Fines: § 9-1A-25(b) (video lottery) and § 9-1E-08(b) (sports wagering) raise the maximum penalty per violation from $5,000 to $50,000. Each day of violation remains a separate violation.","Wager cap: new § 9-1E-09(f) bars any sports wagering licensee from accepting a wager from an individual on any one sporting event in excess of $100,000.","Credit card ban: new § 9-1E-11(b)(2) bars mobile licensees from accepting a credit card as payment for any online sports wagering-related transaction.","Proceeds: the mobile licensee's 80% retention under § 9-1E-12(b)(1)(iv) is unchanged. Beginning FY2027, the 5% General Fund carve-out from mobile proceeds becomes 4% to the General Fund and 1% to the Problem Gambling Fund (§ 9-1A-33), and the wording changes from 'attributable to mobile' to 'from online' sports wagering.","Fantasy: under § 9-1D-04, fantasy operators' retention drops from 85% to 84%, with 1% of fantasy proceeds going to the Problem Gambling Fund.","Video lottery credit: new § 9-1A-24(h) requires regulations mandating 1-800-GAMBLER language on credit applications and additional review where outstanding credit payments exceed the player's net worth and net winnings. It also requires a Commission-run central list of credit and counter-check players that licensees must feed and monitor so cumulative credit does not exceed net worth. Effective July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB3159, which adds a new 2% levy on adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS departments, HB1453 leaves Maryland's sports wagering rate unchanged and only re-splits the existing 5% carve-out. It does, however, effectively raise the fantasy take from 15% to 16%. Its consumer-protection focus (credit card ban, per-event wager cap) overlaps most with CT HB05229, which addresses withdrawals from gaming accounts, customer service and AI-targeted betting rather than deposit methods or bet limits. A hard $100,000 per-event cap on all licensees, retail included, is unusual among the listed bills, none of which impose a statutory maximum wager.","watch":"Watch the $100,000 per-event cap and the credit card ban, which will hit high-limit and VIP business and payment flows (including whether 'any online sports wagering-related transaction' reaches withdrawals and third-party funding), along with per-day exposure under the tenfold fine increase.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":9568,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:28.153Z"},"MD-HB291":{"summary":"HB291, a departmental bill requested by the Lottery and Gaming Control Agency (cross-filed with SB 102), amends the definition of \"proceeds\" in State Government § 9-1E-01(h) to restructure the deduction for free promotional play. It removes the existing unlimited deduction for \"free bets and promotional credits redeemed by bettors\". In its place, it allows promotional play to be excluded from proceeds, but only up to a percentage cap set by the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission by regulation, applying after a licensee's first full fiscal year of operations.","mechanics":["Strikes current § 9-1E-01(h)(3), the uncapped deduction of free bets and promotional credits redeemed by bettors, and retains deductions for winnings paid, cash equivalents of prizes, and federal excise taxes.","New § 9-1E-01(h)(2)(i) excludes from proceeds money given away by a licensee as free promotional play and used by players to bet on a sporting event.","New § 9-1E-01(h)(2)(ii) provides that, after the first full fiscal year of operations, the exclusion may not exceed a Commission-set percentage of the licensee's prior-fiscal-year retained proceeds.","The cap base differs by license type: for facility licensees, proceeds retained under § 9-1E-12(b)(1)(ii) or (iii); for mobile licensees, online proceeds retained under § 9-1E-12(b)(1)(iv).","The bill sets no statutory percentage and delegates the figure entirely to Commission regulation.","Effective July 1, 2026. Hearing in House Ways and Means scheduled for February 5."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills either authorize sports wagering and set a tax (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) or adjust rates and fees in existing markets (IL HB3159's added 2% AGR levy, IL HB2878/HB5034's $15 million license fee). HB291 instead changes the tax base by capping the promotional-play deduction, which raises effective tax without touching the headline rate. Unlike capped-deduction models that fix a percentage in statute, HB291 leaves the percentage entirely to the Commission's rulemaking.","watch":"Because the cap percentage will be set by Commission regulation rather than statute, operators should prepare to engage in the rulemaking. They should also scrutinize how \"first full fiscal year of operations\" and the prior-year retained-proceeds base under § 9-1E-12(b)(1) are interpreted, particularly for established licensees, which would be capped immediately from July 1, 2026.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2869,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:36.767Z"},"MD-HB1587":{"summary":"Chapter 669 loosens Maryland's grandfathered electronic instant bingo regime. It amends the Criminal Law Article's slot-machine and bingo provisions so that eligible commercial and qualified-organization operators face fewer machine and ticket restrictions. It also orders the State Lottery and Gaming Control Agency to commission an independent study, due December 1, 2026, on expanding electronic instant bingo at commercial facilities and adding e-bingo, historical horse racing machines or underutilized VLTs at off-track satellite simulcast facilities.","mechanics":["Slot-machine definition, Crim. Law §12-301(3)(iv): the handheld bingo-device exclusion's cap on cards played at once is changed. The text as supplied shows the cap going from 54 to 100, though the bracketing suggests an earlier version would have repealed the cap entirely; the chaptered final should be checked.","Grandfather conditions, §12-308(a): the requirement that machines were in operation during 2007 or under a commercial license on December 31, 2007 is deleted. The machine cap now reads 'twice' the number in operation on February 28, 2008, and Chapter 603 of 2012, §3 drops the parallel cap on repair and replacement. However, the final title omits 'Maximum Number of Machines', so it should be confirmed whether the doubling survived amendment.","New §12-308(b) and (c): machines need not print a paper ticket for each instant bingo game unless the player elects to, and the Commission must adopt implementing regulations.","New §12-301.1(e)(1): the rule that unlicensed electronic gaming devices as of January 1, 2013 are illegal does not apply to electronic instant bingo machines authorized under §12-308.","Section 2 study: due December 1, 2026, prepared by an independent third-party vendor. The vendor must include the Agency and stakeholders from the e-bingo industry, simulcast facilities both at and away from VLT facilities, and gaming labor unions. Required topics include State revenue, revenue lost to neighboring states, and cannibalization of VLT facility revenue, plus the impact of sports wagering on stand-alone simulcast facilities (§2(d)(4)).","Effective July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What stands out is that Maryland expands a narrow, 2008-era grandfathered class of electronic bingo machines by statute, rather than through casino or VLT licensing. The bill pairs that with a study that could open stand-alone OTB and simulcast sites to historical horse racing machines or VLTs, explicitly weighing cannibalization of the State's six casinos and the effect of sports wagering.","watch":"Watch the December 1, 2026 Agency report and the Commission's implementing regulations. They will set up 2027 legislation on more machines and HHR or VLT placement at satellite simulcast sites, which could reshape retail competition for casino and sportsbook licensees and revive debate over how sports wagering affects legacy gaming outlets.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":12836,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:36.963Z"},"MD-HB195":{"summary":"HB195 adds to the Maryland State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission's study duties under State Government \"9-109(b)(1)(v)\" a mandate to identify trends and emerging technology that make gambling safer and more transparent, specifically cybersecurity, AI-based customer behavior monitoring for responsible gaming, and digital identity, age, facial recognition and biometric verification. Where the Commission lacks resources or expertise for these studies, or for its sports wagering technology and remote surveillance duties under \"9-1E-03(a)(2)\", it must partner with or contract with a Maryland university, a foundation, or a private entity, and the Agency Director or a designee must fully participate. The amended title passed by the chamber 43-0 refers to a Workgroup on Gambling Prevention Technology, but the first-reader text provided does not contain any workgroup provisions.","mechanics":["New \"9-109(b)(1)(v)\" requires studies identifying emerging technology, including: (1) cybersecurity such as data encryption, multifactor authentication, secure payment gateways and blockchain; (2) AI to protect customers, monitor behavioral patterns and promote responsible gaming; and (3) digital identity verification of age and identity, including facial recognition and biometrics to reduce identity fraud.","New \"9-109(c)\": if the Commission lacks resources or expertise, it shall collaborate with, or contract with, an in-state institution of higher education, a foundation, or a private entity to study best practices.","New \"9-1E-03(a)(3)-(4)\" applies the same collaborate-or-contract mechanism to the Commission's existing duty in the sports wagering subtitle to consider technology and remote surveillance (tied to \"9-1E-04\").","The Agency Director or designee must communicate with, and fully participate in, any outside study.","Existing reporting provisions are redesignated as \"9-109(d)\" without substantive change.","Effective October 1, 2026; no new operator obligations, fees, penalties or rulemaking mandates appear in the introduced text."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which would bar operators from using artificial intelligence to target certain bets, MD HB195 treats AI favorably, as a tool the regulator should study for behavioral monitoring and responsible gaming, and it imposes no operator mandate. It is narrower than the operational reforms in CO SB 163, which changes commission-to-division delegation, and the broad Florida rewrite in FL H0189. It is closer in spirit to the problem-gambling education bills IL HB1451 and HB2580, but it targets regulator technology rather than public outreach. Its distinctive feature is the mandatory turn to outside academic, foundation or private partners whenever the Commission lacks the expertise to do the work itself.","watch":"Watch whether the final amended version, including the Workgroup on Gambling Prevention Technology named in the title, turns these studies into recommendations for mandatory operator requirements on AI behavioral monitoring, biometric or facial-recognition identity verification, or cybersecurity standards, and which private entities are engaged to shape them.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"First - State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission - Authority to Conduct Studies on the Use of Emerging Technology - Collaboration","text_chars":7567,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:42.367Z"},"MD-HB46":{"summary":"HB46 would have created a Gambling Prevention Technology Pilot Program in the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission. Under it, Bowie State University and Morgan State University campuses would get designated \"no gambling zones\", and every online sports wagering operator would have to geofence those zones and block wagering inside them. The sponsor withdrew the bill on February 23, 2026, so it is dead for this session.","mechanics":["New State Government Article § 9–1E–18. Bowie State and Morgan State must participate. The Commission, working with the universities and the Maryland Center of Excellence on Problem Gambling, identifies no-gambling-zone boundaries, develops signage, and implements cost-effective technologies, including real-time monitoring of online sports wagering activity (§ (d)).","Each online sports wagering operator must establish a geofence around each designated zone that prevents access to its platform, and must collaborate with the Commission on enforcement (§ (e)(1)).","An individual who attempts to wager inside the geofence must be blocked and shown a warning about the pilot program and options for responsible gambling assistance (§ (e)(2)).","Safe harbor: the Commission may not fine, penalize or sanction an operator for unforeseeable technological failures within the geofenced boundaries during the pilot (§ (g)).","The Commission sets the timeline and implementation steps (§ (f)) and must report to the Governor and General Assembly by January 1, 2031 on whether geofencing is effective and viable (§ (h)).","The Act would take effect July 1, 2026 and sunset automatically on June 30, 2031."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were provided. Most campus-gambling measures elsewhere target marketing to students or university partnership deals. This bill is unusual because it requires operators to geofence and block wagering at specific campus locations, which goes beyond the usual statewide-border geolocation duty. It also pairs that mandate with an explicit safe harbor for unforeseeable technical failures.","watch":"The bill is withdrawn, but the concept could return, so watch for a version that drops the § (g) safe harbor, expands the zones to other campuses, or turns the real-time monitoring language into data-sharing obligations on operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4665,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:48.060Z"},"MD-HB295":{"summary":"HB295 creates a new criminal offense (Criminal Law § 12-115) barring any person from operating, conducting, or promoting an \"interactive game\" in Maryland. The definition targets online dual- or multi-currency sweepstakes-style games that simulate casino games, lottery games, or sports wagering. It also adds licensing conditions under State Government Title 9, which the bill extends to sports wagering licensees through § 9-1E-03. Applicants and licensees must disclose revenue earned from illegal interactive gaming markets, and the Commission is barred from licensing or allowing operations by anyone whose affiliates knowingly take revenue from certain high-risk jurisdictions or from online casino gaming where it is prohibited.","mechanics":["Definition: an \"interactive game\" (§ 12-115(a)(3)) involves consideration, chance, and reward; is available online or on a mobile or similar device; uses multiple currency systems that let the player exchange currency for a prize, cash, or cash equivalent; and simulates casino games, lottery games (draw, instant, keno, bingo), or sports wagering. Games awarding only noncash prizes that are free to play or offline are excluded.","Penalties: a knowing violation is a misdemeanor punishable by up to 3 years' imprisonment and/or a fine of $10,000 to $100,000, with fines paid to the Problem Gambling Fund. A violation is \"knowing\" if the Commission issued and published a cease and desist notice to the person, or if a licensee or applicant keeps doing business with a person who received a published notice. A finding of violation triggers mandatory license denial or revocation.","Vendor reporting: Commission licensees and applicants (Subtitles 1A, 1B, 1D, 1E) must report annually any business relationship with a financial institution, payment processor, geolocation provider, gaming content supplier, platform provider, or media affiliate known to support, operate, conduct, or promote an interactive game in Maryland.","Revenue disclosure (§ 9-1A-07(g)): on application and annually, an applicant or licensee must disclose every jurisdiction where it or any affiliate directly or indirectly accepted revenue from an interactive game in a jurisdiction that prohibits such games during the prior 12 months. A material misrepresentation or omission may support denial or sanctions against the licensee and its officers and board members.","Revenue bar (§ 9-1A-08.1): the Commission may not license, or allow to commence or continue operations, any person whose affiliates (including entities under common control) knowingly accept revenue directly or indirectly derived from (1) FATF call-for-action high-risk jurisdictions, (2) US-designated state sponsors of terrorism, or (3) online casino gaming in jurisdictions where it is prohibited. Revocation requires notice and a hearing plus a public-interest finding.","The bill also changes the false-statement offense in § 9-1A-07(h) from \"an individual\" to \"a person,\" which reaches entities. Effective date is July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization and tax bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), HB295 is an enforcement bill aimed at sweepstakes casinos and at licensed operators' exposure to grey and black markets. FL S1580 and FL H0189 also tighten illegal-gambling penalties, and IL HB5142 pulls prediction markets into the definition of sports wagering. None of the listed bills, however, pairs a sweepstakes ban with affiliate-level disclosure of revenue from illegal markets and a licensing bar tied to FATF, state-sponsor-of-terrorism, and prohibited online casino jurisdictions. The vendor reporting duty, covering payment processors, geolocation, content, platform, and media partners, is also unusual and extends compliance burdens to licensees' supply chains.","watch":"Watch the affiliate-revenue bar in § 9-1A-08.1(a)(3) and the disclosure in § 9-1A-07(g): \"directly or indirectly derived\" revenue from online casino gaming in prohibited jurisdictions, measured across all affiliates under common control, could jeopardize a Maryland sports wagering license held by a multinational group with B2C or B2B activity in unregulated markets, and the annual vendor-relationship reporting could force operators to drop shared payment, geolocation, content, or media partners that also serve sweepstakes platforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":10060,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:54.669Z"},"MD-HB420":{"summary":"HB420 creates a new State Government Article Subtitle 14A (§§ 10-14A-01 to 10-14A-05) letting active service members and their spouses who relocate to Maryland on military orders practice under an occupational or professional license issued by another state for the duration of those orders, without obtaining a Maryland license. It then threads an 'except as provided in § 10-14A-03' carve-out through dozens of licensing statutes, including the video lottery/table games licensing requirement in § 9-1A-06(a) and (c)(1) and the sports wagering licensing requirement in § 9-1E-05(a).","mechanics":["Eligibility (§ 10-14A-03(a)): an active service member (as defined in § 9-901, including reservists on active duty orders) or spouse who relocates because of orders and holds another state's license. The applicant must submit proof of orders, a marriage certificate if a spouse, and a notarized affidavit. The applicant must also be in good standing, have no disciplinary action or pending unprofessional-conduct investigation, and have no surrender of the license while under investigation.","Timing (§ 10-14A-03(b)): if the licensing authority cannot verify the license within 30 days of receiving the application, it may issue a temporary license with the same rights as a permanent one.","Background checks (§ 10-14A-03(c)): the licensing authority may, but is not required to, run a criminal history records check before authorizing practice or issuing a temporary license.","Scope and discipline (§§ 10-14A-03(d), 10-14A-04): practice is limited to the discipline and scope allowed in the issuing state. The individual is subject to the same disciplinary authority as Maryland licensees. A Maryland license can be required only for a different discipline or scope.","Gaming carve-outs: § 9-1A-06(a) and (c)(1) (video lottery operators, manufacturers, suppliers and video lottery employees) and § 9-1E-05(a) (sports wagering operators, online operators, suppliers and individuals directly employed in sports wagering) become subject to the § 10-14A-03 exception.","Exclusion and rulemaking: Subtitle 14A does not apply where an interstate compact already provides portability (§ 10-14A-02). Each licensing authority may adopt implementing regulations (§ 10-14A-05)."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills deal with military licensing portability. AK HB145 and SB194 move the opposite way, building criminal history record checks into mobile sports wagering licensure, whereas HB420 makes such checks discretionary for qualifying service members and spouses entering Maryland's gaming workforce. The Illinois license-structure bills (e.g., IL HB2878, HB5034) and CO SB 163's delegation of licensing duties change how gaming regulators license, but none create a cross-state exemption from gaming licensure. HB420 is unusual in that it reaches sports wagering and casino licensing only as one of many technical conforming amendments in a general occupational licensing bill.","watch":"Watch whether the Maryland Lottery and Gaming Control Agency uses its § 10-14A-05 rulemaking power to require criminal history checks and suitability review for service members and spouses working under out-of-state sports wagering or casino employee licenses, and how it treats operator-level licensees given that § 9-1E-05(a) as amended covers operators and suppliers as well as individual employees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":195198,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:55.550Z"},"MD-HB518":{"summary":"HB518 tightens Maryland's responsible-gaming rules across the lottery, video lottery, fantasy and sports wagering programs. It requires mandatory voluntary exclusion lists with opt-out referral to the Maryland Center for Excellence on Problem Gambling, and raises the fantasy participation age to 21 with a grandfather clause. It bans player-specific proposition bets on collegiate athletes, imposes new mobile-wagering account controls including a credit card ban and mandatory deposit limits, and requires operators to share anonymized transactional data with three state universities.","mechanics":["Voluntary exclusion and referral: §§ 9-109(d), 9-1A-24(e), 9-1D-05 and 9-1E-04(b)(7)-(8) and (d) make voluntary exclusion lists mandatory for the lottery, video lottery, fantasy and sports wagering. The Commission must pass each enrollee's contact information to the Center for Excellence on Problem Gambling unless the individual affirmatively declines. Section 2 requires a 'NO CONTACT' checkbox on the application form by August 1, 2026.","Fantasy age limit: § 9-1D-02(b) bars anyone under 21 from fantasy competitions and bars operators from offering contests or services to them. Accounts opened by 18-20 year olds before July 1, 2026 are exempt.","College player props: § 9-1E-09(f) prohibits licensees from offering or accepting, and individuals from placing, player-specific proposition bets on participants in collegiate sporting events. A 'proposition bet' is defined as a wager on an action, statistic, occurrence or nonoccurrence not directly connected to the final outcome or final score.","Mobile operator duties under § 9-1E-11(b):\n- Verify age and identity at account creation, with periodic reverification.\n- Display 1-800-GAMBLER messaging at log-on and log-off.\n- Require every account holder to set a self-imposed deposit limit and lockout period.\n- Offer a temporary self-suspension tool.\n- Prohibit credit cards for all online wagering transactions.\n- Show a problem-gambling disclosure at login, certified before account opening and monthly thereafter.","Account controls under §§ 9-1E-11(f)-(h):\n- No wagering-related email during a self-suspension of 72 hours or more.\n- Controls cannot be changed while a suspension is in effect.\n- Deposit-limit increases take effect only after the later of 24 hours or expiry of the prior limit period.\n- Withdrawals remain available during a suspension.","Data sharing and effective date: new § 9-1E-18 requires licensees, by September 1 each year, to provide quarterly transactional data and metrics to Morgan State, Bowie State and the UMD School of Medicine. The data covers bans and suspensions, complaints, ages, geolocation, responsible-gaming tool use, and balances, bet amounts, bonus usage, deposits and withdrawals. It must also include counts of players flagged for erratic gambling and the related bans. Personal data under Com. Law § 14-4801 is excluded, the data is exempt from the Public Information Act, and the universities may disclose it only for research or peer-reviewed or prevalence studies. The Act takes effect July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"CT HB05229 is the closest analogue: like HB518 it layers consumer-protection duties on licensees, but it focuses on campus advertising, withdrawal rules and AI bet targeting rather than prop bans, deposit limits and research data-sharing. HB518's under-21 rule for fantasy parallels the under-21 bar that IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act) would impose on prediction markets. IL HB1451 and HB2580 address gambling-disorder education through a state agency rather than operator mandates. DE HB314 also addresses student-athlete protection, but HB518 is unusual in pairing a collegiate player-prop ban with a statutory requirement to hand operator transactional data to named universities.","watch":"Operators should prepare for July 1, 2026 compliance with the credit card ban, mandatory deposit-limit setup and monthly disclosure certification, and should scrutinize the ambiguous September 1 'quarterly' data-delivery obligation and its data-confidentiality safeguards as the bill moves through the Senate.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":18501,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:05.748Z"},"MD-HB731":{"summary":"HB731 (cross-filed with SB 456) expands Maryland's statutory definition of \"fantasy competition\" to expressly cover player-versus-player pick'em contests in which participants select higher or lower projected or actual statistics for two or more real athletes, and carves those contests out of the sports wagering definitions in § 9-1E-01. It also converts the optional fantasy operator registration fee into a mandatory $50,000 annual fee and requires the Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to run a fantasy-specific voluntary exclusion program, with operator duties and prize forfeiture.","mechanics":["§ 9-1D-01(d): fantasy competition now covers contests where participants \"assemble\" teams or rosters. Prizes may be shared and may be fixed, tiered or conditional bonus payouts if the prize value and structure are disclosed in advance. Participants must compete against other participants, expressly including higher/lower pick'em on two or more athletes. The existing bars on outcomes based solely on one athlete or on real-world team scores or spreads are retained.","§ 9-1D-02(a): a compliant fantasy competition \"does not constitute betting, wagering, or gambling for any purpose.\" §§ 9-1E-01(i)(1)(vii), (i)(2)(ii) and (j)(2) exclude regulated fantasy competitions, including the individual-stat carve-out, from \"sporting event\" and \"sports wagering.\" The in-state kiosk ban in § 9-1D-02(b) remains.","§ 9-1D-03(c): the Commission \"shall\" (previously \"may\") impose an annual registration fee of $50,000 per fantasy operator, distributed to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","New § 9-1D-05(a): each operator must maintain a program to curtail compulsive play and display a problem-gambling assistance statement on its website or app and on all marketing materials and advertisements.","New § 9-1D-05(b)-(f): the Commission must create a voluntary exclusion list and fold it into its other self-exclusion programs. Operators may not let listed individuals open accounts or play; they may allow view and download of transaction history only. Operators must report any participation by an excluded individual. Prizes won by excluded individuals are forfeited and remitted quarterly to the Problem Gambling Fund (§ 9-1A-33). The Commission may sanction operators that knowingly fail to exclude.","Effective October 1, 2026; the Commission retains general rulemaking authority under renumbered § 9-1D-06."],"issue_tags":["legalize","prop","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB2342, which builds a fuller Gaming Board regulatory regime for fantasy operators with fees, surcharges, civil penalties and a levy on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts, HB731 keeps Maryland's light registration model and adds only a flat $50,000 fee, with no revenue-based tax. It moves in the opposite direction from IL HB5142, which widens \"sports wagering\" to capture individual athlete performance statistics (there via prediction markets). HB731 instead expressly removes stat-based pick'em from the sports wagering definition when it is offered as a compliant fantasy contest. FL H0189 also revises fantasy sports contest law within a broader gambling overhaul, but HB731 is notable for codifying peer-to-peer higher/lower pick'em as lawful fantasy rather than banning it or pulling it into the sportsbook regime.","watch":"Watch whether the pick'em language survives as written, because the requirement that participants compete against other participants (§ 9-1D-01(d)(3)) and the pre-disclosed prize-value rules would permit peer-to-peer pick'em but likely exclude house-banked fixed-odds props, creating a lower-cost, untaxed competitor channel to licensed sportsbooks; also note the drafting inconsistencies (\"mandatory exclusion list\" in the purpose paragraph and \"licensed\" in § 9-1D-05(b) versus a registration-only regime).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":11262,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:07.075Z"},"MD-HB758":{"summary":"HB758 changes how Maryland's problem gambling money may be spent without touching how the money is raised. It strikes the existing language in Health-General § 19-804(a)(2) that made research and training proper expenses of the Secretary's problem gambling service grants. It also adds a new authorized use of the Problem Gambling Fund under State Government § 9-1A-33(b)(4)(v): reimbursement for peers who provide behavioral health services to people in problem gambling treatment or prevention programs.","mechanics":["Health-General § 19-804(a): repeals paragraph (2), which said that research and training designed to improve or extend problem gambling services are proper items of expense. The seven listed service categories are renumbered but otherwise unchanged.","State Government § 9-1A-33(b)(4)(v): adds a new permitted expenditure from the Problem Gambling Fund. It covers reimbursement to support peers providing behavioral health services to individuals in the treatment and prevention programs under items (iii) and (iv), including programs aimed at sports wagering and fantasy-related problem gambling.","Leaves the rest of the Fund intact: its status as a special nonlapsing fund, the existing uses for a 24-hour hotline, outreach (including to voluntary exclusion list registrants), and treatment programs, the spillover of unspent money to drug and other addiction treatment under (b)(5), and the appropriation requirement under (b)(6).","Does not change any contribution, fee or tax paid by gaming or sports wagering licensees into the Fund.","Effective October 1, 2026. The bill is in House Ways and Means, with a hearing on February 19."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Illinois HB1451, HB2580 and SB 118 amend the Substance Use Disorder Act to expand DHS public education and awareness duties on gambling disorders. HB758 instead narrows one category of spending (research and training) and adds peer-support reimbursement. Hawaii HB1308, HB2570 and SB1569 create problem gambling funding as part of new sports wagering frameworks, while HB758 only reallocates uses within Maryland's existing Fund. Unusually, HB758 removes research and training as authorized expenses at the same time that other states are expanding responsible-gaming research and education.","watch":"Watch whether committee amendments add new or higher licensee contributions to fund the expanded peer-support use, and whether dropping research and training as a grant expense affects state-funded responsible-gaming research that operators rely on or partner with.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5401,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:10.698Z"},"MD-SB102":{"summary":"SB 102, a departmental bill requested by the Lottery and Gaming Control Agency (cross-filed with HB 291), changes how promotional play is treated in Maryland's sports wagering \"proceeds\" definition under State Government Article § 9–1E–01(h). It removes the current uncapped deduction for \"free bets and promotional credits redeemed by bettors.\" In its place it adds an exclusion for free promotional play, which the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission may cap by regulation as a percentage of a licensee's prior-year retained proceeds once the licensee has completed its first full fiscal year of operations. The practical effect is to expand the taxable base for operators that rely heavily on promotional credits.","mechanics":["Strikes former § 9–1E–01(h)(3), the deduction for free bets and promotional credits redeemed by bettors. Proceeds would then equal amounts wagered less winnings paid, the cash value of prizes awarded, and federal excise taxes paid.","New § 9–1E–01(h)(2)(i) excludes from proceeds money a sports wagering licensee gives away as free promotional play and that players use to bet on a sporting event.","New § 9–1E–01(h)(2)(ii) provides that, after the first full fiscal year of operations, the exclusion may not exceed a percentage the Commission sets by regulation. The bill itself sets no figure.","The cap's base differs by licensee type. For facility licensees it is total proceeds retained in the prior fiscal year under § 9–1E–12(b)(1)(ii) or (iii). For mobile licensees it is total online proceeds retained in the prior fiscal year under § 9–1E–12(b)(1)(iv).","Effective July 1, 2026. Hearing in Senate Budget and Taxation was set for January 21."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills that touch tax change rates or add new levies rather than the deduction base. Examples are IL HB3159 (an added 2% of adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS), AK HB145/SB194 (a new tax on mobile sports wagering), and IA SSB 1157 (tax rates and license fees). None of the listed bills targets the promotional-credit deduction. SB 102 is unusual because it raises the effective tax burden without touching the rate, by narrowing the promotional deduction. It also delegates the size of the cap entirely to the Commission's rulemaking rather than fixing a number in statute.","watch":"Operators should engage early in the Commission's rulemaking on the cap percentage, because the statute leaves it uncapped. They should also seek clarity on three points: whether \"after the first full fiscal year of operations\" makes the limit apply immediately to existing licensees on July 1, 2026; what \"retained\" proceeds under § 9–1E–12(b)(1) means as the base; and how unused or forfeited promotional credits are counted.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2880,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:11.381Z"},"MD-SB242":{"summary":"SB242 (Chapter 694 of 2026) creates a new State Government Article Subtitle 10-14A that lets an active service member, or the spouse of one, who relocates to Maryland on military orders practise an occupation or profession under a license issued by another state for as long as the orders last. It writes an exception to that new section into dozens of licensing statutes, including the video lottery licensing requirement (§ 9-1A-06) and the sports wagering licensing requirement (§ 9-1E-05). Per its purpose clause, it also broadens the 'eligible veteran' definition for licensing-process provisions, but that part is not in the excerpt provided.","mechanics":["Eligibility (§ 10-14A-03(a)): the applicant must submit to the licensing authority proof of orders, a marriage certificate if a spouse, and a notarized affidavit covering identity, truthfulness, understanding of Maryland scope of practice, and good standing in all states. The applicant must also be in good standing with the issuing regulator, have no disciplinary action and no pending unprofessional-conduct investigation, and not have surrendered the license while under investigation.","Processing (§ 10-14A-03(b)-(c)): if the authority cannot verify the license within 30 days of receiving the application, it may issue a temporary license with the same rights as a permanent one. The authority may, but is not required to, run a criminal history records check first.","Limits and oversight (§ 10-14A-03(d), § 10-14A-04): the individual may practise only within the discipline and scope of the out-of-state license and cannot be required to obtain a Maryland license unless going beyond that scope. Within that scope they may practise to the same extent as an in-state licensee and are subject to the same disciplinary authority.","Exclusions and terms (§§ 10-14A-01, 10-14A-02): the subtitle does not apply to occupations covered by an interstate compact. 'License' is defined broadly to include any permit, certification, registration or other authorization, and only licenses issued by another state (not another 'jurisdiction') qualify.","Gaming amendments: §§ 9-1A-06(a) and (c)(1) (video lottery operators, manufacturers, service providers and employees) and § 9-1E-05(a) (sports wagering operators, online operators, suppliers and individual sports wagering employees) now begin 'except as provided in § 10-14A-03'.","Rulemaking (§ 10-14A-05): each licensing authority, which would include the gaming regulator, may adopt implementing regulations. No effective date appears in the provided excerpt."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a military-spouse licensing portability measure, and none carves an out-of-state-license exception into a gaming licensing statute. The closest overlap is AK HB145 and SB194, which tie criminal history record checks to mobile sports wagering licensure; Maryland instead makes such checks optional for qualifying service members and spouses. CO SB 163 also deals with gaming licensing administration, but it concerns delegating licensing duties from Colorado's commission to its division rather than recognising other states' licenses. What is unusual here is that a general veterans-affairs reciprocity law quietly reaches the sports wagering and video lottery licensing provisions.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the Maryland Lottery and Gaming Control Commission adopts regulations under § 10-14A-05 to apply § 10-14A-03 to sports wagering and video lottery employee licenses, and how it handles background-check and suitability standards and the 30-day temporary-license rule for service-member and spouse employees holding another state's gaming license.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":187583,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:25.072Z"},"MD-SB282":{"summary":"SB282 (Chapter 4 of 2026) is Maryland's operating budget for fiscal 2027 (July 1, 2026 to June 30, 2027), enacted under Article III, Section 52 of the Maryland Constitution. It sets general, special and federal fund appropriations by agency and program, attaching conditions, contingent reductions and report-based spending restrictions. The portion of the text provided covers payments to civil divisions through the Department of Social and Economic Mobility and contains no provisions on sports wagering, the lottery or casino gaming.","mechanics":["A15O00.03 appropriates $1,600,000 in special funds for Admissions and Amusement Tax distributions to local governments, a general entertainment tax line rather than a wagering-specific one.","A15O00.01 sets Disparity Grants at $203,599,585 in general funds, reduced by $26,996,721 if legislation holds the grant at the fiscal 2026 level.","Several appropriations depend on companion legislation expanding the allowable uses of the Strategic Energy Investment Fund, including $100,000,000 at the PSC (C90G00.01) and $100,000,000 plus $13,000,000 at MEA (D13A13.08).","D23A01 funds the Maryland Cannabis Administration at $29,338,189 in special funds, and D24A01.01 shifts $2,279,883 to the Cannabis Business Assistance Fund, contingent on authorizing legislation.","Many lines withhold funds until agencies submit reports to the budget committees, which then have a 45-day review period, for example the Uninsured Employers' Fund report due October 1, 2026, and the VOCA report due November 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the excerpt is unusual for a gaming practice only because it is a general appropriations act. The nearest touchpoints are the admissions and amusement tax distribution and the cannabis regulatory appropriations, neither of which concerns wagering. Any Maryland Lottery and Gaming Control Agency appropriation or sports-wagering revenue line (for example, Blueprint for Maryland's Future fund transfers) would appear in later sections not included in the text provided.","watch":"Review the remainder of the chaptered budget, which is not in this excerpt, for the Maryland Lottery and Gaming Control Agency appropriation and any conditions, reporting requirements or contingencies tied to sports-wagering revenue or companion Budget Reconciliation and Financing Act legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":168139,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:27.526Z"},"MD-SB112":{"summary":"Maryland SB112, a departmental bill requested by the Lottery and Gaming Control Agency (cross-filed with HB 295), makes it a crime to operate, conduct, or promote sweepstakes-style \"interactive games\" in the State and requires Commission licensees to report business ties to vendors known to support them. It also requires casino and sports wagering applicants and licensees to disclose annually any revenue they or their affiliates took from interactive games in jurisdictions where those games are illegal. It bars licensing, or continued operation, where the licensee or any affiliate knowingly accepts revenue from FATF call-for-action jurisdictions, U.S.-designated state sponsors of terrorism, or online casino gaming in jurisdictions that prohibit it.","mechanics":["New Crim. Law §12-115 defines \"interactive game\" as an internet or mobile game, contest, or promotion that uses multiple currency systems redeemable for prizes, cash, or cash equivalents and simulates casino games, lottery games (including keno and bingo), or sports wagering. Games awarding solely noncash prizes are excluded.","Violation is a misdemeanor punishable by up to 3 years' imprisonment and/or a fine of $10,000 to $100,000. Under §12-115(c)(2), the Commission \"shall\" deny or revoke the violator's license under Subtitles 1A, 1B, 1D, or 1E.","Applicants and licensees must report annually any business relationship with a financial institution, payment processor, geolocation provider, gaming content supplier, platform provider, or media affiliate known to support, operate, conduct, or promote an interactive game in Maryland (§12-115(b)(2)).","New §9-1A-07(g) requires a disclosure on application and annually covering the prior 12 months. It must list the jurisdictions where the applicant, licensee, or any affiliate directly or indirectly accepted revenue from interactive games in an \"illegal interactive gaming market.\" A material misrepresentation or omission can ground denial, or sanctions under §9-1A-25 on the licensee and its officers and board members. The false-statement offense in renumbered (h) is broadened from \"individual\" to \"person.\"","New §9-1A-08.1 bars issuing a license where the applicant or any affiliate, including an entity under common control, knowingly accepts revenue directly or indirectly derived from (1) FATF high-risk call-for-action jurisdictions, (2) U.S.-designated state sponsors of terrorism, or (3) online casino gaming in jurisdictions that prohibit it. The Commission must stop operations on such a finding and may revoke after notice and hearing if that serves the public interest.","§9-1E-03(a)(1) is amended to apply §9-1A-08.1 to sports wagering licensees, their employees, and their contractors. The Act takes effect July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, authorize and tax new sports wagering markets, whereas SB112 is an enforcement and suitability measure for an existing market. It sits closer to FL H0189 and FL S1580, which revise Florida's illegal and internet gambling penalties, and to IL HB3733, which broadens Illinois' \"gambling device\" definition. None of the listed bills appears to tie licensure to an operator group's worldwide revenue sources, as SB112's affiliate revenue bar and illegal-market disclosure do. Its targeting of dual-currency sweepstakes is distinct from IL HB5142, which addresses sports-related prediction markets instead.","watch":"The affiliate-wide revenue bar in §9-1A-08.1(a)(3) and the illegal-market disclosure in §9-1A-07(g) could jeopardize Maryland licenses held by multinational operator groups with grey-market online casino revenue anywhere in the world, so watch closely how \"knowingly,\" \"indirectly derived,\" and \"common control\" are interpreted, especially since there is no de minimis threshold or cure period, alongside the vendor-relationship reporting duty.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8013,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:27.569Z"},"MD-SB418":{"summary":"SB418 (Chapter 493 of 2026) lets a member of the U.S. Foreign Service, or the member's spouse, who relocates to Maryland on an assignment or detail practice an occupation or profession under a license issued by another state for the length of that assignment, without obtaining a Maryland license. It then threads a cross-reference to new State Government § 10–14A–03 through dozens of licensing statutes. These include the video lottery licensing requirement (§ 9–1A–06(a) and (c)(1)) and the sports wagering licensing requirement (§ 9–1E–05(a)), so qualifying individuals can work in those roles without a Maryland gaming license.","mechanics":["New State Government Subtitle 14A (§§ 10–14A–01 to 10–14A–05) broadly defines 'license' as any license, permit, certification, registration or other legal authorization required for an occupation or profession. 'Licensing authority' means any State agency that issues one.","Under § 10–14A–03(a), the individual must hold another state's license and do all of the following: give the licensing authority a copy of the assignment or detail notice; remain in good standing with the issuing regulator and in all states where they hold or held a similar license; have actively used the license in the 2 years before relocating; and comply with Maryland practice standards, discipline and continuing education requirements.","Practice is limited to the discipline and scope of the out-of-state license (§ 10–14A–03(b)(1)). The licensing authority may not require a Maryland license unless the person seeks to practice beyond that scope (§ 10–14A–03(b)(2)).","Authorized individuals practice to the same extent as Maryland licensees and are subject to the same disciplinary authority (§ 10–14A–04). Each licensing authority may adopt implementing regulations (§ 10–14A–05).","§ 10–14A–02 carves out the practice of law and occupations already covered by an interstate compact.","§ 9–1E–05(a) (sports wagering licensees, including online operators, suppliers and employees under item (4)) and § 9–1A–06(a) and (c)(1) (video lottery operators, manufacturers, service providers and employees) now open with 'except as provided in § 10–14A–03.' The effective date is not shown in the portion of text provided."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills address professional licensing portability. Bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308 create sports wagering licensing regimes from scratch, and IL HB2878/HB5034 change master sports wagering license fees. IL HB2880/HB5035 loosen employment restrictions for regulator staff rather than licensees. SB418 is unusual because a general workforce-mobility statute silently amends Maryland's existing sports wagering and video lottery licensing provisions through a cross-reference.","watch":"Watch how the Lottery and Gaming Control Commission implements § 10–14A–03 for sports wagering and casino employees, including whether it can still require background checks and suitability review. Watch also whether operators may rely on another state's gaming employee license, and whether regulations under § 10–14A–05 confine the exception to individual employees under § 9–1E–05(a)(4) rather than entity licenses.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":163720,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:32.340Z"},"MD-SB639":{"summary":"SB639 adds \"historical horse race\" to the definition of \"sporting event\" in Maryland's sports wagering law (State Gov't §9-1E-01(i)(1)(vii)). It lets certain retail sports wagering facility licensees offer pari-mutuel wagering on historical horse races (HHR) on Commission-approved terminals, capped at 4,000 terminals statewide, and sets a separate revenue split for those proceeds. Because it expands commercial gaming, it takes effect only if voters approve it at the November 2026 referendum, with an effective date of January 1, 2027.","mechanics":["Eligible operators under new §9-1E-09(f): Class A-1 and A-2 licensees at video lottery facilities; Class A-2 horse racing licensees at thoroughbred or standardbred race courses; §11-524 licensees at the Timonium fairgrounds; and satellite simulcast facility owners or lessees. All other facility licensees and all mobile licensees are barred.","§9-1E-09.1 requires pari-mutuel play only, with no betting against the house, in a Commission-approved designated area, on approved days and hours, using approved wager types and terminals. The Commission may not authorize more than 4,000 terminals. Minimum wager is 10 cents, and the minimum payout may not be less than the amount wagered.","Terminal rules: race identity (location, date, horses, jockeys or drivers) is hidden until after the bet is placed; past performance data current as of the race date must be shown; odds or payouts must be posted; and results plus a replay must be shown after the wager. Player-funded pools may be used to cover minus pools.","Revenue (§9-1E-12(c)): video lottery operators keep the same percentage they keep on VLT proceeds under §9-1A-27; other licensees keep 60%. From the State share, local impact grants, the Lottery and Gaming Control Agency, the Purse Dedication Account and the Small, Minority, and Women-Owned Businesses Account get the same share they receive from VLT proceeds; 5% goes to the General Fund; the remainder goes to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","Effectiveness: referendum at the November 2026 general election under Art. XIX §1(e) of the Maryland Constitution. If approved, the Act takes effect January 1, 2027; if rejected, it is null and void. The Act takes effect July 1, 2026 solely to allow the referendum to be held."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the listed bills that authorize mobile sports wagering from scratch (AK HB145 and SB194, HI HB1308, HB2570 and SB1569, GA HB686), SB639 extends Maryland's existing sports wagering framework to historical horse racing, a slot-like pari-mutuel product. It also expressly bars mobile licensees from offering HHR. The closest analogues are definitional expansions such as IL HB5142, which would fold prediction markets into \"sports wagering\", but SB639 uniquely pairs the expansion with a VLT-style revenue split and a mandatory voter referendum. It is a reintroduction of SB 982 (2025) and is cross-filed with a companion bill (6lr0860).","watch":"Mobile and Class B operators are excluded, and HHR revenue tracks VLT retention rates or a 60% retention rate rather than the 80–85% sports wagering rates, so watch for amendments widening eligibility or adjusting the split, the 4,000-terminal cap, and whether the referendum makes the November 2026 ballot.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":23839,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:39.415Z"},"MD-SB557":{"summary":"SB557 narrows the definition of \"own\" in Maryland's video lottery subtitle (State Government \\u00a7 9-1A-01(s)), which also governs sports wagering licensees through \\u00a7 9-1E-03(a)(1). Non-voting beneficial or proprietary interests of 15% or less, held directly or through a passive trust, would no longer count as ownership, provided the applicant or licensee files a pre-transfer certification and a fingerprint-based criminal history check with the Commission. The existing 5% ownership threshold otherwise stays in place.","mechanics":["Current rule kept: \"own\" means a beneficial or proprietary interest of at least 5% in the property or business of an applicant or licensee (\\u00a7 9-1A-01(s)(1)).","New carve-out in \\u00a7 9-1A-01(s)(2): an interest of 15% or less is not \"ownership\" if it confers no voting rights. The Senate committee amendment cut this cap from 25%.","The interest must be held directly by a person, or in trust for that person's benefit, and the trustee may not exercise any voting rights or control over the applicant or licensee.","Before the interest is transferred or issued, the applicant or licensee must give the Commission written notice and certification, in the form the Commission requires, that the holder is not disqualified for a video lottery employee license under \\u00a7 9-1A-14(c)(1)(iii) and (iv).","A state and national criminal history records check with fingerprints is also required, under Title 10, Subtitle 2, Part III of the Criminal Procedure Article.","The change applies to sports wagering licensees, employees and contractors through the cross-reference in \\u00a7 9-1E-03(a)(1), which pulls in \\u00a7\\u00a7 9-1A-08 and 9-1A-12. It takes effect October 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses ownership-attribution thresholds for licensure suitability. Illinois HB2878 and HB5034 change master sports wagering license fees and license counts, IL HB2761 changes who may apply for a facility-based license, and Colorado SB 163 changes how licensing duties are delegated within the gaming regulator. Maryland's bill is unusual because it is a targeted capital-structure fix: it lets passive, non-voting investors take up to a 15% stake without full owner qualification, in exchange for a lighter certification and background check.","watch":"Watch three things: whether the House restores the 25% cap or adds conditions; how the Commission defines its certification form and the \"no control\" test for trustees; and the fact that the reenacted \\u00a7 9-1A-05(d)(1) still treats any ownership interest, regardless of percentage, as ownership for the one-video-lottery-facility limit and the ban on legislators holding interests, so the carve-out does not reach those restrictions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":6498,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:43.358Z"},"MD-SB456":{"summary":"SB 456 expands Maryland's fantasy competition definition to cover peer-to-peer higher/lower 'pick'em' contests built on two or more real athletes' projected stats, and declares compliant fantasy contests outside sports wagering 'for any purpose.' It replaces the Commission's discretionary registration fee with a mandatory $50,000 annual fee directed to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund. It also writes a statutory voluntary exclusion regime and responsible-gaming obligations into the fantasy subtitle.","mechanics":["Definition (§9-1D-01(d)): participants 'assemble' teams or rosters. Contests may include picking higher or lower than projected or actual performance statistics for rosters of two or more real athletes. Shared, tiered and conditional bonus prizes are allowed if disclosed in advance, but participants must compete 'against other participants.' The existing ban on outcomes based solely on one athlete or on a team score or point spread is kept.","§9-1D-02(a): a compliant fantasy competition 'does not constitute betting, wagering, or gambling for any purpose.' The ban on in-state kiosks and machines in §9-1D-02(b) is unchanged.","§9-1D-03(c): the Commission 'shall' impose an annual registration fee of $50,000, replacing the discretionary 'may' fee. Proceeds go to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund (§5-206 Education Article).","New §9-1D-05 requires each operator to maintain a compulsive-play mitigation program. Operators must also post a problem-gaming help statement on their site or app and on all marketing materials and advertisements.","The Commission must create a voluntary exclusion list and fold it into its other self-exclusion programs. Excluded individuals may not open accounts or play, though existing accountholders may view and download their transaction history. Operators must report any participation by an excluded individual. Prizes won by self-excluded players are forfeited and remitted quarterly to the Problem Gambling Fund (§9-1A-33). Sanctions apply for knowing failures to exclude.","§9-1E-01(i) and (j): 'sporting event' and 'sports wagering' are amended to carve out fantasy competitions under Subtitle 1D, including for individual player stats. Effective October 1, 2026; cross-filed as HB 731."],"issue_tags":["prop","legalize","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"IL HB5142 moves in the opposite direction by pulling contracts on individual athlete performance statistics into the definition of sports wagering, whereas MD SB456 expressly carves player-stat pick'em contests out of sports wagering when offered as fantasy. IL HB2342 would have the Gaming Board levy fees and charges on adjusted gross fantasy receipts, while SB456 relies on a flat $50,000 registration fee with no receipts-based levy. FL H0189 also revises fantasy sports contest rules, but as part of a broad gambling overhaul. Maryland's bill is narrower and operator-friendly on product scope while adding exclusion and forfeiture obligations.","watch":"Watch whether the 'competes against other participants' element (§9-1D-01(d)(3)) is read to exclude house-banked pick'em formats, and whether the Budget and Taxation committee converts the flat $50,000 fee into a receipts-based tax. Also watch whether the purpose paragraph's reference to a 'mandatory exclusion list' and the voluntary list's coverage of contests 'licensed' (rather than registered) under the subtitle are cleaned up in amendments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":11271,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:44.285Z"},"MD-SB652":{"summary":"SB652 creates a new State Government Article subtitle (§§ 9-1F-01 to 9-1F-16) that prohibits operating, offering, or promoting \"illegal\" online gambling in Maryland and bars anyone from knowingly supporting it, with dual-currency sweepstakes games expressly covered. It lets the Attorney General's Consumer Protection Division and local prosecutors issue cease-and-desist orders and seek ex parte injunctions against operators, payment processors, platform providers, and other supporters. Violations carry criminal and civil fines, forfeiture, mandatory license revocation, and treatment as an unfair, abusive, or deceptive trade practice under the Consumer Protection Act.","mechanics":["Definitions (§ 9-1F-01): \"illegal\" means not expressly authorized by applicable law. \"Online gambling\" covers wagers on any game or contest, including casino games and sweepstakes games, meaning dual-currency systems simulating casino, lottery, or sports wagering. Persons licensed for online sports wagering or fantasy competitions under Subtitles 1D and 1E are excluded (§ 9-1F-02).","Operator cease-and-desist (§ 9-1F-04): an order gives 10 days to geoblock Maryland users. It may be served by publication without first trying to locate the operator, it is prima facie evidence of knowledge, and the AG must publish a public list of named operators and URLs. Out-of-state operators broadcasting into Maryland submit to state jurisdiction.","Penalties (§ 9-1F-06): operating is a misdemeanor punishable by up to 3 years and/or a $50,000 fine for a first offense and $100,000 for later offenses. Knowingly supporting carries fines of $25,000, then $50,000. Each wager and each promotion is a separate violation. Forfeiture of profits and gross receipts is mandatory. The Commission must revoke any gaming license held by a violator and bar the person from future licensure.","Intermediaries (§§ 9-1F-07 to 9-1F-09): financial transaction providers and platform providers are deemed to know about every URL on the AG list. Platforms must obtain a certified license copy or Commission confirmation before hosting any online gambling ad. Orders giving 30 days to comply may go to applicants, licensees, geolocation providers, content suppliers, promoters, celebrity endorsers, and media affiliates, with good-faith blocking protected from tort liability.","Courts (§ 9-1F-10): courts may issue TROs and injunctions, including account freezes and website takedowns. Ex parte injunctions are available, with a hearing within 30 days. A violation is conclusively deemed irreparable harm. Violating an order brings fines of $50,000, then $100,000, per day or per transaction.","Other provisions: the Commission may deny, revoke, suspend, or condition licenses for noncompliance (§ 9-1F-12). The Division gets rulemaking authority (§ 9-1F-14), and the subtitle is to be construed liberally (§ 9-1F-15). The Act takes effect October 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Florida's FL S1580 and FL H0189 also address illegal and internet gambling penalties. SB652 differs by building a consumer-protection enforcement regime run by the Attorney General rather than the gaming regulator. Its core tools are a public blocklist of operators and URLs, deemed knowledge for payment processors and hosting platforms, and ex parte takedown injunctions. Unlike IL HB5142, which pulls prediction markets into the sports wagering definition, and CT HB05038 and IL HB5059, which regulate prediction markets directly, SB652 does not mention prediction markets. Its broad \"wager\" and \"illegal\" definitions could nonetheless be read to reach any unlicensed product. The mandatory revocation and lifetime ineligibility for licensees in § 9-1F-06(D) is unusually severe compared with the advertising-focused CT HB05229.","watch":"Watch three points: the § 9-1F-02 carve-out covers only Subtitle 1D and 1E licensees, so casino licensees, B2B suppliers, and affiliates are not exempt; § 9-1F-06(D) mandatory revocation and lifetime ineligibility could follow from \"indirect\" support of unlicensed iGaming or sweepstakes; and § 9-1F-08(B) license-verification duty for platforms hosting \"online gambling\" ads could slow legitimate sportsbook advertising.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":23912,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:55:44.918Z"},"MD-SB844":{"summary":"Maryland's annual corrective bill, enacted as Chapter 153 of 2026, fixes technical errors across the Annotated Code and the Public Local Laws of Somerset County. The fixes cover omitted words, stale cross-references, renamed agencies and misnomers such as the NMLS name, obsolete death-penalty language, and mis-tabulated subsections. It states that it is not intended to affect any law other than to correct technical errors, and it authorizes the Code's publishers to correct certain errors and obsolete provisions.","mechanics":["Each amended section carries a Drafter's Note identifying the error and the session law that caused it. Examples: the omitted word 'HISTORY' in criminal history records check provisions in Business Occupations and Professions §§ 18-204, 18-303, 18-304 and 18-3A-03, -04 and -08; and 'Nationwide Mortgage Licensing System and Registry' renamed 'Nationwide Multistate Licensing System' throughout Financial Institutions §§ 1-101(q) and 11-604 through 11-622 and Commercial Law § 14-1201(l).","The purpose paragraph states the Act is not intended to affect any law other than to correct technical errors, so the changes are not meant to carry substantive weight.","The bill's list of amended sections includes State Government Article § 9-1E-12(b)(2)(ii), which falls within Maryland's sports wagering subtitle. The operative text of that change is not in the excerpt provided, so its content cannot be confirmed here.","Tax-General § 10-706(c) is also listed for amendment, but its text is likewise not in the excerpt.","Other notable fixes: obsolete 'or is to be executed' language struck from Correctional Services § 9-605(a), Education § 7-1512 subsections redesignated after the 2025 repeal of subsection (g), and Health-General § 19-706(m) repealed as obsolete."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What stands out is that a purely technical, omnibus corrective vehicle reaches into Maryland's sports wagering subtitle (State Government § 9-1E-12). Such changes are expressly framed as non-substantive and usually draw little scrutiny from industry stakeholders.","watch":"Pull the chaptered text of State Government § 9-1E-12(b)(2)(ii), and of Tax-General § 10-706(c) if it touches wagering withholding, to confirm the change is purely clerical and does not quietly alter a cross-reference or obligation affecting sports wagering licensees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":87168,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:55:50.697Z"},"MD-SB862":{"summary":"SB862 directs the Maryland Department of Health to run a separate prevalence study of problem and pathological mobile gambling, with the first study due by July 1, 2031. It also funds the Problem Gambling Fund by cutting the share of proceeds that sports wagering and fantasy operators keep by one percentage point and sending that 1% to the Fund. In practice this raises the effective state take on every operator class by one point, effective July 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Health-General §19-804(b),(d): the existing prevalence study is relabeled as covering in-person gambling. A new mobile gambling prevalence study is added, with the initial study due July 1, 2031 and replication studies at least every 5 years, conducted by an independent researcher.","State Gov't §9-1E-12(b)(1): operator retention falls from 85% to 84% for Class A-1/A-2 and Class B-1/B-2 facility licensees, and from 80% to 79% for mobile licensees. The effective rate therefore rises from 15% to 16% (retail) and from 20% to 21% (mobile).","§9-1E-12(b)(2)(ii): the Commission must send 1% of all sports wagering proceeds to the Problem Gambling Fund (§9-1A-33). The existing 5% General Fund carve-out is reworded from proceeds 'attributable to mobile' to proceeds 'from online' sports wagering.","§9-1D-04: fantasy competition operators' retention falls from 85% to 84%, and 1% of fantasy proceeds goes to the Problem Gambling Fund. The rest still goes to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","New §9-1A-33(b)(7): the Secretary of Health must report to the General Assembly on Problem Gambling Fund spending by November 1, 2027 and every November 1 after that.","The Act takes effect July 1, 2026. It reintroduces SB 706 of 2025 and is cross-filed with 6lr3499."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like IL HB3159, which adds a 2% charge on adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS, SB862 raises the operator burden to fund a dedicated earmark. Maryland's version is smaller (1 point) and is built by lowering operator retention percentages rather than by imposing a new tax. IL HB1451 and HB2580 expand DHS problem-gambling education without touching operator economics. HI HB1308 and HB2570 create a problem-gambling fund as part of a new legalization framework, whereas Maryland is retrofitting funding onto an existing regime. The mandated mobile-specific prevalence study is unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the one-point retention cut, which raises mobile operators' effective rate to 21% and retail and fantasy operators' to 16% from July 1, 2026; watch also whether 'proceeds' and 'online' are reconciled with existing definitions, and how the mobile prevalence findings due in 2031 may be used to justify future restrictions on mobile wagering.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6921,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:55:55.367Z"},"MD-SB883":{"summary":"SB883 authorizes the Maryland Stadium Authority to acquire, finance, and build a Carroll Park Soccer Stadium and Facility in Baltimore City, with up to $216.6 million in Authority bonds outstanding. Debt service on those bonds would come from State sports wagering proceeds: beginning in fiscal 2028, the Comptroller must pay the annual principal and interest into a new Carroll Park fund before any sports wagering money goes to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund or the 5% mobile share for the General Fund.","mechanics":["Adds the Carroll Park Soccer Stadium and Facility (defined in new Econ. Dev. §10-601(p) as a Baltimore City stadium used primarily for professional soccer and as a soccer academy and school) to the Authority's facility list, its site-acquisition power (§10-618(b)(1)(ix)), and its annual tax-revenue reporting (§10-625(a)(2)(viii)).","Sets a bond cap of $216,600,000 for the stadium under §10-628(c)(1)(xii) and creates the nonlapsing Carroll Park Soccer Stadium and Facility Fund (§10-657.7), which keeps its own interest earnings and can be pledged to debt service under §10-634.","Amends State Gov't §9-1E-12(b)(2)(iii): for FY2028 and later, the Comptroller pays the fund the full amount needed for that year's bond principal and interest out of sports wagering proceeds in the State Lottery Fund, ahead of the Blueprint and General Fund distributions.","Leaves operator retention unchanged: 85% for Class A and B facility licensees and 80% for mobile licensees under §9-1E-12(b)(1).","Under new §10-646.5, the Authority must give the legislative fiscal committees a comprehensive financing plan at least 45 days before seeking Board of Public Works approval of each bond issue, and may not issue bonds until it and \"United Partnership\" sign a written operating agreement.","The Act takes effect July 1, 2026; its cross-file is HB 1078."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Most of the listed bills create or restructure wagering markets or licensing, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308. SB883 does neither: it only redirects existing sports wagering revenue. The closest analogue is IL HB3159, which earmarks a fixed 2% of adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS. SB883 instead takes an uncapped, first-priority annual draw equal to stadium debt service. IA SSB 1157 and KS HB 2466 also tie gaming or tax-increment policy to sports and tourism infrastructure, but SB883 is unusual in pledging wagering proceeds directly as security for stadium bonds.","watch":"Operators' retention rates are not touched, but a senior, open-ended claim on sports wagering proceeds for up to $216.6M in bonds would shrink money for the Blueprint education fund and could invite pressure to raise the wagering tax again, so watch for amendments to the §9-1E-12(b)(1) retention percentages as the bill or its cross-file HB 1078 moves.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21455,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:55:57.202Z"},"MD-SB970":{"summary":"SB 970 amends Md. State Government § 9-1E-17(d) to change \"may\" to \"shall,\" so that any sports wagering licensee or operator that advertises in Maryland must contract with an independent evaluator licensed under § 9-1E-17(b) to evaluate and rate its sports wagering content, experts, influencers and content partners. The mandate is contingent on the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission licensing at least three independent evaluators, and it takes effect July 1, 2026, subject to that contingency.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends § 9-1E-17(d), converting the existing permissive authorization (\"may\") into a mandatory requirement (\"shall\").","Covered parties are sports wagering licensees and sports wagering operators that advertise in the State.","The required contract scope is evaluating and rating the licensee's sports wagering content, sports wagering experts, sports wagering influencers and content partners.","Evaluators must be licensed under § 9-1E-17(b).","Section 2 makes the Act contingent on the Commission issuing licenses to at least three independent evaluators.","Section 3 sets the effective date at July 1, 2026, subject to the Section 2 contingency; the text contains no new penalty or enforcement provision."],"issue_tags":["advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569), SB 970 assumes an existing market and targets only marketing content through third-party evaluation. It is closest in spirit to CT HB05229, which restricts gaming advertising on college campuses and limits AI-targeted bets. CT HB05229 imposes direct conduct restrictions, while Maryland instead mandates a paid outside rating relationship for influencer and content-partner marketing. It is also distinct from the age and advertising limits in prediction-market bills such as CT HB05038 and IL HB5059, which address a different product category.","watch":"Operators should track how many independent evaluators the Commission actually licenses, since three triggers the mandate, along with any rules defining the evaluators' rating standards and fees and whether a poor rating will carry advertising consequences that the bill text itself does not specify.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"First - Sports Wagering - Independent Evaluation of Sports Wagering","text_chars":1822,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:04.130Z"},"MD-SB884":{"summary":"SB884 would create a new Subtitle 1F in Maryland's State Government Article letting the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission license and regulate internet poker and other Commission-approved 'skill-based' games, effectively at a 30% rate on proceeds. It also authorizes the Governor to join multistate liquidity-sharing agreements. The sponsor withdrew the bill on March 13, 2026, so it is dead for this session but serves as a template for future Maryland iPoker and online-gaming efforts.","mechanics":["Eligibility (§9-1F-03(b)): licenses may go to video lottery operators, sports wagering facility or mobile sports wagering licensees, applicants with at least 100 full-time Maryland employees, and majority minority-owned applicants or joint ventures. Holders of multiple 1A/1E licenses may apply for only one license, and each licensee may contract with only one operator.","Fees and term: initial license fee is $1,000,000, or $500,000 for minority-owned applicants. The term is 5 years, renewal costs 1% of average annual retained proceeds over the prior 3 years, and the license cannot be transferred for 3 years after issuance.","Tax and distribution (§9-1F-05): the licensee retains 70% of proceeds and the State receives 30%. The State share is split 5.5% to local jurisdictions with video lottery facilities, 6% to the Purse Dedication Account, 1% to Racetrack Facility Renewal, 1.5% to the Small, Minority, and Women-Owned Businesses Account, 1% to the Problem Gambling Fund, and the remainder to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","Bad-actor and labor rules: operator and supplier licensees are barred, and may have their license revoked, if they or their affiliates knowingly take revenue from FATF-blacklisted countries, state sponsors of terrorism, or online casino gaming in jurisdictions that prohibit it. They must file annual disclosures of revenue from illegal interactive gaming markets, and applicants must show a labor peace agreement and union neutrality by clear and convincing evidence.","Player protections (§9-1F-04, -06): minimum age is 21, with ID verification and periodic reverification. Credit cards are banned and deposit limits are mandatory. Advertising may not target excluded or at-risk individuals or be false or deceptive. The Commission must run a voluntary exclusion list, and licensees must report annually on players showing erratic gambling behavior.","Other provisions: tampering with games carries a penalty of up to $1,000,000 plus a suspension of at least 30 days. Live dealer studios must be located in Maryland. The Commission may add new skill games after notice to the Legislative Policy Committee (45-day wait) and must study sweepstakes and microtransaction platforms, reporting by December 1, 2029. The act would have taken effect July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity","sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills address sports wagering, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB686 and the HI bills, whereas MD SB884 assumes mobile sports wagering already exists and builds a separate online poker and skill-game vertical. Its closest analogues are IL HB3080 and HB4797, the Internet Gaming Acts, but those authorize full internet gaming, while SB884 is limited to poker and other games the Commission deems skill-based. SB884 is unusual in combining multistate compact authority, a supplier disclosure requirement on illegal-market revenue, a labor peace mandate, and a mandated study of sweepstakes and microtransaction platforms.","watch":"If revived, the illegal-market revenue bar and annual disclosure duty (§9-1F-02(e)) would reach operators' and suppliers' affiliates everywhere, and the Commission's power to add 'skill-based' games, including live dealer formats, could expand the subtitle well beyond poker, so both deserve close scrutiny.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":40943,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:04.969Z"},"ME-LD1304":{"summary":"LD 1304 was a proposed constitutional amendment to Art. IX, §8 of the Maine Constitution. It would have authorized the Legislature to cap property tax increases at 2% per year on the primary residence of a permanent resident aged 65 or older who has owned the home for at least 12 months. It would also have required the State to reimburse municipalities for at least 90% of the lost revenue, using a 2% tax on lottery tickets and sports betting. The measure died in Legislative Files on May 21, 2025 under Joint Rule 310.3 and would have needed two-thirds of each chamber plus voter ratification at a November statewide election.","mechanics":["New Art. IX, §8, sub-§6 lets the Legislature limit property tax increases to 2% per property tax year on the primary residence of a Maine permanent resident aged 65 or older who has owned it for at least 12 months.","If the property is transferred to someone other than a family member, it reverts to just value at the time of transfer.","Municipal revenue lost to the cap must be reimbursed at least 90% by the State.","The reimbursement must come from a constitutionally specified 2% tax on lottery tickets and sports betting. The text does not define the base (handle, gross receipts or adjusted gross revenue) or who bears the tax; the summary calls it a tax 'imposed on the sale of' lottery tickets and sports betting.","Referendum procedure: two-thirds of each branch must concur, followed by a November statewide ballot question. The amendment takes effect on the Governor's proclamation of a majority vote.","Status: dead as of May 21, 2025 (Joint Rule 310.3)."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Kansas SB303 (House Substitute) is the closest analogue: it also ties a new wagering levy, a 2% excise on the amount wagered, to property tax relief through a property tax relief fund, and it also taxes lottery ticket sales. Kansas did this by statute, whereas Maine would have written the 2% sports-betting and lottery tax into the Constitution itself. Unlike Georgia SR131, which uses a constitutional amendment to authorize sports betting, LD 1304 assumes an existing legal market and earmarks it for senior homeowner relief. It also differs from Illinois HB3159, which adds a 2% adjusted gross receipts levy earmarked for fire and EMS. Because the Maine text leaves the 2% base undefined, it could have been read as a handle tax similar to Kansas SB303 rather than a tax on receipts like Illinois HB3159.","watch":"Although LD 1304 is dead, operators should watch for its reintroduction, because it would constitutionally lock in an additional 2% levy with an undefined base (potentially handle rather than gross gaming revenue) on top of Maine's existing sports wagering tax, insulated from ordinary legislative repeal.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4638,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:15.107Z"},"MD-SB885":{"summary":"SB885 would create a new Subtitle 1F in the State Government Article letting the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission license internet casino gaming (iGaming) and online electronic instant bingo, regulated on the same footing as VLTs, table games, mobile sports wagering and electronic bingo. It sets licensee eligibility (VLT operators, certain combined retail/mobile sports wagering licensees, qualifying Maryland-headquartered firms, and social-equity applicants), effective tax rates of 40% (20% for live dealer), fee levels, responsible-gaming and labor-peace requirements, and a Video Lottery Facility Employee Displacement Fund. According to the purpose clause, the Act is also submitted to a statewide referendum.","mechanics":["Tax: under §9-1F-05(b)(1)(ii) licensees retain 80% of gaming proceeds from live dealer games and 60% from all other iGaming, so the effective state share is 20% and 40%. Online bingo licensees retain 60% (§9-1F-05(c)). For each licensee's first 5 years, gaming proceeds exclude a tiered share of redeemed free play and promotional credits, sliding from 35% (proceeds under $4M) down to 20% (proceeds over $12M), per §9-1F-01(f)(3).","Eligibility (§9-1F-03(b)): VLT operators; sports wagering licensees holding both a facility and a mobile license, each issued on or before Dec 31, 2026; and Maryland firms with a 10-year in-state headquarters, at least 250 in-state FTEs, a prior Commission qualification to hold 5% or more of a VLT operator (2018 to 2025), a Maryland-associated brand, and a $5M live studio or film studio commitment. Holders of multiple 1A/1E licenses get only one license, plus an extra license if they partner with social equity owners holding at least 33%. Five additional licenses go through two competitive rounds, with the first round reserved for applicants at least 33% owned by social equity applicants; all other applicants need at least 5% social equity ownership or an employee profit-sharing alternative. Commercial bingo operators may hold an online bingo license but not an iGaming license.","Fees and term: initial iGaming license fee is $1M, or $500K if live-dealer only. For major minority-owned or joint venture applicants (51%), it is $500K or $250K. The online bingo fee is $500K. Terms are 5 years, and renewal costs 1% of average annual retained proceeds over the prior 3 years. Each licensee may contract with no more than one online gaming operator. Ownership cannot be transferred for 3 years after issuance, with limited exceptions. Reciprocity waivers are available for applicants licensed in Maryland or at least three other states.","Supplier integrity (§9-1F-02(e)): operators and suppliers that knowingly accept revenue from FATF-blacklisted jurisdictions, state sponsors of terrorism, or illegal US gambling are barred or subject to revocation. They must also file annual disclosures of revenue from supplying content to illegal interactive gaming markets, and misstatements can lead to denial, suspension or revocation and penalties on officers.","Consumer protection and responsible gaming (§§9-1F-04, 9-1F-06): players must be 21 or older, with age and identity verified at signup and periodically reverified. Credit cards are banned. Mandatory deposit limits are required, and limit increases are delayed at least 24 hours. Players can set self-suspensions, and no gaming emails may be sent during suspensions of 72 hours or more. Monthly problem-gambling disclosure certifications, a voluntary exclusion list and annual reports on erratic play are required. Advertising targeted at prohibited or at-risk individuals, and false or deceptive advertising, are prohibited.","Labor and enforcement: applicants, licensees and live dealer studio operators must show by clear and convincing evidence a labor peace agreement (unless already under a CBA) that bars strikes for 5 years, plus employer neutrality on unionization (§9-1F-04(c)). Tampering penalties run up to $1M plus a suspension of at least 30 days (§9-1F-07). Revenue distributions begin in FY2028, including up to $10M in year one to the employee displacement fund, set amounts to VLT jurisdictions and racing accounts, 1% to the agency, 1% to problem gambling, 5% to counties for education, and the remainder to the Blueprint Fund."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogues on the list are Illinois' Internet Gaming Act bills, IL HB3080 and HB4797, which likewise authorize iGaming through licensees and contracted management service providers. Maryland's version is distinguished by several features: an explicit 40% headline rate with a lower 20% rate for live dealer play, a tiered promotional-credit deduction, heavy social-equity ownership set-asides, a labor peace requirement, a displacement fund for casino workers, and a referendum requirement. Most other listed bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569) concern sports wagering only. CT HB05229 overlaps only on advertising and responsible-gaming controls.","watch":"Sportsbook clients should note that iGaming eligibility for sports wagering licensees is limited to those holding both a facility and a mobile license issued by December 31, 2026, with only one license per multi-license holder, and should weigh the 40% effective rate, the 5-year-only and declining promotional deduction, the single-operator-per-licensee limit, and the labor peace and neutrality obligations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":61349,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:17.453Z"},"ME-LD1188":{"summary":"As printed, LD 1188 lets Maine commercial harness racetrack licensees (8 MRSA §271) sell pari-mutuel pools and common pari-mutuel pools on simulcast races at any facility licensed for sports wagering under 8 MRSA §1206. This bridges the split between Harness Racing Commission (Agriculture) and Gambling Control Unit (Public Safety) oversight. It also repeals the §275-B(3) commission-approved facility pathway, which was otherwise set to sunset January 1, 2026; note that the enacted title focuses on that §275-B(3) provision, so the final chaptered text may differ from this printed version.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends 8 MRSA §275-B(1) so a §271 racetrack licensee may sell pari-mutuel and simulcast common pools within its track enclosure, at its §1011 slot facility, or at any licensee's facility licensed under §1206 (sports wagering), not only its own.","The printed bill strikes §275-B(3), the commission-approved facility pathway. That pathway was limited to the same county as the track, barred in a municipality hosting an off-track betting facility, required municipal approval, and carried a 5% share of the track's §286(5) commission to the host municipality; it had a January 1, 2026 sunset.","Sec. 2 amends §275-C(1), replacing the reference to commission-approved §275-B(3) facilities with any §1206 licensed facility as a permitted location for common pari-mutuel pool sales.","Off-track betting licensees under §275-D are unchanged and still limited to their licensed facility.","It was enacted as an emergency measure with a two-thirds vote, effective on approval, so it would be in place for the 2025 racing season starting in April."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed. What stands out is that Maine routes horse-race pari-mutuel wagering into sports-wagering retail locations by statutory cross-reference to the §1206 sports wagering license, rather than folding racing into the sports wagering regime itself. The change in title from the printed version (\"Include Harness Racing with Other Sports for Purposes of Sports Wagering\") to the enacted one suggests the committee may have narrowed or restructured it, possibly keeping or extending §275-B(3) rather than repealing it.","watch":"Confirm the final chaptered text, since the enacted title suggests amendment, and check whether §1206 sports wagering licensees must host racetrack pari-mutuel sales, how revenue and space are split, and whether Harness Racing Commission rules now reach operations inside Gambling Control Unit-licensed facilities.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"LD 1188 HP 793","text_chars":5424,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:17.778Z"},"ME-LD1164":{"summary":"LD 1164 creates a new chapter 39 in Title 8 legalizing internet casino gaming (card, dice and other games of chance such as blackjack, poker, craps, roulette and baccarat) in Maine and restricts operator licenses exclusively to the federally recognized Wabanaki tribes, one license each, regulated by the Gambling Control Unit in the Department of Public Safety. Commercial operators can take part only as licensed suppliers or as a tribe's single management services partner, under a director-approved contract with a cap on the partner's share of revenue. The chaptered text shows it became Public Law 2025, chapter 538 without the Governor's signature (dated January 11, 2026), not by signature as the status line suggests.","mechanics":["Eligibility and license limits (§1406): only a federally recognized Indian nation, tribe or band in Maine may hold an Internet gaming license, limited to one per tribe. The license is non-transferable except to a Maine-based entity wholly owned by the tribe. The fee is $50,000 a year plus processing costs, temporary licenses are available, and play is limited to persons physically located in the State.","Management services (§§1403(2)(F), 1406(3), 1408): each tribal licensee may contract with no more than one management services licensee. The contract must be pre-approved by the director, and the partner may receive no more than 30% of adjusted gross Internet gaming receipts, or up to 40% if the director finds the fee commercially reasonable given the partner's capital investment. The partner may not subcontract duties or compensation to anyone without a management services license. Supplier and management services licenses cost $10,000 a year each.","Definitions (§1402): adjusted gross Internet gaming receipts are gross receipts less winnings paid and federal excise tax. Internet gaming excludes chapter 31 table games and slots, pari-mutuel pools, fantasy contests, mobile sports wagering under chapter 35 and advance deposit wagering. The director must levy a tax on adjusted gross receipts (§1403(1)(C)), but no rate appears in the portion of text provided.","Suitability and enforcement (§§1404-1405): 10% ownership and control disclosure, fingerprint-based state and FBI background checks, and 30-day notice of material changes. Sanctions range from reprimand to revocation, with civil penalties up to $25,000 per violation and appeal to Superior Court.","Player protection and advertising (§§1403(2), 1412): 21+ age minimum, identity and geolocation verification, and a self-exclusion list. Rules must bar misleading advertising, require operators to disclose their tribal status, and restrict, to the extent permissible, advertising likely to reach or appeal to persons under 21. Wagers from insiders, interested parties, proxies and Gambling Control Unit staff are prohibited.","Integrity, records and child support (§§1413-1415): operators must report abnormal wagering, keep wager records for 3 years, share anonymized integrity data on request, and may use cloud hosting. Before any payout at or above the W-2G threshold, operators must check the DHHS child-support registry, withhold the debt, remit it within 7 days and may retain $10. Conforming amendments to §1210 allow fee-free cross-licensing of sports wagering and iGaming occupational licensees."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Illinois HB3080 and HB4797 would create a commercial Internet Gaming Act open to multiple licensees and management services providers, whereas Maine LD 1164 gives tribes exclusive operator status and caps each tribe at one platform partner with a regulated revenue split, mirroring Maine's existing tribal mobile sports wagering model. Indiana HB1092 imposes a child-support hold on sports wagering account withdrawals; Maine's §1415 intercept instead triggers at W-2G-level payouts and extends the obligation to the management services licensee. Connecticut HB05229 legislates specific advertising limits such as campus restrictions, while Maine delegates advertising restrictions to director rulemaking, including under-21 reach and tribal-disclosure requirements.","watch":"Operators seeking Maine iGaming market access should focus on the 30% (up to 40%) cap on management services compensation, the one-partner-per-tribe limit and the director's prior approval of contracts and material changes, along with the tax rate and effective date, which do not appear in the excerpt provided.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":63047,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:23.357Z"},"ME-LD1862":{"summary":"LD 1862 would have added a new 8 MRSA §1220 declaring that, notwithstanding any other law, jurisdiction over sports wagering conducted in person on the property of a facility operator rests solely with the Gambling Control Board (established under 8 MRSA §1002, within the Department of Public Safety). It is a one-section jurisdictional clarification and does not change tax rates, licensing fees, or wagering rules. The bill died on May 20, 2025, when it was placed in Legislative Files under Joint Rule 310.3.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 enacts 8 MRSA §1220, titled 'Jurisdiction,' within Maine's sports wagering chapter.","It covers only in-person sports wagering conducted on the property of a 'facility operator'. Mobile and online wagering are not addressed.","Exclusive jurisdiction is vested in the Gambling Control Board under §1002, and the grant applies 'notwithstanding any provision of law to the contrary.'","The text contains no new licensing, tax, enforcement, or effective-date provisions. Default effective-date rules for nonemergency legislation would have applied.","Sponsored by Sen. Baldacci (Penobscot) and referred to the Committee on Veterans and Legal Affairs. It died in committee files on May 20, 2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that the bill does nothing except allocate regulatory authority. Its 'notwithstanding any provision of law to the contrary' clause appears designed to foreclose competing claims of jurisdiction over retail sportsbooks at facility-operator premises. The text does not say whose claims those would be.","watch":"Watch whether this exclusive-jurisdiction language is revived, because the notwithstanding clause could override other statutory or negotiated allocations of authority over retail sports wagering at facility-operator sites. That would affect which regulator licenses, audits, and disciplines operators there.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1022,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:30.669Z"},"ME-LD1838":{"summary":"LD 1838 would have created a new 8 MRSA chapter 39 authorizing licensed electronic wagering terminals offering historical horse racing, electronic beano and virtual sealed tickets, regulated by the Gambling Control Unit director. Eligible operators were limited to Maine's federally recognized tribes, commercial harness tracks without casino licenses, certain charitable games-of-chance licensees, and existing off-track betting facilities. Operators would remit 25% of net terminal income for distribution to agriculture, harness racing, veterans' homes, education, problem-gambling and municipal funds. The bill died in the Legislative Files on June 13, 2025.","mechanics":["Terminal caps under §1503(4): 100 per commercial track, 25 per OTB facility, and 4 per charitable organization, each subject to municipal approval. Tribes may operate up to 100 total, either on tribal territory or in municipalities that approve.","§1501(4) defines an electronic wagering terminal as not a slot machine under §1001(39). Outcomes must come from historical race results, a central-server beano draw of no more than 75 numbers, or a predetermined sealed-ticket deal. Terminals may not pay out cash, only vouchers.","§1504 requires 25% of net terminal income to be remitted to the state. The split is 1% GCU administration, 1% problem gambling, 2% agricultural fairs, 5% harness purses, 1% harness promotion, 1% Sire Stakes, 5% Maine Milk Pool (Agricultural Development Fund from 2035), 5% Veterans' Homes, 3% career and technical education, and 1% host municipality. Late payments accrue 1.5% interest per month, and knowing nonpayment is a Class D crime with possible forfeiture.","Operator license fees under §1503(3) and (6) may not exceed the greater of $500 or $10 per terminal, plus $50 per terminal annually. Terminal distributors pay $5,000 a year plus background investigation costs, and distributor employees pay $250 initially and $50 on renewal (§1505). Leases may not give a distributor more than 30% of net terminal income.","Players must be at least 21 (§1503(8)). Civil fines under §1506 run up to $1,000 per violation, capped at $5,000 per transaction or occurrence. Required rules under §1508 include self-exclusion using the §1003 list and concurrent use of facilities and staff with pari-mutuel, sports wagering and high-stakes beano operations.","Cooperative agreements under §1503(5) may colocate terminals at a tribal or track facility or link them through an in-state shared wagering server, and their terms are confidential."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. On its own terms, the bill is unusual in pairing historical horse racing with electronic beano and virtual pull-tabs on a single terminal type. It also statutorily declares these terminals not to be slot machines, sidestepping Maine's casino framework. Its 25% contribution is earmarked entirely to dedicated funds rather than the General Fund, which reflects a coalition built around agriculture, harness racing, the Wabanaki tribes and veterans.","watch":"Although LD 1838 is dead, operators should watch for reintroduction, particularly the §1501(4) not-a-slot-machine carve-out and the expanded tribal gaming footprint in a state where tribes already hold exclusive mobile sports wagering, as well as §1508(1)(E), which would let licensees run these terminals alongside sports wagering operations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":25188,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:33.849Z"},"ME-LD1892":{"summary":"LD 1892 would have created a dedicated School Construction Debt Service Fund (new 20-A MRSA §15919) with a target of at least $175 million in FY2026-27 and $200 million a year after that, financed by earmarking the first dollars of several existing state revenue streams. Those streams include lottery revenue, slot machine income, the General Fund share of sports wagering receipts, cannabis and tobacco taxes, and General Fund surplus. It would also have set a 75/25 state/local cost share for school construction and created a Maine Public School Financing Authority seeded with $100 million from the Budget Stabilization Fund. The bill died in the Senate at sine die adjournment on April 29, 2026.","mechanics":["Sec. 5 would amend 8 MRSA §1218(1)(F) so that, from FY2026-27, the first $5,000,000 of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts goes to the debt service fund rather than the General Fund. The 6.5% distribution rate itself is unchanged.","Sec. 4 would amend 8 MRSA §1036(1) to divert the first $5,000,000 of gross slot machine income to the fund. That subsection otherwise covers the 1% administrative distribution to the Gambling Control Board, so the drafting is ambiguous about which pool the $5,000,000 comes from.","Secs. 2–3 would divert the first $32,500,000 of State Lottery revenue and agents' license fees (8 MRSA §386) and the first $32,500,000 of Tri-state Lotto receipts (§415). The $65,000,000 combined total matches §15919(1)(A)(7).","The remaining fund sources are General Fund surplus ($35M in FY27, then $60M a year), cigarette tax ($40M), tobacco products tax ($10M), cannabis sales tax ($10M), cannabis excise tax ($5M), federal funds, and any other source. Any shortfall would be backfilled from lapsed General Purpose Aid balances.","Each year $20M would be reserved for projects involving district consolidation. The State would pay at least 75% of debt service and local units at least 25%, adjustable for ability to pay. All projects would have to be green-certified.","The new Financing Authority would sell bonds to fund local shares and could offer zero-interest financing, and its securities would be state tax-exempt. All provisions would take effect July 1, 2026 / FY2026-27."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that the bill does not change any gaming tax rate or licensing term. It instead re-prioritizes existing lottery, slot and sports wagering distributions by taking fixed-dollar 'first money' carve-outs for school construction ahead of the General Fund, bundled with tobacco and cannabis revenue in a single earmark.","watch":"Although the bill is dead, operators should watch for reintroduction because the Sec. 5 and Sec. 4 drafting refers to the first $5 million of gross receipts or income rather than of the State's share, which could be read as a new carve-out beyond current distributions, and because fixed-dollar earmarks tend to create pressure to raise sports wagering rates if receipts fall short.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":20639,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:37.572Z"},"ME-LD2135":{"summary":"Enacts 37-B MRSA §513-B creating a Veterans' Housing Provider Incentive Program and Fund within the Maine Bureau of Veterans' Services to award grants to 501(c)(3) organizations supporting permanent housing for veterans. Section 2 directs the State Controller to transfer $90,000 from the Department of Public Safety, Gambling Control Unit's Sports Wagering program Other Special Revenue Funds account to the new fund by June 30, 2027. Signed as Public Law Chapter 636 on April 3, 2026.","mechanics":["Sec. 1: Creates the Veterans' Housing Provider Incentive Fund under the Bureau of Veterans' Services, accepting state, federal and private funds.","Grants limited to IRS 501(c)(3) organizations with a core program supporting permanent veteran housing; uses include housing provider incentives, risk mitigation payments and past rental debt for homeless veterans.","Bureau may adopt routine technical rules, including grantee reporting requirements; annual report to the veterans affairs committee due February 15, 2027 and each February 15 in a year grants are issued.","Sec. 2: One-time transfer of $90,000 from the Gambling Control Unit Sports Wagering program Other Special Revenue Funds account, notwithstanding any other law, on or before June 30, 2027.","Sec. 3: Allocates $90,000 in Other Special Revenue Funds for FY 2026-27 ($0 in FY 2025-26) to fund N570."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual only in that it uses a 'notwithstanding' transfer to sweep a fixed $90,000 from Maine's sports wagering special revenue account for an unrelated veterans housing purpose, rather than changing wagering tax rates, licensing or statutory distribution formulas.","watch":"Although the $90,000 one-time sweep does not change operator tax rates or obligations, operators should monitor whether the Legislature increasingly treats the Sports Wagering program account as a discretionary funding source, which could create pressure to raise wagering revenue or divert funds from regulatory operations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":3247,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:45.044Z"},"ME-LD811":{"summary":"LD 811 would have amended 8 MRSA §1206 to require an applicant for a Maine facility sports wagering license to obtain approval from the municipal officers of the host municipality (or county commissioners in an unincorporated place), after a local public hearing, before the Gambling Control Unit director could issue the license. Denied applicants could appeal to the director, who could override the local denial only on clear and convincing evidence that it lacked justifiable cause. The bill died in committee on March 20, 2025 under Joint Rule 310.3.","mechanics":["New 8 MRSA §1206(2-A) adds municipal approval as a licensing condition on top of the existing eligibility requirements in subsection 2. It applies to facility sports wagering licenses, which are tied to an underlying off-track betting license.","Municipal officers must hold a public hearing before approving or denying, state their reasons, and send a written decision to both the applicant and the Gambling Control Unit director.","An aggrieved applicant may appeal to the director within 15 days of receiving the written decision. The director must hold a public hearing in the host municipality and may consider all licensure requirements and the municipal findings.","The director may issue a license over a local denial only if the director finds by clear and convincing evidence that the denial was without justifiable cause.","For facilities in unincorporated places, approval comes from the county commissioners.","Amended §1206(5) makes a facility license valid only at the licensee's municipally approved physical location. The existing one-year term (for licenses on or after Sept. 1, 2024) and the voiding of the facility license if the OTB license lapses are unchanged."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. The bill stands out for adding a local-option, liquor-license-style veto layered onto state sports wagering licensing. It is also unusual in putting a heightened clear-and-convincing burden on applicants who appeal a local denial to the state regulator. It reaches only retail facility licenses tied to OTB licensees and leaves Maine's mobile wagering framework untouched.","watch":"Although LD 811 is dead, operators with OTB-linked retail facility plans should watch for reintroduction, because a local hearing-and-approval requirement with a clear-and-convincing appeal standard would give municipalities a practical veto over siting and could also be applied at renewal, given the one-year license term and the location-specific validity language.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"LD 811 HP 518","text_chars":4476,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:47.253Z"},"ME-LD2080":{"summary":"LD 2080 amends Maine's sports wagering law (8 MRSA ch. 31) to bar operators from accepting credit cards to fund wagers and adds a new §1220 listing the payment methods operators may accept. It also directs the Gambling Control Unit director's rules to require both retail facility operators and mobile operators to have methods for ensuring wagers are not made by credit card. The enacted title also references internet gaming, but the printed text supplied covers only sports wagering, which suggests an amendment that is not reflected here.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 adds 8 MRSA §1202(4-A), defining \"digital wallet\" as any digital or electronic technology that stores a person's financial information and allows purchases of goods and services.","Sec. 2 amends the rulemaking mandate in §1203(2)(C) so that rules on the use of credit and checks must provide that a person may not make a wager using a credit card.","Secs. 3 and 4 amend §1203(2)(F) and (G). Rules setting minimum design and security requirements, for facility premises (including self-serve kiosks) and for mobile apps and digital platforms, must include methods for ensuring a person does not use a credit card to make a wager.","Sec. 5 enacts §1220, which lists permitted payment methods: online and mobile payment systems supporting online money transfers; debit cards; digital wallets, but only where the wallet provider gives assurance that no credit card was used to deposit funds; prepaid cards not purchased with credit cards; bank wires; cash; and any other method the director approves.","§1220 closes with a flat rule that an operator may not accept payment for a wager by credit card.","The text sets no specific penalty or effective date. Enforcement runs through the director's existing rulemaking and disciplinary authority, and Maine's general effective date for non-emergency legislation applies."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, Maine's LD 2080 assumes an existing regulated market and targets only how wagers are funded. It is closest in kind to CT HB05229, which regulates withdrawals from gaming accounts, and IN HB1092, which requires withholding mobile account withdrawals for delinquent child support. Both of those address money movement in betting accounts, but neither, as summarized, bans credit card funding. LD 2080 is unusual in pushing the credit-card ban through to indirect channels: it conditions digital wallets on provider \"assurance\" and prepaid cards on not having been bought with a credit card.","watch":"Watch how the director's implementing rules define the \"assurance\" digital wallet providers must give and how operators must verify prepaid card provenance, given the unconditioned \"online and mobile payment systems\" category that may itself be credit-funded, and confirm whether the enacted version extends the ban to internet gaming, as its amended title indicates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"LD 2080 HP 1395","text_chars":4145,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:48.321Z"},"ME-LD2007":{"summary":"LD 2007 (Public Law 2026, c. 645, approved April 6, 2026) bans online sweepstakes games that use a dual-currency payment model to simulate casino-style gaming, including sports wagering. Anyone who operates, promotes or supports the operation or promotion of such a game commits a civil violation punishable by fines of $10,000 to $100,000 under new 8 MRSA §1352. The same conduct is also made criminal unlawful gambling under new 17-A MRSA §954(1-B). Maine gaming licensees who violate the ban face mandatory license revocation and lifetime-style ineligibility for future licenses.","mechanics":["Definition (8 MRSA §1351(6)): an 'online sweepstakes game' is any internet game, contest or promotion that meets three tests. It is accessible on a phone, computer or similar device; it uses a 'dual-currency system of payment'; and it simulates casino-style gaming such as slots, poker, table games, lottery, bingo or sports wagering.","Dual-currency system (§1351(3)): play with direct consideration (purchased or bonus coins) or indirect consideration (free or bundled coins redeemable for prizes or cash), or any design that bars direct purchase of redeemable coins while encouraging purchases to obtain them. The Gambling Control Unit may further define the term by rule, and those rules are major substantive rules requiring legislative approval.","Civil penalty (§1352(1)): fines of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation for operating, promoting or supporting the operation or promotion of an online sweepstakes game. The Unit collects the fines, which are deposited in the Gambling Addiction Prevention and Treatment Fund (5 MRSA §20006-B, amended by Sec. 1).","License consequences (§1352(2)-(3)): revocation is mandatory for any 'licensee' convicted under 17-A §954(1-B) or found in violation through the administrative process for its license. Covered licenses are those under 8 MRSA chapters 31, 33, 35 (sports wagering) and 39, and 17 MRSA chapter 62. Any violator is ineligible for any of those licenses.","Criminal liability (Sec. 3): the same conduct is made unlawful gambling under 17-A MRSA §954(1-B). The text does not set a separate offense class or a specific effective date."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, the closest analogue is CA AB 831. It works by amending California's existing unfair-practices rules for contests and sweepstakes, whereas Maine creates a standalone prohibition chapter keyed to a statutory 'dual-currency' definition, backed by both civil fines and a criminal unlawful-gambling offense. Maine's approach contrasts with Illinois HB3274 and HB5048, which would shield prize and gift kiosk devices from Gaming Board discipline rather than restrict sweepstakes-style products. Unlike the authorization bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), LD 2007 is purely prohibitory and ties sweepstakes enforcement directly to the licensure status of existing Maine sports wagering and casino licensees.","watch":"The 'supports the operation or promotion' language combines with mandatory revocation and permanent ineligibility for chapter 35 sports wagering licensees, so operators should audit any B2B, affiliate, marketing, payment or supplier relationships that touch dual-currency sweepstakes products. They should also monitor the Unit's major substantive rulemaking defining 'dual-currency system of payment' for reach into their own free-to-play or bonus-coin promotions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":5477,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:48.361Z"},"MI-HB4346":{"summary":"This substitute bill amends section 15 of Michigan's Lawful Internet Gaming Act (MCL 432.315) to change how the existing internet gaming tax under section 14 is divided among recipients. It cuts the Michigan agriculture equine industry development fund's share from 5% to 4% and raises the state internet gaming fund's share from 65% to 66%. It also replaces the flat $3,000,000 annual cap on the equine allocation with a stepped cap of $9M, then $12M, then $15M, with any excess going to the section 16 fund. It does not change the tax rate or the obligations of operators.","mechanics":["Sec. 15(1)(a): the host city (Detroit) keeps 30% of internet gaming tax revenue, restricted to listed uses such as police, public safety, blight, youth programs, tax relief, capital improvements and roads.","Sec. 15(1)(b): the state share deposited in the internet gaming fund rises from 65% to 66%.","Sec. 15(1)(c): the equine industry development fund share falls from 5% to 4%. The current $3,000,000 cap is replaced by $9,000,000 for FY ending 9/30/2026, $12,000,000 for FY 2027, and $15,000,000 for FY 2028 and each year after. Any excess goes to the section 16 fund.","Sec. 15(2): keeps the $183,000,000 floor for the host city, made up from the fund, which counts internet gaming, casino wagering tax and lawful sports betting act allocations plus development agreement payments. The city's combined take is capped at 55% of section 14 tax receipts.","Enacting section 1 tie-bar: the bill takes effect only if HB 4347 or Senate request S01712'25 of the 103rd Legislature is enacted.","Status: passed the House. Referred to the Senate Committee on Regulatory Affairs on 2026-03-04."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like IL HB3159, which would earmark 2% of sports wagering adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS, this bill deals with where gaming tax money goes rather than how much is collected. Unlike IL HB3159, it reshuffles percentages within the existing tax instead of adding a new levy on operators. It also resembles the revenue-use provisions of IA SSB 1157 and the earmarking approach of KS HB2063, but it is narrowly focused on agricultural and equine industry support. One unusual feature is that the title says \"remove cap\" while the engrossed substitute in fact keeps a cap, raising it in steps from $3M to $15M.","watch":"Operator tax liability is unchanged, but counsel should track the tie-barred HB 4347 (likely the parallel lawful sports betting act change) and watch whether larger guaranteed earmarks to the equine fund and the Detroit $183M floor squeeze the state fund enough to prompt pressure for rate increases.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":4193,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:59.916Z"},"MI-HB4576":{"summary":"The Senate substitute turns HB 4576 into a multi-department FY2025-26 supplemental appropriations act totaling $633,999,000. It mostly re-appropriates one-time items, and Sec. 206 caps spending at the unexpended balances of the same-purpose FY2024-25 appropriations under 2024 PA 121, 2025 PA 15 and 2025 PA 22. Its only gaming content is a small Treasury carryforward in Sec. 114 for Michigan Gaming Control Board and Lottery operations and IT, funded partly from the internet sports betting, internet gaming and fantasy contest funds.","mechanics":["Sec. 101: gross appropriation of $633,999,000, of which $351,454,600 is general fund and $245,081,600 is other state restricted revenue; Sec. 201 puts state-source spending at $596,536,200.","Sec. 114 (Treasury): one-time lines of $1,584,900 for casino gaming control operations, $1,382,700 for gaming control IT services and projects, $1,050,100 for lottery IT and $1,950,000 for lottery operations.","Sec. 114 funding sources: $853,500 from the internet sports betting fund, $1,707,000 from the internet gaming fund, $407,100 from the fantasy contest fund and $3,000,100 from the state lottery fund.","Sec. 206: all spending is limited to unexpended FY2024-25 balances for the same purposes, so these are carryforwards rather than new money.","The non-gaming bulk includes $159.3M for Make it in Michigan (Sec. 1003 creates the Make it in Michigan competitiveness fund), $102.6M in community enhancement grants, $50M in indigent defense grants and $31.5M for IT investment.","The supplied text cuts off mid-Sec. 1003, and it contains no changes to gaming tax rates, licensing or conduct rules."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. The bill is unusual mainly because its caption describes a Department of Education budget while the Senate substitute is an omnibus carryforward bill. Its gaming relevance is limited to drawing regulator operating and IT money from the internet sports betting, iGaming and fantasy contest funds.","watch":"Watch whether the $853,500 internet sports betting fund draw and the related MGCB operations and IT lines survive conference unchanged, since they show how operator-generated restricted funds are being used to pay for regulator operations and technology.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":55108,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:00.186Z"},"MI-HB5619":{"summary":"HB 5619 is Michigan's FY2026-27 omnibus appropriations act. The portion of the text provided covers only Article 1, the Department of Agriculture and Rural Development: $132,213,600 gross ($74,222,200 GF/GP), plus standard boilerplate on reporting, contracting, E-Verify, foreign-adversary contracting bans, and DEI spending limits. The only gaming-adjacent content is Sec. 108, which funds horse-racing purses and breeder awards from the Agriculture Equine Industry Development Fund.","mechanics":["Sec. 108 (Fairs and Expositions) appropriates $9,332,200, all from the Agriculture Equine Industry Development Fund with $0 GF/GP.","Sec. 108 line items: county fairs $3,000,000; fairs and racing $201,600; purses and supplements for fairs and licensed tracks $4,073,600; standardbred breeders' awards $345,900; standardbred purses and supplements at licensed tracks $991,100; standardbred sire stakes $720,000.","Sec. 101 totals for the department are $132,213,600 gross, $15,292,000 federal, $42,392,800 state restricted, and $74,222,200 GF/GP, for the fiscal year ending September 30, 2027.","Sec. 223 requires E-Verify for contractors and bars contracts with listed foreign adversaries (China, Russia, Iran, North Korea, Cuba, the Maduro regime, Syria).","Secs. 226-227 bar use of state funds for services to non-qualified aliens and for DEI programs as defined by federal EOs 14151, 14168 and 14173.","The provided text is truncated in Article 1. Other departmental articles, which could include the Gaming Control Board or Lottery, are not shown."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. As an omnibus budget, the bill is unusual only in bundling multiple departments into one act. Its sole visible wagering link is the funding of pari-mutuel horse-racing purses and standardbred programs through a dedicated equine fund rather than the general fund.","watch":"Obtain the full engrossed text and check the later articles, especially LARA/Gaming Control Board, Treasury/Lottery and any boilerplate on internet gaming or sports betting fund transfers, for changes to regulatory funding, fee use or restricted-fund diversions, since the visible Article 1 touches gaming only through racing purse appropriations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":1554946,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:00.806Z"},"MI-HB4706":{"summary":"HB 4706, enacted as Public Act 22 of 2025 with immediate effect on October 7, 2025, is Michigan's omnibus appropriations act for FY 2025-2026, with supplemental appropriations for FY 2024-2025, covering multiple departments and the judicial and legislative branches. The text supplied covers only Article 1 (Department of Agriculture and Rural Development) and cuts off in Sec. 701. Within that portion, the only gaming-related content is horse-racing funding in Sec. 108; no sports betting, iGaming, or Gaming Control Board provisions appear in the text provided.","mechanics":["Article 1 appropriates $145,885,000 gross to the Department of Agriculture and Rural Development for FY ending September 30, 2026, including $77,496,400 general fund/general purpose (Sec. 101).","Sec. 108 (Fairs and Expositions) appropriates $4,764,200 for horse racing and fairs. Of that, $4,514,200 comes from the agriculture equine industry development fund and $250,000 from the general fund.","Sec. 108 line items include fairs and racing ($258,600), the horse racing advisory commission ($125,000), purses and supplements for fairs and licensed tracks ($2,073,600), standardbred breeders awards ($345,900), standardbred purses at licensed tracks ($991,100), and standardbred sire stakes ($720,000).","Sec. 210 authorizes contingency appropriations that can be spent only after transfer to a line item under MCL 18.1393: up to $3,000,000 federal, $2,000,000 state restricted, $100,000 local, and $100,000 private.","General boilerplate in the 200-series sections imposes reporting duties, including a Sec. 229 report on federal policy changes affecting regulated industries, plus E-Verify contractor requirements (Sec. 228) and legislatively directed grant controls (Sec. 239).","Effective date is October 7, 2025, with immediate effect."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What stands out is that the provided text touches gaming only through pari-mutuel horse-racing purse and breeder funding, routed through the agriculture equine industry development fund. Any articles funding the Michigan Gaming Control Board, the Lottery, or problem-gambling programs are not included in the excerpt and cannot be assessed here.","watch":"Obtain the full enrolled text and review any later articles covering the Gaming Control Board, Lottery, and Treasury for boilerplate conditions on internet sports betting fund allocations, problem-gambling funding, or regulator reporting duties, none of which appear in the truncated Article 1 excerpt.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1547680,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:01.263Z"},"MI-SB95":{"summary":"SB 95 (House substitute) creates the Hospital Price Transparency Act. It bars a Michigan-licensed hospital from pursuing collection actions, meaning referral to collectors, lawsuits, arbitration enforcement or credit reporting, for debts tied to dates when it was not in material compliance with federal price transparency rules (42 USC 300gg-18(e)) or did not post a state-mandated list of at least 300 shoppable services. It gives patients a civil action with refund, penalty, fee-shifting and credit-report-removal remedies. Wagering appears only in a definitional carve-out.","mechanics":["Sec. 3(1): a hospital may not initiate or pursue a collection action for items and services provided on a date it was out of material compliance with hospital price transparency laws or lacked the required public list.","The required list must cover standard charges for at least 300 shoppable services, including the 70 CMS-specified services where provided. It must show payer-specific negotiated charges, the discounted cash price, de-identified min/max charges and billing codes, and be free, searchable, prominently posted and accessible without registration (Sec. 3(1)(a)-(e)).","Critical access hospitals are covered only beginning 6 months after the effective date (Sec. 3(2)).","Remedies (Sec. 3(4)): if a judge or jury finds noncompliance, the hospital must refund amounts paid, pay the patient a penalty equal to the total debt, dismiss related suits with prejudice, pay attorney fees and costs, and remove credit reports. Collection is stayed while the action is pending, and these remedies are exclusive (Sec. 3(5)).","Gaming reference: Sec. 2(f)(viii) excludes from the definition of 'collection agency' licensees under Michigan's horse racing law, lottery act, bingo act, Gaming Control and Revenue Act, Lawful Sports Betting Act (MCL 432.401-432.419), Fantasy Contests Consumer Protection Act and Lawful Internet Gaming Act.","The bill is tie-barred to SB 94. It passed one chamber, and the House substitute was laid over one day on 2025-12-23."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, the only gaming touchpoint is the Sec. 2(f)(viii) carve-out exempting gaming, sports betting, iGaming, fantasy, lottery, bingo and racing licensees from the 'collection agency' definition. That carve-out appears to track existing Michigan collection-agency definitional language and does not regulate wagering. The bill is otherwise a hospital billing and price-transparency measure.","watch":"There is little operator exposure, since the act reaches only debts owed to hospitals, but confirm that the Sec. 2(f)(viii) exclusion for sports betting, iGaming and fantasy licensees survives any further amendment so it is not cited as precedent for narrowing similar carve-outs in Michigan's general collection statutes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":16203,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:15.673Z"},"MI-HB5833":{"summary":"HB5833 rewrites section 19 of Michigan's Lawful Sports Betting Act (MCL 432.419) to specify which individuals must submit fingerprints for state police and FBI criminal history record checks. It narrows the checks from applicants and licensees generally to individuals tied to sports betting supplier licenses and occupational licenses, then defines those categories by role and ownership. It also adds a bar on sharing check results with private entities and keeps fingerprint retention with ongoing arrest notification.","mechanics":["New subsection (1) requires an applicant to submit, on board forms, a photograph and 2 sets of fingerprints for each individual subject to licensure; subsection (2) requires persons applying for or holding a supplier or occupational license to consent to inspections, background checks, searches and seizures, and handwriting, fingerprint, and photograph exemplars.","Subsection (6) defines covered occupational-license individuals: sports traders and those acting as sports traders, their direct supervisors, supervisors of anyone with access to critical components of an internet sports betting platform, and heads of the fraud and risk, sports trading, infrastructure technology, accounting and finance, and daily state-payment remittance departments.","Subsection (7) covers, for suppliers, directors and principal executive, operations, or accounting officers who are not occupationally licensed. It also covers directors and principal executive, operations, or financial officers of a 'qualifying business': an entity that controls the applicant, holds more than a 5% direct or indirect ownership interest, or shares common ownership and provides a material aspect of the applicant's goods or services in Michigan.","Fingerprints go to the Department of State Police and the FBI. State police may charge the board a fee, and the board may not share criminal history record checks with a private entity (subsection (5)).","Subsections (6) and (7) of the existing text, renumbered, keep state police AFIS retention and FBI Rap Back participation, with immediate notice to the board of new arrest matches.","The board may fingerprint and check its own employees and prospective employees. The prior reference to board agents and contractors appears to be dropped. No effective date is specified in the text."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What stands out is the role-based enumeration of who must be fingerprinted: sports traders, platform-access supervisors, and named department heads, plus a 5% indirect-ownership look-through to parent and affiliate officers. The text also lists only supplier and occupational licenses, not sports betting operator licenses, in the record-check authority. This narrow, individual-specific drafting reads like an effort to satisfy FBI requirements for state statutory authority to run fingerprint checks, though the bill does not say so.","watch":"Operators should map which of their staff (traders, risk, IT, finance, and payment-remittance heads) and which parent and affiliate officers above the 5% threshold will be pulled into fingerprinting and continuous Rap Back monitoring, and should confirm whether the omission of operator licensees from the check authority is intentional or will be addressed elsewhere.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5894,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:17.072Z"},"MI-SB878":{"summary":"SB 878, enacted as Public Act 21 of 2026 with immediate effect on July 21, 2026, is Michigan's omnibus appropriations act for fiscal year 2026-2027, with supplemental appropriations for fiscal year 2025-2026, covering multiple departments, the judicial and legislative branches, and capital outlay. The text supplied is truncated and covers only Article 1, the Department of Agriculture and Rural Development. Its only wagering-related content is a horse-racing purse and fairs line in Sec. 108.","mechanics":["Article 1 appropriates $135,935,700 gross to the Department of Agriculture and Rural Development for the fiscal year ending September 30, 2027, including $72,145,400 in general fund/general purpose money (Sec. 101).","Sec. 108 (Fairs and Expositions) appropriates $4,389,200, entirely from the agriculture equine industry development fund, with $0 from the general fund. The line items are: fairs and racing $258,600; purses and supplements for fairs and licensed tracks $2,073,600; standardbred breeders awards $345,900; standardbred purses and supplements at licensed tracks $991,100; and standardbred sire stakes $720,000.","Sec. 201 sets total state-source spending under Article 1 at $120,784,100, of which $10,400,000 goes to local units of government.","Sec. 217 authorizes contingency appropriations on top of the line items: up to $3,000,000 federal, $2,000,000 state restricted, $100,000 local and $100,000 private. These funds are usable only after transfer to a line item under MCL 18.1393.","Standard reporting, transparency and in-person work requirements apply to the department (Secs. 205-231).","Effective date: July 21, 2026, with immediate effect."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that this is a general omnibus budget, and its only visible link to wagering is the pari-mutuel horse-racing purse and breeder-award funding in Sec. 108. The supplied text contains no sports betting, internet gaming or casino provisions.","watch":"Because the supplied text is truncated at Article 1, review the full enacted act for any Michigan Gaming Control Board or Treasury articles, boilerplate conditions, or fund transfers that could affect gaming regulatory budgets, operator fees or wagering-derived restricted funds, including the agriculture equine industry development fund.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1530228,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:19.579Z"},"MI-SB714":{"summary":"SB 714 adds section 9a to Michigan's Lawful Sports Betting Act (MCL 432.401–432.419) to regulate sports betting advertising, despite a catalog title referring to internet gaming. It requires Michigan Gaming Control Board pre-approval of all sports betting advertisements, bans deceptive ads and marketing to people under 21, sets an under-21 audience-composition limit, and requires responsible-gaming disclaimers. Violations carry escalating civil fines and, on a third violation, revocation of the operator's license, with attorney general enforcement.","mechanics":["Sec. 9a(1): every sports betting advertisement must be approved by the board before use. Sec. 9a(11) broadly defines 'advertisement' as paid marketing, including search, display, video, native, billboard, leaflet, mobile app ads and sponsorships likely to induce a wager, but excludes TV or internet programs that discuss betting or a specific operator's odds.","Sec. 9a(5): no advertising unless the advertiser has reliable evidence that no more than 30% of the TV, radio, website or print audience is reasonably expected to be under 21. Sec. 9a(7) bans marketing and sponsorships targeting under-21s but exempts operator, supplier or platform-provider naming rights for arenas, stadiums and other public places, and for TV channels.","Sec. 9a(4) and (6): bars deceptive, false or misleading ads and statements on products, signs or documents, and requires a problem-gambling disclaimer with the toll-free compulsive gaming helpline number. Sec. 9a(8) exempts ads that appear on live broadcasts because of their physical placement at the venue.","Sec. 9a(2)–(3): ads must comply with municipal ordinances, and municipalities may adopt ordinances setting reasonable restrictions on public sports betting signs, provided they are not unreasonably impracticable and do not conflict with the act or board rules.","Sec. 9a(9)–(10): penalties are up to $1,000 for a first violation, up to $5,000 for a second, and revocation of the sports betting operator license for a third or subsequent violation. The attorney general may sue to enforce."],"issue_tags":["advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills are legalization, licensing or tax vehicles (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569, IL HB2878) and do not regulate advertising in this detail. The closest analogues are CT HB05229, which restricts gaming advertising on college campuses, and CT HB05038 and IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act), which bar prediction-market advertising to or use by under-21s; SB 714 instead targets licensed sportsbooks and is broader. Its unusual features are universal board pre-approval of ads, a 30% under-21 audience cap (somewhat looser than the industry code benchmark of a 73.6% adult audience), express municipal authority over betting signage, and a mandatory-sounding license revocation on a third violation.","watch":"Watch the third-strike revocation in Sec. 9a(9)(c), because the bill does not say whether each ad, impression or day counts as a separate violation; also watch the operational burden of board pre-approval for every ad, the evidentiary standard for the 30% audience test, and the risk of a patchwork of municipal signage ordinances.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3318,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:22.119Z"},"MN-HF4240":{"summary":"HF4240 (Laws 2026, ch. 102) is mainly an election-administration bill covering absentee and early voting, canvass timelines, and county ballot questions. Section 12 adds new Minn. Stat. 211B.25, which makes it a petty misdemeanor for a candidate to place a wager with a prediction market on the outcome of an election in which the candidate is running. Despite the title, the operative text reaches only candidates, not sitting elected officials who are not on the ballot, and it places no obligations on prediction-market operators.","mechanics":["New 211B.25, subd. 1 defines \"prediction market\" broadly as \"a system that allows consumers to place a wager on the future outcome of a federal, state, or local election.\" \"Wager\" is defined as a contract under which parties agree to a gain or loss of money, property, or benefit by one to the other.","Under 211B.25, subd. 2, a candidate who wagers with a prediction market on the outcome of their own election is guilty of a petty misdemeanor. There is no operator liability, no reporting duty, and no ban on betting on other races.","Sec. 13 amends 211B.32, subd. 1 to add 211B.25 to the exceptions from the requirement to exhaust administrative complaints, so a violation can be enforced directly rather than first going through the Office of Administrative Hearings.","Sec. 12 states no specific effective date, so Minnesota's default effective-date rule applies. Other sections run on their own schedules: some take effect the day after enactment, Sec. 3 on September 1, 2027, and the early-voting changes in Secs. 4-5 are tied to the revisor's receipt of the early voting certification.","The rest of the bill is non-gaming: canvass windows extended to the 3rd-14th day after municipal and school elections, the absentee administration election between 46 and 18 days, repeal of Minn. Stat. 5.31, and county special-election timing. No appropriation appears in the engrossed text despite the title."],"issue_tags":["prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What stands out is that Minnesota treats election prediction markets purely as a candidate-ethics problem in its campaign-practices chapter (211B). It does not regulate or prohibit the markets themselves, and it does not address CFTC-regulated event contracts or sports wagering. The sanction is light, a petty misdemeanor, and the conduct covered is narrow: only a candidate's bets on their own race.","watch":"Watch the broad \"prediction market\" definition, which would cover any platform offering election contracts, including CFTC-registered exchanges, because it could become a template for later Minnesota bills that impose know-your-customer, candidate-exclusion, or market-ban obligations directly on operators rather than on individual candidates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":27561,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:31.099Z"},"MN-HF4410":{"summary":"HF4410 creates new Minn. Stat. § 325F.756 in the consumer-protection chapter, banning any person from operating, conducting, or promoting a dual-currency online sweepstakes game that simulates casino-style or other gambling. It extends the ban to a broad list of ancillary actors, including payment processors, geolocation providers, content suppliers, platform providers, and media affiliates. It also directs the commissioner of public safety and the attorney general to bar from operating anyone who, directly or through an affiliate, knowingly accepts revenue derived from such games.","mechanics":["Definitions (subd. 1): \"dual-currency\" is a payment system letting a person play a simulated gambling program for direct or indirect consideration (including consideration tied to a related product or service) with eligibility for a prize, cash, or cash equivalent. Free-entry contests with no consideration are excluded. \"Prize\" cross-references § 325F.755, subd. 1.","\"Online sweepstakes game\" (subd. 1(c)) has three elements: it is offered via the Internet, a mobile device, or a terminal; it uses a dual-currency system exchangeable for a prize, cash, or cash equivalent; and it simulates casino-style or another form of gambling.","Subd. 2(a) prohibits any person or entity from operating, conducting, or promoting an online sweepstakes game in Minnesota.","Subd. 2(b) bars an applicant, licensed entity, financial institution, payment processor, geolocation provider, gaming content supplier, platform provider, or media affiliate from supporting, conducting, or promoting such games.","Subd. 2(c) requires the commissioner of public safety and the attorney general to deny commencing or continuing operations to any person whose own operations or an affiliate's knowingly accept revenue directly or indirectly derived from online sweepstakes, and to enforce penalties.","Subd. 3 imports the violations in § 325F.755, subd. 7. The text sets no specific penalty amounts and no effective date."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CA AB 831, which works within California's existing unfair-practices framework for contests and sweepstakes, HF4410 imposes an outright ban and expressly reaches third-party vendors and payment and geolocation providers. That makes it closer to the supplier-liability approach some states pair with sweepstakes bans. The bill does nothing to authorize or tax sports wagering, in contrast to AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, or HI HB 1308. Its most unusual feature is the subd. 2(c) \"revenue from illegal markets\" clause, which makes a regulator-level bar to operating turn on an affiliate's acceptance of sweepstakes-derived revenue.","watch":"Watch subd. 2(b)–(c): an operator, its licensed entity, or its suppliers could face a bar on commencing or continuing operations in Minnesota if any affiliate knowingly accepts sweepstakes-derived revenue anywhere, so operators should audit corporate affiliates, vendors, and media partners for sweepstakes exposure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2894,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:33.090Z"},"MN-HF4949":{"summary":"HF4949 creates a Minnesota sports and events reimbursement program, modeled on events trust funds, under new section 116J.8753. After a listed major sporting or entertainment event is held in Minnesota, the University of Minnesota estimates the incremental state tax revenue attributable to it, and that amount is diverted to a special revenue account and paid to the local organizing committee. The bill does not mention sports wagering and does not tax, authorize or regulate betting.","mechanics":["Eligible events are a closed list of 29 categories in 116J.8753 subd. 1(c), including the Super Bowl, NFL Draft, NCAA Final Four and Frozen Four, the MLB, NBA, NHL and WNBA All-Star Games, World Cup soccer, UFC and WWE premium events. To qualify, a site selection organization must have chosen Minnesota over at least one out-of-state site, as the sole site or sole regional site, and the event must be held no more than once a year (subd. 3).","Under new 270C.45 subd. 2, the University of Minnesota must begin estimating incremental revenue within 10 days after the event. The estimate covers a one-year window starting two months before the event and includes the state sales tax, rental vehicle taxes, the liquor gross receipts tax (295.75), the 295.81 gross receipts tax, the retail delivery fee (168E.03), and individual and corporate income taxes.","The commissioner must disburse the incremental amount to the requesting local organizing committee within 30 days of the determination (270C.45 subd. 3). The bill sets no dollar cap and no state matching requirement.","Allowable uses under 116J.8753 subd. 5 are event preparation and conduct costs, including facility improvements, renovations, acquisition or construction.","Local organizing committees must apply to the commissioner of revenue and must report audited financials, attendance including non-resident estimates, and economic impact data. The commissioner of revenue has rulemaking authority.","Conforming deposit amendments touch 168E.09, 290.62, 295.75, 295.81 and 297A.94 (new para. (m)). All effective dates are left blank. There are apparent drafting errors: 270C.45 subd. 1(d) cross-references 116J.7853, and subd. 3 refers to the commissioner of employment and economic development."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual for its uncapped, post-event tax-increment reimbursement covering both state income taxes and gross receipts taxes. It also hands the revenue estimate to the University of Minnesota rather than the revenue department or comptroller, which resembles major-events trust fund structures elsewhere.","watch":"Watch whether any future Minnesota sports-wagering tax gets added to the list of captured taxes in 270C.45 subd. 2(c), since that would divert betting handle or revenue from marquee events away from whatever dedicated fund a legalization bill creates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":26478,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:37.966Z"},"MN-HF4437":{"summary":"HF4437 makes it a felony in Minnesota to operate or allow a business that takes wagers, trades, contracts or financial positions on future events tied to sports, contests or casino-style games, people, politics, catastrophes or death. This squarely targets CFTC-registered prediction markets. It also criminalizes certain advertising of those products and payment processing after an attorney general cease-and-desist letter. A conviction bars the person from any gaming-related license for ten years. The analysis is based on the introduced text; a 1st engrossment adopted in committee on April 7, 2026 may differ.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends Minn. Stat. 609.75, subd. 3, clause (2). Futures contracts on securities or commodities are no longer automatically 'not bets' to the extent covered by new 609.76, subd. 9, removing the commodities-contract safe harbor for these event contracts.","New 609.76, subd. 9(b) creates a felony for allowing, maintaining or operating an entity that lets participants place wagers, trades, contracts or financial positions dependent on future events. Six categories are covered: (1) sports, including in-game events; (2) contests or casino-style gaming based on skill, merit, performance or chance, regardless of entry fee; (3) people; (4) federal, state or county politics and government conduct; (5) catastrophes; and (6) death.","Subd. 9(c) makes advertising that promotes the prohibited transactions a felony if any of the following applies: it airs between 8:00 a.m. and 10:00 p.m. or during a live sporting event; 10% or more of the audience is reasonably expected to be under 21; it claims legality although the conduct is not authorized gaming; the operator certified legality to place the ad; or it appears on public property or within 500 feet of a school or playground.","Subd. 9(d) makes it a felony for financial institutions, payment processors, geolocation providers, gaming content suppliers, platform providers, media affiliates and others to knowingly keep processing payments for unauthorized gaming after receiving an attorney general cease-and-desist letter.","Subd. 9(e) bars anyone convicted under the subdivision from receiving any gaming-related license until ten years after conviction. Subd. 9(f) carves pari-mutuel horse racing under chapter 240 and private social transactions out of the sports prong only.","Effective August 1, 2026, for crimes committed on or after that date."],"issue_tags":["prediction","advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Other states' prediction-market bills largely regulate rather than ban. Connecticut HB05038 and Illinois HB5059 (the ORACLE Act) impose a 21+ age floor and advertising limits on prediction market platforms. Illinois HB5142 folds sports prediction markets into the regulated definition of sports wagering, and California AB1840 addresses public officials' use of prediction contracts under the Political Reform Act. Minnesota's bill instead imposes outright felony prohibitions across six event categories. It is unusual in extending criminal liability to payment processors, geolocation providers and media affiliates after a cease-and-desist letter, and in attaching a ten-year licensing bar.","watch":"Watch for two risks: the subd. 9(e) ten-year bar on 'any gaming-related license' could exclude operators running or partnering on prediction-market products (sports event contracts) from any future Minnesota sports-betting market, and the broad 'contests' prong and daytime advertising felony could sweep in DFS and adjacent products; both prongs also invite a federal preemption challenge under the Commodity Exchange Act.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduction","text_chars":13025,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:41.189Z"},"MN-SF3432":{"summary":"SF3432, signed into law, is mainly a public-safety and security appropriations bill covering judicial, legislative, and constitutional-officer security, a new State Patrol legislative services unit for threat assessment, and related Capitol security policy. Its title also says it prohibits prediction-market-related activities and provides criminal penalties, and it amends Minn. Stat. 299L.03, subd. 12 (Gambling Enforcement Division) and 609.75, subd. 3 (the gambling-definitions provision). The supplied text is cut off in Article 3, so the operative prediction-market language is not in the excerpt.","mechanics":["Article 1 appropriates FY2027 security money: $6,062,000 to the Supreme Court (including a $4,000,000 courthouse security grant program with a 50% nonstate match), $60,000 to the Court of Appeals, $843,000 to district courts, $1,012,000 to the BCA for threat assessment, and $100,000 each to the AG, SOS, and State Auditor.","Article 2, sec. 10 creates new Minn. Stat. 299E.10, a legislative services unit within Capitol Security that identifies, assesses, and mitigates threats to legislators. The House and Senate must reimburse personal-protection costs.","Article 2, sec. 2 creates new 299A.96, requiring elected officials to submit emergency contact data annually by January 31 unless they opt out. The data is classified as private.","Article 2, sec. 11 establishes a Security Services Task Force. Its report is due February 1, 2027, and it expires June 30, 2027.","According to the title, the bill amends 299L.03, subd. 12 and 609.75, subd. 3, adds new chapter 609 coding with criminal penalties, and prohibits prediction-market-related activities. The specific text is not in the supplied excerpt.","The bill repeals 2026 S.F. 4760, article 3, section 1 and article 8, sections 1, 2, and 3, if enacted. The supplied text does not show what those sections contain."],"issue_tags":["prediction"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The unusual feature is that a prediction-market prohibition with criminal penalties appears in an omnibus security appropriations bill rather than in standalone gaming legislation. That placement suggests late-session conference committee drafting; the conference committee report deleted everything on May 16, 2026. Because it amends 609.75, subd. 3 and the Gambling Enforcement Division's powers in 299L.03, Minnesota appears to be treating prediction-market activity through its existing criminal gambling framework rather than a licensing regime.","watch":"Obtain the full enrolled text to confirm how the new chapter 609 offense and the 609.75 and 299L.03 amendments define prohibited prediction-market activity, including whether they reach sports event contracts offered on CFTC-registered exchanges, advertising or facilitation, or Minnesota-located users; the bill also raises a likely federal preemption fight.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":90824,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:47.997Z"},"MN-SF2374":{"summary":"SF2374 is Minnesota's 2025 Senate omnibus tax bill, covering income and corporate franchise, sales and use, property, mining, local government aid, tax increment financing and other miscellaneous taxes, and it repeals the chapter 297D tax on illegal cannabis and controlled substances. The text provided covers only the start of Article 1 and contains no sports betting, lawful gambling or wagering provisions. Chapter 297E (lawful gambling taxes) and chapter 349 do not appear in the list of amended or repealed statutes.","mechanics":["Raises the net investment income tax under section 290.033 from 1 percent to 1.5 percent on net investment income above $1,000,000 for individuals, estates and trusts, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.","Ties the elective pass-through entity tax (section 289A.08, subd. 7a) to expire at the same time and on the same terms as the federal SALT cap in IRC section 164(b)(6)(B), for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2027.","Revises the beginning farmer credit (section 41B.0391): replaces 'emerging farmer' with 'limited land access farmer' for the 12 percent sale-credit rate and keeps the $4,000,000 annual allocation cap.","Moves political contribution refunds to electronic receipts with unique validation numbers and daily validation reports from the Campaign Finance Board to the commissioner of revenue, effective for contributions made after December 31, 2026.","Adds new income subtractions for coerced-debt discharges, consumer enforcement compensation payments, critical access dental clinic student loan payments and foreign service pensions, and expands the National Guard subtraction to neighboring states.","Repeals sections 297D.01 to 297D.13 (illegal cannabis and controlled substances tax) and amends section 609.902, subd. 4, the racketeering 'criminal act' definition. The amendment to 609.902 is not visible in the excerpt and may be a conforming change."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Kansas SB303 (House Substitute) shows a general tax package carrying a gaming measure, a 2 percent excise on all sports wagers, but nothing comparable appears in the visible SF2374 text. The same is true of Iowa SF 605 and SSB 1152 (state withholding on sports wagering winnings) and Illinois HB3159 (an added 2 percent tax on sports-wagering adjusted gross receipts). SF2374 is closer in character to Kentucky HB 757, North Carolina H554 and New York's budget bills: a broad revenue vehicle whose visible provisions do not touch gaming. That is unremarkable in itself, since Minnesota has not legalized sports betting.","watch":"Because this is a must-pass omnibus vehicle heading to the floor and likely conference, watch later engrossments and the conference report for any amendments to lawful gambling taxes (chapter 297E), sports wagering authorization, or the section 609.902 racketeering definitions, as well as the NIIT increase's effect on high-income owners.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":267173,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:50.037Z"},"MN-SF4223":{"summary":"As introduced, SF4223 is a campaign finance and ethics bill amending Minnesota Statutes chapter 10A. It narrows the definition of metropolitan governmental unit, extends earmarking, coordination and party-expenditure rules to local candidates, revises special-election reporting deadlines and periods, and raises certain itemization thresholds for ballot question committees. It does not regulate sports betting or gaming; the only gaming-adjacent text is an unchanged clause requiring Minnesota Racing Commission personnel to file statements of economic interest.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 redefines 'metropolitan governmental unit' (10A.01, subd. 24) as a metro-area county, a regional railroad authority, the Metropolitan Council, the Metropolitan Parks and Open Space Commission, the Metropolitan Airports Commission, or the Minnesota Sports Facilities Authority. Secs. 2 to 4 separately carry forward conflict-of-interest and economic-interest filing duties for metro counties and cities over 50,000.","Sec. 4 retains existing 10A.09, subd. 1(5): Racing Commission members, the director, chief of security, medical officer, inspector of pari-mutuels, and stewards (including contractors) must file a statement of economic interest within 60 days of assuming duties. This text is unchanged.","Sec. 5 requires the secretary of state, county auditors, and appointing officials to give the Campaign Finance Board each filer's mailing address, phone number and email.","Secs. 12 and 15 change special-election reporting. If a special primary or general election falls on regular election dates, the regular 15-day and 10-day deadlines apply. A new report is due seven days after the filing period closes, with defined coverage periods.","Sec. 14 sets contributor, loan and receipt itemization at $200, or $500 for ballot question political committees and funds. Sec. 16 adds a $100-per-day late fee (capped at $1,000) for failing to give the 24-hour notice of exceeding spending-limit thresholds. Sec. 17 extends the 10A.27, subd. 17 penalties to ballot question committees.","Sec. 8 extends the earmarking ban (civil penalty up to $3,000; gross misdemeanor) to local candidates. Sec. 18 extends party-unit expenditure exceptions to local candidates and to section 211A.12."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What stands out for gaming clients is only that the bill re-enacts, without change, Minnesota's economic-interest filing requirement for Racing Commission officials and pari-mutuel inspectors and stewards. The bill also adds the Minnesota Sports Facilities Authority to the metropolitan governmental unit definition, a stadium governance body rather than a wagering regulator. Note that the text reviewed is the introduction; the bill passed third reading as amended under an 'omnibus elections' title, so the 2nd engrossment may contain different provisions.","watch":"Review the 2nd engrossment (posted 04/24/2026) for any added provisions touching gaming, such as political contribution limits or disclosure rules for gaming licensees or lobbyists, since the introduced text addresses wagering only through the unchanged Racing Commission disclosure clause.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduction","text_chars":44741,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:55.002Z"},"MN-SF4139":{"summary":"SF4139 would legalize mobile sports betting in Minnesota under the Department of Public Safety (new sections in chapter 299L). Per its title, it would also license operators, platform providers and suppliers, preempt local restrictions, create a sports betting tax in new chapter 297J, and touch charitable gambling tax rates, pari-mutuel racing and amateur sports grants. The excerpt provided covers only Article 1 (Responsible Gaming), which imposes unusually strict player-protection and advertising rules, and the bill stalled on 2026-03-23 when a motion to adopt the committee report failed.","mechanics":["Regulator and rules (§299L.15): the commissioner regulates mobile sports betting and may use expedited rulemaking for rules noticed before Jan. 1, 2027. Account funding by credit card is excluded. Sports governing bodies may request bet-type or event restrictions; operators get notice and a chance to respond, and the commissioner must decide before the event or within 7 days.","Advertising (§299L.37): every ad must carry 1-800-GAMBLER and an addiction warning. Ads are barred in media where 30% or more of the audience is reasonably expected to be under 21. Promotions may not be called \"risk-free\" if the player risks their own money. College partnerships may not promote betting except alumni-only or responsible-gambling content. NIL deals with in-state amateur athletes are banned, as is advertising on K-12 property, on college campuses (unless general-audience) and in college-owned media.","Personal limits (§299L.405): operators must offer deposit, loss/wager, time and cooling-off limits. Default settings are $500 per 24 hours in losses or wagers, $3,000 per 30 days, $500 daily deposit and a 72-hour cooling-off period. Loosening a limit takes effect only after 7 days, and players must review limits at account setup and at least quarterly. Exclusions triggered by limits must be reported to the commissioner and shared across all operators.","Push notifications (§299L.41): banned while the app is inactive, except for fraud, responsible-gaming or identity-verification messages.","Exclusion list (§299L.45): self-exclusion for 1, 3 or 5 years or lifetime, plus entries submitted by guardians, licensees and sports governing bodies. Insiders such as athletes, coaches, referees, owners and holders of nonpublic information are barred from betting. Knowingly accepting a wager from an excluded person is a license violation. Marketing contact is barred during a restriction period and afterward until the person bets again.","Studies (§§299L.75–.751): a baseline gambling-prevalence study before launch, updated every three years, plus a study of sports betting's impact on gambling disorders, suicide and youth. Tax rates, license fees and license counts are not in the excerpt provided."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like AK HB145/SB194, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569 and GA HB 686, SF4139 is a full legalization-and-licensing bill, but its Article 1 player protections go well beyond those models. Its preset default loss and deposit limits, push-notification ban and statewide sharing of limit-based exclusions are features not reflected in the listed bills' summaries. Its campus-advertising restrictions parallel CT HB05229, though Minnesota adds a 30% under-21 audience threshold and an amateur-athlete NIL ban. Its stated preemption of local restrictions echoes IL HB4171's denial of home-rule power to regulate or tax sports wagering.","watch":"Watch the §299L.405 defaults ($500 daily and $3,000 monthly loss/wager caps, $500 daily deposit cap) and the post-exclusion marketing blackout, which would directly cap high-value player activity, and get the full text to see the chapter 297J tax rate and license terms if the bill is revived after its failed committee-report vote.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduction","text_chars":195963,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:58.837Z"},"MN-SF4474":{"summary":"SF4474 adds new section 325F.756 to Minnesota's consumer protection chapter, banning any person or entity from operating, conducting, or promoting an online sweepstakes game in Minnesota, defined as an internet- or mobile-accessible game using a dual-currency system redeemable for prizes or cash that simulates casino-style or other gambling. It also bars a broad set of ancillary parties, including financial institutions, payment processors, geolocation providers, content suppliers, platform providers, and media affiliates, from supporting or promoting such games. Enforcement runs through the existing penalties and remedies of section 325F.755, subdivision 7, with investigative and civil-fine authority given to the commissioner of public safety.","mechanics":["Definition (subd. 1(c)): an online sweepstakes game must (1) be available online or on a mobile or similar device, (2) use a dual-currency system exchangeable for a prize, cash, cash equivalent, or chance to win, and (3) simulate casino-style or another form of gambling.","Dual-currency (subd. 1(b)) covers direct or indirect consideration, including consideration tied to a related product, service, or activity; true no-consideration contests are excluded.","Subd. 2(a) prohibits operating, conducting, or promoting an online sweepstakes game in Minnesota.","Subd. 2(b) extends liability to applicants, licensed entities, financial institutions, payment processors, geolocation providers, gaming content suppliers, platform providers, and media affiliates that support, conduct, or promote such games.","Subd. 3 applies the penalties and remedies of 325F.755, subd. 7, and authorizes the commissioner of public safety to investigate, issue notices of violation, impose civil fines, and bring enforcement actions.","No effective date or specific fine amounts appear in the text; 'prize' is borrowed from 325F.755, subd. 1."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization-and-tax bills on the list (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), SF4474 is purely prohibitory and targets the sweepstakes casino model rather than licensed wagering. It is closest to CA AB 831, which also addresses sweepstakes operations, and to IL HB3733/SB 1705 on gambling-device definitions. What stands out is the express secondary-liability reach to payment processors, geolocation vendors, content suppliers, platforms, and media affiliates, a supply-chain approach not described in the listed bills. It also sits in the consumer-protection chapter (325F) rather than a gaming code.","watch":"Clients that are licensed entities, applicants, content suppliers, or media and affiliate partners should audit any relationships with sweepstakes platforms, because subd. 2(b) explicitly reaches licensed entities and applicants that 'support' or 'promote' these games, which could create collateral licensing exposure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":2945,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:00.449Z"},"MN-SF4365":{"summary":"SF4365 is Minnesota's 2026 omnibus commerce policy bill covering insurance, the premium security reinsurance plan, prescription drug affordability, consumer protection, telecommunications, securities, financial products and unclaimed property. Its only gaming content, per the title, is a modification of charitable gambling law through an amendment to Minn. Stat. section 349.211, subdivision 2b (lawful gambling prize limits), but that section is not in the excerpt provided. The Senate file was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-28 after the House companion, HF4188, was substituted on General Orders, so HF4188 is now the live vehicle.","mechanics":["Article 1 removes the Prescription Drug Affordability Advisory Council from the board's conflict-of-interest and drug-identification provisions and repeals sections 62J.86, subd. 2 and 62J.88.","Article 2 restructures the one-time 2028 assessment on group health carriers for the premium security plan. The commissioner must approve each carrier's amount, payments go to the commissioner by August 1, 2028, and the association must check the assessment's accuracy by March 15, 2029. A premium tax credit for the assessment applies for taxable years after December 31, 2028.","Article 3 requires health insurers, nonprofit health service plans and HMOs with at least 25,000 enrollees to notify the commissioner by April 15 if enrollment grows more than 35 percent, or exceeds a commissioner-set threshold for a product line. The commissioner may then demand financial projections and issue corrective orders (new sections 60A.071 and 62D.085).","New section 60A.593 bars a domestic health organization at or below its company action level RBC from raising officer or director pay, or making preferential payments, without the commissioner's approval.","The title lists an amendment to section 349.211, subd. 2b (charitable gambling prizes), but the operative text is not in the provided excerpt."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that the only gambling content is a charitable gambling prize-limit tweak buried in a sprawling commerce omnibus. Nothing in the visible text touches sports betting, online gaming, taxation of wagering or operator licensing.","watch":"Watch HF4188, the substituted vehicle, for the final text of the section 349.211, subd. 2b charitable gambling amendment and for any sports-betting or gaming language added in conference, since omnibus commerce bills can carry late gaming amendments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":264159,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:05.986Z"},"MN-SF4511":{"summary":"SF4511 (introduction version) makes it a felony under Minn. Stat. 609.76, new subd. 9, to operate or allow a platform where participants place wagers, trades, contracts or financial positions, including securities or commodities, that depend on future events in sports, contests or casino-style gaming, people, politics, catastrophe, or death. It carves these event contracts out of the existing 609.75, subd. 3(2) exclusion for futures and commodities contracts. It also criminalizes certain advertising and payment facilitation, and bars anyone convicted under the subdivision from any gaming-related license for ten years.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends 609.75, subd. 3(2). Futures and commodities contracts are no longer 'not bets' to the extent covered by new 609.76, subd. 9.","Sec. 2, subd. 9(b) makes it a felony to allow or operate an entity offering wagers, trades or contracts on future events in six categories: sports (including in-game events), contests/casino-style gaming, people, politics (federal, state or county elections and government conduct), catastrophe, and death.","Subd. 9(c) makes advertising that promotes prohibited transactions a felony if any of the following apply: it airs between 8 a.m. and 10 p.m. or during a live sports broadcast; 10% or more of the audience is reasonably expected to be under 21; it claims legality; the operator certified legality to place the ad; or it appears on public property or within 500 feet of a school or playground.","Subd. 9(d) makes it a felony for payment processors, financial institutions, geolocation providers, platform providers, content suppliers and media affiliates to keep knowingly processing payments for unauthorized gaming after receiving an attorney general cease-and-desist letter.","Subd. 9(e) bars anyone convicted under subd. 9 from any gaming-related license for 10 years from conviction. Subd. 9(f) exempts pari-mutuel horse racing under ch. 240 and private social bets from the sports clause only.","Effective August 1, 2026, for crimes committed on or after that date."],"issue_tags":["prediction","advertising","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"SF4511 is far more aggressive than the other prediction-market bills listed. IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act) and CT HB05038 regulate prediction markets through age-21 limits and advertising restrictions, and IL HB5142 folds sports prediction markets into the regulated sports-wagering definition. SF4511 instead criminalizes event contracts outright as a felony across six broad categories. CA AB1840 addresses prediction markets only through Political Reform Act ethics rules. The cease-and-desist trigger for felony liability of payment processors and geolocation vendors, and the 10-year licensing bar, have no analogue among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch whether later engrossments keep the broad paragraph (b) 'contests or casino-style gaming' and sports language and the 10-year licensing bar in paragraph (e). Because Minnesota has not legalized sports betting, any future licensed sportsbook, its vendors (geolocation, payments, media), and its affiliates could face felony exposure or license disqualification, and the bill invites a federal CEA preemption fight with CFTC-registered exchanges.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduction","text_chars":13119,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:09.899Z"},"MN-SF4760":{"summary":"The introduced text of SF4760 amends Minnesota's Criminal Offenders Rehabilitation Act (Minn. Stat. ch. 364) to change how public employers and licensing authorities weigh a criminal conviction that directly relates to the job or licensed occupation sought. It requires the applicant to show both rehabilitation and present fitness, and it turns the previously mandatory forms of evidence into factors the authority may consider. The enacted omnibus public safety version, reflected in the 3rd engrossment and the 5/11/2026 conference report, is not in the text provided, so any gaming content it may contain cannot be confirmed here.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends 364.03, subd. 3(a): an applicant with a directly related conviction is not disqualified if they show 'both' competent evidence of sufficient rehabilitation and present fitness to perform the duties.","Sec. 1, new subd. 3(b): the licensing or hiring authority 'may consider' a list of evidence. Previously, rehabilitation could be established by producing specified documents, and the authority 'shall consider' the applicant's other evidence.","The listed evidence includes a DD-214 showing honorable discharge for service after conviction; a release order plus at least one year since release without a new conviction and compliance with probation or parole; a DOC discharge order; and the nature, circumstances, age at offense and time elapsed for the crime, along with any other competent evidence such as reference letters.","Subd. 3(c) is retained: a DD-214 stops counting as rehabilitation evidence if the person is later convicted of a gross misdemeanor or felony after discharge.","Sec. 2 amends 364.05: a written denial notice must state the earliest date the person may reapply with a hiring or licensing authority, and that all rehabilitation evidence presented on reapplication will be considered.","No effective date, gambling, wagering or gaming-specific provisions appear in the introduced text."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is the mismatch between this bill and its title. The introduced text is a narrow two-section amendment to the general occupational-licensing rehabilitation statute, while the signed law is an omnibus public safety bill that was substantially rewritten through three engrossments and a conference committee.","watch":"Pull the conference committee report and the final session law to confirm whether the enacted omnibus added any gambling-related provisions, and check whether ch. 364's discretionary rehabilitation standard reaches Minnesota gaming-related license or employee background determinations for operators' staff.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduction","text_chars":11613,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:12.198Z"},"MN-SF5052":{"summary":"SF5052 is Minnesota's 2026 Senate omnibus tax bill. It updates federal conformity, modifies income, corporate, property, sales, excise and mineral taxes and local government aids, and creates new levies including a social media tax, a Hennepin County health care tax and a tax on amounts acquired by fraud. Nothing in the text provided addresses sports betting, lawful gambling, pari-mutuel or casino taxation, and the long list of amended statutes in the title does not include Minnesota's gambling tax or regulatory chapters (297E, 349, 240).","mechanics":["Art. 1 keeps the general Internal Revenue Code reference date at May 1, 2023. New section 290.0112 selectively adopts only six provisions of the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (P.L. 119-21, sections 70301, 70307, 70404, 70405, 70434 and 70603), as enacted through March 1, 2026.","Art. 2 extends the sustainable aviation fuel credit ($1.50 per gallon) through 2035 and raises total certificate authority from $11.6 million to $36.9 million (section 41A.30).","Art. 2 raises the film production credit from 25% to 40%, plus a 5% bonus up to 45% for Minnesota key creative hires, filming outside the metro area, or a majority-Minnesota crew. The project spending threshold drops from $1,000,000 to $400,000, effective for tax years beginning after December 31, 2026 (section 116U.27).","Art. 2 extends the historic rehabilitation credit placed-in-service window from three to six years, retroactive to allocation certificates issued after June 30, 2021 (section 290.0681).","The title also announces a new social media tax, a Hennepin County health care tax, seasonal tax base replacement aid and a tax on amounts acquired by fraud. Those articles fall outside the excerpt provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike KS SB303, a tax omnibus that carries a 2% excise on all sports wagers, the visible text of SF5052 contains no wagering tax. Unlike IA SF 605, it does not touch withholding on sports-wagering winnings. In this respect it resembles general revenue packages such as IL HB2755 and KY HB 757 more than the gaming-specific bills from AK, GA, HI, MS and NE. Its selective OBBBA conformity list does not appear to include the federal wagering-loss limitation, which bears on the state tax treatment of bettors' losses.","watch":"Minnesota has not legalized sports betting, so operators should watch for floor or conference amendments that graft gaming, lawful-gambling tax or wagering-loss conformity provisions onto this vehicle, and should check whether the new social media tax reaches digital advertising spend.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":282223,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:18.980Z"},"MN-SF5101":{"summary":"SF5101 creates a Minnesota sports and events reimbursement program, similar to an events trust fund, under new section 116J.8753. After a listed major sporting or entertainment event is held in Minnesota, the incremental increase in specified state tax revenues attributable to it is estimated by the University of Minnesota and paid to the local organizing committee. The bill does not address wagering at all; its only gaming link is that many of the eligible events are heavily bet-on.","mechanics":["Eligible events are a closed list of 29 categories in 116J.8753 subd. 1(c). Examples include the Super Bowl, NFL Draft, NCAA Final Four and Frozen Four, MLB/NBA/NHL/WNBA/MLS all-star events, World Cup and Gold Cup soccer, Formula 1 US Grand Prix, UFC, WWE premium live events, X Games, and PGA of America championships.","Under subd. 3, a site selection organization must have considered at least one out-of-state site. Minnesota must be the sole site, or the sole site in a multistate region, and the event may be held no more than once a year.","Under new 270C.45 subd. 2, the University of Minnesota must start an estimate within 10 days after the event. The estimate covers a one-year window beginning two months before the event.","Taxes counted toward the increment are: state sales tax (297A.62 subd. 1), the 297A.64 vehicle rental taxes, the 295.75 liquor gross receipts tax, the 295.81 cannabis gross receipts tax, the 168E.03 retail delivery fee, and income/franchise taxes under 290.02 and 290.03. No gaming or wagering tax is included.","Revenues are diverted to a new special revenue fund account through amendments to 168E.09, 290.62, 295.75, 295.81 and 297A.94. Disbursement is due within 30 days of the determination and may only go to the requesting local organizing committee. The money can fund event preparation and conduct, including facility renovation or new construction (subd. 5).","Local organizing committees must submit an application and site-selection documentation, audited financial statements, and attendance figures including non-resident estimates. Effective dates are left blank, and the commissioner of revenue has rulemaking authority."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is an events-trust-fund style tax-increment rebate for event organizers rather than a gaming measure. Notably, it captures incremental cannabis and liquor gross receipts taxes alongside sales and income taxes, but no wagering revenue. It also contains apparent drafting errors: 270C.45 subd. 1(d) cross-references 116J.7853 instead of 116J.8753, and subd. 3 directs the commissioner (of revenue) to disburse while barring the commissioner of employment and economic development from paying other entities.","watch":"The bill has no direct effect on operators, but watch whether any amendment adds a sports-wagering or other gaming tax to the 270C.45 subd. 2(c) list of incremental revenues, which would divert betting tax receipts from tentpole events like the Super Bowl or Final Four to event organizers.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":26557,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:25.848Z"},"MO-HB2002":{"summary":"HB 2002 is Missouri's FY2027 (July 1, 2026 to June 30, 2027) operating appropriation for the State Board of Education and the Department of Elementary and Secondary Education. It funds the school foundation formula, transportation, early childhood, special education, career and technical education and federal pass-through programs. Its only gaming connection is that it draws on gaming-derived state funds, including the Sports Wagering for Education Fund, the Lottery Proceeds Fund and the Bingo Proceeds for Education Fund.","mechanics":["Section 2.015 appropriates $6,805,078 from the Sports Wagering for Education Fund (1244) to the Foundation Formula program distribution, part of a $4,282,736,000 formula line.","Lottery Proceeds Fund (1291) money is spread across many lines in the introduced text, including $132,479,987 for the foundation formula and $73,873,102 for transportation (both Sec. 2.015) and $37,467,000 for Career Ladder (Sec. 2.040).","Other Lottery Proceeds lines include $19,590,000 for special education excess costs (Sec. 2.275), $16,548,507 for early childhood special education (Sec. 2.285) and smaller amounts in Secs. 2.110, 2.140, 2.235 and 2.250.","Section 2.025 appropriates $1,876,355 from the Bingo Proceeds for Education Fund (1289) for expense and equipment of State Board-operated school programs.","The bill contains no provisions on wagering tax rates, licensing, permitted bet types, advertising by operators, or regulatory enforcement; the 'no advertising' condition in Sec. 2.160 restricts how career and technical education funds are spent and is unrelated to gaming.","The text reviewed is the introduced version and is truncated, so figures in the enacted version (signed and delivered to the Secretary of State 2026-06-30) may differ."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What stands out on its own is the relatively small Sports Wagering for Education Fund draw of about $6.8 million. It sits against roughly $2.58 billion in general revenue in the same formula line, and against far larger lottery contributions across the budget. This indicates sports wagering revenue earmarked for education is a marginal funding source in Missouri's FY2027 education budget.","watch":"Compare the enacted Sec. 2.015 Sports Wagering for Education Fund figure with actual wagering tax receipts, since the gap between projected and appropriated revenue could fuel future arguments to raise Missouri's sports wagering tax rate or narrow its deductions to deliver more education funding.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":65136,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:33.360Z"},"MN-SF757":{"summary":"SF757 would legalize statewide mobile sports betting for people 21 and older, regulated by the commissioner of public safety under new Minn. Stat. 299L.10 to 299L.80. Operator licenses go only to Minnesota's 11 federally recognized Tribes that run class III casinos, and commercial sportsbooks may take part only as licensed platform providers partnered with a Tribe. The title also covers fantasy contests, new tax chapters 297J and 297K, a ban on local restrictions, amateur sports grants, charitable gambling tax changes and pari-mutuel racing, but the supplied text cuts off in Article 1 (sec. 299L.40) before those provisions.","mechanics":["Licenses (sec. 299L.20, 299L.28): up to 11 mobile operator licenses, limited to entities wholly owned by an Indian Tribe conducting class III gaming, one per Tribe, valid 20 years, annual fee $2,125; each operator may contract with no more than one platform provider, whose app must display the Tribe's brand (sec. 299L.35).","Platform providers (sec. 299L.29): up to 11 one-year licenses, with a $16,500 application fee, a $250,000 license fee and $83,000 renewal; Iowa-licensed providers as of Feb. 1, 2024 get provisional licenses for a $250,000 registration fee, must partner with a Tribe, and the provisional license expires by Dec. 31, 2025.","Suppliers (sec. 299L.30): one-year licenses with a $16,500 application fee, a $75,000 license fee and $33,000 renewal; Iowa-licensed suppliers can get a provisional license for $75,000; sports governing bodies supplying raw statistical data are excluded from the supplier definition.","Wagering limits (sec. 299L.40): no bets on player injuries, penalties, disciplinary rulings or replay reviews; no proposition bets on college sports; deposits cannot be wagered until three hours after deposit; rules may not allow credit-card funding (sec. 299L.15, subd. 2(7)); peer-to-peer exchange wagers are excluded from regulation (sec. 299L.10, subd. 21(f)).","Advertising (sec. 299L.37): ads must carry 1-800-GAMBLER and an addiction warning; ads are barred in media where 30 percent or more of the audience is reasonably expected to be under 21, on K-12 property, in college-owned news outlets, and on campuses unless aimed at a general audience; ads may not target excluded persons.","Integrity and responsible gaming (sec. 299L.15): sports governing bodies may ask the commissioner to restrict wagers on a good-cause showing, with operators given notice and a chance to respond and a response due within seven days; the commissioner must run a 24/7 helpline, and may make rules on wager-frequency limits, waiting periods and behavioral monitoring including account suspension."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","advertising","integrity","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145/SB194 and GA HB 686, which create commercially accessible mobile licensing and taxation, SF757 gives Tribes exclusive operator status and leaves national sportsbooks as subordinate platform vendors, one per Tribe. Its Iowa-reciprocity provisional licensing is unusual among the listed bills. Its campus and college-media advertising limits track CT HB05229, and its carve-out of peer-to-peer exchange wagers contrasts with IL HB5142, which would pull sports prediction markets into the definition of sports wagering. HI HB1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 likewise assign regulation to an executive department but are not Tribe-exclusive.","watch":"Operator clients should watch the Tribe-exclusive structure, which caps the market at 11 platform partnerships and requires carrying Tribal branding, along with the operationally heavy consumer rules: the three-hour deposit hold, the bans on college props and injury, penalty and replay markets, discretionary wager-frequency limits, and the 30 percent under-21 audience test. The tax rates in the proposed chapters 297J and 297K are not shown in the excerpt and still need to be reviewed.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":222521,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:34.977Z"},"MN-SF978":{"summary":"SF978 would legalize statewide mobile sports betting for adults 21 and over, run exclusively by Minnesota's 11 federally recognized Tribes as licensed operators, each partnering with at most one licensed platform provider, under regulation by the commissioner of public safety in new chapter 299L. It sets license tiers and fees, detailed problem-gambling and advertising restrictions, and a process for sports governing bodies to seek wager restrictions. The title also references a new tax chapter 297J, a ban on local restrictions, penalties and appropriations, but the supplied text stops at section 299L.40, so the tax rate and later articles cannot be confirmed.","mechanics":["Licensing (299L.20, .28–.30): up to 11 operator licenses, Tribe-only (one per Tribe, must conduct class III casino gaming), valid 20 years, $2,125 annual fee. Up to 11 platform provider licenses, valid 3 years, with a $6,000 application fee, $38,250 license fee and $25,500 renewal. Supplier licenses carry the same fees with no cap. Licenses are non-transferable except platform licenses with the commissioner's approval.","Each operator may contract with no more than one platform provider, and the platform's app must display the Tribe's brand (299L.35). Applicants filing within 30 days of the application window opening share a common launch date (299L.15 subd. 8).","Wagering scope (299L.10, .40): participants must be 21 or older. Exchange wagering and prop, parlay and futures bets are allowed if the commissioner approves the wager type. Excluded are school, college and youth events as organized events, fantasy contests, horse racing, and individual-athlete performance in a single collegiate game. Esports require approval by both the commissioner and the publisher.","Rulemaking (299L.15 subd. 2): no minimum hold requirement may be set. Account funding by credit card or 'electronic wallets' is barred. Operators must monitor for problem gambling, with the power to pause accounts. Expedited rulemaking is available for rules noticed before 1/1/2026. The state must run a 24/7 helpline and audit compliance with the exclusion list.","Advertising (299L.37): ads are banned where 10% or more of the audience is reasonably expected to be under 21, on public property, within 500 feet of a school or playground, and on college campuses or college-owned news outlets. College partnerships may not promote betting except with alumni networks or responsible-gambling content. NIL deals with in-state amateur athletes are banned, as are 'risk-free' or 'free play' claims. Ads must carry a helpline number and warning, and licensees must file an annual ad-placement report by Feb. 1.","Integrity (299L.15 subd. 4): sports governing bodies may request bans on events or wager types. Operators get notice and a chance to respond, and good cause is the standard. The commissioner must rule before the event or within 7 days and may grant provisional relief."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","advertising","integrity","prop","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569, which create state-agency licensing open to commercial operators, SF978 restricts operator licenses to Tribes and lets commercial books in only as one-per-Tribe platform providers. GA HB 686 is a comparable full authorization statute but depends on the constitutional amendments in GA HR450/SR131, a step Minnesota's bill does not require. SF978's college-campus advertising ban parallels CT HB05229, though its NIL ban, 500-foot school buffer and e-wallet funding ban go further. Its express inclusion of 'exchange wagering' contrasts with IL HB5142's move to pull prediction markets into the sports-wagering definition.","watch":"Commercial operators should watch the tethered, one-platform-per-Tribe model capped at 11 partnerships, the unusually broad credit and e-wallet funding ban, the strict advertising rules (10% under-21 audience, public property and campus bans), and the 297J tax rate, which does not appear in the excerpt provided.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":135653,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:39.497Z"},"MO-HB2008":{"summary":"HB2008 is Missouri's FY2027 (July 1, 2026 to June 30, 2027) operating appropriations bill for the Department of Public Safety and the Department of National Guard. It funds the Office of the Director's grant programs, the State Highway Patrol, Capitol Police, Alcohol and Tobacco Control, Fire Safety and the Veterans' Commission. It touches gaming only through several line items drawn from the Gaming Commission Fund (1286) to support Highway Patrol operations.","mechanics":["Gaming Commission Fund (1286) appropriations to the State Highway Patrol: $79,567 for administration (s. 8.135), $550,585 for fringe benefits (s. 8.140), $537,786 for enforcement expense and equipment (s. 8.145), $69,440 for the Law Enforcement Academy (s. 8.165) and $233,721 for technical services (s. 8.180).","Section 8.155 appropriates $1,730,468 from the Gaming Commission Fund toward a $30,865,952 line for vehicles, aircraft and watercraft for the Highway Patrol and the Gaming Commission under s. 43.265 RSMo.","Gaming Commission Fund draws in the portion of the text provided total roughly $3.2 million.","Most line items carry 3% flexibility to Section 8.335, so funds can shift between lines within limits.","Unrelated World Cup items: $59,522,190 in federal funds for the FIFA World Cup Grant Program (s. 8.030) and $12,000,000 in General Revenue for 2026 World Cup security (s. 8.060).","The text supplied is truncated at s. 8.250, so any direct Missouri Gaming Commission operating appropriation appearing later in the bill is not visible here."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What stands out is that gaming money appears only as Gaming Commission Fund line items paying for Highway Patrol personnel, benefits, enforcement, training, vehicles and technology. That reflects Missouri's model, in which Patrol officers perform gaming enforcement funded by gaming revenue. The bill contains no substantive wagering policy, tax rates or licensing provisions.","watch":"Track the total Gaming Commission Fund draws for Highway Patrol enforcement and vehicles, and the Gaming Commission's own appropriation in the untruncated text, since these fee-funded amounts signal regulatory and enforcement capacity now that Missouri sports wagering has launched and can affect licensee fee assessments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":71183,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:40.022Z"},"MO-HB3154":{"summary":"HB3154 adds three new sections to RSMo chapters 311 and 313 authorizing state and federal (FBI) fingerprint-based criminal history background checks through the Missouri State Highway Patrol for alcohol licensees (new 311.661), fantasy sports contest operator license applicants (new 313.912), and retail and mobile sports wagering license applicants (new 313.1000). The checks are discretionary ('may require') for the regulator, but once required they must run through the Highway Patrol's central repository and FBI under section 43.540. Results are made fully available to the Division of Alcohol and Tobacco Control or the Missouri Gaming Commission notwithstanding the closed-record provisions of section 610.120.","mechanics":["Section 311.661: the Division of Alcohol and Tobacco Control may require fingerprints from any 'owner' (an individual holding 10% or greater financial or voting interest) or applicant for six license types: carrier/transport, manufacturer-solicitor/domestic winery/microbrewery, primary retail, solicitor, wholesaler, and wine direct shipper.","Section 313.912: the Gaming Commission may require fingerprint submissions from employees, officers, or directors of a fantasy sports contest operator (as defined by reference to section 313.905) as part of the operator license application.","Section 313.1000: the Gaming Commission may require fingerprints as part of an application for a retail sports wagering license (in person at excursion gambling boats or within a 'sports district') or a mobile sports wagering license (online platform for persons physically in Missouri).","'Sports district' is defined as premises of a facility with capacity of 11,500 or more where one or more professional teams play home games, plus the area within 400 yards; retail locations within the district must be approved by the applicable team.","Procedure in all three sections: fingerprints and fees go to the Highway Patrol central repository, are searched against state records and forwarded to the FBI under section 43.540, and the Patrol reports any record or lack of record to the regulator; section 610.120 closed-record limits are overridden.","No effective date, fee amounts, or disqualifying offenses are specified in the text."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145 and SB194 or GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308, HB2570 and SB1569, which fold criminal record checks into comprehensive bills authorizing and taxing sports wagering, MO HB3154 is a narrow technical bill that layers FBI fingerprint authority onto Missouri's already-authorized sports wagering and fantasy sports regimes without touching tax, advertising, or market structure. It is closer in spirit to CO SB 163 and FL S1580, which adjust licensing administration and personnel eligibility, though HB3154 is purely about background-check mechanics. Its pairing of alcohol and gaming licensing in a single bill is unusual among the listed bills; IL HB5452 touches liquor regulation but not gaming background checks.","watch":"Watch whether the Commission exercises its discretionary fingerprint authority under sections 313.912 and 313.1000 through rulemaking that extends checks broadly to operator employees, officers, and directors (the fantasy section expressly reaches all three, while the sports wagering section says only 'applicants'), adding onboarding delays and costs. The override of section 610.120 also means closed or expunged-type records would be visible to the regulator in suitability reviews.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7991,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:47.296Z"},"NC-H1171":{"summary":"H1171 amends North Carolina's general gambling statute, G.S. 14-292, so that its Class 2 misdemeanor prohibition on operating, playing at, or betting on games of chance expressly reaches online markets and platforms offered to North Carolina residents, including prediction markets. It defines a prediction market broadly as a market or platform where a person can bet on the outcome of a future event. It also appropriates $10,000 to the State Lottery Commission for enforcement.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(a) adds new G.S. 14-292(b): the gambling prohibition applies to any physical location in NC or any online market or platform offered to NC residents, including a prediction market.","Definition: a \"prediction market\" is a market or platform where a person can bet on the outcome of a future event. The definition does not limit it to sports or event contracts, and it has no CFTC or federal carve-out.","Penalty is unchanged: a Class 2 misdemeanor for operators and for bettors. The existing exceptions remain for Chapter 18C (the lottery and the sports wagering regime), Parts 2 and 4 of the Article, and lawful lottery play in any state.","Sec. 1(b): criminal provisions take effect December 1, 2026, for offenses committed on or after that date.","Sec. 2: $10,000 in nonrecurring 2026-27 General Fund money goes to the State Lottery Commission for enforcement, effective July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["prediction","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like MN HF 4437 and SF 4511, H1171 takes a criminal-prohibition approach, but it does so in one sentence by extending the existing general gambling misdemeanor rather than creating a stand-alone offense. The Minnesota bills also add licensing disqualifications. H1171 contrasts sharply with the regulatory ORACLE models in IL HB5059 and NY S09414, which tolerate prediction markets subject to age-21 and conduct rules, and with the CT HB05038 age restrictions and the NJ A5336/S4447 surtax, all of which presuppose that the markets operate legally. Unlike IL HB5142, which folds sports-event prediction markets into the licensed sports wagering definition, H1171 bans all event betting outside Chapter 18C. Its definition is not limited to sports.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the Chapter 18C exception is read to protect licensed NC sports wagering platforms, and whether the open-ended \"bet on the outcome of a future event\" definition and the residents-targeting reach draw a federal preemption challenge under the Commodity Exchange Act from CFTC-registered platforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":1933,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:52.829Z"},"MO-HB3533":{"summary":"HCS HB 3533 raises the per-patron admission fee that Missouri excursion gambling boat (casino) licensees pay, from $2 per person embarking to $5.50 per person for every two hours spent in the gambling area, with annual CPI indexing. It also creates a 1.5% of handle \"remote wagering access fee\" for licensees in place of the admission fee, adds a new 13% tax on adjusted gross receipts on top of the existing tax in section 313.822, and redirects new money mainly to historic preservation, veterans, and developmental disability funds.","mechanics":["Section 313.820.1: the admission fee rises from $2 to $5.50 per person present, charged for every two hours in the gambling area. It is split $0.50 to the DNR historic preservation revolving fund (Capitol bicentennial through FY2030, then a riverboat heritage artifact facility), $2 to the veterans capital improvement trust fund, $1 to DMH developmental disabilities community services, $1 to the gaming commission fund and $1 to the home dock city or county. Up to one cent of the commission-fund share may go to the compulsive gamblers fund.","Section 313.820.1(2) and 313.805(19): the commission must index the fee each year to the Midwest CPI-U, with the new fee taking effect at the start of each fiscal year, initially for the rest of FY2027 and the next ten fiscal years. The fee still applies to complimentary passes.","Section 313.820.2: in \"all present and future forms of remote wagering,\" a boat licensee pays a remote wagering access fee of 1.5% of total monthly amounts wagered, in lieu of the admission fee. The first $35 million goes to the historic preservation fund and the rest to the gaming commission fund under commission rules. \"Remote wagering\" is not defined.","New section 313.823: adds a 13% tax on adjusted gross receipts from gambling games under sections 313.800 to 313.850, on top of the section 313.822 tax. It expressly is not subject to section 313.822(1), the home-dock local-share provision, so the full 13% goes to the state through the Director of Revenue.","Section 313.835.2(2)(d): inserts a fourth tier of $15 million from gaming commission fund net proceeds to the historic preservation fund for artifact collections. However, the residual clause in paragraph (e) still refers only to paragraphs (a) to (c), which is a drafting inconsistency.","Section 313.805(3) keeps the bar on commission rules limiting wager, loss, or buy-in amounts, and the 80% minimum payout stays in subdivision (12)."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. On its own the bill is unusual because it stacks three new costs on Missouri casino licensees at once: a time-based per-patron head tax more than doubled and indexed to inflation, an additional 13% gross gaming revenue tax, and a turnover-based levy of 1.5% of handle on undefined remote wagering. Taxing handle rather than revenue is rare for U.S. gaming, and at typical hold rates 1.5% of handle could equal a large share of gross gaming revenue.","watch":"Watch whether the undefined \"all present and future forms of remote wagering\" fee in section 313.820.2 could be read to reach mobile or online sports wagering offered by excursion boat licensees, because a 1.5% handle levy would stack on Missouri's separately enacted sports wagering tax and raise conflict and preemption questions under that constitutional and statutory framework.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":18393,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:57.544Z"},"MS-SB2369":{"summary":"SB2369 requires Mississippi gaming licensees to check winners against a daily-updated Department of Human Services (MDHS) database. Where the winner owes child support arrearages, the licensee must deduct the arrearage from W-2G-reportable cash winnings, including sports betting payouts, and from progressive slot annuities before paying out. The Gaming Commission and MDHS must jointly adopt implementing rules, and licensees get civil and criminal immunity for good-faith withholding and disclosure plus the right to charge a capped administrative fee.","mechanics":["Scope (new §43-19-63(1)): covers only 'cash gaming winnings' and 'progressive slot machine annuities' for which the licensee must file IRS Form W-2G or an equivalent. §43-19-31(q) expressly names sports betting among the covered winnings, and 'gaming licensee' means any entity licensed or permitted to operate gaming under current law.","Withholding duty (§43-19-63(4)): the licensee deducts the full arrearage from the payout, remits it to MDHS within 7 days, and pays the winner any remainder, or nothing if the arrearage equals or exceeds the win. MDHS releases the funds to the obligee after 30 days unless the obligor requests a hearing, which is limited to mistaken identity or mistaken amount (§43-19-63(5)-(6)).","Licensee protections and fees: licensees and their officers, employees, attorneys, accountants and agents are immune from civil and criminal liability for disclosure, for encumbering assets, and for withholding or failing to withhold based on MDHS information (§43-19-63(3)). Licensees may deduct an administrative fee of up to $35 per payment, starting with the second annuity payment for annuities (§43-19-63(7)).","Operational rules: once a winner clears the database, the licensee need not re-check that winner for 24 hours (§43-19-63(8)). The Commission must require licensees to adopt procedures that stop employees from willfully failing to withhold (§43-19-63(9)).","Timing: the act takes effect July 1, 2026, and the Commission and MDHS must have all policies, procedures and processes in place by January 1, 2027 (§43-19-63(10))."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses intercepting gaming winnings for child support, so there is no direct counterpart. The closest is CT HB05229, which regulates withdrawals from gaming accounts, but as a consumer-protection measure rather than a debt-collection mechanism. The authorization and tax bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308) and the Illinois licensing and tax bills (e.g. IL HB2878, HB3159) do not impose payout-intercept duties. What is unusual here is that Mississippi expressly folds sports betting payouts into a W-2G-triggered intercept regime, with a daily database check, a 7-day remittance deadline, statutory immunity, and a licensee-retained fee of up to $35.","watch":"Watch the joint Commission/MDHS rulemaking due by January 1, 2027, particularly how the W-2G trigger is applied to sports wagers, the database-check and employee-control procedures, and any disciplinary exposure for failures to withhold, which the statutory immunity bars only as to civil and criminal claims by customers and other persons.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":16700,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:59.054Z"},"NC-H384":{"summary":"NC H384 (second edition, committee substitute of 4/8/25) creates a state income tax deduction for federal basic pay received by enlisted Army or Air National Guard members ranked E-1 through E-5. It also rewrites the distribution of remaining sports wagering tax proceeds under G.S. 105-113.128(5). The share going to the Major Events, Games, and Attractions Fund drops from 30% to 29%, and the General Fund's fixed 50% becomes a residual of all proceeds not otherwise credited, which in practice is 51%. The bill does not change the sports wagering tax rate, licensing, or operator obligations.","mechanics":["Section 1 adds G.S. 105-153.5(b)(17): a deduction for the total federal basic pay under Title 37 U.S.C. received by enlisted Army or Air National Guard members holding rank E-1 to E-5. It is effective for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2025.","Section 2 amends G.S. 105-113.128(5)b: the Major Events, Games, and Attractions Fund (G.S. 143B-437.112) share of remaining sports wagering proceeds is cut from 30% to 29%.","Section 2 amends G.S. 105-113.128(5)c: the General Fund receives 'proceeds not otherwise credited under this section' instead of a fixed 50%, an effective shift of 1 percentage point to the General Fund.","The 20% share for athletic departments at 13 listed UNC system institutions in sub-subdivision a is unchanged.","Section 2 is effective July 1, 2025 and applies to net proceeds credited on or after that date. The remainder of the act is effective when it becomes law.","Status: introduced; the committee substitute was reported favorably and re-referred to the House Finance Committee on 2025-04-08."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Several listed bills earmark sports wagering revenue for new purposes by raising operator taxes. IL HB3159 imposes an additional 2% of adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS, and KS SB303 adds a 2% excise on amounts wagered for property tax relief. NC H384 instead reallocates existing net proceeds without touching the rate or the base. That makes it closest to NE LB421/LB438, which change how sports wagering taxes are distributed, though those bills also expand online wagering. The pairing of an unrelated income tax break with a sports wagering revenue shift is unusual, and appears designed to partially offset the deduction's cost through the General Fund.","watch":"The bill imposes no new operator burden as drafted, but because it now sits in House Finance and treats sports wagering proceeds as a General Fund offset, operators should watch for amendments raising the North Carolina sports wagering tax rate or narrowing deductions to fund this or similar tax cuts.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2452,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:00.315Z"},"NC-H828":{"summary":"H828 amends North Carolina's sports wagering article (G.S. Chapter 18C, Article 9) to define \"proposition wager\" and add it to the list of wagers the Article does not authorize when placed on amateur or college sports. It also bars sports facilities from being open to registered players for wagering during the eight hours before, and for the duration of, any college sports event held at or adjacent to the facility.","mechanics":["Section 1(a) adds G.S. 18C-901(13g), defining a proposition wager as a wager on an individual action, statistic, occurrence, or nonoccurrence determined during a sporting event, including those that do not directly affect the final outcome. The definition is broad and reaches both player and game props.","Section 1(b) adds G.S. 18C-902(d)(5), listing \"the placing of a proposition wager on amateur sports or college sports\" among activities the Article does not authorize. It sits alongside the existing exclusions for youth sports, injuries, penalties, disciplinary outcomes and replay reviews.","Section 2 adds G.S. 18C-926(h): notwithstanding any other provision of the section, no sports facility may be open to registered players for placing sports wagers during the eight hours before or during any college sports event at or adjacent to the facility.","No new penalties are created; enforcement would run through the Lottery Commission's existing authority over unauthorized wagers and facility licensees.","Section 3 sets an effective date of July 1, 2025, with no transition period for existing offerings."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"LA SB354 goes further by stripping sports-related proposition bets from permitted wagers entirely and also banning micro-bets, whereas NC H828 limits its ban to college and amateur sports and leaves professional props intact. NY S02616 moves in the opposite direction, expanding authorized season-long props and award futures with limits on in-game props, and NY A 10538 only creates a task force to study prop betting. MD HB518 addresses college athlete protection through problem-gambling regulation rather than market restrictions. H828's eight-hour, event-adjacent shutdown of in-person sportsbooks at college venues has no counterpart among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the breadth of the 18C-901(13g) definition, since \"any occurrence or nonoccurrence determined during a sporting event\" could be read to sweep in college in-game markets beyond player props (such as halftime or quarter lines), and watch the undefined \"adjacent to\" trigger in 18C-926(h), which could force closure of retail sportsbooks near campus venues on college game days.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2117,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:06.793Z"},"NC-H937":{"summary":"H937 amends G.S. 105-113.128, the distribution statute for North Carolina's sports wagering tax, to insert a new priority item (3a) earmarking $11 million a year to the Department of Public Instruction for salary supplements to public school athletic coaches. The money tops up each eligible coach's annual coaching supplement to at least $3,000 in combined State and non-State funds. Any unspent balance at fiscal year end goes to the North Carolina Alliance of YMCAs for youth sports. The bill does not change the tax rate, licensing, or wagering rules; it only reallocates proceeds.","mechanics":["New subdivision (3a) in G.S. 105-113.128 credits $11,000,000 annually from net sports wagering tax proceeds to DPI. It sits in the priority order after the existing items (1) through (3), which are elided in the text.","An eligible athletic coach is a full-time public school unit employee who coaches and receives a non-State coaching supplement of $3,000 or less per school year.","An eligible public school unit must report its prior-year non-State coaching supplement spending to DPI by August 15 each year.","Maintenance of effort applies: a unit must keep non-State supplements at least at the prior-year level, and a unit that supplants funds loses State supplement funds the following school year.","Unexpended funds at fiscal year end go to the North Carolina Alliance of YMCAs, Inc. for youth sports programming.","The act takes effect July 1, 2025, beginning with the 2025-2026 school year; the existing DOR administrative cap of $500,000 and Lottery Commission reimbursement are unchanged."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that this is purely an earmarking bill: it carves a fixed-dollar, education-related line out of the sports wagering tax waterfall without touching the rate, the base, or operator obligations. Tying betting revenue to compensation for public school coaches is a notable policy link, since those coaches oversee student athletes who are a frequent focus of integrity concerns.","watch":"Operators should watch whether adding a fixed $11 million claim to the G.S. 105-113.128 waterfall creates pressure to raise the wagering tax rate or broaden the taxable base so that lower-priority recipients are still funded, especially if handle or gross wagering revenue underperforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":3615,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:08.923Z"},"NC-H554":{"summary":"H554 is an omnibus North Carolina Department of Revenue technical-corrections bill. It adopts a partnership-level audit regime conforming to the federal Bipartisan Budget Act of 2015, updates the IRC conformity date to January 1, 2025, and makes sales, excise, motor fuel and administrative changes. Its only gaming provision, Section 4.10, rewrites the definition of \"gross wagering revenue\" in G.S. 18C-901(7). That definition controls the base on which interactive sports wagering operators are taxed.","mechanics":["Sec. 4.10 defines gross wagering revenue as amounts received from authorized sports wagers less amounts paid as winnings \"before any deductions for expenses, fees, or taxes.\" Bonuses or promotional credits remain included at cash value when returned to the operator as a deposit or wager. The text as reproduced does not show strike/underline, so the precise added language should be confirmed against the official edition.","Sec. 4.11 makes Part IV, including the GGR definition change, effective when the act becomes law. No retroactive application is stated.","Part II (new G.S. 105-154.2) requires a partnership to report a final federal partnership adjustment within 90 days and notify its direct partners. Direct partners must file within six months. Alternatively, the partnership may make an irrevocable election to pay at the entity level within six months, with tiered-partner rules. Part II is effective for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2025.","Sec. 5.1 updates the IRC reference date in G.S. 105-228.90 from January 1, 2023 to January 1, 2025.","Sec. 5.2 raises the frivolous-return penalty from $500 to $2,000.","Sec. 3.1 adjusts remote-seller and marketplace-facilitator nexus timing to the first calendar month at least 60 days after the $100,000 threshold is exceeded.","The title's \"P2P tax parity\" item (extending the alternate highway use tax to peer-to-peer car rentals) is not visible in the truncated text provided."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Several listed bills change rates or add new levies: KS SB303 adds a 2% excise on the amount wagered, IL HB3159 adds a 2% surcharge on adjusted gross receipts, and IL HB5143 sunsets Illinois's per-wager tax. H554 instead leaves NC's rate untouched and adjusts only the tax base definition inside a technical bill. It is closest in spirit to IA SF 605, a narrow administrative tax fix (winnings withholding) rather than a policy overhaul. Unlike AK HB145, GA HB 686, MS HB 4074 or NE LB421, it does not touch authorization, licensing or channel rules.","watch":"Confirm whether the \"before any deductions for expenses, fees, or taxes\" language is new, and whether it forecloses netting federal excise tax, payment or platform fees, or other costs from GGR. Combined with the continued inclusion of promotional credits, this would widen the taxable base from the date of enactment without any rate change.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":76742,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:13.105Z"},"NC-S257":{"summary":"S257 is North Carolina's FY 2026-27 base budget (S.L. 2026-41), appropriating about $34.37 billion in net General Fund spending plus Highway Fund and Highway Trust Fund budgets, and funding several reserves. Its only gaming-related content is a reallocation of Education Lottery Fund and Indian Gaming Education Revenue Fund money. Nothing in the text provided touches sports wagering licensing, tax rates, or operator obligations under Chapter 18C, though the supplied text is truncated partway through Part V.","mechanics":["Sec. 4.2(a) amends S.L. 2025-89, Sec. 2A.1(a), to raise total FY 2026-27 Education Lottery Fund allocations from $1,087,272,612 to $1,143,500,000. School Transportation rises from $186,033,702 to $257,311,891, and the Scholarship Reserve Fund for Public Colleges and Universities drops from $17,748,769 to $2,697,968.","Sec. 4.2(b), effective June 30, 2026, overrides G.S. 18C-164(b3) to send $56,567,388 of FY 2025-26 net lottery revenues, after the (b1) appropriation and (b2) transfer, to school transportation for FY 2026-27. Any remainder goes to the Needs-Based Public School Capital Fund.","Sec. 4.4 amends S.L. 2025-89, Sec. 2A.2(a), to raise FY 2026-27 Indian Gaming Education Revenue Fund allocations from $13.5 million to $19 million. The Classroom Materials Allotment goes from $3.5 million to $9 million, and 'Textbooks and Digital Resources' is renamed the 'Instructional Materials Allotment' (still $10 million).","Sec. 5.5(b)-(c) strips the 'interest-bearing' status from the lottery-funded Needs-Based Public School Capital Fund and repeals G.S. 147-69.2(a)(17m), redirecting investment income to strengthen the General Fund.","Sec. 4.3(b) directs that FY 2026-27 Needs-Based Fund grants prioritize Career and Technical Education Centers serving an entire public school unit."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is a standard appropriations act. Its gaming content is limited to reallocating existing lottery and tribal-gaming revenue streams, notably overriding the statutory lottery revenue waterfall in G.S. 18C-164(b3) for one year. It leaves the sports wagering framework untouched in the text shown.","watch":"Watch whether the legislature's reliance on one-time overrides of G.S. 18C-164 and increased gaming-derived appropriations signals pressure to raise gaming revenue, including sports wagering tax rates, in future budget or revenue bills, and check the truncated remainder of S.L. 2026-41 for any Chapter 18C amendments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":326655,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:17.213Z"},"ND-HCR3002":{"summary":"HCR 3002 proposes amending Article XI, Section 25 of the North Dakota Constitution to permit, but not require, the Legislative Assembly to authorize sports betting on professional and college sports, conducted in the state and licensed and regulated by the state. The amendment would have gone to voters at the 2026 general election, and all sports-betting tax revenue would have been earmarked for K-12 public schools. The resolution failed on second reading on 2025-01-22, 24 yeas to 70 nays.","mechanics":["Restructures Art. XI, § 25 into numbered subsections and keeps the existing multi-state lottery mandate (subsection 1) and charitable gaming authority (subsection 2).","New subsection 3: the Legislative Assembly 'may authorize sports betting on professional and college sports to be conducted in the state and licensed and regulated by the state.'","All tax revenue from sports betting must be appropriated for the benefit of kindergarten through grade twelve public schools.","The text does not address tax rates, licensing structure, mobile versus retail betting, number of operators, or the regulator; all of this is left to later legislation.","Would have been submitted to qualified electors at the 2026 general election under Art. IV, § 16; it failed in the House 24-70, so it will not appear on the ballot."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HR450 and GA SR131, HCR 3002 is a constitutional amendment that only enables the legislature to act later; SR131 also covers casino gambling. By contrast, AK HB145/SB194, HI HB 1308/SB1569 and GA HB 686 are full statutory frameworks with licensing, tax rates and regulators. Unusually, the ND text expressly limits the authorization to professional and college sports and hard-wires a K-12 education earmark for all tax revenue into the constitution, which no listed bill does at the constitutional level.","watch":"The measure is effectively dead after the 24-70 vote, but any revival should be checked for the 'professional and college sports' limitation, which could constitutionally exclude other markets (e.g., esports, awards shows, prediction-style contracts), and for the constitutional revenue earmark, which would constrain future tax and fee design.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2264,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:19.946Z"},"NE-LB1046":{"summary":"LB1046 would have required the Nebraska State Board of Education to adopt, by January 1, 2027, a model policy governing commercial name, image, and likeness (NIL) activity by high school athletes and activity participants, and required member school boards to adopt consistent policies starting in school year 2027-28. Among other restrictions, the model policy had to bar student-participants from NIL deals promoting gambling, lotteries, or sports betting. The bill was indefinitely postponed on April 17, 2026 and is dead.","mechanics":["Section 1(1)(d) covers 'student-participants': high school students in interscholastic athletics or activities at schools belonging to an athletics or activities association.","Section 1(2): the State Board of Education must adopt a model NIL policy by January 1, 2027. The policy must allow NIL activity, including through agents or attorneys, while students keep their eligibility.","Section 1(2)(b)(iv)(C): the policy must prohibit NIL activity that promotes 'gambling, lotteries, or sports betting'. The same list bars alcohol, tobacco, vaping, controlled substances, adult entertainment, weapons, and illegal products.","Section 1(2)(b)(i)-(iii): NIL activity is also barred if it uses school names, logos, uniforms, or facilities, takes place during school hours or school events, or is used to recruit students or induce transfers.","Section 1(2)(c)-(f): students must disclose NIL activity within five business days, with an appeal process, and the school must notify its association. Penalties may not be monetary but may include temporary ineligibility.","Section 1(3)-(4): school boards must adopt conforming policies beginning in school year 2027-28. Students may not be penalized for NIL activity that complies with the policy."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own terms, the bill is an education and NIL measure, and its only gaming touchpoint is a mandatory ban on high school athletes endorsing gambling, lotteries, or sports betting. That ban appears as one item in a standard list of prohibited 'vice' categories rather than in gaming regulation.","watch":"The bill is dead, but operators and affiliates should watch for revived high school NIL bills, which could ban sports-betting endorsements by minors or student-athletes through school-board policy rather than gaming law, broadening restrictions on athlete-based marketing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5186,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:23.179Z"},"NC-S595":{"summary":"S595 (S.L. 2026-31) is North Carolina's omnibus revenue-laws technical bill. It amends personal income tax, withholding, sales tax, motor fuel and other excise provisions, adopts a state regime for federal BBA partnership audit adjustments, and, per its title, also covers vapor directory enforcement, tax foreclosure, credit unions, the IRC reference date, savings banks, penny-rounding and PART board pay. Nothing in the text provided addresses sports wagering, gaming, or gambling taxation.","mechanics":["Section 1.4 moves semiweekly withholding filers (those withholding an average of $2,000+ a month) to quarterly returns due by the last day of the month after the quarter. It replaces federal-linked deposit timing with fixed Wednesday/Friday payment deadlines based on payday.","Section 1.11 replaces the federal section 6654(e) safe harbor for estimated-tax interest with a flat $1,000 threshold. Section 1.2 restricts the NOL statute (G.S. 105-153.5A) to individuals, estates and trusts, retroactive to tax years beginning on or after January 1, 2022.","Part II adds new G.S. 105-154.2, conforming to the federal centralized partnership audit regime. Partnerships must report final federal partnership adjustments within 90 days and direct partners within six months, or an audited partnership may irrevocably elect to pay at the entity level. It applies to adjustments final on or after January 1, 2026, with matching limitation periods in G.S. 105-241.6 and 105-241.8.","Section 3.1 changes the remote-seller and marketplace-facilitator nexus start date: a retailer exceeding the $100,000 threshold becomes engaged in business on the first day of the first month at least 60 days after crossing it. Section 3.5 lets the Secretary reduce the 65% sales tax prepayment for $20,000/month filers where collected tax is held in trust for another person.","Part IV makes excise changes. It adds renewable diesel to the definition of diesel fuel, treats owners of tax-paid fuel entering the terminal transfer system at non-approved terminals as suppliers subject to a $2,000,000 bond, and adds nonresident spirituous liquor vendors to the alcohol excise registration requirement."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a routine North Carolina Department of Revenue technical-corrections package. Its most notable features are the new state partnership audit regime (G.S. 105-154.2) and the shift of large withholding filers off federal deposit schedules. The text supplied is truncated mid-Part IV, and the portion shown contains no wagering provisions.","watch":"Operators should check the truncated remainder, especially the IRC conformity update and any later Part, for changes touching Chapter 18C sports wagering tax administration or for flow-through of federal 2025–26 changes such as the section 165(d) gambling-loss limit. They should also note that the new quarterly withholding schedule in G.S. 105-163.6(d) will apply to any operator withholding $2,000 or more a month.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":202988,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:27.965Z"},"NE-LB280":{"summary":"LB280 would have required every authorized gaming operator licensed under the Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act to obtain an annual independent CPA audit of its Nebraska financial statements and internal controls, and to file it with both the Auditor of Public Accounts and the State Racing and Gaming Commission. It would also have given the Auditor power to review those reports and to examine operators' books on its own initiative, focusing on how gross gaming revenue and the gaming tax are calculated. The bill was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-17 and is dead.","mechanics":["Sec. 7(1): each authorized gaming operator must pay for an annual audit of its financial statements, including a report on internal controls. The audit covers all Nebraska financial transactions for the prior fiscal year and must be done by a CPA authorized to practice in Nebraska.","Sec. 7(2): the audit report is due electronically to the Auditor of Public Accounts and the gaming commission within six months after fiscal year-end, unless the Auditor grants a written extension. The Auditor reviews it for math errors, improper accounting or other deficiencies, especially those affecting gross gaming revenue or the gaming tax, and reports to the Governor, Legislature and commission.","Sec. 7(3): the Auditor may, at its discretion, examine an operator's books, records, funds and accounts relating to the gaming tax under section 9-1203, under GAO Government Auditing Standards. This includes whether the tax is properly calculated, collected and credited to the Property Tax Credit Cash Fund.","Secs. 2 and 7: the Auditor's review and examination costs are paid from the Racing and Gaming Commission's Racetrack Gaming Fund, which section 9-1107 is amended to allow, rather than billed to operators.","Secs. 4-6: existing enforcement provisions are extended to the new section 7. Payment is due by the 15th of each month, late tax carries a 10% penalty plus interest, and willful failure to make a required report or knowingly making a false statement is a Class IV felony (sec. 9-1207).","Sec. 3 keeps the definition of game of chance, which includes sports wagering, so sports wagering revenue at racetrack casinos falls within the audited gross gaming revenue."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a post-licensure financial audit mandate. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569 authorize and tax new sports wagering markets. CO SB 163 reallocates licensing duties between the Colorado commission and division. IA SSB 1157 and IL HB3159/HB5143 change tax rates or how revenue is used. LB280 is unusual because it brings in the state's elected financial auditor, not the gaming regulator, as a second check on tax computation, including the promotional-credit deduction in the gross gaming revenue definition.","watch":"If the concept returns, operators should watch the combination of mandatory internal-control audits, discretionary Auditor examinations of gaming-tax calculations (including promotional credit deductions on sports wagering), and the extension of Class IV felony exposure under section 9-1207 to reporting failures under the new audit section.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":10602,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:33.766Z"},"NE-LB177":{"summary":"LB177 amends Nebraska's Mechanical Amusement Device Tax Act to convert the operator, distributor and cash-device manufacturer licenses issued by the Tax Commissioner from annual to biennial terms. It appears to double the associated fees to match the two-year term, and it narrows the definition of distributor to exclude retail establishments. The bill leaves intact the fingerprint-based FBI background checks, the nine enumerated grounds for denial, and the gambling-winnings debt-setoff duties for cash-device operators and distributors.","mechanics":["Secs. 2–4 (77-3002, 77-3003, 77-3003.03): operator, distributor and cash-device manufacturer licenses become biennial rather than annual.","Sec. 3 (77-3003(3)): the distributor cash-device fee reads as $200 per device, capped at $10,000, per biennial license, replacing $100 per device capped at $5,000 annually; the chaptered text shows both figures, so confirm against the official redline.","Sec. 4 (77-3003.03(1)): the cash-device manufacturer license fee reads as $10,000 per biennial term, replacing $5,000 annually.","Sec. 1 (77-3001(3)): 'distributor' now covers any person 'other than a retail establishment' who places and controls or manages a mechanical amusement device in a retail establishment; 'cash device' still includes devices awarding cash, gift cards, credit or other currency-denominated value.","Retained: a one-time FBI fingerprint background check (liquor licensees exempt for operator and distributor licenses), personal history reports, and nine for-cause denial grounds including gambling, fraud or theft convictions and liquor-license discipline for illegal gambling.","Retained: Commissioner suspension and revocation powers, and the duty of operators and distributors to check the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act collection system before paying cash-device winnings."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the sports-wagering authorization bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), LB177 does not touch sports betting; it is administrative housekeeping for Nebraska's licensed skill-based 'cash device' market. Its closest analogues are Illinois' gray-machine bills. IL HB3733 would broaden the criminal 'gambling device' definition to capture credit-awarding machines, and IL HB3274/HB5048 would shield prize and gift kiosks from Gaming Board discipline. Nebraska instead keeps legal cash devices regulated as taxed amusement devices under the Department of Revenue rather than a gaming commission.","watch":"Watch whether the move to biennial licensing and the doubled fees signal that Nebraska is entrenching revenue-department oversight of skill-based cash devices, which compete with racetrack casino gaming and sports wagering for the same retail customers.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":19736,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:36.032Z"},"NE-LB342":{"summary":"LB342 would have created the Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act, requiring paid-entry fantasy contest operators to be licensed by the Nebraska Department of Revenue, imposing a 10% tax on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts, and setting age, geolocation, integrity and responsible-gaming requirements. It would also have amended the criminal gambling statutes (28-1101, 28-1105, 28-1113) to exempt fantasy contests conducted under the Act. The bill was indefinitely postponed on April 17, 2026 and is dead.","mechanics":["Licensing (Sec. 4, 6): Department of Revenue license required, with a $5,000 nonrefundable application fee, fingerprint background checks for officers, directors and 10%+ owners, and a bar on applicants convicted of any felony or gambling offense. Licenses run four years, with a renewal fee of 1% of the prior four years' adjusted gross receipts.","Initial license fee (Sec. 6): existing operators pay the lesser of 10% of prior-year adjusted gross receipts or $5,000. New entrants pay $5,000 plus a supplemental fee equal to 10% of first-year receipts minus $5,000. Existing operators could keep operating pending licensure (Sec. 4(5)).","Tax (Sec. 9): from July 1, 2026, 10% of adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts, defined as entry fees minus prizes multiplied by the in-state location percentage. The tax is filed monthly on an accrual basis and split 85% to the General Fund and 15% to the Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund.","Definition (Sec. 2(7)): covers online skill contests built on statistical performance of individual athletes, including single-participant contests against an operator-set target score (pick'em style). It excludes free contests and single-sport, season-long peer contests whose administrative fee is 50% or less of entry fees. Sec. 3(4) bars the department from restricting contest types by labelling them sports wagering under 9-1103.","Consumer protection (Sec. 5, 12): minimum age 18, in-state geolocation required, problem-gambling messaging on platforms and all advertising, self-exclusion folded into the department's existing program, and forfeited prizes of prohibited participants donated to problem-gaming programs.","Enforcement and other duties (Sec. 7, 10, 13): civil penalty of up to $1,000 per knowing violation, capped at $10,000 per transaction or occurrence. Licensees must file a CPA audit and an annual compliance report, deferred two years for new licensees. Large operators (adjusted gross receipts of $1 million or more) must report on diverse and small-business procurement."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","legalize","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, the bill is unusual in housing fantasy regulation in the Department of Revenue rather than the Nebraska Racing and Gaming Commission, although Sec. 4(5) mistakenly refers to 'the commission.' It also expressly protects operator-vs-player target-score contests and forbids regulators from reclassifying any contest type as sports wagering. Pairing a 10% tax with a percentage-based license fee and renewal fee is also distinctive.","watch":"Although LB342 is dead, operators should watch for a reintroduced version that keeps the Sec. 2(7)(b) inclusion of single-player target-score (pick'em) contests and the Sec. 3(4) bar on reclassifying contests as sports wagering, since those provisions decide whether prop-style DFS products can be offered in Nebraska outside the sports wagering regime.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":36010,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:39.276Z"},"NE-LB357":{"summary":"LB357 is a General Affairs Committee cleanup of Nebraska's racing and racetrack casino statutes. It creates a separate racetrack enclosure license distinct from the license to conduct race meets, and moves the definition of gross gaming revenue into the Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act's definitions section. That definition includes a deduction for redeemed promotional gaming credits. The bill also lengthens race meet licenses, merges the commission's racing and gaming cash funds into a renamed Racetrack Gaming Fund, and tightens fingerprinting rules for racing licensees and racetrack management.","mechanics":["Sec. 6 creates a racetrack enclosure license. Eligible applicants are the State Fair Board, county fair boards, county agricultural societies, and civic or livestock-exposition nonprofits. The license costs $10,000 to apply, is valid for three years, and costs $10,000 to renew. It is expressly separate from the race meet license under §2-1204.","Sec. 14 (§9-1103) defines gross gaming revenue as dollars collected from all games of chance minus (a) federal non-income taxes on those games and (b) promotional gaming credits, but only to the extent players redeem them. It also adds definitions of 'promotional gaming credit' and 'designated sports wagering area' (a commission-approved area where sports wagering is conducted). The 20% annual gaming tax on gross gaming revenue in §9-1203 continues to apply. §9-1202 is outright repealed.","Sec. 8 (§2-1205) raises the maximum race meet license term from three to five years. The commission must meet at least eight times a year rather than six (§2-1202).","Sec. 5 (§2-1203.02) requires FBI fingerprint checks through the Nebraska State Patrol for licensees aged 16 to 75 and for anyone in racetrack administration or management, including the governing body. Applicants fingerprinted in Nebraska within the past five years are exempt. Non-restricted concession staff, admissions and parking staff, and medical personnel are excluded.","Sec. 16 (§9-1107) renames the fund as the Racetrack Gaming Fund and adds horseracing license fees and gross tax receipts to it, but not §2-1208 taxes. The fund now also covers horseracing administration. The Racing Cash Fund's balance is transferred into it, and the cash fund is eliminated.","Existing operator terms in §9-1106 are carried forward unchanged in the text: the $5 million operator license fee (payable over five years), terms of at least 20 years with a $50,000 annual review fee, fines of up to $50,000 or three times the highest daily gross receipts, and adjudication subcommittee fines of up to $15,000. The provided text is the introduced version and is truncated at Sec. 19."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in two ways. First, it splits the real-property 'racetrack enclosure' license from the race meet license and restricts enclosure licensees to nonprofit or civic entities. Second, it writes into statute a promotional-credit deduction from the 20% gaming tax base that is limited to credits actually redeemed. That is narrower than in states that allow broader free-play deductions.","watch":"Operators should check whether the promotional-credit deduction in the enrolled version is limited to credits redeemed on games the operator itself runs, and how the commission will define 'designated sports wagering area' and implement the new enclosure license, since the operator license is tied to a licensed racetrack enclosure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":60473,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:44.699Z"},"NE-LB370":{"summary":"LB370 would have rewritten Nebraska's Student-Athlete Name, Image, or Likeness Rights Act to let institutions, NIL collectives and third parties pay student-athletes directly, bar collegiate athletic associations from investigating or penalizing activity the Act protects, and regulate athlete-agent agency agreements. Its only gambling touchpoint is an athlete-agent disqualification for certain convictions, including misdemeanors involving gambling or sports wagering. The bill was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-17 and is dead.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 adds definitions of agency agreement (with an agent registered under the Nebraska Uniform Athlete Agents Act), name, image, or likeness collective, parties and third party; Sec. 3 lets student-athletes be paid directly by institutions, athletic program supporters, collectives or third parties, and lets them rescind an agency agreement within 15 calendar days without penalty.","Sec. 8 requires a collective, institution or third party that expects to pay a student-athlete at least $5,000 in a calendar year to first sign a written contract covering payor identity, total compensation, payment timing, obligations, term, revision or termination options, and any non-payment terms.","Sec. 9(2) bars an athlete agent from entering an agency agreement if the agent has a pending civil case (excluding divorce) or a conviction for a felony; a misdemeanor involving physical contact, deception, or gambling or sports wagering; multiple alcohol or drug violations; or multiple misdemeanor sentences. Agents also may not pay inducements to the athlete or a relative, and noncompliant agreements are void under Sec. 9(6).","Sec. 7 bars collegiate athletic associations from accepting complaints, investigating, penalizing, or requiring reporting about NIL activity the Act protects; Sec. 6 bars institutions from complying with conflicting association rules or investigations.","Sec. 10 voids any contract offering an upfront payment in exchange for future NIL rights or professional sports earnings.","Sec. 11 expands civil-action standing to collectives, supporters and third parties, with actual damages, equitable relief and attorney's fees available, and a one-year limitations period for suits against public institutions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"LB370 belongs with the NIL bills listed, including DE HB314, GA HB383, HI HB 2384 and SB3263, LA HB 513, NH HB312, TX HB126 and WI AB1034. It is unusually aggressive in using state law to shield institutions, collectives and third parties from collegiate athletic association enforcement (Sec. 7), and in regulating agency agreements, including the $5,000 written-contract threshold. Unlike MD HB518, which pairs problem-gambling rules with college athlete protections, or NC H828, which would prohibit prop bets on college and amateur sports, LB370 imposes no obligations on wagering operators. Its only gambling hook is the agent disqualification for gambling or sports-wagering misdemeanors.","watch":"The bill is dead, so operators face no direct exposure, but if it is reintroduced they should watch whether the gambling and sports-wagering conviction bar for athlete agents grows into broader athlete-integrity or prop-bet restrictions of the kind pending in NC H828 and MD HB518.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":27991,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:49.925Z"},"NE-LB415":{"summary":"As introduced, LB415 amends the voter-approved Nebraska Healthy Families and Workplaces Act (Initiative Law 2024, No. 436) to narrow who counts as an employee, delay and condition sick-time accrual, and give employers more flexibility in meeting the law. According to its enacted title and last action, the final law also amends the Conveyance Safety Act and, via AM545 importing LB698, lets unemployment-benefit overpayment debt be offset against gambling winnings under the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act. None of that gambling language appears in the introduced text supplied here.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 redefines employee to exclude individual owner-operators, independent contractors, people working in Nebraska fewer than 80 hours a year, and railroad workers covered by the federal Railroad Unemployment Insurance Act. Employers exclude the United States, the State of Nebraska, and its agencies and political subdivisions.","Sec. 2 says accrual begins only after 80 hours of consecutive employment, at one hour per 30 hours worked. Annual earning and use caps are 40 hours for small businesses (fewer than 20 employees) and 56 hours for other employers.","Accrual starts at the later of commencement of employment or October 1, 2025. Paid sick time provided between January 1 and October 1, 2025 counts toward the employer's obligations.","Employers may front-load a year's sick time, pay out unused time at year end instead of carrying it over, or rely on an equivalent existing PTO policy without extra accrual or carryover. Payout at separation is not required.","Sec. 3 deletes the 'in compliance with the Act' qualifier on oral requests and allows documentation after more than three consecutive work days.","The gambling-winnings setoff for unemployment overpayment debt was added by AM545 from LB698. Its rates, thresholds and operator duties are not in the text provided and must be checked against the enrolled version."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that this is a labor bill, a rollback of a 2024 paid-sick-leave ballot initiative, that became a vehicle for a gambling-winnings intercept for unemployment overpayment debt. That intercept piece arrived only by late amendment (AM545, from LB698) and is not in the introduced text.","watch":"Pull the enrolled LB415 text for the LB698/AM545 language, because extending Nebraska's Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act to unemployment overpayment debt likely adds a new debt category that licensed gaming operators must screen for and intercept before paying out qualifying winnings.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":13098,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:54.038Z"},"NE-LB402":{"summary":"LB402 would have added the Nebraska Department of Labor as a claimant under the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act. Operators would then intercept unemployment-benefit overpayment debts from reportable casino, parimutuel, sports wagering and cash device winnings, on top of the child support and state tax debts already covered. It also amended section 48-665 to list the gambling setoff as a recovery method. The bill was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-17 and is dead.","mechanics":["Sections 1–2 add a new purpose (9-1302(3)), a new claimant (9-1303(4)(d)) and a new debt category (9-1303(6)(b)) for overpayments of benefits under section 48-663.01 to which the obligor was not entitled.","Covered sports wagering winnings are unchanged: winnings that must be reported on IRS Form W-2G, have tax withheld by the operator, and come from sports wagers authorized by the State Racing and Gaming Commission.","Under section 3 (9-1304(4)), the Department of Labor may submit certified debts of $25 or more, unless validity is legitimately disputed, on a continuous, fluctuating basis. The collection system shows operators only a total amount owed, without identifying its source.","Operators still deduct the amount shown in the collection system and remit it to the Department of Revenue. Under section 4 (9-1306(3)), Revenue credits remittances to HHS child support debt first, then Department of Labor debt, then state tax liability.","Under sections 5–6, the Department of Labor must notify the obligor within 20 days of a remittance; the obligor has 30 days to request a hearing, with appeals under the Administrative Procedure Act. Section 7 gives the Department of Labor rulemaking authority alongside HHS, Revenue and the Racing and Gaming Commission.","Section 8 adds offset against winnings payments as new clause (d) of 48-665(1). Despite the bill title, the introduced text shows no visible change to any limitations period. No separate effective date is stated; operator obligations follow the Tax Commissioner's implementation date under 9-1312."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Most of the listed bills create or restructure sports wagering, iGaming, fantasy or prediction-market regimes, such as HI HB 1308, HI SB1569, IL HB3080 and IL HB5059. None of them, like LB402, simply expands the categories of state debt operators must intercept from winnings. The closest analogue is MS HB 1581, whose title suggests extending mobile sports betting provisions to child support. LB402 stands out for adding a third claimant to an existing intercept system and setting a statutory priority order among competing state claimants.","watch":"Although dead, the concept could return in a future session; if it does, operators would face a broader intercept obligation on W-2G winnings without seeing the source of the debt, so the operational points to watch are the Tax Commissioner's implementation date and any rules adopted by the Department of Labor or the Racing and Gaming Commission.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21022,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:54.499Z"},"NE-LB438":{"summary":"LB438 would have amended the Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act to let licensed racetrack casino operators (authorized gaming operators) offer statewide online sports wagering to persons located in Nebraska, either directly or through a single contracted platform provider, subject to State Racing and Gaming Commission approval of platform controls. It also would have carved sports wagering tax revenue out of the existing gaming-tax split and redirected it, principally to a new Education Future Fund allocation, with no local share. The bill was indefinitely postponed on April 17, 2026.","mechanics":["Adds definitions in sec. 9-1103: 'online sports wagering platform' (hardware/software/apps offering sports wagering over the Internet to persons located in Nebraska) and 'platform provider' (a sports wagering vendor contracting with an authorized gaming operator).","Amends sec. 9-1110(1) so wagers may be placed in person or at kiosks in the racetrack's designated area, or via an online platform offered by or in partnership with an authorized gaming operator to an individual located in Nebraska.","Tethered model with a one-skin cap: each authorized gaming operator may run its own platform or contract with up to one platform provider. Any vendor without such a contract is barred from conducting sports wagering in Nebraska.","Operators and contracted vendors must submit platform controls for Commission approval and may not launch online wagering until those controls are approved. Online wagering must meet all existing Act and rule requirements, including the existing in-state collegiate and player-prop exclusions and the prohibited-participant rules.","Amends sec. 9-1204(2) so the sports wagering tax under sec. 9-1203 goes entirely to the State Treasurer: 2.5% Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund, 5% Racetrack Gaming Fund, 27.5% Property Tax Credit Cash Fund, 20% General Fund, 45% Education Future Fund. Non-sports gaming revenue keeps the existing 75% state / 25% county-city split.","Does not change the tax rate in sec. 9-1203 or the 21+ age restriction for the physical wagering area. It sets no separate online licensing fee or age-verification and geolocation standards beyond Commission-approved controls."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, LB438 would authorize mobile sports wagering, but it does so by extending an existing racetrack-casino framework rather than creating a standalone licensing regime with new operator and supplier licenses. GA HB 686 and the GA resolutions (HR450, SR131) start from scratch and, in Georgia's case, require constitutional change. LB438 also differs from Illinois bills such as HB2878/HB5034, which adjust license fees and caps for standalone online operators, by tying online access strictly to racetrack licensees with a one-provider limit. Unusually, it leaves the tax rate untouched and only reallocates proceeds, eliminating the host county and city share for sports wagering revenue.","watch":"Watch whether any revived version keeps the tethered 'up to one platform provider' cap per racetrack licensee and the bar on untethered vendors, since that dictates market-access deals, and whether a revival raises the sec. 9-1203 tax rate alongside the reallocation away from local governments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":13209,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:03.681Z"},"NE-LB704":{"summary":"LB704 amends Nebraska's Title IV-D child support review statutes (sections 43-512.12 and 43-512.13) so that the Department of Health and Human Services must automatically start a review of a noncustodial parent's order within fifteen business days of learning the parent will be incarcerated more than 180 days, and must finish it within ninety days. Where the department finds no decrease is warranted, the incarcerated parent gets a hearing, in person or virtual, before a final determination. The only gaming connection is pre-existing language listing lottery, casino, parimutuel, sports wagering and cash device winnings as possible substantial changes in circumstances; the bill does not change that language.","mechanics":["Sec. 1, 43-512.12(3)(a): within 15 business days of learning a noncustodial parent will be incarcerated over 180 calendar days, DHHS must initiate a review rather than just notify of the right to request one.","Notice goes by first-class mail to both parents, who must submit financial information within 30 days; notice to the inmate goes to the facility address and to the facility administrator (as defined in 47-1003).","Sec. 1, 43-512.12(3)(b): DHHS must complete the review and notify the parties within 90 calendar days of learning of the incarceration.","Sec. 2, 43-512.13(2): if no decrease is warranted, DHHS must hold a hearing within 30 days, in person or virtually. Parties may submit evidence 10 business days before the hearing, and the inmate may waive it 5 business days before.","DHHS must issue a final determination within 30 days after the hearing; that determination is not a contested case under the Administrative Procedure Act.","Unchanged 43-512.12(2) keeps 'sports wagering winnings', 'casino winnings' and similar winnings as examples of an ongoing income source that can justify a review within three years."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual for this tracker because its only gaming reference is existing statutory text treating sports wagering and casino winnings as income that can trigger a child support review. The substantive changes concern incarcerated parents only.","watch":"Nothing operational here, but note that Nebraska already treats sports wagering winnings as a basis for child support modification, which could tie into any future child-support intercept or winnings-reporting mandates on operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8876,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:05.661Z"},"NE-LB421":{"summary":"LB421 would have let each Nebraska racetrack casino licensee (authorized gaming operator) offer statewide online sports wagering through one online platform, either directly or with one contracted platform provider, under the existing Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act. It also would have redirected sports wagering tax revenue away from the current split, which includes a 25% county/city share, into a sports-specific distribution that sends 90% to property tax relief. The bill was indefinitely postponed on April 17, 2026 and is dead.","mechanics":["Online wagering is tethered to the tracks (Sec. 3, amending 9-1110(1)(b)). Each authorized gaming operator may run one online sports wagering platform and contract with one platform provider. Anyone without such a contract may not conduct sports wagering in Nebraska. Bettors must be physically located in the state, and servers may sit out of state except where federal law requires in-state servers.","Launch sequencing: the commission must begin accepting platform controls within 30 days of adopting online rules and has 30 days to approve or deny them. Operators or providers that file within the first 30-day window and are approved launch on the same day, no later than 60 days after the commission first accepts controls.","New definitions of 'online sports wagering platform' and 'platform provider' (Sec. 1, 9-1103(12)-(13)). The sports wagering definition appears to add exclusions for wagers on individual athletes under 18 in professional or international events and on individual sporting events excluded by the commission. The strike-and-insert formatting in the reproduced text ('includes, but is not limited to, does not include') is garbled, so the final operative language should be confirmed against the official bill.","Tax distribution (Sec. 4, 9-1204(2)): tax under 9-1203 on gross gaming revenue from sports wagering would go 3% to the Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund, 3.5% to the Racetrack Gaming Fund, 3.5% to the Racing Cash Fund, and 90% to the Property Tax Credit Cash Fund. There is no county or city share for sports wagering. The tax rate itself is unchanged.","The commission is directed to examine other states' online sports wagering rules and, if practicable, adopt a similar framework (new 9-1106(31)). No new license fee for online operation or platform providers is created.","Operative date September 1, 2025, with an emergency clause and a severability clause."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Alaska's HB145 and SB194 and Georgia's HB 686 build standalone mobile sports wagering regimes with their own licensing and tax rates. Hawaii's HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 create new operator and supplier licensing under a designated agency. LB421 instead bolts online wagering onto Nebraska's existing racetrack casino licenses: one skin per track, no new license class or fee for platform providers, and no rate change, only a redistribution of existing tax. It also differs from the Illinois license-fee bills (HB2878 and HB5034, which set a $15 million master license fee) by leaving fees to the commission's general authority, and its simultaneous-launch window is an unusual market-access feature.","watch":"If this approach returns, operators should focus on the one-platform-per-track tether: each national brand would need to win one of a handful of racetrack partnerships before the first 30-day controls window closes. They should also watch whether lost county and city revenue under the new 90% property-tax distribution draws local opposition or prompts a tax-rate change.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":28495,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:08.844Z"},"NE-LB63":{"summary":"LB63 would have amended the definitions section of the Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act (section 9-1103) to narrow what counts as permissible sports wagering. Its clearest substantive change is a new carve-out removing from the definition of sports wagering any wager on the performance or nonperformance of an individual athlete under eighteen who is participating in a professional or international sporting event. The bill was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-17 and is dead.","mechanics":["Amends only section 9-1103, the definitions section, and repeals the original section; no new licensing, tax or enforcement provisions.","Adds new subdivision (14)(c): sports wagering does not include wagers on the performance or nonperformance of any individual athlete under 18 participating in a professional or international sporting event. The existing commission-excluded individual-event carve-out is relettered from (c) to (d).","Restates the existing exclusions for collegiate games involving an in-state team: no individual-athlete wagers in a single game or match, and no in-game wagers.","Restates subdivision (3)(b), which excludes from 'authorized sporting event' in-state collegiate events with an in-state team, minor league and high-school-level events, parimutuel wagers, fantasy contests, and any event excluded by the commission.","Restates the 'prohibited participant' definition in (12), covering proxies, athletes, coaches, referees, people with influence or exclusive information, and people on lists supplied by sports governing bodies.","The introduced text does not show strike-through formatting, so beyond the under-18 carve-out and the relettering, the exact amendments to existing language cannot be confirmed from this copy."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own terms, LB63 is unusual in targeting minors who compete in professional and international events, such as teenage tennis players, gymnasts or Olympic athletes. It does this through the statutory definition of sports wagering rather than leaving it to commission rulemaking, so the restriction would bind regardless of commission action.","watch":"The bill is dead, but operators should watch for the under-18 player-prop carve-out to return in a later session or through State Racing and Gaming Commission rulemaking, since it would require age-verification of athletes across professional and international markets (notably tennis, Olympic sports and esports) and the pulling of affected props.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5927,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:12.027Z"},"NE-LR20CA":{"summary":"LR20CA would have placed on the November 2026 general election ballot an amendment to Nebraska Constitution Article III, section 24(5) letting authorized gaming operators that conduct sports wagering within a licensed racetrack enclosure accept sports wagers through a mobile or electronic platform, whether offered directly or in partnership with another party, from anyone physically located in Nebraska. The current constitutional authorization limits games of chance to licensed racetrack enclosures. The resolution was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-17 and is dead.","mechanics":["Amends Art. III, sec. 24(5), which currently authorizes laws licensing and taxing games of chance conducted by authorized gaming operators within a licensed racetrack enclosure.","Adds a sentence allowing racetrack-casino operators conducting sports wagering to accept wagers via a mobile or electronic platform 'offered by or in partnership with' the operator.","Sole geographic condition: the bettor must be located inside Nebraska when the wager is placed, which implies a geolocation requirement. The text sets no age limit, tax rate, or license count.","Mobile access is tethered to the existing racetrack licensees. The text does not authorize standalone online sportsbook licenses.","Sec. 1 would have submitted the measure to voters at the November 2026 general election. Sec. 2 sets the ballot language, with For/Against options.","The rest of sec. 24, covering the lottery allocation (including the Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund transfers), parimutuel racing and bingo, is restated without substantive change."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HR450 and GA SR131, LR20CA is a constitutional amendment rather than a statute. Unlike them, it does not authorize sports betting from scratch. It only extends Nebraska's existing in-person racetrack sports wagering to mobile. AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB 1308/SB1569 would create full mobile frameworks with taxes and licensing. LR20CA instead ties mobile betting to existing racetrack licensees and leaves tax, licensing and consumer-protection details to later legislation, closer to a tethered model than Illinois's standalone online licenses (IL HB2878, HB5034).","watch":"Because the measure is dead for the 2026 ballot, watch for reintroduction, and note that the 'offered by or in partnership with' language would force national online operators to partner with Nebraska racetrack licensees (a tethered skin model) rather than obtain independent licenses.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5586,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:15.830Z"},"NH-HB1194":{"summary":"HB1194, now Chapter 322 of 2026, is mainly an insurance tax bill. It stretches the premium tax credit for Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Association assessments from 20 percent a year over 5 years to 10 percent a year over 10 years, for assessments made after the effective date. Section 2 rewrites the sports wagering carve-out in the criminal gambling statute, RSA 647:2, V(f), so that it exempts only the conduct of a sports book, or participation in sports wagering, that is authorized by RSA 287-I.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 amends RSA 647:2, V(f). The old exemption covered \"sports wagering as defined by RSA 287-I:1, XV\". The new exemption covers \"the conduct of a sports book, or participation in sports wagering, authorized by RSA 287-I.\"","The amended exemption names both sides of the bet: the operator (conduct of a sports book) and the bettor (participation in sports wagering).","Sec. 2 took effect on passage, 7/15/2026, under Sec. 3, II.","Sec. 1 adds RSA 408-F:13, I-a. For assessments made after its effective date, including additional assessments on insurers already declared impaired or insolvent, a member insurer may offset 10 percent of the assessment against its RSA 400-A premium tax in each of the 10 following calendar years.","Under new I-b, an insurer that ceases doing business may credit all uncredited assessments in that year. Sec. 1 takes effect 9/13/2026, 60 days after passage.","The LBA fiscal note calls Sec. 2 a \"housekeeping measure\" with no fiscal impact."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. What is unusual is that a one-line change to the criminal gambling carve-out rides on an insurance guaranty-fund tax credit bill. Rather than resting the exemption on the statutory definition of sports wagering, it expressly conditions it on authorization under RSA 287-I.","watch":"Because the criminal exemption now turns on wagering being \"authorized by RSA 287-I\" rather than merely meeting the statutory definition, operators and bettors outside the state's licensed sports-book framework (for example, unlicensed or event-contract offerings) may have less room to argue they fall outside the gambling offenses, so clients should confirm their New Hampshire activity fits squarely within 287-I authorization.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":6355,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:23.278Z"},"NJ-A3258":{"summary":"A3258 amends New Jersey's sports wagering law (P.L.2018, c.33) to bar sports wagering licensees from offering or accepting any \"micro bet,\" defined as a live, in-play proposition bet on the outcome of the next play or action in an event. Violations are disorderly persons offenses, fined per wager. The text provided is the introduced version, which bans micro bets in all channels; the current bill title, after the June 1, 2026 committee amendments, refers to micro bets \"online,\" so the amended version likely narrows the ban to online sports pools, but that amended text is not before us.","mechanics":["Section 2 adds two definitions to C.5:12A-10. A \"micro bet\" is a proposition bet wagered live while an event is ongoing that concerns the outcome of the next play or action. A \"proposition bet\" is a side wager on part of an event that does not concern its final outcome.","Section 3 adds new subsection n.(1) to C.5:12A-11: a sports wagering licensee shall not offer or accept any wager on a micro bet.","Under subsection n.(2), any licensee or person who violates the ban commits a disorderly persons offense, fined $500 to $1,000 per offense, and each wager offered or accepted is a separate offense.","The existing \"sports pool\" definition still lists in-game wagering, in-play bets and proposition bets, so the carve-out turns entirely on the \"next play or action\" language.","Section 4 makes the act effective immediately, with no transition period or rulemaking directive to the Division of Gaming Enforcement.","Status: reported out of Assembly committee with amendments on June 1, 2026 (title now says \"online\") and referred to Assembly Appropriations."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills bans a specific bet type the way A3258 does. The closest analogue is CT HB05229, which bars using artificial intelligence to target certain bets at customers; it regulates how bets are marketed rather than prohibiting the bet itself. IL HB5142 and IL HB5059 (the ORACLE Act) concern bringing prediction markets within sports wagering regulation. The AK, GA and HI bills are about authorizing and taxing sports betting, and the IL HB2878/HB5034 bills about license fees. A3258 is also unusual in enforcing a product restriction through per-wager criminal (disorderly persons) liability rather than administrative discipline by the Division.","watch":"Watch how the committee amendments define the scope (online only versus retail and kiosks), and whether \"next play or action\" is tightened. As drafted, it could sweep in common in-play markets such as next pitch, next drive result, next point or next goal scorer, and the per-wager fines could add up to very large exposure across an operator's high-volume live-betting products, with no phase-in.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":30734,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:26.388Z"},"NH-HB312":{"summary":"HB312, the \"New Hampshire Fair Play Act,\" would create RSA 356-D, barring accredited four-year colleges from enforcing rules that stop student-athletes from earning name, image, and likeness (NIL) compensation or hiring agents and lawyers. It also flatly prohibits student-athletes from taking NIL compensation from any person or entity related to or associated with gambling, including casinos, sports betting, and the lottery. A violation costs the athlete their institutional financial aid eligibility. The bill was referred for interim study on January 7, 2026, which in New Hampshire usually means it is effectively shelved for the session.","mechanics":["356-D:1 covers accredited four-year institutions that support intercollegiate athletics, and 356-D:4 applies the chapter to all full-time students eligible for intercollegiate competition.","356-D:2 I bars institutions from blocking NIL earnings or professional representation and protects scholarships from revocation over NIL income. Institutions may not themselves pay prospective student-athletes for NIL.","356-D:2 IV(a) prohibits NIL compensation from anyone related to or associated with the development, production, distribution, wholesaling, or retailing of casinos and gambling, expressly including sports betting, the lottery, and betting on video, online, and mobile games. The same ban covers adult entertainment, alcohol, tobacco and vapes, prescription drugs, controlled substances, and weapons.","356-D:2 IV(b) makes revocation of institutional financial aid eligibility the penalty for taking NIL compensation from a prohibited category. 356-D:3 II(b) bars athletes from signing NIL contracts that conflict with the category ban.","356-D:3 requires athletes to disclose NIL contracts to a designated institutional official. An institution asserting a category conflict must disclose the conflicting provisions to the athlete and the athlete's representative.","Agents must comply with the federal Sports Agent Responsibility and Trust Act (15 U.S.C. 7801 et seq.). The stated effective date of September 1, 2025 has already passed and would need amending if the bill is revived."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike Connecticut HB05229, which restricts gaming advertising on college campuses by regulating licensees, NH HB312 places the burden and penalty on the student-athlete. Under HB312 the athlete loses aid eligibility for any NIL tie to a gambling-associated entity, and no licensee obligation is imposed. Maryland HB518 pairs college athlete protections with problem-gambling regulation by the gaming commission, whereas HB312 sits entirely outside gaming law in a consumer-protection chapter. As described, the NIL bills in Hawaii (HB 2384, SB3263), Louisiana (HB 513), Wisconsin (AB1034), Texas (HB126), and Georgia (HB383) focus on affirming NIL rights and institutional policy. None of those descriptions indicates an express statutory gambling-sponsor ban with a financial-aid forfeiture penalty like HB312's.","watch":"If the bill is revived from interim study, watch the breadth of \"related to or associated with\" gambling in 356-D:2 IV(a), which could sweep in operator affiliates, media partners, and lottery vendors and expose any operator NIL or influencer program involving New Hampshire college athletes to athlete aid forfeiture and reputational risk.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5873,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:26.408Z"},"NJ-A4002":{"summary":"A4002 supplements New Jersey's sports wagering law (P.L.2018, c.33) to require sports wagering licensees and their contracted operators to adopt Division of Gaming Enforcement-approved rules governing limitations placed on wagering accounts, and to publish those rules. Operators must give patrons written notice and an explanation whenever an account is limited, periodically review limited accounts under DGE standards, and report annually to DGE on the frequency and kind of limitations imposed.","mechanics":["Section 1a: licensees and contracted operators must adopt rules on account limitations, which may include play-time limits, maximum wager amounts, and deposit and withdrawal restrictions. The rules must be submitted to DGE for approval.","Section 1a: approved rules must be conspicuously displayed in the sports wagering lounge, posted on the operator's website, included in account terms and conditions, and made readily available to patrons.","Section 1b: any limitation triggers written notice to the patron with an explanation. Limited accounts must be reviewed at periodic intervals under DGE-set standards to decide whether the limits stay or are removed.","Section 1c: annual reporting to DGE on the frequency and kind of limitations imposed, and DGE may request additional information to assess compliance.","Section 1d: DGE has rulemaking authority under the Administrative Procedure Act. Section 2 makes the act effective immediately.","The bill sets no specific penalties, so enforcement rests on DGE's existing authority."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills address authorization, taxation, or licensing, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308, and IL HB2878. A4002 instead targets the industry practice of limiting, or 'stake-limiting,' sharp or winning bettors. The closest analogue is CT HB05229, which regulates withdrawals from gaming accounts and customer service obligations, but CT does not mandate published limitation policies, per-patron written explanations, or periodic review of limits. Unusually, A4002 applies to contracted operators directly and creates a recurring transparency reporting duty on account limitations, which no other listed bill does.","watch":"Watch how DGE defines the periodic-review standards and the required content of the written 'explanation,' since disclosing risk-management or sharp-bettor criteria could expose proprietary trading practices and invite patron disputes over limits.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3651,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:27.213Z"},"NJ-A4003":{"summary":"A4003 bars New Jersey sports wagering licensees and their contracted operators from offering promotional credits, incentives, bonuses, complimentaries, or similar inducements to wager to any person currently using a responsible gaming program, including self-exclusion, time or wager limits, and deposit restrictions. It applies \"notwithstanding any law, rule, or regulation to the contrary,\" sets a minimum fine of $500 per offense, and directs the Division of Gaming Enforcement, in consultation with the Casino Control Commission, to adopt implementing rules.","mechanics":["Section 1a: covers sports wagering licensees and contracted operators; prohibits offering promotional credits, incentives, bonuses, complimentaries, or similar benefits designed to induce wagering.","Protected class: any person currently utilizing a responsible gaming program, whether operator-offered or required by the Casino Control Act or DGE/CCC regulation. Listed examples are self-exclusion, time or wager limits, and deposit restrictions, and the list is expressly non-exhaustive.","Section 1b: fine of not less than $500 per offense, with no stated maximum and no definition of what counts as an offense (for example, per communication or per patron).","Section 1c: the Division, in consultation with the Commission, must promulgate rules under the Administrative Procedure Act.","Section 2: effective immediately on enactment, with no transition period. The bill supplements P.L.2018, c.33, the sports wagering act."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, the closest analogue is CT HB05229. That bill addresses responsible gaming and targeting through limits on campus advertising, withdrawal rules, customer service lines, and a bar on AI-targeted bets, while NJ A4003 is a single-purpose ban on promotional inducements to patrons using responsible gaming tools. Most of the other listed bills, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, are authorization, licensing, and tax frameworks rather than promotional-conduct rules, and the IL HB1451/HB2580/HB5368 bills focus on public education about gambling disorders. What is unusual in A4003 is how broadly it defines the protected class: it reaches any patron using any limit tool, including a voluntary deposit or time limit, not just self-excluded persons.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the final text or DGE rules narrow \"utilizing responsible gaming programs\" and define \"per offense,\" because as drafted, any patron who sets a routine deposit limit would have to be suppressed from all bonus and CRM marketing immediately on enactment, with uncapped per-offense fines.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2594,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:35.358Z"},"NJ-AR150":{"summary":"AR150 is a non-binding single-house Assembly resolution urging New Jersey sports wagering licensees, casino licensees, their contracted operators and Internet gaming affiliates to ensure their VIP programs do not target patrons known to be gambling addicts, or who have shown signs of problem gambling, with personalized marketing, including content featuring professional athletes or celebrities. It also urges MLB, the MLBPA and all professional leagues and players associations to refuse participation in such targeted VIP marketing. It creates no legal obligation, penalty or rulemaking mandate.","mechanics":["Section 1: urges (does not require) sports wagering licensees, casino licensees, contracted operators and Internet gaming affiliates to keep VIP programs from targeting known gambling addicts or patrons exhibiting signs of problem gambling with personalized marketing intended to induce continued gambling.","Section 1 specifically flags marketing that uses professional athletes or celebrities, and urges MLB, the MLBPA and all leagues and players associations to reject participation when directed at vulnerable patrons.","Section 2: the Clerk of the General Assembly must transmit copies to all NJ sports wagering and casino licensees, contracted operators and Internet gaming affiliates, the MLB Commissioner, the MLBPA Executive Director and NJ's congressional delegation.","Whereas clauses name FanDuel and describe a 2024 personalized athlete video sent via a VIP manager to a customer reported to have lost over $1.5 million.","No definitions of 'signs of problem gambling', no enforcement mechanism, no DGE directive and no effective date beyond adoption; status shows filed with the Secretary of State on 2026-09-24."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which would impose binding restrictions such as limits on campus gaming advertising and a bar on using artificial intelligence to target certain bets to customers, AR150 is purely hortatory and carries no statutory force. It also differs from MD HB518 and MD HB 195, which direct the Maryland commission to adopt problem-gambling regulations or study gambling-prevention technology, and from study requests like LA SR 178, because AR150 targets VIP marketing practices and athlete endorsements specifically and names an operator. Its appeal to professional leagues and players associations is unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Although non-binding, the resolution signals legislative and possibly Division of Gaming Enforcement appetite for binding rules on VIP host programs, personalized athlete or celebrity content, and duties to identify problem-gambling markers, so operators should audit VIP targeting and responsible-gaming flag integration now and track any follow-on bill or regulation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8614,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:41.221Z"},"NJ-A5327":{"summary":"A5327 is New Jersey's FY2027 general appropriations act (P.L.2026, c.27), signed with line-item vetoes on June 30, 2026, appropriating about $60.7 billion in State funds and $30.5 billion in federal funds. It does not change gaming law, tax rates, or licensing. Its relevance to gaming is limited to the anticipated-revenue schedules, which book projected receipts from sports betting, internet gaming, casino taxes, casino license fees, and fantasy sports fees.","mechanics":["The Casino Revenue Fund is projected at $1,004,359,000. Internet Gaming accounts for $670,249,000, Gross Revenue Tax for $181,465,000, Sports Betting for $126,986,000, and Other Casino Taxes and Fees for $13,641,000. Investment Earnings ($11,942,000) and the Casino Simulcasting Fund ($76,000) make up the rest.","A separate Treasury 'Sports Betting' line of $102,886,000 flows to the General Fund. Combined anticipated sports-betting receipts across both funds are therefore about $229.9 million.","Casino Control Fund license fees are anticipated at $81,325,000. These fees fund the regulatory apparatus.","The Department of Law and Public Safety's revenue lines include a Fantasy Sports Operations Fee of $3,231,000, Casino Fines of $1,103,000, and Legalized Games of Chance Control of $469,000.","Section 1 makes appropriations available for FY ending June 30, 2027, plus a one-month tail, after which unexpended balances lapse. The excerpt is truncated before the Law and Public Safety and Treasury spending sections, so any Division of Gaming Enforcement or Casino Revenue Fund language provisions are not visible here."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual only in that it shows how heavily New Jersey's casino-dedicated revenue relies on iGaming: projected internet gaming receipts ($670.2M) are more than three and a half times the land-based gross revenue tax ($181.5M). Sports betting revenue is split between the Casino Revenue Fund and the General Fund rather than fully dedicated.","watch":"Watch whether the sports-betting and iGaming revenue projections exceed what current rates would yield, which would signal pressure for companion tax or fee legislation, and review the untruncated Division of Gaming Enforcement and Casino Revenue Fund language provisions for any budget-language changes to fee or receipt handling.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":978584,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:41.721Z"},"NJ-S2027":{"summary":"S2027 is New Jersey's fiscal year 2027 general appropriations act. It appropriates $60.74 billion in State funds and $30.50 billion in federal funds, and lists anticipated resources that include casino, internet gaming, sports betting and fantasy sports revenue. It was substituted by A5327 on June 30, 2026, and in the text provided it does not change gaming tax rates, licensing rules or wagering law.","mechanics":["Casino Revenue Fund anticipated resources total $1,004,359,000. That figure includes Internet Gaming at $670,249,000, Gross Revenue Tax at $181,465,000, Sports Betting at $126,986,000, Other Casino Taxes and Fees at $13,641,000, Investment Earnings at $11,942,000 and the Casino Simulcasting Fund at $76,000.","A separate Sports Betting line of $102,886,000 is booked to the General Fund under Department of the Treasury miscellaneous revenues. This is in addition to the Casino Revenue Fund sports betting figure.","Casino Control Fund license fees are anticipated at $81,325,000.","Three gaming items appear under Department of Law and Public Safety revenues: Fantasy Sports Operations Fee at $3,231,000, Casino Fines at $1,103,000 and Legalized Games of Chance Control at $469,000.","Section 1 makes appropriations available for FY ending June 30, 2027, plus one month. Unexpended balances lapse unless encumbered or pre-encumbered as of June 30, 2027.","The text supplied is truncated after the Department of Agriculture. Any Division of Gaming Enforcement, Casino Control Commission or Casino Revenue Fund spending language is not visible."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What stands out is how much New Jersey's gaming revenue now leans on iGaming. Projected internet gaming receipts of $670.2 million are roughly 3.7 times the land-based casino gross revenue tax of $181.5 million. Sports betting revenue is also split between the Casino Revenue Fund and the General Fund, for a combined total of about $229.9 million.","watch":"Operators' counsel should check the full A5327 text and any companion revenue legislation for language provisions (for example, redirecting Casino Revenue Fund or sports betting receipts, or new DGE funding conditions), since the revenue projections may assume rate or fee changes enacted elsewhere.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":978331,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:49.072Z"},"NJ-S2356":{"summary":"S2356 directs the Division of Gaming Enforcement to commission a study, due within 12 months of enactment and published online, on which responsible-gambling words, phrases, placements and fonts work best in casino and sports pool advertising. The DGE must then adopt between three and ten key phrases by regulation, at least one of which must appear in ads alongside the existing 1-800-GAMBLER message. It also amends N.J.S.A. 5:12-70 to require regulations keeping gaming ads and promotions, to the extent practicable, away from locations such as schools and college campuses and off media predominantly targeting underage or self-excluded persons.","mechanics":["Section 1: DGE must arrange a study evaluating six factors, including resonance of phrases, effect on responsible betting, placement, font size and emphasis (bold, underline), effectiveness for problem gamblers, self-excluded and underage persons, and message fatigue from repetition. It must be completed within 12 months and published on the DGE website.","Section 1(d): after the study, DGE develops 3 to 10 key words or phrases, or takes other action informed by the study. It may revise them periodically and must adopt APA regulations requiring at least one phrase in casino and sports pool ads at a frequency that optimizes effectiveness.","Section 2 amends C.5:12-70(a)(16)(a). It deletes the mandatory 'Bet with your head, not over it' on-site casino signage language, keeps 1-800-GAMBLER, and adds a DGE-selected phrase to all print, billboard and sign ads, with clear communication in radio, TV, Internet and other media. Coverage expressly extends to contracted operators, including online gaming and online sports wagering operators.","Section 2 also adds new C.5:12-70(a)(16)(b): regulations must ensure ads and promotions are kept, to the extent practicable, out of or away from schools, college campuses, and media predominantly targeting underage or self-excluded persons.","Section 3 amends C.5:12A-13(a)(11) to require the DGE phrase, in addition to 1-800-GAMBLER, in all print, billboard, sign, online and broadcast sports pool and online sports pool ads and in every sports wagering lounge.","Effective immediately (Section 4). Status: introduced, referred to Senate Budget and Appropriations on 2026-05-21."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"CT HB05229 also restricts gaming advertising at college and university campuses, but it pairs that with withdrawal, customer-service and AI-targeting rules; NJ S2356 is narrower and defers most of its substance to DGE rulemaking after a study. MI SB0714 and NY A 382 regulate operator advertising directly, and NY S 10092 targets platforms that advertise gambling to minors, whereas S2356's distinctive feature is an evidence-based, rotating mandatory-message regime. PA HR60 and VA SB819 are study measures too, but S2356 converts its study directly into binding content requirements on every ad.","watch":"Watch the DGE rulemaking under Section 1(d) and new C.5:12-70(a)(16)(b). The 'frequency that optimizes effectiveness' standard, the open-ended 'other action' authority, the undefined 'in or near' schools and campuses buffer, and the 'predominantly target' media test could impose costly creative-rotation and media-buy restrictions on online operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":18093,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:57.464Z"},"NJ-A5336":{"summary":"Despite its surtax title, A5336 as introduced is a comprehensive prediction-market regulation bill. It bans death, catastrophic-event and political markets, and imposes settlement-source disclosure and anti-manipulation duties on all platforms. It lets sports and horse-racing event contracts (\"athletic event markets\") operate only through a New Jersey sports wagering licensee or a newly licensed athletic event market operator partnered with one, taxed at the 19.75% online sports rate plus a 10% surcharge, and adds a 10% surcharge on all other prediction-market revenue from New Jersey residents. The bill was reported as a committee substitute on 2026-06-28, and the text analysed here is the introduced version, so the substitute may differ.","mechanics":["Tax (secs. 8 and 14): a 10% surcharge applies to sums an operator receives from non-athletic speculative positions opened by New Jersey residents, paid quarterly to the Division of Taxation under the State Uniform Tax Procedure Law. Athletic event market receipts bear the 19.75% tax under C.5:12A-16 plus a further 10% surcharge, administered by the Division of Gaming Enforcement (DGE). \"Sums received\" is not defined.","Licensing (secs. 9-11): athletic event markets may be offered only by a sports wagering licensee, or by an athletic event market operator licensed by DGE that has a DGE-approved agreement with such a licensee. The initial operator license costs $5 million with annual renewal fees set by DGE, and qualifiers must meet casino key employee standards and section 86 disqualification criteria. Each licensee is capped at three branded sites or apps, and servers must sit at the racetrack or in Atlantic City.","Prohibitions and enforcement (secs. 3-7): death, catastrophic-event and political markets are banned. All platforms must publish settlement sources, may not settle on proprietary information, and must report suspected manipulation or insider trading to the Attorney General. The Attorney General may seek an injunction for a \"persistent course of conduct\" in violation, and continued operation after an order carries a $1 million per day civil penalty. Section 3 disclaims any conflict with the Commodity Exchange Act.","Consumer protection (secs. 12-13): DGE must write rules mirroring online sports pool rules, covering age verification, position limits, promotional credits, deposit and time limits, self-exclusion and 1-800-GAMBLER messaging in advertisements. Excluded participants include anyone under 21, self-excluded persons, operator and settlement-source personnel, and anyone with insider information. Offering athletic event markets without DGE approval is a fourth-degree crime, with fines up to $25,000 for individuals and $100,000 for entities.","Integrity and ethics (secs. 17-20): State and local officials and their immediate families may not trade political markets or markets tied to their official duties, a fourth-degree crime for the official and a disorderly persons offense for family members. Candidates and campaign staff may not trade political markets during a campaign. A five-year revolving-door bar applies to work for athletic event market licensees or applicants.","Effective date: the act takes effect 30 days after enactment, with anticipatory administrative action authorized. DGE may adopt emergency regulations effective for 180 days under sec. 15."],"issue_tags":["prediction","tax","licensing","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the outright bans in MN HF 4437/SF 4511, NC H1171 and VT H0913, NJ A5336 takes a permit-and-tax approach. It channels sports event contracts into the existing sports wagering licensing system and adds revenue surcharges no other listed bill imposes. IL HB5142 reaches a similar result by folding sports prediction markets into the statutory definition of sports wagering, whereas NJ creates a parallel \"athletic event market\" category with its own $5 million license. The bill overlaps with the age-21 and consumer rules in IL HB5059 and NY S09414 (the ORACLE Acts) and CT HB05038, and its official-trading restrictions parallel NY S09825 and CA AB1840, but it pairs those with the heaviest tax load in the group.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the committee substitute keeps the stacked 29.75% rate and 10% surcharge on athletic event markets offered by existing sportsbook licensees, how undefined \"sums received\" is interpreted against gross revenue, and whether new athletic event market skins count against the three-skin cap or sit alongside existing online sports pool skins.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":44161,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:57.498Z"},"NJ-S2160":{"summary":"S2160 would bar New Jersey sports wagering licensees from offering or accepting micro bets, defined as live proposition bets on the outcome of the next play or action in an ongoing sports event. It amends the definitions section of the Sports Wagering Act (C.5:12A-10) and adds a new subsection n to C.5:12A-11. Violations would be a disorderly persons offense, with each wager offered or accepted counted as a separate offense.","mechanics":["New definition (C.5:12A-10): \"micro bet\" means a proposition bet wagered live, while a sport or athletic event is ongoing, that concerns the outcome of the next play or action in the event (the findings give a next-pitch strike bet as an example).","New definition of \"proposition bet\": a side wager on a part of a sport or athletic event that does not concern its final outcome. The existing \"sports pool\" definition still lists in-game wagering, in-play bets and proposition bets as permitted.","New C.5:12A-11(n)(1): a sports wagering licensee shall not offer or accept any wager on a micro bet. The ban applies to retail lounges, kiosks and online sports pools alike, with no carve-out by channel.","Penalty, C.5:12A-11(n)(2): any licensee or person who violates the ban commits a disorderly persons offense, fined $500 to $1,000 per offense, with each wager offered or accepted a separate offense.","Section 4: effective immediately. There is no phase-in period and no direction to the Division of Gaming Enforcement to adopt implementing rules.","Status: introduced and referred to the Senate Budget and Appropriations Committee on 2026-03-23. Sponsors are Senators Moriarty and Diegnan."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills bans a specific in-play bet type. CT HB05229 comes closest on consumer protection, since it bars using artificial intelligence to target certain bets to customers, but it regulates targeting rather than prohibiting a product. The Illinois bills IL HB5142 and IL HB5059 address prediction markets, and the Alaska, Georgia and Hawaii bills (AK HB145, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308) authorize and tax sports wagering, so S2160 stands out as a product-level prohibition in a mature market. It relies on both problem-gambling and match-fixing rationales.","watch":"Watch the breadth of \"next play or action,\" which could reach next-drive, next-at-bat or next-point markets, together with the uncapped per-wager penalty exposure, the immediate effective date with no wind-down, and the penalty clause reaching \"any licensee or person,\" which could expose Internet sports pool operators and platform suppliers, not just licensees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":29790,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:00.723Z"},"NJ-S3419":{"summary":"S3419 supplements New Jersey's sports wagering law (P.L.2018, c.33) to require each sports wagering licensee and its contracted operators to adopt Division-approved rules on when and how wagering accounts may be limited, and to publish those rules. Operators must give patrons written notice and an explanation whenever an account is limited, periodically review limited accounts under Division standards, and report annually on the frequency and type of limitations imposed.","mechanics":["Section 1a: licensees and contracted operators must adopt rules on account limitations. Examples given are limits on play times, maximum wager amounts, and deposit and withdrawal restrictions. The rules must be submitted to the Division of Gaming Enforcement for approval.","Section 1a: once approved, the rules must be displayed conspicuously in the sports wagering lounge, posted on the operator's website, included in the account terms and conditions, and made readily available to patrons.","Section 1b: any limitation triggers a duty to give the patron written notice and an explanation. Limited accounts must be reviewed at periodic intervals, under Division-set standards, to decide whether the limits stay or are lifted.","Section 1c: annual reporting to the Division on the frequency and kind of limitations imposed. The Division may request additional information to assess compliance.","Section 1d: the Division may adopt implementing regulations under the Administrative Procedure Act. Section 2 makes the act effective immediately.","The First Reprint, reported by the Senate State Government, Wagering, Tourism & Historic Preservation Committee on May 21, 2026, only corrected a typo ('pstron' to 'patron'). The bill is now referred to Senate Budget and Appropriations."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills directly regulates operator-imposed account limiting, the practice known as 'limiting sharps.' The closest is CT HB05229, which addresses withdrawal of funds from gaming accounts, customer service phone lines, and AI-targeted bets, but it does not reach bet-size restrictions or require notice of limits. Most of the other bills, such as AK HB145 and SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and SB1569, and IL HB2878 and HB5034, concern authorization, licensing fees, or tax rather than operator conduct toward individual patrons. That makes S3419 an unusual patron-transparency measure aimed at a commercial risk-management practice.","watch":"Watch the Division standards for periodic review and how specific the required 'explanation' must be, because they could force disclosure of proprietary risk-management and trader-profiling criteria, make limiting decisions easier for patrons to dispute, and turn the annual reports into a public record of how often operators limit winning bettors.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2518,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:01.598Z"},"NJ-S4447":{"summary":"The committee substitute imposes a 9 percent surtax on prediction market operators, layered on top of New Jersey's existing taxes. Corporations pay it on allocated taxable net income under the Corporation Business Tax. Individuals and pass-through owners pay it on gross income derived from operating a prediction market under the Gross Income Tax. Nearly all credits are barred against the surtax, and the corporate piece applies retroactively to privilege periods beginning on or after July 1, 2025.","mechanics":["Section 1 (CBT): a 9% surtax on allocated taxable net income, as defined in C.54:10A-4(w), for privilege periods beginning on or after July 1, 2025. It is administered under the CBT, and the only credits allowed are installment payments, extension payments and prior overpayments.","Section 2 (GIT): a 9% surtax on gross income, not net, derived from operating a prediction market operator, for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2026. For S corporations and partnerships it applies to each shareholder's pro rata share or partner's distributive share.","\"Prediction market operator\" is defined broadly. It covers a board of trade or other person and its affiliates, and expressly reaches anyone who owns, operates or controls a digital distribution service, platform, online portal or application store where a prediction market may be accessed.","\"Future events\" covers elections, pop-culture events, athletic events or games of skill (including portions and individual player statistics), card, dice and device games, and legal proceedings. \"Prediction market\" covers bid-ask systems \"and any other form,\" regardless of mechanism.","Section 3: the act takes effect immediately, with the retroactive CBT start date described above. The Senate Budget and Appropriations Committee reported the substitute on June 28, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax","prediction"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The other listed bills regulate, restrict or ban prediction markets rather than tax them. Examples include the age-21 and consumer-protection rules in IL HB5059, NY S09414 (the ORACLE Acts) and CT HB05038; the outright prohibitions in MN HF 4437/SF 4511, NC H1171 and VT H0913; IL HB5142, which folds sports prediction markets into the Sports Wagering Act; and the ethics and insider-use limits in CA AB1840 and NY S09825. New Jersey's S4447 is the only bill listed that uses the tax code to target prediction-market income. Its premise is to treat these platforms as taxable businesses operating in the state, not to license or prohibit them.","watch":"Watch three features: the operator definition sweeps in affiliates and app stores, which could pull in sportsbook parents with prediction-market affiliates and distribution platforms; the GIT surtax falls on gross rather than net income; and the CBT surtax reaches back to July 1, 2025.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":6976,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:08.062Z"},"NY-A10003":{"summary":"A10003-D is New York's FY2026-27 Aid to Localities budget bill, which appropriates and reappropriates state, federal and special revenue funds to local governments and grantees from April 1, 2026. The text provided covers only the general provisions in Section 1 and the Office for the Aging schedule, and it contains no sports-betting or gaming provisions. The Assembly bill was substituted by its Senate twin, S9003-D, on 2026-05-27.","mechanics":["Section 1(e) lets the budget director withhold all or part of any appropriation in the bill if a cumulative general fund imbalance of $2,000,000,000 or more is projected for FY2026-27. The director must first deplete the $2B transaction risk reserve. The Legislature has 10 business days to adopt its own withhold plan by concurrent resolution.","Withholds may not be applied to public assistance and SSI-related payments, reductions that would violate federal law, debt service, or court-ordered payments.","Section 1(h) repeals appropriations and reappropriations in chapter 53 of the laws of 2025 that would otherwise lapse on March 31, 2027.","The Office for the Aging receives $430,736,841 in new appropriations and $671,058,513 in reappropriations.","Office for the Aging rate-setting is capped at a 2.7 percent targeted inflationary increase for April 1, 2026 through March 31, 2027.","No expenditure may be made until the director of the budget issues a certificate of approval (Section 1(d))."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The bill is a general appropriations vehicle rather than gaming legislation. In the excerpt provided, nothing addresses wagering, licensing, tax rates or advertising. Any gaming-related lines, such as local aid from gaming revenue or problem-gambling program funding, would sit in agency schedules not included in the text supplied, and their presence or amounts cannot be confirmed from this excerpt.","watch":"Check the full enacted S9003-D text for any appropriations tied to gaming revenue (for example host-community aid or problem-gambling services funded by mobile sports wagering) and whether they could be reached by the Section 1(e) withhold authority if a $2B general fund imbalance arises.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A10003","text_chars":4708148,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:14.052Z"},"NY-A10008":{"summary":"A10008-C (same as S9008-C), signed as Chapter 58 of 2026, is New York's FY2026-27 transportation, economic development and environmental conservation (TED) Article VII budget bill, made up of Parts A through JJJ covering motor vehicle fees, speed enforcement, utility ratemaking, SEQRA reform, insurance, public pensions, bonding authority and other matters. Neither the summary nor the excerpted text authorizes, taxes or regulates sports wagering, casino gaming, lottery or prediction markets. The only provision of possible tangential interest to online operators is Part Y, the 'Safe by Design Act,' which lets the Attorney General adopt rules on age-assurance methods for 'covered platforms.'","mechanics":["Part D adds VTL §1642-b, letting New York City require intelligent speed assistance devices on vehicles whose owners are found liable on 16 school-zone camera speeding notices within 12 months. Installation runs 12 months for a first order, 24 months for a second within 10 years, 36 months for a third within 15 years, and indefinitely after that.","Part D also makes owners pay device costs, with a presumptive waiver at or below 200% of the federal poverty level. It bars disclosure of device data and requires the data to be destroyed when the installation term ends.","Parts A and B extend DMV transaction-fee provisions and the accident prevention course internet pilot from April 1, 2026 to April 1, 2028, retroactive to April 1, 2026.","Part Y amends the General Business Law to enact the 'Safe by Design Act.' The AG may promulgate rules on reasonable and technically feasible age assurance, considering platform size, resources, technical capability, cost and effectiveness of age-determination techniques, audience and industry practice. The definition of 'covered platform' is not in the excerpted text.","Part GGG covers administration of budget funds and accounts plus bonding authorizations. The excerpt does not show any gaming-revenue fund among them."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the gaming-specific bills listed, this bill does not legalize, license or tax wagering. Examples include the mobile sports wagering bills (AK HB145 and SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 2570), the tax-rate changes in IL HB3159 and IA SSB 1157, and the operator-conduct rules in CT HB05229. The closest thematic overlap is CT HB05038, which bars prediction-market platforms from serving or advertising to people under 21. New York's Part Y instead sets up general AG rulemaking on age assurance for 'covered platforms,' and the excerpt does not show it targeting gambling. What stands out is that New York's age-assurance framework was enacted inside a transportation and economic development budget vehicle rather than standalone consumer-protection or gaming legislation.","watch":"Confirm from the full Part Y text whether the 'covered platform' definition could reach online sportsbook, DFS or prediction-market apps, and track the Attorney General's age-assurance rulemaking for compliance obligations layered on top of existing NY Gaming Commission age-verification rules.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A10008","text_chars":703865,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:16.115Z"},"NY-A10329":{"summary":"A10329 amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1367-a(4)(a) by adding subparagraph (xvi), which requires every mobile sports wagering operator to give each authorized sports bettor a monthly account activity statement. The statement must be pushed electronically, kept continuously accessible in the bettor's account, and list deposits, amounts wagered, winnings and losses, net result, number of wagers, time logged in, promotional credits used, responsible gaming resources, and access to lifetime wagering history. The Gaming Commission must set standardized formatting and clarity rules by regulation.","mechanics":["New §1367-a(4)(a)(xvi): the statement must be delivered by push notification and stay continuously accessible through the bettor's account.","Minimum contents (A)-(I): total deposits, total wagered, total winnings and losses, net gain or loss, number of wagers, total time logged into the platform, an itemized list of promotional credits, bonuses and free wagers used, a prominent disclosure of the self-exclusion program and problem gambling services, and access to lifetime wagering history.","Deadline: no later than 15 days after the end of each calendar month.","The Gaming Commission must issue regulations on standardized formatting, clarity requirements, and any additional disclosures.","Effective the January 1 after enactment. Same as S09415. Sponsor Kassay, cosponsors Woerner and Griffin."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"CT HB05229 also adds consumer-facing account obligations for licensees (withdrawal rules, customer service phone lines, limits on AI-targeted bets), but A10329 is narrower and focuses only on periodic disclosure of account activity. The authorization bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) and the tax and licensing bills in Illinois (HB2878, HB3159, HB5034) deal with market structure, not post-launch consumer reporting. Requiring operators to disclose session time and lifetime history in a mandatory monthly push statement is unusual among the bills listed and goes beyond typical responsible-gaming page requirements.","watch":"Watch the Commission's implementing regulations, because the session-time tracking, the push-notification delivery mandate, the 15-day deadline, and the lifetime history access requirement may impose meaningful engineering and data-retention costs, and the regulations could add further disclosures before the January 1 effective date.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A10329","text_chars":5075,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:17.568Z"},"NY-A10005":{"summary":"This is New York's FY2026-27 public protection and general government budget bill (S.9005-C/A.10005-C), an omnibus covering Parts A through OO. It extends the National Guard tuition program and the 911 surcharge suspension, creates new 3D-printed gun and convertible pistol crimes, requires blocking technology on 3D printers, and creates a drone misuse offense. Later Parts cover insurance fraud, liquor licensing, ethics and lobbying training and fees, procurement thresholds, auto insurance rating, and immigration enforcement limits. Nothing in the text supplied (which is truncated partway through Part F) addresses sports betting, gaming, or wagering, and none of the Part titles suggest that it does.","mechanics":["Part C, Subpart A: from May 31, 2027, a dealer or gunsmith who sells or transfers a 'convertible pistol' commits a class D felony (Penal Law 265.10(10)). Distributing digital firearm manufacturing code to anyone who does not hold both a state gunsmith license and a federal firearms license is a class A misdemeanor (265.10(11)).","Part C, Subpart B: GBL 396-eeee bars selling 3D printers in New York unless they have blocking technology. The ban starts one year after DCJS rules, which follow a working-group feasibility review. Gun industry members face a $5,000 civil penalty per product, and a private right of action is created.","Part D: new Penal Law article 280 makes 'unlawful use of a drone' a class A misdemeanor. It covers operating a drone in a 'nefarious manner' or within 500 feet of 'prohibited space' without approval. Prohibited space expressly includes large public gatherings of 500 or more people, such as sporting events.","Part D: Executive Law 236 lets police and peace officers use drone mitigation, kinetic only on probable cause. It also creates a 'New York state blue list' of approved vendors for public purchases of drones and counter-drone technology. Part D takes effect 90 days after enactment.","Parts U and Z, described in the bill title but not in the supplied text: more frequent ethics and lobbying training, fees for late completion, and changes to lobbyist and client registration fees.","Part B takes effect April 1, 2026 (retroactive if enacted later). Part A extends the National Guard program to September 1, 2031."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable, because this bill contains no gaming provisions. Examples include AK HB145/SB194 and GA HB 686 (authorizing sports wagering), IL HB3159 (sports wagering tax), CT HB05038 (prediction markets) and CT HB05229 (gaming advertising). Unlike those, A10005 is a general government budget vehicle. It touches the industry only indirectly, through lobbying registration and training rules and drone restrictions around large sporting events.","watch":"Operators' government-affairs teams should check the Part Z lobbyist and client registration fee changes and the Part U training-frequency rules and late fees. Operators with stadium or event partnerships should note that Part D's 500-foot drone 'prohibited space' covers sporting events of 500 or more people.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":275069,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:19.147Z"},"NY-A10538":{"summary":"A10538 creates a temporary, independent four-member proposition betting task force to study prop bets in New York, focusing in particular on \"under bets\" (wagers that an athlete will fall below a stated statistical threshold). The task force must report findings and legislative or administrative recommendations by December 31, 2026, and the act sunsets 90 days after the report is submitted. It does not itself restrict or prohibit any wager type.","mechanics":["Section 1(2) defines \"proposition bet\" as a wager on the occurrence or nonoccurrence of an act, statistic or event during a sports event that does not depend on the final outcome, and \"under bet\" as an individual-athlete wager won if the athlete achieves less than a stated measure such as points, rebounds, yards, strikeouts or minutes played.","Section 1(3) sets four unpaid members: two appointed by the governor, one by the temporary president of the senate and one by the speaker. All appointments are due by July 1, 2026.","Section 1(4) lists the study topics: types, prevalence, wagering volume and revenue of under bets; integrity risks; economic impact on operators and the market; harassment of and threats to athletes; consumer protection and problem gambling; integrity monitoring and the extent of reporting; conflicts of interest; and potential solutions.","Section 1(5)(b) requires the State Gaming Commission and mobile sports wagering licensees to cooperate and to provide aggregated, anonymized data to the extent permitted by law. The task force must consult operators, regulators, researchers, responsible-gaming advocates, leagues and collegiate associations, and may hold public hearings.","Section 1(6) requires the report by December 31, 2026, delivered to the governor and legislative leaders and posted on the Gaming Commission website. Section 2 makes the act effective immediately and repeals it 90 days after the report is submitted."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike LA SB354, which removes sports-related prop bets from permitted wagers and bans prop and micro-bets outright effective 8/1/26, and NC H828, which prohibits prop bets on college and amateur sports, A10538 is a study-only measure. It singles out athlete \"under\" bets as a distinct category, a focus none of the listed bills shares. It is closer in form to MD HB 195's study and workgroup approach, though narrower in subject. CT HB05229 and MD HB518 legislate operational and problem-gambling rules directly rather than commissioning a report.","watch":"Operators should prepare for the mandatory data-sharing duty under Section 1(5)(b) and engage early as consulted stakeholders, because the December 31, 2026 report is likely to be the vehicle for recommending restrictions or bans on player under props, and potentially on props generally.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A10538","text_chars":7589,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:26.515Z"},"NY-A10513":{"summary":"A10513 amends Economic Development Law §353(5) to add mobile sports wagering operators, as defined in Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1367, to the list of entities ineligible for the Excelsior Jobs Program tax credit. It also extends ineligibility to any parent company or subsidiary of every excluded entity. It takes effect immediately and is the same as S10259.","mechanics":["Restructures §353(5) into a lettered list of ineligible entities: (a) not-for-profits; (b) primarily service or utility providers; (c) predominantly retail or entertainment businesses, excluding defined entertainment companies and music production; (d) electricity, natural gas and steam generators or distributors; and new (e) mobile sports wagering operators.","Ties the definition of 'mobile sports wagering operator' to RPWBL §1367, so it reaches the state-licensed mobile platform providers.","New lead-in language makes 'any parent company or subsidiary thereof' ineligible for the credit 'or any component thereof'. Because the language sits in the lead-in, it applies to all five categories, not only sports wagering operators.","Effective immediately on enactment, with no grandfathering or transition provision for entities already admitted to the program.","Sponsored by Steck with cosponsors K. Brown, Hyndman, Kassay and McDonald. Introduced March 6, 2026 in Economic Development and referred to Ways and Means on April 21, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills touch economic-development incentive eligibility. AK HB145/SB194 and IL HB3159 (an added 2% of adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS) work through new or higher wagering taxes, and IL HB5143 would end Illinois's per-wager tax. A10513 instead raises operators' effective tax burden indirectly, by cutting off access to a state jobs credit. The parent and subsidiary exclusion is unusual because it reaches corporate affiliates outside the wagering business itself.","watch":"Watch the breadth of the parent-and-subsidiary exclusion, which could disqualify affiliated media, technology, casino or hospitality entities in a corporate group from Excelsior credits, and the immediate effective date with no grandfathering, which leaves it unclear whether already-certified participants lose remaining credit components.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A10513","text_chars":3015,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:28.924Z"},"NY-A3003":{"summary":"A03003-D is New York's FY2025-26 Aid to Localities appropriations bill, submitted by the Governor under Article VII, which appropriates and reappropriates state, federal and other funds to localities and grantees for the fiscal year beginning April 1, 2025. The text provided covers only the general provisions in Section 1 and the Office for the Aging schedule, and contains no sports betting, gaming or wagering provisions. On May 8, 2025 it was substituted by its Senate same-as, S3003-D.","mechanics":["Section 1(d): no appropriated money is payable until the Director of the Budget issues a certificate of approval and files it with the Comptroller and the fiscal committee chairs.","Section 1(e): the Budget Director may withhold appropriations if a cumulative general fund imbalance of $2,000,000,000 or more arises in FY2025-26. The Director must first deplete the $2B transaction risk reserve and notify legislative leaders within 10 business days. The Legislature then has 10 business days to adopt its own withhold plan by concurrent resolution.","Section 1(e) also exempts certain payments from withholding: public assistance and SSI-related payments, reductions that would violate federal law, debt service, and court-ordered payments.","Section 1(g): appropriations and reappropriations in chapter 53 of the laws of 2024 that would otherwise lapse on March 31, 2026 are repealed on enactment.","The Office for the Aging receives $417,304,100 in all-funds appropriations ($244,080,100 General Fund) and $636,450,011 in reappropriations. A recurring rider limits rate adjustments to a 2.6 percent targeted inflationary increase."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The bill is a general Aid to Localities appropriations measure, not gaming legislation. Its main notable feature is the broad mid-year withholding authority given to the Budget Director, triggered by a $2B general fund imbalance.","watch":"The excerpt supplied stops in the Office for the Aging schedule, so review the full enacted S3003-D for any Gaming Commission local-aid, problem-gambling or sports-wagering revenue lines, and for whether the $2B-imbalance withholding authority in Section 1(e) could reach them.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A3003","text_chars":4477757,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:30.269Z"},"NY-A3005":{"summary":"A03005-C (chapter 55 of 2025) is New York's Public Protection and General Government Article VII budget bill for FY 2025-26. It extends dozens of expiring criminal justice provisions to September 1, 2027 (Part A), changes police and correction officer eligibility and age rules (Parts C and ZZ), and makes a wide range of changes on victim services, public campaign finance, pensions, courts, prison closures and AI-generated child sexual abuse material. Nothing in the text provided, or in the Part-by-Part summary, authorizes, taxes or regulates sports wagering or other gaming.","mechanics":["Part A extends sunsets from September 1, 2025 to September 1, 2027 for items including prisoner furloughs, work release, the earned eligibility reporting under chapter 261 of 1987, the VTL §1809 mandatory surcharge and the ignition interlock program. NYC pre-criminal proceeding settlements are extended to March 31, 2027.","Part C waives state residency under Public Officers Law §3(9-a) for correction officers and trainees at state facilities. It raises the State Police mandatory retirement age from 60 to 63 (RSSL §381-b(e)). It also raises the maximum entry age for State Police (Executive Law §215(3)) and municipal police (Civil Service Law §58) to 43.","Part ZZ lowers the minimum hiring age for correction officers to 18. Part BBB authorizes DOCCS to close up to three correctional facilities in FY 2025-26.","Part J requires certain employers to adopt a model gender-based violence and the workplace policy, amending the State Finance Law and the Executive Law. Part L criminalizes AI-generated child sexual abuse material under the Penal Law.","Part RR addresses civil enforcement actions brought by the Attorney General. Part OO revises public campaign financing thresholds and procedures. Part Q extends alcoholic beverage liquidator's and temporary retail permits.","Each Part has its own effective date in its last section. Part A takes effect immediately (§24)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Every other listed bill is a gaming measure, so none is directly comparable. CT HB05038 restricts prediction market access and advertising for under-21s, IL HB3159 adds a 2% sports wagering tax, GA HB 686 would legalize sports betting, and AK HB145 and SB194 would authorize mobile wagering. NY A03005 has no wagering, gaming tax, advertising or licensing provisions at all. New York's gaming-related budget changes, where enacted, sit in other Article VII bills or the Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law, not this one.","watch":"There is little here for sports-betting operators, but counsel should review the full text of Part RR, since any expansion of the Attorney General's civil enforcement authority could reach licensed operators. They should also check Part J's model workplace policy requirement in case it applies to operators as state contractors or New York employers.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":336806,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:38.528Z"},"NY-A382":{"summary":"A382-A amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1363 so that the existing advertising rules for casino gaming facility licensees and applicants also apply expressly to mobile sports wagering licensees as defined in § 1367. It also adds a requirement that, in video ads, the problem gambling hotline number stay on screen for the entire ad.","mechanics":["§ 1363(1)(a) and new (1)(c): 'advertisement' now covers any notice, communication, or promotional activity about the gaming-related business of a mobile sports wagering licensee (per § 1367), including electronic dissemination.","§ 1363(2): mobile sports wagering licensee ads must be factual and not false, deceptive, or misleading. They may not obscure material facts, omit material conditions or limiting factors, or depict anyone under 21 gaming. The existing (2)(d) duty to name the facility and its location is worded around gaming facilities.","§ 1363(3): every ad must clearly and conspicuously state a problem gambling hotline number. New language requires the number to be visible for the full duration of any video ad.","§ 1363(4): direct ads must offer at least two opt-out methods (telephone, U.S. mail, or email), and licensees must block opted-out individuals in their databases within 15 days.","§ 1363(5)-(6): licensees must file complete copies of all ads with the Gaming Commission within 5 business days of dissemination, stop an ad on Commission notice, and keep ad records for at least 2 years.","Effective on the 90th day after enactment. Same as S09436."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which targets particular advertising settings (college campuses) and AI-driven bet targeting, A382-A does not create new substantive bans. It instead brings mobile operators under New York's existing casino advertising regime and adds a video hotline-duration rule. CT HB05038 and IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act) aim advertising and age limits at prediction markets rather than licensed sportsbooks. Most of the other listed bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308) are authorization and tax frameworks, not advertising-specific measures.","watch":"Watch the full-duration video hotline requirement, the 5-business-day ad filing and Commission takedown power applied to high-volume digital and promotional campaigns, and how § 1363(2)(d)'s facility name-and-location rule will be read for online-only mobile operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A382","text_chars":5576,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:41.026Z"},"NY-A4279":{"summary":"A04279-A, the \"regulating addictive notifications act,\" adds a new paragraph (k) to Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1367-a(4) barring New York mobile sports wagering operators and platforms from initiating or sending push notifications or text messages, including alerts, that solicit sports wagers or deposits. It defines \"push notification\" as an automatic electronic message shown on an account holder's device while the operator's app is not open or visible, prompting the user to check or engage with the platform. It takes effect immediately.","mechanics":["Amends RWB Law §1367-a(4) by adding paragraph (k); companion bill is S05537.","Covers both mobile sports wagering operators and mobile sports wagering platforms.","Bans push notifications and text message notifications, including alerts, relating to solicitation of sports wagers or deposits.","\"Push notification\" means an automatic electronic message displayed while the operator's user interface is not actively open or visible, prompting the account holder to check or engage with the platform.","The text contains no opt-in exception and no separate penalty provision, so enforcement would rely on existing §1367-a licensing and Gaming Commission authority.","Effective immediately on enactment; the bill was ordered to third reading (cal. 123) on 2026-01-07."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills authorize or tax sports wagering, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and IL HB3159. CT HB05229 is the closest analogue, because it also limits operator marketing conduct: it restricts campus advertising, regulates withdrawals and bars AI-targeted bets. New York's bill is narrower but more absolute, imposing a flat ban on one marketing channel (push notifications and texts soliciting wagers or deposits) rather than age- or location-based limits like those in CT HB05038 or IL HB5059 for prediction markets. Unusually, it contains no consent or opt-in safe harbor, so even customers who have opted in could not receive such messages.","watch":"The undefined scope of \"relating to the solicitation of sports wagers or deposits\" could sweep in odds boosts, promo alerts, game-start alerts and possibly transactional or responsible-gaming messages, and with immediate effect, no opt-in carve-out and the bill on the third-reading calendar, operators should be ready to reconfigure CRM and notification systems quickly.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A4279","text_chars":4632,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:46.791Z"},"NY-A3006":{"summary":"A03006-C is New York's 2025-26 Education, Labor, Housing and Family Assistance (ELFA) Article VII budget bill, a 30-plus-part omnibus covering school aid, free school meals, student device policies, scholarships, housing, social services, labor law penalties, unemployment insurance, discovery reform and fund administration. Nothing in the title, the Part-by-Part summary or the portion of text provided authorizes, regulates or taxes sports wagering or other gaming. The Assembly version was substituted by the Senate companion S3006-C on May 8, 2025, so this bill number is not the operative vehicle.","mechanics":["Part A, §10: 2025-26 foundation aid equals the greater of total foundation aid or 1.02 times the foundation aid base (a 2% minimum increase).","Part A, §§3-7: the 'poverty count' now uses an economically disadvantaged student count and three-year average Census SAIPE rates, each weighted at 65%, replacing free and reduced-price lunch and Census 2000 data. Part A, §4-a raises the English language learner weight from 0.50 to 0.53 starting in 2025-26.","Part A, §9: the state sharing ratio cap for foundation aid rises to 0.93 for 2025-26 and later. Part A, §10-a sets a 1.351 regional cost index for Westchester County.","Part A, §10-b: the approved salary ceiling for BOCES aid rises in steps from $30,000 to $60,000 by 2028-29. Part A, §10-c raises career education aid to $4,100 per pupil after 2024-25.","Other Parts: universal free school meals (Part B), limits on internet-enabled devices in schools (Part C), higher child labor and wage-payment civil penalties (Parts V-W), a higher maximum unemployment insurance benefit (Part KK), discovery reform (Part LL), and fund transfers and bonding authority (Part MM).","Effective dates are set Part by Part in each Part's last section, as provided in §1."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI SB1569 would authorize and tax sports wagering, while IL HB3159 and HB4171 amend an existing sports wagering act, and this bill does neither. The closest structural analogue is IL HB2755, a multi-subject revenue omnibus, but HB2755 is a revenue measure and the provided text of A03006 contains no gaming tax or wagering provision. Any New York gaming-related budget changes would more likely appear in other Article VII bills, such as the revenue bill, not in ELFA.","watch":"Because the supplied text is truncated, counsel should review Part MM's fund-transfer and sweep provisions in the enacted S3006-C to confirm that no lottery, commercial gaming or mobile sports wagering education accounts are redirected.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A3006","text_chars":669242,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:47.520Z"},"NY-A3009":{"summary":"New York's FY2025-26 revenue budget bill (Chapter 59 of 2025, signed May 9, 2025) is mostly a broad tax package covering income tax rate cuts, the high-income surcharge extension, the inflation refund credit, business credits and MTA financing. It also has a cluster of gaming Parts. These address pari-mutuel tax simplification and simulcasting extenders (Part FF), tax rates on gaming revenues in certain regions (Part GG), OTB capital funds (Part HH), vendor-track fees (Part MM), NYRA franchise board members (Part NN), and a larger problem-gambling carve-out from mobile sports wagering tax revenue (Part OO). The supplied text is truncated partway through Part B, so the gaming Parts can be described only from the bill summary and title.","mechanics":["Part OO: mobile sports wagering tax revenue set aside for problem gambling education and treatment is fixed at $6 million per fiscal year through FY2026 and $12 million per fiscal year after that, spendable only under a plan approved by the Director of the Budget.","Part GG: sets rates for the tax on gaming revenues in certain regions under the Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law, and amends the effectiveness of Part OOO of Chapter 59 of the Laws of 2021. Repeal provisions apply on expiration.","Part FF: Subpart A simplifies the pari-mutuel tax rate system and repeals RPWBL §908. Subpart B extends the simulcast licensing, out-of-state thoroughbred and harness simulcast, and wager-distribution provisions (chapters 281/1994 and 346/1990). Subpart C sets amounts of market origin credits and fees and the state's use of the funds collected.","Part MM: amends the Tax Law on vendor fees paid to certain VLT vendor tracks, with a sunset and repeal on expiration.","Part NN: changes the terms and licensing requirements of members of the franchised corporation appointed by NYRA. Part HH addresses use of Capital OTB capital acquisition fund money.","Part II funds research on thoroughbred health and safety. Under §1 of the act, each Part's effective date is set in that Part's last section; for example, Part A (the inflation refund credit) takes effect immediately."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), NY A3009 assumes an existing mobile market and adjusts how its revenue is used. On the face of the summary, it does not change the mobile sports wagering tax rate itself. Its doubled problem-gambling earmark is closest to IL HB3159, which would dedicate 2% of sports wagering AGR to fire and EMS. It also resembles the problem-gambling program bills IL HB1451 and HB2580. Those bills, however, build out program content, whereas NY simply fixes a dollar amount gated by Budget Director approval. Part GG's regional casino rate setting has no direct counterpart among the listed bills, though IA SSB 1157 similarly bundles tax rates and revenue use across gambling verticals.","watch":"Operators should track how the jump from $6 million to $12 million under Part OO, and the Budget Director's spending plan, feed into future pushes to raise New York's mobile tax or responsible-gaming obligations. They should also obtain the full text of Part GG, which was not included in the supplied excerpt, to confirm which regional gaming revenue rates were reset and when they sunset.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A3009","text_chars":601354,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:48.095Z"},"NY-A6745":{"summary":"A06745-A adds a new section 912 to New York's Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law that makes it unlawful to operate, conduct or promote online sweepstakes games in New York. It also bars applicants, licensees, payment processors, geolocation providers, content suppliers, platform providers and media affiliates from supporting such games. Violators face fines of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation and loss of, or ineligibility for, a gaming license, enforced by the Gaming Commission, State Police or Attorney General.","mechanics":["Definition (§912(1)(a)): an \"online sweepstakes game\" is any internet or mobile game, contest or promotion that uses a dual-currency system exchangeable for cash prizes or cash equivalents and simulates casino-style gaming (slots, video poker, table games, lottery games, bingo or sports wagering); the Gaming Commission decides what counts as a dual-currency system.","Direct prohibition (§912(2)(a)): it is unlawful for any applicant, licensed entity, gaming or key employee, individual investor or investment firm, board member, or any other person to operate, conduct or promote online sweepstakes games in New York.","Support prohibition (§912(2)(b)): it is unlawful for applicants, licensed entities, financial institutions, payment processors, geolocation providers, gaming content suppliers, platform providers or media affiliates to support the operation, conduct or promotion of such games.","Penalties (§912(3)): a fine of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation, plus possible loss of a gaming license and/or ineligibility for one; fines are deposited in the commercial gaming revenue fund (State Finance Law §97-nnnn) for problem gambling education and treatment.","Enforcement (§912(4)): the Gaming Commission, State Police or Attorney General may investigate, hold hearings, issue cease-and-desist letters and issue subpoenas.","Effective immediately; the Assembly bill was substituted by S5935-A on 2025-06-13."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization-and-tax bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), this is a pure prohibition aimed at dual-currency sweepstakes casinos and the vendors that support them. CA AB 831 also targets sweepstakes, but it works through California's unfair-practices framework for contests and sweepstakes, whereas the NY bill ties violations to gaming-license consequences and reaches payment processors, geolocation providers and media affiliates. It runs opposite to IL HB3274/HB5048, which would shield prize and gift kiosk operators from Gaming Board discipline. It is closer in spirit to FL H0189 and S1580, which tighten illegal-gambling enforcement.","watch":"Operators and their suppliers face exposure under the §912(2)(b) \"support\" and \"promote\" language, which could put a licensee's New York license at risk because of a vendor, affiliate or media partner's sweepstakes ties. Note that the legislative purpose's \"knowingly accepts revenue\" from sweepstakes standard does not appear in the operative text, and the Commission has undefined discretion to define \"dual-currency system.\"","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A6745","text_chars":5936,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:54.925Z"},"NY-A7903":{"summary":"A7903-C amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1367(1)(r) to add a new class of 'prohibited sports bettor': individuals convicted of (or found in a final civil protection order to have committed) enumerated Penal Law offenses against athletes, coaches, officials, jockeys and other sports or horse-race participants, or sports bribery/tampering and pari-mutuel integrity offenses, where the conduct arose from or related to a wager or the event. The Gaming Commission must set a tiered schedule of fixed ban periods by regulation, notify designated individuals, hear limited written appeals, and distribute a consolidated list to casinos, mobile operators and horse-race wagering entities, which must block those individuals from wagering or holding accounts.","mechanics":["New § 1367(1)(r)(xi) covers convictions on or after the effective date for listed offenses (assault, menacing, stalking, harassment, coercion, burglary, arson, robbery, intimidation, false bomb in a stadium, sports bribing §§ 180.40-180.53, etc.) or substantially similar out-of-state offenses, plus final orders of protection, where the victim is a sports or horse-race participant and the conduct relates to a wager or the event; disorderly conduct and harassment 2nd degree (clause C) qualify only if the Commission determines designation is warranted.","New § 1367(17) requires a Commission-set schedule with fixed tiers: Tier I (class B misdemeanors/violations) 1-3 years, Tier II (specified class A misdemeanors) 3-5 years, Tier III (felonies, specified class A misdemeanors and all sports bribery/tampering offenses) 5-10 years; consecutive periods for multiple incidents and the tier maximum for offenses committed during an existing ban; no ban may be indefinite or exceed 10 years for a single offense; report to the Governor and legislative leaders every five years.","Individuals receive notice and may file a written appeal limited to identity errors, vacated convictions/orders, expired periods, lack of a wager/event nexus, or (for clause C) misapplication of regulations; designation cannot rest solely on constitutionally protected fan criticism or commentary.","New § 1367(18) requires the Commission, with OCA and DCJS, to adopt information-sharing rules respecting sealing and confidentiality laws, and to distribute at least annually (with interim updates) a consolidated list to casinos, mobile sports wagering licensees, racing associations, OTBs, simulcast facilities, account wagering licensees and multi-jurisdictional ADW providers.","Licensees must use the list to prevent sports wagers, account opening or maintenance, and horse-race wagers; § 1367(17)(c), § 1367(18)(d) and new § 1012(19) let operators rely on the list in good faith and require no independent criminal-history investigation beyond identity verification.","Takes effect 180 days after becoming law; rulemaking by the Commission, the Chief Administrator of the Courts and DCJS is authorized immediately."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills creates a conviction-based bettor exclusion list tied to abuse of athletes. Bills such as AK HB145/SB194, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569 and GA HB 686 address criminal history only for operator licensure, and CT HB05229 regulates operator conduct (withdrawals, advertising, AI targeting) rather than individual bettor eligibility. NY A7903-C instead pairs the existing prohibited-bettor framework with a statutory tiered penalty schedule and a court/DCJS data pipeline, an approach aimed at bettor harassment of athletes that has no analogue in the listed bills.","watch":"Operators should watch the Commission's implementing regulations, which will govern list format and update frequency, identity-matching standards for account screening, and how far the good-faith reliance safe harbor protects licensees against enforcement for missed matches or for blocking the wrong person.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A7903","text_chars":25132,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:00.975Z"},"NY-A9113":{"summary":"A9113-B amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1344(2) to require the Gaming Commission's self-exclusion regulations to include procedures allowing a person to submit a self-exclusion request (placement or removal under §1344(1)) electronically. The procedures must ensure parity between electronic and paper requests for record retention and for transmission of requests to licensed gaming facilities and mobile sports wagering platforms. The existing obligations on facilities to strip self-excluded persons from marketing and deny them credit, comps, check cashing and club benefits are left unchanged.","mechanics":["Amends RWB Law §1344(2), added by ch. 174 of 2013, so that Gaming Commission regulations must provide for electronic submission of self-exclusion requests under §1344(1).","Requires parity between electronic and non-electronic requests in record retention and in transmission to licensed gaming facilities and mobile sports wagering platforms.","Expressly names mobile sports wagering platforms as recipients of self-exclusion transmissions alongside licensed gaming facilities.","Retains the existing requirement that licensed gaming facilities remove self-excluded persons from targeted mailings and promotions and deny them credit, complimentaries, check cashing, club programs and similar benefits.","Takes effect immediately on enactment, though implementation depends on Commission rulemaking.","The bill is the B-print and is the same as S8636-B. It passed the Assembly (Cal. No. 240) and was returned to the Assembly on 2026-06-04."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills address authorization, licensing or tax rather than self-exclusion administration. Examples include AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, and Illinois' license-fee bills HB2878/HB5034. The closest analogue is CT HB05229, which layers consumer-protection duties on licensees, such as withdrawal rules, customer service lines and AI targeting limits. NY A9113 is narrower: a procedural change aimed at the regulator's intake process rather than new operator conduct rules. Illinois HB1451/HB2580 likewise address problem gambling, but through DHS public education rather than exclusion-list mechanics.","watch":"Watch the Gaming Commission's implementing regulations, since electronic intake will likely increase the volume and speed of list updates that mobile operators must ingest, and parity in transmission may bring tighter timelines for honoring exclusions and suppressing marketing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A9113","text_chars":3606,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:01.738Z"},"NY-A8167":{"summary":"A08167-B amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law \\u00a71367(8)(c) to rework the existing carve-out of New York mobile sports wagering tax revenue for a statewide youth sports grant program administered by OCFS. It fixes the annual amount at $5 million through FY2026 and $23 million each year after, splits it between capital and programming, and routes the money through county youth bureaus, NYC's Department of Youth and Community Development, and a qualifying sports-for-youth nonprofit. It does not change the mobile sports wagering tax rate or impose any new obligation on operators.","mechanics":["\\u00a71367(8)(c): starting in FY2022, the commission pays one percent of the state mobile sports wagering tax to the general fund for an OCFS youth sports activities and education grant program for underserved youth under 18. The amount is fixed at $5M a year through FY2026 and $23M a year after that. The bill's title and summary describe 'up to two percent' capped at $23M, but the operative text says one percent.","Split: 45% for capital expenditures and 'the remaining fifty percent' for programming. These shares add to 95%, which looks like a drafting error.","Distribution: county youth bureaus distribute all capital funding and the programming funding outside NYC. In NYC, 75% of programming funds go through DYCD and 25% through a not-for-profit with an established history of sports-based youth development grantmaking in underserved communities.","Reporting: each distributing entity must report to the commissioner on recipients, use of funds and impact within six months after the end of each grant period.","Unchanged surrounding provisions: under \\u00a71367(8)(a), mobile tax revenue otherwise goes to the lottery fund for education. Under (8)(b), problem gambling funding is $6M a year through FY2026 and $12M after. Under (8)(d), platform providers must make at least monthly deposits and file a reconciliation by the 10th of each month.","Effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Illinois HB3159 also dedicates a slice of sports wagering money to a local public purpose: 2% of adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS departments from July 1, 2026. That bill is framed as the State imposing and collecting the 2%, so it may add to operator cost. NY A08167-B only reallocates revenue the state already collects under the existing mobile tax. Iowa SSB 1157 likewise channels gambling and sports wagering revenue into sports tourism and infrastructure funds, but it does so alongside tax-rate and license-fee changes that this NY bill does not attempt. Unlike the Alaska, Georgia and Hawaii authorization bills, A08167-B assumes a mature market and deals purely with downstream appropriation.","watch":"There is no direct compliance or tax burden on operators, but watch whether a growing set of fixed-dollar earmarks from mobile tax revenue ($23M youth sports, $12M problem gambling) builds constituencies that make future reductions in New York's mobile tax rate harder, and whether the 1%-versus-2% and 45/50 drafting inconsistencies get fixed in a further amendment.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":5606,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:04.118Z"},"NY-A9146":{"summary":"A09146 amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1367-a(4)(a) to require that, whenever a New York mobile sports wagering operator provides information on compulsive play, every link and referral for counseling, therapy or treatment points account holders only to the problem gambling services webpage maintained by the Office of Addiction Services and Supports (OASAS). It also bars operators from advertising or promoting any other organization that provides such services, while expressly preserving operators' ability to offer responsible gaming tools.","mechanics":["Inserts a new subparagraph (xv) in RWB Law §1367-a(4)(a): all links and referrals for counseling, therapy or treatment for compulsive play must direct account holders to the OASAS problem gambling services webpage.","Prohibits mobile sports wagering operators from advertising or promoting any other organization or entity that provides counseling, therapy or treatment services for compulsive play.","Carve-out: operators may still provide responsible gaming tools, including deposit limits, wager limits, time limits and voluntary self-exclusion.","Existing responsible-play page requirements in (xiv) are unchanged, and the annual problem gaming plan requirement, approved by the Gaming Commission in consultation with OASAS, is renumbered from (xv) to (xvi) without substantive change.","Applies to licensed mobile sports wagering operators; takes effect immediately on enactment, with no transition period.","Same-as bill: S10154. Passed one chamber and was referred to Racing, Gaming and Wagering on 2026-05-14."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills address authorization, licensing or taxes, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569; A09146 does none of these and regulates only operator-facing problem gambling referrals. Illinois HB1451 and HB2580 also deal with gambling disorders, but they direct the Department of Human Services' public education program rather than restricting what operators may link to. Connecticut HB05229 is the closest in spirit, imposing operator conduct rules on advertising, withdrawals, customer service and AI-targeted bets, but it does not channel treatment referrals to a single state provider. A09146 is unusual in giving a state agency's webpage an exclusive role in treatment referrals and banning operators from promoting third-party treatment providers.","watch":"Operators should audit existing responsible-gaming partnerships and in-app content, such as links to national helplines, private treatment providers or sponsored RG nonprofits, because the bar on advertising or promoting any other treatment organization takes effect immediately and the bill does not define 'referrals' or 'promote'.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A9146","text_chars":4368,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:08.845Z"},"NY-J493":{"summary":"Senate Resolution 493, sponsored by Senator Fernandez with a long bipartisan co-sponsor list, asks Governor Hochul to proclaim March 2025 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month in New York, alongside National Problem Gambling Awareness Month. It is a non-binding memorializing resolution, adopted 2025-03-18. Despite the 'signed into law' status, it creates no legal obligations, appropriations or regulatory changes.","mechanics":["Resolving clause only memorializes the Governor to proclaim March 2025 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month and urges citizens to raise awareness of prevention, treatment and recovery services.","Recitals cite an OASAS survey finding 5% of adults (668,000 people) showed problem gambling behaviors in the past year.","Recitals cite a survey finding 10% of 7th–12th graders (140,000 students) showed signs of problem gambling, with 10% of those needing treatment.","Recitals tie the concern directly to mobile sports betting growth and note the State has collected more than $1.55 billion in taxes on mobile bets.","No operator duties, funding, enforcement or effective-date provisions; the only operative step is transmitting an engrossed copy to the Governor."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like NJ AR150 and LA SR 178, this is a non-binding resolution rather than a statute. It is weaker than both: AR150 urges a specific ban on targeted ads to people showing signs of problem gambling, and SR 178 asks for a health-department study, while J493 only asks for a proclamation. It has none of the operative responsible-gaming mandates in MD HB518 (self-exclusion list), MD HB1409/SB862 (mobile gambling prevalence studies and fund revenue) or NJ A 4003 (no incentives to patrons using responsible-gaming tools).","watch":"It has no direct compliance impact, but the recitals explicitly link New York's $1.55 billion in mobile betting tax receipts to an obligation to fund prevention and treatment, so watch for this framing being used to support binding bills raising problem-gambling contributions or responsible-gaming mandates on New York mobile operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"J493","text_chars":4126,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:14.946Z"},"NY-J1707":{"summary":"Senate Resolution J1707 is a non-binding memorializing resolution, adopted March 10, 2026, asking Governor Hochul to proclaim March 2026 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month in New York, in line with National Problem Gambling Awareness Month. It urges citizens to raise awareness of problem gambling and of prevention, treatment and recovery services. Although the status line says 'signed into law,' it is a one-house resolution that creates no legal obligations, regulatory changes or funding.","mechanics":["Form: Senate resolution sponsored by Senator Fernandez with roughly 60 co-sponsors; adopted 2026-03-10; transmitted to the Governor; no statutory amendments.","Operative clause: memorializes the Governor to proclaim March 2026 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month and urges public awareness of prevention, treatment and recovery services.","Findings cite an OASAS survey showing 5% of adults (668,000 people) had problem gambling behaviors in the past year, and 10% of grade 7-12 students (140,000) showed signs of problem gambling.","Expressly ties concern to the growth of mobile sports betting and notes the State has collected more than $1.55 billion in taxes on mobile bets.","Imposes no requirements on operators, the Gaming Commission or OASAS, and has no effective date or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like LA SR 178 and NJ AR150, J1707 is a non-binding resolution, but it is narrower than both. LA SR 178 asks the Louisiana Department of Health to study and recommend modernized problem gambling programs, and NJ AR150 urges a substantive policy, a ban on targeted ads to people showing signs of problem gambling. J1707 only asks for an awareness proclamation. By contrast, Maryland's HB1409/SB862 (prevalence studies and fund revenue) and HB518 (voluntary exclusion list and problem gambling regulations), and New Jersey's A 4003 (no incentive-based wagering offers to patrons using responsible gaming tools), would impose actual duties affecting operators.","watch":"The resolution has no direct legal effect, but its explicit framing of mobile sports betting growth and the $1.55 billion tax take as reasons for more problem-gambling resources signals bipartisan Senate appetite for future responsible-gaming mandates or funding demands on New York mobile operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"J1707","text_chars":4078,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:16.999Z"},"NY-A9584":{"summary":"A09584-A adds a new Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1367-b imposing detailed know-your-customer, multi-factor authentication, account-sharing/proxy-betting monitoring, responsible gaming, risk-assessment, recordkeeping and consumer-data obligations on New York mobile sports wagering licensees. It requires licensees to tier bettors as at-risk, elevated-risk or high-risk under commission-set thresholds, lets bettors who knowingly share accounts or proxy bet be designated prohibited sports bettors, and redirects penalties for problem gaming and responsible gaming violations to a three-way split. It takes effect 180 days after enactment.","mechanics":["§ 1367-b(2): before account activation, licensees must collect full legal name, residential address, DOB, last four SSN digits (or alternative ID), email, phone, a photo of government ID and a live photograph. All data must be entered directly by the bettor with no pre-population or auto-fill. Race, gender and income must be requested on a voluntary basis. Bettors must attest to being 21+, having only one account per skin, no account sharing or proxy betting, and no bots. Bettors may close accounts at any time, and funds must be returned without unreasonable delay.","§ 1367-b(3)-(4): written identity verification procedures using independent data sources, with enhanced verification for red flags. Licensees remain liable when they use third-party vendors. Multi-factor authentication is mandatory, with a re-authentication window of no more than one hour on a verified device. Accounts flagged for material risk of sharing, proxy betting or fraud must be promptly suspended. Confirmed cases must be reported to the commission and stay suspended until corrective measures are taken. Knowing participants may be designated prohibited bettors.","§ 1367-b(5)-(7): written responsible gaming procedures subject to commission approval, a senior 'responsible gaming lead' who reports to management at least annually, and employee training. Licensees must monitor risk factors such as wager or deposit escalation, late-night sessions, limit changes, withdrawal reversals and failed payments. Risk assessments are triggered by commission-set evidence-based thresholds, and bettors receive at-risk, elevated-risk or high-risk designations.","§ 1367-b(8)-(9): records kept at least 5 years, an at-risk bettor list provided to the commission on request, and no obligation to verify income or credit. Licensees may not sell or share KYC data except where strictly necessary. Bettors have a data access right. On account deletion, a bettor may request deletion of KYC data within 30 days, and that deletion right overrides the § 1367-b retention rules, subject to exceptions for federal law, court orders and investigations.","§ 1367-b(10): commission rulemaking, examination, deficiency findings, corrective action plans and practice-suspension orders. Violations are subject to civil penalties, license conditions, suspension or revocation. There is no private right of action.","§ 1367(8) as amended: penalties for violations of § 1367-a(4) problem gaming plans and § 1367-b are split one-third to the lottery education fund, one-third to problem gambling education and treatment via the commercial gaming revenue fund, and one-third retained by the commission for enforcement. § 1367-a(4)(a)(xv) is amended so that annual problem gaming plans must include the § 1367-b procedures."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the listed bills that authorize or tax sports wagering (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) or adjust licensing fees and taxes (IL HB2878, HB5034, HB3159), NY A09584 is a pure operator-conduct bill aimed at an existing mobile market. Its closest analogue is CT HB05229, which also regulates gaming-account practices such as withdrawals, customer service and AI-targeted bets. NY goes much further, mandating codified KYC data fields, MFA, a statutory tiered risk-designation scheme for bettors, and consumer data deletion rights. Earmarking penalty revenue to fund the commission's own enforcement is also unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the commission's implementing regulations on the 'objective, evidence-based thresholds' for risk assessments and tier designations, the mandatory-suspension trigger for suspected account sharing, and the tension between the 30-day data deletion right and five-year retention and AML obligations, since these will drive compliance cost and enforcement exposure, which is heightened by the commission retaining a third of penalties.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A9584","text_chars":35629,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:19.703Z"},"NY-K1093":{"summary":"Assembly Resolution K1093 is a non-binding resolution that asks Governor Hochul to proclaim March 2026 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month in New York, alongside National Problem Gambling Awareness Month, and urges residents to raise awareness of prevention, treatment and recovery services. It creates no legal obligations, and despite the 'signed into law' status, it was simply adopted by the Assembly on 2026-03-25. Its recitals tie the need for awareness directly to the growth of mobile sports betting.","mechanics":["Memorializing resolution only: no statutory amendments, mandates, appropriations, or enforcement provisions.","Recitals cite an OASAS survey finding 5% of adults (668,000 people) showed problem gambling behaviors in the past year.","Recitals cite a survey finding 10% of 7th–12th graders (140,000 students) showed signs of problem gambling, with 10% of those needing treatment.","Recitals point to record mobile betting revenue and more than $1.55 billion in state taxes collected on mobile bets.","Resolved clauses ask the Governor to make the proclamation, urge public awareness, and direct that an engrossed copy be sent to the Governor.","Sponsored by Woerner, with 22 multi-sponsors."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like LA SR 178, which asks the Louisiana Department of Health to study modernizing problem gambling prevention, K1093 is a non-binding resolution, but it does not even request a study or report. It is also softer than NJ AR150, which urges a specific ban on targeted gambling ads to at-risk individuals. By contrast, Maryland's HB518 (voluntary exclusion list and problem gambling regulations), HB1409/SB862 (prevalence studies and fund revenue) and HB758 (problem gambling fund uses), along with NJ A 4002/A 4003, would impose real obligations or funding changes on operators and agencies.","watch":"The resolution has no direct legal effect, but its explicit link between mobile sports betting growth and problem gambling, including youth gambling figures, signals legislative appetite for future responsible-gaming mandates, advertising limits, or problem-gambling funding carve-outs from mobile wagering tax revenue in New York.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"K1093","text_chars":3548,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:20.328Z"},"NY-S10092":{"summary":"S10092 adds a new General Business Law §396-kkk making it unlawful for operators of covered platforms (profile-based websites and apps, including social gaming platforms) and social media platforms to show New York users paid ads, sponsored content, influencer or affiliate marketing, algorithmic promotion, targeted amplification or cross-platform placements for services offering online gaming-related gambling, prediction market wagering, online sweepstakes gaming, sports-related gambling or traditional online gambling, unless the operator has reasonably determined that the user is not a minor (under 18) under attorney general age-assurance regulations. Violations are treated as false advertising under GBL §350-a and can be enforced by the attorney general or the State Gaming Commission.","mechanics":["Duty falls on platform operators, not gambling advertisers. Ads covered by §396-kkk(2) cannot be shown to any NY user unless the operator has reasonably determined the user is 18 or older, using age-assurance rules the AG must promulgate under subdivision 6.","Broad product definitions: 'sports-related gambling' includes bracket and fantasy contests, DFS, esports and in-game betting, and sports wagering as defined in RPWBL §1301. 'Predictive market wagering' covers wagers, trades or financial positions on future events. 'Online sweepstakes gaming' covers dual-currency games that simulate gambling. Loot boxes, skins and pay-to-win microtransactions are also covered.","Data minimization under subdivision 3: age-verification data may be used only for age determination and must be deleted immediately after the attempt, unless state or federal law requires otherwise.","Enforcement under subdivision 4: a violation is false advertising under GBL §350-a, enforceable by the AG or the Gaming Commission. The AG must also run a complaint website, and the Gaming Commission must maintain industry guidance on online advertising to people under 18.","Takes effect 180 days after enactment, with rulemaking authorized immediately. Includes a severability clause. Same-as bill: A10712."],"issue_tags":["advertising","prediction","sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"CT HB05038 and IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act) apply age limits to prediction markets at age 21, regulating the platforms' own users and marketing. S10092 instead sets the line at 18 and puts the obligation on the social media and gaming platforms that carry the ads. CT HB05229 restricts gaming advertising on college campuses by placement, while S10092 relies on age assurance across all online ad formats. Its single advertising rule covering sports betting, prediction markets, sweepstakes, loot boxes and skins is broader in product scope than any listed bill.","watch":"Watch how strict the AG's age-assurance regulations turn out to be, because platforms may respond by over-blocking or demanding contractual indemnities. That could sharply cut licensed sportsbooks' social, influencer and affiliate reach in New York even though the operators themselves are not the regulated party.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"S10092","text_chars":12217,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:29.688Z"},"NY-S2616":{"summary":"S2616 amends the definition of \"sports wagering\" in Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1367(1)(x) to expressly include wagers on annual awards or honors for individual athlete or team performance during or throughout a season, and adds season-long proposition bets to the list of authorized wager types. It conditions in-game and season-long prop bets on a Gaming Commission determination that they are not at risk of undue interference, insider trading or other integrity problems. It also permits coin toss bets that affect gameplay at all sporting events unless the Commission prohibits them for integrity reasons.","mechanics":["Expands § 1367(1)(x) to cover wagering on \"annual awards or honors for an individual athlete or team performance during or throughout a season\" (e.g., MVP and other futures award markets).","Adds \"in-game and season-long proposition bets\" to the non-exhaustive list of authorized sports wagers, alongside single-game, teaser, parlay, over-under, money line, pools, in-game, in-play and straight bets.","Limits in-game and season-long prop bets to wagers the Commission determines are not at risk of undue interference, insider trading of information, or other issues compromising integrity and fairness, making Commission approval the gatekeeper.","Permits coin toss bets that affect gameplay at all sporting events by default, subject to Commission prohibition for integrity and fairness reasons.","Effective immediately upon enactment; no tax, licensing or fee changes."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals specifically with authorized bet types such as season-long props or award futures. Most of them concern authorization and tax (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), license fees (IL HB2878/HB5034), consumer protection (CT HB05229), or prediction markets (IL HB5059, IL HB5142). S2616 is unusual in widening the wagering menu rather than restricting it, which runs against the recent trend of states curbing player props. It pairs that expansion with a Commission integrity screen, which in practice gives regulators discretion to approve or deny individual markets.","watch":"Watch how the Commission would implement the \"not at risk of undue interference, insider trading\" standard, since that discretion could produce market-by-market approvals or restrictions on award futures and season-long props (including possible college or individual-athlete carve-outs), and track whether the bill moves out of the Racing, Gaming and Wagering committee given the national push to restrict props.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2378,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:30.150Z"},"NY-S3003":{"summary":"S3003-D/A3003-D is New York's FY2025-26 Aid to Localities appropriations bill, enacted with line-item vetoes on 2025-05-20, which funds local assistance programs for the fiscal year beginning April 1, 2025. The excerpt provided covers only the general provisions in Section 1 and the start of the Office for the Aging schedule. Nothing in that excerpt addresses sports wagering, gaming taxation, licensing or problem gambling.","mechanics":["Section 1(e) lets the Budget Director withhold appropriated aid if a general fund imbalance of $2,000,000,000 or more is projected for FY2025-26. The Director must first deplete the $2 billion transaction risk reserve, and the Legislature gets 10 business days to adopt its own withhold plan by concurrent resolution.","Section 1(g) repeals any appropriations and reappropriations in chapter 53 of the laws of 2024 that would otherwise lapse on March 31, 2026.","The Office for the Aging receives $417,304,100 in all-funds appropriations: $244,080,100 General Fund, $172,244,000 federal and $980,000 other special revenue. It also receives $636,450,011 in reappropriations.","Aging program lines limit rate increases to the 2.6 percent targeted inflationary increase authorized by a separate 2025 chapter. They also earmark up to $53,000,000 and $15,000,000 for unmet needs of the elderly under Elder Law section 214.","Many line items require a Director of the Budget–approved spending plan before any expenditure, and some Senate-designated grants require a Senate resolution adopted by majority roll-call vote."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a general appropriations vehicle rather than gaming policy legislation. The excerpt provided contains no wagering-related appropriations. Any gaming touchpoints would sit in agency schedules not reproduced here, such as Gaming Commission or OASAS lines.","watch":"Review the full enacted schedules, not just this Office for the Aging excerpt, for any Gaming Commission, problem-gambling or gaming-revenue local aid lines. Also note that the Section 1(e) withholding authority could reduce payments funded from gaming receipts if a $2 billion general fund imbalance arises.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"S3003","text_chars":4477757,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:31.549Z"},"NY-S3005":{"summary":"S3005-C is New York's FY2025-26 public protection and general government budget bill, a Governor's program bill split into Parts A through BBB covering criminal justice extenders, police and correction officer hiring, victim services, public employee retirement, campaign finance, court operations and prison closures. Nothing in the supplied text authorizes, regulates, taxes or otherwise addresses sports betting, gaming, prediction markets or sweepstakes. The Senate version was substituted by A3005C on 2025-05-07.","mechanics":["Part A pushes dozens of criminal-justice sunsets, including work release, furloughs, the ignition interlock program and the VTL §1809 mandatory surcharge, from September 1, 2025 to September 1, 2027; NYC pre-criminal proceeding settlements move from March 31, 2025 to March 31, 2027.","Part C §1 adds Public Officers Law §3(9-a), waiving state residency for correction officer trainees and correction officers at state facilities.","Part C §§2-3 raise mandatory State Police retirement from 60 to 63 (Retirement and Social Security Law §381-b(e)) and widen the trooper appointment age range to 21-43 (Executive Law §215(3)), replacing the prior 29, extendable to 35.","Part C §4 raises the maximum age for municipal and DEC police officers under Civil Service Law §58 from 35 to 43, and drops the bar on localities setting a stricter maximum age.","Other Parts, as summarized in the title, cover an AI-generated child sexual abuse material offense (Part L), an office of gun violence prevention (Part MM), public campaign finance changes (Part OO), Attorney General civil enforcement actions (Part RR), and authority to close up to three correctional facilities in FY2025-26 (Part BBB)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. Georgia's HB 686, Hawaii's HB 1308 and Alaska's HB145 authorize and tax sports or mobile wagering, and Illinois HB2878 and HB3159 change license fees and taxes under the Sports Wagering Act. S3005-C contains no gaming provisions in the supplied text. New York's FY2026 gaming and revenue measures sit in other budget bills, not this public protection and general government bill.","watch":"The only provision with plausible spillover for operators is Part RR on Attorney General civil enforcement actions, which is not in the supplied excerpt, so counsel should review the enacted A3005C text to see whether it broadens the Attorney General's consumer-protection or Executive Law §63(12)-style powers that could reach gaming companies.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":336806,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:35.217Z"},"NY-S3006":{"summary":"S3006-C (Chapter 56 of 2025) is New York's FY2025-26 education, labor, housing and family assistance budget implementation bill, split into Parts A through MM covering school aid, free school meals, scholarships, housing programs, labor penalties, unemployment insurance, criminal procedure and state fund administration. Nothing in the text provided addresses sports wagering, casino gaming, lottery or other gambling, so its relevance to an operator practice is at most incidental.","mechanics":["Part A rewrites foundation aid. The ELL weight rises from 0.50 to 0.53 for 2025-26. The state sharing ratio cap rises to 0.93. 2025-26 foundation aid equals the greater of total foundation aid or 1.02 times the foundation aid base. Westchester gets a regional cost index of 1.351.","Part A replaces free and reduced-price lunch and census 2000 poverty measures with a three-year average economically disadvantaged rate and a three-year average Census SAIPE rate in the poverty count (Education Law §3602(1)(q)).","Part A phases up the BOCES aidable salary cap from $30,000 to $60,000 by 2028-29 and raises career education aid per pupil from $3,900 to $4,100.","Part B creates universal free school meals. Part C restricts student use of internet-enabled devices during the school day.","Labor Parts limit liquidated damages for certain frequency-of-pay violations (Part U), raise wage payment and child labor civil penalties (Parts V and W), and increase the maximum unemployment insurance benefit rate (Part KK).","Part MM governs administration of state funds and accounts, bond authorizations and inter-fund transfers. The text supplied is truncated within Part A, so Part MM's contents were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and FL H0189 all authorize or overhaul gaming or sports wagering. IA SF 605 and IL HB3159 adjust wagering taxes or withholding. The nearest structural analogue is IL HB2755, a multi-subject revenue omnibus, but unlike that bill S3006 contains no visible gaming or revenue-raising provisions; New York's gaming-related budget items typically sit in the separate revenue budget bill rather than this education, labor and housing vehicle.","watch":"Review the full text of Part MM, which was not in the excerpt, for any transfers or sweeps touching lottery or commercial gaming revenue accounts that fund education aid, since the visible text contains no gaming provisions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"S3006","text_chars":669242,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:46.553Z"},"NY-S3632":{"summary":"S3632 adds a new section 25.04 to the Mental Hygiene Law creating a 13-member Problem Gambling Advisory Council to make findings and recommendations to the governor and legislature on preventing and treating problem gambling in New York. The council has no regulatory power over operators. It must study the impact of mobile sports betting on problem gambling services, recommend how certain casino machine and table fees are allocated, and file an annual report.","mechanics":["Composition (§25.04(b)): 13 members. These are the OASAS commissioner, the Gaming Commission chair, four appointees each from the Senate temporary president and the Assembly speaker (each must include at least two community-based behavioral health providers), one each from the two minority leaders, and one from the governor.","Duties (§25.04(c)): meet at least twice a year under the Open Meetings Law and develop access, prevention and public-awareness strategies. The council must also recommend allocation of machine and table fees collected under Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1348 and distributed under State Finance Law §97-nnnn.","Mobile sports betting review (§25.04(c)(5)): the council must examine mobile sports betting's effect on problem gambling services. This covers hopeline call volume, outreach to resource centers, staffing needs, and growth in self-exclusion list enrollment at gaming facilities.","Reporting: an annual report is due to the governor and legislature by October 1 each year, with the first due the October 1 after the effective date.","Terms and information (§25.04(d)-(e)): appointees serve unpaid four-year terms with expense reimbursement and may vote by written designee. The council may require information from state and local agencies, excluding personally identifying or otherwise legally protected information.","Effective date: 180 days after enactment. Companion bill is A02151; the bill was last reported and committed to the Senate Finance Committee on 2026-01-29."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike NJ A 4002 and A 4003, which impose direct obligations on sports wagering licensees (account-limit rules, and no incentives for patrons using responsible gaming tools), S3632 creates only an advisory body with no operator mandates. It is closest to MD HB1409/SB862 (mobile gambling prevalence studies and fund revenue), MD HB 195 (a gambling prevention technology workgroup) and LA SR 178 (a study of problem gambling prevention). It differs from those by setting up a standing council with an annual reporting cycle and a recommending role over specific casino fee revenue. Maryland HB758 alters authorized uses of problem gambling funds directly, whereas S3632 only lets the council recommend allocations of existing §1348 fees.","watch":"Operators should watch the council's annual reports and its mandated review of mobile sports betting's impact on hopeline calls and self-exclusion, since these findings could seed future legislation on responsible gaming mandates, advertising limits or additional problem gambling funding from mobile sports wagering revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"S3632","text_chars":6293,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:46.562Z"},"NY-S3009":{"summary":"S3009-C is New York's FY2025-26 revenue budget bill (Parts A through XX), mostly income tax, credit and MTA financing changes, with a cluster of racing and gaming Parts (FF, GG, HH, II, MM, NN, OO). The gaming Parts simplify the pari-mutuel tax rate system, extend simulcasting authority, set tax rates on gaming revenues in certain regions, adjust VLT vendor-track fees, address NYRA franchise board licensing, and raise the problem-gambling earmark from mobile sports wagering tax revenue. The Senate version was substituted by A3009-C on 2025-05-08, so the Assembly bill is the operative vehicle.","mechanics":["Part OO: mobile sports wagering tax revenue dedicated to problem gambling education and treatment is fixed at $6 million per fiscal year through FY2026 and $12 million per fiscal year after that, spendable only under a plan approved by the Director of the Budget. The underlying mobile tax rate is not changed in the text provided.","Part GG: sets tax rates on gaming revenues in certain regions and extends part OOO of chapter 59 of the laws of 2021, with provisions repealed when they expire. The specific rates are not in the excerpt provided.","Part FF: Subpart A simplifies the pari-mutuel tax rate system and repeals RPWBL §908. Subpart B extends simulcast facility licensing, out-of-state thoroughbred and harness simulcasting, and wager distribution provisions under chapter 281 of 1994 and chapter 346 of 1990. Subpart C sets market origin credits and fees and how the state uses those funds.","Parts MM and NN: Part MM sets vendor fees paid to certain vendor tracks under the Tax Law, sunsetting on expiration. Part NN amends the terms and licensing requirements of members of the franchised corporation appointed by NYRA.","Parts HH and II: Part HH governs use of the Capital OTB capital acquisition fund. Part II funds research on thoroughbred health and safety, with a sunset.","The supplied text runs only through Part B, which covers the inflation refund credit and the PIT rate cuts and surcharge extension to 2032. The gaming details above come from the bill summary and title, not the operative text."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the legalization bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI SB1569, New York's bill assumes an existing mobile market and only adjusts revenue plumbing, racing taxes and casino gaming tax rates inside an omnibus budget. IL HB2755 is the closest analog as a multi-subject revenue vehicle. IL HB3159, which would earmark 2% of sports wagering AGR for fire and EMS, parallels Part OO's earmarking of mobile tax proceeds. IL HB1451 and HB2580 address problem gambling through program mandates rather than a fixed dollar carve-out like New York's $6M to $12M step-up.","watch":"Watch the operative A3009-C text of Part GG for the actual casino gaming revenue tax rates and sunset dates, and Part OO's budget-director plan requirement, since future budgets could use the same vehicle to change the 51% mobile tax or redirect sports wagering revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"S3009","text_chars":601354,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:49.970Z"},"NY-S5640":{"summary":"S5640-D amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law section 1367(8)(c) to raise the fixed annual carve-out of New York's mobile sports wagering tax revenue for the OCFS statewide youth sports grant program. The carve-out stays at $5 million per fiscal year through FY2026 and rises to $23 million per fiscal year after that. The bill also prescribes how the money is split between capital and programming, and who distributes it.","mechanics":["Section 1367(8)(c): the youth sports allocation, nominally described as one percent of the state mobile sports wagering tax, is fixed at $5 million per fiscal year through FY2026 and $23 million per fiscal year thereafter. Note that the bill title and summary say 'up to two percent,' but the operative text still says one percent.","Split: 45% goes to capital expenditures and 'the remaining fifty percent' to programming. The shares add up to only 95%, a drafting defect worth flagging.","Capital money is distributed by county youth bureaus. Programming money is also distributed by county youth bureaus, except in New York City, where 75% goes through the Department of Youth and Community Development and 25% through a not-for-profit with an established history of sports-for-youth-development grantmaking.","Each distributing entity must report to the commissioner on recipients, use of funds and impact within six months after each grant period ends.","Unchanged in the reprinted text: 1367(8)(b) keeps the problem gambling allocation at $6 million per year through FY2026 and $12 million thereafter, subject to a Division of the Budget-approved plan. The remaining mobile tax revenue still goes to the lottery fund for education, and platform providers still make monthly deposits and file reconciliation reports by the 10th of each month. The tax rate itself is not changed.","Takes effect immediately. Status: reported from committee and committed to Senate Finance on March 17, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like IL HB3159, which dedicates 2% of sports wagering adjusted gross receipts to fire and EMS departments starting July 1, 2026, NY S5640-D earmarks sports betting tax revenue for a local public purpose. The difference is that the NY bill only re-allocates revenue already collected under the existing mobile tax and does not change the rate or the base on which it is calculated. It is narrower than IA SSB 1157, which restructures tax rates, license fees and revenue uses together. It is also narrower than the Hawaii bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) and AK HB145/SB194, which build new licensing and tax regimes from scratch. Unusually, it names a role for an unnamed qualifying not-for-profit in distributing state gaming tax revenue.","watch":"Operator costs do not change because the 51% mobile rate and the deposit mechanics are untouched, but counsel should watch whether Finance amendments resolve the 'one percent' versus 'two percent' and 45/50 split inconsistencies in a way that turns the earmark into an add-on levy rather than a carve-out from existing revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":5711,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:50.945Z"},"NY-S5935":{"summary":"S5935-A, signed as Chapter 605 of 2025, adds a new section 912 to New York's Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law. It bans operating, conducting or promoting dual-currency online sweepstakes games that simulate casino-style gaming, including sports wagering, and it bans payment processors, geolocation providers, content suppliers, platform providers and media affiliates from supporting them. Violators face fines of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation and loss of, or ineligibility for, a gaming license, enforced by the Gaming Commission, State Police or Attorney General.","mechanics":["Definition (§912(1)(a)): an 'online sweepstakes game' is any internet or mobile game, contest or promotion that uses a dual-currency system exchangeable for cash prizes or cash equivalents, or for chances to win them, and that simulates casino-style gaming such as slots, video poker, table games, lottery games, bingo or sports wagering. The Gaming Commission decides what counts as a dual-currency system.","Direct prohibition (§912(2)(a)): unlawful for any applicant, licensed entity, gaming employee, key employee, individual investor or investment firm, board member, or any other person or entity to operate, conduct or promote online sweepstakes games in New York.","Supplier and ancillary prohibition (§912(2)(b)): unlawful for applicants, licensed entities, financial institutions, payment processors, geolocation providers, gaming content suppliers, platform providers or media affiliates to support the operation, conduct or promotion of such games in New York.","Penalties (§912(3)): $10,000 to $100,000 per violation, plus loss of a gaming license and/or ineligibility for one. Fines go to the commercial gaming revenue fund (State Finance Law §97-nnnn) for problem gambling education and treatment.","Enforcement (§912(4)): the Gaming Commission, State Police and Attorney General may investigate, hold hearings, issue cease-and-desist letters and issue subpoenas.","Effective immediately on signing (December 5, 2025). The purpose clause in §1 also refers to denying operations to anyone whose affiliate knowingly accepts revenue derived from sweepstakes, but the operative text of §912 contains no separate revenue-acceptance provision."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like CA AB 831, which tightens California's sweepstakes and contest rules, NY S5935 targets dual-currency sweepstakes casinos. New York's law differs by attaching gaming-license consequences and by reaching ancillary vendors such as payment processors, geolocation providers and media affiliates, not just operators. Unlike the authorization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), it legalizes nothing and only polices the unlicensed market. It sits closer to the illegal-gaming enforcement focus of FL S1580 and FL H0189, and to the gambling-device definitional fights in IL HB3733 and IL HB 2879.","watch":"Licensed New York operators and their suppliers, affiliates and media partners should audit any business ties to sweepstakes or dual-currency platforms, since 'support' and 'promote' are undefined, the Gaming Commission has open-ended power to define 'dual-currency system,' and the penalty includes losing or being barred from a New York gaming license.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":4781,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:58.259Z"},"NY-S8636":{"summary":"S8636-B amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1344(2) to require the Gaming Commission's self-exclusion regulations to include procedures letting a person submit placement or removal requests electronically. Those procedures must ensure parity between electronic and non-electronic requests in record retention and in how requests are transmitted to licensed gaming facilities and mobile sports wagering platforms. The existing duties on facilities, such as removing self-excluded persons from marketing and denying credit, comps and club benefits, are unchanged.","mechanics":["Amends RPWBL § 1344(2), added by ch. 174 of 2013, which governs the commission's list of self-excluded persons.","Commission regulations must provide procedures for electronic submission of self-exclusion requests made under § 1344(1), covering both placements on and removals from the list.","Electronic and paper requests must be treated with parity for record retention and for transmission to licensed gaming facilities and mobile sports wagering platforms.","Mobile sports wagering platforms are now expressly named as recipients of transmitted self-exclusion requests.","Existing requirements that facilities remove self-excluded persons from targeted mailings and promotions and deny them credit, complimentaries, check cashing and club programs are retained.","Takes effect immediately. The bill was substituted by Assembly companion A9113B on 2026-06-04."],"issue_tags":["integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the comprehensive authorization and tax bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), S8636 is a narrow responsible-gaming amendment to an existing self-exclusion regime. It is closest in spirit to CT HB05229's consumer-protection measures and IL HB1451/HB2580's gambling-disorder provisions, but it focuses only on the procedural channel for self-exclusion requests. Its distinctive feature is the parity mandate, which forces regulators to treat online requests the same as in-person ones in how they are recorded and transmitted to operators.","watch":"Watch the commission's implementing regulations, which take effect immediately with no lead time, for how quickly and in what format electronically submitted exclusions (and removals) must be transmitted to and honored by mobile sports wagering platforms, and the compliance exposure if they are not.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2211,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:59.358Z"},"NY-S7908":{"summary":"S7908-B amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law sections 1367 and 1367-a to require mobile sports wagering operators to conduct age assurance at account registration using commission-specified \"commercially reasonable and technically feasible\" methods, expressly barring bare self-reported age. It also directs the Gaming Commission to create a separate exclusion list of \"identifying information\" (including IP addresses) that adults, or parents and guardians on behalf of minors, can register to block account creation. Operators must refuse registrations and wagers tied to that excluded data.","mechanics":["New definitions in §1367(1)(dd)-(ee): \"identifying information\" is any data that identifies or could reasonably be linked, directly or indirectly, to a specific person or device, and may include an IP address; \"Internet protocol address\" is also defined.","New §1367(12)(k): the commission must maintain an identifying-information exclusion list, separate from the §1344 self-exclusion list, with electronic registration by individuals or by parents and guardians for minors. Operators may not permit account registration using listed data once the commission has given them access. Under new §1367(12)(i)(iii), operators may not accept wagers through accounts created or maintained with such data.","Removal from the list: registrants may rescind at any time. Parent-submitted minor data is automatically removed and deleted on the date the minor turns 21, which must be supplied at submission. In both cases the commission must notify operators and delete the data. The commission must also adopt data-security regulations covering encryption, firewalls, and access limits.","§1367-a(4)(a)(iii)(A): age assurance is required only at the point of account registration, not at each login or wager. Commission rules must set acceptable methods and accuracy levels, taking into account operator size, resources, and capabilities, cost and effectiveness, audience, and industry practice. Self-reported age with no supporting evidence is not acceptable. Age-assurance data must be deleted immediately after the check unless retention is required by state or federal law.","§1367-a(4)(a)(iii)(B): an operator must treat a person as a minor if, at registration, the person's device signals minor status through a privacy setting, device setting, or other mechanism.","Effective 180 days after enactment, with rulemaking authorized immediately. The bill includes a severability clause."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills are authorization, tax, or licensing vehicles: AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569, and the IL license-fee bills HB2878/HB5034. They do not focus on age verification. The closest analogue is IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act), which bars under-21 registration on prediction markets, though it targets prediction-market platforms rather than licensed sportsbooks. CT HB05229 also adds consumer-protection duties for gaming licensees but addresses advertising, withdrawals, and AI targeting rather than age assurance. Unusual features of the NY bill are its parent-initiated, device- and IP-level exclusion registry and its obligation to honor device-level minor signals, both borrowed from children's online privacy frameworks.","watch":"Operators should watch the commission's rulemaking on age-assurance methods and accuracy thresholds, and should also note several features of the statute itself: the broad \"identifying information\" definition (including shared or dynamic IP addresses) could block legitimate adult registrations, the device-signal mandate in §1367-a(4)(a)(iii)(B) is open-ended, and the immediate-deletion rule may clash with KYC and AML record-retention obligations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":11340,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:03.868Z"},"NY-S7482":{"summary":"S7482-C adds a new category to New York's definition of \"prohibited sports bettor\" in Racing Law § 1367(1)(r)(xi): individuals convicted of, or found in a final civil protection order to have committed, enumerated Penal Law offenses (assault, stalking, harassment, threats, sports bribing and tampering, and others) against athletes, coaches, officials, jockeys or other sports or racing participants, or involving corruption of sports contests or pari-mutuel systems, where the conduct arose from or related to a wager or the event. The Gaming Commission must set tiered fixed prohibition periods by regulation, notify designated individuals, allow limited written appeals, and distribute a consolidated list at least annually. Casinos, mobile sports wagering licensees, account wagering licensees and horse-race wagering entities must use the list to block wagers and accounts, and may rely on it in good faith.","mechanics":["Coverage applies only to convictions or final protection orders on or after the effective date, in New York or for substantially similar out-of-state offenses. The conduct must arise from or relate to a sports wager, pari-mutuel betting, or the sporting event or horse race. Disorderly conduct (PL 240.20) and harassment 2nd (PL 240.26) qualify only if the Commission determines designation is warranted under its regulations (clause C).","New § 1367(17) requires fixed, non-discretionary tiers. Tier I (class B misdemeanors and violations, e.g., menacing 3rd, harassment 1st) carries 1–3 years. Tier II (specified class A misdemeanors, e.g., stalking 3rd, aggravated harassment 2nd) carries 3–5 years. Tier III (felonies, plus sports bribing and tampering under PL 180.40–180.53) carries 5–10 years.","Repeat offenses from separate incidents run consecutively. A new offense committed during an existing ban triggers the maximum period for the higher tier, starting when the existing ban ends. No single-offense ban may be indefinite or exceed 10 years, and the Commission must report on the schedule every 5 years.","The Commission must notify each designated individual. Written appeals are limited to these grounds: identity errors, a vacated or reversed conviction or order, an expired ban, lack of a wagering or event nexus, or, for clause C, failure to apply the regulations. Designation may not be based solely on constitutionally protected fan expression.","Under § 1367(18) the Commission, with OCA and DCJS, sets information-sharing rules subject to sealing and confidentiality laws, and distributes the list at least annually with interim updates. Operators need not make legal-sufficiency determinations beyond identity verification and may rely on the list in good faith. New § 1012(19) bars account wagering licensees from opening or maintaining accounts for, or taking horse-race wagers from, designated individuals.","The act takes effect 180 days after enactment, and rulemaking by the Commission, the Chief Administrator of the Courts and DCJS is authorized immediately."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills creates a statutory, conviction-based exclusion list for bettors who abuse or threaten athletes. The closest analogues are only adjacent. DE HB314 addresses student-athlete protection, and CT HB05229 imposes account and consumer-protection duties on gaming licensees. Bills such as GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and AK HB145 contain conventional prohibited-bettor definitions (insiders, minors) within new authorization frameworks. New York's bill is unusual in tying bettor exclusion to criminal and civil protection-order records, with fixed tiered ban lengths and an operator safe harbor.","watch":"Watch the Commission's implementing regulations on list format, update frequency and matching standards, because operators must block existing accounts and wagers from listed individuals. The statutory safe harbor covers only good-faith reliance on the list, so exposure for list-lag or identity-matching failures between annual or interim updates remains uncertain.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":23526,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:05.837Z"},"NY-S9003":{"summary":"S09003-D/A10003-D is New York's FY2026-27 Aid to Localities appropriations bill, signed with line-item vetoes, which appropriates and reappropriates state, federal and special revenue funds to local governments and grantees. The excerpt provided covers only the general provisions in section 1 and the Office for the Aging schedule. It contains no sports betting or gaming provisions.","mechanics":["Section 1(e) lets the budget director withhold appropriated aid if a cumulative general fund imbalance of $2 billion or more is projected for FY2026-27. The director must first deplete the $2 billion transaction risk reserve. The Legislature then has 10 business days to adopt its own withhold plan by concurrent resolution.","Withholding exemptions: public assistance and SSI-related payments, reductions that would violate federal law, debt service, and court-ordered payments.","Section 1(h) repeals 2025 (chapter 53) appropriations and reappropriations that would otherwise lapse on March 31, 2027.","Office for the Aging receives $430,736,841 in all-funds appropriations and $671,058,513 in reappropriations. Several program lines bar any inflationary or cost-of-living increase except a 2.7 percent targeted inflationary increase authorized by a separate 2026 chapter.","Many grants require a plan approved by the director of the budget before any spending. Some legislative member items, such as Project Guardianship, require a Senate resolution adopted by roll-call vote."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. On its own, this is a general appropriations vehicle rather than gaming policy legislation. In the portion provided, the only feature with possible indirect relevance to gaming is the broad mid-year withholding authority in section 1(e). That authority could reach any gaming-revenue-funded local aid lines elsewhere in the bill, though none appear in the excerpt.","watch":"Review the agency schedules not included in this excerpt for any gaming-funded or problem-gambling aid lines and for line-item vetoes affecting them, and note that section 1(e) lets the budget director withhold such aid if a $2 billion general fund imbalance arises.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":4707234,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:11.194Z"},"NY-S9008":{"summary":"S09008-C/A10008-C is New York's FY2026-27 transportation, economic development and environmental conservation budget bill, an omnibus of Parts A through JJJ covering motor vehicle fees, traffic enforcement, utility ratemaking, SEQRA reform, insurance, pensions and bond financing. It was substituted by A10008-C on 2026-05-26. None of the text provided authorizes, taxes or regulates sports wagering or other gaming.","mechanics":["Part A extends certain DMV transaction fee increases and the related state finance provisions from April 1, 2026 to April 1, 2028, retroactive to April 1, 2026.","Part D adds VTL §1642-b. It lets New York City require an intelligent speed assistance device on a vehicle after final liability on 16 school-zone speed camera notices within 12 months.","Part D installation periods escalate from 12 to 24 to 36 months, and then run indefinitely until the administering agency approves removal. Tampering, or failing to install, carries fines of $1,500 to $2,500 and registration suspension.","Part D costs are waived for owners at or below 200% of the federal poverty level, and device data is shielded from disclosure and discovery.","Part G expands automated work zone speed enforcement to additional New York roadways.","Part Y enacts the 'Safe by Design Act' in the General Business Law, authorizing the Attorney General to adopt age assurance rules for covered platforms. The excerpt provided does not define 'covered platforms', so it is unclear whether online wagering apps fall within scope."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a true analogue. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and IL HB2878 directly authorize, license or tax sports wagering, and CT HB05038 and HB05229 target prediction-market and gaming advertising to minors. This New York bill contains no wagering authorization, tax or advertising rule in the text provided. Its only conceivable overlap is Part Y's age assurance mandate, which addresses a theme similar to CT HB05038's under-21 restrictions but through a general online-platform framework rather than gaming-specific rules.","watch":"Watch whether the Attorney General's Part Y 'Safe by Design Act' age assurance rules define 'covered platforms' broadly enough to reach online sportsbook or prediction-market apps, which would layer AG-imposed age verification standards on top of Gaming Commission requirements.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":694111,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:18.468Z"},"NY-S9414":{"summary":"S09414 adds a new Article 48 to the General Business Law (the ORACLE Act) that regulates prediction market platforms offered to New York residents. It imposes a 21+ age floor, bans catastrophic, political, death, security and athletic event markets, sets responsible-trading, advertising, settlement-source and anti-manipulation duties, and gives the Attorney General enforcement and rulemaking power. A new Racing Law §913 separately bars any entity that is licensed, would need a license, or is prohibited under the Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law from offering prediction markets in New York.","mechanics":["Covers platforms offering bid-ask speculative positions on future events to NY residents, but excludes from 'prediction market platform' any entity licensed, required to be licensed, or prohibited under the Racing Law. Those entities are instead barred outright by new §913, with fines of $10,000–$100,000 per violation, loss of or ineligibility for a gaming license, and enforcement by the Gaming Commission, State Police or AG.","§1804 prohibits NY users from opening positions on catastrophic event, political, death, security (public company price) and athletic event markets. Athletic event markets expressly include horse racing and prop betting.","§1802 sets a 21+ age requirement. §1803 requires exclusion of self-excluded persons, platform and settlement-source insiders, and persons with insider information. §1806 mandates self-exclusion, deposit/spend limits (14-day wait to raise), session reminders, HOPE NY hotline only, and blocking of at-risk traders.","§1807 advertising rules: no 'risk-free' promotions, no push notifications about bonuses or markets the user has no position in, no targeting of under-21s, and hotline display for the full duration of video ads. Operators are liable for affiliate compliance. §1808 bans gift certificates and credit card funding.","§1809 bars markets in which any market maker or liquidity provider, or any affiliate, knowingly engages in gaming in the ordinary course of business anywhere, or in which such a person holds a contract or revenue share. §1805 requires disclosed, non-proprietary settlement sources.","Penalties under §1812: up to $10,000 per violation, or $50,000 for a persistent course of conduct. Violations of §§1803/1809 carry the greater of 2x profits or $50,000. A mandatory court-ordered cessation of NY operations applies for persistent violations, with $1 million per day for operating after such an order. The act takes effect one year after enactment."],"issue_tags":["prediction","advertising","prop","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The bill is nearly identical to IL HB5059, the Illinois ORACLE Act, sharing the 21+ age floor and platform-regulation framework. It goes well beyond CT HB05038, which addresses only age and advertising. Unlike MN HF 4437/SF 4511 and NC H1171, which prohibit prediction-market wagering outright with criminal penalties, it permits regulated prediction markets but carves out athletic, political and other categories. Unlike IL HB5142, which folds sports prediction markets into the Sports Wagering Act, and NJ A5336/S4447, which tax prediction market income, its distinctive feature is the §913 and §1809 firewall that bars licensed gaming operators and their affiliates from offering prediction markets or acting as market makers or revenue partners.","watch":"Watch the operator-facing provisions: new Racing Law §913 and GBL §1809 would bar any NY-licensed sportsbook or casino (and its affiliates, parents and JV partners) from offering prediction markets, acting as a market maker, or sharing revenue with a platform anywhere, on pain of loss of the NY gaming license. Watch also for federal CFTC preemption challenges to the §1804 athletic-event ban, which could reshape how the statute applies in practice.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21886,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:20.278Z"},"NY-S9005":{"summary":"S9005-C/A10005-C is New York's FY2026-27 public protection and general government budget implementation bill, signed as Chapter 55 of 2026. It covers firearms, drones, insurance fraud, liquor licensing, ethics and lobbying, procurement, auto insurance rating, and immigration-enforcement limits. Nothing in the text provided authorizes, taxes, or regulates sports betting, gaming, or prediction markets.","mechanics":["Part C, Subpart A adds 3D-printed and ghost-gun offenses to Penal Law 265.10. Selling or sharing digital firearm manufacturing code to anyone who lacks both a state gunsmith license and a federal firearms license is a class A misdemeanor. Dealers who sell convertible pistols on or after May 31, 2027 commit a class D felony.","Part C, Subpart B adds GBL 396-eeee, which bars selling 3D printers in New York unless they have blocking technology. The ban takes effect one year after DCJS promulgates performance standards and is subject to a working-group finding that the technology is feasible. Gun industry members face a $5,000 penalty per product.","Part D creates Penal Law article 280, under which unlawful use of a drone is a class A misdemeanor. 'Prohibited space' includes the area within 500 feet of any 'large public gathering' of 500 or more people, expressly including sporting events. Part D also authorizes police drone mitigation and creates a state 'blue list' of approved drone vendors. It takes effect 90 days after enactment.","Parts U and Z change ethics and lobbying training frequency, add fees for late training, and amend lobbyist and client registration fees under the Legislative Law. These changes apply to any registered lobbyist, including those retained by gaming companies.","Part Y raises certain state purchasing and advertising thresholds to $150,000. These are procurement-notice thresholds, not rules on gambling advertising.","Each Part carries its own effective date in its last section; the act as a whole was signed May 27, 2026 as Chapter 55."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and the Illinois Sports Wagering Act amendments (e.g., IL HB2878, HB3159) all directly authorize, license, or tax wagering. S9005 contains no wagering provisions in the text provided. CT HB05038 is also a governor's budget implementer, but it targets prediction-market access and advertising to people under 21, and S9005 has no analogous gaming component. New York's gaming changes, if any were enacted this cycle, would sit in the separate revenue budget bill rather than this public protection bill.","watch":"Watch Parts U and Z, which change lobbyist registration fees and ethics and lobbying training deadlines for gaming companies' New York lobbying programs, and the Part D drone restrictions around sporting events with 500 or more attendees, which may affect stadium-based sportsbooks and event operations; note that the bill text supplied was truncated after Part F, so later Parts should be checked against the chaptered text.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":275069,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:23.961Z"},"NY-S9415":{"summary":"S09415 adds a new subparagraph (xvi) to Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1367-a(4)(a). It requires New York mobile sports wagering operators to give every authorized sports bettor a monthly account activity statement, pushed electronically and kept continuously available in the bettor's account. The statement must itemize deposits, wagers, wins and losses, time on platform, promotional credits and responsible-gaming resources, and the Gaming Commission must set standardized formatting by regulation.","mechanics":["Statement contents under new § 1367-a(4)(a)(xvi)(A)-(I): total deposits; total amount wagered; total winnings and losses; net gain or loss; number of wagers placed; total time logged into the platform; an itemized accounting of promotional credits, bonuses or free wagers used; a prominent disclosure of responsible gaming resources, including the state voluntary self-exclusion program and problem gambling services; and access to the bettor's lifetime wagering history.","Delivery: the statement must be sent electronically by push notification and remain continuously accessible through the bettor's account.","Timing: the statement is due no later than 15 days after the end of each calendar month.","Regulation: the Gaming Commission must promulgate rules on standardized formatting, clarity requirements and any additional disclosures needed so statements are readily understandable.","Covered parties: all mobile sports wagering operators. The obligation sits alongside the existing responsible-play page requirement in (xiv) and the annual problem gaming plan in (xv).","Effective date: January 1 following enactment. Status: on 2026-06-02 it was substituted by Assembly companion A10329, which is now the operative vehicle."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, CT HB05229 is the closest analogue: it also imposes player-facing consumer protection duties on licensees, but through withdrawal-of-funds rules, customer service phone lines and a bar on AI-targeted betting rather than periodic activity statements. IL HB1451 and HB2580 address problem gambling through a Department of Human Services public education program, not operator-delivered disclosures. Most other listed bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, IL HB2878) concern legalization, licensing fees or tax. Mandatory itemized monthly statements that include time-on-platform and lifetime wagering history are unusual among them.","watch":"Watch the Assembly vehicle A10329 and the Commission's implementing rules, which will drive the real compliance burden: push-notification delivery, time-on-platform tracking, itemized bonus accounting and indefinite retention and display of lifetime wagering history, all on a 15-day monthly deadline.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4118,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:25.458Z"},"NY-S9825":{"summary":"S9825 amends the existing code of ethics in Public Officers Law §74(3)(c), which already bars state agency officers and employees, legislators and legislative employees from disclosing or using confidential information acquired in their official duties to further personal interests. It adds that this prohibited personal use includes using that information to participate in prediction markets and/or mobile sports wagering platforms, and gives a non-exhaustive list of named platforms. It also makes gender-neutral wording changes and takes effect 90 days after enactment.","mechanics":["Amends Public Officers Law §74(3)(c). It covers officers or employees of a state agency, members of the legislature and legislative employees.","Treats using information acquired in the course of official duties to participate in prediction markets and/or mobile sports wagering platforms as a form of furthering personal interests.","Lists eight platforms as 'include, but are not limited to': Polymarket, Kalshi, PredictIt, Iowa Electronic Market, 'Auger' (likely meant to be Augur), Robinhood, FanDuel and DraftKings.","Imposes no direct obligations on operators. The restriction runs only to the public officer. §74(3) is framed as a 'should' standard of conduct, and the bill adds no new penalty provision.","Effective on the 90th day after it becomes law. Referred to Senate Ethics and Internal Governance on 2026-04-07."],"issue_tags":["prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue is CA AB1840, which also uses a government-ethics statute (the Political Reform Act) to address public officials trading prediction market contracts. NY S9825 is narrower: it adds an insider-information clause to an existing ethics standard rather than creating new disclosure or prohibition sections. Unlike CT HB05038 and IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act), which regulate prediction market platforms directly through age-21 limits and consumer protections, S9825 places no duties on platforms. It is also unusual in naming specific licensed sportsbooks (FanDuel, DraftKings) alongside CFTC-regulated event-contract venues, treating both as functionally equivalent for insider-use purposes.","watch":"Watch whether amendments move the obligation from officials to platforms, for example through identity screening, reporting or account-blocking duties for New York public employees, since naming FanDuel and DraftKings in statute could become the hook for such operator-facing compliance requirements.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1773,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:31.018Z"},"OH-HB209":{"summary":"HB209 amends R.C. 718.01, 5747.01, and 5748.01 to exempt tips from Ohio state, municipal, and school district income taxes. In the municipal income tax definitions, it adds \"Tips\" as a new category of exempt income at R.C. 718.01(C)(21), renumbering the existing constitutional/federal-law exemption to (C)(22). It does not change the existing treatment of lottery, sweepstakes, gambling, and sports winnings, which remain municipal \"income\" under 718.01(B)(4).","mechanics":["New R.C. 718.01(C)(21) lists \"Tips\" as exempt income for municipal income tax purposes.","\"Tips\" is not defined in the visible text, so under 718.01's preamble it takes its federal income tax meaning.","Existing 718.01(R)(1)(e) deducts exempt income from \"qualifying wages,\" so tips would also fall out of the municipal wage base used for employer withholding.","Parallel amendments to 5747.01 (state income tax) and 5748.01 (school district income tax) are referenced in the title, but those sections are cut off in the text provided.","No rate change, threshold, cap on the exemption, or effective date appears in the visible text.","Status: introduced and referred to House Ways and Means on 2025-04-02."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses tip income. The closest tax-administration analogues are Iowa SF 605, SSB 1152, and HSB 91, but those concern state withholding on sports-wagering winnings rather than wages or gratuities. The many bills in Alaska, Georgia, Hawaii, Illinois, Kansas, Nebraska, and Mississippi impose or redistribute taxes on operators' wagering revenue, which HB209 does not touch. On its own, HB209 is a state- and local-level version of the federal \"no tax on tips\" concept, and it is notable for reaching Ohio's municipal and school district income taxes as well as the state tax.","watch":"Casino and racino operators that employ tipped dealers and service staff should watch whether the final 5747.01 and 5748.01 language and any definition of \"tips\" clearly cover dealer toke pools, and how withholding and reporting obligations change; they should also confirm the exemption is not later broadened to touch the 718.01(B)(4) treatment of gambling and sports winnings.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":127739,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:37.086Z"},"OH-HB116":{"summary":"Sub. H.B. 116, the Ohio Blockchain Basics Act, deals with digital assets (cryptocurrency), not gambling. It bars charter counties, limited home rule townships and municipalities from imposing taxes or fees on digital assets used to pay for goods and services, and it carves capital gains on small digital-asset payments out of grandfathered municipal taxation of intangible income. It also enacts new R.C. 1352.01 to 1352.04 and amends R.C. 1315.01 and 5747.01 to address mining, taxation and regulation of digital assets, but the supplied text is truncated before those sections.","mechanics":["Local tax preemption: amended R.C. 301.30(B) (charter counties), 504.04(B)(9) (limited home rule townships) and 715.013(B)(2) (municipalities) prohibit any fee, tax, assessment or other charge on digital assets used as a method of payment. The ban covers charges based on that use, on the sale, use or consumption of the assets, or on receipts from their sale.","Parity exception: each of these preemption provisions still allows a charge that would apply if the same transaction had been made in U.S. legal tender.","Municipal income tax: R.C. 718.01(C)(2)(b) stops municipalities that were grandfathered in 1988 to tax intangible income from taxing capital gains on the sale of a digital asset used to pay for goods or services. The exclusion applies only where the payment does not exceed the deduction threshold set in R.C. 5747.01(A)(44), which is not reproduced in the excerpt.","Gambling content: the only wagering language is existing, unamended text in R.C. 718.01(B)(4), which treats lottery, sweepstakes, gambling and sports winnings as municipal 'income' and lets professional gamblers deduct wagering losses. Related existing text in 718.01(S) excludes gambling winnings from 'intangible income.'","Definitions: 'digital asset' is defined in new R.C. 1352.01, but that section and R.C. 1352.02 to 1352.04 are not included in the supplied text.","Status: passed the House and referred on 2025-06-25 to the Senate Financial Institutions, Insurance and Technology committee."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses digital assets or blockchain. The gaming tax bills on the list work through different mechanisms. KS SB303 imposes a 2% excise tax on the amount wagered, IL HB3159 adds 2% of sports wagering adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS, and IA SF 605 changes state withholding on sports wagering winnings. OH HB116 touches gambling only because it amends R.C. 718.01, whose existing treatment of gambling and sports winnings as municipal taxable income it leaves unchanged. What is unusual is its use of local tax preemption, with a legal-tender parity exception, to protect payments made in cryptocurrency.","watch":"Operators should confirm that the R.C. 718.01(B)(4) gambling-winnings provision stays unchanged in the Senate, and should review the not-yet-visible R.C. 1352 definitions and the R.C. 1315.01 money-transmitter amendment for any effect on accepting digital assets for sportsbook deposits or withdrawals.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":146736,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:38.415Z"},"OH-HB298":{"summary":"HB 298 would legalize and regulate internet gambling in Ohio through a new Revised Code Chapter 3771 (sections 3771.01 to 3771.14 and 3771.99), levy a tax on businesses that provide internet gambling (new sections 5753.022 and 5753.032, plus amendments to the casino tax chapter 5753), and prohibit online sweepstakes games. The excerpt provided covers only the amendments to 109.572 (criminal records checks) and part of 2915.01 (gambling definitions). The new chapter's licensing structure, tax rate and sweepstakes prohibition language are not in the text supplied.","mechanics":["New R.C. 109.572(A)(20): applicants under new section 3771.03 must undergo a BCI fingerprint check plus an FBI check for any 'disqualifying offense' as defined in the existing casino statute, 3772.07.","Amended R.C. 2915.01(C): a slot machine authorized under new Chapter 3771 or existing Chapter 3772 is excluded from the criminal definition of 'scheme of chance', carving internet gambling out of the criminal gambling statutes.","Existing 2915.01(C) electronic-device sweepstakes tests are retained, and 'conduct' in 2915.01(P) expressly covers sweepstakes. These include the 50% redemption thresholds and the rule that prize payouts above 20% of gross revenue at a location indicate valuable consideration. The bill's title states it will prohibit online sweepstakes games.","Tax: the bill enacts 5753.022 and 5753.032 and amends 5753.01 through 5753.12 to tax internet gambling providers, with conforming changes to income tax (5747) and commercial activity tax (5751) provisions. The rate and base are not in the excerpt.","Conforming amendments reach the Casino Control Commission statutes (3772.01 to 3772.062), horse racing (3769.08) and child support (3123.90), with penalties in new 3771.99. Their details are not in the excerpt."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like IL HB3080 and HB4797 (Internet Gaming Act), OH HB298 creates a standalone internet casino framework rather than a sports wagering regime. Ohio already has legal sports betting under Chapter 3775, so this bill extends online offerings beyond sports. Unlike the Illinois bills, it pairs iGaming legalization with an express ban on online sweepstakes games, a theme otherwise seen in CA AB 831's sweepstakes restrictions and FL H0189's broad internet gambling overhaul. It also ties licensee suitability to the existing casino disqualifying-offense standard in 3772.07, rather than creating a new background-check regime as AK HB145 and SB194 do for mobile sports wagering.","watch":"Obtain the full text of Chapter 3771 and new sections 5753.022 and 5753.032 to see who is eligible for licenses (for example, whether licenses are tethered to existing casinos and racinos), the tax rate and deductions, and how broadly 'online sweepstakes game' is defined, since that definition could reach social casino or promotional products offered by sportsbook operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":181648,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:42.151Z"},"OH-HB476":{"summary":"Sub. H.B. 476 amends Ohio's Charitable Gaming Law (R.C. Chapter 2915) to authorize charitable organizations to conduct online raffles, selling electronic tickets with unique platform-assigned identifiers and having the platform randomly select winners. It folds online raffle platforms into the bingo-supplies regime, carves them out of the slot-machine and scheme-of-chance definitions, and creates new registration and licensing provisions in enacted sections 2915.17 to 2915.20. The bill carries an emergency clause.","mechanics":["R.C. 2915.01(CC) splits raffles into a 'traditional raffle', which uses physical tickets drawn from a receptacle, and an 'online raffle', which is sold through an 'online raffle platform', defined as an internet website, application or electronic service, with each ticket carrying a unique identifier and the winner chosen by random platform selection.","Online raffle platforms are expressly excluded from the definitions of 'slot machine' (2915.01(QQ)(2)) and of 'electronic device' in the scheme-of-chance definition (2915.01(C)). A person operating a platform is excluded from 'bingo game operator' (2915.01(Q)).","Online raffle platforms become 'bingo supplies' under 2915.01(Z), while physical traditional-raffle tickets are removed from that definition. This appears to bring platform providers under the existing distributor and manufacturer framework in 2915.081 and 2915.082.","New sections 2915.17 and 2915.18 impose registration and license fees collected by the Attorney General. Under R.C. 109.32, those fees go to the charitable law fund for enforcement of Chapter 2915. The fees also count as deductible 'expenses' under 2915.01(GG)(2).","R.C. 2915.07, the illegal-bingo felony, is revised to except activity permitted by renumbered 2915.16 (formerly 2915.092, the raffle section). The prior blanket carve-out for charitable raffles is deleted.","'Net profit from the proceeds of the sale of instant bingo, electronic instant bingo, or online raffles' (2915.01(RR)) now covers online raffles, including the property-tax deduction for veteran's, fraternal and sporting organizations. Details in 2915.19 and 2915.20 are not visible in the truncated text."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills authorize or regulate sports wagering, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308. By contrast, OH HB476 is a narrow digitization of an existing charitable game. Its closest analogue is IN HB1078, which moves state lottery draw and eInstant games online, though Ohio's bill is limited to charitable raffles regulated by the Attorney General. Its technique of carving a defined device out of the 'slot machine' definition resembles the safe-harbor approach in IL HB3274 and HB5048 for prize and gift kiosks under the Video Gaming Act.","watch":"Watch the not-yet-visible terms of new sections 2915.17 to 2915.20, which are the registration and licensing rules for platforms and raffle conductors, and the precise scope of the slot-machine and scheme-of-chance exclusions for 'online raffle platforms', since they determine whether gaming technology vendors can supply charitable online raffles and whether the carve-out could be stretched to cover other app-based prize products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":150164,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:58.977Z"},"OH-HB344":{"summary":"HB 344 would let electronic instant bingo be offered in more locations and in more ways, and would create a new category of retailer video lottery terminals under new R.C. 3770.31 to 3770.41. It would also levy a new tax on electronic instant bingo through new R.C. 5753.022 and 5753.032. Retailer VLT operators would be brought into the municipal income tax withholding regime that already applies to casinos, racino video lottery sales agents and type B sports gaming proprietors.","mechanics":["R.C. 718.031 adds retailer video lottery terminal operators as mandatory municipal income tax withholders. They must withhold on prize awards that are IRS-reportable under IRC 6041, including prizes from lottery sports gaming (as defined in R.C. 3770.23) offered at a retailer video lottery terminal.","Withholding rules for retailer VLT operators: monthly electronic returns are due by the 10th, annual returns and player information returns by January 31, and the operator is personally liable for amounts withheld but not remitted. A successor buyer must hold back purchase money until a tax clearance receipt or certificate is produced.","Penalties under 718.031(H): 50% of tax withheld for late remittance or non-remittance, and $500 per monthly or annual return filed late or not filed, plus interest at the R.C. 5703.47 rate.","R.C. 2915.01 is amended to accommodate expanded electronic instant bingo. An electronic instant bingo system is excluded from the scheme-of-chance 'electronic device' definition and from the slot machine definition. A new 'ideal gross profit of a deal' definition is added, apparently as a measure for the new tax. 'Bingo game operator' now excludes people servicing e-bingo systems and an 'instant bingo host's' staff whose duties are nongaming only.","The tax rate, base and effective date for the new electronic instant bingo tax (5753.022 and 5753.032), and the licensing terms for retailer VLTs (3770.31 to 3770.41), are not in the excerpt provided and need review in the full text."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue on the list is IL HB3276, which creates a lottery-run sports wagering pilot using sports lottery terminals. HB 344 similarly pushes lottery-controlled terminals, potentially including lottery sports gaming, into retail locations, but it pairs this with expanded charitable electronic instant bingo. Its withholding provisions parallel IA SF 605, HSB 91 and SSB 1152 on withholding from sports wagering winnings, but HB 344 operates at the municipal income tax level. Unlike the Illinois video gaming kiosk bills (HB3274, HB3473, HB5048, HB5139), which carve out devices from Gaming Board discipline, HB 344 legalizes and taxes a new terminal class outright.","watch":"Watch whether the full text of R.C. 3770.31 to 3770.41 lets retailer VLTs offer lottery sports gaming in bars and other retail sites, which could compete with or dilute Type C kiosk and mobile sports gaming markets, and check the e-bingo tax rate against Ohio's sports gaming tax.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":179658,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:00.040Z"},"OH-HB617":{"summary":"HB 617 would exempt capital gains from both Ohio state income tax and municipal income tax by amending R.C. 718.01 and 5747.01 and repealing R.C. 5747.79. It is a general income-tax bill. Its only connection to gaming is that the restated municipal definitions keep existing law, which taxes gambling and sports winnings as income and excludes them from tax-exempt 'intangible income'.","mechanics":["R.C. 718.01(A)(1)(b)(i) and (c) define municipal taxable income for resident and nonresident individuals as income reduced by exempt income 'and any net capital gain that is not exempt income to the extent otherwise included in income'. This is the apparent new municipal capital-gains carve-out.","Under R.C. 718.01(C)(2) and (S), capital gains remain within exempt 'intangible income' for municipal purposes. Division (S) still provides that intangible income does not include lottery, gambling, or similar game-of-chance winnings.","R.C. 718.01(B)(4) is unchanged. It continues to treat lottery, sweepstakes, gambling and sports winnings as municipal 'income', and professional gamblers may deduct wagering losses to the extent the Internal Revenue Code allows.","R.C. 718.01(X) is unchanged. It continues to list casino operators and video lottery terminal sales agents as 'other payers' for withholding purposes.","The bill repeals R.C. 5747.79 and amends R.C. 5747.01 to remove capital gains from state taxation. The supplied text is truncated before the 5747.01 amendments, so the state-level mechanics and any effective date cannot be confirmed.","Status: introduced and referred to House Ways and Means on 2026-02-04."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike IA SF 605, IA SSB 1152 and IA HSB 91, which change state income tax withholding specifically on sports-wagering winnings, HB 617 leaves the tax treatment of winnings alone and targets only capital gains. It also imposes no wagering excise or handle tax like KS SB303's 2% tax on amounts wagered, and it does not alter operator tax rates as IL HB3159 and IL HB5143 would. Its closest analogues are general tax vehicles such as MN SF2374 and KY HB 757, but even those are broader revenue packages, whereas this bill is a single-purpose capital-gains repeal.","watch":"Confirm in the full text of the R.C. 5747.01 amendments that the new capital-gains exclusion cannot be read to reach gambling or sports-wagering winnings, and that it does not disturb the 718.01(B)(4) and (S) treatment of winnings or casino-operator withholding obligations, particularly if the bill becomes a vehicle for broader tax amendments in committee.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":126305,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:00.290Z"},"OH-HB642":{"summary":"HB 642 amends Ohio's uniform municipal income tax law (R.C. 718.01, 718.81 and 718.84) to remove the five-year cap on carrying forward net operating losses, so post-2017 NOLs can be deducted against municipal net profit in later years until fully used. It is a general business tax measure, not a gaming bill; wagering appears only in existing, apparently unchanged definitions in R.C. 718.01.","mechanics":["R.C. 718.01(D)(3)(a): NOLs incurred in taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2017 may be deducted to reduce municipal taxable income to zero, with the unused portion carried to the next succeeding taxable year or years until fully used, replacing the 'not more than five consecutive taxable years' limit.","R.C. 718.01(B)(1)(b)(i): a resident's own NOL and distributive share of pass-through NOLs is likewise carried forward until fully used rather than for 'the following five taxable years.' Under (B)(1)(d), cumulative deductions still cannot exceed the original loss.","Existing limits stay in place: NOLs cannot offset qualifying wages under (D)(3)(b), and the 2018–2022 50% limitation and its carryover rules in (D)(3)(c) and (e) remain.","The redrafted text appears to drop the separate 'pre-2017 net operating loss carryforward' definition in former division (SS) and its references. This cannot be confirmed because the supplied text shows no strike-through formatting and is truncated before the 718.81 and 718.84 amendments.","Existing gaming references appear unchanged in the supplied text: gambling and sports winnings are taxable 'income' under (B)(4), with professional gamblers allowed to deduct wagering losses, and casino operators and video lottery terminal sales agents are 'other payers' under (X).","No effective date or applicable-tax-year provision appears in the supplied text. The bill was referred to House Ways and Means on 2026-02-04."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. The gaming tax bills (IL HB3159's 2% AGR levy, IL HB5143 ending the per-wager tax, KS SB303's 2% handle excise, the AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569 authorization-and-tax bills) target wagering revenue directly. IA SF 605 and its companion bills deal with withholding on winnings. HB 642 instead changes a generally applicable municipal net profit tax base, closer in kind to IL HB5318's combined-reporting change or KY HB757's revenue package. Its relevance to gaming comes only from operators being municipal net profit taxpayers.","watch":"Ohio casino, racino and sportsbook entities with accumulated losses filing municipal net profit returns should check whether the unlimited carryforward applies to losses already older than five years, and how the redrafted pre-2017 carryforward language and the truncated 718.81/718.84 changes affect them, since no applicability date appears in the supplied text.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":65720,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:05.727Z"},"OH-SB197":{"summary":"Ohio SB 197 is an omnibus gaming bill that would legalize internet gambling and tax the businesses that offer it, authorize internet lottery gaming and online wagering on horse racing, and restructure other parts of Ohio's Gambling Law, including moving charitable gaming from R.C. Chapter 2915 into a new Chapter 3768. It also amends the existing sports gaming chapter (3775) and the casino control chapter (3772) and makes an appropriation. The supplied text is truncated after the early conforming sections, so the operative internet gambling provisions, including the tax rate, are not visible.","mechanics":["Enacts a new R.C. Chapter 3771 (3771.01–3771.13 and penalty section 3771.99), adds many new 3772 sections (3772.311–3772.317, 3772.38–3772.41), and adds new tax sections 5753.022 and 5753.032 in the casino-tax chapter, apparently to carry the internet gambling licensing and tax framework; the rate and license fees are not in the excerpt provided.","Authorizes internet lottery gaming through new R.C. 3770.211–3770.214 and online horse-race wagering through amendments to Chapter 3769, including new 3769.01, 3769.081, 3769.11 and 3769.99.","Amends R.C. 109.572 by adding division (A)(19), which requires fingerprint-based BCI and FBI criminal records checks for applicants under R.C. 3771.03 or 3775.03, screened against the 'disqualifying offense' definition in R.C. 3772.07; 3771.03 and 3772.132 are also added to the list of requests that may access sealed records.","Amends multiple sports gaming sections (3775.01, .02, .03, .06, .09, .10, .11, .13, .99) and repeals R.C. 3775.16, 3772.25, 3770.23, 3769.02, 3769.021 and 5747.064 (the last relating to income tax).","Recodifies charitable gaming from 2915.08–2915.15 into new Chapter 3768 and creates a charitable gaming fund (3768.23) whose moneys may be transferred to the attorney general's charitable law fund (amended 109.32); moves 2915.06/2915.061 into new Chapter 3777.","Status: introduced and referred to the Senate Select Committee on Gaming on 2025-05-14; no effective dates are visible in the excerpt."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the sports-wagering legalization bills in AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, Ohio already has legal sports gaming under Chapter 3775, so SB 197 is an expansion into iGaming, iLottery and online pari-mutuel wagering. It is closest to IL HB3080 and HB4797, the Illinois Internet Gaming Act bills, but it goes further by packaging iGaming with iLottery, horse racing and a charitable gaming recodification. In that respect it resembles the breadth of FL H0189's omnibus rewrite.","watch":"Watch the Chapter 3771 and 5753.022/5753.032 provisions for the internet gambling tax rate, for whether iGaming licenses are tethered to existing casino or sports gaming licensees, and for how the amendments to Chapter 3775 and the repeal of R.C. 3775.16 change current sports gaming licensees' obligations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":179344,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:20.135Z"},"OK-HB1101":{"summary":"HB1101 is a legislative referendum that, if approved by voters at the next General Election, would offer Oklahoma's compacted tribes a model Gaming Compact Supplement adding sports betting as a covered game under their existing state-tribal compacts, in exchange for a 10% exclusivity fee on adjusted sports-betting revenue. Racetrack organization licensees are expressly excluded, so the market would be tribal-only. Under Section 1, the referendum is not sent to voters if Enrolled HB1047 (60th Legislature, 1st Session) becomes law.","mechanics":["Section 4(A) defines \"sports betting\" broadly as any wagering on the outcome of sporting events \"or other events,\" other than horse or animal races. Operation is conditioned on federal law permitting it, and each supplement takes effect only on Interior Secretary approval and Federal Register publication, with no further state action required (Part 3).","Part 2(B) sets the fee at 10% of monthly \"adjusted transaction total.\" That base is gross handle less winnings paid, voided wagers, free play and promotional credits, \"generally accepted associated operating costs,\" and federal excise tax. The fee is due by the 20th of the following month to the State Treasurer and is deemed an exclusivity payment under Part 11(A)(2) of the compact.","Section 4(E) also lets the tribe \"keep an amount equal to state payments\" from sports betting, again deemed an exclusivity payment. The text does not explain how this works.","Section 3 routes sports-betting supplement fees through the existing 3A O.S. §280 split: 12% to the General Revenue Fund and 88% to the Education Reform Revolving Fund. A monthly carve-out goes first to the Department of Mental Health and Substance Abuse Services for problem gambling. The amended text shows that carve-out at $20,833.33, but the Section 5 ballot title says it rises to $25,000.","Section 2 amends §262(H) and Section 4(F) bars racetrack organization licensees from any new gaming and bars any additional electronic or machine gaming. Section 4(G) carves sports betting out of the criminal gambling provisions in 21 O.S. §§941-988 once a supplement is effective."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in several ways. It uses a self-executing offer of a compact supplement rather than a licensing regime, and it gives commercial operators and racetracks no role at all. Its fee base allows tribes to deduct \"operating costs\" as well as promotional credits, which is far more generous than typical GGR-based taxes. It is also conditioned on the companion bill HB1047 failing to become law.","watch":"Watch three things: the fate of HB1047, which would moot this referendum; the absence of any commercial or mobile licensing pathway, meaning operators could participate only as tribal vendors or partners; and the drafting defects (the unexplained Section 4(E) \"keep an amount equal to state payments\" clause, the unused \"net win\" definition, and the $20,833.33 versus $25,000 mismatch with the ballot title), which could draw legal challenges or be fixed by amendment.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":24170,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:20.210Z"},"OK-HB1047":{"summary":"HB1047 would offer Oklahoma's compacting tribes a model 'Gaming Compact Sports Betting Supplement' that adds sports betting as a covered game under their existing State-Tribal Gaming Compacts, in exchange for a 10% fee on a net revenue measure. The supplement is deemed approved by the state once executed and approved by the Interior Secretary, and it expressly bars racetrack organization licensees from offering sports betting or any new machine gaming. The bill passed one chamber, but its last action on 2026-04-28 was 'Motion expired.'","mechanics":["Section 3 (new 3A O.S. 280.2) defines 'sports betting' as any wagering on the outcome of sporting events or other events, other than horse or other animal races. The offer is conditioned on federal law permitting it, and the tribe certifies it will not offer sports betting until that is legal under federal law.","The fee is 10% of the monthly 'adjusted transaction total', due by the 20th of the following month and paid to the State Treasurer. That total is gross sports betting transactions less winnings, voided wagers, free play and promotional credits, 'generally accepted associated operating costs,' and federal excise taxes. The payment is deemed an exclusivity and fee payment under Part 11(A)(2) of the compact.","Section 2 routes supplement fees through the existing split: 12% to the General Revenue Fund and 88% to the Education Reform Revolving Fund, after the first $20,833.33 each month goes to the Department of Mental Health and Substance Abuse Services for compulsive gambling treatment and education.","Section 1 amends 3A O.S. 262(H) to keep the ban on sports wagering by racetrack organization licensees, carving out only Section 3. Section 3(F) states the offer does not authorize any additional gaming by organization licensees or any additional electronic or machine gaming.","No state licensing or regulatory regime is created. Sports betting is governed by the existing compact's enforcement and exclusivity terms, and the supplement takes effect on Interior approval and Federal Register publication without further state action. A Section 21 O.S. 941-988 criminal carve-out applies. The stated effective date is November 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the commercial frameworks in AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, HB1047 creates no state regulator, operator or supplier licensing, or commercial tax. It works entirely through a tribal compact supplement with a 10% revenue-share fee, and it excludes racetracks and any non-tribal operators. The Illinois bills (e.g., HB2878/HB5034's $15 million master license fee) adjust an existing commercial licensing market, which is a different posture altogether. The fee base is unusually generous to operators because it deducts promotional credits and 'generally accepted associated operating costs.' The bill is also silent on mobile or online wagering, so off-reservation online betting would depend on federal law under the 'federal laws permit' contingency.","watch":"Watch whether the bill is revived given the expired motion, how the 'federal laws permit' contingency and the silence on mobile wagering are read to limit tribal online or off-reservation betting, and whether commercial operators can participate only as tribal vendors given the express exclusion of racetracks and the exclusivity framing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":21840,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:20.316Z"},"OH-HB96":{"summary":"Ohio HB 96 is the FY 2026-27 biennial operating budget, signed with appropriations effective June 30, 2025 and most other provisions effective September 30, 2025. It amends hundreds of Revised Code sections across state government, and only a small subset touches gambling. The excerpt supplied ends early in Section 101.01, so none of the gaming-related amended text actually appears in it.","mechanics":["Effective dates: operating appropriations took effect June 30, 2025; other provisions are generally effective September 30, 2025, with some special effective dates.","The amendment list includes sports gaming law section 3775.16, Casino Control Commission section 3772.02, and casino tax sections 5753.031 and 5753.07; the substance of these changes is not shown in the excerpt.","Section 2915.01, the gambling definitions section in the criminal code, is listed as amended; the change is not visible but could matter for skill-game or sweepstakes treatment.","Lottery provisions 3770.071-3770.073, 3770.10, 3770.12, 3770.121, 3770.13 and 3770.25 are amended, new sections 3770.074 and 3770.075 are enacted, and horse racing section 3769.088 is amended.","The bill also levies and changes numerous non-gaming taxes (income, sales, commercial activity, cigarette and tobacco) and enacts unrelated policy provisions such as the sex-definitions section 9.05."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the standalone legalization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), HB 96 assumes Ohio's existing sports gaming framework under Chapter 3775 and only adjusts it inside an omnibus budget. Illinois bills on the list target specific operator economics: IL HB3159 adds a 2% AGR earmark, IL HB2878/HB5034 raise license fees to $15 million, and IL HB5142 brings prediction markets within the definition of sports wagering. Nothing in the visible HB 96 text matches those changes; its gaming touches appear limited to fund, tax-administration and definitional sections. Any increase to Ohio's sports gaming tax rate would sit in section 5753.021, which is not on the amendment list.","watch":"Pull the enrolled text of sections 3775.16, 5753.031, 5753.07, 3772.02 and 2915.01 to check whether the budget redirects sports gaming or casino tax proceeds, changes Commission duties, or alters gambling definitions in ways that affect sweepstakes, skill games or competing products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":330955,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:22.078Z"},"PA-HR60":{"summary":"HR60 is a simple House resolution, not a statute, directing Pennsylvania's Joint State Government Commission to study sports betting and related interactive (online) gambling and to recommend further regulation aimed at reducing problem gambling, gambling debt and children's exposure to gambling advertising. It creates no binding obligations on licensees but sets up a formal record that could support future legislation or Gaming Control Board action. It was adopted by the House 189-14 on May 14, 2025; despite the listed status, a House resolution is not signed into law.","mechanics":["Study scope (amended resolved clauses (1)-(4)): licensee marketing strategies including promotions, in-game advertising, ads during sports broadcasts and webcasts, and use of celebrities and animated spokespersons; loan, bonus, incentive and VIP programs offered in Pennsylvania; recommendations, 'if needed,' to further regulate in order to reduce problem gambling and gambling debt; and possible employee-training protocols addressing major losses and gambling addiction.","The Commission must also collect information on how sports betting affects the mental health and well-being of Pennsylvania college student athletes, including harassment, coercion or retaliation by bettors.","The Commission must consult qualified legal counsel so that its recommendations do not conflict with the U.S. or Pennsylvania Constitutions, expressly including First Amendment free speech, which signals awareness of commercial-speech limits on ad restrictions.","A report of findings and recommendations is due to the General Assembly within 12 months of adoption, roughly by May 14, 2026.","The recitals cite PGCB data that, as of January 2025, 95% of Pennsylvania sports wagers and 60% of slot wagers were placed online, and that more than $300 million was spent on sports-betting TV advertising in 2022."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which would directly restrict gaming advertising on college campuses, impose withdrawal requirements and bar AI-driven bet targeting, PA HR60 only commissions a study and imposes no operator obligations. It also does not reach prediction markets, unlike CT HB05038 and IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act), which bar under-21 use of and advertising by prediction market platforms. Its problem-gambling focus is closer to IL HB1451 and HB2580, which expand DHS public education on gambling disorders, but HR60 aims at industry practices such as VIP programs, bonuses and celebrity endorsements rather than public awareness.","watch":"Watch the Joint State Government Commission report due around May 2026, especially any recommendations to restrict VIP, bonus and loan-type programs, celebrity or animated-spokesperson advertising and in-broadcast marketing, or to mandate employee training, because these are likely templates for follow-on Pennsylvania legislation or PGCB rulemaking.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":7895,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:36.537Z"},"RI-S3118":{"summary":"S3118 Sub A moves Rhode Island's state-run sports betting from a single vendor to a multi-vendor market. The Lottery Division must solicit and award contracts until 4 to 6 sports-wagering vendors are operating, beginning no later than January 1, 2027. The bill also rewrites the revenue split: the state keeps 51% until it recoups its FY2025 take and then drops to 12%. Vendors get more operational control over marketing and promotions under a Division-approved written marketing plan.","mechanics":["Revenue split (§42-61.2-5(a)): the state takes 51% and vendors 40.5% until the state has received the amount sports wagering generated in FY2025. After that, the state gets 12% and vendors 79.5%. Host facilities drop from 17% to 8.5%, with a $4.5 million floor. Lincoln and Tiverton each keep their $200,000 annual host fee.","Deficit guarantee (§42-61.2-5(a)(1)(i)): if combined online sports betting revenue to the state in any fiscal year falls below FY2025 online revenue, each vendor must make an equal additional payment to close the gap.","Market opening (§42-61.2-3.3(c)): by January 1, 2027 the Division must issue an open invitation and award contracts until 4 to 6 vendors are operating. The RFP is to prioritize: integrity and responsible-gaming capability, compliance history, out-of-state operating history, and a commitment to maximizing revenue. Rules are due within 90 days, and vendors bear their own server costs (§3.3(d)).","Marketing deduction (§42-61.2-1(28)): for vendor contracts entered on or after the effective date, online sports-wagering revenue is calculated without a marketing-expense deduction. Pre-existing contracts, including renewals and successor agreements, keep the deduction.","Marketing control (§42-61.2-1(56); §2.4(e),(g),(h)): adds a high-level annual 'written marketing plan' that cannot require campaign-level detail or spend commitments. Approval may be withheld only if the plan violates the chapter, regulations, or responsible-gaming standards. Vendors may make marketing, advertising and promotion decisions, use player data for approved marketing, and honor promotions regardless of the jurisdiction where they were awarded.","Other: the Division may not make host facilities pay expenses of hosting vendors (§2.4(f)). Settlement rules now cover 'the process by which wagers are settled.' The act takes effect upon passage."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed. Rhode Island is a state-operated lottery model, not a licensing regime, so this bill reshapes the market through contract counts and a negotiated revenue split rather than a tax rate. Several features are unusual. The split is tiered on a FY2025 revenue hurdle, after which the state's share falls sharply from 51% to 12%. Vendors must jointly backstop any shortfall against FY2025 online revenue in equal shares. And the marketing-expense deduction is grandfathered for the incumbent's contract but denied to new entrants.","watch":"Watch three points in House Finance: the equal-share deficit make-up obligation (it is unrelated to each vendor's market share, and its drafting is ambiguous, comparing 'revenue to the state' with 'total' FY2025 revenue); how the FY2025 hurdle is measured; and the grandfathered marketing deduction that favors the incumbent over new vendors.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":44769,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:39.228Z"},"RI-H8186":{"summary":"H8186 would move Rhode Island's state-run sports wagering from a single vendor to a multi-vendor model, requiring the Lottery Division to award four to six sports-wagering vendor contracts through an open invitation by January 1, 2027. It rewrites the revenue split in § 42-61.2-5 so the state takes 51% only until it recoups its FY2025 sports-wagering revenue and 12% after that, with vendors rising to 79.5% and host facilities cut from 17% to 8.5%. It also gives vendors operational control over marketing, advertising and promotions, and changes how online sports-wagering revenue is calculated for new contracts.","mechanics":["§ 42-61.2-5(a): state share is 51% until it has received the amount generated in FY2025, then 12%. Vendors get 40.5% (up from 32%), then 79.5% once the threshold is met. Host facilities (Lincoln and Tiverton) drop from 17% to 8.5%, with an unclear proviso tied to the hosts having received $4,500,000. The Lincoln and Tiverton $200,000 annual flat fees are retained.","§ 42-61.2-3.3(c): by January 1, 2027 the Division must issue an open invitation and award four to six vendor contracts. The RFP prioritizes platform integrity and responsible gaming, regulatory compliance history, operating history in other states, and revenue maximization. Implementing rules are due within 90 days of the effective date.","§ 42-61.2-1(28): for vendor contracts entered on or after the effective date, online sports-wagering revenue excludes the marketing-expense deduction, so only payouts and federal excise tax are deducted. Pre-existing contracts, including renewals and successor agreements, keep the marketing deduction.","§ 42-61.2-2.4(f)-(g): vendors may make operational decisions on marketing, advertising and promotions, subject to Division rules. Vendors may accept wagers using promotions, bonuses or cash equivalents regardless of the jurisdiction where they were first awarded.","§ 42-61.2-2.4(e): player data remains Division property, but marketing-use rights shift from the hosting facilities to the sports-wagering vendor.","Effective upon passage. On 2026-04-29 the House Finance Committee recommended the bill be held for further study."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual because it would dismantle Rhode Island's state-operated, single-vendor model and replace it with a competitive four-to-six vendor market while keeping the state-as-operator, revenue-share structure rather than adopting a license-and-tax regime. The split is also unusual: the state's share collapses to 12% once FY2025 revenue is recouped, which pushes economics heavily toward vendors.","watch":"Watch the drafting ambiguities: the host facilities' $4.5 million proviso, whether the FY2025 threshold resets each fiscal year, and the stray reference to a 'commission' in § 42-61.2-3.3(c). Also watch the two-tier revenue definition, which denies new vendors the marketing deduction that incumbent contracts and their renewals keep.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":42870,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:41.924Z"},"SC-H3625":{"summary":"H3625 would legalize mobile and online-only sports wagering in South Carolina by adding Chapter 55 to Title 39 and creating a nine-member South Carolina Sports Wagering Commission to license and regulate it. It caps operators at eight, imposes a 12.5% privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts, and sets bettor-eligibility, KYC, geolocation and self-exclusion rules. The title also references a new Section 16-19-135 exempting lawful bettors from gambling prohibitions, but the text provided ends at Section 39-55-270.","mechanics":["Tax (39-55-140): 12.5% privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts. Promotional credits, free bets, federal excise tax, voids and uncollectibles (capped at 2%) are deductible, and negative months carry forward. Revenue splits 82% general fund, 10% per-capita local infrastructure, 5% Department of Mental Health problem gambling, 3% commission. No other state or local tax applies.","Operator licensing (39-55-150): no more than eight operators, each paying a $100,000 nonrefundable application fee and a $1,000,000 license fee (refundable if denied). Licenses run five years and renew at the same fees. The commission must decide within 60 days.","Operator qualifications: applicants need proof of active operation in at least five states, plus character and financial suitability shown by clear and convincing evidence. Each must name a South Carolina 'resident representative' entity owned by at least two state residents with public-service records. Applicants licensed in five jurisdictions can get a temporary license on paying fees.","Suppliers (39-55-160): license of $20,000 plus processing costs, valid five years, with temporary licenses available. 'Supplier' is defined narrowly as geolocation and KYC providers.","Wagering rules: bettors must be 18 or older and physically in the state. Credit and youth-sports wagers are barred, collegiate events are permitted, and official league data is not required (39-55-200). Bettors get one account each, SSN-based KYC, and in-state servers with cloud computing allowed (39-55-260(H)). If a bettor intentionally avoids identification, the operator must retain winnings and refund deposits.","Integrity and responsible gaming: there is an extensive list of prohibited bettors (39-55-190), with misdemeanor fines of $1,000 to $10,000. Self-exclusion periods are 2 years, 5 years or lifetime, with no early removal (39-55-220). Disputes go to a 48-hour protest, a 7-business-day operator decision, and appeal to the commission."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, H3625 is a mobile-only legalization bill with operator and supplier licensing, but it adds an eight-license cap and an unusual mandatory South Carolina 'resident representative' entity. Its $1 million five-year license fee is modest next to the $15 million master license fee proposed in IL HB2878/HB5034. Its bar on additional state or local taxation (39-55-140(E)) parallels IL HB4171's preemption of home-rule taxation. Unlike GA, where HR450/SR131 seek a constitutional amendment alongside the HB 686 statute, the SC bill proceeds by statute alone.","watch":"Watch the eight-license cap and five-state experience requirement, the politically connected 'resident representative' mandate and in-state server requirement, and whether the 12.5% rate and the free-bet deduction in the AGR definition survive amendment.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":65196,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:42.780Z"},"SC-H5281":{"summary":"H5281 creates a new South Carolina retail alcoholic beverage caterer license (new Article 15, Chapter 6, Title 61), issued by the Department of Revenue, letting for-profit caterers with a restaurant or commercial kitchen serve beer, wine and liquor with prepared meals at private off-site events. It also authorizes 24-hour special event liquor permits, allows event-host contracts at licensed premises, raises the number of permittee-sponsored free-drink functions under Section 61-4-160 from two to twelve per year, and repeals Section 61-6-2000 on nonprofit temporary permits. The bill contains no gambling, wagering or gaming provisions.","mechanics":["Section 61-6-4800 defines a caterer as a for-profit business with a restaurant or permitted commercial kitchen, primarily engaged in preparing and serving meals. A private event is an off-site, non-public, temporary event, and food trucks, residential kitchens and shared kitchens are excluded.","Section 61-6-4810(J) sets a $300 nonrefundable filing fee and a $2,300 biennial license fee. Liquor sales are barred from 2 a.m. to 10 a.m., Sunday service requires a favorable local referendum, and SLED must get 24 hours' notice before each event.","Liquor liability insurance is required for events after 5 p.m. (Section 61-6-4810(G)-(I)). Inclusive per-guest bar packages may be taxed on actual consumption or a per-guest drink-allocation method, with three-year record retention (Section 61-6-4810(L)).","Section 61-6-4820 creates special event liquor permits. These cost $35 per event or up to 15 days at $50 per day, require a SLED criminal records check within 90 days of application, and allow up to 25 permits per application in a 12-month period.","Section 61-4-160 is amended to raise permittee-sponsored functions from two to twelve per year. The act takes effect on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. Alaska HB145/SB194, Georgia HB 686, Hawaii HB 1308 and the many Illinois Sports Wagering Act amendments all address authorization, taxation or regulation of sports wagering, while H5281 is purely an alcohol beverage licensing bill. It does not mention wagering at all and was likely surfaced by keyword overlap on licensing.","watch":"There is nothing in H5281 that directly affects sports-betting operators, so monitoring is warranted only if a future amendment attaches gaming language to this Title 61 vehicle while it sits in the Judiciary Committee.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":17146,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:54.081Z"},"SC-S1001":{"summary":"S. 1001 is a South Carolina alcoholic-beverage bill. It creates a biennial retail alcoholic beverage caterer license (new Sections 61-2-400 to 61-2-420) that lets bona fide caterers serve beer, wine and liquor at off-site private events and lets event hosts charge entry fees at licensed premises. It also loosens free-drink and special-event permit rules in Sections 61-4-160, 61-6-2000 and 61-4-550, and does not address sports betting, wagering or gaming in any way.","mechanics":["Section 61-2-410(J): the caterer license carries a $300 nonrefundable filing fee plus $2,300 biennially. Licensees may buy beer and wine from wholesalers and liquor from retail liquor stores holding a federal wholesaler's permit (no 1.75L bottles). Wholesalers may credit unused, unopened stock if it is not held at the caterer's premises more than 72 hours after the event.","Service limits: no liquor between 2 a.m. and 10 a.m. Sunday service is allowed only in counties or municipalities with a favorable Sunday referendum under Section 61-6-2010. Liquor liability insurance is required for events after 5 p.m. The caterer must give the division 24 hours' notice of each event. Off-premises retail sales are barred.","Section 61-2-410(L): caterers may remit liquor-by-the-drink excise tax and sales tax on fixed per-guest inclusive bar packages, using actual consumption or a per-guest drink-allocation method. A drink is defined as 12 oz beer, 5 oz wine or 1.5 oz spirits, and records must be kept for 3 years.","Section 61-2-420: caterer licensees and business liquor-by-the-drink licensees may contract with event hosts, who may charge entry fees for charitable purposes and costs without a special event permit.","Section 61-4-160: the number of sponsored free-beverage functions rises from two to twelve per year. Section 61-6-2000 extends special event liquor permits to caterers and food establishments as well as nonprofits, adds 15-day permits at $50 per day, and requires 24-hour notice.","Section 8: the act takes effect on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. Bills such as AK HB145, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308, IL HB5059 and IL HB5142 deal with sports wagering, prediction markets or gaming regulation, while S. 1001 is purely an alcohol licensing and tax bill. Despite the 'licensing' overlap in subject matter, it contains no wagering, gaming or betting provisions at all. It is most likely in this set only by keyword or topic-matching error.","watch":"There is nothing sports-betting-specific to watch; the only tangential point is for operators running hospitality or watch-party events in South Carolina, who should note the Section 61-2-420 entry-fee and event-host framework and the Section 61-4-160 expansion of sponsored free-drink functions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":23425,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:56.882Z"},"SC-H4176":{"summary":"H. 4176, the \"I-95 Economic and Education Stimulus Act,\" would add Chapter 36 to Title 1 to create a South Carolina Gaming Commission and authorize a single, site-specific casino license in an I-95 county classified as Tier IV, issued only at the request of that county's council under a development agreement. Casino gaming is defined as all Class III gaming under 25 C.F.R. 502.4 except lottery, and the licensee would pay a 15% monthly privilege tax on adjusted gross gaming revenue. The April 30, 2025 Ways and Means committee amendment would expand the commission from three to five members, delete the \"host county\" definition, and send 97% of the tax to the general fund and 3% split equally among eligible counties, with an appropriated portion going to compulsive gambling programs; the bill was recommitted to Ways and Means on 2026-01-28.","mechanics":["Eligibility and exclusivity (1-36-10(9), 1-36-60(A)): the county must contain part of I-95, be a Tier IV county under 12-6-3360(B) per DOR Information Letter #23-18, and pass a requesting ordinance. After the initial license issues, no other license may issue for 10 years. The initial term is 15 years, renewable in 5-year increments.","Fees (1-36-60(C), (K), (L)): $500,000 nonrefundable application fee and $2,000,000 license fee (refunded if denied). An annual fee equal to the application fee is due each year of operation, and the same fees apply on renewal. The commission must approve or deny within 30 days of receipt.","Applicant requirements (1-36-60(D)): at least 10 years of casino gaming experience and at least 2 years of ownership or control of the site before the development agreement. Also required: an economic impact study assuming at least $200M in real property and infrastructure investment, national background checks on each 5%+ owner, no elected office in the prior 5 years, and no felony or illegal-gambling convictions.","Tax (1-36-70): 15% privilege tax on adjusted GGR, filed monthly by the 15th, with negative GGR carried forward. GGR is exempt from other state or local taxes, including local business license taxes, except state income tax. Ancillary sales remain subject to sales and use tax. The committee amendment splits proceeds 97% general fund and 3% to eligible counties.","Commission (1-36-30 as amended): five members appointed by the Governor, Senate President, Senate Finance Chair, Speaker, and Ways and Means Chair, with staggered initial terms of 2 to 4 years and a quorum of three. Members may not hold a direct pecuniary interest in the sports wagering or gaming industry. The commission must be self-funded from fees, with no general fund money (1-36-90), and is subject to a Legislative Audit Council performance audit after year one and every three years after that.","Integrity and participation (1-36-60(H)): suitability licenses are required for 5%+ owners, audit and compliance committee members, and persons with significant influence. Players must be 21 or older. Commission staff, licensee and vendor principals, and employees directly involved in gaming are barred from playing. Application materials are confidential and FOIA-exempt. The act takes effect on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the sports-wagering authorization bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, which build multi-operator mobile wagering regimes, SC H4176 authorizes a single land-based casino and never expressly addresses mobile or online wagering. It is closer to FL H0189 and CO SB 163 in that it deals with gaming-commission structure. However, it builds a regulator from scratch around one license tied to a county ordinance and development agreement, rather than reallocating powers within an existing agency. Its 10-year exclusivity window and its 10-year experience and 2-year site-control requirements are unusually restrictive gatekeeping features.","watch":"Because 25 C.F.R. 502.4(c) treats sports betting as Class III gaming, the bill's casino-gaming definition could let the sole licensee offer retail sports wagering for at least 10 years under a 15% GGR tax, so watch for amendments that clarify, exclude, or extend this to mobile wagering, and for how the commission defines \"adjusted\" GGR.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":36441,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:57.830Z"},"SC-S344":{"summary":"S. 344 creates a South Carolina Equine Commission, chaired by the Director of Revenue, that licenses advance deposit wagering (ADW) operators to take pari-mutuel bets on live or simulcast horse races, and makes ADW the only lawful channel for pari-mutuel wagering. Fee revenue goes to an Equine Industry Development Fund that makes grants to the horse industry. The March 18, 2026 Senate Finance committee amendment limits ADW wagering to bettors physically present within the confines of the horse racing event, shifts background-check costs to applicants, and adds a detailed advertising code (new Section 11-61-175).","mechanics":["Licensing (Section 11-61-140): ADW licenses run one year, renewable for an additional year, with a $5,000 initial and $1,000 renewal fee. Applicants face SLED and FBI fingerprint checks, and the commission must decide within 30 days of receiving the results. The committee amendment makes the applicant, not the commission, bear the check costs.","Fees: the introduced text set a monthly 1% of in-state handle paid to the commission plus an annual 5% of handle paid to the Equine Industry Development Fund (Section 11-61-140(D)). The committee amendment appears to strike that subsection and the fee-collection power in Section 11-61-130(9), yet Section 11-61-170(A) still funds the Fund from 'fees collected pursuant to Section 11-61-140(D)', so the funding mechanism is unclear.","Physical presence and scope: per the committee amendment, ADW and pari-mutuel wagering require the bettor to be physically present within the confines of the horse racing event. Section 11-61-120(D) bars the commission from expanding gambling or approving historical horse racing, slot machines, or video games of chance.","Penalties (Section 11-61-160): offering an ADW app to residents without a license is a misdemeanor punishable by up to a $5,000 fine and/or one year, with each wager a separate offense and a permanent licensing bar after conviction. Pari-mutuel wagering outside a licensed ADW app is a misdemeanor punishable by up to a $2,000 fine and/or one year.","Advertising (new Section 11-61-175): licensees must retain materials for the commission and include the National Problem Gambling Helpline number. The section also bars targeting minors, appeals to or depictions of persons under 21, saturation advertising, success or guaranteed-win claims, and marketing to self-excluded individuals, and requires an unsubscribe option. The text repeatedly refers to 'sports betting.'","Age and timing: the wagering age is 18 (Section 11-61-190), while the advertising rules use a 21 standard. The commission must form within 60 days of approval and adopt grant regulations within 12 months. Section 5's April 1, 2026 licensing deadline has already passed. The act is effective on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize","advertising","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the sports-wagering authorization bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308, S. 344 authorizes only pari-mutuel horse racing ADW and does not legalize sports betting. Its handle-based fee (if it survives) contrasts with AK's tax on mobile sports wagering revenue. Its advertising code resembles CT HB05229's gaming-advertising restrictions, but it is written for sports betting and grafted onto a horse-racing bill. The explicit ban on historical horse racing and on any commission-driven gambling expansion is more restrictive than FL H0189's broad revision of pari-mutuel permitholder rules.","watch":"Watch whether the 'sports betting' advertising template, the under-21 marketing standard and the anti-expansion clause in Section 11-61-120(D) become South Carolina's baseline for any future sports-wagering bill, and whether the on-site physical-presence requirement and the conflicting fee cross-reference are resolved on the floor.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":24457,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:07.262Z"},"SC-S444":{"summary":"S0444 would enact the South Carolina Sports Wagering Act, adding Chapter 55 to Title 39 to legalize mobile and online-only sports wagering by up to eight licensed operators, regulated by a new nine-member South Carolina Sports Wagering Commission. It imposes a 12.5% privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts, sets operator and supplier licensing and bettor-eligibility rules, and adds Section 16-19-135 to exempt lawful sports wagering from the state's gambling prohibitions. The supplied text is truncated partway through Section 39-55-270, which covers the commission's rulemaking.","mechanics":["Tax (39-55-140): 12.5% privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts. AGR deducts winnings paid, promotional credits and free bets, federal excise tax, voided wagers, and uncollectibles capped at 2%. Negative months carry forward. The tax is split 82% to the general fund, 10% to local governments per capita for infrastructure, 5% to the Department of Mental Health for problem gambling, and 3% to the commission. Licensees are not subject to any other state or local tax.","Operator licensing (39-55-150): no more than eight operators. Each pays a $100,000 nonrefundable application fee and a $1,000,000 license fee, refundable if denied, and the same amounts again at renewal. Licenses run five years. Applicants need proof of active operation in at least five states. The commission must decide within 60 days. Temporary licenses are available to applicants licensed in five or more jurisdictions.","Resident representative (39-55-150(D)): each applicant must name an SC-organized company wholly owned by at least two SC residents with a demonstrated record of public service, such as legislatively elected or Senate-confirmed board service. Supplier licenses (39-55-160) cost $20,000 plus processing costs, run five years, and are available on a temporary basis; covered critical services are defined as geolocation and KYC.","Bettors (39-55-180 to 39-55-210, 39-55-260): the minimum age is 18 and bettors must be physically in SC. Credit, youth sports wagers, and wagers from self-excluded persons are prohibited. Registration requires an SSN, physical address and email, with one account per bettor. Licensees must use geolocation and keep their wagering servers in SC, though cloud computing is permitted.","Integrity (39-55-190, 39-55-200): lists prohibited bettor categories, including athletes, team owners of 10% or more, officials, insiders holding material nonpublic information, and resident representatives. Violations are misdemeanors with fines of up to $1,000, $5,000 and $10,000 for first, second and later offenses. Official league data is expressly not required. Operators must run a dispute process: bettors protest within 48 hours, operators decide within 7 business days, and appeals go to the commission within 7 business days.","Responsible gaming (39-55-220): voluntary exclusion periods of 2 years, 5 years or lifetime, with no early removal. The exclusion list is confidential and exempt from FOIA. Licensees must make reasonable efforts to stop direct marketing to excluded persons. DMH runs grant programs and must report annually."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like AK HB145 and SB194, S0444 is a mobile-only legalization bill pairing a revenue tax with criminal background checks for licensure. Unlike Georgia's approach, which pairs the statutory GA HB 686 with the constitutional amendments GA HR450 and GA SR131, S0444 proceeds by statute alone. Its $1M license fee is far below the $15M master license fee proposed in IL HB2878. Its express bar on additional state or local taxes (39-55-140(E)) parallels IL HB4171's denial of home-rule taxing power. The mandatory SC resident-representative entity owned by public-service-connected residents is unusual and has no analogue among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the resident-representative mandate and the eight-license cap, which together could create costly local-partner gatekeeping. Also watch whether the promotional-credit deduction, the 12.5% rate, the in-state server requirement, the 18 age floor and the no-official-league-data provision survive committee amendment.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":65092,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:13.553Z"},"TN-HB2079":{"summary":"Tennessee Public Chapter 1109 (enacted as SB1992, substituted for HB2079) creates a new theft-chapter felony for anyone who, to obtain a pecuniary benefit, acts to influence whether an event occurs while they or someone acting in active concert with them hold a prediction-market contract that will pay out based on that event. It is in effect a manipulation and insider-integrity offense aimed specifically at prediction-market contracts, not at licensed sportsbook wagers.","mechanics":["New section added to Tenn. Code Ann. Title 39, Chapter 14, Part 1 (theft and property offenses).","Offense elements: purpose of deriving a pecuniary benefit, conduct intended to influence the occurrence or nonoccurrence of an event, and the person or another acting in active concert being a party to a prediction-market contract that settles on that event and pays the person directly.","Penalty: Class E felony (§1(b)).","\"Prediction-market\" is defined broadly as any marketplace or exchange on which individuals trade contracts based on the occurrence or nonoccurrence of specified future events (§1(c)). There is no carve-out for CFTC-registered exchanges and no limit to sports events.","Effective July 1, 2026 (§2)."],"issue_tags":["prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own terms, the bill is unusual because it targets prediction-market event contracts by name rather than sports wagering. It also places the offense in the property-crime title instead of the Sports Gaming Act's integrity provisions. It does not regulate or authorize prediction markets; it only criminalizes manipulation tied to holding a contract.","watch":"Operators should watch how prosecutors and courts read the broad \"prediction-market\" definition and the \"active concert\" language, including possible federal preemption challenges by CFTC-regulated exchanges. They should also watch whether this signals Tennessee treating sports event contracts as within state criminal reach, which would bear on competition between licensed sportsbooks and prediction-market platforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1514,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:14.821Z"},"TN-HB1409":{"summary":"Tennessee's general appropriations act (Public Chapter 530) funds state government for FY2025-26 and amends FY2024-25 appropriations. It is a budget bill and makes no substantive change to sports wagering law. Its only gaming connections in the text provided are funding lines for the sports wagering regulator and lottery-funded education accounts.","mechanics":["Title III-2, Item 2.8 appropriates $6,835,500 for FY2025-26 to the 'Sports Wagering Advisory Council', listed among the executive-branch Commissions (total $157,714,300).","Title III-10, Item 1.7 appropriates $504,800,000 to the Lottery for Education Account (Lottery-Funded) under Higher Education administration.","Title III-9, Item 2.1(k) funds the After-School Programs Special Account (Lottery-Funded). By line order, the amount appears to be $19,332,600, though the scanned layout makes this mapping less certain.","Effective dates are 05/21/2025 and 07/01/2025. The bill substituted for SB 1431.","The supplied text is truncated partway through Title III-22 (Miscellaneous Appropriations), so any later provisos, earmarks or fund transfers involving sports wagering revenue could not be reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The listed bills are substantive gaming measures, including CO SB 163 (commission delegation of licensing), HI HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 (legalizing and licensing sports wagering), and the MD bills on promotional-play deductions (HB291/SB102) and problem-gambling funding (HB1409/SB862). TN HB1409 changes none of those rules. Its relevance is limited to setting the operating budget of Tennessee's sports wagering regulator and appropriating lottery proceeds. It is closest in spirit to the Maryland problem-gambling fund bills only insofar as it touches how gaming-related money is allocated, but it does so purely as a line-item appropriation.","watch":"Watch whether the regulator's $6.8 million appropriation, and any provisos in the untruncated sections, signal expanded enforcement capacity or fee and privilege-tax-funded spending that could lead to higher licensee assessments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":409242,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:17.071Z"},"TN-HB2281":{"summary":"HB2281 (companion SB2330) gives Tennessee's fiscal review committee staff broad access, including direct electronic access, to all records and data systems of executive branch agencies, confidential or otherwise, where directly related to the committee's statutory duties. The definition of \"agency\" expressly includes the Sports Wagering Council (§ 4-49-105) and the Tennessee Education Lottery Corporation. Section 2 separately requires the commissioner of finance and administration to give the office of legislative budget analysis full access to the BEARS, Sherpa and Office of Evidence and Impact budget data.","mechanics":["New T.C.A. § 3-7-115 defines \"agency\" to include the AOC, all executive branch instrumentalities, and expressly the Sports Wagering Council, Education Lottery Corporation, ABC, Wildlife Resources Agency and THEC.","Access covers inspecting, querying and retrieving contracts, transaction-level financial data, and program, performance and outcome data. It is delivered by direct secure electronic access, data extracts, or on-site access (§ 3-7-115(b)-(c)).","Access is excluded only where disclosure to fiscal review staff is expressly prohibited by state or federal law, a federal grant agreement with the force of law, or a court order. An agency claiming a bar must give a written explanation within 10 business days and work toward redacted, de-identified or aggregated alternatives (subsections (d)-(e)).","Confidential records keep their confidential status, are not public records under Title 10, Chapter 7, and may be disclosed only in aggregate, de-identified or summary form to the General Assembly or as otherwise authorized by law (subsection (f)).","Agencies must respond within 15 business days unless a longer schedule is mutually agreed. They must also list all data systems and data sets within 30 days of enactment and every January 1 after that (subsections (g), (j)).","The act takes effect upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual because it is a general legislative-oversight data-access measure that names the Sports Wagering Council as a covered agency, which would bring the regulator's licensing, financial and operator-submitted data within legislative staff's reach. It does not change wagering rules, taxes or licensing.","watch":"Operators should watch whether confidential licensee filings, wagering transaction data or investigative records held by the Sports Wagering Council become accessible to fiscal review staff, and whether the § 3-7-115(f) confidentiality safeguards and the de-identified-reporting limit are enough to protect proprietary and customer information.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":8001,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:23.598Z"},"TN-HB1885":{"summary":"Tennessee Public Chapter 1117 (SB 2136, substituted for HB1885) adds a new part 59 to the Consumer Protection Act, Title 47, Chapter 18. It declares online sweepstakes casinos, other online or app-based gambling, and gambling devices unlawful, and makes offering, promoting, or facilitating them an unfair or deceptive practice enforceable by the attorney general. It also gives the attorney general a general civil investigative demand power, removes the six-month limitation period for gaming prosecutions, and expands enforcement under Title 4, Chapter 49, Part 2 (the Fantasy Sports Act) to reach non-licensees.","mechanics":["Definition (§47-18-5901(5)): an \"online sweepstakes game\" is internet or mobile gambling that uses a virtual, dual, or multi-currency system. The currency may be purchased, given as a bonus, or received free with another purchase, and is redeemable for cash, prizes, or the chance to win them. The game must simulate gambling, including slots, video poker, table games, lottery, bingo, or \"unlicensed sports wagering.\"","Prohibited acts (§47-18-5902(b)): offering wagers or entry fees to, or accepting them from, persons located in Tennessee; operating, conducting, or commercially promoting online sweepstakes or app-based gambling; selling or distributing gambling devices; permitting devices on premises one controls; and supporting, facilitating, or assisting sweepstakes or other gambling.","Carve-out (§47-18-5902(c)): lawful games under the Tennessee Sports Gaming Act and Fantasy Sports Act (Title 4, Ch. 49), nonprofit gaming (Title 3, Ch. 17), and the Tennessee Education Lottery are excluded.","Enforcement: violations are TCPA violations and deemed unfair or deceptive acts carrying TCPA penalties and remedies (§47-18-5903; §47-18-104(b)), with no costs taxed against the state. AG records are confidential (§47-18-5904). New §8-6-409 gives the AG general civil investigative demand power, with a $1,000-per-document civil penalty for destroying or concealing records.","Section 3 deletes the six-month limitation period for gaming offenses in §40-2-102(a). Sections 5–9 amend the Fantasy Sports Act: the sports wagering council may investigate and sanction \"other persons,\" not just licensees (§4-49-208(b)); the AG may investigate §4-49-205 violations under the TCPA with venue in Williamson County (§4-49-206(b)); and the body is renamed the \"sports wagering council\" throughout Part 2.","Effective upon becoming law (approved May 2026); the act includes a severability clause."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like CA AB 831, this targets dual-currency sweepstakes casinos, but Tennessee enforces mainly through the Consumer Protection Act and the attorney general rather than criminal-code amendments. It also expressly imposes liability on anyone who \"supports, facilitates, or assists\" these operations. FL S1580 and IL HB3733 address illegal gaming and gambling-device definitions through the gaming and criminal codes, whereas Tennessee pairs a civil UDAP regime with a new statewide AG investigative demand power. Unlike the authorization bills listed (AK HB145, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), it leaves legal sports wagering untouched and carves it out.","watch":"Watch the breadth of the \"supports, facilitates, or assists\" and \"commercially promotes\" prongs in §47-18-5902(b), which could expose suppliers, payment processors, affiliates, and media partners that also serve sweepstakes or unlicensed operators, and the council's new power under §4-49-208 to sanction non-licensees in the fantasy market.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":17032,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:26.409Z"},"TN-HB2080":{"summary":"Public Chapter 1084 (SB 1723, substituted for HB 2080) vacates the Tennessee Education Lottery Corporation's board of directors on July 1, 2026 and replaces it with a nine-member board. The governor, the speaker of the senate and the speaker of the house each appoint three members, expressly modeled on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council's appointment structure. It also tightens the TELC's public-records and open-meetings obligations, broadens distribution of audits, and preserves the corporation's status as an independent instrumentality.","mechanics":["Section 1 rewrites TCA 4-51-103. The current board is vacated July 1, 2026, and a new nine-member board appointed 3/3/3 by the governor, senate speaker and house speaker immediately assumes full authority, with all existing contracts and obligations remaining in force.","Initial terms are staggered at four, three and two years per appointing authority, calculated from July 1, 2026. Later terms are four years with holdover until replaced. Directors serve at the pleasure of the appointing authority, may be removed with or without cause, and are unpaid apart from per diem and travel.","Conflict-of-interest rule in new 4-51-103(l). Neither a director nor an immediate family member may have held a conflicting interest, including in a major procurement contract or a participating retailer, at appointment or in the prior two years. The only exception is an incidental holding of up to 1% of a participating retailer's stock. Directors are also barred from contributing to campaigns for the general assembly or governor.","Section 2 amends TCA 4-51-124 by deleting subdivision (a)(9). It makes the CEO's or president's hiring, compensation, evaluation and contract terms public records under title 10, chapter 7, with personally identifying information redacted under 10-7-503(a)(5). It also requires the TELC to comply with all state-agency public records and open meetings laws (title 10, ch. 7; title 8, ch. 44), except as provided in subsection (a).","Section 3 requires comptroller and independent CPA audits to go to the governor, both speakers, specified committee chairs, the state treasurer and, if applicable, the comptroller. Section 4 confirms the TELC is not subject to supervision by any department except as provided by law.","Effective on becoming law for administrative purposes and July 1, 2026 for all other purposes. Passed April 21, 2026 and signed by Governor Lee."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual because it is a pure governance overhaul of a state lottery corporation. It wholesale vacates a sitting board and gives the legislative leadership two-thirds of the appointments. Its findings expressly cite the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council's appointment model as the template, rather than changing any wagering, tax or licensing rules.","watch":"Operators should watch how the reconstituted, leadership-appointed TELC board, now subject to full open-records and open-meetings rules, approaches procurement and its relationship with the Sports Wagering Council. The findings signal the legislature is applying the SWC governance model across Tennessee gaming and may pursue further structural changes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":8361,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:27.635Z"},"TN-HJR162":{"summary":"HJR 162 is a joint resolution of the Tennessee General Assembly confirming Governor Bill Lee's appointment of Hanes Torbett to the Fish and Wildlife Commission under Tenn. Code Ann. § 70-1-201(b)(1), for a term expiring February 28, 2030. The only gaming connection is a recital noting that Mr. Torbett currently serves on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council. It makes no change to sports wagering law or to the Council's composition or powers.","mechanics":["Confirms Hanes Torbett's appointment to the nine-member Fish and Wildlife Commission created by Tenn. Code Ann. § 70-1-201(a).","Term expires February 28, 2030.","Confirmation is by joint resolution, as required by § 70-1-201(b)(1), after review by the relevant House committee and the Senate Energy, Agricultural and Natural Resources Committee.","A recital notes Torbett currently sits on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council and formerly served on the Tennessee Historical Commission.","Signed by the Governor on 2025-03-19."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a routine Tennessee confirmation resolution for a wildlife commission seat, and its only link to gaming is the biographical reference to the appointee's current Sports Wagering Council membership. It contains no substantive gaming provisions.","watch":"The only point of interest is whether Torbett's new Fish and Wildlife Commission role leads him to leave, or creates a vacancy or change in attention on, the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council, which licenses and regulates operators; the resolution itself does not address this.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":2611,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:30.671Z"},"TN-HJR903":{"summary":"House Joint Resolution 903 confirms Governor Bill Lee's appointment of Mike Keeney to the state university board of the University of Memphis for a term expiring June 30, 2031, as required by Tenn. Code Ann. § 49-8-201. The only gaming connection is a recital noting that Keeney is a member of the Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission. The resolution makes no change to sports wagering law or to the Commission.","mechanics":["Confirms the gubernatorial appointment of Mike Keeney to the University of Memphis state university board under Tenn. Code Ann. §§ 49-8-101 and 49-8-201.","Sets the term to expire June 30, 2031.","Recitals note that § 49-8-201 requires the governor to appoint six members, at least three of them alumni, and requires confirmation by both chambers.","Recitals list Keeney's other roles, including membership on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission and chairing the Memphis-Shelby County Airport Authority.","The resolution was signed by the Governor on 2026-03-18 and has no regulatory, tax or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a routine confirmation resolution for a university board seat. Its only tie to wagering is a biographical recital that the appointee also sits on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission.","watch":"Nothing substantive to watch for operators, beyond noting that a sitting Sports Wagering Commission member now also holds a University of Memphis board seat, which could matter for any collegiate-wagering or integrity question involving that institution.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":2384,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:33.734Z"},"TN-HB2631":{"summary":"This is Tennessee's general appropriations act for fiscal year 2026-2027 (enacted as SB 2690, Pub. Ch. 1142, with HB 2631 substituted), funding the legislative, judicial and executive branches. It does not change sports wagering law. Its only gaming touchpoint in the text provided is a line-item appropriation for the Sports Wagering Advisory Council, plus lottery-funded education accounts.","mechanics":["Section 1, Title III-2, Item 2.7 appropriates $6,955,200 for FY 2026-2027 to the Sports Wagering Advisory Council, within a $126,903,600 total for Commissions.","Title III-10, Item 1.7 appropriates $382,916,100 to the Lottery for Education Account (Lottery-Funded), and Title III-9, Item 2.1k appropriates $19,346,300 to the After-School Programs Special Account (Lottery-Funded). These are lottery items, not sports wagering items.","The text provided contains no sports wagering tax rate changes, licensing fees, advertising rules or problem-gambling earmarks.","The chaptered text supplied is truncated at Title III-22, Item 10 (Amendment and Legislation), so any later general provisions, restrictions or earmarks cannot be reviewed here.","Effective for the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2026; the act also addresses FY 2025-2026 appropriations and provides provisional continuing appropriations."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike HI HB 1308, HI HB 2570 and HI SB1569, which would build a sports wagering licensing and tax framework from scratch, and IL HB5443, which amends substantive sports wagering law, TN HB2631 only funds Tennessee's existing regulator through a budget line. It also differs from MD HB1409 and SB862, which redirect gaming revenue to problem gambling programs, and from KY HB 757, a revenue bill: on the text provided, HB2631 neither moves wagering revenue nor adds operator obligations.","watch":"Watch whether the $6,955,200 Sports Wagering Advisory Council appropriation signals expanded enforcement or staffing, and review the truncated remainder of the act for any general provisions that earmark or redirect sports wagering privilege tax proceeds.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":429708,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:34.602Z"},"TN-SB1431":{"summary":"This is Tennessee's general appropriations act for state government (enacted as Public Chapter 530, House Bill 1409, which was substituted for SB1431). It funds the legislative, judicial and executive branches, primarily for the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2025. Its only gaming content is a budget line funding the Sports Wagering Advisory Council and appropriations of lottery-funded education accounts; it makes no change to sports-wagering law, tax rates or licensing.","mechanics":["Section 1 appropriates funds for FY 2025-26 by title and line item across all three branches.","Title III-2, Item 2.8 (Commissions) funds the Sports Wagering Advisory Council. Read in sequence, the Commissions column gives the Council $6,835,500, within a Commissions total of $157,714,300.","Lottery-funded lines include the Lottery for Education Account at $504,800,000 (Title III-10, Item 1.7). They also include the After-School Programs Special Account, which appears to be $19,332,600 (Title III-9, Item 2.1.k).","The text provided is truncated. No visible provision amends the sports-wagering privilege tax, licensing fees or operator obligations.","Some appropriations may float with fund receipts. Examples are the Criminal Injuries Compensation Fund and the Wildlife Resources Fund, each subject to Tenn. Code Ann. Title 4, Ch. 3, Part 10 and Title 9, Ch. 4, Part 51."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a general budget act. Kentucky's HB 757 is a revenue measure, but it changes tax law rather than just appropriating funds. The substantive sports-wagering bills listed (Hawaii HB 1308 and SB1569, Illinois HB5443, Maryland HB291 and SB102, New Jersey A 4002 and A 4003) set licensing, tax, promotional-play or consumer-protection rules. By contrast, TN SB1431 only sets the operating budget of Tennessee's sports-wagering regulator. What is distinctive is that Tennessee funds a standalone advisory council for sports wagering as its own line item in the general budget.","watch":"Track the Sports Wagering Advisory Council's appropriation, about $6.8 million for FY 2025-26, and any changes in later budgets, because it signals the regulator's enforcement and rulemaking capacity, and its costs are ultimately backed by licensee fees and privilege-tax revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":409242,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:43.734Z"},"TN-SB1662":{"summary":"SB1662 would add a new section to Tennessee's sports gaming statute (Title 4, Chapter 49) requiring a vendor to pay the council a fee equal to 25% of all compensation and payments it receives from a licensee under a revenue sharing agreement. The money would go into a new \"public institution athletic program fund\" administered by the Tennessee Higher Education Commission. The commission would make grants to smaller public four-year universities for athletic recruiting expenses. The bill was deferred to summer study on March 4, 2026.","mechanics":["Section 1(a): a vendor owes the council 25% of all compensation and payments received from a licensee under a revenue sharing agreement, due within 30 days of receipt and deposited into the fund created by new § 49-7-3202.","Section 1(b): both vendors and licensees must give the council all information needed to support the fee; this information is confidential and exempt from the Public Records Act (Title 10, Chapter 7).","New §§ 49-7-3202 and 49-7-3203: the fund is a non-reverting special account that may also take gifts and grants, with interest credited to the fund and commission administrative costs capped at 5% of any grant.","Grants may be used only for \"eligible recruiting expenses\" such as lodging, meals, travel, visits and recruiting materials, and expressly not for athlete compensation under § 49-7-2802(a).","Grants are distributed by an inverse full-time-equivalent enrollment formula, with priority for the six locally governed state universities; board of regents institutions are excluded, and recipients must compete in at least three intercollegiate sports.","Rulemaking authority is effective on enactment, all other provisions on July 1, 2026, and annual reports to legislative leadership are due starting December 31, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Most listed bills that earmark wagering money tax the operator's own receipts: IL HB3159 takes 2% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts for fire and EMS, and IA SSB 1157 adjusts tax rates to fund sports tourism programs. TN SB1662 is unusual because it does not tax licensees directly; instead it levies 25% on the revenue-share payments that third-party vendors receive from licensees. It is also distinct from the college-athlete bills listed (HI HB2384 and SB3263, GA HB383 and DE HB314), which address name, image and likeness rights or athlete protection without any gaming-sourced funding.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the 25% levy on vendor revenue-share receipts gets passed back through renegotiated platform, data or marketing contracts, how broadly \"revenue sharing agreement\" will be read, and what new disclosure duties to the council Section 1(b) places on licensees, particularly during the summer study.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":6650,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:46.699Z"},"TN-SB1723":{"summary":"Public Chapter 1084 dissolves the Tennessee Education Lottery Corporation's board of directors on July 1, 2026 and replaces it with a nine-member board appointed three each by the governor, the speaker of the Senate, and the speaker of the House. The whereas clauses say this copies the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council's appointment model. The act also subjects the TELC to state-agency public records and open meetings laws, makes the CEO's employment and compensation terms public, and widens the list of officials who receive TELC audits.","mechanics":["Section 1 (rewriting § 4-51-103): The existing board is vacated on July 1, 2026, and the new nine-member board takes over without interruption. All existing contracts and obligations stay in force.","Terms are counted from July 1, 2026. Each appointing authority staggers its initial picks at 4, 3 and 2 years; later terms are 4 years. Directors serve at the pleasure of whoever appointed them and can be removed with or without cause.","Section 1(l) covers conflicts and contributions. A director or immediate family member cannot hold, at appointment or in the prior two years, any conflicting interest, including in a major procurement contract or a participating retailer. The one exception is an incidental stake of up to 1% of a participating retailer's stock. Directors are also barred from contributing to campaigns for governor or the General Assembly.","Section 2 (amending § 4-51-124): Deletes subdivision (a)(9). Makes records on the CEO or president's hiring, retention, performance evaluation, compensation and contract terms public, with personally identifying information redacted. Requires the corporation to comply with public records (Title 10, Ch. 7) and open meetings (Title 8, Ch. 44) laws as a state agency would, except as provided in subsection (a).","Section 3 sends comptroller and independent CPA audits to the governor, both speakers, the relevant government and finance committee chairs, the state treasurer and, if applicable, the comptroller. Section 4 confirms TELC remains an independent instrumentality.","Effective upon becoming law for administrative purposes (05/22/2026) and July 1, 2026 for all other purposes."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual because it removes a state lottery's board wholesale and splits appointment power between the governor and both legislative speakers. Each director can be removed at will by the official who appointed them. This mirrors Tennessee's own Sports Wagering Council model rather than any multistate template, and pairs that political-control shift with full open-records and open-meetings coverage.","watch":"The act itself contains no sports-wagering provisions, but operators should watch whether the reconstituted, legislatively appointed TELC board, now under open-meetings and records rules, becomes a vehicle for future proposals to bring sports wagering or other gaming back within the lottery's orbit, or a precedent for further restructuring of the Sports Wagering Council.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":8361,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:47.715Z"},"TN-SB1831":{"summary":"SB1831 requires Tennessee interactive sports wagering licensees to geofence out wagering on the campuses of public colleges and universities that field sanctioned athletics. That includes off-campus venues used for sanctioned collegiate sporting events, which must be blocked for the entire calendar day of the event. It separately requires those public institutions to block access to interactive sports wagering through any server or network they operate on campus.","mechanics":["New TCA 4-49-134(a): a licensee must prohibit access to interactive sports wagering on the campus of a covered institution of higher education.","\"Campus\" (4-49-134(c)(1)(A)) means the primary contiguous campus, plus any non-contiguous parcel or satellite facility within one mile of its boundary used for student residential or communal purposes. Parcels used solely for classrooms, maintenance, administration or storage are excluded.","\"Campus\" also includes (c)(1)(B) every in-state sports venue used by the institution for a sanctioned collegiate sporting event governed by a sports governing body, whether or not the institution owns it. Under (b), blocking at these venues applies for the entire calendar day of the event, and \"sports venue\" extends to contiguous or ancillary parking and event-related property controlled by the venue (c)(3).","Covered institutions are limited to public institutions of higher education that sanction at least one collegiate sport governed by a sports governing body; private colleges are not covered.","New TCA 49-7-194: covered public institutions providing internet access must block interactive sports wagering, as defined in 4-49-102, on their campus servers and wired or wireless networks.","The act takes effect July 1, 2026. It contains no specific penalty provision, so enforcement would fall to the existing Sports Wagering Council authority under Title 4, Chapter 49."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Connecticut HB05229 also targets college campuses, but it restricts gaming advertising there rather than mandating geolocation blocking of wagering itself. SB1831 goes further, imposing an affirmative geofencing duty on operators and a network-blocking duty on the universities themselves. Unusually, it treats off-campus venues hosting collegiate games, such as NFL stadiums or arenas, as campus for the whole event day. None of the listed Illinois, Hawaii, Alaska or Georgia bills contains a comparable campus wagering ban.","watch":"Watch the geofencing compliance burden. Operators would have to map one-mile satellite residential parcels and set full-day blocks at every in-state venue hosting a sanctioned college event, including non-university stadiums and their parking areas, and the absent penalty and safe-harbor language could leave operators exposed to Council discipline for geolocation edge cases.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":3680,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:48.433Z"},"TN-SB1992":{"summary":"Tennessee Public Chapter 1109 creates a new criminal offense in Title 39, Chapter 14, Part 1 for anyone who, to obtain a pecuniary benefit, engages in conduct intended to influence whether an event occurs while that person, or someone acting in active concert with them, holds a prediction-market contract that will pay out based on that event. A violation is a Class E felony. The law takes effect July 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Offense (Section 1(a)): conduct intended to influence the occurrence or nonoccurrence of an event, done to derive a pecuniary benefit, while the actor or someone in active concert with the actor is a party to a prediction-market contract that will pay the actor directly on settlement based on that event.","Penalty (Section 1(b)): Class E felony, the lowest felony class in Tennessee.","Definition (Section 1(c)): a 'prediction-market' is a marketplace or exchange on which individuals trade contracts based on the occurrence or nonoccurrence of specified future events. The definition is not limited to sports or to CFTC-registered exchanges.","Covered persons: any person, including athletes, officials, insiders and their co-conspirators ('active concert'). It does not regulate the operators or exchanges themselves.","Effective date (Section 2): July 1, 2026. The bill was signed after passing the Senate on April 21, 2026, and was substituted for HB 2079."],"issue_tags":["prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own terms, the law is unusual because it criminalizes event manipulation specifically tied to prediction-market contracts, rather than to traditional sportsbook wagers. It does so in Tennessee's property-offense chapter, not in the Sports Gaming Act. The law does not license, prohibit or tax prediction markets. It reaches only the manipulator who stands to profit from settlement, and it uses a broad event definition that covers non-sports events such as elections or corporate outcomes.","watch":"Operators should note that Tennessee is treating prediction-market contracts as a distinct product in its criminal code, which could foreshadow regulation or a ban, and should watch how prosecutors read 'active concert' and 'directly from the settlement,' since those terms decide whether tipsters, syndicates, or counterparties on licensed sportsbooks fall outside this statute and under existing Sports Gaming Act integrity provisions instead.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1514,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:55.973Z"},"TN-SB2330":{"summary":"SB2330 adds a new TCA § 3-7-115 giving the staff of the legislature's Fiscal Review Committee access to all agency information and records, confidential or otherwise, including direct electronic access to agency data systems, and expressly lists the Sports Wagering Council and the Tennessee Education Lottery Corporation as covered agencies. It also amends § 3-14-202(h) to give the Office of Legislative Budget Analysis full access to the BEARS and Sherpa budget systems and to data held by the Office of Evidence and Impact. It does not regulate wagering itself; the gaming link is that the Sports Wagering Council's records, potentially including licensee data, become reachable by legislative staff.","mechanics":["Covered 'agency' (§ 3-7-115(a)(1)) includes the Administrative Office of the Courts, executive branch instrumentalities, and expressly the Sports Wagering Council (§ 4-49-105) and the Tennessee Education Lottery Corporation (§ 4-51-101).","Access under subsection (c) covers contracts, transaction-level financial data and performance data. Agencies must provide it through direct secure electronic access, data extracts, or on-site access, within 15 business days (subsection (g)) unless both sides agree to an alternative schedule.","Under subsection (d), access may be withheld only where disclosure to committee staff is expressly prohibited by state or federal law, a federal grant agreement with the force of law, or court order. Under subsection (e), an agency claiming a bar must explain the legal basis in writing within 10 business days and work toward redacted, de-identified, aggregated or limited-access alternatives.","Under subsection (f), confidential records keep their confidential status, are not public records under Title 10, Chapter 7, may be used only for committee duties, and may be disclosed only in aggregate or de-identified form to the General Assembly or as otherwise authorized by law.","Under subsection (j), every agency must give the committee an inventory of all its data systems and data sets within 30 days of the effective date, and annually by January 1 after that.","The act takes effect upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that a general legislative-oversight data-access bill singles out the Sports Wagering Council by name as a covered agency, potentially opening its regulatory files and transaction-level data to Fiscal Review Committee staff. That access would apply notwithstanding contrary law, except where disclosure to committee staff is expressly prohibited.","watch":"Watch whether the Sports Wagering Council's existing confidentiality provisions for licensee financial, wagering-transaction and integrity-monitoring data under Title 4, Chapter 49 count as an express prohibition under subsection (d); if they do not, operators' proprietary data submitted to the council could flow to legislative staff, protected only by the subsection (f) confidentiality safeguards.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":7997,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:58.510Z"},"TN-SJR631":{"summary":"Senate Joint Resolution 631 confirms Governor Bill Lee's appointment of Mike Keeney to the state university board of the University of Memphis for a term expiring June 30, 2031, as required by Tenn. Code Ann. 49-8-201. Its only connection to gaming is a recital noting that Keeney currently serves as a member of the Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission.","mechanics":["Confirms the gubernatorial appointment of Mike Keeney to the University of Memphis state university board under Tenn. Code Ann. 49-8-101 and 49-8-201.","Term of the appointment expires June 30, 2031.","Joint resolution requires Senate adoption with House concurrence, per the statutory confirmation requirement in 49-8-201.","A recital lists Keeney's membership on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission among his civic roles, but the resolution makes no change to that commission or to any wagering law."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The resolution is a routine confirmation of a university board appointment. It is notable only because it records that a sitting Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission member also holds several other public posts, including chair of the Memphis-Shelby County Airport Authority.","watch":"Nothing in the resolution affects wagering regulation, though operators may want to note that Commissioner Keeney's additional board role adds to his outside commitments, which could matter for potential recusal or conflict questions at the Sports Wagering Commission.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":2385,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:02.197Z"},"TN-SJR99":{"summary":"Senate Joint Resolution 99 confirms Governor Bill Lee's appointment of Hanes Torbett to the Tennessee Fish and Wildlife Commission for a term expiring February 28, 2030, as required by Tenn. Code Ann. § 70-1-201(b)(1). Its only connection to sports betting is a recital noting that Torbett currently serves on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council. The resolution makes no change to wagering law or regulation.","mechanics":["Confirms Hanes Torbett's appointment to the Fish and Wildlife Commission under Tenn. Code Ann. § 70-1-201.","The term expires February 28, 2030.","Confirmation requires approval by the relevant House committee, the Senate Energy, Agriculture and Natural Resources Committee, and a joint resolution of the General Assembly (§ 70-1-201(b)(1)).","A recital states that Torbett currently serves on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council. Nothing in the text says he must leave that seat.","On 2025-03-10 the companion bill, HJR 162, was substituted for this SJR."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The resolution is a routine confirmation of a wildlife commissioner. It is notable only because it shows a sitting Sports Wagering Council member taking on a second state appointment.","watch":"Nothing here affects operators directly, but counsel may want to note whether Torbett keeps his Sports Wagering Council seat alongside the new commission role, since that bears on council composition and any future vacancy.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":2609,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:03.958Z"},"TN-SB2690":{"summary":"Tennessee's general appropriations act (Public Chapter 1142) for fiscal year 2026-27, which funds the legislative, judicial and executive branches and sets conditions on spending. Sports wagering comes up only as one budget line: an appropriation to the Sports Wagering Advisory Council, the state's sports betting regulatory body, among the executive commissions in Title III-2.","mechanics":["Section 1, Title III-2, Item 2.7 appropriates $6,955,200 for FY 2026-27 to the Sports Wagering Advisory Council, out of $126,903,600 for all executive commissions.","Lottery-funded education lines are separate from the sports wagering line: Title III-9, Item 2.1k After-School Programs Special Account at $19,346,300, and Title III-10, Item 1.7 Lottery for Education Account at $382,916,100.","Item 2.12 appropriates $4,860,400 to the Alcoholic Beverage Commission, which sits alongside the Council in the commissions list.","The act was signed into law with effective dates of May 22, 2026 and July 1, 2026; the appropriations schedule covers the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2026.","The supplied text is truncated partway through Title III-22 (Miscellaneous Appropriations, Item 10, Amendment and Legislation), so any later sections or earmarks touching gaming revenue could not be reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a general appropriations act. KY HB 757 is the nearest analogue as an omnibus revenue measure, but it changes tax law, whereas this bill only funds agencies. Bills such as MD HB1409/SB862 and MD HB758 redirect gaming fund revenue or uses, while TN SB2690, in the portion reviewed, only sets the Council's operating budget. Unlike HI HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569, it makes no change to licensing, tax rates or regulatory structure.","watch":"Track whether the Council's $6,955,200 appropriation supports or limits its enforcement, licensing and rulemaking capacity, and review the untruncated remainder of the act for any earmark or diversion of sports wagering privilege tax revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":429708,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:06.270Z"},"TN-SB2136":{"summary":"Tennessee Public Chapter 1117 creates a new civil enforcement part in the Consumer Protection Act (Tenn. Code 47-18-5901 to -5904) declaring online sweepstakes casinos, other online or app-based gambling and gambling devices unlawful, and makes violations unfair or deceptive acts enforceable by the attorney general. It also gives the attorney general a general civil investigative demand power (new 8-6-409), removes the six-month statute of limitations for gaming prosecutions, and expands the Sports Wagering Council's and the AG's enforcement reach under the Sports Gaming Act to unlicensed 'other persons.'","mechanics":["'Online sweepstakes game' (47-18-5901(5)) means internet gambling using a virtual, dual or multi-currency system where coins are purchased, given as a bonus, or received free with another purchase, can be exchanged for cash or prizes, and simulate casino games, lottery, bingo or 'unlicensed sports wagering.'","Covered conduct (47-18-5902(b)) includes offering or accepting wagers from persons in Tennessee, operating or 'commercially promoting' online sweepstakes or app-based gambling, selling or distributing gambling devices, permitting devices on controlled premises, and anyone who 'supports, facilitates, or assists' such operations. Lawful activity under the Sports Gaming Act, Fantasy Sports Act, nonprofit gaming laws and the education lottery is carved out (47-18-5902(c)).","Violations are per se TCPA violations and are added to the 47-18-104(b) list (Section 2), with TCPA penalties and remedies. No costs may be taxed against the state, and AG investigative records are exempt from the Public Records Act even when shared with other agencies.","Section 3 deletes the six-month limitations period for gaming offenses in 40-2-102(a). Section 4 (8-6-409) authorizes AG civil investigative demands, sworn examinations and court orders, including ex parte preservation orders against persons leaving the state, with a civil penalty of up to $1,000 per document for spoliation.","Sections 5-9 amend Title 4, Chapter 49. The council may investigate and sanction 'a licensee or other person' (4-49-208(b)), and the AG may investigate violations of 4-49-205 under the TCPA with venue in Williamson County (4-49-206(b)). The 'sports wagering advisory council' is renamed the 'sports wagering council.'","The act takes effect on becoming law, recorded as effective 05/22/2026, and includes a severability clause."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like CA AB 831, which targets contests and sweepstakes using dual-currency mechanics, TN SB2136 bans online sweepstakes casinos. Tennessee relies on Consumer Protection Act civil enforcement by the attorney general rather than a gambling-code prohibition. It also reaches anyone who 'supports, facilitates, or assists' an operator. Unlike FL S1580 and FL H0189, which revise criminal gambling penalties and regulator authority, Tennessee pairs the ban with a broad new AG investigative-demand statute and removes the gaming limitations period. It does not expressly address prediction markets, unlike IL HB5142.","watch":"Operators and their B2B suppliers, payment processors, affiliates and marketers should watch how broadly the AG and the Sports Wagering Council read 'supports, facilitates, or assists' and 'commercially promotes,' since that language, the council's new jurisdiction over 'other persons,' and the reference to 'unlicensed sports wagering' could reach vendors and offshore or prediction-market-style products. They should also note that confidential AG investigative demands, backed by $1,000-per-document penalties, are now available.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":17032,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:09.864Z"},"TX-SB844":{"summary":"SB 844 rewrites the Texas municipal zoning protest procedure in Local Government Code Chapter 211, exempting \"proposed comprehensive zoning changes\" that increase residential density from the landowner protest/supermajority mechanism and setting new protest thresholds for all other zoning changes. Its only gaming-relevant provision keeps a low 20 percent neighbor-protest trigger, and with it a three-fourths council supermajority, for any zoning change that would allow a building to be used for any form of gambling or sports wagering. The bill also adds website notice for zoning hearings and a 60-day conclusive presumption of validity for density-increasing changes.","mechanics":["New Sec. 211.0061(b): a written protest triggers heightened voting if signed by owners of at least 20% of the land covered by the change, or at least 60% of land within 200 feet of it, or only 20% of the adjoining 200-foot area if the change would allow use of a building or structure for the operation of any form of gambling or sports wagering.","Sec. 211.0061(d): a protest by 20% of covered owners or a gambling/sports-wagering-related protest requires approval by three-fourths of all members of the governing body; a general 60% adjoining-owner protest requires only a majority of all members.","Sec. 211.0011 defines \"proposed comprehensive zoning change\" (density-increasing uniform district changes, citywide new codes or maps, and density-increasing overlays along major roadway, highway or transit corridors), which are excluded from the protest procedure and receive notice-only treatment under Sec. 211.0063.","Sec. 211.006(a) amended to require hearing notice, before the 15th day before the hearing, in a newspaper and on the municipal website if one exists; land area for protests is calculated in aggregate including streets and alleys (Sec. 211.0061(c)).","Sec. 211.0065: density-increasing zoning changes are conclusively presumed valid unless challenged before the 60th day after their effective date; former Secs. 211.006(d) and (f) are repealed.","Applies to proposals made on or after the September 1, 2025 effective date."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that a housing-density zoning reform singles out gambling and sports wagering facilities for preserved local veto leverage: those projects keep the 20% neighbor-protest trigger and three-fourths supermajority, while other non-comprehensive changes need a 60% neighbor protest and only a simple majority. Texas does not currently authorize casinos or sports wagering, so the provision functions as a pre-emptive land-use barrier should legalization occur.","watch":"If Texas later legalizes casinos or sports betting, Sec. 211.0061(b)(3) would let owners of just 20% of land within 200 feet of a proposed site force a three-fourths council supermajority for any rezoning that would permit a retail sportsbook, casino or other gambling facility, so operators should track the broad undefined phrase \"any form of gambling or sports wagering\" and its interaction with any future enabling legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":8726,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:14.739Z"},"TX-HB126":{"summary":"HB126 amends Texas Education Code Section 51.9246 so Texas institutions can pay student athletes directly for name, image and likeness (NIL) and can offer NIL compensation to recruits. It also lets institutions, athletes and others take any action authorized by an athletic association, a conference, or a final court order (aimed at the House v. NCAA settlement framework), subject to those bodies' enforcement rules. The existing ban on NIL contracts endorsing sports betting and casino gambling is kept and renumbered.","mechanics":["Section 51.9246(c) deletes the old bar on institutions providing or soliciting NIL compensation to prospective student athletes. (g)(2)(B) deletes the bars on compensation paid by the institution, for accepting an admission offer, or for acts during official team activities. Compensation in exchange for athletic performance is still prohibited.","Section 51.9246(g)(2)(B)(ii) retains the prohibition on a student athlete entering an NIL contract that pays for endorsing alcohol, tobacco, e-cigarettes or other nicotine devices, anabolic steroids, sports betting, casino gambling, firearms the athlete cannot legally buy, or sexually oriented businesses.","New (c-2) and (c-3) apply where state law conflicts with association, conference or court-order requirements. Institutions, athletes and other parties may act as those bodies authorize or a final court order requires, but they remain subject to those bodies' rules and enforcement provisions.","New (k-1) bars any individual, company or institution from entering an NIL arrangement with a prospective student athlete under 17, or with that athlete's relatives by blood or marriage, unless the athlete is enrolled at an institution of higher education.","Section 2 repeals Subsections (g-1) and (j); their content is not reproduced in the text.","The changes apply only to NIL compensation paid on or after the effective date. The bill passed with two-thirds votes in both houses and took immediate effect, recorded as of 2025-06-05."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like HI HB 2384, HI SB3263, WI AB1034, NE LB370 and LA HB 513, HB126 is mainly an NIL statute aligning state law with the post-settlement model of direct institutional payments. Its only gaming content is the carried-over ban on sports-betting and casino endorsements. By contrast, MD HB518 and CT HB05229 regulate operators directly, through college-athlete protections and problem-gambling rules in Maryland and campus advertising limits in Connecticut. NC H828 and LA SB354 target prop bets on college and amateur athletes. HB126 creates no operator obligations or wagering-market restrictions and does not touch Texas's lack of a legal sports betting regime.","watch":"Operators and their affiliates still cannot use NIL deals with student athletes at covered Texas institutions to promote sports betting or casino gambling. Watch whether conference or settlement rules adopted under the new (c-2) carve-out, or future amendments, add further gambling-related restrictions on athletes or institutional sponsorships.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":11884,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:19.311Z"},"UT-HB243":{"summary":"HB 243 amends Utah's criminal gambling definitions in Utah Code 76-9-1401 to add a new defined term, \"proposition bet,\" and to state expressly that a proposition bet is included within \"gambling.\" Because Utah prohibits essentially all gambling, the practical effect is to remove any argument that wagers on individual actions or statistics fall outside the prohibition. The listed status of \"Failed / Dead\" conflicts with the last action, which records the Governor's signature filed with the Lieutenant Governor on 2026-03-25, so the bill appears to have been enacted and the status should be verified.","mechanics":["New Subsection 76-9-1401(17) defines \"proposition bet\" as \"a gambling bet on an individual action, statistic, occurrence, or non-occurrence.\"","Subsection (8)(b) now provides that \"gambling\" includes (i) a lottery and (ii) a proposition bet.","The core definition of gambling in Subsection (8)(a) is unchanged: risking anything of value on an outcome based on an element of chance under an agreement that someone receives value on a certain outcome, regardless of preview or pre-reveal features.","\"Gambling bet\" in Subsection (9) already covers money, checks, credit, or any other representation of value. The existing \"online gambling\" definition in Subsection (14) reaches internet and mobile-device play, so online prop wagering is captured.","The remaining changes are renumbering of Subsections (17) through (21) plus stylistic edits, such as \"internet\" and the Utah Constitution Article VI, Section 27 citation format.","The bill takes effect May 6, 2026, with no appropriation and no new penalties. Enforcement runs through the existing criminal provisions of Title 76, Chapter 9, Part 14."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. Most state prop-bet legislation operates inside a legal sports-betting framework, banning or restricting particular prop markets such as college player props for integrity reasons. Utah's bill is unusual because it works through a total criminal prohibition: it codifies that any proposition bet is gambling. The definition is broad enough to reach player-stat contests and potentially event-style contracts on any \"occurrence or non-occurrence,\" though the text does not mention daily fantasy, pick'em, or prediction markets by name.","watch":"Watch whether Utah prosecutors or the Attorney General use the broad \"individual action, statistic, occurrence, or non-occurrence\" language against pick'em-style fantasy products and CFTC-regulated event contracts offered to Utah residents online, which would raise skill-versus-chance and federal preemption disputes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":11577,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:24.149Z"},"VA-HB1001":{"summary":"House Education Committee substitute for HB1001 that would create a Virginia College Access and Affordability Scholarship Fund under the State Council of Higher Education for Virginia, in new Article 5 (§§ 23.1-235 through 23.1-237) of Chapter 2 of Title 23.1. It would also amend the Commonwealth Savers Plan statutes (§§ 23.1-700, -701, -702, -704, -706) and the closed-meeting provisions of § 2.2-3711, and set up a work group and a report. The supplied text is cut off inside § 2.2-3711, so the fund's actual terms (funding source, eligibility, award amounts) cannot be confirmed here; the bill was left in House Appropriations on February 18, 2026.","mechanics":["Adds new §§ 23.1-235 to 23.1-237 creating the Scholarship Fund under SCHEV, with a work group and report per the title; the substantive text of these sections is not in the supplied excerpt.","Amends §§ 23.1-700, 23.1-701, 23.1-702, 23.1-704 and 23.1-706 governing the Commonwealth Savers Plan, its Board and its Investment Advisory Committee.","Amends § 2.2-3711 in both its current version and the version effective July 1, 2026. The visible text tracks existing closed-meeting authority, including Commonwealth Savers Plan Board items in subdivisions 20, 25 and 38.","Subdivision 54 of § 2.2-3711 is reproduced without visible change. It covers Virginia Lottery Board closed deliberations on appeals over sports betting licenses or permits under § 58.1-4007; subdivision 53 does the same for casino gaming.","Procedural posture: House Education substitute of February 9, 2026 (original patron Delegate Tran); left in Committee on Appropriations on February 18, 2026, effectively ending it for the session."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike TN SB1662, which expressly funds public-institution athletic programs through a fee on vendors registered with the Tennessee sports wagering council, nothing in the visible HB1001 text ties the scholarship fund to sports betting revenue or operator fees. It also has nothing in common with the college-gaming and integrity bills listed, such as MD HB518, CT HB05229, NC H828 and LA SB354, or with the NIL bills (e.g., HI HB 2384, TX HB126). Its only wagering contact is the reproduced Lottery Board closed-session authority for sports betting license appeals in § 2.2-3711(A)(54).","watch":"Confirm from the full text of §§ 23.1-235 to 23.1-237 that the fund is not capitalized by a sports betting tax diversion or operator assessment, and that the § 2.2-3711 reenactment leaves the Lottery Board's closed-session authority for sports betting license appeals in subdivision 54 unchanged, if the bill is revived.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":86945,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:25.689Z"},"VA-HB1272":{"summary":"HB1272, as rewritten by the Second Joint Conference Committee substitute of March 14, 2026, would create a regulatory regime for electronic skill gaming devices under the Virginia Lottery Board. It adds a new Chapter 42 to Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4200 through 58.1-4226, Articles 1-5), amends the criminal gambling statutes, and amends existing Lottery and casino provisions. The conference report failed on March 14, 2026, so the bill is dead for the session.","mechanics":["Adds new Title 58.1, Chapter 42 (§§ 58.1-4200 to 58.1-4226) on 'regulation of gaming commerce and development'; its substantive text (fees, tax rate, device caps, locations) is not in the excerpt provided.","Adds § 2.2-3711(A)(56), letting the Virginia Lottery Board close meetings for § 58.1-4007 appeals on denial, revocation, suspension or non-renewal of licenses for electronic skill gaming devices, mirroring existing subdivision 54 for sports betting permits.","Amends criminal gambling provisions §§ 18.2-325, 18.2-331.1, 18.2-334 and 18.2-339, and adds § 18.2-334.7 and § 19.2-386.30:1 (a forfeiture-chapter section), signalling new penalties and seizure of unlicensed devices.","Amends core Lottery Board powers and licensing sections (§§ 58.1-4002, -4003, -4006, -4007, -4015.1), § 58.1-4048 and casino section § 58.1-4103, plus problem-gambling statute § 37.2-314.2.","The short title references a sunset, but the sunset clause and effective-date enactments are not in the supplied text."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the listed mobile sports wagering authorization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), HB1272 targets skill-game and electronic gaming machines rather than sports wagering. Its closest analogues are Illinois device bills. IL HB3733 and HB 2879 expand the criminal 'gambling device' definition. IL HB3274 and HB5048 address prize and gift kiosks under the Video Gaming Act. Virginia's approach is distinctive because it would build a full licensing chapter under the same Lottery Board that regulates sports betting and casinos, rather than only tightening criminal definitions.","watch":"Although the bill failed in conference, operators should watch for reintroduction and for how the conforming amendments to §§ 58.1-4007, 58.1-4048 and 58.1-4103 would put skill-game licensees into the same Lottery Board appeal, enforcement and problem-gambling framework as sports-betting and casino permit holders; the full Chapter 42 text was not supplied and should be reviewed.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":208812,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:30.755Z"},"VA-HB1403":{"summary":"HB1403 (Chapter 842, approved April 13, 2026) creates a Severe Maternal Morbidity Surveillance and Review Program in a new Article 25, § 32.1-73.33, of Title 32.1 (Health). It also amends Virginia FOIA and the Administrative Process Act to protect the Program's work. The bill has nothing to do with gaming; sports betting appears only because the reenacted closed-meetings statute, § 2.2-3711, carries existing, unchanged Virginia Lottery Board provisions.","mechanics":["Adds new § 32.1-73.33, which establishes the Severe Maternal Morbidity Surveillance and Review Program and defines 'severe maternal morbidity'. The full text of that section is truncated in the excerpt provided.","Amends § 2.2-3705.5(7) to add clause (vi). It excludes from mandatory FOIA disclosure information acquired during the Program's reviews, to the extent § 32.1-73.33 makes it confidential. The Developmental Disabilities Mortality Review Committee clause is relettered to (vii).","Amends § 2.2-3711(A)(21), in both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective July 1, 2026, to let the Program discuss severe maternal morbidity cases in closed meeting.","Section 2.2-4002 (Administrative Process Act exemptions) is also amended, but the excerpt does not show the specific change.","Reenacted § 2.2-3711(A)(17), (53) and (54) keep, without visible change, the existing closed-meeting authority for Virginia Lottery Board deliberations on lottery, casino and sports betting license denials, revocations and appeals under §§ 58.1-4007 and 58.1-4105."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. This is a maternal health surveillance bill. Its only gaming connection is that a full reenactment of § 2.2-3711 restates the existing Lottery Board closed-session authority for sports betting and casino licensing appeals in subdivisions 53 and 54, without substantive change in the text shown.","watch":"Nothing operator-specific; the only point to confirm is that the reenactment of § 2.2-3711 (both versions) leaves subdivisions 53 and 54 on Lottery Board closed deliberations over casino and sports betting license actions unchanged.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":78741,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:36.176Z"},"VA-HB1398":{"summary":"HB1398 would create a Fetal and Infant Mortality Review Team in a new Code of Virginia § 32.1-283.9. It also makes conforming changes so that information the Team gathers is excluded from mandatory FOIA disclosure and the Team can discuss individual fetal and infant death cases in closed meetings. The bill has nothing to do with gaming; wagering appears only because § 2.2-3711, which the bill amends, already contains existing Virginia Lottery Board closed-meeting provisions that are reprinted unchanged.","mechanics":["Adds new § 32.1-283.9 establishing the Fetal and Infant Mortality Review Team; the title references definitions, a penalty and a report, but the supplied text is truncated before that section, so its substantive terms cannot be confirmed.","Amends § 2.2-3705.5(7)(v) to exclude information the Team acquires during death reviews from mandatory FOIA disclosure, to the extent § 32.1-283.9 makes it confidential.","Amends § 2.2-3711(A)(21), in both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective July 1, 2026, to allow closed meetings for the portions in which the Team discusses individual fetal and infant death cases.","Also amends § 2.2-4002, an Administrative Process Act section; the amending language is not in the supplied text.","House Appropriations substitute dated February 11, 2026, which folds in HB 336 (Delegates Franklin and Anthony). Senate Finance and Appropriations continued the bill to the next session on March 9, 2026, by a 14-0 vote."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed, and the bill is not comparable to gaming legislation. The only gaming-related text is existing language in § 2.2-3711(A)(17), (53) and (54). Those subdivisions authorize closed Virginia Lottery Board deliberations on lottery, casino and sports betting license denials, revocations, suspensions and appeals under §§ 58.1-4007 and 58.1-4105. They appear only because the whole section is reprinted, and HB1398 leaves them untouched.","watch":"No action is needed beyond confirming that any later substitute does not alter the existing § 2.2-3711(A)(54) closed-meeting authority for Lottery Board sports betting license appeals; the bill is in any case carried over to the 2027 session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":83469,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:36.206Z"},"VA-HB1476":{"summary":"HB1476 adds a new closed-meeting exemption to Virginia's FOIA, § 2.2-3711(A)(56), so that law-enforcement civilian oversight bodies can privately discuss criminal investigative files, audit findings, and deliberations on police operations tied to specific complaints involving sexual offenses or confidential juvenile matters. The chapter also amends §§ 16.1-301 (juvenile law-enforcement records) and 19.2-11.2 on disclosure of law-enforcement records, but those amendments fall outside the excerpt provided. Nothing in it concerns sports betting or gaming.","mechanics":["New § 2.2-3711(A)(56), added to both the current version and the version effective July 1, 2026, covers civilian oversight bodies established under § 9.1-601. It also covers bodies a local governing body set up before July 1, 2020 that operate consistently with § 9.1-601.","Closed discussion is allowed only for criminal investigative files, audit findings, and deliberations regarding police operations related to a specific complaint before the body.","The complaint must involve an actual or attempted offense under Article 7 of Chapter 4 of Title 18.2 (criminal sexual assault) or under § 18.2-361, 18.2-370, or 18.2-370.1. Alternatively, it must involve a juvenile whose records are not open to public inspection under § 16.1-301 or 16.1-305.","The existing § 2.2-3711(B) requirement still applies: any action taken must be voted on in a reconvened open meeting before it is effective.","The chapter was approved April 13, 2026 as Chapter 861. The text of the §§ 16.1-301 and 19.2-11.2 amendments is not in the excerpt provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed. Wagering appears only because the reenacted § 2.2-3711 reproduces existing, unamended exemptions. These include subdivision 17 (Lottery Board lottery sales agent appeals), subdivision 53 (casino license denials and revocations) and subdivision 54 (Lottery Board appeals on sports betting licenses or permits under § 58.1-4007, plus related investigations excluded under § 2.2-3705.3(1)). The bill's substance is police oversight transparency, not gaming.","watch":"Nothing here affects operators; confirm only that the existing Lottery Board closed-meeting authority for sports-betting license appeals in § 2.2-3711(A)(54) carries over unchanged into the version effective July 1, 2026.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":63522,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:48.684Z"},"VA-HB145":{"summary":"HB145 (Chapter 565) repeals Virginia's Fantasy Contests Act (Chapter 51 of Title 59.1) and re-creates fantasy contest regulation as new Chapter 42 of Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4200 through 58.1-4214), placing permitting and oversight under the Virginia Lottery Board and Department alongside sports betting and casino gaming. It sets a permit regime with a $50,000 application fee, consumer-protection and integrity conditions, Board investigative and injunctive powers, and a revenue base for taxation. Conforming amendments fold fantasy contests into the State Police gaming enforcement definitions, the voluntary exclusion programs, FOIA exclusions, and the Problem Gambling Treatment and Support Fund.","mechanics":["Definition (§ 58.1-4200): a fantasy contest must be online with an entry fee, have two or more players competing against each other, and have prizes fixed and disclosed in advance. Outcomes must reflect skill via accumulated statistics and may not rest on any single team's score or point spread, or solely on a single athlete's single performance. No player may compete against the operator, which excludes house-banked formats.","Permit required before offering contests in Virginia (§ 58.1-4202). Permits are valid three years, require a nonrefundable $50,000 initial application fee deposited to the Problem Gambling Treatment and Support Fund, and a lapsed permit means filing a new initial application.","Permit conditions (§ 58.1-4202 D) include 21+ age verification, bars on operator and employee play, confidential-information controls, and restrictions on athletes entering contests based on their own statistics. Operators must also offer self-exclusion, disclose and enforce entry limits, and segregate player funds with a reserve sufficient to cover all prizes.","Taxable base: 'fantasy contest revenue' means entry fees less prizes paid, multiplied by a Virginia 'location percentage.' § 58.1-4208 also directs moneys to the Problem Gambling Fund, but the tax rate itself is in sections beyond the excerpt provided.","Enforcement: the Board may investigate, issue subpoenas (hearings held in Richmond), and seek circuit court injunctions (§ 58.1-4201). Knowingly falsifying a permit application is a Class 1 misdemeanor (§ 58.1-4202 G), and the civil penalty referenced in the title appears in sections not shown.","Conforming changes: new § 11-16.3 exempts lawful fantasy contests from Title 11 gaming-contract rules, and the 15% principal stockholder threshold applies. Fantasy contests are added to the lottery and casino voluntary exclusion lists (§§ 58.1-4015.1, 58.1-4103), the FOIA investigative exclusion (§ 2.2-3705.3), and the gaming laws definition (§ 52-53)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue on the list is IL HB2342 (Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act), which would likewise hand fantasy oversight to the state gaming regulator with fees, civil penalties and a tax on adjusted gross fantasy receipts; Virginia's version, unlike that proposal, is enacted. FL H0189 addresses fantasy sports only as one piece of an omnibus gambling rewrite. Most other listed bills, such as GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and AK HB145/SB194, authorize sports wagering rather than restructuring fantasy regulation. Virginia's move is distinctive in consolidating fantasy under the same lottery agency that already runs sports betting and casinos, with a single statewide self-exclusion list.","watch":"Watch how the Department applies the § 58.1-4200 definition, particularly the bans on player-versus-operator play and on outcomes based solely on a single athlete's single performance, since it could exclude pick'em and prop-style products; also confirm the tax rate, civil penalty amounts and effective/transition dates in §§ 58.1-4204 to 58.1-4214, which fall outside the excerpt.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":61658,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:48.760Z"},"VA-HB161":{"summary":"The Senate Finance substitute for HB161 would authorize internet casino gaming (iGaming) in Virginia, licensed by the Virginia Lottery Board and tethered to the casino operators in the four eligible host cities under § 58.1-4107 A 1-4. Each operator could run up to three skins (a fourth if run with a 'qualified entertainment company'). Revenue would be taxed at 20% plus a 6% economic development fee that funds a Casino Gaming Hold Harmless Fund. The bill also declares unlicensed paid-entry sweepstakes to be illegal internet gaming. It would not take effect unless reenacted by the 2027 Session, and it ultimately failed in conference on 2026-03-14.","mechanics":["Licensing (§§ 58.1-4143, 58.1-4144): the operator license goes only to the casino licensees in host cities A1-4, issued as an amendment to the casino license, for a $500,000 initial fee and $250,000 renewal over a 5-year term. The platform provider license costs a $2 million application fee and a $1 million renewal per platform. Qualified internet gaming entities licensed in two or more U.S. states can get a one-year temporary license for $10,000. Applicants filing in the first 30 days get a simultaneous launch.","Platforms (§ 58.1-4143 D-E): up to three platforms per operator, each under a single brand plus an optional second brand for poker. No casino co-branding is required. A fourth platform is allowed only if the platform provider contracts with a qualified entertainment company, which must meet a size threshold, conduct community outreach and file a DEI plan.","Tax and fees (§§ 58.1-4152 to 58.1-4157): 20% of adjusted gross internet gaming revenue, allocated 3% to the Gaming Regulatory Fund, 2% to problem gambling and 95% to a new Modern Public Education Fund, and in lieu of other taxes. A separate 6% fee funds equal monthly hold-harmless payments to the casinos until July 1, 2032. After that date, casinos must show a revenue loss against FY ending June 30, 2027. Recipients must put at least 25% of payments toward promotional spending.","Operator conditions: a job-loss mitigation plan, published by the Director. At least 25% of promotional free play must be redeemable online or at the associated land-based casino. Accounts may be shared with sports betting (§ 58.1-4146 E). Players must be 21+, located in-state and geolocated. Dormant account balances are split 50/50 between the operator and the general fund. No internet gaming terminals are allowed in places of public accommodation other than the operator's own casino.","Responsible gaming (§ 58.1-4147): Board regulations covering advertising guidelines, deposit limits and time-outs. Operators must use player data and automated triggers to flag at-risk players and apply a three-phase intervention: communications, video tutorial, then access to a responsible gaming professional. Operators also need pop-up break-in-play messages and 12-month account statements.","Enforcement (§§ 58.1-4102(8), 58.1-4149 to 58.1-4154): the Board gets cease-and-desist and injunction power against unlicensed iGaming, sports betting or sweepstakes. Unlicensed iGaming is a Class 6 felony, with fines up to $25,000 for individuals and $1 million for entities. Tampering is a Class 5 felony, with fines up to $50,000 or $200,000. Unlicensed paid-entry sweepstakes carry civil penalties of $100,000 for the first offense and $250,000 for each later offense, with each day a separate violation. Regulations are due by January 1, 2028, and the act requires 2027 reenactment."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","sweeps","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the listed sports-wagering legalization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), HB161 legalizes iGaming on top of an existing Virginia sports betting regime. Its closest analogues are Illinois's Internet Gaming Act bills, HB3080 and HB4797, which also authorize operators and management or platform providers. The Virginia substitute differs in several ways: it tethers licenses to the four host-city casinos, uses a 6% hold-harmless fee that pays casinos directly, requires a job-loss mitigation plan, and mandates a cross-channel free-play allocation. Its sweepstakes ban and three-phase responsible gaming intervention go further than the advertising and account-withdrawal limits in CT HB05229.","watch":"Even though the bill failed in conference, watch how a 2026 or 2027 version handles several points: the market-access constraints (casino tethering, three skins, a fourth only through the DEI-linked qualified entertainment company); the combined 26% effective burden and the $2 million platform fee; the mandated player-data intervention protocols; and the Board's new cease-and-desist authority over unlicensed sports betting and sweepstakes, which could reach sweepstakes-adjacent products and affiliates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":44277,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:59.198Z"},"VA-HB271":{"summary":"HB271, as substituted by House General Laws on February 12, 2026, creates a Virginia Gaming Commission and recodifies Virginia gambling law into a new Title 29.5. The new title (§§ 29.5-100 through 29.5-913) replaces the current statutes on illegal gambling, charitable gaming, the Lottery, sports betting, horse racing and casino gaming, which the bill repeals. The excerpt provided consists mostly of conforming amendments, so the substantive regulatory terms in Title 29.5 cannot be reviewed from this text.","mechanics":["Repeals the current gaming chapters and folds them into new Title 29.5: Va. Code § 18.2-325 et seq. (illegal gambling), § 18.2-340.15 et seq. (charitable gaming), Ch. 40 of Title 58.1 (Lottery), Ch. 41 of Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4100 to -4141, sports betting), Ch. 29 and 29.1 of Title 59.1 (racing), Ch. 51 of Title 59.1 (casino gaming), and §§ 2.2-2455 and -2456.","Title 29.5 has three subtitles. Subtitle I is Chapters 1 to 6 (§§ 29.5-100 to -650), with charitable gaming at Chapter 2 (§ 29.5-200 et seq.). Subtitle II is Chapters 7 and 8 (§§ 29.5-700 to -814), where lottery crimes now sit at §§ 29.5-713 and 29.5-803 to -807. Subtitle III is Chapter 9 (§§ 29.5-900 to -913).","Revenue now earmarked for the Virginia Indigenous People's Trust Fund (§ 2.2-401.01(D)) is cross-referenced to § 29.5-327 instead of § 58.1-4125, the current sports betting tax allocation section. This suggests the sports betting tax provisions move to Chapter 3 of Title 29.5, but the rate and distribution are not shown in the excerpt.","Charitable gaming oversight moves from the Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services to the Gaming Commission and a 'Virginia Gaming Commission Board', including approval of supplier equipment (§ 2.2-3705.6(14), § 29.5-225).","The Attorney General may assist in prosecuting violations of Title 29.5 with the concurrence of the local Commonwealth's attorney (§ 2.2-511(A)(xvii)).","Gaming Commission licensing investigations are added to the FOIA exclusion in § 2.2-3705.3(1), and the Virginia Racing Commission is placed under the Secretary of Agriculture and Forestry (§ 2.2-203.3)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual for its scope: it does not adjust one gaming vertical but dismantles Virginia's fragmented structure of Lottery, sports betting, casino, charitable gaming, racing and criminal gambling statutes. It replaces that structure with a single recodified title under a new consolidated Gaming Commission.","watch":"Because the provided text cuts off before the operative Title 29.5 provisions, operators should obtain the full substitute and check whether the recodification preserves current sports betting permits, regulations, tax rate and § 29.5-327 revenue allocation unchanged, and how the transition from the Virginia Lottery to the new Commission and Board is handled when the bill is taken up in the 2027 session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":910738,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:00.739Z"},"VA-HB281":{"summary":"VA HB281 (Chapter 1041, approved April 22, 2026) amends Va. Code § 55.1-1241 of the Virginia Residential Landlord and Tenant Act. It governs when a tenant may defend an action for possession or rent for nonpayment by showing that the landlord failed to remedy dangerous conditions or material noncompliance, and it sets the remedies the court may order. The bill has no connection to sports betting or gaming.","mechanics":["Subsection A: the defense covers conditions that are a fire hazard or a serious threat to life, health or safety, including lack of heat, running water, light, electricity or sewage disposal, rodent infestation, and material noncompliance by the landlord with the rental agreement or law.","Precondition: before suit, the landlord must have received the tenant's written notice or a state or local violation or condemnation notice and refused, or unreasonably failed, to fix the condition; there is a rebuttable presumption that more than 30 days after the landlord receives notice is unreasonable.","A tenant in possession must pay into court the rent found due, held pending the court's order under subsection C.","Subsection B: the landlord defeats the defense by showing the condition does not exist, has been remedied, was caused by the tenant or the tenant's household or guests, or that the tenant unreasonably refused entry.","Subsection C remedies: equitable rent reduction, termination or surrender at the tenant's request, referral to an agency with a continuance and rent escrow (funds usable for mortgage, lien or repairs), or an order that the landlord remedy the condition.","Fee-shifting: costs and attorney fees may be imposed on a bad-faith tenant (D) or, at the court's discretion, on the landlord when the tenant prevails (E); subsection F preserves the landlord's right to amend the amount sought at trial to include ongoing rent."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They cover gaming and wagering matters such as Hawaii's sports wagering bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569), Illinois' internet gaming and prediction-market bills (HB3080, HB5059), and Maryland's promotional-play and problem-gambling bills (HB291, HB1409), while HB281 is purely a residential landlord-tenant habitability and eviction-defense measure. Its likely appearance on a gaming tracker is a keyword or classification error; the text never mentions wagering, gaming or gambling.","watch":"Nothing for sports-betting operators; this bill can be dropped from the gaming tracker unless the client has residential rental property exposure in Virginia.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":4895,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:01.645Z"},"VA-HB30":{"summary":"HB30 is Virginia's 2026 Appropriation Act, appropriating about $206.9 billion across the 2026-28 biennium (FY2027-FY2028) and amending numerous Code sections, many in Title 4.1 (alcohol and cannabis) and Title 58.1 (tax). In the portion of the text provided, gaming appears only incidentally: Item 1 funds a legislative joint subcommittee studying a Virginia Gaming Commission, and § 3 lists Lottery Proceeds Fund revenues. The text shows the bill was chaptered as Chapter 1 of the 2026 Special Session I, approved June 29, 2026, even though the listed status says it passed only one chamber.","mechanics":["§ 7 allocates total appropriations of $206,907,394,349 for the 2026-28 biennium: $75.17 billion general fund and $131.74 billion nongeneral fund.","§ 3 lists Lottery Proceeds Fund revenues of $887,725,168 in FY2027 and $877,725,168 in FY2028, totaling $1,765,450,336, as part of nongeneral fund resources.","Item 1, paragraph P.1 appropriates $15,000 in each year from the general fund to the Joint Subcommittee to Study the Feasibility of Establishing the Virginia Gaming Commission, authorized by HJR 10 (2024).","The title lists amended tax provisions (§§ 58.1-322.03, 58.1-339.8, 58.1-602, 58.1-605, 58.1-605.1, 58.1-606.1, 58.1-301) and consumer-protection provisions (§ 59.1-200); none is identified in the provided text as gaming-related.","The provided text is truncated partway through Item 1, so any later items on the Virginia Lottery, sports betting tax distributions, or problem-gambling funding were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. What stands out is that Virginia keeps funding, through the budget, a legislative study of whether to consolidate gaming oversight into a standalone Virginia Gaming Commission, rather than leaving it with the Virginia Lottery. The bill itself makes no change to that structure in the portion provided.","watch":"Watch the Gaming Commission feasibility study funded in Item 1.P, and review the untruncated agency items (Virginia Lottery and Department of Taxation) for any changes to sports betting tax revenue distribution, problem-gambling fund transfers, or Lottery staffing that the excerpt does not show.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":378569,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:04.146Z"},"VA-HB336":{"summary":"HB336, as substituted by House Rules on February 6, 2026, creates a Fetal and Infant Health Data and Quality Improvement Review Team in new Code § 32.1-283.9 to review fetal and infant deaths. It also amends the Virginia Freedom of Information Act so that information the Team gathers is excluded from mandatory disclosure (§ 2.2-3705.5(7)) and so that the Team may discuss individual fetal and infant death cases in closed meetings (§ 2.2-3711(A)(21)), with a conforming change to § 2.2-4002. The bill has no substantive gaming content and was incorporated into HB1398 by House Appropriations on February 11, 2026.","mechanics":["Adds new § 32.1-283.9 establishing the Fetal and Infant Health Data and Quality Improvement Review Team, with duties, confidentiality rules, a penalty and a reporting requirement; the text of that section is truncated in the excerpt provided.","Amends § 2.2-3705.5(7)(v) to exclude information acquired by the Team from mandatory FOIA disclosure to the extent § 32.1-283.9 makes it confidential.","Amends § 2.2-3711(A)(21), in both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective July 1, 2026, to authorize closed meetings for discussion of individual fetal and infant death cases.","Amends § 2.2-4002, apparently a conforming change.","The only references to wagering are the unchanged existing text of § 2.2-3711(A)(53)-(54), which already lets the Virginia Lottery Board hold closed deliberations on casino gaming and sports betting license denials, suspensions and revocations; the bill does not alter those subdivisions.","Status: incorporated into HB1398 (Franklin) by House Appropriations on a voice vote, February 11, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. The bill is a public-health fatality-review measure modeled on Virginia's existing Maternal Mortality Review Team (§ 32.1-283.8) and other fatality review teams. Its only connection to gaming is that it reprints the full closed-meetings statute, which already contains Lottery Board provisions for casino and sports betting licensing appeals.","watch":"Nothing operator-specific is at stake here, but confirm that the vehicle bill HB1398 does not alter the Virginia Lottery Board's existing closed-meeting authority under § 2.2-3711(A)(53)-(54) for casino and sports betting license actions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":83979,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:12.103Z"},"VA-HB32":{"summary":"Virginia HB32 (Chapter 907, approved April 13, 2026) amends Code § 37.2-304(14) to add two members to the Problem Gambling Treatment and Support Advisory Committee, which the Commissioner of Behavioral Health and Developmental Services must maintain so that treatment providers and legal gaming operators can work together to reduce problem gambling harms. The new seats go to a representative of the Virginia Council on Problem Gambling and a representative of the Virginia State Police Gaming Enforcement Unit. The bill creates no new obligations, fees or restrictions for operators.","mechanics":["Amends Va. Code § 37.2-304(14), part of the DBHDS Commissioner's statutory duties to establish and maintain the Advisory Committee.","Adds a new clause (iii), a representative from the Virginia Council on Problem Gambling, and renumbers the existing clauses (iii) through (viii) as (iv) through (ix).","Adds a new clause (x), a representative from the Virginia State Police Gaming Enforcement Unit.","Existing membership is unchanged. It still includes one representative from each gaming regulator (lottery, casino, sports betting, pari-mutuel racing, historical horse racing, charitable gaming), one casino operator, one sports betting permit holder, one HHR operator and one charitable gaming permit holder.","The DBHDS Problem Gambling Prevention Coordinator remains chair, and a vice-chair is elected from the members.","The text contains no special effective-date clause, no funding change, and no enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"VA HB32 is a narrow governance change that only alters who sits on an advisory body. By contrast, Maryland's HB1409/SB862 mandate prevalence studies of mobile gambling and address fund revenue, and MD HB758 changes how Problem Gambling Fund money may be spent. Other bills impose direct operator duties, such as NJ A 4003, which bars incentive-based wagering offers to patrons using responsible-gaming tools, and MD HB518, which requires a voluntary exclusion list. HB32 resembles study- and process-oriented measures like LA SR 178 and MD HB 195, but it is even more limited because it creates no new study, mandate or funding.","watch":"Adding the State Police Gaming Enforcement Unit and an advocacy group (the Virginia Council on Problem Gambling) alongside the single sports betting permit holder seat could shift the committee's recommendations toward enforcement and harm reduction, which may later feed into operator-facing rules or legislation, so operators should keep active engagement through that seat.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":7359,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:14.108Z"},"VA-HB513":{"summary":"HB513 (Acts 2026, Chapter 158, approved April 6, 2026) authorizes local and regional suicide fatality review teams in a new Code of Virginia section, 32.1-283.9, which sets out membership and a penalty. It also amends Virginia's FOIA so that team information is shielded from disclosure and team discussions of individual deaths can happen in closed meetings. It is a public-health and open-government bill with no sports-betting or gaming provisions of its own.","mechanics":["Adds new §32.1-283.9 establishing local and regional suicide fatality review teams, including membership and a penalty per the title. The operative text of that section is not in the excerpt provided, so its specifics (for example what the penalty applies to) cannot be confirmed here.","Amends §2.2-3705.5(7) by adding clause (vii), which excludes from mandatory FOIA disclosure information acquired during a death review by a local or regional suicide fatality review team, to the extent §32.1-283.9 makes it confidential.","Amends §2.2-3711(A)(21), in both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective on that date, to allow closed meetings for the portions in which individual death cases are discussed by these teams.","The reprinted §2.2-3711 includes existing subdivisions 17, 53 and 54, which cover closed Virginia Lottery Board deliberations on lottery, casino and sports-betting license appeals and investigations. These are carried over unchanged and are not amended by HB513."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and the HI bills authorize and tax sports wagering, while the IL bills address licensing, prediction markets and internet gaming. The closest in theme are IL HB1451, HB2580 and HB5368, which direct the Department of Human Services to run gambling-disorder education. HB513 is different in kind: it creates suicide death-review bodies and FOIA exemptions without mentioning gambling, and its only gaming references are pre-existing Lottery Board closed-meeting provisions reprinted in §2.2-3711.","watch":"There is little here for operators beyond confirming that the full text of §32.1-283.9 does not let suicide review teams demand player or account records from licensed sports-betting operators, and noting that the existing closed-meeting protection for Lottery Board sports-betting license appeals (§2.2-3711(A)(54)) is untouched.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":68363,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:15.679Z"},"VA-HB515":{"summary":"HB515, signed as Chapter 926 of the 2026 Acts of Assembly, bars credit cards as a funding method for Virginia sports betting accounts. It directs the Lottery Director not to approve credit cards as a deposit method under § 58.1-4031(D), and requires permit holders under § 58.1-4034(A)(7) to take reasonable measures to prohibit accepting credit cards to fund accounts on their platforms.","mechanics":["§ 58.1-4031(D): the Director still approves account-funding methods (ACH, debit cards, wire transfers and other Board-approved methods) but now 'shall not approve the use of credit cards' to fund sports betting accounts.","§ 58.1-4034(A)(7): permit holders 'shall take reasonable measures to prohibit the acceptance of credit cards' to fund sports betting accounts on their platforms. This is a reasonable-measures standard, not strict liability.","The chaptered text as reproduced still lists 'credit cards' among the enumerated methods in both sections; the amendments' strike-throughs are not visible, so the operative effect is carried by the new prohibitory sentences.","Technical change: 'clearing house' becomes 'clearinghouse' in both sections.","No special effective date or enactment clause is included, so the default Virginia effective date for regular-session acts (July 1) would ordinarily apply.","Enforcement runs through existing permit tools, including the Director's power to suspend, revoke or refuse to renew permits under § 58.1-4031(B)(2) and Board regulations."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest listed analogue is CT HB05229, which also targets account-level consumer protections, but it addresses withdrawals from gaming accounts, customer-service lines, campus advertising and AI-targeted bets rather than deposit methods. The AK (HB145, SB194), GA (HB 686) and HI (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) bills are full authorization and licensing frameworks. Illinois's many bills deal with license fees, taxes, prediction markets and iGaming. Virginia's bill is unusual as a narrow, single-issue payments restriction layered onto an existing mature market.","watch":"Watch how the Board and Director define 'reasonable measures' in regulations, especially for indirect credit funding through e-wallets (e.g., PayPal balances funded by credit card), prepaid cards loaded by credit, and card-type detection via BIN screening, since compliance gaps could expose permits to action under § 58.1-4031(B)(2).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":8830,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:19.275Z"},"VA-HB642":{"summary":"HB642 is an omnibus cannabis bill. It would amend dozens of Virginia Code sections and add new chapters to Title 4.1 to create a regulated adult-use retail marijuana market under the Virginia Cannabis Control Authority, with related criminal-penalty changes and a reporting requirement. The version provided is a Governor's substitute dated April 13, 2026, and the Governor ultimately vetoed the bill on May 19, 2026; it does not address sports betting or gaming.","mechanics":["Adds new Title 4.1 provisions: §§ 4.1-629 and 4.1-630, Chapter 7 (§§ 4.1-700 and 4.1-701), Chapter 10 (§§ 4.1-1000 through 4.1-1007), Chapter 12 (§§ 4.1-1200 through 4.1-1206), new Chapter 13 sections, and Chapter 17 (§§ 4.1-1700 through 4.1-1704), together building out the retail cannabis licensing and regulatory framework.","Repeals Article 4 of Chapter 41.1 of Title 3.2 and §§ 3.2-5145.4:1, 4.1-611, 4.1-1101.1, 4.1-1105.1, 18.2-248.1 and 18.2-251.1, and amends many Title 18.2 and Title 19.2 criminal-law sections on possession, distribution, search and seizure, forfeiture and records.","Brings the Virginia Cannabis Control Authority in line with the ABC Authority on personnel matters: its employees are added to the state employee health plan (§ 2.2-2818 D), and its CEO, agents, officers and employees are exempted from the Virginia Personnel Act (§ 2.2-2905, new subdivision 29).","Adds members of the Cannabis Control Authority Board of Directors to the statement-of-economic-interests filing requirement in § 2.2-3114 A, alongside the Virginia Lottery Board and the ABC Board.","Amends § 58.1-301 (tax conformity) and adds § 6.2-108 (banking) and § 54.1-4426. The specific tax rates and banking terms are not visible in the truncated text provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills were listed. The bill's only contact with gaming is incidental: existing statutory lists that already name the Virginia Lottery and Lottery Board (§§ 2.2-2905 and 2.2-3114) are amended to add the Cannabis Control Authority alongside them. What is unusual is the procedural posture: the Governor rewrote the bill through a substitute and then vetoed it anyway.","watch":"Because the bill was vetoed and nothing in the provided text touches wagering, there is little to monitor beyond the general precedent of Virginia housing a new vice-industry regulator outside the Personnel Act and subjecting its board to the same disclosure regime as the Lottery Board.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":691287,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:27.357Z"},"VA-HB65":{"summary":"Chapter 23 (HB 65) repeals the Virginia State Apple Board and the Apple Fund by repealing Chapter 12 of Title 3.2 (§§ 3.2-1200 through 3.2-1217), with the repeal taking effect on a delayed date of July 1, 2028. It also makes conforming amendments elsewhere in the Code, including Virginia's FOIA exclusions and closed-meeting provisions. The bill has nothing to do with sports betting or gaming regulation.","mechanics":["Repeals Chapter 12 of Title 3.2 (§§ 3.2-1200 through 3.2-1217), which establishes the Virginia State Apple Board and the Apple Fund; per the title, the repeal is effective July 1, 2028.","Deletes the FOIA exclusion in § 2.2-3705.6(15) for apple producer sales information provided to the Apple Board under § 3.2-1215. The excerpt renumbers the following subdivisions, which suggests the obsolete E-911 CMRS trade-secret exclusion is also removed.","Updates cross-references in the closed-meeting statute, § 2.2-3711 (both the current and future versions), to match the renumbered § 2.2-3705.6 subdivisions. For example, the Commonwealth Health Research Board reference changes from subdivision 17 to 16.","Makes conforming amendments to §§ 3.2-1100, 3.2-1104, 3.2-1105, 3.2-1106, 10.1-1458, and 15.2-2103.1.","The bill title references a report requirement, but its details are not in the excerpt provided.","Approved March 31, 2026, as Acts of Assembly Chapter 23."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. Bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/2570, MS HB 4074, and IL HB5443 authorize, tax, or regulate wagering, while VA HB65 is an agricultural-board sunset. Its only contact with gaming is that it reprints, without substantive change, the existing FOIA exclusion for charitable gaming supplier information (§ 2.2-3705.6(14)) and the Virginia Lottery Board closed-meeting authority (§ 2.2-3711(A)(17)).","watch":"Nothing substantive for sports-betting operators; at most, confirm that the § 2.2-3705.6 renumbering does not disturb any FOIA or closed-meeting cross-references relied on for confidential Virginia Lottery or gaming submissions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":89693,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:27.742Z"},"VA-HB972":{"summary":"HB972 (Chapter 1120, approved May 14, 2026) is a Virginia election-administration law that restructures voter registration list maintenance, cancellation procedures, required record matches, identification information, and data standards in Title 24.2. It has nothing substantive to do with gaming. Sports betting appears only because the bill reenacts the whole closed-meetings statute, § 2.2-3711, to update a single cross-reference.","mechanics":["Amends § 2.2-3711(A)(34), in both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective on that date, so that the State Board of Elections' and local electoral boards' closed-meeting authority for voting security matters cites new § 24.2-407.2 instead of § 24.2-410.2, which is repealed.","Adds new § 24.2-407.2 and a new Article 2.1 in Chapter 4 of Title 24.2 containing §§ 24.2-407.3 and 24.2-410.3 through 24.2-410.6, plus new § 24.2-426.1. The substantive text of these sections is not included in the excerpt provided.","Repeals §§ 24.2-404.3, 24.2-404.4, 24.2-410.2, 24.2-428, 24.2-428.1 and 24.2-428.2, and amends §§ 24.2-401, -404, -408, -409, -410, -410.1, -427, -435, -444, -610, -611, -703.1 and -709.","The reenacted § 2.2-3711(A)(54) carries forward, apparently unchanged, the Virginia Lottery Board's authority to deliberate in closed session on appeals under § 58.1-4007 involving denial, revocation, suspension or non-renewal of sports betting licenses or permits, and to discuss related investigations excluded from disclosure under § 2.2-3705.3(1). Subdivisions 17 (lottery) and 53 (casino) are likewise carried forward."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable, because HB972 is an election-law bill rather than a gaming bill. Bills such as AK HB145 and SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308 and SB1569 authorize and tax sports wagering, and IL HB5059 and HB5142 address prediction markets. HB972's only connection to wagering is that it republishes Virginia's closed-meeting exemptions, including the existing Lottery Board exemption for sports betting license appeals in § 2.2-3711(A)(54).","watch":"Nothing in this bill changes sports betting regulation, so the only thing to confirm is that the reenacted § 2.2-3711(A)(54) closed-session protection for Lottery Board sports betting license appeals survives unaltered in both effective-date versions; the excerpt provided is truncated before that subdivision appears.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":108842,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:28.507Z"},"VA-SB118":{"summary":"The House General Laws substitute for SB118 would authorize internet casino gaming (iGaming) in Virginia under a new Article 12 of Chapter 41, Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4142 through 58.1-4159). Licenses would be available only to casino operators in the host cities in § 58.1-4107(A)(1)-(4), with up to three skins each and a fourth tied to a 'qualified entertainment company.' The bill taxes adjusted gross internet gaming revenue at 20% plus a 6% economic development fee, bans unlicensed sweepstakes, and does not take effect unless reenacted by the 2027 Session; it ultimately failed in conference on 2026-03-14.","mechanics":["Licensing (§§ 58.1-4143 to -4145): The operator license is an amendment to an existing casino license, with a $500,000 initial fee and $250,000 renewal. Each platform costs $2M, prorated, plus $1M per renewal. Platform providers pay a $2M application fee. An entity licensed in two or more US jurisdictions can get a temporary one-year platform license for $10,000. Terms run five years.","Platforms: Each operator may offer up to three platforms, each under one brand plus an optional separate poker brand, with no co-branding required. A fourth platform is allowed only if run with a 'qualified entertainment company' that meets DEI or minority-investment plan criteria. Applicants must submit a job-loss mitigation plan and signed labor peace agreements covering the casino and any live game studio.","Tax and fees (§§ 58.1-4153, -4158): A 20% tax on adjusted gross internet gaming revenue is in lieu of other taxes. The uncollectible-receivables deduction is capped at 2% unless the Board sets otherwise. A separate 6% fee goes to a Casino Gaming Hold Harmless Fund, which pays equal monthly amounts to eligible casinos until July 1, 2032, and then requires proof of revenue loss against 2025-26 or year 3-4 baselines.","Tax distribution (§ 58.1-4154): 5% goes to problem gambling. Until January 1, 2037, 6% goes to an Internet Lottery Hold Harmless Fund and 89% to the general fund; after that date, 95% goes to the general fund.","Penalties and enforcement: Offering iGaming without a license is a Class 6 felony, with fines up to $25,000, or $1M for entities. Tampering is a Class 5 felony, with fines up to $50,000, or $200,000 for entities, and suspension of the casino license. Unlicensed paid-entry sweepstakes are deemed illegal internet gaming, with civil penalties up to $100,000 for a first offense and $250,000 thereafter, each day counted separately. New § 58.1-4102(8) lets the Board seek cease-and-desist orders and injunctions against unlicensed iGaming, sports betting or sweepstakes.","Player protections and timing: Players must be 21 or older and geolocated; the bill also requires debit and online payment options, a single wallet shared with sports betting accounts, responsible-gaming triggers in three intervention phases, the 1-800-MY-RESET helpline, and dormant account balances split 50/50 between operator and state. Regulations are due January 1, 2028 (exempt from the Administrative Process Act), notices of intent open July 1, 2027, and reenactment in 2027 is required."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","sweeps","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like IL HB3080 and HB4797 (Internet Gaming Act), SB118 creates a full iGaming licensing regime. SB118 differs by tethering licenses strictly to existing casino operators with capped skins and by stacking a 6% casino hold-harmless fee and a lottery hold-harmless carve-out on top of a 20% tax. Its explicit civil ban on dual-currency sweepstakes, with per-day penalties, is a more direct sweepstakes attack than the general internet gambling penalty revisions described in FL H0189 and FL S1580. Its responsible-gaming mandates overlap with CT HB05229's consumer-protection focus, though SB118 does not address AI targeting or campus advertising.","watch":"Watch whether the 2027 Session revives or reenacts this framework, particularly the 26% effective rate, the three-skin cap with the qualified-entertainment-company fourth skin, labor peace preconditions, the conflict between the 60-day-from-enactment launch window in enactment clause 4 and the July 1, 2027 notice-of-intent start, and the sweepstakes and unlicensed sports-betting enforcement powers that could be split off into standalone legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":49065,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:41.151Z"},"VA-SB30":{"summary":"SB30 is the Governor's introduced biennial budget bill for Virginia's fiscal years ending June 30, 2027 and June 30, 2028, appropriating general fund and nongeneral fund revenues across state government. It also amends several Code of Virginia tax provisions, mostly income tax and sales tax sections, and repeals the third enactments of Chapters 256 and 257 of the 2022 Acts of Assembly for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2027. The excerpt provided contains only the enacting clause and the opening appropriation sections, with no gaming or wagering provisions.","mechanics":["Appropriates for the biennium ending June 30, 2028, pursuant to § 2.2-1509, including unexpended prior balances and all taxes and nontax moneys (fees, licenses, grants) coming into the treasury.","Defines 'moneys' to include projected revenues from proposed legislation contingent on General Assembly passage (§ 1.B).","Amends § 58.1-416 and §§ 58.1-322.02, 58.1-322.03, 58.1-332, 58.1-339.8, 58.1-390.3, 58.1-422.4, 58.1-422.5 and 58.1-609.3, which are income tax, pass-through, withholding and sales tax exemption provisions, not gaming tax sections.","Repeals the third enactments of 2022 Acts Chapters 256 and 257, effective for taxable years beginning on and after January 1, 2027.","Status: left in Senate Finance and Appropriations on March 14, 2026, so this vehicle is effectively dead for the session."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The bill is a general appropriations act, and the supplied text contains no wagering provisions. Any gaming relevance would sit in budget items not reproduced here, such as Virginia Lottery appropriations or allocations of sports betting tax revenue.","watch":"Because the excerpt is truncated, review the full item-level text and the enacted budget vehicle for any Virginia Lottery appropriations, changes to sports betting tax revenue distributions (for example to the Problem Gambling Treatment and Support Fund), or skill-game and gaming language that could be added through budget amendments rather than standalone legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2299,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:41.924Z"},"VA-SB195":{"summary":"SB195 would consolidate Virginia's gaming regulation into a new Title 29.5 creating a Virginia Gaming Commission, recodifying and repealing the existing lottery (Ch. 40, Title 58.1), sports betting (Ch. 41, §§ 58.1-4100 through 58.1-4141), horse racing (Ch. 29, Title 59.1), casino (Ch. 51, §§ 59.1-556 through 59.1-570), charitable gaming (Art. 1.1:1, Ch. 8, Title 18.2), and illegal gambling (Art. 1, Ch. 8, Title 18.2) statutes. The supplied text is truncated after the conforming amendments, so the substantive Title 29.5 provisions on licensing, taxes, and penalties are not visible and cannot be summarized here.","mechanics":["Adds Title 29.5 with Subtitle I (Chs. 1-6, §§ 29.5-100 to 29.5-650), Subtitle II (Chs. 7-8, §§ 29.5-700 to 29.5-814), and Subtitle III (Ch. 9, §§ 29.5-900 to 29.5-913), and repeals §§ 2.2-2455 and 2.2-2456 along with the existing gaming chapters.","The sports betting tax revenue reference that feeds the Virginia Indigenous People's Trust Fund moves from § 58.1-4125 to § 29.5-327 (§ 2.2-401.01 D). This suggests sports betting tax distribution is recodified in Chapter 3 of the new title.","Amends § 2.2-511 to give the Attorney General authority, with the local Commonwealth's attorney's concurrence, to assist in prosecuting violations of Title 29.5 (new clause xvii).","FOIA amendments (§ 2.2-3705.3(1)) shield Virginia Gaming Commission investigations of license applicants and licensees, replacing references to the Lottery's Chapter 40/41 role. Lottery crime references are remapped to §§ 29.5-713 and 29.5-803 through 29.5-807.","Charitable gaming oversight shifts from the Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services to the Gaming Commission and its Board, including supplier proprietary information under § 29.5-225 (§ 2.2-3705.6(14)).","Adds the Virginia Racing Commission to the agencies under the Secretary of Agriculture and Forestry (§ 2.2-203.3)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What stands out is the scope: rather than amending sports betting rules piecemeal, SB195 folds lottery, sports betting, casinos, racing, charitable gaming, and the criminal gambling statutes into one omnibus title under a single new regulator. Few states attempt a recodification that broad in a single bill.","watch":"Obtain the full Title 29.5 text to check whether the transfer from the Virginia Lottery to the new Commission alters existing sports betting permit terms, tax rates or deductions under the recodified § 29.5-327, transition and grandfathering of current permits, and the penalty and AG enforcement provisions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":909935,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:44.409Z"},"VA-SB129":{"summary":"SB129 repeals Virginia's Fantasy Contests Act (Chapter 51 of Title 59.1) and recreates fantasy contest regulation as new Chapter 42 of Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4200 through 58.1-4214). This places permitting, investigation and enforcement under the Virginia Lottery Board and Department, the same body that regulates sports betting and casinos. It adds a $50,000 application fee, a 21+ age floor, operator integrity and player-fund controls, and, per the title and cross-references, a tax and civil penalties whose details fall outside the excerpt provided.","mechanics":["Definition (§ 58.1-4200): a fantasy contest must be online and charge an entry fee. Prizes must be fixed and disclosed in advance, and outcomes must rest on accumulated statistical results of individuals. No outcome may be based on a single team's score or point spread, or solely on a single athlete's performance in a single event, and no player may compete against the operator.","Permit (§§ 58.1-4202, 58.1-4203): no operator may offer contests without a Department permit, valid for three years. A nonrefundable $50,000 initial application fee goes to the Problem Gambling Treatment and Support Fund. A lapsed permit requires a fresh application unless an extension is granted. Knowingly falsifying an application is a Class 1 misdemeanor.","Permit conditions (§ 58.1-4202(D)): operators must verify players are 21 or older and bar themselves, employees and household relatives from cash-prize contests. They must block the sharing of confidential information, keep athletes out of contests based on their own team's statistics, and offer self-restriction. They must also disclose entry limits, segregate player funds, and hold a reserve sufficient to pay all prizes.","Revenue base: fantasy contest revenue means entry fees minus prizes paid, multiplied by a location percentage (the share of entry fees from players in Virginia). The tax rate in § 58.1-4208 is not in the excerpt, but § 37.2-314.2 shows at least some § 58.1-4208 moneys go to the Problem Gambling Fund. A principal stockholder is anyone holding 15% or more.","Board powers (§ 58.1-4201): the Board can investigate, take sworn statements, issue subpoenas (hearings held in Richmond), seek injunctions in circuit court, and pursue contempt in Richmond Circuit Court.","Conforming changes: FOIA exemption for licensing investigations under § 2.2-3705.3 (current and July 1, 2026 versions). New § 11-16.3 exempts lawful contests from the gaming-contracts chapter. State Police gaming-law definition (§ 52-53), the voluntary exclusion programs (§§ 58.1-4015.1, 58.1-4103) and the sports betting definitions (§ 58.1-4030) are updated to point to § 58.1-4200."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"SB129 is the closest analogue to IL HB2342 (Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act), which likewise gives an existing gaming regulator, the Illinois Gaming Board, power over fantasy operators, with fees, civil penalties and a tax on adjusted gross fantasy receipts. Unlike the Illinois proposal, Virginia's bill is enacted and folds fantasy into the same agency, exclusion list and State Police enforcement as sports betting and casinos. FL H0189 also touches fantasy sports, but as one piece of an omnibus gambling rewrite. The remaining listed bills (e.g., GA HB 686, HI HB 1308, AK HB145/SB194) authorize sports wagering rather than regulating fantasy.","watch":"Watch how the Lottery Board applies the § 58.1-4200 bars on player-versus-operator contests and outcomes based solely on a single athlete's single-event performance to pick'em and prop-style products. Also confirm, against the full chaptered text, the § 58.1-4208 tax rate and the civil penalty amounts that were not in the excerpt.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":61658,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:51.877Z"},"VA-SB375":{"summary":"SB375, as substituted by Senate Finance and Appropriations on February 12, 2026, would establish a Virginia College Opportunity Fund tied to the Commonwealth Savers Plan, Virginia's 529 college savings program, and adds reporting requirements. The visible text consists almost entirely of Virginia's open-meetings statute (§ 2.2-3711). The only wagering references are existing closed-meeting exemptions for Virginia Lottery Board casino and sports betting licensing deliberations (subdivisions A 53 and A 54), which the bill carries forward unchanged. The bill was tabled in House Appropriations 21-1 on March 4, 2026, so it is effectively dead for the session.","mechanics":["Amends § 2.2-3711 (both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective July 1, 2026) and §§ 23.1-700, 23.1-701, 23.1-704 and 23.1-706.","Adds a new Article 5 (§§ 23.1-235 and 23.1-236) to Chapter 2 of Title 23.1, plus a new § 23.1-707.2, to establish the Virginia College Opportunity Fund and its report.","Closed-meeting authority in § 2.2-3711 A 20 and A 38 covers the Commonwealth Savers Plan Board's discussion of confidential analyses of non-exchange-traded investments.","Existing subdivisions A 53 and A 54 are reproduced without visible change; they let the Virginia Lottery Board deliberate in closed session on casino gaming licenses and on sports betting license denials, revocations, suspensions and non-renewals under § 58.1-4007.","The supplied text is truncated before the new Title 23.1 sections, so the Fund's funding source, eligibility and reporting terms cannot be confirmed from it."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike Kansas SB303, which imposes a 2% excise tax on all sports wagers, and Illinois HB3159, which earmarks 2% of sports wagering adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS departments, nothing in the visible SB375 text taxes or earmarks gaming revenue. It is a higher-education savings vehicle bill. Its only gaming touchpoint is boilerplate FOIA language on Lottery Board sports betting licensing appeals. It is also unrelated to the college-athlete and campus-advertising measures in Connecticut HB05229 and Maryland HB518.","watch":"Confirm from the full text of new §§ 23.1-235, 23.1-236 and 23.1-707.2 whether the Fund draws on sports betting or casino tax revenue; if the concept returns in a later session, also check whether any change is made to the Lottery Board closed-session authority in § 2.2-3711 A 54.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":80514,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:57.456Z"},"VA-SB390":{"summary":"Chapter 24 (SB 390), signed March 31, 2026, abolishes the Virginia State Apple Board by repealing Chapter 12 of Title 3.2 (§§ 3.2-1200 through 3.2-1217) and makes conforming amendments elsewhere in the Code. Per the bill title, the repeal of the Board and the Apple Fund takes effect July 1, 2028 and a report is required; those enactment clauses are not in the excerpt provided. It has nothing to do with sports betting; its only contact with gaming is that two Virginia FOIA sections are reenacted with their existing gaming provisions unchanged.","mechanics":["Repeals Chapter 12 of Title 3.2 (§§ 3.2-1200 through 3.2-1217), which establishes the Virginia State Apple Board and its funding structure.","Deletes the FOIA exclusion in § 2.2-3705.6 for apple producer sales information provided to the Apple Board under § 3.2-1215, and renumbers the later subdivisions (for example, the Commonwealth Health Research Board exclusion moves from 17 to 16).","Updates cross-references in the closed-meeting statute, § 2.2-3711, to match the renumbering (for example, subdivisions 30, 32 and 33 now point to the new subdivision numbers).","Amends §§ 3.2-1100, 3.2-1104 through 3.2-1106, 10.1-1458 and 15.2-2103.1 as conforming changes; those amendments are not visible in the excerpt provided.","Per the title, the effective date is delayed to July 1, 2028 and a report is required; the excerpt does not include that text."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They all address gaming policy directly, such as mobile sports wagering authorization (AK HB145, MS HB 4074), sports wagering tax changes (IL HB3159, KS SB303) and fantasy contest regulation (IL HB2342, KY HB 904); SB 390 is an agricultural commodity board repeal. Its only overlap with gaming is incidental: the reenacted § 2.2-3705.6 keeps, unchanged, the subdivision 14 FOIA exclusion for proprietary information that charitable gaming suppliers give the Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services, and § 2.2-3711(A)(17) keeps, unchanged, closed-meeting authority for Virginia Lottery Board licensing appeals and proprietary lottery matters.","watch":"Little to watch for operators beyond confirming that the renumbering of § 2.2-3705.6 leaves the charitable gaming supplier confidentiality exclusion and the Lottery Board closed-meeting authority in § 2.2-3711 substantively intact.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":89694,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:58.493Z"},"VA-SB542":{"summary":"SB542 would have created a licensed retail marijuana market in Virginia under the Virginia Cannabis Control Authority, rewriting dozens of Title 4.1 cannabis provisions and related criminal, court, tax, and consumer-protection statutes. The version provided is a Governor's amendment in the nature of a substitute dated April 13, 2026, but the Governor vetoed the bill on May 19, 2026. Nothing in the excerpt regulates sports betting or gaming.","mechanics":["Adds new Title 4.1 chapters 7, 10, 12, and 17, plus new sections in chapters 6, 11, and 13, to build the retail cannabis framework. The substantive text of these chapters is not in the excerpt provided.","Repeals Article 4 of Chapter 41.1 of Title 3.2, and §§ 3.2-5145.4:1, 4.1-611, 4.1-1101.1, 4.1-1105.1, 18.2-248.1, and 18.2-251.1, which are existing cannabis-related provisions.","Amends § 2.2-2905 to add the CEO, agents, officers, and employees of the Virginia Cannabis Control Authority to the list of positions exempt from the state personnel chapter (new item 29). That list already exempts Virginia Lottery employees (item 16).","Amends § 2.2-3114 to require members of the Cannabis Control Authority Board of Directors to file annual financial disclosure statements by February 1, alongside the Virginia Lottery Board and ABC Authority board members.","Amends § 2.2-2818 so that Cannabis Control Authority employees are covered by the state employee health plan.","Also amends § 58.1-301 (tax) and § 59.1-200 (consumer protection), and adds new § 6.2-108 (financial institutions). The content of these changes is not shown in the excerpt."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What is unusual is the procedural history: the Governor proposed a full substitute merging the Aird, Rouse (SB 671), and Lucas (SB 826) bills, yet the bill was ultimately vetoed. The only gaming touchpoint is that the new Cannabis Control Authority is structured in parallel with the Virginia Lottery and ABC Authority for personnel exemptions and ethics disclosures.","watch":"The bill is dead after the May 19, 2026 veto and has no direct sports-betting impact, so the main thing to watch is whether a 2027 reintroduction carries over advertising, tax, or consumer-protection (§ 59.1-200) language that could later be borrowed as a template for gaming regulation in Virginia.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":691324,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:00.791Z"},"VA-SB579":{"summary":"The Senate Courts of Justice substitute for SB579 drops the illegal-gambling and civil-penalty approach suggested by the title and replaces it with an uncodified directive (§ 1) for the Virginia Lottery Board to convene a stakeholder work group to study interactive online sweepstakes games. The work group must report findings and recommendations by December 1, 2026. The bill was continued to the next session in Courts of Justice on a 14-1 vote, so even the study is not yet in motion.","mechanics":["The Virginia Lottery Board must convene a work group of relevant stakeholders, expressly including representatives of the online sweepstakes and social games industry.","Scope covers the legal, economic, technological and public policy implications of interactive online sweepstakes games.","Clause (i) requires review of how sweepstakes are used as marketing and promotional tools by online and retail businesses, including online social games and licensed casino operators.","Clause (ii) requires the group to identify differences between online sweepstakes games and real-money gambling or internet gaming.","Clause (iii) requires the group to develop a definition of \"online sweepstakes games\"; clause (iv) allows other relevant research.","The report is due December 1, 2026 to the Senate General Laws and Technology and Courts of Justice Committees and the House General Laws and Courts of Justice Committees. The substitute contains no penalties, prohibitions or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":["sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, CA AB 831 is the closest analogue: it amends California's existing contest and sweepstakes prohibitions on simulated gambling devices and acts directly. Virginia's substitute instead defers to a study and asks the work group to draft a definition. FL S1580 and FL H0189 also address illegal gaming and internet gambling through penalty and regulatory changes rather than study. Unlike those, VA SB579 as substituted creates no new offenses and gives the sweepstakes industry a seat at the table.","watch":"If the study proceeds, the work group's definition of \"online sweepstakes games\" and its analysis of how licensed casino operators use sweepstakes promotions could shape a later ban or civil-penalty bill, which the original title suggests was the intent, and could reach operators' own promotional sweepstakes, so operators should seek participation in the work group.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":1496,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:09.082Z"},"VA-SB661":{"summary":"SB661 would create a new Chapter 42 in Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4200 through 58.1-4226, in five articles) to license, regulate and tax electronic skill gaming devices (\"skill games\") under the Virginia Lottery Board, with conforming changes to Virginia's criminal gambling statutes and the Lottery Board's general powers. The Governor vetoed the enrolled bill, and on 2026-04-22 it was passed by for the day at the reconvened session, so it is not law unless a veto override succeeds.","mechanics":["Adds new Chapter 42 of Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4200 to 58.1-4226) to regulate and tax skill game machines; the rates, license classes, device caps and fees are in sections not included in the excerpt provided.","Amends the Lottery Board's general statutes (§§ 58.1-4002, -4003, -4006, -4007, -4015.1, -4048) and the casino chapter (§ 58.1-4103), which places skill-game oversight with the existing lottery, sports-betting and casino regulator.","Adds subdivision A 56 to § 2.2-3711, letting the Lottery Board deliberate in closed session on appeals under § 58.1-4007 over the denial, revocation, suspension or nonrenewal of electronic skill gaming device licenses, and on related investigative records.","Amends criminal gambling provisions (§§ 18.2-325, -331.1, -334, -339), adds a new § 18.2-334.7, and adds a new forfeiture-related § 19.2-386.30:1, giving licensed devices a carve-out while setting penalties for unlicensed ones.","Also touches § 37.2-314.2 (problem gambling), § 19.2-389 (criminal history record access) and adds § 11-16.3 in Title 11."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the listed sports-wagering bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), SB661 creates a licensed, taxed category for retail skill game terminals rather than for sports betting. It runs in the opposite direction from IL HB3733, which would widen Illinois's \"gambling device\" definition to capture these kinds of machines, and from CA AB 831, which targets sweepstakes devices that simulate gaming. It is closer in spirit to IL HB3274 and HB3473, which would shield video gaming licensees operating devices not considered gambling, but it goes further by building a full Lottery Board licensing and tax regime.","watch":"Watch whether a veto override or a re-filed version succeeds, and how the new Chapter 42 tax rates, device caps and changes to the Lottery Board's § 58.1-4007 licensing and appeals process interact with existing sports-betting and casino permit holders and with the problem-gambling fund.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":208674,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:14.946Z"},"VA-SB558":{"summary":"SB558 would create a single Virginia Gaming Commission and move Virginia's gaming statutes into a new Title 29.5 (§§ 29.5-100 through 29.5-814). It would repeal the existing lottery and sports betting chapter (Ch. 40, Title 58.1), casino gaming (Ch. 41, Title 58.1), horse racing and pari-mutuel wagering (Ch. 29, Title 59.1), fantasy contests (Ch. 51, Title 59.1) and charitable gaming (Art. 1.1:1, Ch. 8, Title 18.2), with conforming amendments across the Code. On January 28, 2026, General Laws and Technology voted 15-0 to incorporate the bill into SB195 (Aird), so it will not move on its own.","mechanics":["Adds a new Title 29.5 in two parts: Subtitle I, Chapters 1-6 (§§ 29.5-100 to 29.5-648), and Subtitle II, Chapters 7-8 (§§ 29.5-700 to 29.5-814). The provided text is cut off before the substantive Title 29.5 sections, so their licensing, tax and enforcement terms cannot be confirmed here.","Repeals §§ 2.2-2455 and 2.2-2456, Ch. 40 (§§ 58.1-4000 to 4048), Ch. 41 (§§ 58.1-4100 to 4141), Ch. 29 (§§ 59.1-364 to 405), Ch. 51 (§§ 59.1-556 to 570) and Art. 1.1:1 (§§ 18.2-340.15 to 340.37), folding these programs into the new title.","Amending § 2.2-203.3 removes the Virginia Racing Commission from the Secretary of Agriculture and Forestry. The FOIA investigative-records exclusion in § 2.2-3705.3(1) is redrafted to cover the Virginia Gaming Commission in place of the Lottery, Racing Commission and VDACS charitable gaming references. Lottery crimes are recodified at §§ 29.5-713 and 29.5-803 to 29.5-807.","Charitable gaming supplier and manufacturer proprietary information (FOIA § 2.2-3705.6(14)) would go to the Gaming Commission rather than VDACS, and equipment approval rules would be set by a 'Virginia Gaming Commission Board.' Former § 18.2-340.34 is recodified as § 29.5-225.","The tax revenue that funds the Virginia Indigenous People's Trust Fund (§ 2.2-401.01(D)) is re-pointed from § 58.1-4125 to § 29.5-327, which suggests the casino tax distribution carries over into the new title.","Also amends the general gambling offense provisions (§§ 18.2-325, 18.2-326, 18.2-334.2 to 334.5), local taxing provisions (§§ 58.1-3510, 58.1-3732.1) and procurement and APA exemption sections (§§ 2.2-4002, 2.2-4346)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What sets SB558 apart is its scope: it does not adjust one product but replaces the regulators for lottery, sports betting, casinos, racing, fantasy contests and charitable gaming with one commission and recodifies all of them in a new title. Its committee-approved incorporation into SB195 (Aird) means SB195 is now the vehicle to track.","watch":"Track SB195, which absorbed this bill, for whether sports betting permits, tax rates (formerly in Ch. 40, Title 58.1), regulations and confidentiality protections move into Title 29.5 unchanged, and how existing operator permits transition to the new Gaming Commission and its Board.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":857255,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:15.701Z"},"VA-SB609":{"summary":"The House General Laws substitute for SB609 would repeal Virginia's scattered gaming statutes and recodify them in a new Title 29.5 under a single regulator, the Virginia Gaming Commission. The repealed law covers illegal gambling and charitable gaming in Title 18.2, the lottery and casino chapters in Title 58.1 (Chapters 40 and 41), and Title 59.1 Chapters 29, 29.1 and 51. The bill failed to pass from conference on March 14, 2026, so the current structure stays in place. The text provided is truncated and shows only the conforming amendments, not the substantive Title 29.5 provisions.","mechanics":["Creates new Title 29.5 in three subtitles: chapters 1–6 (§§ 29.5-100 to 29.5-650), chapters 7–8 (§§ 29.5-700 to 29.5-814) and chapter 9 (§§ 29.5-900 to 29.5-913).","Repeals: Title 18.2 Article 1 (§§ 18.2-325 to -340) and Article 1.1:1 (charitable gaming, §§ 18.2-340.15 to -340.37); Title 58.1 Chapters 40 and 41; Title 59.1 Chapters 29, 29.1 and 51; and §§ 2.2-2455 and 2.2-2456.","Amends § 2.2-511 so the Attorney General may assist in prosecuting violations of Title 29.5, with the local Commonwealth's Attorney's concurrence.","Renumbers lottery crimes to §§ 29.5-713 and 29.5-803 through 29.5-807 for FOIA purposes, and lists the Virginia Gaming Commission among the licensing bodies whose applicant and licensee investigation records are excluded from mandatory FOIA disclosure (§ 2.2-3705.3).","Transfers charitable gaming oversight from the Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services to the Commission and a Commission Board, including protection of supplier proprietary information (§ 29.5-225) and approval of electronic and mechanical equipment (§ 2.2-3705.6(14)).","Redirects the tax revenue feeding the Virginia Indigenous People's Trust Fund from § 58.1-4125 to new § 29.5-327. The bill also places the Virginia Racing Commission under the Secretary of Agriculture and Forestry (§ 2.2-203.3)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is unusual on its own because it is a wholesale recodification and regulator consolidation rather than a change to rates or activities. It folds lottery, casino, charitable gaming, racing and other gaming chapters into one title under one commission. The caption still refers to a 'Virginia Lottery and Gaming Authority' while the substitute text uses 'Virginia Gaming Commission', which signals the drafting shifted during the session.","watch":"Because this vehicle died in conference, watch for it to return in 2027 and get the full Title 29.5 text to check that the recodified licensing, tax and enforcement rules carry over without substantive change. The provided excerpt does not show the sports-betting tax rate or licensing terms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":910816,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:22.616Z"},"VA-SB819":{"summary":"Senate substitute directing the Virginia Lottery to study whether to regulate advertising for lottery, casino gaming and sports betting, specifically mail and SMS marketing, targeted digital ads using personal data, and use of individual player data, plus mandatory age/addiction warnings and helpline numbers on ads. It also directs the Lottery Board and Director to develop a phased plan to require age verification software on all lottery terminals, with both reports due October 1, 2026. The bill passed the Senate but was left in House General Laws on March 10, 2026, so it is effectively dead for the session.","mechanics":["§ 1 study covers regulations restricting (i) mail and SMS advertising, (ii) targeted digital advertising using personal data such as geolocation, consumer profiles or purchase history, and (iii) use of individual player data for marketing, across lottery, casino gaming and sports betting.","§ 1 also studies requiring all advertisements to carry a warning that the lottery is for persons 18 or older and can be addictive, plus the Virginia Problem Gambling Helpline toll-free number.","§ 2 requires an implementation plan for age verification software (18+) on all lottery terminals, defined as devices interacting with the Department's gaming system to issue tickets and process game transactions. The plan must include a phased schedule, a completion date, cost estimates, alternative schedules, and recommended statutory or appropriation changes, and it may track the normal terminal replacement cycle.","Both reports are due by October 1, 2026, to the General Laws chairs; the § 2 plan also goes to the Governor and the appropriations and finance chairs.","Implementation costs must be built into the Lottery Board's revenue estimates, and work may proceed only after a general appropriation act adopts a forecast that includes those costs.","The bill is study-only: it creates no binding rules, penalties or operator obligations."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which directly restricts gaming advertising on campuses and bars AI-driven bet targeting, VA SB819 only commissions a study of advertising limits, though its scope (SMS, geolocation and player-data targeting) overlaps heavily. It resembles study vehicles such as MD HB 195 (emerging technology and gambling prevention workgroup), MD HB1409 (prevalence studies) and LA SR 178, but is distinctive in targeting the use of operator-held player data for marketing. Its age-verification piece concerns retail lottery terminals at 18+, not the 21+ prediction-market age limits in CT HB05038 and IL HB5059.","watch":"Although SB819 stalled, watch whether the Virginia Lottery takes up the study administratively or the idea returns in 2027, because restrictions on SMS, mail and player-data-driven targeted marketing would directly hit sportsbook CRM and promotional practices.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":3474,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:28.363Z"},"VA-SB756":{"summary":"SB756 would add a sixth eligible casino host locality in Virginia: a large urban-county-executive county, which in practice means Fairfax County. It does this by replacing \"city\" with \"locality\" throughout the casino chapter and confining any Fairfax casino to a narrowly drawn Silver Line, Tysons-area site. It also broadens the definition of \"casino gaming establishment\" to cover the entire property, such as a hotel, conference center, entertainment district or large-scale concert venue. The Governor vetoed it, and on 2026-04-22 it was passed by for the day, so it is not law.","mechanics":["New § 58.1-4107(A)(6) adds as an eligible host locality any county with (i) a 2020 population over 1.15 million, (ii) at least 6% of assessed real estate tax-exempt (FY2021 Tax Department report) and (iii) the urban county executive form of government.","New § 58.1-4107(E) limits that county's casino site to one (i) within a quarter mile of an existing Metro Silver Line station, (ii) within a coordinated mixed-use development of at least 1.5 million sq ft, (iii) within two miles of a regional enclosed mall of at least 1.5 million sq ft gross building area, and (iv) outside the I-495 Beltway.","§ 58.1-4100 expands \"casino gaming establishment\" to the entire property at the licensed address, including a conference center, hotel, entertainment district or large-scale concert venue. This also widens the footprint for on-premises mobile casino gaming and sports betting facilities.","§ 58.1-4032(D)(6) gives sports betting permit applicants that invest at least $300M in a casino and are named by an \"eligible host locality\" substantial and preferred consideration. Under (D)(2), those permits do not count toward the 4–12 permit cap.","The existing process otherwise applies to the new county: local referendum (§ 58.1-4123), Department preliminary review within 45 days, one operator license per locality, the $300M capital investment requirement, $50,000-per-principal application fee, and the 10-year license term.","Gaming Proceeds Fund distributions (6/7/8% tiered local share of AGR under § 58.1-4125) are unchanged in substance; they are simply redirected to the \"locality\" rather than the \"city.\""],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills deal with siting land-based casinos. The Alaska (HB145, SB194), Hawaii (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) and Georgia (HB 686) bills would authorize new sports wagering regimes, while the Illinois bills (e.g., HB2878, HB5034, HB2761) adjust sports wagering licensing and fees. The closest parallel is Illinois HB2761, which, like SB756's link to § 58.1-4032(D)(6), ties sports wagering licensure to a physical venue. SB756 is unusual in using census, tax-exemption and transit criteria so specific that they fit a single county and a single site.","watch":"If the bill is revived, a Fairfax casino licensee would gain a preferred-consideration sports betting permit outside the 12-permit cap and an expanded \"entire property\" footprint for retail and on-premises mobile wagering in the Northern Virginia market, so operators should track it closely.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":46977,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:29.621Z"},"VA-SB826":{"summary":"SB826 would create a regulated adult-use retail cannabis market in Virginia, amending dozens of Code sections across Titles 2.2, 4.1, 18.2, 19.2 and others. It would add new Chapters 7 through 10 and 12 to Title 4.1 under the Virginia Alcoholic Beverage and Cannabis Control Authority, and it is titled as relating to taxation, revenues, and cannabis. On February 12, 2026, Senate Finance and Appropriations voted 15-0 to incorporate it into SB542 (Aird), so it no longer moves as a standalone bill.","mechanics":["Adds §§ 4.1-700 through 4.1-1008 (new Chapters 7–10 of Title 4.1) and §§ 4.1-1200 through 4.1-1206 (new Chapter 12), plus new sections in Chapters 6, 11, 13 and 14 of Title 4.1, building out the retail cannabis framework; the substantive licensing and tax provisions are not in the excerpt provided.","Repeals §§ 4.1-1101.1, 4.1-1105.1, 18.2-248.1 and 18.2-251.1 and amends many Title 18.2 and 19.2 criminal and procedure sections, including marijuana distribution, possession and record provisions.","Amends § 58.1-301 (tax) and adds § 6.2-108 (Title 6.2, financial institutions) and § 54.1-4426, suggesting tax-conformity and banking-access provisions for cannabis businesses; specifics are not in the excerpt.","Keeps the Virginia Alcoholic Beverage and Cannabis Control Authority under the Secretary of Public Safety and Homeland Security (§ 2.2-221), including oversight of its law-enforcement operations (§ 2.2-221(B)(19)).","Reproduces § 2.2-2696 (Virginia Addiction Recovery Council), which covers problem gambling as defined in § 37.2-314.2 and includes two problem-gambling recovery members; the excerpt shows no substantive gambling change."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a cannabis bill. The AK (HB145, SB194), GA (HB 686), HI (HB 1308, HB 2570), MS (HB 4074) and NE (LB421, LB438) bills authorize or regulate sports wagering, while IA SF 605, KS SB303 and IL HB3159 adjust wagering taxes. The closest analog is IL HB5452, which places hemp products under a liquor-control commission, much as SB826 places cannabis under Virginia's alcohol regulator. SB826's only gaming touchpoint is the existing problem-gambling mandate of the Virginia Addiction Recovery Council in § 2.2-2696.","watch":"Track SB542 (Aird), the vehicle SB826 was folded into, for any expansion of the Cannabis Control Authority's powers, any shared problem-gambling or addiction funding, or any advertising rules that could later serve as a model for gaming, though nothing in the provided text directly affects sports-betting operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":910842,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:33.563Z"},"VA-SB91":{"summary":"SB91 (Chapter 482, approved April 8, 2026) is a conforming name-change bill that renames the Eastern Virginia Health Sciences Center at Old Dominion University as Virginia Health Sciences at ODU across a dozen Code of Virginia sections covering ethics, FOIA, open meetings, education, health and insurance law. It does not regulate sports betting or gaming. Wagering appears only because § 2.2-3711, the closed-meetings statute, is reenacted in full, and that statute already contains the Virginia Lottery Board's closed-session authority for casino and sports-betting licensing matters.","mechanics":["Amends §§ 2.2-3106, 2.2-3705.4, 2.2-3711 (both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective July 1, 2026), 22.1-209.2, 23.1-2002, 23.1-2006, 23.1-2007, 32.1-69.3, 32.1-71.5, 32.1-71.6, 38.2-5008 and 54.1-2961 to substitute the new institutional name.","§ 2.2-3106 D: the medical center's federally compliant conflict-of-interest policies continue to satisfy subdivisions C 8 and C 9, subject to annual notice to the Secretary of the Commonwealth by January 31.","§ 2.2-3705.4 A 5 and § 2.2-3711 A 22: the existing FOIA exclusion and closed-meeting authority for proprietary health-care business information of the UVA Medical Center and the ODU health sciences entity carry over under the new name.","§ 2.2-3711 A 53 and A 54: the existing closed-session authority for Virginia Lottery Board deliberations on casino-gaming license actions (under § 58.1-4105) and sports-betting license or permit appeals (under § 58.1-4007) is reenacted. The text shown indicates no substantive change.","The supplied text is truncated and does not display the strike/insert markup, so the exact name-change edits cannot be confirmed line by line."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What is unusual is only that a higher-education renaming bill reenacts Virginia's entire closed-meetings statute. As a result, the Lottery Board's sports-betting and casino deliberation exemptions in § 2.2-3711 A 53 and A 54 appear in the chaptered text even though the bill does nothing to them.","watch":"Nothing here affects operators substantively, but confirm against the chaptered markup that § 2.2-3711 A 53 and A 54, which let the Lottery Board deliberate in closed session on sports-betting and casino license denials, suspensions and revocations, were carried forward unchanged in both the pre- and post-July 1, 2026 versions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":98370,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:34.092Z"},"VT-H133":{"summary":"H.133 is a Vermont short-form bill that proposes to repeal 31 V.S.A. chapter 14 (State Lottery) and chapter 25 (sports wagering) in their entirety. It would also ensure that anyone offering a lottery or sports wagering is subject to the criminal gambling and lottery provisions of 13 V.S.A. chapter 51. Because it is a short-form bill, only the statement of purpose is available and the operative text is omitted.","mechanics":["Repeals 31 V.S.A. chapter 14, which authorizes the Vermont State Lottery.","Repeals 31 V.S.A. chapter 25, which authorizes and regulates sports wagering, including the existing mobile operator framework.","Brings any person offering a lottery or sports wagering within the criminal provisions of 13 V.S.A. chapter 51 (gambling and lotteries).","Short-form bill: no effective date, transition or wind-down terms, or treatment of existing operator contracts and licenses appears in the text.","Introduced by Reps. Stevens, Headrick and Mrowicki; referred 2025-02-04 to House Government Operations and Military Affairs."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Nearly every listed bill moves toward expansion or regulation of legal wagering. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308/SB1569 would authorize sports betting, and IL HB3080/HB4797 would authorize internet gaming. CT HB05229 and IL HB5142 tighten rules within an existing legal market, and FL S1580 adjusts illegal-gambling penalties. H.133 is unusual in proposing outright repeal of an operating sports wagering market and a state lottery, and in re-criminalizing the activity under 13 V.S.A. chapter 51.","watch":"Watch whether the bill gets committee action and a full-text draft, since any version would need to address termination of existing Vermont mobile operator agreements and licenses, wind-down of customer accounts and funds, and the timing of criminal exposure under 13 V.S.A. chapter 51.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":829,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:36.529Z"},"VT-H141":{"summary":"H.141 is Vermont's fiscal year 2025 budget adjustment act, amending the FY2025 Big Bill (2024 Acts and Resolves No. 113) and related 2024 acts to revise appropriations, fund transfers, reversions, reserves and position authorizations across state government. Its only gaming-related provision is in Sec. 52, which lowers the FY2025 transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund (#50250) to the General Fund from $7,000,000 to $6,139,162. The bill was vetoed, and the last recorded action recommitted it to House Appropriations.","mechanics":["Sec. 52 amends Sec. D.101(b)(1)(C): the transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund (#50250) to the General Fund is cut from $7,000,000 to $6,139,162 for FY2025.","Same section revises other General Fund sweeps, including the Cannabis Regulation Fund ($12,000,000 to $15,417,084.32), the Liquor Control Fund ($21,100,000 to $9,543,353) and the Unclaimed Property Fund ($6,500,000 to $10,995,595).","Secs. 1–49 adjust line-item appropriations, with large increases in human services: total human services rises from $5.40 billion to $5.66 billion (Sec. 38).","Sec. 58 delays repeal of the telephone personal property tax to July 1, 2026 and of the alternative telephone gross revenues tax to January 1, 2027.","Sec. 54 sets reserve actions, including $15,168,660.85 to the Budget Stabilization Reserve and $5,480,000 to the 27/53 reserve."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What sets this bill apart for gaming clients is that it touches sports wagering only through a single General Fund sweep figure from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund. It does not change tax rates, licensing or operator obligations.","watch":"Watch whether the reworked budget adjustment, or later appropriations bills, change the size of sweeps from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund, since the $6,139,162 figure signals revenue expectations that could feed pressure to revisit Vermont's operator revenue-share terms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":108717,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:40.957Z"},"VT-H489":{"summary":"H.489 is Vermont's fiscal year 2025 budget adjustment act, amending the FY2025 Big Bill (2024 Acts and Resolves No. 113) and several other 2024 acts to revise line-item appropriations, fund transfers, reversions and reserves across state government. Its only gaming-related provision is a downward revision of the FY2025 transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund to the General Fund. The bill was vetoed and recommitted to House Appropriations on April 9, 2025.","mechanics":["Sec. 52 amends Sec. D.101(b)(1)(C) to reduce the FY2025 transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund (#50250) to the General Fund from $7,000,000 to $6,139,162.","The same section revises other fund sweeps, including the Cannabis Regulation Fund (raised from $12,000,000 to $15,417,084.32) and the Liquor Control Fund (cut from $21,100,000 to $9,543,353).","Large human services increases, including total human services rising from about $5.40 billion to $5.66 billion (Sec. 38), plus $10 million in Provider Stabilization Grants and $11 million for Brattleboro Retreat (Sec. 50).","Sec. 54 allows up to $133.5 million of General Fund surplus otherwise subject to 32 V.S.A. § 308c(a) to be reserved for housing, property tax relief and other uses.","Secs. 59–60 delay the repeal of the telephone personal property tax and the alternative telephone gross revenues tax by one year, to 2026 and 2027."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is notable only because it shows Vermont treating sports wagering enterprise revenue as a General Fund source, and because the downward revision to $6,139,162 suggests FY2025 sports-wagering proceeds came in below the original $7 million estimate. It makes no change to Vermont's sports wagering statute, tax rate or licensing terms.","watch":"Track the reworked budget adjustment bill that follows the veto to see whether the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund transfer figure changes, since lower-than-projected transfers can prompt legislators to revisit operator revenue-share terms or market structure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":86081,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:42.719Z"},"VT-H288":{"summary":"H.288 expands Vermont's veterans' motor vehicle registration fee exemption (23 V.S.A. § 378) to give resident veterans discharged under other than dishonorable conditions a 50 percent reduction on registration fees for a range of vehicles and boats, plus one 100 percent reduction per calendar year. It funds the foregone registration revenue by diverting sports wagering revenues and fees under 31 V.S.A. chapter 25 from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund to the Transportation Fund.","mechanics":["Sec. 1, 23 V.S.A. § 378(b): 50 percent reduction of registration fees for pleasure cars, motorcycles, motor-driven cycles, all-surface vehicles, exhibition vehicles, diesel pleasure cars, snowmobiles, antique snowmobiles, motorboats, ATVs and the Clean Air Fund fee under 3 V.S.A. § 2822(m), on submission of an approved DD Form 214; the veteran must be listed as owner or co-owner.","Sec. 1, § 378(c): one 100 percent reduction per calendar year for the same fees, unavailable to veterans already receiving the VA-assisted vehicle exemption under subsection (a).","Sec. 1, § 378(a): the existing full exemption for VA-financed vehicles is extended from 'honorably discharged' veterans to those discharged under other than dishonorable conditions.","Sec. 2, 31 V.S.A. § 1304: sports wagering revenues and fees still go to the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund, except an amount needed to offset foregone § 378 registration fees, which goes to the Transportation Fund.","The bill does not cap or quantify the diverted amount and does not change operator tax rates.","Sec. 3: effective January 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills uses existing sports wagering revenue to backfill an unrelated tax expenditure. Bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308 authorize and tax wagering, and IA SSB 1157 and IL HB5143 change rates or revenue use. H.288 leaves Vermont's wagering framework and rates untouched and only reallocates the proceeds by amending the § 1304 allocation provision, with no dollar cap.","watch":"Watch whether an uncapped diversion of sports wagering receipts to the Transportation Fund creates fiscal pressure on the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund, which could later prompt efforts to raise Vermont's operator revenue share or fees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4288,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:46.053Z"},"VT-H493":{"summary":"H.493 (Act No. 27), the \"BIG BILL – Fiscal Year 2026 Appropriations Act,\" is Vermont's general operating budget for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2026. It appropriates funds for all state agencies by function, and it sets general rules on federal funds, new positions and interim adjustments. In the portion of the text provided, the only gaming-related item is a single appropriation line for the Liquor and Lottery Commission, which administers Vermont's sports wagering program.","mechanics":["Sec. B.236.1 appropriates $18,282,300 to the Liquor and Lottery Commission: $10,866,788 personal services and $7,415,512 operating expenses.","That appropriation is funded mainly from enterprise funds ($17,836,721), with $125,000 in special funds, $250,579 from the Tobacco Fund and $70,000 in interdepartmental transfers.","Sec. A.102(c): narrative portions apply only to FY2026 unless codified. Sec. A.104: the act does not impair existing laws except as specifically provided.","Sec. A.107 freezes total authorized state positions in FY2026, except positions authorized in the 2025 session and limited-service positions under 32 V.S.A. § 5.","Signed by the Governor on May 21, 2025.","The text supplied is truncated at Sec. B.514 (about page 69 of 277), so the D sections (fund transfers and reversions), E sections (program language) and G section (effective dates) were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. What stands out here is that sports wagering appears only implicitly, through the funding line for the Liquor and Lottery Commission's operations. Nothing in the reviewed text changes wagering tax rates, licensing or the operator revenue-share structure.","watch":"Review the untruncated D and E sections for any transfers, reversions or program language involving sports wagering revenue or the Commission's enterprise fund, since those would be where an appropriations act could quietly redirect wagering proceeds or alter Commission operations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":75887,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:48.684Z"},"VT-H913":{"summary":"H.913 is a Vermont short-form bill whose statement of purpose would amend the criminal gambling statute (13 V.S.A. chapter 51) so that offering prediction market securities or commodities contingent on sports, contests, natural persons, politics and campaigns, disasters, war, all-hazards, or death counts as prohibited wagering. It would also amend 9 V.S.A. chapter 101 to void agreements for such contracts and allow civil actions to recover lost money. It would add a $0.50 per-wager fee on all wagers placed through Vermont's licensed sports wagering operators. Because it is a short-form bill, no operative text exists yet and the details are unknown.","mechanics":["Short-form bill: the statutory text is omitted; only the statement of purpose is available, so definitions, penalties and effective dates are not yet drafted.","Criminal: amends 13 V.S.A. ch. 51 so the wagering prohibition covers offering prediction market securities or commodities on sports, contests, natural persons, politics and campaigns, disasters, war, all-hazards, or death.","Civil: amends 9 V.S.A. ch. 101 (wagering contracts) so these contracts are void and losses are recoverable by civil action.","Fiscal: adds a $0.50 per-wager fee on all wagers made through the State's sports wagering operators. Who bears the fee and how it is remitted is not specified.","Referred 2026-02-25 to House Government Operations and Military Affairs; it has 13 bipartisan-area sponsors led by Rep. Stevens of Waterbury."],"issue_tags":["prediction","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like MN HF 4437/SF 4511 and NC H1171, H.913 takes a prohibition approach, folding prediction markets into the criminal gambling ban rather than regulating them. That contrasts with the ORACLE Act model in IL HB5059 and NY S09414, which licenses or conditions platforms with age-21 limits, and with CT HB05038's age and advertising rules. Its event categories go well beyond IL HB5142, which reaches only sports-event contracts by redefining sports wagering, to include politics, war, disasters and death. Its civil voiding and loss-recovery remedy is distinctive. Unusually, it pairs the ban with a $0.50 per-wager fee on licensed sportsbooks, whereas NJ A5336/S4447 tax prediction market operators themselves.","watch":"Watch the full text when it replaces the short form, particularly how the $0.50 per-wager fee is imposed on licensed operators (absorbed or passed to bettors, and its effect on low-stakes and parlay wagers) and whether the definitions of prediction market securities could sweep in licensed sportsbook products or exchange-style offerings.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1753,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:52.331Z"},"VT-H790":{"summary":"H.790 (Act No. 74) is Vermont's fiscal year 2026 budget adjustment act, amending line-item appropriations, fund transfers, reversions and reserves in the FY2026 appropriations act (2025 Acts and Resolves No. 27). It also makes unrelated statutory changes, including a new Child Care Contribution Reserve (32 V.S.A. § 308f) and conforming references to a new Office of Legislative Operations. Its only gaming content is a downward revision of the estimated FY2026 transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund to the General Fund.","mechanics":["Sec. 53 amends Sec. D.101(c)(1) to cut the estimated transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund (#50250) to the General Fund from $7,600,000 to $6,678,828, a reduction of about $921,000.","Under D.101(c), enterprise-fund transfers may not exceed actual fiscal year-end profits, net of any amount needed to cure a fund deficit, and the stated figures are estimates for budget development only.","For comparison, the Liquor Control Fund transfer estimate rises from $14,849,754 to $16,802,502. The Cannabis Regulation Fund retention threshold rises to $4,665,150, with the estimated 70/30 split to the General Fund and the Substance Misuse Prevention Fund revised to $9,182,689 and $3,935,438.","Sec. 58 adds 32 V.S.A. § 308f, requiring an annual consensus estimate of fourth-quarter Child Care Contribution receipts and a General Fund reserve of that amount during year-end closeout.","The act was approved by the Governor on March 5, 2026. The text provided is truncated at Sec. 74 of a 60-page act, so later sections were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is a routine mid-year budget true-up. It does not change Vermont's sports wagering statute, tax or revenue-share terms, licensing or consumer protection rules; it only re-estimates the year-end profit transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund. The roughly 12 percent downward revision is notable as a signal that the State's FY2026 sports wagering receipts are running below the original budget assumption.","watch":"Watch whether the downward revision of the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund transfer to $6,678,828 prompts FY2027 budget proposals to adjust Vermont's operator revenue-share terms or enterprise-fund cost allocations to close the gap, and confirm that the untruncated remainder of the act contains no wagering provisions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":68662,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:53.030Z"},"VT-H951":{"summary":"H.951 (Act No. 144) is Vermont's FY2027 general appropriations act (the \"BIG BILL\"), funding state operations and non-bonded capital spending for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2027. In the portion of the text provided, its only gaming-related content is the operating appropriation for the Department of Liquor and Lottery, the agency that regulates Vermont's sports wagering market. No substantive sports-betting policy appears in the reviewed text.","mechanics":["Sec. B.236.1 appropriates $17,210,042 to the Liquor and Lottery Commission: $11,295,690 personal services and $5,914,352 operating expenses.","That appropriation is funded mainly by enterprise funds ($16,762,179), plus $252,863 from the tobacco fund, $125,000 from special funds and $70,000 from interdepartmental transfers.","Sec. A.107 freezes the total number of authorized state positions for FY2027, apart from positions the 2026 session authorized and limited-service positions, which constrains regulator headcount.","Sec. A.102(c) limits narrative provisions to FY2027 unless codified, and Sec. A.104 preserves existing law except as specifically provided.","The text supplied is truncated at Sec. B.504. The D sections (fund transfers and reservations) and E sections (program language), where any routing of sports wagering revenue would likely appear, were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this is a routine omnibus budget: wagering appears only as a line-item appropriation for the Liquor and Lottery Commission, with no change to sports wagering tax rates, licensing or consumer-protection rules in the reviewed text.","watch":"Check the untruncated D and E sections for any transfer, reservation or earmark of sports wagering revenue, or any change to Department of Liquor and Lottery staffing or regulatory funding, that could affect operator obligations or revenue-share arrangements.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":82337,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:57.525Z"},"WA-HB2205":{"summary":"SHB 2205 amends Washington's tribal-only sports wagering statute to allow betting on in-state college teams (while barring proposition bets on named athletes enrolled at Washington institutions), ban wagering on minor league sports, and let tribes accept on-premises wagers at any tribe's gaming facility rather than only their own. It also creates a new class C felony for threatening sports officials, coaches, or participants in connection with a wagerable event and bars violators from placing sports wagers. It does not authorize statewide mobile or commercial (non-tribal) sports betting.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 deletes the exclusion of Washington-located colleges from the 'collegiate sport or athletic event' definition, so wagering on in-state college teams becomes permissible, but bets on the performance or nonperformance of any specifically named participant enrolled at a Washington institution are prohibited.","Sec. 2 excludes minor league sports from 'professional sport or athletic event' and expressly prohibits sports wagering on any minor league sport.","Sec. 3 changes compact amendment authority from sports wagering on 'its' Indian lands to 'Indian lands' generally, and Sec. 4 changes the physical-presence requirement from 'that' tribe's gaming facility to 'a' tribe's gaming facility, opening the door to cross-tribal or hosted wagering arrangements while keeping on-premises-only acceptance.","Sec. 5 adds RCW 9.46.037(5): threatening a sports official, coach, or participant about a wagerable event (threat of bodily injury or property damage, communicated directly or indirectly) is a class C felony under RCW 9A.20.021.","Sec. 5 adds RCW 9.46.037(6): any person who violates the threat provision may not place sports wagers, with no stated mechanism for exclusion lists or operator obligations.","Sec. 7 names the act the 'sports wagering integrity act'; Sec. 6 is a severability clause. No effective date beyond the default applies, and as of 2026-02-19 the bill sits in the House Rules 'X' file, signalling it is unlikely to advance this session."],"issue_tags":["integrity","legalize","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own the bill is unusual because it expands the betting menu (in-state college games) while narrowing it elsewhere (minor leagues, in-state college player props). It pairs this with a criminal threat-to-participants offense that carries a wagering ban, similar to bettor-harassment measures seen in some other states, all while preserving Washington's tribal-exclusive, on-premises-only model.","watch":"Operators and tribal partners should watch how the 'a tribe's gaming facility' and 'Indian lands' changes would be implemented through compact amendments, since they could enable shared or off-reservation tribal wagering, and how the threat-based wagering ban would be enforced without a defined exclusion list or operator duty.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":7357,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:09:05.204Z"},"WI-AB1034":{"summary":"AB1034 creates Wis. Stat. 36.11(12), authorizing the UW Board of Regents to pay student athletes for name, image, and likeness, barring UW policies that block NIL use outside official team activities or block professional representation, and prohibiting student athletes from entering NIL deals that pay for athletic performance or for endorsing gambling, including sports betting and casino gambling. It also shifts debt service for UW–Madison athletic facility maintenance from program revenue (the 40 percent allocation in s. 20.285(1)(gj)) to $14.6 million annual GPR, and adds $200,000 annually each for the UW–Milwaukee Klotsche Center and the UW–Green Bay soccer complex. The status shows the bill was signed with partial vetoes and that a veto override failed, so the enacted text may differ from this introduced version.","mechanics":["New s. 36.11(12)(h)2.b. bars a student athlete from any NIL agreement compensating endorsement of 'gambling, including sports betting and casino gambling,' alongside tobacco, vaping, alcohol, banned substances, illegal activity, and any other activity the board proscribes.","Student athletes must disclose each third-party NIL agreement before signing, in the manner the board or institution prescribes (s. 36.11(12)(g)); agreements conflicting with board, institution, or athletic program policies or agreements are barred (h)1.","The board may enter NIL compensation agreements and affiliation agreements to facilitate deals and manage program revenue (b), (k), but may not use GPR to enable NIL opportunities (d); NIL pay is not a grant under sub. (6) and does not make the athlete a system employee (e), (f).","Third-party payors that are separate legal entities not owned or controlled by the board are not treated as acting on the board's behalf (j).","Open-records exemption under s. 19.35 for NIL agreement terms and for athletic program revenue records kept confidential for competitive reasons (i).","Appropriations: $14,600,000 GPR annually under new s. 20.285(1)(cj) for UW–Madison athletic facilities debt service, replacing the 40 percent program-revenue allocation in (gj); $200,000 each under (ck) Klotsche Center and (cL) Green Bay soccer complex, for 2025-26 and 2026-27."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike MD HB518 and CT HB05229, which regulate operators directly through college-athlete protections and campus advertising limits, WI AB1034 reaches gambling only on the athlete side, through a flat ban on NIL endorsement deals for sports betting and casino gambling. It is closer to the NIL frameworks in HI HB 2384/SB3263, TX HB126, NE LB370, and LA HB 513, which set institutional NIL authority and disclosure rules. TN SB1662 instead funds athletic programs from sportsbook vendor fees, whereas Wisconsin funds facility debt service from general purpose revenue.","watch":"Watch whether the gambling endorsement ban in s. 36.11(12)(h)2.b. survived the partial veto, and how broadly the Regents use their power to proscribe 'any other activity,' because together they effectively close off UW student-athlete NIL partnerships for sportsbooks and casino operators and may extend to adjacent products such as fantasy or prediction platforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":11961,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:09:05.647Z"},"WA-SB6137":{"summary":"SB 6137 (Chapter 246, Laws of 2026, the 'sports wagering integrity act') amends Washington's tribal-only sports wagering statutes. It deletes the bar on wagering on games involving in-state colleges and replaces it with narrower bans on in-state player props, coach decisions and officiating decisions. It also loosens the 'its Indian lands' and 'that facility' wording in the compact and internet-transmission provisions, and creates a new gross misdemeanor for wager-related threats against officials, coaches and participants.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 (RCW 9.46.038): deletes the clause excluding Washington-located institutions from 'collegiate sport or athletic event,' so wagering on in-state college games becomes permissible. It still prohibits wagers on (i) the performance or nonperformance of any named individual participant enrolled at a Washington institution, (ii) in-game decisions (substitutions, timeouts, replay challenges, appeals) by coaches employed by Washington institutions, and (iii) officiating decisions in games involving Washington institutions.","Sec. 2 also defines 'officiating decision' broadly to include officiating assignments and penalty, foul, violation or replay calls. The definition of sports wagering covers esports and portions of events, excludes horse racing under ch. 67.16 RCW, and prohibits wagering on any minor league sport. The chaptered text does not show underlining, so which of these definitions are new is not clear from the copy provided.","Sec. 3 (RCW 9.46.0364): tribal compacts may be amended to authorize sports wagering on 'Indian lands' rather than 'its Indian lands.' Sec. 4 (RCW 9.46.0368): internet wagers may be placed while the customer is physically present at 'a tribe's gaming facility' rather than 'that' facility. Together these appear to open the door to cross-tribe or shared-premises arrangements, but still require on-premises placement.","Sec. 5 (RCW 9.46.037): existing match-fixing and insider-information offenses remain class C felonies. New subsection (2) makes it a gross misdemeanor to knowingly threaten bodily injury or property damage to a sports official, coach or participant where the threat relates to a wager on that event, including threats sent by electronic communication. The offense does not apply where the target can see the person lacks the ability to carry out the threat.","Anyone convicted under new RCW 9.46.037(2) may not place any sports wagers.","Severability clause (Sec. 6). Effective June 11, 2026. The exclusive tribal model is unchanged (Sec. 1 intent statement), with no commercial or statewide mobile licensing."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. The bill is unusual in pairing a market expansion (lifting Washington's in-state collegiate ban) with targeted in-state college player-prop, coach-decision and officiating-decision bans. It also criminalizes bettor threats against athletes and officials and imposes a statutory wagering ban on those convicted, rather than relying on operator self-exclusion. It does all of this while keeping sports wagering confined to tribal compacts and on-premises placement.","watch":"Watch how the Gambling Commission and tribes implement the new in-state college market restrictions, since any compact amendments and the reach of the 'officiating decision' ban affect permissible markets. Also watch how the Commission and tribes read the 'Indian lands' and 'a tribe's gaming facility' changes, which may enable cross-tribe or shared-platform arrangements. Finally, watch how operators will be expected to identify and block bettors convicted under new RCW 9.46.037(2).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":9951,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:09:16.406Z"},"CT-HB5035":{"summary":"Connecticut HB 5035 (substitute) requires public school students to keep personal wireless communication devices powered off and stored during the school day unless access is authorized for instructional purposes, and bars students from accessing social media through school-provided Internet except for instruction. Local and regional boards of education must adopt implementing policies, including emergency communication and discipline procedures, and notify parents annually. It has nothing to do with sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles; the only gaming reference is to 'gaming device' and 'gaming' as device functions.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(a)(1): 'wireless communication device' covers any portable device capable of voice, messaging or data communication, including cell phones, tablets, computers, gaming devices and smart watches, but excludes district-provided instructional technology.","Sec. 1(b)(1): no student may access such a device on school property during the school day (scheduled start to end time, grades K-12) unless authorized for instructional purposes; devices must be powered off and stored as the board prescribes, e.g. lockers.","Sec. 1(b)(2): exceptions for use required by an IEP under IDEA or a Section 504 accommodations plan, or deemed necessary for health by a licensed physician, PA or APRN.","Sec. 1(c): bars student access to social media platforms via school-provided Internet during the school day except for instructional purposes.","Sec. 1(d)-(e): boards must adopt policies covering emergency parent communication and discipline, notify parents annually, and include the policies in student handbooks.","Effective July 1, 2027; passed the House, and on 2026-04-29 was moved to the foot of the Senate calendar."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable in subject. The iGaming authorization bills (IL HB3080/HB4797, MA H332/S235, MD HB1343/SB885, ME LD1164), sports wagering bills (MS HB 4074, NJ A 3258) and advertising bills (MI SB0713/SB0714, NJ AR150) all regulate gambling directly. The closest overlap is with the youth and social-media bills (MI HB4388, NC H462, CO HB1255), but HB 5035 regulates school device use, not platform operators. Its only gaming link is that 'gaming' appears as a device function in the definition of 'access' in Sec. 1(a)(3).","watch":"Nothing here imposes obligations on betting, iGaming or card-break operators; at most, note that it restricts minors' in-school access to gaming and social-media functions, so monitor whether later amendments add youth-gambling or app-specific provisions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":4003,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:23.289Z"},"DC-B26-0659":{"summary":"This enacted act adopts the local portion of the District of Columbia's Fiscal Year 2027 budget, with total operating appropriations capped at $21,876,166,228, allocated agency by agency. Its only gaming-related content is a carryover clause for the Office of the Chief Financial Officer. That clause makes all money deposited in the Internet Gaming Revenue Fund, regardless of fiscal year, available for spending through September 30, 2027, and does the same for FY2027 deposits into the DC Lottery Reimbursement Fund. It does not create, amend or regulate any gaming, wagering or lottery program.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 caps FY2027 operating appropriations at the lesser of total District revenues or $21,876,166,228. Of that, $12,242,076,569 is local funds and $782,454,817 is dedicated taxes.","OCFO appropriation (Governmental Direction and Support, para. (21)) is $221,181,516. All funds deposited into the Internet Gaming Revenue Fund, without regard to fiscal year, are authorized for expenditure and remain available until September 30, 2027.","Under the same paragraph, funds deposited during FY2027 into the DC Lottery Reimbursement Fund are authorized for expenditure through September 30, 2027.","General provisos let local and other funds be reprogrammed through November 15, 2027 (proviso (8)). Unspent balances in non-lapsing funds roll into FY2027 (proviso (14)).","The act became law as L26-0179, effective September 15, 2026. The supplied text is truncated partway through the Public Education Systems section, so later agency lines were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like the omnibus budgets in MI HB5619 and NJ A5327/S2027, this is a general appropriations act in which gaming appears only as a fund line. MI HB6127 and MI HB4346 are different: they substantively change how internet gaming fund money is spent or allocated, whereas DC B26-0659 merely carries forward spending authority for an already existing Internet Gaming Revenue Fund. Unlike the iGaming authorization bills (IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885, MA H332/S235), it contains no licensing, tax-rate or regulatory provisions.","watch":"Watch the companion FY2027 Budget Support Act and the underlying statute governing the Internet Gaming Revenue Fund, since any change to DC gaming revenue use, rates or regulation would appear there rather than in this appropriations act.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":81923,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:27.583Z"},"CT-SB240":{"summary":"SB 240 directs the Commissioner of Mental Health and Addiction Services to develop a comprehensive public health plan to identify, prevent and reduce the risks of Internet gaming disorder, and to identify any link between that disorder and gambling behaviors. 'Internet gaming disorder' is not defined; the term usually refers to compulsive video or online game play rather than licensed online casino wagering. The bill creates no operator obligations and is a planning and reporting mandate only.","mechanics":["Section 1 assigns the Commissioner of Mental Health and Addiction Services (DMHAS) to develop the plan; it is a new, uncodified section.","The plan must cover data monitoring, public education, early screening, health care provider training and evidence-based treatment pathways.","The plan must also coordinate with existing gambling harm prevention efforts, with particular attention to youth and other vulnerable populations.","The plan must identify any link between Internet gaming disorder and gambling behaviors.","The report is due to the Public Health Committee by January 1, 2027, under CGS 11-4a.","The act takes effect from passage; it sets no enforcement, penalties, fees or appropriation, and does not define 'Internet gaming disorder.'"],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the iGaming authorization bills in IL (HB3080, HB4797), MD (HB1343, SB885, SB884) and MA (H332, S235), SB 240 neither authorizes nor regulates any form of gaming. It also imposes no direct operator restrictions, unlike ME LD 2080 (credit card ban) or NJ A 3258 (micro-bet ban). It is closest in spirit to NJ AR150's harm-reduction focus, but it is a public-health study aimed at video-game-style 'gaming disorder' and its possible crossover into gambling. That framing is unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch whether the DMHAS plan, due January 1, 2027, treats gambling-like mechanics such as loot boxes, mystery packs, live card breaks or social and sweepstakes casinos as a bridge between gaming disorder and gambling, and whether it recommends youth-focused screening, advertising or product restrictions that later legislation could impose on operators and collectibles companies.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1916,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:28.965Z"},"DC-B26-0660":{"summary":"This is the District of Columbia's emergency act adopting the local portion of the FY2027 budget (fiscal year ending September 30, 2027), setting agency-by-agency appropriations and fund-availability rules. Its only gaming content in the text provided is a proviso under the Office of the Chief Financial Officer keeping the Internet Gaming Revenue Fund and the DC Lottery Reimbursement Fund available for expenditure through September 30, 2027. It creates no new wagering rules, tax rates, or licensing requirements.","mechanics":["Caps total FY2027 operating appropriations at the lesser of total District revenues or $21,876,166,228, of which $12,242,076,569 is local funds and $782,454,817 is dedicated taxes (Sec. 2, proviso (1)).","Office of the Chief Financial Officer receives $221,181,516. All funds deposited in the Internet Gaming Revenue Fund, regardless of fiscal year, are authorized for expenditure and remain available until September 30, 2027.","All funds deposited during FY2027 into the DC Lottery Reimbursement Fund, also under the CFO, are authorized for expenditure and remain available until September 30, 2027.","Unspent balances in non-lapsing funds at the end of FY2026 carry forward and are appropriated for FY2027 (proviso (14)). Reprogramming and transfers are allowed through November 15, 2027 (proviso (8)).","Emergency act: published as A26-0378 at 73 DCR 011097 and expires October 27, 2026, so a permanent or temporary companion measure is needed for continuity.","The text supplied is truncated partway through the Public Education Systems heading (at Special Education Transportation), so any lottery or gaming-agency appropriations appearing later in the act cannot be confirmed."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like the omnibus appropriations bills listed, including MI HB5619, MI SB0878, NJ A5327 and NJ S2027, this act touches gaming only as a budget line. Unlike MI HB4346 and MI HB6127, it does not change how internet gaming revenue is allocated or how the gaming fund may be spent. It only preserves expenditure availability of the existing Internet Gaming Revenue Fund. It contains nothing comparable to the substantive iGaming authorization bills (IL HB3080, MD HB1343, MA S235), the advertising limits (MI SB0713), or the prediction-market measures (CT HB05038, NJ A5336).","watch":"Operators should check whether the untruncated text or the companion Budget Support Act redirects Internet Gaming Revenue Fund or lottery-related balances, or changes sports-wagering fees or taxes, since this appropriations act only carries those funds forward.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":81898,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:37.395Z"},"CO-HB1255":{"summary":"HB26-1255 would have required large social media platforms to keep a 24/7 law-enforcement contact process and staffed hotline, and to comply with search warrants for user-account information within 24 hours. It also required operators to report to the user's local police or sheriff, within 24 hours, any human-reviewed account restriction, suspension, or termination imposed for publicly posted content violating the platform's policies on specific or imminent threats. The Governor vetoed it on 2026-05-28, so none of it takes effect.","mechanics":["Coverage (new 16-3-314(1)(b)): a public or semipublic site or app with at least 1,000,000 discrete monthly users, user profiles, and user-generated content viewable by others. Services whose predominant function is interactive or virtual gaming, as described in 6-1-1601(4)(b)(VII), are expressly included. Excluded are internal enterprise tools, pure e-commerce marketplaces, health or reproductive-care services, review-only comment features, and predominantly provider-selected news, sports, entertainment, or e-commerce content whose chat features relate to that content.","Warrants (16-3-314(2)-(4)): operators must run an always-available process and staffed hotline, acknowledge a warrant within 8 hours, give status updates, and comply within 24 hours. This is subject to the Colorado Privacy Act, the Reproductive Health Equity Act, and the state constitution, and a court may extend the deadline on a written good-cause finding with no 'adverse result' under 18 U.S.C. 2705(a)(2).","Enforcement of the warrant section: the AG or a district attorney may seek an injunction, restitution, disgorgement, damages, and civil penalties of up to $250,000 per violation.","Adverse-action reporting (new 6-1-1602): operators must report to the user's municipal police or county sheriff within 24 hours, including the content at issue. Reporting is barred where the operator believes in good faith that the content is legally protected health-care activity. A violation is a deceptive trade practice under 6-1-105(1)(vvvv), and the AG has rulemaking authority.","Amendments to other law: Section 5 would have amended 16-3-313, as added by SB26-011, cutting that bill's warrant deadline for covered platforms from 72 to 24 hours and raising its penalty from $5,000 to $250,000 per violation, contingent on SB26-011 becoming law. Section 4 also amends the active-user threshold in 6-1-1601(4)(a)(I); the scanned strike-through is garbled, so the new figure is unclear.","Effective date would have been the 90-day post-adjournment date (August 12, 2026), subject to referendum, but the bill was vetoed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close analogue. AK HB47 targets criminal liability for generated sexual imagery and CSAM rather than platform warrant or reporting duties. AL HB351 is a general consumer data-privacy bill. AL HJR51 only creates a children's internet safety study commission. What stands out about CO HB1255 is the combination of a 24-hour warrant-compliance mandate, compulsory proactive reporting of threat-based moderation decisions to local police, and $250,000-per-violation penalties, along with its express inclusion of interactive or virtual gaming services in the social media definition.","watch":"If this is reintroduced after the veto, check whether a sportsbook, iGaming, or card-break app with user profiles, community chat or live-stream comments, and 1 million or more monthly users falls outside the provider-selected sports or entertainment content and e-commerce exclusions, or inside the interactive-gaming inclusion, because that would trigger 24-hour warrant compliance, threat-report duties, and $250,000-per-violation exposure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":14535,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:39.320Z"},"DC-B26-0661":{"summary":"DC B26-0661 is the District's omnibus Fiscal Year 2027 Budget Support Act, enacted as D.C. Law L26-0189 effective October 2, 2026. It amends dozens of unrelated laws covering telework, lobbying fees, the Attorney General's litigation fund, economic development incentives, education funding, tax conformity, TIF districts and special fund transfers. The table of contents lists a Title II Subtitle DD titled \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund\" (p. 59), but the text supplied is cut off in Title I, so the substance of that subtitle cannot be confirmed here.","mechanics":["Title II, Subtitle DD, \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund\" (p. 59 of the enrolled text) is the only gaming-related heading in the act; its operative text is not in the excerpt provided.","Title VII includes Subtitle M (Special Fund Transfers) and Subtitle N (Special Fund Sweep Reversals), which could move money into or out of dedicated funds; whether any gaming fund is affected cannot be confirmed from the excerpt.","Sec. 1032 raises the lobbyist registration fee from $350 to $500, and the fee for nonprofit lobbyists from $100 to $250, extending the nonprofit rate to 501(c)(4) organizations. It also raises the late-filing penalty to $200 per day for up to 60 days.","Sec. 1072 redirects most Office of the Attorney General litigation recoveries into the Litigation Support Fund, with carve-outs for listed funds. Where an agency referred the matter or gave significant support, recoveries are split 50/50 with the agency's special fund or the General Fund. Outside contingency-counsel amounts are excluded from the $23.5 million cap.","Effective October 2, 2026, published in the DC Register Vol. 73, p. 013120."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like the other omnibus budget measures listed, AK HB53, AL HB238, and CA AB101 and AB109, DC B26-0661 is primarily a fiscal vehicle; none of those listings indicate gaming provisions. What sets DC's bill apart is a dedicated \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund\" subtitle. That points to the District earmarking or restructuring proceeds from online gaming through the budget process rather than through standalone gaming legislation.","watch":"Obtain the full text of Subtitle DD and the Title VII fund transfer and sweep subtitles to confirm whether internet gaming or sports-wagering revenue is being re-earmarked, swept to the General Fund, or tied to new operator obligations or a broader iGaming authorization.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":246898,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:43.211Z"},"MD-HB1255":{"summary":"HB1255 places a question on the November 2026 general election ballot asking Maryland voters whether to expand commercial gaming to authorize Internet gaming (iGaming), as required by Article XIX, § 1(e) of the Maryland Constitution. If voters approve, the General Assembly may by later legislation authorize the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to issue iGaming licenses, limited to video lottery operation (casino) licensees and entities under contract with them. The bill itself sets no tax rate, license fee, or operating rules; it only states legislative intent that the State's share of revenue go to dedicated purposes including public education.","mechanics":["Section 1(a): the General Assembly may authorize, by law, the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to license Internet gaming; the bill does not itself authorize operations.","Section 1(b)(2): licenses may issue only to holders of a video lottery operation license (as defined in State Government § 9–1A–01) and, subject to further eligibility criteria set by law, entities under contract with such holders. This effectively ties iGaming to Maryland's six casinos and their platform partners.","Section 1(b)(1): implementing legislation must set criteria for eligible applicants and specify the permissible forms and means of conducting Internet gaming.","Section 2: if the referendum passes, the State's share of iGaming revenue is to be used for dedicated purposes including public education. No tax rate or fee is specified.","Section 4: ballot question at the November 2026 general election. If a majority votes 'For', the Act takes effect 30 days after the official canvass. If a majority votes 'Against', the Act is null and void with no further legislative action required.","Section 5: the Act takes effect July 1, 2026, solely to enable the referendum. Cross-filed with SB 761; assigned to Ways and Means, with a hearing set for 3/05."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB3080/HB4797 and OH SB197, which are full statutory iGaming frameworks setting licensing, taxes and operating rules, MD HB1255 is only an enabling referendum that defers every operational and fiscal term to future legislation. It most resembles the MA bills (H332, H4431, S235) in aim, but Maryland's constitutional requirement for voter approval of gaming expansion makes a ballot question a precondition. Its land-based tether, limiting licenses to casino licensees and their contracted partners, contrasts with ME LD1164, which would channel iGaming to the Wabanaki Nations.","watch":"Watch whether the cross-filed SB 761 or the implementing legislation that follows a successful November 2026 vote limits standalone online operators to contracted 'skin' status under casino licensees, and what tax rate, skin caps, and eligibility criteria it sets under Section 1(b).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4328,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:48.585Z"},"MD-SB761":{"summary":"SB761 would place a question on the November 2026 Maryland general election ballot, as required by Article XIX, § 1(e) of the Maryland Constitution for any expansion of commercial gaming, asking voters whether to authorize Internet gaming. If voters approve, the General Assembly may by later law authorize the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to issue Internet gaming licenses, limited to video lottery operation licensees and entities under contract with them. The bill itself contains no tax rate, licensing fee or operating rules, and it was withdrawn by its sponsor on March 13, 2026.","mechanics":["Section 1(a): the General Assembly may by law authorize the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to license Internet gaming in Maryland.","Section 1(b)(2): licenses are available only to holders of a video lottery operation license (the casino licensees, as defined in State Government Article § 9–1A–01) and, subject to further eligibility criteria set by law, entities under contract with those holders.","Section 1(b)(1): implementing legislation must set the eligibility criteria for applicants and specify the permissible forms and means of conducting Internet gaming.","Section 2: the State's share of Internet gaming revenue is to go to dedicated purposes, including public education funding; no rate or amount is specified.","Sections 3 and 4: the ballot question for November 2026 reads 'Do you favor the expansion of commercial gaming in the State of Maryland to authorize Internet gaming for dedicated purposes including the funding of public education?' If voters approve, the Act takes effect 30 days after the canvass; if they reject it, the Act is null and void.","Section 5: the Act takes effect July 1, 2026, solely to allow the referendum to be held."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB3080 and HB4797 and OH SB197, which are full statutory iGaming frameworks with licensing and tax provisions, MD SB761 is only an enabling referendum that defers every operational and tax detail to later legislation. It is narrower than ME LD1164, which steers iGaming opportunity to the Wabanaki Nations, because it confines eligibility to Maryland's existing video lottery (casino) licensees and their contracted partners. It does not address advertising or revenue allocation mechanics, which are the focus of MI SB0713/SB0714 and MI HB4346/HB6127.","watch":"Although this bill was withdrawn, watch whether the cross-filed companion (6lr3217) or a later session revives the referendum, because Section 1(b)(2) would effectively shut out standalone online operators except as contracted partners of Maryland casino licensees, and all tax and licensing terms would be set in later implementing legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4336,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:49.087Z"},"DC-B26-0724":{"summary":"This is the District of Columbia's emergency Fiscal Year 2027 Budget Support Act, an omnibus measure that enacts or amends dozens of unrelated provisions across government operations, economic development, public safety, education, human services, infrastructure, and finance and revenue. Its only gaming-related element visible in the provided text is a table-of-contents entry for Title II, Subtitle DD, \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund\"; the operative text of that subtitle is not included in the excerpt, which cuts off in Title II, Subtitle B. As an emergency act it is temporary and, per the status line, expires November 11, 2026.","mechanics":["Omnibus structure: nine titles covering government direction (telework, ANC funding, lobbying fees), economic development (downtown conversions, tax abatements, TIFs), public safety, education, human services, operations and infrastructure, finance and revenue, technical corrections, and applicability.","Title II, Subtitle DD is captioned \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund,\" but its text is not in the provided excerpt, so the fund's revenue source, deposit rules and permitted uses cannot be confirmed.","Title VII includes Subtitles M (Special Fund Transfers) and N (Special Fund Sweep Reversals), which could affect gaming-related special funds; their text is also not in the excerpt.","Sample non-gaming changes in the text: lobbyist registration fee raised from $350 to $500 (Sec. 1032); a $300,000 cap on mayoral transition payments (Sec. 1044); and the Central Washington activation abatement extended to 2027 with lowered annual caps (Sec. 2002).","Emergency act published as A26-0416 at 73 DCR 011509, expiring November 11, 2026, so any gaming-fund provision lapses unless carried in temporary or permanent companion legislation."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The comparable listed bills are general budget vehicles: AK HB53 (operating budget), AL HB238 (Education Trust Fund appropriations), and CA AB101, AB102, AB109 and AB111 (Budget Acts of 2025 and 2026). Like those, DC B26-0724 touches gaming only as one line item within a broad fiscal package. It is unusual in carrying a dedicated subtitle captioned \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund,\" a fund keyed to online gaming revenue, whereas none of the listed state budget bills shows a comparable gaming-specific fund in its title or description.","watch":"Obtain the full text of Subtitle DD and the special fund transfer and sweep subtitles to see whether the District is earmarking or diverting internet gaming or sports-wagering revenue, which could signal iGaming expansion or changes to the treatment of operator payments, and track the permanent FY2027 Budget Support Act that will replace this emergency act after November 11, 2026.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":197009,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:50.772Z"},"MD-SB932":{"summary":"SB932 adds new Commercial Law § 14-1330, requiring operators of social media platforms with Maryland users to show those users the general geographical location (state, province, or comparable subdivision plus country, derived from IP address) of each user whose account is visible to them. Platforms may not display the location of known minors or of participants in Maryland's Address Confidentiality Program. Violations are unfair, abusive, or deceptive trade practices under the Consumer Protection Act, effective October 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Under § 14-1330(a)(2), 'general geographical location' means the state, province, or comparable subdivision and the country, derived at the time of access from the IP address of the user's network connection. It expressly excludes precise geolocation data as defined in §§ 14-4701 and 14-4801. The engrossed text appears to strike 'city.'","Under § 14-1330(a)(3), a 'social media platform' is a website, app, or AR/VR application with a Maryland user that meets four conditions: it allows accounts or profiles for creating, sharing, and viewing user-generated content; it lets users view each other's content; it primarily serves as a medium for social interaction; and it populates a list of users with whom a user shares a social connection.","The definition excludes services that qualify only because they offer email or direct messaging, services composed primarily of preselected provider content, and services 'for interactive gaming.' 'Interactive gaming' is not defined in the section.","Under § 14-1330(b)(1), operators must display to a Maryland user the general location of each user whose account is visible to that user.","Under § 14-1330(b)(2)–(3), operators may not display the location of a user the operator knows or has reason to know is a minor, or of a user who certifies participation in the Address Confidentiality Program (State Government Art. Title 7, Subtitle 3). Operators must build a process for users to make that certification.","Under § 14-1330(c) and § 13-301(14)(xlix), a violation is a UDAP practice enforceable under Title 13, except §§ 13-407, 13-408, and 13-411. The effective date is October 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill sits alongside the social-media consumer protection bills on the list rather than the gaming bills. CO HB1255 compels platforms to report adverse actions to law enforcement. MI HB4388 and OK HB1275 regulate minors' access and parental consent, and NC H462 pairs data privacy with social media safety. None of those, as described, mandates public display of every user's IP-derived location, which makes SB932 unusual: it imposes an affirmative transparency obligation rather than an access or privacy restriction. Its only gaming touchpoint is the carve-out for services 'for interactive gaming.'","watch":"Because 'interactive gaming' is undefined and sports wagering and collectibles marketplaces are not separately excluded, operators should assess whether any social features could bring their service within the four-part platform definition and trigger location-display duties and UDAP exposure from October 1, 2026; examples include sportsbook bet-sharing feeds, live card-break streaming apps with follower lists and user profiles, and community features.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":6050,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:59.595Z"},"MI-HB4388":{"summary":"HB4388 creates the Social Media Regulation Act, requiring social media companies with at least 5,000,000 account holders worldwide to verify the age of Michigan account applicants and existing account holders and to obtain express parental or guardian consent before a minor (under 18, unemancipated and unmarried) may hold an account. For minor accounts it imposes design restrictions, parental access rights, a default 10:30 p.m. to 6:30 a.m. curfew, and limits on data collection. It is enforced by the Attorney General after a 30-day notice-and-cure period and by a private right of action.","mechanics":["Coverage (Sec. 3(l)-(m)): applies to interactive computer services running a social media platform with at least 5,000,000 account holders worldwide. A long list of exclusions includes email, direct messaging, e-commerce where interaction is generally limited to reviews and listings, and 'interactive gaming, virtual gaming' services where communication relates to the content (Sec. 3(m)(i)(F)).","Age verification and consent (Sec. 5): starting 180 days after the effective date, age verification and express parental consent are required at application. Existing account holders must comply within 14 days of their first access attempt or be locked out. The AG writes rules on verification methods, which may not be limited to government ID, and verification data may be used only for compliance.","Minor-account restrictions (Sec. 7): minor accounts may not appear in search results except through friend links. Targeted or suggested groups, services, products, posts, accounts or users are banned in minor accounts. Personal information from a minor account's posts, messages or activity may not be collected or used unless needed to comply with state or federal law.","Parental access and curfew (Secs. 9, 11): parents get a password or other means to view all of the minor's posts and message responses. Access is blocked from 10:30 p.m. to 6:30 a.m. based on IP address, and parents may change or remove the curfew or set daily hour limits.","AG enforcement (Secs. 13, 15): the AG must investigate consumer complaints. Civil fines are up to $2,500 per violation, plus actual damages, injunctive relief, fees and costs. A 30-day written notice and cure period applies, and a cure with a written assurance bars suit.","Private action and anti-waiver (Secs. 17, 21): consumers may recover the greater of actual damages or $2,500 per violation. Contract terms entered into on or after the effective date that waive the act's protections, the complaint right or the private action are void."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest analogues are OK HB1275 and OK HB4356, which also tie minors' social media access to age verification and parental permission. Michigan goes further, adding a statutory curfew, full parental viewing access to posts and messages, a ban on targeted or suggested content and products in minor accounts, and a $2,500-per-violation private right of action. NC H462 pairs social media safety with broader data privacy, whereas HB4388 limits data use only for minor accounts and verification data. CO HB1255 (law-enforcement reporting) and MD SB932 (user location display) address different platform duties, and none of the listed gaming bills, such as CT HB05038's under-21 prediction-market ban, regulate wagering through social media rules.","watch":"Sportsbook and iGaming operators fall outside the 5,000,000-holder social media definition and benefit from the interactive-gaming exclusion in Sec. 3(m)(i)(F), but collectibles clients running live card breaks with chat should check whether a platform's interactive features exceed the narrow e-commerce exclusion in Sec. 3(m)(i)(E). Separately, Sec. 7(1)(b)'s ban on targeted or suggested products in minor accounts, combined with the $2,500-per-violation private action, could push large platforms to tighten ad-delivery controls in ways that affect all operators' Michigan social media marketing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":15680,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:07.700Z"},"MI-HB5834":{"summary":"HB 5834 rewrites section 19 of Michigan's Lawful Internet Gaming Act (MCL 432.319) to specify which individuals the Gaming Control Board must fingerprint and run through state police and FBI criminal history record checks. It replaces the general reference to applicants, licensees, and \"other persons\" with defined lists of individuals tied to internet gaming supplier and occupational licenses. Fingerprints are kept for ongoing arrest notifications (rap back).","mechanics":["Sec. 19(2) and (4): Anyone applying for or holding an internet gaming supplier license or occupational license must consent to inspections, criminal history record checks, searches and seizures, and to providing handwriting exemplars, fingerprints, and photographs; the Board may run checks on these individuals and on Board employees and prospective employees. The prior reference to Board agents and contractors and the separate photo-plus-two-fingerprint-sets requirement in former subsection (1) appear to be struck.","Sec. 19(6): Occupational licensees subject to checks include live internet game dealers, infrastructure technology personnel who can directly affect gameplay, their supervisors, and managers of an internet gaming operation, including the heads of fraud and risk, accounting and finance, and operations, and whoever remits daily payment to the state.","Sec. 19(7): Supplier-side individuals include directors, and principal executive, operations, or accounting officers who are not occupationally licensed. It also covers directors and principal officers of any \"qualifying business,\" meaning an entity that controls the applicant, holds more than a 5% direct or indirect ownership interest, or shares common ownership and provides a material aspect of the applicant's goods or services in Michigan.","Sec. 19(5): Fingerprints go to the Michigan State Police and the FBI. State Police must perform every check the Board requests and may charge the Board a fee. The Board may not share check results with any private entity.","Sec. 19(6)-(7) (renumbered retention provisions): State Police keep fingerprints in an automated identification system that flags new arrests and must notify the Board immediately. The FBI may also retain prints in its automatic notification system if State Police participates.","No effective date, penalties, or fee amounts are stated in the text."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed iGaming bills would build new regimes from scratch, including IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885/SB884, MA H332/S235/H4431, and ME LD1164. HB 5834 instead is a narrow technical amendment to an existing, operating Michigan iGaming market. It focuses solely on the statutory authority for fingerprint-based background checks. The closest analogue is OH SB197, which amends Ohio's criminal-records-check statute (R.C. 109.572) as part of legalizing internet gambling. Michigan's bill is unusual in listing specific job functions and parent-company officers by role rather than relying on general \"applicant/licensee\" language.","watch":"Watch the expanded reach to directors and principal officers of parent and affiliate entities holding more than 5% or sharing common ownership, which creates continuing FBI rap-back monitoring of their arrests, and watch for a parallel amendment to the Lawful Sports Betting Act (2019 PA 149) that would extend the same regime to sportsbook personnel.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6874,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:09.828Z"},"MI-HB4347":{"summary":"HB 4347 rewrites how Michigan's agriculture and equine industry development fund under the Horse Racing Law of 1995 is distributed. It replaces open-ended sums and caps with fixed percentages of the fund's prior fiscal-year-end balance, calculated after set carve-outs, including a backfill to the school aid fund if internet gaming deposits fall below FY2024 levels. It also tightens section 26 so that pari-mutuel wagering, including through third-party facilitators, may be offered only by a race meeting licensee and only at the racetrack named in its license.","mechanics":["Sec. 20(18) sets the distribution base as the prior fiscal-year-end fund balance after these deductions: $2.5M to the Michigan Gaming Control Board; MDARD's administrative costs; testing costs under 20(13); the compulsive gaming prevention deposit of 1/15 of 1% of gross wagers under 20(15); and, if current-year Lawful Internet Gaming Act school aid deposits (MCL 432.316) fall below the FY ending 9/30/2024, the shortfall paid to the state school aid fund.","Standardbred and fair shares under 20(5): 16% purses; 6% split equally among county fairs; 25% special 2- and 3-year-old purses; 12% breeders' awards, with the cap raised from 10% to 20% of gross purse; 2% fair training and stabling; 8% fair operations (judges, photo finish, vet, drug and alcohol testing); 5% 4-year-old races; 15% standardbred sire stakes.","Thoroughbred shares under 20(6): 3% owner awards; 3% breeders' awards, with the cap raised from 10% to 30%; 1% 4-year-old-and-older races; 3% sire stakes. If no thoroughbred meet runs, top-5 earner award pools pay out 50/25/12/8/5. Research and development grants under 20(11) receive 0.5% each.","New 'eligible recipient' definition (resident of Michigan) in sec. 2(h) is used to restrict owner and breeder awards and stallion ownership. Owners must file eligibility forms by January 15, and MDARD must certify to Treasury by February 15.","New 20(19) sends any fund allocation above $8,000,000 in a fiscal year to the pari-mutuel horse racing disbursement account under sec. 19. Third-party facilitator wager tax money stays excluded from the fund under 20(2).","Sec. 26(2): wagering on live or simulcast races, in person or via a third-party facilitator, may be offered only by a race meeting licensee holding all required approvals, and only at the licensed racetrack identified in the license; fair racetracks need a current track license. Enacting section 1 makes the bill contingent on enactment of HB 4346 or the Senate companion (request S01228'25)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses horse racing or pari-mutuel fund allocation. The internet gaming authorization bills (IL HB3080 and HB4797, MD HB1343 and SB885, OH SB197, MA H332 and S235) create new iGaming markets and tax structures. MI HB4347 instead treats existing Michigan iGaming revenue as a benchmark, using equine fund money to make the school aid fund whole if Lawful Internet Gaming Act deposits drop below FY2024. That cross-subsidy, paired with a tie-bar to HB 4346, is unusual and suggests the package anticipates changes to iGaming revenue flows, though HB 4346's text is not provided here.","watch":"Operators should track tie-barred HB 4346 and the 20(18)(e) school-aid backfill keyed to FY2024 iGaming deposits, which signals possible changes to iGaming revenue allocation. Online horse-wagering partners should also note that sec. 26(2) restricts third-party facilitator wagering to race meeting licensees tied to a specific licensed racetrack.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":31810,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:12.182Z"},"MI-HB6127":{"summary":"Public Act 93 of 2026 amends sections 14 and 16 of Michigan's Lawful Internet Gaming Act (MCL 432.314 and 432.316), mainly to change how money in the internet gaming fund is spent. The chaptered text restates the operator tax in section 14 and sets out a revised spending order (waterfall) in section 16 that now ends with a $1,000,000 annual allocation for tribal governments before the remainder goes to the state school aid fund. It took immediate effect on September 21, 2026.","mechanics":["Sec. 14(1) restates the graduated tax on non-tribal operators' annual adjusted gross receipts: 20% under $4M, 22% from $4M to under $8M, 24% from $8M to under $10M, 26% from $10M to under $12M, and 28% at $12M or more. Tribal operators instead make payments under section 7(1)(f).","Sec. 14(3) requires monthly payment, due on the 10th of the following month. Sec. 14(5) keeps the 1.25% city municipal services fee on the adjusted gross receipts of operators that hold a casino license in a city imposing that fee on casinos.","Sec. 14(4) bars any other tax, payment or fee imposed only on internet gaming operators, except as provided in the act and in section 12(17) of the Michigan Gaming Control and Revenue Act (MCL 432.212). It preserves existing city development agreements.","Sec. 16(4) waterfall, in order: (a) the board's internet gaming regulatory costs; (b) costs of administering millionaire party activity under the bingo act; (c) $3M a year to the compulsive gaming prevention fund; (d) up to $7M a year to the Christopher R. Slezak first responder presumed coverage fund, to cover 50% of claims and administration costs.","Sec. 16(4)(e)-(f): $1M a year goes to the Michigan strategic fund for distribution to federally recognized tribal governments for essential government services, under an allocation plan recommended by the Inter-Tribal Council of Michigan. All remaining money goes to the state school aid fund.","Immediate effect: approved and effective September 21, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Ohio SB197, Illinois HB3080/HB4797, Maryland HB1343/SB885 and Massachusetts H332/S235 would create or legalize and tax iGaming from scratch, whereas Michigan HB6127 adjusts revenue distribution inside an established iGaming regime without changing operator obligations in substance. New Jersey A5336/S4447 add a new prediction-market surtax, while Michigan's bill leaves the operator rate schedule as restated. Its $3M problem-gambling allocation is a funding counterpart to Connecticut SB00240, which addresses internet gaming disorder through a public health plan rather than earmarked operator tax revenue.","watch":"Because the chaptered text does not show strikeouts, operators should confirm against the prior version that the section 14 rates and the new cross-reference to MGCRA section 12(17) do not add any payment obligation, and should watch whether diverting more fund money to non-gaming uses (the first-responder fund and tribal grants) signals future pressure to raise the 20–28% iGaming rates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":6138,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:15.897Z"},"MI-SB713":{"summary":"SB 713 adds section 9a to Michigan's Lawful Internet Gaming Act (2019 PA 152) to create a statutory advertising and promotions regime for internet gaming. It requires Gaming Control Board pre-approval of all internet gaming advertisements, bars deceptive ads and marketing to people under 21, and sets a 30% under-21 audience cap and mandatory responsible-gaming disclaimers. Violations carry escalating civil fines and, on a third violation, revocation of the operator's license, with Attorney General enforcement.","mechanics":["Sec. 9a(1): every internet gaming advertisement must be approved by the board. Sec. 9a(3) lets municipalities adopt ordinances placing reasonable restrictions on public signs related to internet gaming, provided they are not unreasonably impracticable and do not conflict with the act or rules.","Sec. 9a(5): no advertising or marketing unless the advertiser has reliable evidence that not more than 30% of the TV, radio, website or print audience is reasonably expected to be under 21. Sec. 9a(7) bans marketing and sponsorships targeting under-21s, but allows named sponsorship of arenas, stadiums and other public places and of television channels.","Sec. 9a(4) bans deceptive, false or misleading ads and statements. Sec. 9a(6) requires every ad or promotion to carry a problem-gambling disclaimer and the toll-free compulsive gaming helpline number.","Sec. 9a(8) exempts ads that appear on live TV or sports broadcasts only because of their physical placement at the venue. Sec. 9a(11) defines \"advertisement\" broadly as paid marketing likely to induce internet wagering, including search, display, native, video, app ads, billboards, leaflets and sponsorships. The definition excludes TV or internet programs that discuss gaming and name a specific operator, supplier or platform provider.","Sec. 9a(9)-(10) sets penalties: a civil fine of up to $1,000 for a first violation, up to $5,000 for a second, and license revocation for a third or subsequent violation. The Attorney General may bring enforcement actions. No separate effective date is stated."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills listed, which create full iGaming licensing frameworks with advertising as one component, SB 713 is a standalone advertising statute for an already-legal iGaming market. These include IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885, OH SB197 and MA H332/S235. NJ AR150 is only a non-binding resolution urging limits on targeted ads to problem gamblers. CT HB05038 applies an under-21 advertising ban to prediction markets rather than licensed casinos. SB 713 is unusual for combining universal board pre-approval, local sign ordinances and mandatory license revocation on a third violation, which is far harsher than the modest fine amounts suggest.","watch":"Operators should watch the automatic license revocation on a third violation, which could be triggered by low-level ad infractions given universal pre-approval and helpline disclaimer requirements, along with how the board and Attorney General will define \"reliable evidence\" for the 30% under-21 audience test and whether affiliate or influencer content falls within the broad \"advertisement\" definition.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3280,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:24.997Z"},"NC-H198":{"summary":"H198 is North Carolina's 2026 alcoholic beverage control omnibus. It authorizes the ABC Commission to borrow up to $310 million for a new automated warehouse, makes technical fixes across Chapter 18B, and creates new permit types, including a service business permit and a special multiple event permit for nonprofits and political organizations. Its only gaming-adjacent content is letting nonprofit and political organizations offer sealed alcoholic beverages as raffle prizes or auction items at fund-raising events.","mechanics":["Sec. 1: one-time ABC Commission loan of up to $310,000,000, repaid only from permit fees and with no pledge of State credit or real property; effective July 1, 2026.","Sec. 6: permit year runs to May 31, a safe harbor bars revocation for unpaid renewal or registration fees until June 1, and a 25% late fee applies after April 30; the Commission gains discretion to renew despite tax noncompliance, and late-fee refund requests are due by May 31, 2027.","Sec. 11 (new G.S. 18B-1001(26)): a service business permit lets occupational-licensed service businesses serve complimentary beer and wine, capped at two servings per customer per day (16 oz beer, 8 oz wine); the application fee and the annual registration fee are each $50; effective September 1, 2026.","Sec. 16 (G.S. 18B-1002(a)(5)): special one-time permits for fund-raisers are no longer limited to ticketed events, and nonprofits may offer alcoholic beverages in the manufacturer's original closed container as raffle prizes or sell them at auction.","Sec. 17 (new G.S. 18B-1002.2): a special multiple event permit covers sales, brown-bagging, raffle prizes and auctions at multiple fund-raising events, with at most two permit holders per organization and 30 days' written notice to the Commission and local government; the provided text is truncated at this point.","Sec. 15: local boards must give mixed beverage permittees 30 days' notice of any lottery or random drawing for apportioned liquor; this is an allocation mechanism, not gambling; effective October 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike OH HB344, which restructures electronic instant bingo and lottery terminals and levies a tax, H198 does not regulate any wagering product; its raffle references concern only alcohol as a prize at nonprofit fund-raisers. It also differs from NY S 10092, which restricts gambling advertising to minors across sports betting, prediction markets and sweepstakes. Structurally it resembles the single-subject regulatory omnibuses and budget vehicles listed (IL HB2949, MI HB5619, OH HB96), but it is confined to Chapter 18B, with no tax or appropriation provisions touching gaming.","watch":"Because the bill is now in conference and its title covers raffles, watch whether conferees add raffle-law or other gaming provisions beyond the alcohol-as-prize language, and review the truncated remainder of Section 17 and later sections for any changes to North Carolina's raffle statutes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":129597,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:28.306Z"},"NC-H425":{"summary":"H425 creates a new Chapter 18D requiring anyone selling tobacco, vapor, or alternative nicotine products to North Carolina consumers to hold a permit from the ABC Commission, enforced with the ALE Division, and sets 21 as the purchase age. It builds an ABC-style licensing regime covering suitability review, fingerprint background checks, inspections, administrative penalties, and seizure and forfeiture. Gambling appears only incidentally, as prohibited conduct on permitted premises and as a disqualifier in the change-of-ownership rules.","mechanics":["Three permit types, each with a nonrefundable $400 application fee and the same renewal fee (§ 18D-302(d)): retail seller (face-to-face sales on fixed premises), delivery seller, and remote seller. Permits run May 1 to April 30, with a 25% late renewal fee (§ 18D-303).","Owners, officers, managers, and holders of 25% or more must be at least 21, generally NC residents, and free of a felony within three years and of a tobacco or misdemeanor drug conviction within two years; they must also be current on state taxes (§ 18D-300).","Retail sellers must check ID for anyone appearing under 30 (§ 18D-400(d)). Delivery and remote sellers must use independent third-party age verification and require an adult (21+) signature on delivery (§ 18D-400(e)). Vending-machine sales are banned (§ 18D-402(b)(3)).","Penalties (§ 18D-103): suspension of up to three years, revocation, or fines of $500, $750, and $1,000 for first through third violations within three years; offers in compromise of up to $5,000; and a Class 2 misdemeanor for obstructing an inspection (§ 18D-102).","§ 18D-401(a)(3) makes it unlawful for a permittee to knowingly allow any violation of the gambling statutes, among other unlawful acts, on the premises. A gambling violation within the prior 12 months also bars successor-operation rights on a change of ownership (§ 18D-303(d)(2)d).","The supplied text is truncated at § 18D-402(b)(4), so effective dates and any remaining provisions are not shown."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No listed bill is a standalone tobacco retail-licensing measure. IL HB2755 touches tobacco products only as one item in a revenue omnibus. The closest structural analogues are the Virginia cannabis bills (VA HB642, SB542, SB671, SB826), which likewise build a new licensing authority with suitability, background-check, and penalty frameworks for an age-restricted product. NY S10092 shares the youth-protection theme but targets gambling advertising to minors rather than retail sales.","watch":"Watch § 18D-401(a)(3), which would turn any gambling-statute violation on a tobacco or vape retailer's premises into a permit violation and give ALE agents broad warrantless inspection authority, raising the risk for sweepstakes terminals or card-break and mystery-pack activity hosted at convenience and vape stores.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":80431,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:28.863Z"},"NC-H430":{"summary":"H430 creates a new Chapter 18D requiring anyone selling tobacco, nicotine, or vapor products to North Carolina consumers to hold a tobacco retail sales permit from the ABC Commission, enforced with the ALE Division. It raises the legal purchase age to 21 and sets age-verification, signage, and product restrictions. The bill is a tobacco measure and touches gambling only in two places that tie permit status to gambling violations on the premises.","mechanics":["Under § 18D-200 it is unlawful to sell a tobacco product to a consumer without a Commission permit. There are three permit types (retail seller, delivery seller, remote seller), each with a $400 non-refundable application fee and an equal annual renewal fee. Permits run May 1 to April 30, with a 25% late fee (§§ 18D-302, 18D-303).","Under § 18D-103 the Commission may suspend a permit for up to 3 years, revoke it, or impose fines of up to $500, $750, and $1,000 for first, second, and third violations within 3 years. It may instead accept an offer in compromise of up to $5,000.","Applicants must be 21 and are fingerprinted, with SBI/FBI background checks. Disqualifiers include a felony within 3 years and a tobacco-sales or misdemeanor drug conviction within 2 years. Owners of 25% or more and managers must also qualify, and a change of 25% or more in ownership terminates the permit (§§ 18D-300, 18D-302, 18D-303(d)).","Retail clerks must check ID for anyone who appears under 30 (§ 18D-400(d)). Delivery and remote sellers must use third-party age verification and require an adult (21+) signature at delivery (§ 18D-400(e)).","Under § 18D-401(a)(3) it is unlawful for a permittee to knowingly allow violations of the gambling statutes on licensed premises. Under § 18D-303(d)(2)d, the successor-operation grace period is denied to establishments found responsible for a gambling violation within the prior 12 months.","Section 18D-402 bans vending-machine sales, sales of products not authorized by the FDA, sales outside original sealed packaging, and self-service displays except in 21+ establishments. The provided text is truncated after § 18D-402."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a tobacco retail licensing measure. IL HB2755 touches tobacco products only within an omnibus revenue bill. The closest structural analogues are the Virginia cannabis bills (HB642, SB542, SB671, SB826), which likewise build a new permit-based retail regime with background checks and age limits. NY S10092 shares the youth-protection theme but targets advertising of gambling to minors, whereas H430's only gambling hook is a premises-conduct rule tying tobacco permit status to gambling violations.","watch":"Convenience stores and other tobacco permittees that host sweepstakes terminals, skill games, or other contested gaming devices could lose their tobacco permits under § 18D-401(a)(3) and § 18D-303(d)(2)d if those devices are found to violate North Carolina gambling statutes, which creates indirect enforcement pressure on venue partners.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":80227,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:33.191Z"},"NC-H462":{"summary":"Part I of NC H462 (committee substitute, 4/29/25) enacts a comprehensive consumer data privacy law, new Chapter 75F, modeled on the Connecticut/Virginia template. It gives North Carolina residents rights to access, correct, delete and port their data and to opt out of targeted advertising, data sales and significant automated profiling, and it imposes controller and processor duties. The bill's title also references a Social Media Safety Act, but the supplied text is truncated partway through G.S. 75F-110, so neither that Part nor the enforcement provisions can be reviewed.","mechanics":["Applicability (G.S. 75F-103): covers persons doing business in NC or targeting NC residents that, in the prior year, either processed personal data of at least 35,000 consumers (excluding payment-only data), or processed data of at least 10,000 consumers and derived more than 20% of gross revenue from data sales. State bodies and GLBA-covered financial institutions are exempt at the entity level.","Consumer rights (G.S. 75F-104): access, correction, deletion, portability, and a list of specific third-party recipients. Consumers may opt out of targeted advertising, sale, and profiling for legal or similarly significant decisions. Controllers must respond within 45 days, extendable by 45, and must offer an appeal process answered within 60 days.","Universal opt-out (G.S. 75F-105, 75F-106(e)): controllers must honor authorized agents and browser or global-device opt-out preference signals. An opt-out signal overrides conflicting controller-specific settings or loyalty enrolment, though the controller may ask the consumer to confirm.","Sensitive data and minors (G.S. 75F-102(27), 75F-106(a)(4) and (7)): sensitive data, which includes precise geolocation within a 1,750-foot radius and biometrics, requires opt-in consent. Targeted advertising to, or sale of data of, consumers aged 13 to 17 requires consent where the controller has actual knowledge or wilfully disregards their age. Dark-pattern consent is invalid.","Data protection assessments (G.S. 75F-108): required for controllers processing data of at least 100,000 consumers, covering targeted advertising, sales, risky profiling and sensitive data. The Attorney General can demand them in an investigation; they are confidential but usable in enforcement.","Effective January 1, 2026 (Section 1.2). Exclusions in G.S. 75F-110 include compliance with law and regulatory inquiries, and fraud and security prevention. Penalty and enforcement terms are not in the supplied excerpt."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the gaming-specific advertising bills on the list, NC H462 regulates advertising only through general privacy rules. Michigan SB0713/SB0714 add internet gaming advertising and promotion rules, and Connecticut HB05038 bars prediction market use by or advertising to under-21s; H462 instead imposes targeted-ad opt-outs and minor-consent rules that apply to every covered business. Its social media component sits alongside Colorado HB1255 (platform reporting to law enforcement), Maryland SB932 (display of user location) and Michigan HB4388 (minors' social media use), but the visible text is a horizontal privacy statute. That statute is notable for a low 35,000-consumer threshold and a mandatory universal opt-out signal.","watch":"Operators should check whether geolocation compliance checks (precise geolocation is sensitive data needing consent), cross-site targeted and affiliate marketing, player-behaviour profiling, and loyalty or bonus programs fit within the G.S. 75F-110 compliance-with-law and fraud exclusions and the G.S. 75F-106(b) loyalty carve-out, and should obtain the missing enforcement and Social Media Safety Act text before the January 1, 2026 effective date.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":65891,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:45.504Z"},"NM-HB2":{"summary":"HB2 is New Mexico's General Appropriation Act of 2026 (signed as Chapter 67 on March 11, 2026). It makes fiscal year 2027 operating appropriations to state agencies, with amounts stated in thousands of dollars, sets reversion rules for unexpended balances, and attaches performance measures to agency programs. The portion of the text provided covers the legislative branch, the judiciary, district attorneys, the public defender, and general control agencies through part of the Department of Finance and Administration, and contains no gaming, wagering, sweepstakes, or collectibles provisions.","mechanics":["Section 3(C) appropriates Section 4 amounts for expenditure in FY2027. Section 3(D)-(E) revert unexpended FY2026 and FY2027 balances to the general fund by October 1, 2026 and October 1, 2027 respectively, unless otherwise provided.","Section 3(F) directs the state budget division to cut the operating budget of any agency whose non-general-fund revenue falls short of projections, with notice to the legislative finance committee.","Section 3(H) requires DFA to present a deficit plan to the legislative finance committee if FY2027 revenues are not expected to meet appropriations.","DFA may transfer up to $6,000,000 in aggregate from the general fund operating reserve to the board of finance emergency fund on certification of a critical emergency.","The Attorney General's legal services program draws $23,057,300 from the consumer settlement fund, including $1,500,000 reserved for the office of the child advocate.","Taxation and Revenue Department tax administration carries targets of collecting 23% of prior-year outstanding collectible balances and 50% of prior-year audit assessments."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"HB2 is a general operating budget, comparable in form to AK HB53 (operating budget), CA AB109 (Budget Act of 2026), and AL HB238 (Education Trust Fund appropriations), rather than to any substantive gaming bill on the list. None of the listed bills addresses sports betting, iGaming, sweepstakes, or card breaks either. Unlike California's practice of amending prior budget acts by item (e.g., CA AB111, AB112), New Mexico uses a single annual act with program purpose statements and embedded performance targets. The excerpt ends mid-DFA, so any appropriation to the New Mexico Gaming Control Board or Racing Commission is not in the provided text and cannot be assessed.","watch":"Check the later, unexcerpted sections of HB2 for the Gaming Control Board and Racing Commission appropriations, any staffing or enforcement language, and any performance measures that could signal regulatory priorities, along with any contingent appropriations tied to gaming legislation passed in the same session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":159138,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:47.627Z"},"NC-H680":{"summary":"H680 creates a new Chapter 18D that brings hemp-derived consumable products (finished goods for ingestion or inhalation with no more than 0.3% delta-9 THC on a dry weight basis, but possibly higher levels of other cannabinoids such as delta-8, delta-10 and THCA) under a permit regime run by the ABC Commission, with enforcement by the ALE Division. It requires a permit for any sale to a North Carolina consumer, whether in store or by delivery. It also sets permit eligibility rules, age-verification duties, inspection powers, administrative penalties, and seizure and forfeiture procedures. Gambling appears only once, as a disqualifier in the change-of-ownership provision.","mechanics":["Under § 18D-200 it is unlawful to sell hemp-derived consumable products to a consumer in NC without a Commission permit. There are two permit types: retail seller (in-person sales on fixed premises only) and delivery seller (in-state or out-of-state, covering online, phone and mail orders). Delivery services carrying goods for a permittee are exempt.","Under §§ 18D-302 and 18D-303 the application fee is $2,000 per location or per delivery website, capped at $50,000 for entities with more than 25 locations or websites. Fees are non-refundable and go to the General Fund. Permits run May 1 to April 30, renewal costs the same as the application fee, and late renewal carries a 25% surcharge.","Under § 18D-300 owners, officers and holders of 25% or more must be at least 21 and must meet residency requirements (or appoint a resident attorney-in-fact). They must have no felony conviction within 3 years, no hemp-law or misdemeanor drug conviction within 5 years, and no permit revocation within 5 years, and must be current on state taxes. Applicants submit fingerprints for SBI/FBI background checks.","Under § 18D-400 retail sellers must demand proof of age from anyone who reasonably appears to be under 30. Delivery sellers must use an independent third-party age verification service that checks against public records. The text is truncated at this point; the preamble targets under-21s.","Under § 18D-103 the Commission may suspend a permit for up to 3 years, revoke it, or impose fines of up to $500, $750 and $1,000 for first, second and third violations within 3 years. It may instead accept an offer in compromise of up to $5,000. Under § 18D-102, obstructing an inspection is a Class 2 misdemeanor.","Under § 18D-303(d)(2)d, a successor owner may keep operating under the prior permit during a change of ownership only if the establishment has not been found responsible for a gambling, assault, disorderly conduct, prostitution or controlled substance violation in the prior 12 months. Under § 18D-304(c), no permits are issued on public school, college or university property."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest listed bills are the cannabis bills: OH SB56 and VA HB642, SB542, SB671 and SB826. Those build full adult-use marijuana markets with dedicated licensing authorities, and in Virginia's SB826 a 12.875% marijuana tax plus sales and local taxes. NC H680 is narrower: it regulates only intoxicating hemp-derived products under the federal 0.3% delta-9 threshold. It bolts that regulation onto the existing ABC Commission and ALE structure, and the excerpt contains no excise tax. Its only gaming touchpoint is the gambling-violation bar on successor operation, which is far more tangential than gaming-focused bills like NY S 10092 (youth gambling ad ban) or OH HB344 (electronic instant bingo).","watch":"The bill is essentially irrelevant to wagering, but operator counsel should note two things: the § 18D-303(d)(2)d gambling-violation disqualifier could affect convenience-store or bar licensees that also host gaming, sweepstakes or lottery activity, and the delivery-seller third-party age-verification and permit-number-in-advertising requirements could become a template for future NC online gaming or sweepstakes rules.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":73395,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:48.283Z"},"NC-H999":{"summary":"H999 creates a new Article 11 in Chapter 18C authorizing video gaming terminals (VGTs), classified as a lottery game, regulated by the NC Lottery Commission and placed with ABC-permitted retailers through licensed operators and manufacturers. It also creates Article 2F of Chapter 105, a $10,000-per-machine excise stamp tax on unauthorized gaming machines, including illegal video sweepstakes devices. Several of its general Chapter 18C amendments, on records, gifts and dispute resolution, reach existing sports wagering licensees under Articles 9 and 10.","mechanics":["Placement (18C-1102): up to 3 VGTs per location, plus up to 3 more based on a Commission net machine revenue benchmark. Terminals must be at ABC-permit merchants, more than 1,000 feet from churches and schools, on no government property, and connected at all times to a central monitoring system.","Revenue split of net machine revenue (18C-1103): 32% to the NC Video Gaming Fund; 8% for administration and enforcement, including at least $3M a year to ALE, $1M in local anti-illegal-gaming grants and $225K for a prosecutor; 30% to operators; 30% to merchants.","Licensing (18C-1114, 18C-1120): initial fees are $150K for manufacturers, $250K plus $150 per terminal for operators, and $1,500 per location for merchants; renewals are $100K, $100K plus $150 per terminal, and $1,000. Operators need 2 years of uninterrupted in-state amusement operation and residency or organization, and no foreign change of control within 2 years. Strict tier separation (18C-1122).","Play rules: 21+ only; maximum cash award of $1,199 per wager; odds posted on or near each terminal; a voluntary exclusion program; and a total advertising ban, including websites, social media and broadcast (18C-1115), apart from a standard Commission entry sign.","Criminal penalties (18C-1142): possessing an unauthorized VGT or tampering is a Class 1 misdemeanor, escalating to a Class G felony on a third offense; manipulation or false revenue reporting is a Class G felony, then Class F.","Unauthorized machine tax (G.S. 105-113.152): $10,000 excise per unauthorized gaming machine, payable within 48 hours of placement and evidenced by a stamp. It covers unpermitted VGTs, illegal slots and devices under G.S. 14-306, 14-306.1A, 14-306.3(b) and 14-306.4 (electronic sweepstakes). Paying the tax gives no immunity from prosecution."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity","sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest listed analog is OH HB344, which likewise expands lottery-run terminal gaming (electronic instant bingo and lottery terminals) with a tax. H999 instead builds a full private three-tier VGT market under the Lottery Commission with a fixed 30/30 operator-merchant revenue share. H999's flat ban on all VGT advertising is far broader than NY S10092, which only bars gambling ads directed at minors on covered platforms. The NE LB402 and LB 415 winnings-setoff bills have no counterpart here beyond H999's statement that winnings are subject to state income tax, and the remaining listed bills (budgets, cannabis, revisory) are not comparable.","watch":"Sports wagering licensees should watch three spillover provisions in the Chapter 18C amendments: new 18C-114(a)(17), which lets the Commission impose mandatory dispute-resolution policies on Article 9 and 10 licensees that must be exhausted before suing; the new 18C-117 gift restrictions applying to all Chapter 18C licensees (drafted oddly as 'not exceeding $100'); and the unexplained repeals of G.S. 18C-143(e) and 18C-151(f).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":68041,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:54.504Z"},"NY-S8578":{"summary":"S08578 is the City of Staten Island Act. It incorporates Staten Island (Richmond County) as a city separate from New York City, enacts a full city charter, and sets a staged transition. It also makes conforming amendments across the education, judiciary, housing, public authorities, county, election, finance and tax laws, including provisions letting the new city continue taxes currently applicable there. The portion of the text provided contains no provision on sports wagering, casino gaming, lotteries or collectibles.","mechanics":["Timeline (§1-003, §1-004): incorporation on January 1 after enactment; the charter takes effect the following November 1; full municipal powers devolve on July 1 of the second year after enactment; a judiciary transition period runs through December 31 of the fifth year after incorporation.","During the transition period New York City, the 'preceding municipality', keeps full municipal powers over the territory except as the act provides.","Charter §2-01 grants a broad general power of local self-government, stating that enumerated powers are not exclusive. The executive is a mayor elected to a four-year term (§3-02); the legislature is a 15-member common council split into 'A' and 'B' districts (§4-02).","The mayor has finance-board powers under the local finance law (§3-08(c)), and an elected comptroller has audit authority (§5-02).","The title and table of contents reference §13-001 (taxes and tax collection authority) and amendments to the general city law, chapter 772 of the laws of 1966 (city business tax) and the tax law, so the new city can continue presently applicable taxes. The substance of those sections is not in the excerpt provided."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing listed is truly comparable. The nearest analogues are large structural or omnibus vehicles, such as IL SB3731 (general revisory), IL HB2949 and OH HB96 (budget implementation) and MI HB5619 (omnibus appropriations), where gaming can be touched only through cross-references or tax continuations. That contrasts with bills squarely on gaming like NC H999 (video gaming terminals) and OH HB344 (electronic instant bingo and lottery terminals). What is unusual here is the creation of an entirely new city of roughly half a million residents, carved out of New York City, with a broad home-rule grant and wholesale re-mapping of state statutes that currently refer to 'the city of New York'.","watch":"Review the untruncated §13-001 tax-continuation provisions and the conforming statutory amendments to see whether any New York City-specific wagering-related taxes, local gaming or licensing powers, or statutory references to 'the city of New York' (for example, in casino siting or local-share provisions) are reallocated to or newly exercisable by the city of Staten Island under its broad charter §2-01 home-rule grant.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5009470,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:03.342Z"},"OK-HB1275":{"summary":"HB1275 bars social media companies from letting Oklahoma users under 16 hold accounts on covered platforms and requires express parental consent for 16- and 17-year-olds, backed by mandatory age verification. It also imposes minor-specific data limits on platforms accessed by minors, including no profiling without safeguards, no sale or sharing of minors' data, and limits on geolocation and dark patterns. Enforcement is by the Attorney General plus a private right of action. Gambling appears only in the legislative-intent analogy and in a carve-out for interactive gaming services.","mechanics":["Sec. 3: no accounts for Oklahoma minors under 16. Ages 16–17 need express parent or guardian consent, confirmed when the account is opened. Age verification can use a digital ID, government ID, any commercially reasonable method, or app-store age confirmation, and may be done through a third-party vendor.","Sec. 2(B)(11): a covered 'social media platform' must have Oklahoma users, use algorithmic content selection, feature infinite scrolling, and have social interaction as a substantial function. Exclusions cover e-commerce where user interaction is generally limited to reviews and lists, preselected sports or entertainment content with incidental chat, and businesses with under $100 million in annual gross revenue from US users.","Sec. 2(B)(10)(b)(2): companies that exclusively offer interactive or virtual gaming, or user content created for gaming or entertainment, are outside the 'social media company' definition.","Sec. 4: platforms accessed by minors may not process minors' data where they know, or willfully disregard, that it risks substantial harm. They also may not profile minors without safeguards, collect precise geolocation unless strictly necessary and visibly signalled, use dark patterns, or sell or share minors' personal information or geolocation. Violations go to the AG, who must give notice, followed by a 45-day cure period.","Sec. 5: knowing violators are liable for failing to verify age or to comply with Sec. 4. The AG may bring enforcement actions. Individuals may recover $2,500 per violation, or damages for a minor's access without consent, plus costs and attorney fees. There is a safe harbor where a minor gets in by fraud or fake ID after reasonable verification.","Age-verification data retention conflicts within the bill: Sec. 5(A)(2) bars keeping identifying information after access is granted, while Sec. 6 allows retention for audit and testing for up to 30 days. Sec. 6 also creates a private damages action for knowing retention. No effective date appears in the text."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill sits alongside the minors-and-social-media bills on the list, NC H462 and MI HB4388. It goes further than a parental-consent model by imposing an outright account ban under 16, layering in a Sec. 4 data-minimization regime, and adding a $2,500-per-violation private right of action. It is unrelated to the platform-reporting focus of CO HB1255 and the location-display mandate of MD SB932. Unlike CT HB05038, which restricts under-21 access to prediction markets, it does not regulate wagering platforms; gambling is invoked only as a policy analogy in Sec. 1.","watch":"Watch whether the exclusive-interactive-gaming and e-commerce carve-outs are narrowed. Live card-break and collectibles streaming marketplaces with algorithmic feeds, infinite scroll and live chat that goes beyond reviews and lists could fall within the platform definition if they exceed the $100 million revenue threshold, triggering under-16 bans, age verification and private suits.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":22491,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:09.170Z"},"OH-SB56":{"summary":"Ohio SB 56, now signed, overhauls the state's medical and adult-use marijuana laws. It repeals the voter-initiated adult-use chapter (R.C. 3780) and folds that program into the Division of Cannabis Control's Chapter 3796. It also revises hemp law (R.C. 928), enacts new provisions in 3779.21 to 3779.30, amends the commercial driver's license law, and makes an appropriation. It does not regulate wagering or gaming.","mechanics":["Repeals R.C. 3780.01 to 3780.99 and renumbers 3780.37 as 3796.34, consolidating adult-use cannabis regulation under Chapter 3796 and the Department of Commerce's Division of Cannabis Control.","Amends R.C. 9.79(I)(10) to exempt Division of Cannabis Control licenses from the general rules limiting how licensing authorities may use criminal convictions. Disqualifying offenses for those licenses are instead set by rules under 9.79 and 3796.03 (see 109.572(A)(13) and (A)(14)).","Requires criminal records checks under 109.572(A)(13) and (A)(14) for owners, officers, board members, administrators and employees of Chapter 3796 licensees.","Enacts R.C. 3779.21 to 3779.30 and 3779.99, and separately repeals those same newly enacted versions effective December 31, 2026. It also enacts new hemp provisions at 928.01, 928.031 and 928.08.","Amends R.C. 4506.01 (commercial driver's licenses) effective December 31, 2026, and makes an appropriation; the appropriations provisions took effect December 19, 2025.","The excerpt provided is truncated, so the marijuana tax rates referenced in the title cannot be confirmed from the text supplied."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest listed analogues are the Virginia retail cannabis bills (VA HB642, SB542, SB671 and SB826), which build a new retail market from scratch. SB826 sets a 12.875% marijuana tax, a 1.125% sales tax and a 3% local tax. Ohio SB 56 instead restructures an existing voter-approved adult-use market and moves it into the medical marijuana chapter. NC H680 addresses youth cannabis protection, and none of the listed bills, including NC H999 on video gaming terminals or NY S 10092 on gambling ads, overlaps with SB 56 on gaming. The only gaming touchpoints are existing cross-references in R.C. 109.572 to casino and sports-gaming background checks (3772.07, 3775.03) and charitable gaming (2915.081, 2915.082), which appear to carry over unchanged.","watch":"Operators should confirm that the cross-references in R.C. 109.572(A)(11), (A)(18) and (A)(19) to Casino Control Commission and sports-gaming background checks were left unchanged, and should watch whether Ohio extends SB 56's approach of carving a regulated vice industry out of the general 9.79 criminal-record limits to gaming licensing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":310187,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:09.964Z"},"OK-HB1388":{"summary":"HB1388 creates the Protecting Oklahoma's Children on Social Media Act of 2025, which imposes school-technology duties on Oklahoma public school districts and charter schools. Each must adopt an internet acceptable-use policy and a social media policy that bars students from reaching social media platforms on school equipment or networks, subject to State Department of Education and State Board of Education review. It does not regulate gambling, gaming operators, or commercial platforms directly.","mechanics":["Acceptable-use policy due by October 1, 2025 (Sec. 4). It must block obscene material, child pornography, and material harmful to minors on school equipment. Policies go to the State Board of Education each October 15, and districts get a 30-day cure period after a notice of noncompliance.","Social media policy due by April 1, 2026, and submitted by April 15, 2026 (Sec. 6). Student access is allowed only when directed and supervised by staff, for age-appropriate educational resources, during a school activity. Districts must use blocking and monitoring software or servers, and parents may opt their child out of specific platforms.","The 'social media platform' definition (Sec. 2(10)) excludes 22 categories. These include (f) interactive gaming and virtual gaming, (e) e-commerce where interaction is limited to reviews and lists of goods for sale, and (b) services that bar minors and use commercially reasonable age assurance.","The Department of Education must set annual technology-protection-measure standards by April 1, 2026 (Sec. 5). It must also publish a list of compliant vendors, require providers to verify compliance each year, and issue staff training guidelines by December 1, 2025.","Sec. 7 sets an effective date of July 1, 2025, and Sec. 8 adds an emergency clause. The Senate floor version is a committee substitute, placed on General Order on April 22, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest analogues on the list are CT HB05035, which bans cellphones in classrooms, and MI HB4388 and NC H462, which regulate minors' social media use. Unlike the Michigan and North Carolina bills, HB1388 places no duties on platform operators; every obligation falls on school boards and state education agencies. CO HB1255 and MD SB932 likewise impose operator-facing duties that this bill lacks. Its only gaming touchpoint is the Sec. 2(10)(f) carve-out of interactive and virtual gaming from the 'social media platform' definition, which differs from the iGaming bills listed (IL HB3080, MD HB1343) that actually authorize or regulate wagering.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the 'interactive gaming' and 'age assurance' carve-outs in Sec. 2(10)(b) and (f) are carried into later Oklahoma operator-facing social media bills, since those exemptions could shield adult-only betting and iGaming apps. Collectibles clients should note that the e-commerce exclusion in (e) is narrow, so streamed card breaks on social platforms would likely fall within school blocking requirements.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":29434,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:12.187Z"},"OK-HB4130":{"summary":"HB4130 amends Oklahoma's core gambling-game crime, 21 O.S. Section 941, to expressly cover online casino games and to extend criminal liability beyond owners and dealers to geolocation providers, gaming suppliers, platform providers, promoters and media affiliates who provide support for an illegal game. It defines 'representatives of value' by reference to dual-currency systems, which squarely targets sweepstakes-model online casinos. The House Judiciary and Public Safety Oversight committee reported it Do Pass as amended on 02/26/2026.","mechanics":["Section 941(A) makes it a Class C2 felony to open, conduct or carry on poker, roulette, craps, banking or percentage games, or any gambling game played with dice, cards or any device, 'including, but not limited to, online casino games'; the penalty is a $500 to $2,000 fine plus imprisonment under subsections B through F of 21 O.S. Section 20M.","Covered persons expand from 'owner or employee' to include any geolocation provider, gaming supplier, platform provider, promoter or media affiliate who, whether for hire or not, 'deals or provides support for' those engaged in the game.","Section 941(B)(1) defines 'online casino games' as any gambling game accessed over the Internet by computer, phone, smartwatch, tablet or similar device that, upon risking representatives of value, simulates a Section 941 game or any other gambling, including slot machines (21 O.S. 964), lotteries (21 O.S. 1051), bingo and U-PIK-EM bingo (3A O.S. 402), and prohibited games under 3A O.S. 262(H).","Section 941(B)(2) defines 'representatives of value' as any currency used in a dual-currency payment system that can be exchanged for a prize, award, cash or cash equivalent, or a chance to win one.","The existing carve-outs remain for the Oklahoma Charity Games Act and for tribal gaming conducted under the Indian Gaming Regulatory Act.","The act takes effect November 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like MD HB295 and SB112, which prohibit operating or promoting 'interactive games' and require licensees to report ties to illegal-market actors, HB4130 is a prohibition bill aimed at sweepstakes and offshore online casinos rather than a legalization framework. It contrasts sharply with IL HB3080 and HB4797, MD HB1343 and SB885, MA H332 and S235, and ME LD1164, all of which would authorize and license iGaming. HB4130 is unusual in using the existing criminal gambling statute, rather than a regulatory act, to reach B2B vendors and marketing affiliates. Those vendors include geolocation providers, platform providers and media affiliates, and they face direct felony exposure.","watch":"Watch the felony exposure for geolocation providers, platform providers, gaming suppliers, promoters and media affiliates who 'provide support' to dual-currency or online casino games, which could reach vendors and affiliates that serve licensed operators in other states. Also watch whether the broad 'simulates any gambling game' and dual-currency definitions could be read to sweep in prize-redeemable digital collectibles, mystery packs or card-break platforms that use coin or credit systems.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":3867,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:20.753Z"},"OK-SB1589":{"summary":"SB1589 amends Oklahoma's core gambling-house offense (21 O.S. Section 941) to expressly cover online casino games, including sweepstakes-style casinos that use a dual-currency system. It also extends felony liability beyond owners and dealers to geolocation providers, gaming suppliers, platform providers, promoters and media affiliates that deal or provide support for such games. It was signed and filed with the Secretary of State and takes effect November 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Section 941(A)(1) defines \"online casino games\" as any internet-accessed game on a computer, phone, smartwatch, tablet or similar device that, upon risking any representative of value, simulates a gambling game or any other form of gambling. The listed examples are slot machines (21 O.S. 964), lotteries (21 O.S. 1051(A)), bingo and U-PIK-EM bingo (3A O.S. 402), and prohibited games under 3A O.S. 262(H).","Section 941(A)(2) defines \"representative of value\" to include any currency in a dual-currency system that can be exchanged for any prize, award, cash or cash equivalent, or for any chance to win one. This targets the sweeps-coin model directly.","Section 941(B) makes it a crime to open, conduct or carry on poker, roulette, craps, banking or percentage games, or any game played with dice, cards or any device, including online casino games, for money, checks, credits or representatives of value. Liability reaches anyone acting as owner, employee, geolocation provider, gaming supplier, platform provider, promoter or media affiliate who deals or provides support, whether for hire or not.","The penalty is a Class C2 felony with a fine of $500 to $2,000 plus imprisonment under subsections B through E of 21 O.S. Section 20M.","Activities under the Oklahoma Charity Games Act and tribal gaming conducted on Indian lands in compliance with IGRA are carved out. There is no exemption for sweepstakes promotions, and none of the new supporting-party roles (such as \"media affiliate\" or \"platform provider\") is defined.","Effective November 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogs are Maryland HB295 and SB112, which also prohibit operating, conducting or promoting unlicensed \"interactive games.\" Those bills work through the gaming regulator, requiring licensees to report business relationships with illegal-market actors. Oklahoma's bill instead works through the criminal code, imposing felony exposure on third-party vendors such as geolocation, platform, supplier and media/affiliate businesses. It sits at the opposite end from the iGaming legalization bills in Illinois (HB3080, HB4797), Massachusetts (H332, S235), Maryland (HB1343, SB885) and Maine (LD1164), offering no licensing pathway at all.","watch":"Watch the undefined supporting-party categories, especially \"media affiliate,\" \"promoter\" and \"platform provider,\" and the broad dual-currency and \"simulates any other form of gambling\" language. These could expose affiliates, payment and geolocation vendors, and potentially online card-break or digital mystery-pack sellers using redeemable currencies to felony liability starting November 1, 2026, because the text contains no express knowledge requirement and no sweepstakes safe harbor.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":3977,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:26.967Z"},"OK-HB4356":{"summary":"HB4356 would bar social media companies from letting Oklahoma users under 16 hold accounts on covered platforms and would require age verification of every account holder. It also restricts how platforms accessed by minors (under 18) may process their data, including profiling, precise geolocation and dark patterns. Enforcement is by the Attorney General, with a private right of action. The bill does not address wagering, and its exclusion for exclusively interactive-gaming services means it only touches gaming or collectibles businesses at the definitional margins.","mechanics":["Sec. 2: no Oklahoma user under 16 may be an account holder, and all account holders must be age-verified. Permitted methods include a digitized ID or driver license, government ID, any 'commercially reasonable' method, or app-store confirmation, and third-party vendors may be used.","Sec. 1(B)(11): a covered 'social media platform' must have Oklahoma users, use algorithmic content selection, feature infinite scrolling and have social interaction as a substantial function. Entities under $100M in annual gross revenue from U.S. users are carved out, as are e-commerce services whose user interaction is limited to reviews, lists of goods or wish lists, and services that exclusively offer interactive gaming (Sec. 1(B)(10)(b)(2)).","Sec. 3: platforms accessed by minors (defined as under 18) may not process personal information that risks substantial harm, profile minors without safeguards and necessity, collect unnecessary data, or collect or sell precise geolocation unless strictly necessary and visibly signalled. They also may not use dark patterns. The AG gives notice of violations and there is a 45-day cure period.","Sec. 4: knowing violators are liable, the AG may bring enforcement actions, and individuals may recover $2,500 per violation or damages for a minor's access without parental consent, plus costs and attorney fees. There is a safe harbor where a minor gets access by fraud or fake ID after reasonable verification.","Sec. 4(A)(2) and Sec. 5: identifying information may not be retained after access is granted, with an audit and testing exception of up to 30 days in Sec. 5. Knowing retention creates a private damages claim.","Effective November 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"MI HB4388 and NC H462 are the closest comparators: both regulate minors' social media use and data, although their texts are not provided here for a detailed comparison. MD SB932 instead mandates displaying adult users' locations, and CO HB1255 concerns platform reporting to law enforcement. Neither imposes an account ban. The age-gating approach loosely parallels CT HB05038's under-21 bar on prediction markets, but HB4356 has no gambling nexus. Unusually, it sets a hard under-16 account ban while defining 'minor' as under 18 for its data-practice rules. It also pairs a 45-day AG cure period with an uncured private right of action at $2,500 per violation.","watch":"Clients running livestreamed card breaks, collector communities, or social or community features in betting and casino apps should check whether their feeds meet the algorithm, infinite-scroll and social-interaction tests. The e-commerce carve-out is limited to reviews and lists, and the interactive-gaming exclusion requires that gaming be the 'exclusive' offering, so neither may protect them.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":20039,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:30.653Z"},"PA-HB257":{"summary":"Although framed as a transportation omnibus, HB257 (Senate-amended, PN 2211) contains one gaming provision. Section 1 amends 4 Pa.C.S. § 13B52(d)(2)(ii) to divert money from the restricted receipt account funded by 10% of the interactive gaming tax imposed under § 13B52(a)(3) to transit and secondary-road funding. It does not change any interactive gaming tax rate or operator obligation; the rest of the bill covers transit funding, fare indexing, a mass-transit special prosecutor, new transit crimes and driver licensing.","mechanics":["One-time FY2025-26 transfers within 30 days of effectiveness from the § 13B52(d)(2)(ii) restricted receipt account: $87.5M to the Public Transportation Trust Fund and $87.5M to the new Supplemental Funding for Three and Four Digit State Routes Account in the Motor License Fund (new clause (C.1)).","Recurring transfers beginning FY2025-26: $43M each year to the Public Transportation Trust Fund and $43M each year to the Three and Four Digit State Routes Account, paid at least quarterly (new clause (C.2)).","Floor in new clause (D.1): the account balance may not fall below $20M, except as needed for county distributions under existing clauses (B) or (C). The year-end 60-day distribution deadline in clause (D) no longer applies to the new transfers.","Within the Public Transportation Trust Fund, iGaming-sourced deposits (new 74 Pa.C.S. § 1506(c)(3.5)) are split 86.76% to the operating program (§ 1513) and 13.24% to programs of statewide significance (§ 1516).","No change to tax rates, licensing, advertising or operator conduct; the gaming section only reallocates existing receipts. The available text is truncated, so the effective date for Section 1 is not shown."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Like Michigan HB4346 (removing the cap on internet gaming revenue for the equine industry fund) and MI HB6127 (modifying internet gaming fund expenditures), PA HB257 reallocates existing iGaming receipts rather than changing the rate or regulatory framework. It differs from the authorization bills, IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885 and MA H332/S235, which would create iGaming markets and set taxes from scratch. Unusually, it ties fixed dollar amounts ($175M one-time, $86M a year) from a county-oriented iGaming account to statewide transit and road funding, and attaches them to unrelated transportation and criminal provisions.","watch":"Watch whether committing fixed annual sums ($86M plus a $20M floor) from the iGaming restricted receipt account to transit and roads creates pressure to raise the § 13B52 interactive gaming tax or expand the base if receipts fall short, and how the bill fares in House Rules after Senate amendment.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":58670,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:33.574Z"},"PA-HB416":{"summary":"Act 45 of 2025 is Pennsylvania's omnibus Fiscal Code amendment implementing the 2025-2026 budget. Among many unrelated provisions, it creates a child care staff bonus program, extends the 911 surcharge, decouples the corporate net income tax from several One Big Beautiful Bill Act changes, restructures the Joint Underwriting Association and takes $100 million from it, and creates a Rural Health Transformation Program. Its only gaming connection is a title reference to amending distributions from the Pennsylvania Race Horse Development Fund, which falls in a part of the text not provided.","mechanics":["Child Care Staff Recruitment and Retention Program (Art. I-H.1): DHS pays qualified subsidized providers an individual retention bonus of at least $450 per direct-care staff member, with remaining funds allocated by provider type and licensed capacity. Applications are due within 45 days of the form being posted, and annual reports start September 15, 2026.","The 911 surcharge under 35 Pa.C.S. § 5306.2(a) stays at $1.95 until February 1, 2029, extended from 2026 (§ 102-K).","CNIT decoupling (§§ 216-217): R&E deductions under IRC §§ 174, 174A and 59(e), § 481 adjustments, and § 168(n) qualified production property depreciation are added back and recovered at 20% per year. For tax years after December 31, 2024, interest expense under § 163(j) is computed as the federal rule stood on December 31, 2024. DOR must report on the impact by December 31, 2026.","Joint Underwriting Association (§§ 208-D to 216-D): the 2017 sunset is repealed and $100 million must be transferred to the General Fund within 30 days for Medicaid capitation. From June 1, 2026, the Commissioner certifies amounts available for transfer when risk-based capital exceeds 1,000%. A new seven-member board is created, and the association is treated as an independent agency for Right-to-Know and procurement purposes.","Treasury (§ 313): the Treasurer may waive interest on 2025-26 budget-impasse loans to counties, domestic violence, rape crisis, Head Start and Pre-K Counts entities. From FY 2026-27, such loans replacing anticipated appropriations are barred. Separately, the Governor's disaster transfer cap under § 1508 rises from $20 million to $40 million.","The title lists amendments to distributions from the Pennsylvania Race Horse Development Fund, but the provided text is truncated before that section, so its terms cannot be confirmed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses gaming, and this act is closest in form to the omnibus budget vehicles on the list, such as CA AB101 (Budget Act of 2025) and AK HB53 (operating budget). Unlike those pure appropriation acts, PA HB416 is a substantive Fiscal Code trailer. It makes permanent tax-base changes, notably decoupling the CNIT from federal § 174A, § 168(n) and § 163(j) changes, and restructures a state insurance instrumentality. Its only gaming touchpoint is the Race Horse Development Fund distribution amendment named in the title.","watch":"Operators should obtain and review the truncated Race Horse Development Fund distribution amendments, since that fund is fed by slot gaming revenue, and should model the impact of the § 217 interest-expense and § 216 R&E/depreciation decoupling on their Pennsylvania CNIT liability for tax years beginning after December 31, 2024.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":165454,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:45.162Z"},"PA-SB146":{"summary":"Act 21 of 2026 is Pennsylvania's omnibus Fiscal Code amendment implementing the 2026-2027 budget. The excerpt provided covers COVID-19 fund reallocation, child care retention bonuses, cigarette stamping agent sourcing limits, Joint Underwriting Association transfers, a new food processing residuals regulatory article, and a Veterans' Trust Fund Board. The title also lists amendments on distributions from the Pennsylvania Race Horse Development Fund, the State Gaming Fund, and an amusement tax clarification, but those sections are not in the text supplied, so their content cannot be confirmed.","mechanics":["New Section 197-C: PHFA must transfer $11,789,348 of ARPA housing relief funds to the COVID-19 Response Restricted Account by September 10, 2026, for the Department of Corrections. Funds unspent by December 11, 2026 are swept by December 18, and all such money must be spent by December 31, 2026.","Section 103-H.1: the child care retention bonus rises from at least $450 to at least $540 per qualified staff member, and the application decision window extends from 45 to 60 days.","New Section 204.1-A: licensed cigarette stamping agents may buy only directly from a tobacco product manufacturer or from a newly defined 'related entity supplier' (majority common ownership), and may not sell unstamped cigarettes to other dealers.","Section 209-D: $14,300,000 is transferred from the Joint Underwriting Association to the General Fund for FY beginning July 1, 2026, earmarked for Medical Assistance capitation payments.","New Article XIII (food processing residuals) requires land application systems and three-year notifications to the State Conservation Commission, with civil penalties up to $500 for the first day and $100 per day after (Section 1314). It also creates a Department of Agriculture hauler/broker certification program with penalties up to $1,000 for the first day and $500 per day after (Section 1323).","The Veterans' Trust Fund Board is created as an advisory board with a quorum of four, and the Auditor General must deliver a performance audit by July 31, 2028 and every three years thereafter."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills address gaming. The closest analogues are other omnibus budget vehicles such as AK HB53, AL HB238, and California's budget and trailer bills (CA AB101, AB109, AB111). Unlike those appropriation acts, PA SB146 is a substantive Fiscal Code rewrite that carries unrelated regulatory programs, such as food processing residuals certification and cigarette stamping agent sourcing rules, alongside budget transfers. Its title also flags gaming-fund items (Race Horse Development Fund distributions and the State Gaming Fund) that the other states' bills do not touch.","watch":"Obtain the full enacted text to review the Race Horse Development Fund distribution, State Gaming Fund, and amusement tax clarification sections, which are listed in the title but absent from this excerpt and could redirect gaming tax revenue or affect local amusement tax exposure for gaming or entertainment operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":165230,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:46.095Z"},"RI-H7953":{"summary":"H7953 would create a new chapter 6-63 requiring social media companies with at least 5 million account holders worldwide to bar Rhode Island residents under 18 from holding accounts and to verify the age of all new and existing Rhode Island account holders starting January 1, 2027. It is enforced by the Department of Business Regulation and the Attorney General, subject to a 30-day cure period, and it also creates a private right of action. Wagering appears only in an exclusion: platforms providing interactive or virtual gaming are carved out of the definition of social media platform.","mechanics":["Coverage (6-63-1(8)-(9)): an interactive computer service with at least 5,000,000 account holders worldwide that lets users create profiles, upload posts, view others' posts and interact. There are 21 exclusions, including interactive or virtual gaming services (9)(ii)(E) and e-commerce where interaction is 'generally limited' to reviews, comments and lists of goods (9)(ii)(D).","Outright ban and age verification (6-63-2): from January 1, 2027, no minor (under 18) Rhode Island resident may be an account holder, and there is no parental-consent path despite the section heading. Age must be verified when an account is opened, or within 14 days of an existing user's attempt to access the account. Accounts that are not verified must be denied access.","DBR rulemaking: rules must cover verification methods, specifically valid government-issued ID. Verification data must be used only for compliance and securely disposed of, and any verification agent must have its principal place of business in the U.S.","Agency enforcement (6-63-4): DBR may impose administrative fines up to $2,500 per violation. A 30-day notice-and-cure period applies before any action. Repeat violators of an order face civil penalties up to $5,000. The AG may seek injunctions, disgorgement and damages, and the AG can act without a referral.","Private right of action (6-63-6): suit is filed in superior court and the plaintiff recovers the greater of $2,500 per incident or actual damages, plus attorneys' fees. Waivers, arbitration limits and choice-of-law clauses are void (6-63-7).","Status: held for further study in committee on April 8, 2026. The effective date would be January 1, 2027."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is closest to MI HB4388 (regulation of social media use by minors) and NC H462 (personal data privacy/social media safety). Unlike a parental-consent model, H7953 imposes a flat under-18 ban with mandatory government-ID-based verification and a $2,500 statutory-damages private right of action. CO HB1255 (operator reporting to law enforcement) and MD SB932 (display of user location) regulate platforms on different axes. The gaming-focused bills on the list, such as CT HB05038 (under-21 bar on prediction-market use and advertising) and the IL, MD and MA iGaming bills, regulate wagering directly, whereas H7953 expressly excludes interactive gaming services from its scope.","watch":"Operators should confirm their products fall within the interactive-gaming exclusion in 6-63-1(9)(ii)(E). Collectibles clients running live card breaks or community features on e-commerce platforms should check whether live chat and social interaction push them beyond the narrow 'generally limited' e-commerce carve-out in (9)(ii)(D) and the 5-million-account threshold, which would expose them to the under-18 ban and the private right of action if the bill revives.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":16405,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:46.406Z"},"RI-S2968":{"summary":"S2968 would create a Rhode Island Social Media Regulation Act (new chapter 6-63) requiring social media companies with at least 5 million account holders worldwide to verify the age of every Rhode Island account holder and bar minors under 18 from holding accounts without express parental or guardian consent. Minor accounts would be subject to an advertising ban, data-collection limits, direct-messaging and search restrictions, parental access, and a default 10:30 p.m. to 6:30 a.m. curfew. The Department of Business Regulation would enforce the Act, and there is a private right of action. The bill is not a gaming bill, and Senate Judiciary held it for further study on 2026-04-07.","mechanics":["Coverage (6-63-1(8)-(9)): the Act applies to interactive computer services running a social media platform with 5,000,000 or more account holders worldwide. Twenty-one carve-outs are listed, including interactive or virtual gaming services (9)(ii)(E), licensed-media streaming (B), preselected news and sports content (C), and e-commerce where user interaction is generally limited to reviews, product lists and wish lists (D).","Age verification and consent (6-63-2): starting January 1, 2027, companies must verify age at account opening, or within 14 days of an existing user's attempted access, and deny access until verification and parental consent are complete. DBR must write rules on acceptable ID methods, consent confirmation, and data retention and disposal, and any verification agent must have its principal place of business in the U.S.","Restrictions on minor accounts (6-63-3): no advertising may be displayed, no personal data may be collected beyond what compliance requires, no direct messages or search visibility for unlinked (non-friend) users, and no targeted or suggested content, groups, products or accounts.","Parental access and curfew (6-63-4, 6-63-5): consenting parents must get full access to the minor's posts and messages. A default 10:30 p.m. to 6:30 a.m. lockout applies, measured by the minor's IP address, and parents can change it and set daily hour limits.","Agency enforcement (6-63-7): DBR may impose administrative fines of up to $2,500 per violation, sue for injunction, disgorgement and damages, and recover attorney fees. Repeat violators of an order face up to $5,000. A mandatory 30-day notice-and-cure period applies before DBR can act.","Private action and waivers (6-63-9, 6-63-10): subsection (a) authorizes private suits only for 6-63-2 violations, but subsection (c) awards relief for violations of 6-63-2, 6-63-3 or 6-63-4, which is internally inconsistent. Relief is the greater of $2,500 per incident or actual damages, plus attorney fees. Contractual waivers and choice-of-law limits on these protections are void."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a minor-access social media bill most comparable to MI HB4388 (regulation of social media use by minors) and NC H462 (personal data privacy and social media safety). It differs from MD SB932, which requires location display, and CO HB1255, which covers law-enforcement reporting and warrants. Its only gaming touchpoint is the express exclusion of interactive gaming services from the definition of social media platform. That makes it unlike CT HB05038, which age-gates prediction markets to 21 and restricts advertising to under-21s directly, and unlike the iGaming authorization bills listed (for example IL HB4797 and MD HB1343).","watch":"Operators should note that the 6-63-1(9)(ii)(E) gaming exclusion and the narrow e-commerce exclusion in (D) may not shelter live-streamed card breaks or mystery-pack platforms that offer open chat, and that the ban on displaying advertising in minor accounts would affect any operator or affiliate social media marketing that reaches Rhode Island minors if the bill is revived after being held for study.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":19905,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:53.089Z"},"SD-SB111":{"summary":"SB111 amends South Dakota's electronic transactions chapter (ch. 53-12) to require very large social media services, those with more than 100 million monthly active users whose primary focus is not charity or religion, to give South Dakota users their personal data in a portable format. It also requires those services to maintain a transparent, third-party-accessible interoperability interface, including one-time or continuous export of a user's social graph. It does not regulate gambling, and its only connection to gaming is that it excludes 'interactive gaming' services from the definition of social media service.","mechanics":["Coverage (Sections 3 to 6): applies only to a social media service or company with more than 100 million active monthly users whose primary focus is not charity or religion. Section 7 exempts entities owned or operated by a religious organization that are property-tax exempt.","Definitions (§53-12-1(19)): a 'social media service' excludes services identifiable as email, cloud storage, interactive gaming, or document sharing, and excludes services consisting primarily of e-commerce where chat or comments are incidental. A 'user' is a South Dakota resident acting in an individual or household context.","Data access (Section 3): on request, a covered service must provide the user's personal data in a format that is portable, readily usable, and transmittable to another social media service without impediment.","Social graph export (Section 5): users may export all or a subset of their data (by type, by date, or since the last transfer). Exports must be machine-readable and use a royalty-free, patent-free public standard. Users may choose a single export or continuous exports at least every 24 hours. Reasonable privacy and security terms are permitted if they are non-discriminatory and do not self-preference.","Security and safe harbors: Section 6 requires securing personal data obtained through the interface. Section 5 preserves platforms' ability to act on security, fraud, harassment, or illegal activity, and Section 8 imposes no obligation to import data.","Effective July 1, 2027 (Section 9). The text contains no express enforcement mechanism or penalty provision."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Several listed bills also regulate social media platforms but take different approaches. CO HB1255 requires reporting adverse user actions to law enforcement, MD SB932 requires displaying users' general location, MI HB4388 targets minors' use, and NC H462 pairs data privacy with social media safety. SD SB111 is distinct as a data-portability and interoperability mandate limited to platforms above 100 million monthly active users. None of the listed gaming bills, such as the IL HB3080/HB4797 or MD HB1343 iGaming authorizations, overlap substantively, because SB111 expressly carves interactive gaming out of its scope.","watch":"Operators should confirm that the 'interactive gaming' and primarily-e-commerce exclusions in §53-12-1(19)(e)-(f) keep betting, iGaming, sweepstakes, and live card-break marketplaces out of scope, though the 100 million monthly active user threshold makes coverage unlikely in practice; they should also note that player data they receive via social-graph imports from major platforms after July 1, 2027 may trigger the Section 6 security obligations only if they themselves are a covered service.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":12417,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:03.979Z"},"SC-H3431":{"summary":"The text provided (House amendment 1A of January 14, 2026) adds a new Chapter 80 to Title 39, an 'Age-Appropriate Code Design' law that imposes a duty of reasonable care on large online services reasonably likely to be accessed by minors under 18. It requires default-on safety and privacy settings, data minimization, bans on targeted advertising and profiling of known minors, parental tools, and annual independent third-party audit reports. It also prohibits facilitating ads for gambling and other age-restricted products to known minors, and is enforced by the Attorney General with treble damages and possible personal liability for officers. The bill's long title still describes a different social-media account bill (Article 9, Chapter 5), so the enacted Act No. 96 should be checked against this amendment text.","mechanics":["Coverage (39-80-10(4), (17)): applies to entities doing business in SC that meet one of three tests: over $25M annual gross revenue (CPI-adjusted), data on 50,000+ consumers, or 50%+ of revenue from selling or sharing personal data. The service must also be 'reasonably likely to be accessed by minors', meaning it has actual knowledge, including inferred or marketing-attributed age, or is directed to children under COPPA.","Duty of care (39-80-20): reasonable care to prevent compulsive usage, severe psychological harm, financial injury and other listed harms, limited to harms actionable notwithstanding 47 U.S.C. 230. 'Covered design features' include gamification (streaks, badges, rewards), in-game purchases of digital items or tokens, notifications, infinite scroll and autoplay.","User tools and defaults (39-80-30, -40): known minors get default-on safeguards, including opt-outs from design features, time limits, user-set caps on purchase value, and opt-out of personalized recommendations. Other rules: no targeted advertising to minors, no profiling, data minimization, deletion of age-verification data, and a notification-blocking option for 10 p.m.–6 a.m. daily and 8 a.m.–3 p.m. on weekdays from August to May.","Advertising and dark patterns (39-80-60(B)-(C)): bars facilitating ads directed to known minors for products prohibited to minors, expressly including gambling, tobacco, alcohol and narcotics. Dark patterns are an unfair trade practice under SCUTPA (39-5-20).","Parental tools and audit (39-80-50, -70): parental controls over settings, purchases and time-of-day use are on by default. An annual public report by an independent auditor is due by July 1 to the Attorney General and must cover design features, data use, age-verification methods and algorithms.","Enforcement (39-80-80, Sections 2–4): the Attorney General enforces, with treble financial damages. Officers and employees face personal liability for wilful and wanton violations. Where laws conflict, the most protective law controls. The act is severable and effective on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a comparable age-appropriate design code. AL HB351 is a general consumer data-privacy bill, AL HJR51 only creates a study commission on AI and children's internet safety, CA AB1159 addresses student personal information, and AK HB47 targets AI-generated sexual depictions. Unusual features of SC H3431 on its own are: personal officer and employee liability for wilful violations; treble damages; a mandatory third-party audit posted by the Attorney General; a statutory school-hours notification blackout; and an express bar on gambling ads to known minors embedded in a platform-design law.","watch":"Operators should watch whether ad-delivery platforms over-filter gambling and iGaming ads to avoid 'facilitating' them to users with inferred minor age under 39-80-60(B). Collectibles companies selling digital or mystery packs or running gamified breaks on covered platforms should watch whether 'in-game purchases' and 'gamification' design features pull them into the duty-of-care and purchase-limit requirements, especially given treble damages and personal officer liability.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":26133,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:04.088Z"},"SC-S268":{"summary":"S. 268 adds Chapter 80 to Title 39, an Age-Appropriate Code Design act imposing a duty of reasonable care on large online services reasonably likely to be accessed by minors (under 18) to prevent listed harms, including compulsive use and material financial injury. It requires default-on safety and privacy settings, parental tools, data minimization, a ban on targeted advertising to minors, and annual independent third-party audits filed with the Attorney General. Gambling appears only in 39-80-60(B), which bars covered services from facilitating ads for gambling and other age-restricted products to users they know are minors.","mechanics":["Covered online service (39-80-10(4)): an entity doing business in SC, reasonably likely to be accessed by minors, that determines the purposes and means of processing personal data, and that either has over $25M in annual gross revenue (CPI-adjusted), handles personal data of 50,000 or more consumers, households or devices, or derives 50% or more of revenue from selling data. Exempt are COPPA-compliant interactive gaming platforms, educational and career services, telecom, broadband and video programming services, and the sale of physical products.","Actual knowledge of minor status includes any age the service has inferred or attributed for marketing, advertising or product development (39-80-10(7)). Covered design features include infinite scroll, rewards for visits or engagement, push notifications, in-game purchases, and appearance filters.","Required tools default to the highest protection (39-80-30, 39-80-40(F)): minors must be able to opt out of covered design features, limit or block in-game purchases and other transactions, and control geolocation sharing. Parents must be able to restrict purchases and financial transactions, set time limits, and block use during school hours and at night (39-80-50).","Prohibitions: no targeted advertising to minors (39-80-40(B)); no notifications to known minors from 10 p.m. to 6 a.m. daily, or from 8 a.m. to 3 p.m. on weekdays August through May (39-80-40(D)); no facilitating ads for narcotics, tobacco, gambling or alcohol to known minors (39-80-60(B)); no dark patterns that undermine safeguards (39-80-60(C)).","Annual public report due July 1, prepared by an independent third-party auditor and posted by the Attorney General (39-80-70). It must cover user counts by age band, time spent, sensitive data use, age verification methods, and algorithms.","Enforcement (39-80-80): the Attorney General enforces; services are liable for treble financial damages; officers and employees face personal liability for willful and wanton violations. Harms are limited to those actionable under 47 U.S.C. 230 (39-80-20(B)). Where laws conflict, the most protective law controls. The act is effective on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a comparable age-appropriate design code. AL HB351 is a general consumer data privacy bill, AL HJR51 only creates a study commission on AI and children's internet safety, CA AB1159 addresses student personal information, and AK HB47 targets generated sexual imagery. S. 268 is unusual for combining a statutory duty of care, school-hours and overnight notification curfews, mandatory third-party audits published by the Attorney General, treble damages, and personal liability for officers and employees. It also expressly names gambling in an ad-facilitation ban tied to actual or inferred knowledge of a user's age.","watch":"Watch 39-80-60(B) and the broad inferred-age definition of 'know to be a minor': platforms may respond by restricting or over-filtering sportsbook and iGaming ad buys. Collectibles clients running card-break or mystery-pack apps or streams that minors use could themselves be covered services, triggering purchase-limit tools, reward-feature opt-outs, notification curfews, audits, and treble or personal liability, so the drafting errors in the definitions (duplicate numbering, the stray 'subsection (5)' cross-reference) are worth tracking in House Judiciary.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":24841,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:05.433Z"},"TN-SB1799":{"summary":"SB1799 adds a new part to Tennessee's consumer protection chapter (Title 47, Chapter 18, new §§ 47-18-3501 to -3503) requiring a social media platform to delete all personal information tied to an account when the account holder deletes that account. Account holders who request deletion of personal information without deleting the account fall back on the Tennessee Information Protection Act, including § 47-18-3304. Violations are enforceable through a private right of action carrying actual and punitive damages, injunctive relief, and attorney fees.","mechanics":["Deletion duty (§ 47-18-3502(a)): when an account holder deletes an account, the platform must delete all personal information about that account, or collected through it, by the account holder or another user.","Definitions (§ 47-18-3501): 'social media platform' means a site or app that allows account creation and lets account holders communicate through posts. It excludes email, broadband, cloud services, career sites, preselected-content services, online shopping, and peer-to-peer payment platforms where user interaction is limited. 'Content' excludes interactive gaming and educational entertainment.","'Personal information' excludes publicly available information, de-identified data, and aggregate consumer information.","TIPA preserved (§ 47-18-3502(b)): requests to delete data from an account that remains open are governed by TIPA, part 33, to the extent TIPA applies.","Private right of action (§ 47-18-3503): any person who believes a platform violated the deletion duty may sue for an injunction, actual damages, punitive damages, and reasonable costs and attorney fees.","Effective January 1, 2027, applying to conduct on or after that date. The bill passed the Senate and is held on the House desk as of 2026-03-12."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a data-deletion privacy bill and shares no gaming subject matter with the listed gambling bills, such as IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885, NJ A 3258, or MI SB0713/SB0714. Its closest peers are the social media bills: MI HB4388 regulates minors' use of social media, NC H462 combines data privacy with social media safety, MD SB932 requires platforms to display user location, and CO HB1255 requires platforms to report adverse actions to law enforcement. SB1799 is unusual among these because it focuses narrowly on post-deletion data purging and backs that duty with a private right of action that includes punitive damages, rather than relying on attorney general enforcement.","watch":"Operators should check whether community features in their betting, casino, or card-break apps (chat, social feeds, user posts) could make them a 'social media platform', since the gaming carve-out in § 47-18-3501(2) applies only to the 'content' definition and not to the platform definition, and blanket deletion of account data could conflict with gaming-regulator record-retention, KYC, and AML obligations while exposing the operator to private suits for punitive damages.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":5169,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:07.261Z"},"TX-HB499":{"summary":"HB 499 adds Subchapter Z to Chapter 161 of the Texas Health and Safety Code. It requires covered social media platforms to show a mental-health warning label on their landing page every time a user opens the platform, and to block use until the user confirms they understand the warning. The Health and Human Services Commission executive commissioner must adopt evidence-based rules on the label's form and content. Wagering appears only through an exemption for sites or apps used primarily for interactive gaming.","mechanics":["Sec. 161.801 borrows the definitions of 'social media platform' and 'user' from Bus. & Com. Code Sec. 120.001, and Sec. 161.8015(a) limits coverage to platforms described in Sec. 120.002(b).","Sec. 161.8015(b) exempts any website or application 'used primarily for the purpose of interactive gaming.' That term is not defined in the bill.","Sec. 161.802(a) requires the label on every open. It must include mental-health resources, including the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline phone number or website link, and a statement on the platform's use of personal data and the addictive nature of social media.","Sec. 161.802(b) requires a gate: the user cannot operate the platform until they verify they understand the warning.","HHSC rules are due by January 1, 2026, compliance is not required before April 1, 2026, and the Act takes effect September 1, 2025.","The bill text contains no penalty or enforcement provision."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a youth social-media health bill, closest to MI HB4388 (regulation of minors' social media use), NC H462 (data privacy and social media safety), MD SB932 (location display) and CO HB1255 (platform reporting duties), none of which use a per-session warning gate. Unlike the gambling-focused youth measures listed, such as CT HB05038 (no prediction-market use or advertising to under-21s) and CT SB00240 (internet gaming disorder and its link to gambling), HB 499 does not regulate wagering at all. Its one gaming-related feature is a carve-out for interactive gaming platforms. The mandatory click-through acknowledgment on every open, coupled with agency-written label content, is unusual.","watch":"Watch whether the undefined 'interactive gaming' exemption in Sec. 161.8015(b) is read to cover gambling-adjacent apps with social or chat features (and, for the collectibles clients, live card-break streaming communities), and whether the warning-label-plus-acknowledgment model is later borrowed for responsible-gaming or prediction-market messaging.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":3778,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:16.869Z"},"UT-HB505":{"summary":"Utah 1st Sub. H.B. 505 reclassifies Utah's vehicle registration charges into service fees, regulatory fees, and vehicle taxes, and reorganizes the related imposition and revenue-distribution provisions in Title 41, Chapter 1a, Part 12. It also creates a new Motor Vehicle Division Restricted Account and eliminates the six-month registration option. The bill has no sports-betting, iGaming, sweepstakes, or collectibles content, and it died when it was filed in the House file for bills not passed on March 6, 2026.","mechanics":["Most provisions take effect January 1, 2027, under a special effective date. Section 41-1a-1201 is repealed and reenacted, along with sections 41-1a-1206, -1211 and -1218, to restructure vehicle taxes and fees and how they are distributed.","New section 41-1a-124 creates the Motor Vehicle Division Restricted Account within the General Fund, funded from deposits described in 41-1a-1201. Appropriations from the account to the Tax Commission are nonlapsing.","The six-month registration under 41-1a-215.5(1) is repealed. The 24-month registration remains available for trailers, electric motor vehicles, off-highway vehicles and street-legal ATVs at double the 12-month tax or fee, but apportioned interstate vehicles are excluded.","Fleet registration-period adjustments under 41-1a-215(3)(c) are charged at 25%, 50%, 75% or 100% of the regular vehicle tax or fees, depending on adjustment length in three-month bands.","The bill appropriates $9,883,900 in General Fund capital project funds for FY2027. It also makes conforming changes that replace 'registration fee' with 'vehicle tax or fee' across insurance, health, education, tax and transportation code sections."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is substantively comparable. The closest analogue is IL HB2755, an Illinois revenue omnibus that touches Road Fund deposits, but HB 505 is a narrow vehicle-fee recodification with no gaming component. It shares nothing with the gaming-focused bills on the list: NC H999 (video gaming terminals), OH HB344 (electronic instant bingo tax), NY S 10092 (gambling ad restrictions for minors) and NE LB402/LB415 (gambling winnings setoff). The provided text is truncated, but nothing in the visible portion references wagering.","watch":"Nothing in this bill affects sports-betting, iGaming or card-break clients, and because it is dead no action is needed beyond confirming that any 2027 reintroduction does not add unrelated gaming or excise provisions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Substitute","text_chars":232989,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:18.309Z"},"TX-HB186":{"summary":"HB 186 would bar anyone under 18 from using a social media platform in Texas and require platforms to verify that a new account holder is 18 or older, using a commercially reasonable method based on public or private transactional data. It amends the Business & Commerce Code definition of social media platform (Sec. 120.001(1)) to add an exclusion for services used primarily for interactive gaming. Violations are enforceable by the Attorney General as deceptive trade practices.","mechanics":["New Sec. 120.112 says a child (under 18) may not use a social media platform, to the extent permitted by federal law including COPPA. Sec. 120.113 requires platforms to bar children from contracting for accounts and to verify age before accepting an account holder.","Age verification under Sec. 120.113(b) must use a commercially reasonable method relying on public or private transactional data. Under Sec. 120.113(c), the data may be used only for verification, may not be retained or transferred, and must be deleted immediately once verification is complete.","Sec. 120.114 requires a company to delete a child's account on all of its platforms within 10 days of a verified request from a parent or guardian, and to provide an accessible, verifiable way to make that request.","The amended definition in Sec. 120.001(1)(D) excludes any online service, application, or website used primarily for interactive gaming. 'Interactive gaming' is not defined. The existing exclusion for provider-preselected news, sports, or entertainment content with only incidental chat is retained.","Under Sec. 120.115, a violation requires a knowing failure to verify, knowingly allowing a child to use the platform, misuse of verification data, or failure to remove an account. Violations are DTPA deceptive trade practices enforceable by the AG consumer protection division under Secs. 17.47, 17.58, 17.60 and 17.61.","The Act takes effect September 1, 2025 and applies to platform access on or after January 1, 2026. The bill passed the House and was reported favorably 8-3 by Senate State Affairs on May 23, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Among the listed bills, MI HB4388, which regulates minors' use of social media, and NC H462 (Personal Data Privacy/Social Media Safety) are the closest analogues. Texas's version is unusually blunt: it is an outright under-18 ban with mandatory transactional-data age verification, not a consent or design-code model. MD SB932 (displaying user location) and CO HB1255 (law-enforcement reporting and warrants) address other social media duties and impose no age ban. CT HB05038, which bars prediction-market use by and advertising to under-21s, is the nearest gaming-side parallel, but it targets wagering platforms directly, whereas TX HB186 expressly carves 'interactive gaming' services out of its scope.","watch":"Track how 'used primarily for the purpose of interactive gaming' is read, since the term is undefined and could shield gaming apps, along with whether live-stream commerce or card-break platforms with user chat fall within the social media definition. Also watch the downstream effect on operator and collectibles marketing that reaches teen audiences through mainstream platforms, which would have to purge under-18 users.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":8132,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:21.159Z"},"VA-SB201":{"summary":"SB201 would have amended Virginia's Consumer Data Protection Act by adding § 59.1-577.2, which requires covered controllers and processors to obtain verifiable parental consent before registering anyone under 18 or before collecting, using, or disclosing that minor's personal data. This would raise the effective age for parental-consent obligations from the Act's general under-13 'child' definition to under 18. The bill died in Senate General Laws and Technology on February 4, 2026, failing to report on a 7-7-1 vote.","mechanics":["New § 59.1-577.2(A) defines 'child' as under 18 for that section only; the general § 59.1-575 definition stays at under 13, so 'sensitive data' from a 'known child' is still tied to under-13s.","Under § 59.1-577.2(B), verifiable parental consent is required before registering a child or collecting, using, or disclosing the child's personal data, and parents must be offered the option to consent to collection and use without consenting to disclosure to third parties.","Under § 59.1-577.2(C), operators must make 'reasonable efforts,' considering available technology, to verify the consenting adult is the parent or guardian, via (1) a signed consent form, (2) a credit card, debit card, or online payment transaction that notifies the primary account holder, or (3) any valid government-issued ID.","Under § 59.1-576(A), the Act applies only to entities that control or process personal data of at least 100,000 Virginia consumers a year, or at least 25,000 consumers while deriving over 50% of gross revenue from data sales; § 59.1-576(B) exempts state bodies, GLBA financial institutions, HIPAA entities, nonprofits, and higher-ed institutions.","Under § 59.1-576(D), compliance with COPPA's verifiable parental consent requirements is deemed compliance with the chapter's parental consent obligations, although COPPA itself covers only under-13s.","No new penalty or enforcement mechanism is added, so the Act's existing enforcement would apply. Status: defeated in committee on February 4, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05038, which bars prediction-market platforms from serving or advertising to under-21s, SB201 is a general privacy measure and does not target wagering. Its only gaming reference is the existing exclusion of 'interactive gaming' services from the 'social media platform' definition. It is closest to NC H462 (personal data privacy and social media safety) and MI HB4388 (regulation of minors' social media use), but it is not limited to social media: it applies to any covered controller's 'product or service' for all users under 18. The listed iGaming and sports-wagering bills (e.g., IL HB3080, MD HB1343, NJ A 3258) do not address data privacy for minors.","watch":"Although the bill is dead for 2026, a reintroduction would push teen-facing collectibles, card-break, and mystery-pack platforms that meet the 100,000-consumer threshold toward verifiable parental consent for every 13–17-year-old user, and because § 59.1-577.2(B) has no actual-knowledge qualifier, it would effectively require age-screening all users.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":18014,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:25.207Z"},"VA-SB245":{"summary":"Virginia SB245 (Chapter 338, approved April 6, 2026) adds Va. Code § 22.1-79.3:2, requiring each school board to bar public K-12 schools, school board employees, and school volunteers from using a social media platform as the sole means of communicating with students to facilitate school-related extracurricular activities. Division-approved communication or messaging apps remain permitted, and superintendents may grant revocable written exceptions. The bill has nothing to do with sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles.","mechanics":["Definitions cross-reference existing law: \"extracurricular activities\" as defined in § 22.1-370 and \"social media platform\" as defined in § 59.1-575 (subsection A).","Covered actors are public elementary and secondary schools, school board employees, and school volunteers (subsection B).","The prohibition is narrow. It bars social media only as the sole means of communication with students for school-related extracurricular activities, and it expressly preserves school division-approved communication or messaging applications (subsection B).","A division superintendent or designee may grant a written exception where social media use is required to meet an objective that cannot reasonably be achieved otherwise, must give clear written instructions, and may revoke the exception at any time (subsection C).","The text specifies no penalty or enforcement mechanism and no special effective date, so Virginia's default July 1 effective date would presumably apply."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close analogue. The nearest overlap is with child online-safety and student-data measures such as AL HJR51 (AI and Children's Internet Safety Study Commission), CA AB1159 (student personal information), and AK HB47 (generated child sexual abuse material and teaching certificates). Those target platforms, data processors, or criminal conduct, whereas SB245 regulates only how school personnel communicate with students. What is unusual is its modest design: it does not ban social media outright, but forbids it as the exclusive channel for extracurricular communications, with a superintendent exception process.","watch":"There is nothing for sports-betting, iGaming, or trading-card clients here beyond a general signal of Virginia's interest in minors' online exposure, which could resurface in age-verification or youth-marketing proposals affecting social-media advertising or live card-break streams.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1639,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:27.242Z"},"VA-SB615":{"summary":"SB615 adds a new § 59.1-577.2 to Virginia's Consumer Data Protection Act that bars controllers and processors from setting a price offered to a consumer through the consumer's online device based on the device's hardware or hardware state, the presence or absence of software on the device, or precise geolocation data used to infer things about the consumer. It also adds supporting definitions to § 59.1-575 and carves out device repair and trade-in pricing, real-time local demand pricing, legitimate location-based cost and tax differences, and generally available coupons and discounts.","mechanics":["Prohibited inputs (§ 59.1-577.2(A)): in connection with a 'consumer transaction' (as defined in § 59.1-198), a price may not be generated 'in whole or in part' from (1) device hardware or hardware state, (2) presence or absence of any software, or (3) precise geolocation data (within 1,750 feet) when used to infer things about the consumer.","New definitions in § 59.1-575: 'hardware state' (battery life, number of wireless connections detected, performance characteristics, device age, and volatile and persistent data); 'online device' (laptop, tablet, phone, or other smart hardware); 'coupon'; 'discount'; and 'consumer transaction' by cross-reference to the Virginia Consumer Protection Act.","Exceptions (§ 59.1-577.2(B)): hardware-based pricing for device repair, maintenance, or trade-in value; geolocation pricing based on real-time demand nearby for goods or services provided immediately on request, or on legitimate location cost differences including state or local taxes, duties, and fees.","Promotions safe harbor: coupons, discounts, rebates, sales, or promotions are allowed only if they are separate from the price, offered to the general public on the same terms, and do not use any of the prohibited inputs.","Applies to controllers and processors through the existing CDPA framework; the bill text adds no separate penalty or effective-date clause.","Status: continued to the 2027 session by Senate General Laws and Technology, 15-0."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses algorithmic or device-based price discrimination. The closest are general privacy and platform bills such as NC H462 (personal data privacy/social media safety), MD SB932 (displaying social media users' locations), and MI HB4388 (minors' social media use), none of which regulates pricing inputs. The gaming bills listed, such as MI SB0713/SB0714 on iGaming advertising and promotions and NJ A 3258 on micro-bets, regulate wagering products directly rather than through consumer data law. What stands out in SB615 is that it targets 'surveillance pricing' based on device fingerprinting and inferred location, not data collection or disclosure.","watch":"Operators should watch whether odds, deposit bonuses, or personalized promotions could be treated as a 'price' in a 'consumer transaction', since the promotions safe harbor covers only offers made to the general public on the same terms without device or geolocation inputs, which could reach tailored, device-segmented, or geolocation-informed offers and personalized pack or card-break pricing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":14440,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:37.502Z"},"VA-SB232":{"summary":"SB232 amends Virginia's Consumer Data Protection Act to add a general \"minor\" definition (under 18) and new duties for controllers of any \"online service, product, or feature\" toward known minors. It bars such controllers from entering terms of service, user, or account-creation agreements with a known minor unless a parent gives verified consent, and requires parental controls at that point, including the ability to block the minor's purchases. It also imposes advertising and algorithm disclosure duties and a duty of reasonable care to prevent specified harms to minors, including exposure to ads for gambling and other products unlawful for minors.","mechanics":["Scope is unchanged under § 59.1-576 A: businesses that control or process personal data of at least 100,000 Virginia consumers a year, or at least 25,000 consumers if over 50% of gross revenue comes from data sales. Nonprofits, state agencies, GLBA financial institutions, HIPAA entities and higher education remain exempt.","New § 59.1-577.2 bars agreements (terms of service, user, or account creation) with known minors under 18 absent verified parental consent. Permitted consent methods include a signed form, toll-free call, videoconference, government ID check with deletion afterward, email plus additional verification, or any other commercially reasonable method.","At consent, the parent must be offered controls under § 59.1-577.2 C. These include the highest privacy setting, data minimization, no sale of the minor's data, no precise geolocation, no targeted ads, and the ability to prevent the minor from making purchases or financial transactions.","§ 59.1-577 B gives parents an expanded access right to all data held on the minor. The response must cover third-party recipients, sources, retention period, and any index or score assigned to the minor and how it is used.","Under § 59.1-577.3 A–B, controllers that let advertisers reach known minors must disclose at display time the brand, subject matter, why and how the ad was targeted, and whether the content is an ad. Controllers using recommendation algorithms on known minors must describe them in the terms of service.","§ 59.1-577.3 C imposes a duty of reasonable care to prevent listed harms to known minors, including addiction-like patterns of use and ads for products unlawful for minors (expressly including gambling), plus predatory, unfair, or deceptive marketing. Effective January 1, 2027; enforcement runs through the existing Act's Attorney General mechanism."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05038, which directly bars prediction-market platforms from serving or advertising to under-21s, SB232 reaches gambling only through a general duty of care to prevent gambling ads to known minors under 18 on any covered online service. It is closer to NC H462 and MI HB4388, which address minors' data and social media. However, SB232 goes beyond social media to cover any online service, product, or feature, and adds a parental-consent gate on minors' account agreements. Its existing social-media definition expressly excludes services \"for interactive gaming,\" but the new §§ 59.1-577.2 and 59.1-577.3 duties are not limited to social media.","watch":"Watch the § 59.1-577.3 C(5) duty to prevent gambling ads to known minors and the \"addiction\" and \"predatory marketing\" prongs, which could be invoked against operator advertising on third-party platforms. For card-break and mystery-pack sellers, the § 59.1-577.2 parental-consent requirement for minors' accounts and the parental purchase-blocking control apply to any business meeting the 100,000-consumer threshold.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":29873,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:37.956Z"},"VA-SB85":{"summary":"SB85, as substituted by the House Communications, Technology and Innovation Committee, amends Virginia's Consumer Data Protection Act to extend deletion and portability rights to \"social graph data\" held by social media platforms and \"contextual data\" held by deployers of general-purpose generative AI models. It requires both to build open-protocol, third-party-accessible interoperability interfaces so users can share that data in real time with competing platforms or AI models. The House committee tabled the bill 18-3 on March 9, 2026, so it is effectively dead for the session.","mechanics":["New definitions in § 59.1-575 cover \"social graph data\" (user content, followers and follows, engagements, public profile, metadata and relational references, but not other users' content), \"contextual data\" (prompts, conversation histories, files, preferences and metadata across sessions, excluding model trade secrets), \"deployer\", \"general-purpose generative AI model\" and \"open protocol\".","§ 59.1-577(A)(3)-(4) adds social graph and contextual data to the existing deletion and portability rights; the portability right expressly does not require a controller to reveal trade secrets.","New § 59.1-577.2(A)-(C) requires platforms and deployers to offer interoperability interfaces with continuous, real-time sharing on an open protocol and on non-discriminatory terms. Covered entities must also provide consent mechanisms and disclosures, and may set reasonable request thresholds, including a reasonable access fee.","§ 59.1-577.2(D) exempts internally derived inferences, proprietary algorithms and ranking systems, and certain proprietary-format data, and does not require platforms to import data. Subsection (E) limits use of received data to security, the user-requested service and maintaining interoperability.","The existing \"social media platform\" definition excludes services that are primarily news, sports, entertainment or ecommerce with only incidental interactive features, as well as services \"for interactive gaming\".","The effective date is July 1, 2027, with enforcement through the existing CDPA Attorney General framework."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"AL HB351 is a general consumer data privacy bill that regulates processing and consumer actions, but on its description it does not impose the social-graph and AI-context interoperability mandate found in SB85. The AI bills listed, such as CA AB1018 (automated decision systems), CA AB1609 (chatbots) and AZ SB1786 (content verification), address disclosure, accuracy or verification rather than data portability to competitors. SB85 is unusual in bringing a competition-style, real-time interoperability obligation into a state privacy statute and applying it to generative AI conversation histories.","watch":"The bill touches wagering only through the existing \"interactive gaming\" and \"sports\" carve-outs in the social media platform definition, so operators should watch whether any revived version narrows those exclusions in a way that pulls in betting apps with social or community features, or AI chat features that would count as a deployer.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":23591,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:38.425Z"},"VA-SB671":{"summary":"SB671 would create a licensed adult-use retail cannabis market in Virginia under the Virginia Cannabis Control Authority. It adds new licensing chapters (4.1-700 through 4.1-1008), rewrites the social-equity program as a micro business program, and revises criminal penalties, including repealing §§ 18.2-248.1 and 18.2-251.1. Nothing in the provided text concerns sports betting, iGaming, sweepstakes, or collectibles.","mechanics":["§ 4.1-606 B 7 raises the edible THC caps from 5 mg to 10 mg per serving and from 50 mg to 100 mg per package.","§ 4.1-606 B 13 replaces social-equity applicants with micro business applicants. These need 12 months' Virginia residency and at least 66% ownership and control by persons meeting criteria such as a prior marijuana misdemeanor (or a family member with one), residence or schooling in a historically economically disadvantaged community, Pell Grant receipt, or veteran status. Licensing priority rises with the number of criteria met.","The license categories are restructured: 'manufacturing' becomes 'processing' and 'wholesaler' becomes 'transporter', with each type defined in new §§ 4.1-800 to 4.1-804. 'Canopy' is defined for cultivation limits.","§ 4.1-603 D 3 requires the Cannabis Public Health Advisory Council to approve or deny Board public-health regulations within 30 days; if it does not act in time, the Board may adopt them without approval.","§ 4.1-604 gives the Board inspection access to any business selling cannabinoid-labeled consumables, quarterly reporting duties, and authority to retain its own counsel. The Cannabis Equity Reinvestment Fund (§ 2.2-2499.8) would also fund the Indigent Defense Commission and the Cannabis Equity Business Loan Fund.","§ 4.1-606 B 16 directs time, place, and manner limits on outdoor cannabis advertising. The provided text is truncated, so tax provisions such as the § 58.1-301 amendment are not visible."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest listed analog is OH SB56, which also revises adult-use marijuana law and levies marijuana taxes, and NC H680 (Protect Children from Cannabis Act). VA SB671 differs in building the retail market from scratch around a micro business preference system and expanded equity funds. None of the listed gaming bills, such as NC H999 (video gaming terminals), OH HB344 (electronic instant bingo) or NY S 10092 (gambling ads to minors), overlaps in substance.","watch":"There is no gaming or collectibles exposure in the visible text, but the truncated remainder should be checked for any amendments to § 59.1-200 (consumer protection) or other general provisions that could reach promotional or chance-based sales practices.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":473629,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:45.570Z"},"VA-SJR133":{"summary":"Virginia SJR 133 is a commemorative joint resolution noting the death of Winchester resident Evan Morgan Jewell on February 2, 2026, and directing the Clerk of the Senate to give a copy to his family. It creates no legal obligations and does not touch gaming, wagering, or collectibles regulation. The only arguably relevant reference is a biographical note that he was an avid Magic: The Gathering trading card game player.","mechanics":["Agreed to by the Senate on March 5, 2026 and by the House of Delegates on March 9, 2026; enrolled as SJ133ER.","Operative clauses only record the General Assembly's sadness at his death and direct the Clerk of the Senate to prepare a copy for his family.","Recitals mention his swimming, 4-H, Masonic lodge membership, conservation club work, Kenpo Karate black belt, and Magic: The Gathering play.","No definitions, rates, licensing, enforcement, or effective-date provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing in the listed bills is comparable. Those include Illinois revenue and budget vehicles (IL HB2755, HB2949), Michigan omnibus appropriations (MI HB4706, HB5619, SB0878), NC H999 on video gaming terminals, OH HB344 on electronic instant bingo, and NY S 10092 restricting gambling ads to minors. All of them carry substantive policy or fiscal provisions. SJR 133 is a purely ceremonial memorial resolution, and its passing mention of a trading card game has no regulatory significance.","watch":"Nothing to watch: this is a ceremonial memorial resolution with no regulatory effect on sports betting, iGaming, or trading-card and card-break businesses, and it can be removed from the tracking list.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":2200,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:47.975Z"},"WA-HB1203":{"summary":"HB 1203 would ban retailers from selling, offering, displaying, marketing, or advertising any flavored tobacco or nicotine product (including menthol and cooling-sensation products) or any \"entertainment vapor product\" in Washington starting January 1, 2026. Enforcement runs through Liquor and Cannabis Board licensing sanctions and the Consumer Protection Act. The bill's references to \"video games\" and \"interactive gaming\" describe vape devices with built-in screens or games and have nothing to do with wagering.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 defines \"entertainment vapor product\" as any vapor product with interactive gaming or entertainment features, such as playing music, displaying photos or video, playing virtual games, or showing animations. \"Flavored tobacco or nicotine product\" covers any non-tobacco taste or smell, including menthol and mint, or any cooling or numbing sensation. FDA-authorized products and cannabis are excluded.","Sec. 3 bars retail sale, display, marketing, or advertising of covered products from January 1, 2026. A rebuttable presumption of flavoring arises from manufacturer or retailer communications using terms such as \"cool,\" \"chill,\" \"ice,\" \"fresh,\" \"arctic,\" or \"frost.\"","Sec. 6 makes a violation a per se unfair or deceptive practice under RCW 19.86.020 (the Consumer Protection Act).","Sec. 9 adds violations of Sec. 3 to the higher RCW 70.155.100(3)(b) penalty tier: $1,000, then $2,500, then $5,000 plus a 6-month suspension, then $10,000 plus a 12-month suspension, then 5-year revocation, all within a three-year period. Sec. 11 adds Sec. 3 violations to the vapor-license sanctions in RCW 70.345.180.","Secs. 8 and 10 require new prohibition signage. Sec. 4 directs a Department of Health awareness campaign. Sec. 7 authorizes tribal compact consultations. Secs. 12 and 13 fold the new product definitions into the vapor and tobacco tax chapters."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They address iGaming authorization (e.g., IL HB3080, MD HB1343, MA H332), gaming advertising (MI SB0713/SB0714), prediction markets (CT HB05038), and social media, while HB 1203 is a tobacco and nicotine flavor ban. Its only overlap is linguistic: the Sec. 2 definition of \"entertainment vapor product\" uses the phrase \"interactive gaming\" for vape devices with built-in games, which likely triggered the keyword match. The one structurally notable feature is its marketing-language presumption combined with per se Consumer Protection Act liability, a model sometimes borrowed for other restricted-product advertising regimes.","watch":"Nothing in HB 1203 regulates wagering, iGaming, or collectibles, so operators need only note that its \"interactive gaming\" language refers to vape devices; the advertising-terms presumption and per se Consumer Protection Act enforcement model is worth tracking only as a potential template for future restricted-product marketing bills in Washington.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":38203,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:53.386Z"},"WA-HB1834":{"summary":"Third Substitute HB 1834, requested by the Attorney General, creates a new chapter in Title 19 RCW that bars operators of \"addictive internet-based services or applications\" from serving algorithmically personalized \"addictive feeds\" to Washington minors and restricts push notifications to minors during overnight and school hours without verifiable parental consent. It also requires all users to be offered feed time limits, hidden like counts, a non-personalized default feed, and a private-account mode. Violations are enforceable as unfair or deceptive acts under the Consumer Protection Act, chapter 19.86 RCW. The bill does not address wagering or gaming at all.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 defines \"addictive feed\" as user-generated or user-shared media recommended, selected, or prioritized using information tied to the user or device. Carve-outs include non-persistent data, search terms, express user requests, private messages, and next-in-sequence content with no autoplay. Sites whose user interactions are limited to commercial transactions or consumer reviews, and cloud-storage feeds, are excluded.","Sec. 2 sets a two-stage age standard for providing addictive feeds. Before July 1, 2027, an operator is liable only with actual knowledge that the user is a minor (under 18 and a Washington resident). From July 1, 2027, the operator must have \"reasonably determined\" that the user is not a minor.","Sec. 3 bans push notifications to minors from 12–6 a.m., and from 8 a.m.–3 p.m. on weekdays September through May, absent verifiable parental consent. The same actual-knowledge and reasonable-determination standards apply before and after July 1, 2027. Software update, security, and account-integrity notices are exempt.","Sec. 4 requires tools available to all users, not only minors: daily time limits on the addictive feed, hiding likes and other feedback, a non-personalized default feed, and a private account mode.","Sec. 6 provides that compliance is not a defense to mental-health harm claims by minors. Sec. 7 limits use and retention of personal data collected for age determination.","Sec. 8 makes violations per se unfair or deceptive acts under RCW 19.86. Sec. 11 voids the act unless specific funding is provided in the omnibus appropriations act by June 30, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest listed analogues are the minor social media bills: MI HB4388 (regulating social media use by minors) and NC H462 (personal data privacy and social media safety). WA HB1834 targets feed design and notification timing rather than account access or data privacy generally. It differs from CO HB1255 (law-enforcement reporting of adverse actions) and MD SB932 (displaying user location), which regulate platform disclosures rather than algorithmic feeds. Unlike CT HB05038, which bars prediction-market platforms from serving or advertising to under-21s, HB1834 contains no gambling-specific age gate or advertising rule.","watch":"The bill is in the House Rules \"X\" file and dies without funding under Sec. 11, but operators should check whether any app or live card-break streaming platform with personalized feeds of user-shared video, chat, or social content falls outside the Sec. 1(2)(b)(i) commercial-transaction exclusion and would trigger the post-July 1, 2027 age-determination and push-notification limits.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":9711,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:57.394Z"},"WA-HB2439":{"summary":"Substitute HB 2439 is a tobacco and vapor product bill. It creates a voluntary responsible vendor program for tobacco and vapor retailers and tightens licensed-distributor sourcing rules. It also requires nicotine vapor manufacturers to be certified by the Liquor and Cannabis Board from January 1, 2027, mandates digital ID verification for all tobacco and vapor sales, and from 2027 bans 'entertainment vapor products' and vapes that imitate products marketed to minors. It does not regulate wagering, gaming, or collectibles.","mechanics":["Secs. 2-4: the Liquor and Cannabis Board must create a free, voluntary responsible vendor program. Participating licensees get a 50 percent reduction of the monetary penalty for a single violation in any 12-month period.","Secs. 5-10: retailers may buy tobacco, vapor products, and cigarettes only from licensed distributors or wholesalers. Product bought from unlicensed sources, including directly from a manufacturer, is contraband subject to seizure unless the retailer also holds a distributor license. Operating without a vapor license is a class C felony (Sec. 7).","Secs. 11-13: from January 1, 2027, nicotine vapor manufacturers must be certified, at $1,000 per product type or model initially and $500 per model on each annual renewal. Nonresident manufacturers not registered in Washington must post a $25,000 surety bond and appoint an in-state agent for service of process.","Secs. 21-22: retailers must verify every tobacco or vapor purchaser's ID with a digital device or application. The defense to prosecution under RCW 26.28.080 is tied to a digital verification showing the purchaser is 21 or over.","Secs. 23-24: from January 1, 2027, the bill bans selling or advertising 'entertainment vapor products', meaning devices with interactive gaming, music, video, or animation features. It also bans vapes that imitate candy, school supplies, or characters, celebrities, or mythical creatures known to appeal to minors.","Sec. 26 declares these practices matters affecting the public interest for the Consumer Protection Act, chapter 19.86 RCW (the text is truncated there). Sec. 19 orders an Ecology study of vape extended producer responsibility, with a report due May 1, 2028."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable; they concern internet gaming, sports wagering, prediction markets, social media, and budgets. The nearest thematic overlaps are age-gating and youth-appeal rules. CT HB05038 bars prediction markets from use by or advertising to under-21s, and MI SB0713/SB0714 regulate iGaming advertising and promotions. CT SB00240 likewise treats 'internet gaming' as a public-health issue. Unusually, the only 'gaming' reference in HB 2439 (Sec. 23) is to video-game features built into vape devices, not wagering.","watch":"There is no direct operator exposure, but Sec. 24's ban on imitating characters, celebrities, or mythical creatures known to appeal to minors, backed by the Consumer Protection Act, and the mandatory digital ID check in Secs. 21-22 are templates that Washington could carry over to gambling advertising or to youth-appealing collectibles and mystery-pack products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":80170,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:19:04.724Z"},"WA-HB2068":{"summary":"HB 2068 would ban the retail sale, display, marketing, and advertising of all flavored tobacco and nicotine products, including menthol, and of 'entertainment vapor products' (vapes with built-in games, music, video, or animations) starting July 1, 2027. It also raises retailer penalties and license fees, bans nicotine analogues, adds a $2 per pack cigarette surtax, and revises vapor product taxation. It is a tobacco and nicotine control bill and does not regulate gambling.","mechanics":["Sec. 4 bars retailers from selling, displaying, marketing, or advertising flavored tobacco or nicotine products or entertainment vapor products from July 1, 2027. Marketing terms like 'cool,' 'ice,' or 'arctic' create a rebuttable presumption that a product is flavored. Flavored shisha sold in 21+ premises is exempt.","Sec. 3(1) defines 'entertainment vapor product' as any vapor product with interactive gaming or entertainment features, such as playing music, showing photos or video, playing virtual games, or displaying animations.","Enforcement sits with the Liquor and Cannabis Board (Secs. 6, 12, 19). Penalties for underage-sale and flavor-ban violations rise to $2,000, $4,000, $8,000, and $12,000 across successive violations, and to $20,000 plus five-year license revocation for a fifth violation within three years. Under Sec. 7, a violation is also a per se Consumer Protection Act violation.","Sec. 21 adds a $2 per pack cigarette tax, CPI-adjusted every three years from December 2028. The first $5M goes to the youth tobacco and vapor prevention account.","Most retailer, wholesaler, distributor, and delivery-seller license fees rise to $1,000 (Secs. 14-16, 22-23). The combined tobacco and vapor license fee discount is repealed.","Sec. 20 bans selling or furnishing any product containing a nicotine analogue. The provided text cuts off partway through the vapor product tax rewrite in Sec. 25."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They cover internet gaming authorization (IL HB3080, MD HB1343, MA H332), sports wagering consumer protection (CT SB 296, ME LD 2080, NJ A 3258), and prediction-market age limits (CT HB05038), while HB 2068 is a tobacco control bill. Its only gaming-adjacent element is the Sec. 3 'entertainment vapor product' category, which targets devices with embedded 'virtual games.' That concerns device features, not wagering, and is unrelated to the gaming-disorder focus of CT SB00240. It is notable mainly as an example of a youth-protection ban framed around game features and advertising restrictions.","watch":"There is no direct exposure for betting, iGaming, or collectibles clients, but the bill's model of banning 'display, market, or advertise' combined with a per se Consumer Protection Act violation and steep escalating penalties is a youth-protection template worth tracking in case Washington legislators apply it to gaming or mystery-pack products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":81736,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:19:04.743Z"},"WA-SB5183":{"summary":"SB 5183 is a tobacco-control bill, not a gaming bill. Starting January 1, 2026, it bars retailers from selling, offering, displaying, marketing or advertising any flavored tobacco or nicotine product (including menthol and cooling agents) or any \"entertainment vapor product\" in Washington. It is enforced through Liquor and Cannabis Board license penalties and makes any violation a per se Consumer Protection Act violation. The only \"gaming\" reference is to vapes with built-in video games, which the findings illustrate with pac-man and tetris.","mechanics":["Sec. 2(1) defines \"entertainment vapor product\" as any vapor product with \"interactive gaming or entertainment features,\" such as playing music, showing photos or video, playing virtual games or displaying animations on the device.","Sec. 2(2) defines \"flavored tobacco or nicotine product\" as any product imparting a non-tobacco taste or smell (including mint, menthol and wintergreen) or a cooling or numbing sensation.","Sec. 3(2) creates a rebuttable presumption that a product is flavored if manufacturer or retailer communications use terms like \"cool,\" \"ice\" or \"arctic.\"","Sec. 3 imposes a sale, display, marketing and advertising ban on retailers effective January 1, 2026. That date has now passed, since the bill was reintroduced in 2026 and the date would need amendment.","Sec. 9 sets Liquor and Cannabis Board penalties for licensed tobacco retailers: $1,000, $2,500, $5,000 plus a 6-month suspension, $10,000 plus a 12-month suspension, then 5-year revocation, all within a 3-year window.","Sec. 11 applies a parallel $200 to $3,000 penalty scale with suspensions and revocation to vapor licensees. Sec. 6 makes violations a per se unfair or deceptive practice under RCW 19.86. Sec. 7 authorizes tribal compact consultations, and Sec. 8 and Sec. 10 add point-of-sale signage requirements."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. SB 5183 appears to have been flagged only because Sec. 2(1) uses the phrase \"interactive gaming\" to describe video-game features built into vape devices. It shares nothing substantive with the iGaming authorization bills (IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885, MA H332/S235), the advertising bills (MI SB0713/SB0714), or CT SB00240 on Internet gaming disorder. Its only structural point of interest for gaming counsel is the enforcement model: a product ban backed by a per se state consumer protection act violation and a rebuttable presumption triggered by marketing vocabulary. Washington could reuse that model in other consumer-product contexts.","watch":"There is no direct exposure for sports-betting, iGaming or collectibles clients, but the \"interactive gaming or entertainment features\" definition and the marketing-language presumption are drafting templates worth noting if Washington later targets gamified consumer products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":38228,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:19:08.824Z"}};
